window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 142 kg man with a body mass index of 43 needs Tc-99m sestamibi imaging, and a same-day protocol is planned. A prior rest image taken with 10 mCi was count-poor, and the camera schedule is flexible. Which protocol change is best?", "options": {"A": "Same-day protocol with 40 mCi at rest and 10 mCi at stress, giving rest more counts", "B": "A two-day protocol with 25 to 30 mCi each day, since higher doses avoid carryover limits", "C": "Same-day protocol with the usual 8 and 24 mCi doses, with acquisition shortened to reduce motion", "D": "Same-day protocol with stress first at 10 mCi, then 30 mCi at rest, to keep counts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A two-day protocol with 25 to 30 mCi each day, since higher doses avoid carryover limits): Same-day protocols use a rest dose of about 8 to 10 mCi followed by a stress dose about three times larger to overcome residual rest activity. In large patients that rest dose gives poor counts, and the stress dose cannot rise freely. A two-day protocol removes the carryover and allows 25 to 30 mCi on each day. Inverting the ratio fails because residual rest activity swamps the second study."}, {"id": 2, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 38-year-old woman asks whether the 9 mSv from her Tc-99m stress-rest study could cause hair loss or skin burns, like those reported after long fluoroscopic procedures. How should the effects be classified when the physician answers her question about radiation risk today?", "options": {"A": "Both are deterministic, so cancer risk appears only above a threshold near 100 mSv or more", "B": "Hair loss and burns are deterministic and need far larger doses; cancer is stochastic", "C": "Both outcomes are stochastic, so even this dose carries a small chance of hair loss", "D": "Skin burns are stochastic and hair loss is deterministic, so only burns carry a small chance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hair loss and burns are deterministic and need far larger doses; cancer is stochastic): Deterministic effects such as skin injury and epilation have a threshold, a severity that rises with dose, and doses in the gray range, far above a nuclear cardiology study. Cancer induction is stochastic: probability, not severity, rises with dose and no threshold is assumed in regulation. The other options misplace one or both effects in the wrong category."}, {"id": 3, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with large breasts has a supine stress Tc-99m SPECT showing a fixed anterior defect. Gated images show normal anterior wall thickening and motion, and the ECG during stress was normal. The clinician asks whether this is a prior infarct. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report the study as normal without comment, since an attenuation pattern has no clinical relevance", "B": "Report an anterior infarct and recommend catheterization, since any fixed defect represents myocardial scar", "C": "Check gated thickening, then confirm attenuation with correction or prone imaging before reporting scar", "D": "Repeat the study with double the Tc-99m dose to overcome count loss from breast tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check gated thickening, then confirm attenuation with correction or prone imaging before reporting scar): A fixed anterior defect with preserved thickening and motion in a woman with large breasts suggests attenuation. Prone or attenuation corrected images, or a PET study, confirm it, and reporting should say so. Fixed defects can be scar but scar usually shows reduced thickening, and more dose does not help."}, {"id": 4, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During dobutamine stress a patient has chest pain, 3 mm ST depression and a heart rate of 148. The infusion is stopped and tracer has been injected. If symptoms persist, which agent best reverses the drug effect on the heart?", "options": {"A": "Atropine 0.5 mg IV, which will slow the heart rate and reduce myocardial demand", "B": "Stop the infusion and only observe, since dobutamine effects are gone within 30 seconds", "C": "Aminophylline, which antagonizes adenosine receptors and reverses the effects of dobutamine", "D": "Intravenous esmolol or metoprolol, which blunts the beta-1 stimulation produced by dobutamine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intravenous esmolol or metoprolol, which blunts the beta-1 stimulation produced by dobutamine): Dobutamine acts on beta-1 receptors, so a short-acting intravenous beta blocker reverses persistent ischemia and tachycardia. Aminophylline treats adenosine and regadenoson effects, atropine raises heart rate, and dobutamine has a half-life of about 2 minutes so effects linger beyond 30 seconds."}, {"id": 5, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A cardiology department compares organ dose reports for rest and stress Tc-99m and a thallium-201 protocol and wants one number reflecting whole-body risk across protocols with different organ distributions. The director wants it in the report. Which quantity should it use?", "options": {"A": "Administered activity in megabecquerels, since activity equals the dose delivered to the patient.", "B": "Effective dose in millisieverts, which sums organ equivalent doses weighted by tissue sensitivity.", "C": "Equivalent dose to the highest-dose organ in sieverts, since the worst organ drives risk.", "D": "Absorbed dose to the heart wall in gray, since gray directly measures biologic damage."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Effective dose in millisieverts, which sums organ equivalent doses weighted by tissue sensitivity.): Effective dose applies tissue weighting factors to organ equivalent doses and sums them, giving one value that allows comparison of studies with different biodistributions. Gray measures energy per kilogram, equivalent dose handles radiation type for one organ, and activity does not account for biokinetics."}, {"id": 6, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist must set an exercise target for a 62-year-old man whose medications do not blunt heart rate. Using the 220 minus age formula, the laboratory defines adequate stress as 85 percent of the age-predicted maximum heart rate. What heart rate should the technologist record as the target for this patient?", "options": {"A": "187 beats per minute, calculated as 85 percent of 220 without subtracting age.", "B": "140 beats per minute, from 208 minus 0.7 times age and then 85 percent.", "C": "134 beats per minute, which is 85 percent of 158.", "D": "126 beats per minute, calculated as 80 percent of the predicted maximum of 158."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (134 beats per minute, which is 85 percent of 158.): Predicted maximum is 220 minus 62, which is 158. Multiplying by 0.85 gives 134. Using 80 percent gives 126. Using the 208 minus 0.7 times age formula gives 164.6, and 85 percent of that is 140. Skipping the age subtraction gives 187. Only the first follows the stated formula and percentage."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit summarizes a stress-only SPECT study in a 64-year-old man who exercised adequately and had a normal ECG. Stress images show a moderate anterior defect, and gated images show mild inferior hypokinesis with an ejection fraction of 52 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report diaphragmatic attenuation, since inferior hypokinesis supports an artifact on the stress images.", "B": "Report ischemia in the anterior territory, since a stress-only defect defines reversible disease.", "C": "Refer for angiography without rest images, since the study is positive and rest adds nothing.", "D": "Acquire rest images, since an abnormal stress image cannot separate ischemia from scar."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acquire rest images, since an abnormal stress image cannot separate ischemia from scar.): A stress-only protocol is valid only when the stress images and function are normal. An abnormal or equivocal stress image requires rest imaging to differentiate ischemia from scar or artifact. Anterior defects are not explained by diaphragm attenuation, and a stress-only defect cannot define reversibility.", "image": "question_images/q_7.png"}, {"id": 8, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62 year old man taking metoprolol 100 mg this morning stops treadmill exercise at 5 METs for dyspnea, reaching 104 beats per minute, 66 percent of maximum. There is no angina or ST change. What explains the low heart rate, and what is the next step?", "options": {"A": "Sinus node disease, so abandon stress testing and refer for pacemaker evaluation", "B": "Beta blocker chronotropic blunting, so convert to regadenoson during low-level exercise", "C": "Poor effort by the patient, so repeat exercise testing on another day without changing medicines", "D": "Hypovolemia from dehydration, so give fluids and restart the same treadmill protocol"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Beta blocker chronotropic blunting, so convert to regadenoson during low-level exercise): Metoprolol blunts the heart rate response to exercise, and 5 METs of effort with a rate near 104 is typical of chronotropic limitation rather than poor effort. With no ischemic findings and a submaximal rate, the standard approach is to inject a vasodilator during low-level exercise. Repeating the same test on the same drug gives the same result."}, {"id": 9, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exhibit is a technologist's quarterly dose report with the facility's ALARA investigation levels. She dispenses and injects Tc-99m and F-18 doses every working day. Based on the finding, which statement about responsibilities and the required action is now correct?", "options": {"A": "The RSO investigates her dispensing technique and records corrective action; no NRC report is due", "B": "No action is needed because year to date dose remains under the 500 mSv annual limit", "C": "The authorized user alone investigates, since the RSO role covers only waste, packages and records", "D": "The NRC must be notified within 24 hours because the quarter exceeded the facility Level II"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The RSO investigates her dispensing technique and records corrective action; no NRC report is due): The ring result of 160 mSv exceeds the facility's Level II of 150 mSv for the quarter. Investigation levels are internal triggers: the RSO, who has authority to stop unsafe work, investigates the cause, here syringe handling, and records corrective action. The 500 mSv annual extremity limit has not been reached, so no NRC report is due.", "image": "question_images/q_9.png"}, {"id": 10, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A Tc-99m generator in a Yellow II labeled package is delivered at 17:50. The department closed at 17:30 and reopens at 08:00 the next day. Which statement describes the required receipt monitoring for external radiation and removable contamination under 10 CFR 20.1906?", "options": {"A": "Monitor the package within 24 hours of delivery, whatever the time of day", "B": "Monitor the package within 3 hours after the department reopens the next morning", "C": "Monitor the package only if its label is Yellow III, because Yellow II needs no survey", "D": "Monitor the package within 3 hours of the delivery time that evening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Monitor the package within 3 hours after the department reopens the next morning): Packages labeled White I, Yellow II or Yellow III must be monitored for radiation and wiped for contamination within 3 hours of receipt if received during working hours, or within 3 hours of the start of the next working day if received after hours. This package arrived after hours, so the clock starts at 08:00. Yellow II packages are included in the requirement."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A one-day Tc-99m protocol needs a 30 mCi (1110 MBq) stress dose at 11:00. The eluted vial held 150 mCi (5550 MBq) in 5 mL at 07:00. Using a half-life of 6.02 hours, what volume should the technologist withdraw at 11:00?", "options": {"A": "Withdraw 2.51 mL, because the decay correction is applied to the volume twice.", "B": "Withdraw 1.59 mL, since the vial concentration has decayed to about 18.9 mCi/mL by 11:00.", "C": "Withdraw 0.63 mL, because multiplying the volume by the decay factor corrects for lost activity.", "D": "Withdraw 1.00 mL, because 150 mCi in 5 mL gives 30 mCi per milliliter."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withdraw 1.59 mL, since the vial concentration has decayed to about 18.9 mCi/mL by 11:00.): Four hours is 0.664 half-lives, so the decay factor is 0.631 and the vial holds 150 x 0.631 = 94.6 mCi, or 18.9 mCi/mL. The needed volume is 30 / 18.9 = 1.59 mL. Using the 07:00 concentration gives 1.00 mL, multiplying by the factor gives 0.63 mL, and double correction gives 2.51 mL. Drawing 1.00 mL would deliver only about 19 mCi."}, {"id": 12, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man has regadenoson SPECT with a moderate reversible anterior defect and no symptoms. The rest EF is 57 percent and the post stress gated EF is 44 percent, with new anterior hypokinesis. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassure and discharge, because a moderate defect without symptoms carries low risk.", "B": "Repeat the gated study in 4 weeks, as post stress EF changes reflect gating variation.", "C": "Report the EF as 44 percent and diagnose a new cardiomyopathy needing echocardiography only.", "D": "Recommend angiography, since this post stress EF fall signals severe extensive ischemia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recommend angiography, since this post stress EF fall signals severe extensive ischemia.): A post stress EF drop beyond a few points with new wall motion abnormality indicates stunning after severe ischemia and is linked to multivessel or left main disease. Together with a moderate defect it justifies referral. Variation of 13 points is far above gating noise, and lack of symptoms does not lower risk."}, {"id": 13, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A breast-feeding mother is scheduled for a rest-stress Rb-82 PET study at the cardiac imaging center and worries that she must pump and discard her milk afterward. Rb-82 has a half-life of 76 seconds. What is the correct advice for her?", "options": {"A": "Avoid skin contact with the infant for 7 days, as advised after I-131 therapy", "B": "Pump and discard milk for 24 hours, as is commonly advised after Tc-99m agents", "C": "No interruption of breast-feeding is needed, because the activity decays within minutes", "D": "Interrupt feeding for 3 days because Sr-82 breakthrough may be present in the injected dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No interruption of breast-feeding is needed, because the activity decays within minutes): With a 76 second half-life, Rb-82 is gone within minutes of injection, so no measurable activity reaches the milk in a way that justifies interruption. Tc-99m and I-131 rules apply to longer lived agents, and Sr-82 breakthrough is a generator limit checked before injection, not a reason to stop feeding."}, {"id": 14, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old diabetic woman with exertional chest tightness undergoes regadenoson stress with Tc-99m sestamibi. Baseline ECG is normal. At 3 minutes she develops 1.5 mm horizontal ST depression in V4 to V6 without chest pain, resolving by 5 minutes. Gated images show normal perfusion and an EF of 62 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Positive study for ischemia, since ST depression overrides normal perfusion and calls for angiography.", "B": "Normal perfusion study; the ST depression lacks specificity here and leaves risk low.", "C": "Nondiagnostic study because the stress ECG was abnormal, so imaging should be repeated with treadmill exercise.", "D": "Balanced multivessel ischemia, so a repeat study with dobutamine should be scheduled to unmask it."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal perfusion study; the ST depression lacks specificity here and leaves risk low.): With vasodilator stress, ST depression is uncommon and is not a reliable marker of flow-limiting disease; it can reflect coronary steal or microvascular dysfunction, and perfusion carries the diagnostic weight. Normal perfusion with a normal EF stays low risk. Calling the study positive applies exercise ECG criteria to a different stress. Balanced ischemia needs supporting signs such as TID or an EF fall, and an abnormal ECG does not make good images nondiagnostic."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The weekly flood on head 1 of a dual-head camera, in the exhibit, shows a deep defect near the detector center. Service cannot arrive for four days. The manager notes a uniformity correction map was built last month and proposes building a new one today to keep the head working. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Narrow the energy window to 10 percent to exclude affected counts and continue scanning patients today.", "B": "Recheck the flood tomorrow after the camera thermally equilibrates and keep imaging in the meantime.", "C": "Build a new 120 million count map and resume imaging, since maps compensate for tube defects.", "D": "Withdraw the head from SPECT, since a new map cannot recover signal from a failed tube."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withdraw the head from SPECT, since a new map cannot recover signal from a failed tube.): A uniformity map scales pixels to even out small sensitivity differences, but a deep defect has too few counts to scale without large noise amplification, so it only masks the problem. Thermal equilibration and narrower windows do not restore a failed tube. The correct step is to remove the head from service until the tube is repaired.", "image": "question_images/q_15.png"}, {"id": 16, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 61 year old woman with mild asthma has regadenoson stress imaging for chest pain. Six minutes after injection she is wheezing and uncomfortable, with a blood pressure lower than at rest. The exhibit shows her vital signs. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Observe without treatment, since regadenoson effects resolve within a few minutes", "B": "Give aminophylline slowly by IV and oxygen, with continued monitoring", "C": "Give an IV beta blocker, to slow the sinus tachycardia and ease the discomfort", "D": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin for suspected myocardial ischemia during stress"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give aminophylline slowly by IV and oxygen, with continued monitoring): Wheeze with oxygen saturation falling to 88 percent at 6 minutes in an asthmatic patient indicates bronchospasm from adenosine receptor stimulation. Aminophylline antagonizes the receptors and oxygen treats hypoxemia. Beta blockers worsen bronchospasm, nitroglycerin is unwise with a blood pressure of 92 over 50, and watching a patient with falling saturation delays treatment.", "image": "question_images/q_16.png"}, {"id": 17, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A nuclear cardiology lab plans a first-pass radionuclide angiogram in a patient whose resting heart rate is 110 bpm. The camera must collect enough counts in each beat during a fast bolus transit. Which collimator choice best fits this acquisition?", "options": {"A": "A low-energy high-sensitivity parallel-hole collimator, trading some resolution for counts per beat", "B": "A pinhole collimator placed close to the chest wall to magnify the ventricles on each frame", "C": "A medium-energy parallel-hole collimator, since the Tc-99m photon needs thicker septa to prevent penetration", "D": "A low-energy high-resolution parallel-hole collimator, since sharp septal definition matters most during bolus transit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A low-energy high-sensitivity parallel-hole collimator, trading some resolution for counts per beat): First-pass angiography records a few seconds of tracer transit, so counts per frame are the limiting factor and sensitivity wins over resolution. A low-energy high-sensitivity collimator has shorter, wider holes and passes more photons. The high-resolution design is tempting for image quality but starves each beat of counts. Medium-energy septa are meant for photons above about 200 keV, and a pinhole gives very low sensitivity."}, {"id": 18, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68 year old man with stable wheezing from chronic obstructive lung disease cannot walk on a treadmill. His resting electrocardiogram shows Mobitz type II second-degree AV block, and he has no pacemaker. Stress perfusion imaging is requested for exertional chest pain. How should the vasodilator order be handled?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson is given with aminophylline ready, since the antidote reverses any block that develops", "B": "Regadenoson is withheld, because stable obstructive lung disease is a labeled contraindication to A2A agonists", "C": "Dipyridamole is substituted, since its effect on AV nodal conduction is weaker than that of regadenoson", "D": "Regadenoson is withheld, because AV block above first degree without a pacemaker is a labeled contraindication"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson is withheld, because AV block above first degree without a pacemaker is a labeled contraindication): Regadenoson, adenosine and dipyridamole all act through adenosine receptors and can slow AV conduction, so high-grade block without a pacemaker excludes all three. Stable lung disease is a caution rather than a bar to regadenoson. Aminophylline reverses effects but does not justify exposing a patient to known conduction risk. Dobutamine or another approach is chosen."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with typical angina reaches 3 mm of horizontal ST depression at 6 METs. Gated SPECT shows no perfusion defect, TID 1.0 and EF 60 percent. He takes no digoxin and the baseline ECG is normal. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Treat as high risk, since ST depression with normal perfusion may mean balanced ischemia.", "B": "Report a normal study with risk under 1 percent, since perfusion overrides the ECG.", "C": "Recommend urgent angiography based on the ECG alone, without weighing the normal perfusion data.", "D": "Attribute the ST depression to digoxin effect and discount it in the final report."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat as high risk, since ST depression with normal perfusion may mean balanced ischemia.): Marked ST depression with angina at low workload is an ominous response, and relative perfusion can look normal when multivessel disease produces balanced flow reduction. The report should integrate both and flag the discordance. Dismissing the ECG, blaming digoxin which he does not take, or ignoring perfusion would each mislead the clinician."}, {"id": 20, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man with BMI 41 and a protuberant abdomen has atypical chest pain. Exercise SPECT shows slightly lower inferior counts that look the same on stress and rest rows, with summed scores of 0 and 0 against the normal database. Gated inferior thickening is normal and ejection fraction is 56 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact, with normal inferior thickening supporting a healthy wall", "B": "Reversible right coronary ischemia, since inferior counts are lower than other walls at stress", "C": "Left circumflex territory scar, since posterior wall counts follow that artery when the defect is fixed", "D": "Fixed inferior scar from right coronary infarction, since any unchanged defect equals myocardial scar"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact, with normal inferior thickening supporting a healthy wall): A raised diaphragm and abdominal tissue attenuate the inferior wall on both acquisitions, and scores of 0 against the normal database show no true perfusion defect. A fixed defect with normal thickening points to artifact, since scar would also lose contraction. Ischemia would change between rows, and the inferior wall is not left circumflex territory in a right dominant system.", "image": "question_images/q_20.png"}, {"id": 21, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A woman learns at six weeks of gestation that she was pregnant when she had a one day Tc-99m sestamibi study. A physicist estimates the fetal absorbed dose at 3 mGy. She asks about termination. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Recommend termination, since any fetal dose above 1 mSv exceeds the accepted limit for diagnostic use", "B": "Delay counseling until week 12 so a repeat estimate can follow organ formation", "C": "Report it as a medical event, since fetal dose exceeds the 1 mSv public limit", "D": "The dose is far below 50 mGy, so malformation risk is not raised; counsel and document"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The dose is far below 50 mGy, so malformation risk is not raised; counsel and document): Malformation and growth effects are deterministic and appear only above roughly 50 mGy, and 3 mGy is well under that level. The 1 mSv public limit does not apply to a patient's medical exposure, and this is not a medical event. The right step is calm counseling with a documented dose estimate, not termination or delay."}, {"id": 22, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A planar Tl-201 anterior view soon after stress in a 64-year-old man shows a lung ROI of 14,400 counts and a heart ROI of 24,000 counts. He was referred for exertional dyspnea. What is the lung to heart ratio and what does it suggest?", "options": {"A": "0.40, which falls within normal limits for early post-stress planar images of a man.", "B": "0.24, which suggests that the lung ROI was drawn to include the breast tissue.", "C": "0.60, above the usual 0.5 limit, suggesting extensive coronary disease or stress LV dysfunction.", "D": "1.67, which is normal because lung counts normally exceed heart counts in healthy patients."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (0.60, above the usual 0.5 limit, suggesting extensive coronary disease or stress LV dysfunction.): The ratio equals lung counts divided by heart counts, 14,400 over 24,000, which is 0.60. Increased lung uptake of Tl-201 after stress, with a ratio above about 0.5 on planar images, is linked to extensive disease or left ventricular dysfunction. Inverting the ratio or choosing other values does not match the arithmetic."}, {"id": 23, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man receiving anthracycline chemotherapy has an equilibrium gated blood pool study before cycle four. Background-corrected counts on the left ventricular time-activity curve are 17,910 in the first frame and 13,482 at the lowest frame. Echocardiographic windows were poor. Which result and action are best?", "options": {"A": "EF about 25 percent, severely reduced; hold the next anthracycline dose for review", "B": "EF about 33 percent, moderately reduced, using the count difference over end-systolic counts", "C": "EF about 75 percent, preserved, from end-systolic counts divided by end-diastolic counts", "D": "EF about 25 percent, but acceptable to continue therapy because the curve remains smooth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (EF about 25 percent, severely reduced; hold the next anthracycline dose for review): Ejection fraction equals end-diastolic counts minus end-systolic counts, divided by end-diastolic counts: (17,910 - 13,482) / 17,910 = 4,428 / 17,910, about 25 percent. This is far below the range at which chemotherapy is usually continued, so holding the dose for review is appropriate. A smooth curve shows good gating, not good function. Dividing the lowest counts by the first counts gives 75 percent, which is the fraction remaining, and dividing the difference by 13,482 gives 33 percent, which uses the wrong denominator.", "image": "question_images/q_23.png"}, {"id": 24, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old woman coughed once during a rest SPECT acquisition, according to the technologist. The rotating projections look acceptable and the sinogram band is unbroken. She was injected with Tc-99m tetrofosmin and the stress images are complete. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Accept the data; the unbroken band shows no meaningful motion, so reconstruct as acquired", "B": "Apply a motion correction algorithm before reconstruction to be safe", "C": "Reconstruct but drop the middle projections where the cough occurred", "D": "Repeat the rest acquisition immediately because the reported cough implies that the heart moved"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accept the data; the unbroken band shows no meaningful motion, so reconstruct as acquired): The sinogram records where the heart sat at each angle. An unbroken band means any cough produced no displacement large enough to matter. Repeating the scan costs time and patient burden without benefit, motion correction on stationary data can add its own artifact, and removing projections leaves gaps in angular sampling that degrade the reconstruction.", "image": "question_images/q_24.png"}, {"id": 25, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A monthly center of rotation test uses a Tc-99m point source on a dual-head camera set for 180 degree cardiac orbits with a 64 by 64 matrix and 6.4 mm pixels. The reconstructed point source slice is in the exhibit. The vendor tolerance is under 1 pixel. What is the correct interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Pixel size miscalibration; recalibrate millimeters per pixel and continue clinical studies today.", "B": "Detector tilt error; recheck head levels and keep imaging cardiac patients in the meantime.", "C": "Center of rotation offset exceeds tolerance; recalibrate it before any further patient SPECT acquisitions.", "D": "Nonuniform detector response; acquire a new flood and rebuild the correction map first."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Center of rotation offset exceeds tolerance; recalibrate it before any further patient SPECT acquisitions.): A point source should reconstruct to a small point. A tuning-fork or arc shape indicates the electronic center of the projections is displaced from the mechanical axis of rotation, which is the pattern of a center of rotation error. This blurs the myocardium and distorts walls, so recalibration is needed before patient use. Tilt, pixel size, and uniformity faults produce different appearances.", "image": "question_images/q_25.png"}, {"id": 26, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exhibit shows a dose record after a stress study. The measured dose is 30 percent above the ordered value. The nuclear cardiologist and radiation safety officer must decide how to classify and handle the event. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Write a directive afterward to classify the event, then report to the NRC within 30 days.", "B": "Take no action, since a dose within 30 percent of the order falls inside normal tolerance.", "C": "Telephone the NRC by the next calendar day as a medical event, since dose exceeded order.", "D": "Document and review it with the safety officer and authorized user; no directive applied."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document and review it with the safety officer and authorized user; no directive applied.): Medical event reporting under 10 CFR 35.3045 applies to administrations that require a written directive, and a diagnostic Tc-99m sestamibi dose does not. The 30 percent excess is still a dosing error, so it is documented, reviewed by the radiation safety officer and authorized user, and used to correct practice. Writing a directive afterward is improper, and no regulatory tolerance of 30 percent is established for diagnostic doses.", "image": "question_images/q_26.png"}, {"id": 27, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient with suspected cardiac sarcoidosis is scheduled for FDG PET at 9 a.m. She ate pasta the night before and had toast and orange juice this morning. The protocol calls for suppression of normal myocardial glucose uptake. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Proceed after heparin alone, since heparin suppresses myocardial glucose uptake in every patient.", "B": "Proceed after a four-hour fast and image at sixty minutes, since a short fast suffices.", "C": "Proceed with a 25 gram oral glucose load, since insulin-driven uptake makes inflammation visible.", "D": "Reschedule after high-fat, low-carbohydrate meals and a fast of at least 12 hours, with heparin."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reschedule after high-fat, low-carbohydrate meals and a fast of at least 12 hours, with heparin.): FDG for sarcoid requires suppression of normal myocardial glucose use so that inflamed tissue stands out. The standard preparation is high-fat, low-carbohydrate meals, a prolonged fast of 12 hours or more, and sometimes heparin. A carbohydrate meal makes diffuse uptake likely and the study nondiagnostic. A glucose load is for viability studies."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 54 year old man with no cardiac history worries about radiation after a same day rest and stress Tc-99m sestamibi study with about 9 mSv effective dose. He asks how this compares with everyday exposure. Which statement is the best counseling?", "options": {"A": "About 9 mSv, roughly 3 years of natural background, with a small added lifetime cancer risk", "B": "About 9 mSv, but the risk is zero because the dose is below 100 mSv", "C": "About 90 mSv, near the yearly occupational limit, so repeat studies should be avoided", "D": "About 0.9 mSv, similar to a few chest radiographs, so the risk is negligible"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 9 mSv, roughly 3 years of natural background, with a small added lifetime cancer risk): A same day rest and stress sestamibi study gives roughly 9 mSv, and average natural background is about 3 mSv per year, so the study equals about 3 years of background. Risk models estimate a small increase in lifetime cancer risk, which should be weighed against the diagnostic benefit. Calling the dose a few chest radiographs or 90 mSv misstates it by tenfold, and claiming zero risk is not supported by the linear no threshold model."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Rest Tc-99m sestamibi imaging begins 15 minutes after injection in a 58-year-old woman who was given no fatty meal. The images show intense subdiaphragmatic activity touching the inferior wall. A reader asks which pharmacokinetic property best explains the problem. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Hepatobiliary clearance keeps liver activity high early; rest imaging needs 45 to 60 minutes.", "B": "Sestamibi binds red cells, so the nearby inferior blood pool keeps wall counts elevated.", "C": "Rapid myocardial washout by 15 minutes moves apparent counts toward the nearby abdominal organs.", "D": "Renal excretion of free pertechnetate adds background counts over the inferior wall region."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hepatobiliary clearance keeps liver activity high early; rest imaging needs 45 to 60 minutes.): Sestamibi is a lipophilic cation held in mitochondria, with little myocardial washout, and it clears from blood into liver and bile. Subdiaphragmatic activity falls only slowly, so rest imaging after 45 to 60 minutes gives a better heart to liver ratio. Red cell labeling and pertechnetate excretion do not apply to this tracer."}, {"id": 30, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A draft report lists a summed stress score of 14 and a summed rest score of 5, states a summed difference score of 19, and describes mild ischemia with old scar. A senior reader reviews it before signing. Which revision best meets ASNC reporting standards?", "options": {"A": "Remove summed scores and report qualitative terms, because standards do not use semiquantitative scoring.", "B": "Correct the difference score to 9, then state extent, dilation, gated function and the stress response.", "C": "Change the difference score to 5, because the rest score alone measures the reversible component.", "D": "Keep the difference score at 19, because it equals the stress and rest scores added."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Correct the difference score to 9, then state extent, dilation, gated function and the stress response.): The summed difference score equals the summed stress score minus the summed rest score: 14 minus 5 is 9. A complete report also gives the ischemia and scar extent and severity, transient dilation, gated ejection fraction, and the hemodynamic and ECG response. Adding the scores gives an impossible value, the rest score measures scar, and ASNC standards recommend semiquantitative scoring."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient on a treadmill at Bruce stage 3 develops chest pain with 3 mm ST depression, heart rate 120 and blood pressure 150/80. He wants to stop and the technologist offers a sublingual nitroglycerin tablet. What is the best sequence?", "options": {"A": "Stop at once without injection and reschedule, since ischemia has occurred.", "B": "Continue exercise to 85 percent of maximum predicted heart rate before injecting.", "C": "Inject tracer now, stop soon after, and give nitroglycerin only after injection.", "D": "Give nitroglycerin first and inject the tracer after the pain resolves."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inject tracer now, stop soon after, and give nitroglycerin only after injection.): The tracer must be injected at the point of ischemia so that the image records the flow abnormality. Nitroglycerin before injection would relieve the defect and hide ischemia. Exercise should end once the endpoint is reached, and nitroglycerin can be given after injection."}, {"id": 32, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 76-year-old man with heart failure has 1-hour Tc-99m PYP planar images showing visual grade 2 uptake and a heart-to-contralateral ratio of 1.6. SPECT shows the tracer confined to the ventricular cavities, not myocardium. What best explains the planar result?", "options": {"A": "Definite ATTR amyloid, since a one-hour ratio above 1.5 is diagnostic whatever SPECT shows", "B": "Rib and sternal uptake overlapping the heart, which would be separated on the lateral planar view", "C": "Blood pool retention mimicking myocardial uptake on planar images; SPECT shows it is not amyloid", "D": "Free pertechnetate in the PYP kit, which accumulates in the myocardium and raises the ratio"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blood pool retention mimicking myocardial uptake on planar images; SPECT shows it is not amyloid): Planar PYP can overestimate myocardial uptake because blood pool activity overlaps the heart, so SPECT is required to confirm that the signal is in the myocardium rather than the cavity. A heart-to-contralateral ratio of 1.5 or more at one hour supports amyloid only when SPECT localizes the uptake to myocardium. Here the cavity pattern means the planar result is a false positive. Bone overlap and free pertechnetate do not explain cavity-confined activity."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A hot lab holds a bin of used syringes and vials contaminated with Tc-99m and Tl-201 from nuclear cardiology studies. The licensee uses decay-in-storage instead of a waste vendor. How long must the bin be held before survey and disposal as ordinary trash, using the longer-lived isotope?", "options": {"A": "About 30 days, ten half-lives of Tl-201, followed by a survey showing no activity above background.", "B": "About 60 hours, ten half-lives of Tc-99m, since the shorter-lived isotope governs the hold time.", "C": "About 73 hours, a single Tl-201 half-life, once the bin reads below the meter alarm.", "D": "About 9 days, three half-lives of Tl-201, enough to bring the activity below one tenth."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 30 days, ten half-lives of Tl-201, followed by a survey showing no activity above background.): Decay-in-storage requires holding material for at least ten half-lives, then surveying with a meter at the container surface to show readings indistinguishable from background before labels are removed. For a mixed bin, the longer-lived isotope governs: Tl-201 has a 73 hour half-life, so 10 x 73 = 730 hours, about 30 days. Ten Tc-99m half-lives would be only about 60 hours."}, {"id": 34, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On Tl-201 imaging, a 60-year-old man has an inferior defect 15 minutes after exercise that has resolved on 4 hour images, with preserved wall thickening on gated images. What is the best interpretation and the underlying mechanism of this change?", "options": {"A": "Scar with partial volume effect, because late imaging improves count recovery in infarcts", "B": "Nonviable myocardium, because redistribution to the defect requires dead tissue to fill with tracer", "C": "Attenuation by the diaphragm, because defects that change over time are typical of soft tissue artifacts", "D": "Inducible ischemia in viable myocardium, since normal tissue washes out thallium faster"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inducible ischemia in viable myocardium, since normal tissue washes out thallium faster): After stress injection, Tl-201 uptake is low in an ischemic region, but normal myocardium washes out faster than ischemic tissue, so the defect fills in at 4 hours. A reversible defect therefore means inducible ischemia in viable myocardium. Attenuation does not change with time, and scar or nonviable myocardium would not fill in."}, {"id": 35, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "First-pass radionuclide angiography is performed through a 22 gauge hand vein. The superior vena cava time-activity curve is broad, multi-peaked and has a long tail, and the calculated left ventricular ejection fraction is 38 percent. The patient has no known cardiomyopathy. What is the best interpretation of this study?", "options": {"A": "Average the ejection fraction over all beats so the broad bolus no longer matters", "B": "Report an ejection fraction of 38 percent with severe dysfunction since the count rate is adequate", "C": "Extend the region of interest to include the vena cava tail to offset the broad bolus", "D": "The bolus is too prolonged to separate chambers, so repeat through a large proximal vein"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The bolus is too prolonged to separate chambers, so repeat through a large proximal vein): First-pass studies need a tight bolus so that right and left heart chambers can be separated in time, and a broad multi-peaked curve means chambers overlap. The ejection fraction is therefore unreliable. The study should be repeated with a bolus through a proximal large vein and rapid flush, because adjusting regions or averaging cannot restore temporal separation."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A PET laboratory's daily quality control eluate from its rubidium-82 generator measures strontium-82 at 0.025 microcuries per millicurie of rubidium-82. The limit is 0.02 microcuries per millicurie. A patient is waiting, and the technologist reports the value to the supervising physician. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Elute and discard the first volume, then proceed, since this clears contamination from the column.", "B": "Continue today and repeat the breakthrough test next week to confirm the trend.", "C": "Remove the generator from patient use and notify the radiation safety officer and manufacturer.", "D": "Continue imaging with half the usual dose, which halves the strontium given to the patient."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the generator from patient use and notify the radiation safety officer and manufacturer.): Strontium breakthrough above 0.02 microcuries of Sr-82 per millicurie of Rb-82 exceeds the regulatory limit, so the generator cannot be used for patients. The cause is column exhaustion, which flushing does not fix. Reporting is required, and halving the dose still delivers excessive strontium."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A nuclear technologist who handles about 20 Tc-99m doses weekly declares her pregnancy in writing. Dosimetry shows an estimated 3.2 mSv to the embryo or fetus since conception, now at five months. The radiation safety officer is asked what the regulations require going forward. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Apply the 50 mSv annual occupational whole-body limit, since she is still a radiation worker.", "B": "Remove her from all radiation work today, because the dose has passed the permitted threshold.", "C": "Keep embryo or fetus dose below 5 mSv for the whole gestation, with monthly monitoring.", "D": "Apply the 1 mSv public limit now, since pregnancy makes her equal to a public member."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep embryo or fetus dose below 5 mSv for the whole gestation, with monthly monitoring.): For a declared pregnant worker, NRC rules limit the dose to the embryo or fetus to 5 mSv (0.5 rem) over the entire pregnancy, with efforts to avoid a substantial uneven monthly pattern. At 3.2 mSv she remains below the limit, so continued work with monitoring and ALARA practices is allowed. The 50 mSv and 1 mSv figures apply to other situations."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man had an inferior myocardial infarction treated with stenting of the RCA six days ago and now has no angina. Tl-201 stress and 4 hour images are summarized in the exhibit. What is the best interpretation of the inferior wall?", "options": {"A": "Nonviable scar with no recovery potential, since the defect became worse over time.", "B": "Inducible inferior ischemia that needs repeat angiography because the defect worsened on delayed images.", "C": "Soft tissue attenuation from the diaphragm that grows on delayed imaging.", "D": "Reverse redistribution in a reperfused infarct, meaning viable myocardium and a patent artery."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reverse redistribution in a reperfused infarct, meaning viable myocardium and a patent artery.): Reverse redistribution means the defect is worse at redistribution than at stress. It is typical of a recently reperfused infarct with a mix of viable and scarred myocardium and a patent culprit artery, not of new ischemia. Scar would not suggest improved flow, and attenuation does not change between early and late images.", "image": "question_images/q_38.png"}, {"id": 39, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62 year old man with an older ICD that is not MRI conditional has poor echo windows and a borderline echocardiographic EF of 35 percent. Device therapy depends on an accurate EF value. Which test best answers the question?", "options": {"A": "Equilibrium radionuclide angiography, since MRI is excluded and count-based EF ignores windows", "B": "Repeat echocardiography with contrast by the same sonographer, since contrast removes window limits", "C": "Gated sestamibi SPECT, since its EF stays accurate at every heart size and rhythm", "D": "Cardiac MRI with the device in magnet mode, since modern scanners override device restrictions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Equilibrium radionuclide angiography, since MRI is excluded and count-based EF ignores windows): EF near a treatment threshold needs a method with low variability. A non-conditional ICD rules out standard MRI, and echo is window dependent. Equilibrium blood pool imaging is count based and reproducible. Gated SPECT underestimates or overestimates EF in small cavities and at low counts, so it is a weaker choice."}, {"id": 40, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A woman with a small left ventricle had Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT acquired with LEGP collimators, and the reader sees blurred, thick-appearing walls with a poorly separated septum and lateral wall. What is the best next step for the repeat study?", "options": {"A": "Keep LEGP and narrow the window to 5 percent to sharpen the septum.", "B": "Reconstruct with a higher Butterworth cutoff to recover resolution lost by the collimator.", "C": "Reacquire with LEHR collimators and longer time per projection, trading sensitivity for resolution.", "D": "Switch to a medium energy collimator, which improves both resolution and sensitivity at 140 keV."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reacquire with LEHR collimators and longer time per projection, trading sensitivity for resolution.): LEGP collimators have larger or shorter holes that pass more photons but blur the image, while LEHR collimators give better resolution at lower sensitivity. A small heart needs the resolution, and longer time per stop makes up for the lost counts. Medium energy collimators are designed for higher energies. A filter cannot recover detail never recorded."}, {"id": 41, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old diabetic man with no symptoms has a normal gated SPECT perfusion study and an attenuation CT calcium score of 1,450 Agatston units. His physician asks how long the normal result can be relied on before reassessment. Which statement is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "The calcium score is invalid when perfusion is normal and belongs outside the clinical report.", "B": "Heavy calcification shortens the normal study's warranty, so earlier reassessment is sensible.", "C": "A score this high proves obstructive disease, so he should go straight to invasive angiography.", "D": "A normal perfusion study resets his risk to that of zero calcium, giving a long warranty."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heavy calcification shortens the normal study's warranty, so earlier reassessment is sensible.): The warranty of a normal perfusion study is longest in patients with little or no calcium and shorter when calcium burden is high, and diabetes shortens it further. Calcium indicates plaque burden, not luminal stenosis, so angiography is not automatic. The calcium score adds independent prognostic information and belongs in the report beside the perfusion result."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A wipe test from the injection room floor after a day of cardiac studies, counted in the well counter, gives 9,000 cpm. Recounting the same wipe exactly 24 hours later gives 560 cpm. The counter passes its own constancy checks. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201 contamination, whose 73 hour half-life explains a 16-fold drop over one day.", "B": "Counter drift, because true contamination would have stayed unchanged overnight on the floor wipe sample.", "C": "Short-lived Tc-99m contamination decayed through four half-lives, matching the drop expected from physical decay.", "D": "Mo-99 breakthrough from the generator, whose 66 hour half-life is brief on a daily scale."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Short-lived Tc-99m contamination decayed through four half-lives, matching the drop expected from physical decay.): Tc-99m has a 6 hour half-life, so 24 hours is four half-lives and activity falls to one sixteenth, from 9,000 to about 560 cpm. Tl-201 and Mo-99 have half-lives of about 73 and 66 hours, so they would barely change in a day. A drifting counter would need to be checked with a long-lived source, not explained by a drop that matches decay."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A nuclear medicine technologist who injects Tc-99m and F-18 doses every day declares her pregnancy in writing to her employer. Under NRC rules, what is the dose limit to the embryo or fetus for the remainder of her gestation period?", "options": {"A": "5 mSv (0.5 rem) total for the entire gestation period", "B": "20 mSv (2 rem), the averaged annual limit used in some systems", "C": "1 mSv (0.1 rem), the annual limit for members of the public", "D": "50 mSv (5 rem), the annual whole body limit for adult workers"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5 mSv (0.5 rem) total for the entire gestation period): 10 CFR 20.1208 limits the dose to the embryo or fetus of a declared pregnant worker to 5 mSv (0.5 rem) over the gestation period, and the licensee should avoid uniform high monthly exposure. The other figures are the public limit, an ICRP style worker average, and the NRC adult limit."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes describes his chest symptoms to the nurse before a stress test is planned. The laboratory scores history using standard guideline definitions for risk assessment. Which description most strongly raises the pretest probability of obstructive coronary disease and qualifies as typical angina?", "options": {"A": "Substernal pressure brought on by exertion and relieved within minutes by rest or nitroglycerin.", "B": "Burning upper abdominal pain after large meals that improves after taking an antacid.", "C": "Sharp left-sided pain pointed to with one finger, lasting seconds and worse with deep inspiration.", "D": "Constant aching pain at rest lasting hours, unrelated to activity and reproduced by chest wall palpation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Substernal pressure brought on by exertion and relieved within minutes by rest or nitroglycerin.): Typical angina has three features: substernal discomfort, provoked by exertion or stress, and relieved by rest or nitroglycerin. Pleuritic, reflux-like or palpation-reproducible pain lowers the likelihood of coronary disease."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 63-year-old man develops 2.5 mm of horizontal ST depression and chest pain at peak exercise. Three minutes into recovery the pain persists. BP is 138/82, HR 118, and he took sildenafil 36 hours ago. The tracer has been injected. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Walk him slowly on the treadmill for ten more minutes to ease the pain", "B": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg, keep him supine, and keep monitoring", "C": "Withhold nitroglycerin because sildenafil was taken within the last 48 hours", "D": "Give intravenous aminophylline 100 mg to reverse the ischemia caused by exercise stress"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg, keep him supine, and keep monitoring): Persistent angina with ST depression after exercise is treated first with sublingual nitroglycerin when systolic pressure is adequate and no recent phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor use. Sildenafil needs a 24-hour gap and tadalafil 48 hours, so 36 hours after sildenafil is acceptable. Aminophylline reverses adenosine-type stress agents and is not an anti-anginal. Further exertion risks worsening ischemia."}, {"id": 46, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A vial holds 120 mCi (4440 MBq) of Tc-99m tetrofosmin in 6 mL, calibrated at 08:00. A rest dose of 10 mCi (370 MBq) is needed at 10:00. The half-life of Tc-99m is 6.02 hours. What volume should the technologist withdraw into the syringe?", "options": {"A": "Draw 0.50 mL, because 10 mCi from 120 mCi in 6 mL needs no decay correction.", "B": "Draw 0.63 mL, because the vial has decayed to about 95 mCi in 6 mL.", "C": "Draw 0.40 mL, after multiplying the needed volume by the decay factor instead of dividing.", "D": "Draw 0.79 mL, after applying the decay correction a second time to the already corrected volume."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Draw 0.63 mL, because the vial has decayed to about 95 mCi in 6 mL.): Two hours is 0.332 half-lives, so the decay factor is 2^-0.332 = 0.794 and the vial holds 120 x 0.794 = 95.3 mCi, a concentration of 15.9 mCi/mL. The required volume is 10 / 15.9 = 0.63 mL. Ignoring decay gives 0.50 mL, multiplying by the factor gives 0.40 mL and correcting twice gives 0.79 mL; a rest dose drawn with the uncorrected volume is about 20 percent low."}, {"id": 47, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist drops a syringe holding 740 MBq (20 mCi) of Tc-99m sestamibi in a corridor beside the patient waiting area. About 30 mL spreads across the floor and nobody is contaminated. She has warned people nearby. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Leave the floor untouched for 60 hours until the Tc-99m has decayed through ten half-lives", "B": "Survey the waiting area with a GM meter before limiting anyone's access to the corridor", "C": "Keep people out of the area, cover the liquid with absorbent paper, and notify the RSO", "D": "Mop the spill at once with a wet cloth so the liquid cannot dry and spread"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep people out of the area, cover the liquid with absorbent paper, and notify the RSO): The first priorities are to prevent spread and limit exposure: restrict access, cover the liquid with absorbent material, and bring in the RSO, who directs cleanup from the outside inward and surveys afterward. Wet mopping before containment smears activity over a larger area. Surveying first delays access control, and waiting for decay leaves a contaminated public corridor for 60 hours."}, {"id": 48, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man has frequent PVCs and tall T waves. The gating system triggers on both R and T waves, producing about 20 percent extra triggers during an 8 frame gated SPECT acquisition of the left ventricle. What is the likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Mixed phases blur end systole, so EF falls falsely and wall motion looks hypokinetic.", "B": "Only perfusion distribution changes, while function measurements remain unaffected.", "C": "Ventricular volumes are overestimated, but EF is unchanged because it is a ratio.", "D": "EF rises falsely because extra triggers add counts to the end diastolic frame."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mixed phases blur end systole, so EF falls falsely and wall motion looks hypokinetic.): Double triggering mixes counts from different phases into each frame. Temporal blurring raises the end systolic volume and reduces apparent wall excursion, so EF drops and motion looks falsely hypokinetic. Perfusion is less affected, but function is not spared, and blurred volumes change the ratio."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man has stress images showing a cavity larger than on rest, with reduced anterior and anteroseptal counts (summed stress score 13, rest score 0, difference score 13). Rest images look normal. A colleague argues the defect covers only one territory, so the cavity size adds nothing. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "The cavity size is irrelevant, since risk is fully described by the defect scores", "B": "Dilation signals ischemia more extensive than the visible defect, so risk is higher", "C": "Dilation is a stress dose artifact, so it can be disregarded when the defect is limited", "D": "Dilation matters only if the rest ejection fraction is below 35 percent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dilation signals ischemia more extensive than the visible defect, so risk is higher): A larger stress cavity with a normal rest cavity reflects widespread subendocardial hypoperfusion at stress, which can exceed what the regional defect shows. This marker raises risk independent of the summed scores. Stress dose does not enlarge the cavity, and the finding does not depend on a reduced resting ejection fraction.", "image": "question_images/q_49.png"}, {"id": 50, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit is the stress record of an 80 kg patient with asthma who received dipyridamole and sestamibi. Three minutes after tracer injection, wheezing and chest tightness persist with blood pressure 98/60 and no ST elevation. Which reversal is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Aminophylline 50 to 250 mg slowly intravenously, blocking adenosine receptors.", "B": "Naloxone 0.4 mg intravenously, since the reaction is a reversible pharmacologic response.", "C": "Metoprolol 5 mg intravenously, to slow the heart rate and settle the chest tightness.", "D": "Glucagon 1 mg intravenously, to counter bronchospasm and low blood pressure together."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aminophylline 50 to 250 mg slowly intravenously, blocking adenosine receptors.): Dipyridamole blocks adenosine reuptake, so adenosine accumulates and acts on its receptors. Aminophylline antagonizes those receptors and is given slowly in doses of 50 to 250 mg. Beta blockers can worsen bronchospasm, and naloxone and glucagon act on other receptors.", "image": "question_images/q_50.png"}, {"id": 51, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A gated blood pool curve for a 64-year-old woman with nonischemic cardiomyopathy shows 17,957 counts in the first frame and 12,524 counts at the lowest point, with background already subtracted. Which calculation gives the resting left ventricular ejection fraction from this curve?", "options": {"A": "Subtract lowest counts from first-frame counts and divide by lowest counts, giving about 43 percent", "B": "Divide the lowest counts by the first-frame counts, giving about 70 percent", "C": "Average the first-frame and lowest counts and divide by the first-frame counts, giving about 85 percent", "D": "End-diastolic minus end-systolic counts over end-diastolic counts, giving an EF near 30 percent"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (End-diastolic minus end-systolic counts over end-diastolic counts, giving an EF near 30 percent): Ejection fraction is stroke counts over end-diastolic counts: (17,957 - 12,524) / 17,957 = 5,433 / 17,957, about 30 percent. The stroke volume is a difference, never a quotient of the minimum over the maximum. Dividing the lowest by the highest gives 70 percent, the residual fraction. Dividing the difference by the lowest counts gives 43 percent, which inflates the value. Averaging the two counts removes the subtraction and gives an impossible 85 percent.", "image": "question_images/q_51.png"}, {"id": 52, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman not taking a beta blocker stops treadmill exercise at 7 minutes because of leg fatigue. Peak heart rate is 121 bpm, she has no chest pain, and the ST segments are flat. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Reschedule dobutamine stress next week, since conversion to a vasodilator after exercise is not permitted.", "B": "Inject at 121 bpm and read as negative, since no angina and flat ST segments suffice.", "C": "Rate is 79 percent of predicted and the ECG is nondiagnostic, so add regadenoson with exercise.", "D": "Give atropine 1 mg to raise heart rate above 131 bpm, then inject tracer."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rate is 79 percent of predicted and the ECG is nondiagnostic, so add regadenoson with exercise.): Age-predicted maximum is 154 bpm, and 85 percent is about 131 bpm, so 121 bpm is 79 percent and falls short. Perfusion imaging at submaximal rate lowers sensitivity, and the ECG cannot exclude ischemia. Adding a vasodilator with low-level exercise in the same session is a validated approach that raises flow reserve contrast. Atropine is used in dobutamine protocols, not to rescue treadmill stress."}, {"id": 53, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "After a regadenoson bolus through a 22-gauge dorsal hand IV, Tc-99m sestamibi is injected. The patient reports pain at the site, and images show very low cardiac counts with an intense focus at the hand injection site. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report the study as normal because low cardiac counts show normal flow at a low dose", "B": "Image the site to confirm extravasation, inform the physician, and repeat stress another day", "C": "Reinject a second full dose at once and rescan, since low counts mean underdosing", "D": "Apply warm compresses and rescan in one hour, since the tracer will reach the circulation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Image the site to confirm extravasation, inform the physician, and repeat stress another day): Tracer infiltrated into tissue is not available to the myocardium, so counts are low and the stress component is nondiagnostic. The site should be imaged to confirm and quantify extravasation, the physician informed, and the event documented. Because the pharmacologic stress cannot be repeated immediately, a repeat stress injection is scheduled on another day. Normal perfusion does not produce low counts, and infiltrated tracer clears slowly."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "On the monthly check, the center of rotation on a SPECT camera exceeds the manufacturer's tolerance, and the point source reconstruction shows a tuning fork artifact. A rest MPI for a 55-year-old woman is scheduled in one hour. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Do not image patients until recalibration, since the offset creates false perfusion defects.", "B": "Proceed and select a smoother reconstruction filter to hide the tuning fork artifact.", "C": "Finish today's list and recalibrate at the next quarterly service visit.", "D": "Use a 360 degree orbit, which averages out center of rotation errors."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not image patients until recalibration, since the offset creates false perfusion defects.): A center of rotation error misaligns projections during reconstruction, producing tuning fork or ring artifacts and false perfusion defects. The camera should not be used clinically until recalibration passes. A smoother filter hides detail without fixing the cause. A 360 degree orbit does not cancel the misalignment."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A nurse must stand beside a patient who emits 4 mrem/h at 1 m for 6 minutes per procedure. She can either double her distance to 2 m at the same time, or keep 1 m and halve the time to 3 minutes. Which choice gives the lower dose, and what is it?", "options": {"A": "Both options give the same 0.2 mrem, since time and distance act equally on dose", "B": "Doubling the distance, because the rate falls to 2 mrem/h and the dose becomes 0.2 mrem", "C": "Doubling distance, because the dose rate falls to 1 mrem/h and the dose to 0.1 mrem", "D": "Halving the time, because it gives the larger reduction, bringing the dose down to 0.2 mrem"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Doubling distance, because the dose rate falls to 1 mrem/h and the dose to 0.1 mrem): Dose rate follows the inverse square law, so doubling distance divides it by four: 4 mrem/h becomes 1 mrem/h, and 6 minutes is 0.1 hour, giving 0.1 mrem. Halving the time at 1 m gives 4 times 0.05 hour, or 0.2 mrem. Treating the distance effect as linear gives 2 mrem/h and wrongly equalizes the two choices."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55 year old man received Tc-99m sestamibi at peak exercise. He then vomits repeatedly and cannot lie still for acquisition until nearly 2 hours after injection. He is now stable and cooperative, with a normal electrocardiogram. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Discard the injection and repeat the whole stress study after the tracer has washed out", "B": "Image now, since sestamibi redistributes minimally and still reflects flow at injection", "C": "Reinject sestamibi now to refresh the stress distribution before the images are acquired", "D": "Image after another 4 hours, as with thallium, until redistribution fills the stress defects"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Image now, since sestamibi redistributes minimally and still reflects flow at injection): Sestamibi is trapped by mitochondria after a first-pass extraction of about 60 percent, so it redistributes very little and the stress image stays representative for hours. A delay of 1 to 2 hours is acceptable. A second injection would add a new tracer distribution, and waiting longer gains nothing, unlike thallium, which redistributes."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman has a stress Tl-201 SPECT today. A prior study from another facility, performed eight months ago, used Tc-99m sestamibi and a different slice orientation. How should the reader use the prior study when reporting today's findings? The rest ejection fraction is similar.", "options": {"A": "Compare normalized scores and report change, noting the tracer difference and interval events.", "B": "Interpret today's study without reviewing the prior study to avoid biasing the current read.", "C": "Report a new defect whenever today's polar map looks visually larger than the prior map.", "D": "Compare absolute count values between the two studies to quantify disease progression over time."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Compare normalized scores and report change, noting the tracer difference and interval events.): Prior studies help separate old from new findings, but tracer, camera, and processing differences mean raw counts are not comparable. Normalized scores such as summed stress and rest scores, and EF, can be compared, with the tracer difference and interval clinical events stated. Ignoring the prior wastes information, and reporting change by eye can be misleading."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman receives regadenoson with no exercise, followed by Tc-99m sestamibi. The technologist wants to begin imaging 20 minutes later, as is done for treadmill patients in the lab for years. In this patient, what is the best imaging start time?", "options": {"A": "About 20 minutes, since regadenoson clears the liver as quickly as treadmill exercise does", "B": "About 4 hours, since regadenoson studies need late redistribution to show ischemia", "C": "About 5 minutes, since the heart reaches peak counts quickly after a vasodilator bolus", "D": "About 45 to 60 minutes, since splanchnic flow stays high without exercise and slows clearance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 45 to 60 minutes, since splanchnic flow stays high without exercise and slows clearance): Exercise shifts blood away from the gut and speeds tracer clearance, so imaging can begin around 15 to 30 minutes. With pharmacologic stress alone, splanchnic flow remains high and hepatobiliary activity clears more slowly, so 45 to 60 minutes is usual, with low-level exercise or fluids helping. Waiting for hours is unnecessary for a technetium agent, and imaging at 5 minutes leaves heavy abdominal activity."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old nondiabetic man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and ejection fraction 22 percent has fasted 8 hours before F-18 FDG viability imaging. His glucose is 88 mg/dL and no insulin is planned. Why is a 50 g oral glucose load given before FDG injection?", "options": {"A": "It inhibits hexokinase in normal myocardium so only hibernating segments retain FDG afterward.", "B": "It lowers blood pool activity by pushing glucose toward the liver, improving target to background contrast.", "C": "It prevents hypoglycemia during the uptake period, which is its main purpose in fasting patients.", "D": "It raises insulin so myocytes switch from fatty acids to glucose, increasing FDG uptake."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It raises insulin so myocytes switch from fatty acids to glucose, increasing FDG uptake.): After a fast, circulating fatty acids dominate myocardial energy supply and FDG uptake is low and patchy. A glucose load or insulin shifts metabolism to glucose, and hibernating myocardium takes up FDG avidly, which allows perfusion-metabolism mismatch to be read. Typical practice targets a glucose of about 100 to 140 mg/dL at injection with an insulin or clamp adjustment when needed."}, {"id": 60, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 48-year-old woman with large breasts has supine sestamibi SPECT. There is a mild anterior and anterolateral defect of identical appearance at stress and rest. Gated images show normal thickening and motion, EF is 62 percent, TID is 1.0 and she has no angina. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Prior anterior infarction, so a viability study should be recommended for the anterior wall segments.", "B": "Breast attenuation, supported by normal thickening and motion in the segments with a fixed defect.", "C": "Diaphragmatic attenuation of the anterior wall, which would improve if the patient were imaged supine instead.", "D": "Hibernating myocardium in the LAD territory, which should be revascularized without further imaging or testing."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Breast attenuation, supported by normal thickening and motion in the segments with a fixed defect.): A fixed defect in a woman with large breasts and normal thickening in the same wall is the typical signature of breast attenuation, which can be confirmed by attenuation correction or prone imaging. Infarcted or hibernating myocardium would have abnormal motion or thickening. The diaphragm attenuates the inferior wall, not the anterior wall, and supine position does not reduce it."}, {"id": 61, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist proposes giving the 30 mCi Tc-99m dose first at stress and the 10 mCi dose second at rest on the same day, hoping for a better stress image because the patient is large. Which statement correctly assesses this plan?", "options": {"A": "Acceptable if rest imaging is delayed 4 hours so stress activity decays below the rest dose.", "B": "Give the smaller dose first, since residual activity contaminates the larger study.", "C": "Acceptable, since the half-life of technetium is 6 hours and decay clears activity quickly.", "D": "Wrong, because stress imaging must follow rest to allow comparison with baseline images."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give the smaller dose first, since residual activity contaminates the larger study.): On a one-day technetium protocol the first injection leaves activity in the myocardium and background when the second is given. Using a smaller first dose and a second dose about three times larger lets the second study dominate the counts. Giving 30 mCi first would swamp the following 10 mCi study. Stress-first is valid with a small stress dose, and decay over a few hours alone is not enough."}, {"id": 62, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 100 cm2 floor wipe in the injection room gives 1,410 gross cpm in a well counter with efficiency of 30 percent, and background of 60 cpm. The facility action level is 2,000 dpm per 100 cm2. What result and action are correct?", "options": {"A": "About 4,500 dpm, above the action level, so decontaminate and rewipe.", "B": "About 1,350 dpm, below the action level, since counts per minute equal disintegrations.", "C": "About 4,700 dpm, above the action level, since background need not be subtracted.", "D": "About 405 dpm, below the action level, so no further action is needed."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 4,500 dpm, above the action level, so decontaminate and rewipe.): Net counts are 1,410 minus 60, or 1,350 cpm. Dividing by the 30 percent efficiency converts counts to disintegrations, giving 1,350 over 0.30, or 4,500 dpm. That exceeds the 2,000 dpm action level, so the area is decontaminated and wiped again. Skipping either step gives a wrong number and sometimes a wrong decision."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man with exertional chest tightness reaches 10 METs, and gated Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT shows an EF of 58 percent at stress and rest. The image shows a summed stress score of 2 and rest score of 0, confined to the mid lateral wall. How should the findings be applied to management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the study with FDG PET to measure viability in the lateral wall before choosing therapy.", "B": "Send him for coronary angiography, because any reversible defect marks a high risk patient needing revascularization.", "C": "Report small mild reversible lateral ischemia and begin optimized medical therapy given low-risk features.", "D": "Call the study normal, because a defect limited to two segments is below the reporting threshold."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report small mild reversible lateral ischemia and begin optimized medical therapy given low-risk features.): Risk follows the size and severity of reversible defects, EF and exercise capacity. A defect in two mid lateral segments (summed difference score 2) with preserved EF and 10 METs of capacity is low risk, so medical therapy is the sound first step. Angiography is for large or severe ischemia, normal reporting would miss a real defect, and viability imaging answers a scar question that does not apply here.", "image": "question_images/q_63.png"}, {"id": 64, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "An air conditioning failure raises the temperature of a camera room by 12 degrees C in under an hour. The uncovered gantry holds a 3/8 inch NaI(Tl) crystal detector. The physicist is asked about the most likely consequence of this rapid temperature change for the detector.", "options": {"A": "Photomultiplier gain doubles, so counts rise uniformly across the field and the window must be lowered.", "B": "Thermal stress can crack the NaI(Tl) crystal, producing a cold defect that needs crystal replacement.", "C": "Collimator septa expand and narrow the holes, improving resolution while cutting sensitivity across the field.", "D": "Thallium doping washes out of the crystal, permanently shifting the photopeak lower on the spectrum."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Thermal stress can crack the NaI(Tl) crystal, producing a cold defect that needs crystal replacement.): NaI(Tl) is brittle and sensitive to rapid temperature changes, with limits of only a few degrees per hour, so cracking is the main risk. A crack scatters scintillation light and appears as a cold region on a flood image. Tube gain drift is possible but would not be uniform or helpful. Thermal expansion of lead septa is trivially small."}, {"id": 65, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "An older Tc-99m SPECT camera uses a gadolinium-153 line source for transmission scanning. Reviewing a heavy patient's AC images, a reader sees a fixed anterior defect and notes truncated outlines in the transmission map. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Truncation of the transmission map by the limited field of view at the chest edge", "B": "Emission cross-talk from the Tc-99m dose, which has the same energy as the gadolinium-153 source", "C": "A CT misregistration with lung overlap, which occurs only with CT-based attenuation correction", "D": "Metal lead artifact from an implanted device, which overestimates attenuation in the nearby myocardium"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Truncation of the transmission map by the limited field of view at the chest edge): Line-source systems measure transmission through the patient, and in large patients the body can extend beyond the field of view, truncating the map and giving wrong coefficients at the chest edge that can create a false defect. CT misregistration applies to hybrid CT systems, and metal artifact needs an implanted device, which is not described. Gadolinium-153 emits at 97 and 103 keV, so cross-talk is managed by energy windows."}, {"id": 66, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 59-year-old man has 1.5 mm of horizontal ST depression at peak treadmill exercise and finishes the test. In recovery at 5 minutes, the ST depression persists and he is asymptomatic. What is the correct approach to stress ECG monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Continue ECG and blood pressure monitoring in recovery until ST changes resolve or return to baseline", "B": "Stop monitoring once heart rate falls below 100, since ST changes then no longer matter", "C": "Remove the limb leads to the wrists to improve recording, since torso leads affect recovery tracings", "D": "Stop monitoring at 5 minutes, since a fixed time is the only requirement for recovery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue ECG and blood pressure monitoring in recovery until ST changes resolve or return to baseline): Recovery monitoring is part of the stress ECG, because ischemic ST depression that appears or persists in recovery has diagnostic and safety value. Monitoring continues for at least several minutes and until ST changes, symptoms, heart rate and blood pressure return near baseline. A fixed time or rate does not address persistent changes. Stress ECG limb leads are placed on the torso, not the wrists, to reduce motion artifact."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old woman receiving doxorubicin followed by trastuzumab has a baseline MUGA LVEF of 64 percent. Her follow-up MUGA after several cycles shows an LVEF of 49 percent, and she remains asymptomatic. How should this finding be interpreted and handled?", "options": {"A": "It is not cardiotoxicity, since the LVEF remains above 40 percent.", "B": "Stop all cancer therapy permanently without repeat imaging because LVEF fell by fifteen points.", "C": "It meets criteria for cardiotoxicity, so repeat imaging in two to three weeks and involve cardio-oncology.", "D": "It is within test-retest variability, so continue the regimen and repeat MUGA at the usual interval."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It meets criteria for cardiotoxicity, so repeat imaging in two to three weeks and involve cardio-oncology.): A fall of more than 10 points to below 53 percent defines cardiotoxicity in imaging consensus, and a 15-point drop is far beyond MUGA variability of a few points. Confirmation with repeat imaging in two to three weeks and a cardio-oncology assessment come before decisions on therapy. An LVEF above 40 percent does not exclude cardiotoxicity, and stopping permanently without confirmation is premature."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with left bundle branch block and exertional chest pain completes 9 METs on the treadmill with Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Ejection fraction is 58 percent. The summed stress score is 8 and the rest score is 4, with all abnormal segments in the septum. What is the best interpretation and recommendation?", "options": {"A": "True septal ischemia, because the target heart rate was reached, so angiography should be scheduled.", "B": "A normal study, because septal counts are lower for technical reasons, so no comment is needed.", "C": "Probable heart rate related bundle branch artifact, so any repeat study should use vasodilator stress.", "D": "A fixed anteroseptal infarct scar, so viability testing should be arranged."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Probable heart rate related bundle branch artifact, so any repeat study should use vasodilator stress.): With left bundle branch block, exercise raises the heart rate and produces septal defects that are not due to coronary stenosis, and the defect is still present at rest. Abnormal segments confined to the septum, with the other walls scoring 0, fit this pattern. The report should say the pattern is probably artifactual and recommend vasodilator stress if repeat testing is needed. Reading it as ischemia leads to needless angiography. Calling it normal without comment omits information the referring physician needs.", "image": "question_images/q_68.png"}, {"id": 69, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A diabetic patient fasting for 6 hours has a glucose of 210 mg/dL at FDG injection for viability assessment before revascularization. Images show poor myocardial uptake with high blood pool activity in a segment with a perfusion defect. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Poor metabolic preparation lowers uptake and may falsely suggest scar, so reimage after optimizing.", "B": "Absent FDG uptake in the perfusion defect proves transmural scar and ends the viability evaluation.", "C": "High blood pool activity is expected and confirms hibernating myocardium in the perfusion defect.", "D": "Hyperglycemia increases FDG uptake in viable myocardium, so this study overestimates true viability."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Poor metabolic preparation lowers uptake and may falsely suggest scar, so reimage after optimizing.): In a hyperglycemic or insulin-resistant patient, circulating glucose and fatty acids compete with FDG, so myocardial uptake is low and blood pool activity stays high. The study may falsely suggest nonviability. The remedy is to optimize metabolic preparation, for example with an insulin protocol or clamp, and repeat. Hyperglycemia lowers, not raises, FDG uptake."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64 year old man with a suspected high-grade LAD stenosis takes metoprolol and isosorbide mononitrate on the morning of an exercise perfusion study. The study is meant to diagnose coronary disease, not to assess therapy. What is the most likely consequence of testing on these drugs?", "options": {"A": "No effect on the results, since sestamibi uptake reflects flow and is independent of drugs taken", "B": "Increased subdiaphragmatic uptake will degrade inferior images, since nitrates raise tracer clearance in the liver", "C": "Ischemia may be overestimated and a false positive may result, since beta blockers lower coronary flow", "D": "Ischemia may be underestimated, since drugs blunt rate response and reduce ischemia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ischemia may be underestimated, since drugs blunt rate response and reduce ischemia): Beta blockers limit the heart rate and blood pressure rise, and nitrates dilate coronary arteries and reduce ischemia, so both can reduce defect size or remove it. When the study is meant to diagnose disease, these drugs are usually held when safe. Tracer uptake depends on flow, so drug-modified stress changes the picture."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with typical angina and a left bundle branch block is scheduled for treadmill exercise MPI by his cardiologist. A technologist asks what could go wrong. What is the most likely consequence of choosing exercise over vasodilator stress in this patient?", "options": {"A": "A fixed inferior defect from diaphragmatic attenuation that hides a true right coronary lesion", "B": "A false negative study because the bundle branch block lowers coronary flow reserve", "C": "A false reversible septal defect at faster heart rates, producing a false positive study", "D": "Transient ischemic dilation that incorrectly suggests left main disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A false reversible septal defect at faster heart rates, producing a false positive study): In left bundle branch block, exercise-induced tachycardia worsens septal activation asynchrony and can produce an apparent septal or anteroseptal reversible defect even with a normal LAD. Vasodilator stress, which raises heart rate less, avoids that artifact. Diaphragmatic attenuation, false negatives from lowered reserve, and transient cavity dilation do not describe the bundle branch block problem."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A generator eluate contains 20 mCi of Tc-99m, with 4.0 uCi of Mo-99 measured in a lead-shielded assay. Radiochemical purity of the pertechnetate is 98 percent. The technologist argues the eluate is acceptable because purity passed. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Reject the eluate because 4.0 uCi exceeds an absolute limit of 0.15 uCi per vial", "B": "Reject the eluate for patient use, because 0.2 uCi Mo-99 per mCi exceeds the 0.15 limit", "C": "Use the eluate for patients after waiting one hour so Mo-99 can decay", "D": "Use the eluate, since 98 percent radiochemical purity shows it is acceptable for kit preparation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reject the eluate for patient use, because 0.2 uCi Mo-99 per mCi exceeds the 0.15 limit): Mo-99 breakthrough is judged as a ratio: 4.0 uCi divided by 20 mCi is 0.2 uCi per mCi, above the 0.15 uCi per mCi limit at administration. Radiochemical purity tests a different property and cannot offset excess Mo-99. Mo-99 has a 66-hour half-life, so an hour changes nothing, and the limit is per millicurie of Tc-99m rather than an absolute amount."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist prepares Tc-99m sestamibi from a kit, heats it, and runs thin-layer chromatography before dosing a patient with chest pain scheduled for rest and stress SPECT. The result is reported as radiochemical purity of the bound complex. What minimum radiochemical purity is required before the preparation may be given?", "options": {"A": "At least 75 percent, because free pertechnetate clears quickly through the kidneys and adds little dose.", "B": "At least 99.5 percent, the same standard applied to the purity of the generator eluate itself.", "C": "At least 90 percent of the activity must be present as the intact Tc-99m sestamibi complex.", "D": "At least 80 percent, since the remaining impurity is cleared by the liver before imaging begins."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (At least 90 percent of the activity must be present as the intact Tc-99m sestamibi complex.): The labeling of Tc-99m sestamibi is checked by chromatography, and the package insert sets a minimum radiochemical purity of 90 percent. Unbound pertechnetate and reduced hydrolyzed technetium add blood pool, thyroid and gastric activity and degrade images. The generator eluate figure is a different quality test, and tolerating 75 or 80 percent would accept a poor label that harms image quality."}, {"id": 74, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient with a moderate left anterior descending stenosis has hyperemic flow of about 3 mL/min/g, but the Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT shows no defect. A trainee asks why. Which tracer property best explains why moderate stenoses can be missed by this tracer?", "options": {"A": "Photon energy of 140 keV is attenuated more in the stenotic region and hides the defect", "B": "Higher extraction at high flow exaggerates uptake in the normal region and hides the stenosis", "C": "Redistribution of sestamibi during imaging fills the defect and erases the difference between regions", "D": "Extraction falls as flow rises, so uptake plateaus and narrows the normal to stenotic difference"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extraction falls as flow rises, so uptake plateaus and narrows the normal to stenotic difference): Sestamibi first-pass extraction is about 60 percent at normal flow and falls at high flow, so uptake rises less than flow does. This roll-off narrows the contrast between a moderate stenosis and normal myocardium. Sestamibi redistributes little, and photon energy does not selectively reduce counts in stenotic regions."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A routine extrinsic flood image shows a round cold area about 6 cm across in the upper central detector field. The camera has already been scheduled for patient studies this afternoon. What is the most likely consequence of imaging patients without correction?", "options": {"A": "A count void crossing the heart at some angles produces ring-like artifacts and false defects.", "B": "A uniform loss of total counts with no regional effect, so interpretation of defects stays accurate.", "C": "A hot spot at the failed tube position that mimics a reversible defect on stress images.", "D": "Only planar contrast is reduced, since SPECT reconstruction normalizes nonuniformity across the field."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A count void crossing the heart at some angles produces ring-like artifacts and false defects.): A failed or drifting photomultiplier tube leaves a focal count void. In SPECT, a void that moves across the field as the detector rotates reconstructs as a ring or arc artifact and can imitate a perfusion defect. The camera needs service before clinical use because reconstruction does not fix such defects."}, {"id": 76, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A hospital's Geiger counter used for daily hot lab surveys has a calibration sticker dated 15 months ago. The technologist notes it reads correctly against the check source today. What is wrong with continuing to use this meter for required surveys?", "options": {"A": "Survey instruments must be calibrated at least annually, so the 15 month interval has lapsed.", "B": "Nothing is wrong, since a correct check source reading verifies calibration, needed just every two years.", "C": "Calibration is needed every quarter, so the meter is overdue by a full interval and unusable.", "D": "Nothing is wrong, since calibration is needed only after repair and not at set intervals."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Survey instruments must be calibrated at least annually, so the 15 month interval has lapsed.): NRC rules (10 CFR 35.61) require survey instruments to be calibrated before first use, at intervals not exceeding 12 months, and after servicing. A daily check source reading confirms operability, not calibration. A 15 month gap exceeds the annual limit, so the meter should be removed from regulatory survey use until it is recalibrated."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 55-year-old woman in sinus rhythm with occasional ectopic beats has a blood pool study. The curve falls from 17,935 counts in the first frame to 2,570 at the end and never recovers. Which step best confirms the cause before repeating the study?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the labeling of red cells to improve in vivo labeling efficiency", "B": "Review the R-R histogram for beats shorter than the accepted window", "C": "Check camera uniformity with a flood field acquisition", "D": "Compare the first and last frame images for change in ventricular size"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Review the R-R histogram for beats shorter than the accepted window): A curve that fails to return suggests the acceptance window admitted beats shorter than the mean. The R-R histogram displays the beat lengths directly and shows how many fell outside the window, so it identifies the cause and guides a narrower window. Poor red cell labeling raises background but does not create late-frame count loss. A flood field tests detector uniformity, and frame images do not show beat timing.", "image": "question_images/q_77.png"}, {"id": 78, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "After regadenoson stress alone with Tc-99m sestamibi, a technologist began imaging 15 minutes after injection to save time. The images show intense hepatobiliary activity abutting the inferior wall, producing an apparent inferior defect, and gated wall motion is normal. Which action is best before the study is reported?", "options": {"A": "Delay acquisition to 45 to 60 minutes after injection with oral water to aid hepatobiliary clearance", "B": "Reinject a higher dose of tracer to improve the heart to liver activity ratio", "C": "Proceed and apply attenuation correction to remove the subdiaphragmatic activity from the inferior wall", "D": "Image immediately in the prone position because it moves the liver away from the inferior wall"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Delay acquisition to 45 to 60 minutes after injection with oral water to aid hepatobiliary clearance): After vasodilator stress alone, splanchnic flow is high and liver uptake clears slowly, so imaging is delayed to about 45 to 60 minutes, with water or other measures to speed clearance. Attenuation correction cannot remove hot adjacent activity, a larger dose does not alter the ratio, and prone imaging mainly addresses diaphragmatic attenuation rather than hepatic clearance."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A syringe of Tc-99m reads 100 mrem/h at its surface. Lead has a half-value layer of 0.3 mm for 140 keV photons. A technologist wants a shield that lowers the surface reading to below 6 mrem/h. What is the minimum lead thickness?", "options": {"A": "5.0 mm of lead, found by dividing 100 by 6 and multiplying by 0.3", "B": "1.5 mm of lead, which is five half-value layers at 0.3 mm each", "C": "1.2 mm of lead, since 4 half-value layers appear to give about 6 mrem/h", "D": "0.9 mm of lead, since 3 half-value layers cut the reading by a factor of 6"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1.5 mm of lead, which is five half-value layers at 0.3 mm each): Each half-value layer halves the rate. Four layers give 100 divided by 16, which is 6.25 mrem/h, still above 6. Five layers give 3.1 mrem/h, which meets the target, so the thickness is 5 times 0.3, or 1.5 mm. Using 4 layers rounds the wrong way, dividing the ratio directly overstates the thickness, and 3 layers give 12.5 mrem/h."}, {"id": 80, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old diabetic man with a BMI of 44 and suspected left main or three-vessel disease has an equivocal SPECT study from soft tissue attenuation. The team wants the most reliable noninvasive answer. Which feature explains why PET perfusion imaging performs better in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Rubidium-82 emits lower-energy photons than Tc-99m, so tissue attenuation is much smaller in obese patients.", "B": "PET studies are acquired without gating, which removes diaphragmatic motion artifact in obese patients.", "C": "CT attenuation correction, higher-energy photons and absolute flow quantification help reveal balanced ischemia.", "D": "Its long half-life allows delayed redistribution images that separate scar from ischemia in obese patients."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (CT attenuation correction, higher-energy photons and absolute flow quantification help reveal balanced ischemia.): PET uses coincidence detection of 511 keV photons, routine attenuation correction and the ability to compute myocardial blood flow and flow reserve, which unmasks balanced multivessel disease that relative SPECT uptake can miss. Rb-82 photons are higher, not lower, in energy. Rb-82 half-life is 76 seconds, and PET is gated."}, {"id": 81, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with atrial fibrillation at 90 bpm despite maximal rate control has stable atypical chest pain, no prior coronary testing and a coronary calcium score of 1,400. Which test is the best choice to evaluate him for ischemia?", "options": {"A": "Coronary CT angiography with additional beta blockade, because it is the most accurate test", "B": "CT-derived fractional flow reserve alone, because it removes any need for good CT images", "C": "Stress PET or SPECT MPI, since calcium blooming and irregular rate degrade CT angiography", "D": "Repeat coronary calcium scoring, because the score already shows high plaque burden"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stress PET or SPECT MPI, since calcium blooming and irregular rate degrade CT angiography): Heavy calcification causes blooming that overestimates stenosis, and a fast irregular rate adds motion artifact, so CT angiography is likely nondiagnostic. Perfusion imaging tests for ischemia regardless of calcium load. Calcium scoring does not show ischemia, and CT-derived flow reserve depends on a diagnostic CT dataset that this patient will not provide."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient with an ICD lead has SPECT/CT. On CT corrected images an intense apparent increase in uptake appears in the basal inferoseptal wall beside the lead, but the uncorrected images show normal uptake. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Subtract the counts in the lead region and report the remaining image as normal.", "B": "Repeat the CT at higher mAs to reduce streaks from the lead.", "C": "Report hibernating myocardium in the basal inferoseptal wall, since uptake is increased.", "D": "Review uncorrected images and the CT map for metal streaks first."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review uncorrected images and the CT map for metal streaks first.): Metal on CT produces streaks that overestimate attenuation, so the correction inflates the adjacent wall. The uncorrected image is the check, and the CT map should be reviewed for streaks. A higher mAs does not remove metal artifact."}, {"id": 83, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old obese man without known coronary disease has an inferior defect on non-corrected stress Tc-99m images. The exhibit compares inferior counts with and without attenuation correction. Gated thickening in the inferior wall is normal. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "True right coronary ischemia, because the inferior defect remains mildly reduced after attenuation correction.", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact, supported by normal thickening and count recovery, so report no ischemia.", "C": "Overcorrection artifact, because corrected images are considered less reliable than the uncorrected ones.", "D": "Inferior scar from prior infarction, because a fixed defect at rest indicates myocardial necrosis."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact, supported by normal thickening and count recovery, so report no ischemia.): Inferior counts rising from 62 to 88 percent after correction and normal thickening are typical of attenuation by the diaphragm in a large patient. A true lesion or scar would be expected to persist on corrected images and show abnormal thickening. Residual mild reduction of a few percent is within normal variation, and overcorrection would be suspected only with registration errors.", "image": "question_images/q_83.png"}, {"id": 84, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A myocardial perfusion study reconstructed by filtered backprojection with a ramp filter alone looks extremely noisy, with grainy count distribution across the myocardium. Which property of the ramp filter best explains this appearance, and what is the usual remedy applied by the laboratory?", "options": {"A": "It increases noise only during the first iteration and then converges, as OSEM does", "B": "It averages neighboring pixels and reduces noise but blurs edges, so a sharper filter is added", "C": "It corrects for photon attenuation but increases scatter, so scatter correction is added", "D": "It amplifies high-frequency noise, so it is paired with a low-pass window such as Butterworth"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It amplifies high-frequency noise, so it is paired with a low-pass window such as Butterworth): The ramp filter removes the blurring of simple backprojection but boosts high spatial frequencies, where noise dominates, so it is always combined with a low-pass window. The other statements describe a smoothing filter, attenuation correction or iterative reconstruction, none of which is the ramp."}, {"id": 85, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit shows an adenosine order and the pump setting for an 82 kg patient, who now has second-degree AV block and blood pressure 84/50 at minute 2. Based on the exhibit, what is the error, and what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Stop infusion and give atropine, since the rate error is irrelevant after short half-life.", "B": "The rate is three times too high; stop the infusion and monitor closely.", "C": "Reduce the pump to 3.8 mL per minute and continue, since the overdose is slight.", "D": "The rate is correct; continue infusion and give atropine for the block."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The rate is three times too high; stop the infusion and monitor closely.): The dose is 140 mcg x 82 kg = 11.5 mg per minute, and at 3 mg per mL that is 3.8 mL per minute. A setting of 11.5 mL per minute confuses mg with mL and delivers about three times the dose. The infusion should be stopped and the patient observed, with aminophylline for persistent hypotension or block.", "image": "question_images/q_85.png"}, {"id": 86, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During a dual-isotope study, a technologist gives the Tc-99m stress dose before the rest thallium-201 images are finished because the schedule is tight. The rest images then show an inferior wall that looks normal, while the stress images show a defect there. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Thallium-201 photons spill into the Tc-99m window and exaggerate the stress defect.", "B": "Thallium redistribution continues and lowers stress counts in all walls equally.", "C": "Tc-99m downscatter into the thallium window can fill in rest defects, so scar may look reversible.", "D": "Residual thallium-201 competes for sodium-potassium uptake sites and blocks Tc-99m delivery."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tc-99m downscatter into the thallium window can fill in rest defects, so scar may look reversible.): Tc-99m emits 140 keV photons that scatter down into the lower-energy thallium window and add counts, which can fill in a rest defect and make fixed scar look reversible. The sequence thallium first, then Tc-99m after thallium imaging, exists to prevent this. Thallium crosstalk into the Tc window is smaller."}, {"id": 87, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old woman has a Tc-99m rest-stress SPECT with regadenoson. The transient ischemic dilation ratio is 1.28, with only a small mild inferior defect and a stress ejection fraction of 52 percent versus 56 at rest. Stress images were acquired 20 minutes after injection. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Confirm identical processing, reorientation and masks, then review raw images and lung uptake.", "B": "Dismiss the ratio, since only gated ejection fraction can define true cavity size on perfusion studies.", "C": "Report severe three-vessel disease on the dilation ratio alone and recommend urgent angiography now.", "D": "Repeat the stress study after doubling the tracer dose to remove the apparent cavity dilation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirm identical processing, reorientation and masks, then review raw images and lung uptake.): A ratio above the protocol threshold may reflect real dilation from severe multivessel disease, but processing differences such as mismatched reorientation, slice selection or masks can create or hide it. With a small defect and preserved ejection fraction, confirming technical consistency and checking raw images and lung uptake is the right first step. Treating the ratio as proof, or dismissing it, are both errors, and dose changes do not alter cavity size."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old nondiabetic man with ischemic cardiomyopathy is referred for FDG viability imaging. He fasted 8 hours, and fingerstick glucose is 82 mg/dL. Both oral glucose loading and a euglycemic insulin clamp are available. Which preparation is the most suitable?", "options": {"A": "Insulin 10 units IV with no glucose, then inject FDG immediately to get the strongest uptake", "B": "Oral glucose load of about 50 g, then confirm glucose near 100 to 140 mg/dL", "C": "A euglycemic clamp is required, since oral glucose cannot drive FDG uptake in nondiabetic patients", "D": "Continue fasting to 12 hours, because low glucose makes the heart use glucose avidly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oral glucose load of about 50 g, then confirm glucose near 100 to 140 mg/dL): Fasting pushes myocardium toward fatty acid oxidation, which suppresses FDG uptake in viable tissue. A glucose load raises insulin and shifts the heart to glucose use, and in a nondiabetic patient it is usually adequate. A clamp is also acceptable but not required. Insulin without glucose risks hypoglycemia, and extending the fast reduces uptake."}, {"id": 89, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Automatic reorientation of a stress Tc-99m SPECT study set the long axis about 25 degrees off the true ventricular axis because intense hepatic uptake lies against the inferior wall. Short-axis slices now show apparent anteroseptal and inferolateral reductions. The raw projection images show no patient motion. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reinject tracer and repeat the stress acquisition because the reconstructed slices cannot be corrected", "B": "Manually reset the long axis through apex and valve plane, then reprocess stress and rest identically", "C": "Report multivessel ischemia since the reductions span two separate coronary artery territories", "D": "Apply CT-free attenuation correction to remove the apparent anteroseptal and inferolateral reductions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Manually reset the long axis through apex and valve plane, then reprocess stress and rest identically): A tilted long axis distorts the short-axis slices so that normal walls look reduced in opposite segments, a pattern that crosses vascular territories and is a clue to artifact. Raw projections are clean, so the data are intact and only the reorientation needs repair. Repeating injection adds dose without cause, attenuation correction does not alter geometry, and calling it multivessel disease would misdiagnose a processing error."}, {"id": 90, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist who is 8 weeks pregnant tells the physician in charge, in the hallway, that she is expecting. Her recent monthly badge readings are 12 mrem (0.12 mSv). The radiation safety officer is asked whether the embryo and fetus dose limit now formally applies to her. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Keep the 5 rem (50 mSv) annual limit until the second trimester begins", "B": "Apply the 0.5 rem (5 mSv) limit now, because a spoken statement to a supervisor counts", "C": "The limit applies after a written declaration with a conception date; ask her to submit one", "D": "Remove her from radiopharmaceutical work now, because nuclear laboratories cannot meet the fetal limit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The limit applies after a written declaration with a conception date; ask her to submit one): Under 10 CFR 20.1208 the embryo and fetus limit of 0.5 rem (5 mSv) for the gestation period applies to a worker who has declared pregnancy in writing, with the estimated date of conception. A hallway remark does not meet that requirement, although a supervisor should invite her to declare. Typical nuclear cardiology exposures are low enough that reassignment is not required. The limit does not wait for a trimester."}, {"id": 91, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with severe COPD cannot exercise and cannot stop his twice-daily theophylline because he was hospitalized recently for a COPD exacerbation. His MPI requires pharmacologic stress and the laboratory has all common stressors available. Which protocol is best for him?", "options": {"A": "Adenosine at a doubled infusion rate, because extra drug can overcome theophylline antagonism", "B": "Standard-dose regadenoson, because its selective receptor binding is unaffected by theophylline", "C": "Standard-dose dipyridamole, because it acts indirectly and is unaffected by theophylline", "D": "Dobutamine with atropine, since methylxanthines would blunt each adenosine receptor stressor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dobutamine with atropine, since methylxanthines would blunt each adenosine receptor stressor): Theophylline and caffeine antagonize adenosine receptors, so regadenoson, adenosine and dipyridamole are all blunted. When the drug cannot be held, dobutamine, which stimulates beta receptors, is the reliable stressor. Doubling adenosine is unpredictable and risks side effects, and selectivity or an indirect mechanism does not avoid receptor blockade."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man walks 9 minutes on a Bruce protocol with 3 mm of ST depression and exercise-limiting angina, angina index 2. Sestamibi polar maps show reduced anterior and apical stress counts, normal at rest, stress score 7. The Duke score is minutes minus five times ST depression minus four times angina index. Which combined interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Duke score minus 14, high risk, with reversible anterior and apical ischemia; angiography is reasonable.", "B": "Duke score minus 10, moderate risk, with reversible inferior ischemia; continue medical therapy alone.", "C": "Duke score minus 6, moderate risk, with a fixed anterior scar; no further testing is needed.", "D": "Duke score plus 4, low risk, with apical attenuation artifact; reassure and discharge him."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Duke score minus 14, high risk, with reversible anterior and apical ischemia; angiography is reasonable.): The calculation is 9 minus 5 times 3, which is 15, minus 4 times 2, which is 8, giving 9 minus 15 minus 8, or minus 14. A score of minus 11 or lower is high risk. Reduced anterior and apical counts at stress that normalize at rest are reversible left anterior descending ischemia, so both the treadmill score and the images point to significant disease and angiography is reasonable. An angina index of 1 gives minus 10, omitting angina gives minus 6, and a fixed scar or artifact would not reverse at rest.", "image": "question_images/q_92.png"}, {"id": 93, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with a BMI of 38 arrives in the emergency department and cannot exercise. Her physicians need a perfusion result within about 90 minutes to decide on admission. A PET camera with an Rb-82 generator and a SPECT camera are both available. What is the main advantage that favors PET here?", "options": {"A": "Lower photon energy that interacts more with tissue and so improves count density in obesity", "B": "A complete study in about 30 minutes, with attenuation correction preserving quality in obesity", "C": "Lower tracer cost per patient, which lets the study run faster than SPECT in the ED", "D": "A long tracer half-life, allowing stress and rest injections to be spread across several hours"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A complete study in about 30 minutes, with attenuation correction preserving quality in obesity): Rb-82 has a half-life of 76 seconds, so rest and stress images are acquired in minutes with a total study time near 30 minutes, and routine CT attenuation correction preserves quality in obesity. PET photons are 511 keV, higher in energy, not lower. The very short half-life is the speed advantage, and tracer cost is not lower than SPECT."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man with diabetes, an LDL of 130 mg/dL and a father who had a myocardial infarction at 58 asks for a nuclear stress test for peace of mind. He has no symptoms, a normal ECG and walks 5 miles weekly. What is the best recommendation from the nuclear cardiologist?", "options": {"A": "Perform exercise SPECT now, since diabetes alone makes screening perfusion imaging appropriate in an asymptomatic patient", "B": "Decline routine perfusion imaging, and use risk estimation with calcium scoring to guide lipids", "C": "Perform regadenoson PET flow quantification, so a baseline value exists for later comparison", "D": "Schedule exercise perfusion imaging yearly, since a normal study today does not protect later"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline routine perfusion imaging, and use risk estimation with calcium scoring to guide lipids): For an asymptomatic person with good functional capacity and a normal ECG, perfusion imaging rarely changes management and risks false positives. Risk calculation and calcium scoring help decide on statin therapy. Diabetes raises risk but does not by itself make screening MPI appropriate, and yearly imaging adds radiation without benefit."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old man had treadmill exercise and was injected at peak with Tc-99m sestamibi. Acquisition began 10 minutes later. The sinogram band climbs progressively across the acquisition. What explains the pattern, and what change would prevent it in the next patient?", "options": {"A": "Upward creep from early post-exercise imaging; start later, once breathing and heart position settle", "B": "Respiratory gating failure from irregular breathing; use respiratory binning during acquisition", "C": "Lateral drift from sliding on the pallet; use better arm support and secure strapping", "D": "An abrupt shift from a cough; coach the patient to remain still"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Upward creep from early post-exercise imaging; start later, once breathing and heart position settle): After vigorous exercise, ventilation depth and rate decline and the diaphragm rises, so the heart moves upward over the first minutes of imaging. That produces a progressive upward band shift. Starting later lets the heart settle. A cough produces a step, sliding produces sideways motion, and cardiac gating does not govern drift of this kind.", "image": "question_images/q_95.png"}, {"id": 96, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A survey meter reads 36 microsievert per hour at 1 m from a patient who just received Tc-99m sestamibi for a rest study. The technologist wants to know the expected rate at the nurse's desk 3 m away. Assuming a point source, what rate is expected?", "options": {"A": "About 12 microsievert per hour, since exposure falls in direct proportion to distance.", "B": "About 18 microsievert per hour, since tripling the distance halves the rate.", "C": "About 4 microsievert per hour, since exposure falls with the square of distance.", "D": "About 0.4 microsievert per hour, since exposure falls with the cube of distance."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 4 microsievert per hour, since exposure falls with the square of distance.): For a point source, the inverse square law says rate is proportional to 1 divided by distance squared. Moving from 1 m to 3 m reduces the rate by a factor of 9, so 36 divided by 9 equals 4 microsievert per hour. This explains why distance is an effective protection measure."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A department moves from filtered back projection to iterative OSEM for sestamibi SPECT. Previously, intense gut activity next to the inferior wall caused dark streaks and an apparent inferior defect. What is the expected effect of OSEM, and the cost of adding more iterations?", "options": {"A": "More streaks near the gut, because subsets amplify any activity outside the field of view.", "B": "Fewer streak artifacts beside hot extracardiac activity, with noise rising as iterations increase.", "C": "Complete removal of gut activity effects, because OSEM excludes counts that lie outside the myocardium.", "D": "Identical artifacts, because both methods rely on the same ramp filter in the reconstruction."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fewer streak artifacts beside hot extracardiac activity, with noise rising as iterations increase.): Filtered back projection uses a ramp filter that creates negative lobes and streaks next to very hot structures. OSEM avoids negative values by updating the estimate in subsets, which reduces that artifact. More iterations sharpen detail but amplify noise, so a limited number is chosen. OSEM does not remove extracardiac counts."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man receives the standard dipyridamole infusion, but his heart rate rises only 6 bpm and he has no flushing or chest tightness. He admits having two espresso drinks five hours earlier. What is the most likely cause of the blunted response?", "options": {"A": "Caffeine blocked adenosine receptors, so the raised adenosine could not dilate arterioles", "B": "A beta blocker he took that morning prevented the expected vasodilator response in arterioles", "C": "The infusion was started too slowly, so adenosine failed to accumulate", "D": "An isosorbide dose that morning prevented adenosine from reaching the arterioles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Caffeine blocked adenosine receptors, so the raised adenosine could not dilate arterioles): Dipyridamole raises endogenous adenosine, which needs adenosine receptors to dilate coronary arterioles. Caffeine is a competitive antagonist, and recent intake of 12 or more hours before testing should be avoided to prevent a blunted response. Beta blockers and nitrates do not block adenosine receptors, and a normal infusion rate is not the issue."}, {"id": 99, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A patient asks how the dose of her Rb-82 rest-stress PET study, about 3 mSv, compares with everyday background exposure and with other nuclear cardiology studies she has read about online before today's visit. Which statement is the best comparison?", "options": {"A": "Roughly ten years of natural background, similar to a dual isotope Tl-201 protocol", "B": "About the same as a one day Tc-99m study, since both use cardiac perfusion tracers", "C": "About one year of natural background in the United States, well below a typical Tc-99m study", "D": "Less than a single chest radiograph, so it is comparable to a few days of background"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About one year of natural background in the United States, well below a typical Tc-99m study): Average annual natural background in the United States is about 3 mSv, and Rb-82 PET delivers roughly 3 mSv, because Rb-82 decays within minutes. A one day Tc-99m study gives about 9 mSv and dual isotope protocols more. A chest radiograph is about 0.1 mSv, so it is far smaller."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 38-year-old man has exertional chest pain. SPECT shows reversible septal and apical defects, a small left ventricular cavity, an LVEF of 72 percent, and a markedly thickened septum. Coronary risk factors are absent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal study since the EF is above 70 percent and no scar is seen.", "B": "Refer directly for stenting of the left anterior descending artery to treat the reversible defect.", "C": "Obtain echocardiography or cardiac MRI to evaluate for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.", "D": "Start nitrates and repeat the stress study in six months to assess response."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain echocardiography or cardiac MRI to evaluate for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.): Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy can cause reversible defects and angina through microvascular dysfunction and small-cavity physiology despite normal coronary arteries. A thick septum, small cavity, and hyperdynamic EF in a young man point to it. Imaging that characterizes hypertrophy is the next step. Nitrates can worsen outflow obstruction, and stenting treats a lesion that may not exist."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with large breasts has a fixed anterior defect on supine SPECT with a summed stress score of 9 and a summed rest score of 9. Prone stress images show normal anterior perfusion with a small inferior artifact scoring 2. What is the summed difference score for the supine pair and its meaning?", "options": {"A": "Two, reflecting mild ischemia equal to the prone score recorded for the inferior artifact.", "B": "Zero, a fixed defect that resolves prone, supporting breast attenuation and a low-risk study.", "C": "Seven, reflecting substantial reversible ischemia, calculated by subtracting the prone score from the supine stress score.", "D": "Eighteen, reflecting extensive combined ischemia and scar, calculated by adding the stress and rest scores."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Zero, a fixed defect that resolves prone, supporting breast attenuation and a low-risk study.): The summed difference score equals the summed stress score minus the summed rest score, so 9 minus 9 is zero, meaning no reversibility. A fixed anterior defect that resolves with prone positioning is typical of breast attenuation. The prone inferior artifact is positional and does not enter the supine calculation."}, {"id": 102, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A laboratory records daily Geiger counter surveys of the hot lab bench, which always read at background, but never performs wipe tests. Over several weeks Tl-201 residue is carried by gloves to a door handle and keyboard. What is the most likely consequence of relying on the meter alone?", "options": {"A": "Low-level removable contamination stays undetected until a wipe sample counted in a well counter reveals it.", "B": "Fixed contamination is mistaken for removable contamination and triggers needless decay-in-storage of the bench surface.", "C": "Thallium contamination decays to background within hours, so gloves transfer no meaningful activity to other surfaces.", "D": "The institution is cited because daily surveys must be wipe tests rather than meter surveys."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low-level removable contamination stays undetected until a wipe sample counted in a well counter reveals it.): A meter survey finds gross contamination, but thin-window detectors respond poorly to the 70 to 80 keV x-rays of Tl-201 at low levels, and small removable amounts are only quantified by counting a wipe sample. NRC rules call for both daily area surveys where written-directive drugs are used and weekly checks, with wipes used for removable contamination. Tl-201 has a 73 hour half-life, so the residue persists for days."}, {"id": 103, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A monthly badge report lists a deep dose of 340 mrem for a technologist. The facility's ALARA log records values in mSv and sets an investigation level of 1.0 mSv for the month. Which entry and comparison with that level is correct?", "options": {"A": "340 mSv, since mrem and mSv are treated as equal units in the log", "B": "3.4 mSv, which exceeds the 1.0 mSv investigation level", "C": "34 mSv, which exceeds the 1.0 mSv investigation level by a wide margin", "D": "0.34 mSv, which stays below the 1.0 mSv investigation level"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (3.4 mSv, which exceeds the 1.0 mSv investigation level): One rem equals 10 mSv, so 1 mrem equals 0.01 mSv and 340 mrem equals 3.4 mSv. That is above the 1.0 mSv level and triggers review. The other values come from a wrong power of ten or from ignoring the unit difference."}, {"id": 104, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 45-year-old woman was imaged soon after treadmill stress, and the sinogram band shows gradual drift. Before correction, the reader wants direct confirmation from the raw data. Which review best confirms upward heart movement? The tracer was Tc-99m sestamibi, and imaging began within about ten minutes of injection.", "options": {"A": "Review the lung to heart count ratio on the first projection", "B": "Compare the first and last gated frames for change in wall thickening", "C": "Review the rotating raw projection cine with a crosshair on the apex for vertical shift", "D": "Review the summed polar map for a uniform change in count density across all walls"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the rotating raw projection cine with a crosshair on the apex for vertical shift): Raw projection images show the heart directly, and a fixed crosshair on the apex lets the reader see whether it rises across projections. Polar maps and gated data are processed outputs that cannot show position change, and the lung to heart ratio relates to tracer distribution, not movement. Confirming from raw data supports a correction decision.", "image": "question_images/q_104.png"}, {"id": 105, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist reviews a sinogram after a SPECT acquisition and reports that the patient sneezed once midway, while otherwise lying quiet and still for the 15 minute scan. The sinogram in the exhibit accompanies the report. Which sinogram appearance corresponds to a single sudden movement of the heart?", "options": {"A": "A band whose center slides sideways steadily through the whole acquisition time", "B": "A smooth band that climbs gradually and steadily across all of the projection angles shown", "C": "A continuous unbroken sinusoidal band with no breaks or offsets across projection angles", "D": "An abrupt step in the band at one angle, with the path offset afterward"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An abrupt step in the band at one angle, with the path offset afterward): A sudden displacement shifts the heart between two adjacent projections, so the band shows a sharp step and then continues along an offset path. A gradual climb is creep, a smooth unbroken sinusoid means no motion, and a steady sideways slide is lateral drift. Each pattern reflects a different kind of movement and a different corrective response.", "image": "question_images/q_105.png"}, {"id": 106, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 45-year-old man has new heart failure, a dilated left ventricle and an ejection fraction of 30 percent. He has no angina, and his lipids and blood pressure are normal. The team wants to exclude obstructive coronary disease. Which test best matches this patient?", "options": {"A": "Treadmill exercise SPECT, since a normal scan would prove the epicardial arteries are open and patent.", "B": "Coronary CT angiography, because perfusion defects in dilated cardiomyopathy are nonspecific for epicardial stenosis.", "C": "Dobutamine stress SPECT, since inotropic stress reliably unmasks flow-limiting stenosis in ventricles with reduced systolic function.", "D": "Thallium-201 redistribution imaging, because delayed images separate scar from ischemia even in dilated, thin-walled ventricles."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Coronary CT angiography, because perfusion defects in dilated cardiomyopathy are nonspecific for epicardial stenosis.): Dilated cardiomyopathy and myocarditis produce patchy uptake, fixed defects and false positive perfusion findings from microvascular disease, fibrosis and wall stress. Anatomic imaging with CT angiography, or invasive angiography, answers whether obstructive stenosis exists. A normal scan would lower odds, but an abnormal one would remain unclear. Thallium and dobutamine do not fix the specificity problem."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 62-year-old man receiving heparin and doxorubicin needs a MUGA. His previous in vivo labeled study showed poor blood pool activity with prominent stomach and bladder activity. The cardiology team needs a reliable EF this week. For the best labeling efficiency this time, which labeling method fits?", "options": {"A": "Modified in vivo labeling with pertechnetate injected first and pyrophosphate 20 minutes afterward.", "B": "Switch to Tc-99m sulfur colloid, which labels red cells during a brief blood draw.", "C": "Repeat in vivo labeling with a larger stannous pyrophosphate dose given 5 minutes before pertechnetate.", "D": "In vitro labeling with a commercial kit, which gives about 95 percent labeling."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (In vitro labeling with a commercial kit, which gives about 95 percent labeling.): Heparin and some chemotherapy drugs lower in vivo labeling efficiency. In vitro labeling, done with a kit, gives about 95 percent labeling and the cleanest blood pool. Modified in vivo labeling needs pyrophosphate first and pertechnetate about 20 minutes later. Sulfur colloid goes to the reticuloendothelial system and does not label red cells."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man who had an inferior infarction years ago has rest sestamibi imaging reviewed for viability. The slices show a persistent inferior and inferoseptal defect, with inferior counts at only about 25 to 35 percent of peak at rest. Which statement gives the conventional counts criterion used to call a defect viable on Tc-99m perfusion imaging?", "options": {"A": "Rest counts must reach at least 80 percent of peak before myocardium is considered viable.", "B": "Rest counts of at least 50 to 60 percent of peak suggest viable myocardium.", "C": "Rest counts of at least 30 percent of peak are enough to call myocardium fully viable.", "D": "Viability is judged by defect uptake compared with the liver rather than with peak myocardial counts."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rest counts of at least 50 to 60 percent of peak suggest viable myocardium.): On Tc-99m perfusion imaging, rest counts of at least 50 to 60 percent of peak suggest viable myocardium. This patient's inferior counts of about 25 to 35 percent fall below that range, so the wall is mostly scar and little recovery is expected. An 80 percent requirement is too strict, 30 percent is too low to call fully viable, and liver uptake is not a viability reference.", "image": "question_images/q_108.png"}, {"id": 109, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist is positioning a patient for a standard 180-degree Tc-99m SPECT acquisition from 45 degrees RAO to 45 degrees LPO. She wants to minimize attenuation of the lateral wall and keep the patient comfortable and still. How should the arms be placed?", "options": {"A": "Both arms raised above the head and supported, with the detectors close to the chest.", "B": "Both arms crossed over the chest, to bring them away from the detector paths.", "C": "Both arms resting at the sides, to reduce shoulder discomfort and patient motion during the scan.", "D": "Right arm raised and left arm at the side, so the left detector passes close."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Both arms raised above the head and supported, with the detectors close to the chest.): Raising both arms removes them from the path between the lateral wall and the detector in the left-sided projections. Supporting the arms limits shoulder discomfort and motion. Arms at the sides or crossed place tissue in the photon path, and leaving the left arm down attenuates the lateral wall."}, {"id": 110, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with atypical chest pain has a normal exercise ECG and an LVEF of 62 percent. The exhibit plots normalized stress counts by segment from the Tc-99m sestamibi polar map. Wall motion and thickening are normal in every segment. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Report a small fixed apical scar and start secondary prevention therapy for prior infarction.", "B": "Report a normal study with expected apical thinning and recommend no further ischemia testing.", "C": "Refer for left anterior descending angiography because the apical count drop signals a distal stenosis.", "D": "Repeat the stress acquisition with Tl-201 to confirm the apical finding before issuing a report."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report a normal study with expected apical thinning and recommend no further ischemia testing.): The apex is physiologically thinner than the other walls, so counts of roughly 70 percent of maximum in segment 17 are a recognized normal variant. Preserved thickening, a normal ECG, and an EF of 62 percent in a low-risk woman support a normal study. A distal left anterior descending lesion or a scar would show abnormal motion or thickening and a defect beyond the apex alone. Repeating the study with another tracer adds radiation without changing the answer.", "image": "question_images/q_110.png"}, {"id": 111, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Eight minutes after regadenoson, a 61-year-old man reports a headache and mild dyspnea. The exhibit lists his monitoring values, and the ECG shows sinus rhythm with no ST change. He is awake, speaks normally and is lying supine. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Discharge him now with advice to drink fluids, since the tracer is injected.", "B": "Give aminophylline intravenously now, since any symptom after regadenoson should be reversed before discharge.", "C": "Keep him supine on monitoring until pressure and symptoms recover, since the effect fades.", "D": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin for the dyspnea, since regadenoson angina responds to nitrates."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him supine on monitoring until pressure and symptoms recover, since the effect fades.): Regadenoson's hemodynamic effects wane over roughly 30 minutes. A mild fall in pressure with a headache, no ST change and stable oxygenation is managed by monitoring in a supine position. Aminophylline is reserved for significant or persistent ischemia, bronchospasm or severe hypotension. Nitrates would lower pressure further, and discharge is premature.", "image": "question_images/q_111.png"}, {"id": 112, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A cardiologist reviewing a laboratory's tracer choices wants a perfusion tracer whose first-pass myocardial extraction stays high at hyperemic flow, so that flow reserve is less underestimated at stress. The lab currently offers four options. Which tracer best fits this requirement for high-flow measurement?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201, whose extraction plateaus early and which tracks flow poorly at high rates.", "B": "Rb-82, whose extraction rises linearly as coronary flow increases during hyperemia.", "C": "F-18 flurpiridaz, which has very high first-pass extraction and little roll-off at high flow.", "D": "Tc-99m sestamibi, whose extraction is high and nearly constant across the whole flow range."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (F-18 flurpiridaz, which has very high first-pass extraction and little roll-off at high flow.): Flurpiridaz has an extraction fraction near 94 percent and keeps a nearly linear relationship with flow, so its roll-off at high flow is small. Rb-82 extraction is about 65 percent and falls as flow rises. Sestamibi extraction is lower and rolls off at high flow, and thallium also shows plateau at higher flows."}, {"id": 113, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with exertional angina has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT, and the stress and rest slices are in the image. Basal segments are well perfused on both rows. If coronary angiography were done, which lesion location would best account for the perfusion pattern in the image?", "options": {"A": "A stenosis in the proximal left circumflex artery before the first obtuse marginal branch.", "B": "A stenosis in the mid LAD beyond the first diagonal and first septal branches.", "C": "A stenosis in the distal right coronary artery with a dominant posterior descending branch.", "D": "A stenosis in the proximal LAD before the first diagonal and septal branches, affecting basal walls."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A stenosis in the mid LAD beyond the first diagonal and first septal branches.): The reversible defect (summed stress score 9, rest score 0) involves the mid anterior and mid anteroseptal segments and the apical anterior and apical septal segments, while the basal anterior and anteroseptal walls and the apex stay normal. Those basal walls are fed by the first diagonal and first septal branches, so the culprit must lie beyond them. A proximal LAD lesion would reduce the basal segments too, and the circumflex and right coronary territories do not include the anterior wall and apex.", "image": "question_images/q_113.png"}, {"id": 114, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During gated SPECT in a 70-year-old man with frequent ectopy, the system accepted 450 of 600 recorded beats and rejected 150 outside the R-R window. Perfusion counts were adequate. What percentage of beats was rejected, and what does it imply for the study?", "options": {"A": "Seventy-five percent rejected, which invalidates the perfusion images and requires reinjecting tracer.", "B": "Thirty-three percent rejected, from dividing by accepted beats, a rate that still gives reliable function.", "C": "Twenty-five percent rejected, so gated function is less reliable though perfusion images remain interpretable.", "D": "Twenty-five percent rejected, which is acceptable since rejection does not affect ejection fraction."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Twenty-five percent rejected, so gated function is less reliable though perfusion images remain interpretable.): The rejection rate is rejected beats divided by all recorded beats: 150 divided by 600 equals 25 percent. Counts for perfusion remain adequate, but a high rejection rate degrades temporal sampling and makes ejection fraction and volumes less reliable, which should be stated in the report. Dividing by accepted beats gives 33 percent, and 75 percent is the accepted fraction."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man with severe orthopnea cannot lie flat for more than five minutes. He has a BMI of 31 and needs stress myocardial perfusion imaging. The lab owns both a conventional dual-head camera and a dedicated cardiac CZT camera. Which approach suits him best?", "options": {"A": "Use the conventional camera supine for 20 minutes, giving an anxiolytic to reduce movement", "B": "Switch to a planar anterior view, which needs the shortest time on the table", "C": "Use the CZT camera seated or reclined, centering the heart in its small field", "D": "Use the CZT camera supine, since its high sensitivity removes any positioning concern"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use the CZT camera seated or reclined, centering the heart in its small field): Solid-state CZT cameras have high sensitivity and good energy resolution, so a complete study takes a few minutes and can be done sitting or reclined, which fits a patient with orthopnea. Their drawback is a small field of view, so the heart must be centered and not truncated. The conventional approach lengthens a time he cannot tolerate. Planar imaging sacrifices tomographic localization of defects."}, {"id": 116, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Daily QC on a Rb-82 generator gives Sr-82 at 0.0025 µCi per mCi of Rb-82, above the 0.002 alert limit but below the 0.02 expiration limit. Sr-85 is also under its alert limit. What is the correct way to continue?", "options": {"A": "Test every eluate from now on and stop use if any result reaches an expiration limit", "B": "Remove the generator today, because reaching the alert limit equals the expiration limit", "C": "Keep the daily morning test only, since alert limits are advisory and carry no extra duty", "D": "Test only Sr-85 from here on, since Sr-82 above the alert limit is already known"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Test every eluate from now on and stop use if any result reaches an expiration limit): An alert limit is an early warning, not an expiration. Once Sr-82 exceeds 0.002 µCi/mCi, the generator may continue only while each eluate is tested and stays below 0.02 µCi/mCi for Sr-82 and 0.2 for Sr-85. Dropping either measurement or reverting to one test a day would miss a limit crossing."}, {"id": 117, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54 year old woman with exertional chest pain, intermediate pretest probability and no prior testing takes digoxin and has 1.5 mm of baseline ST depression. She can reach 9 METs. Two tests are appropriate by the criteria. Which should be ordered?", "options": {"A": "Resting echocardiography, because normal wall motion at rest excludes obstructive coronary disease in most patients", "B": "Exercise treadmill ECG alone, because she can exercise and the pretest probability is only intermediate here", "C": "Regadenoson perfusion imaging, because vasodilator stress is more sensitive than exercise in women with chest pain", "D": "Exercise SPECT or PET perfusion imaging, since baseline ST depression makes the exercise ECG uninterpretable"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exercise SPECT or PET perfusion imaging, since baseline ST depression makes the exercise ECG uninterpretable): Appropriate use criteria allow exercise ECG for patients able to exercise with an interpretable ECG, but digoxin and baseline ST depression make ST analysis unreliable. Adding perfusion imaging solves that and keeps the prognostic value of exercise. Switching to vasodilator wastes functional capacity, and normal resting wall motion does not rule out ischemia."}, {"id": 118, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The chart plots the segment scores from a rest-stress SPECT in a 60-year-old man, on the standard 0 to 4 scale across the 17-segment model. Using the chart, which statement correctly gives the summed stress score and its percentage of the maximum score of 68?", "options": {"A": "Summed stress score 14, from adding rest scores to stress scores, about 21 percent.", "B": "Summed stress score 12, which is about 18 percent of the maximum score of 68.", "C": "Summed stress score 10, the stress minus rest difference, about 15 percent of the maximum.", "D": "Summed stress score 12, which is 71 percent of the maximum since it divides by 17."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Summed stress score 12, which is about 18 percent of the maximum score of 68.): The stress series gives scores of 2, 2, 3, 3 and 2 in segments 7, 8, 13, 14 and 17, for a sum of 12. The rest series gives 1 in segments 13 and 14, a sum of 2, so the difference score is 10. The stress sum divided by the maximum 68 is about 18 percent. The divisor is 68, not 17, and the rest sum is not added.", "image": "question_images/q_118.png"}, {"id": 119, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist measures 12 mrem/h at 50 cm from an injection syringe and spends 20 minutes at that distance on one task. She can either shorten the task to 10 minutes at 50 cm, or keep 20 minutes but work at 100 cm. Which choice gives the lower dose?", "options": {"A": "Work at 100 cm, since dose falls to one quarter, about 1 mrem against 2 mrem", "B": "Shorten to 10 minutes, since halving time cuts dose more than doubling distance does", "C": "Either choice, since halving the time and doubling the distance both reduce dose by half", "D": "Work at 100 cm for 20 minutes, giving about 2 mrem, matching the 10 minute task"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Work at 100 cm, since dose falls to one quarter, about 1 mrem against 2 mrem): The starting dose is 12 mrem/h times one third of an hour, or 4 mrem. Doubling distance cuts the rate to one quarter by the inverse square law, giving 1 mrem. Halving time gives 2 mrem. Distance is therefore the stronger measure. Treating distance as linear gives the same 2 mrem as the shorter task, which understates the benefit."}, {"id": 120, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During a Bruce protocol treadmill test, a 54-year-old man exercises for 7 minutes, develops 2 mm of horizontal ST depression, and has nonlimiting angina that does not stop the test. He is on no rate-lowering drugs. Using the Duke treadmill score, which result is correct?", "options": {"A": "Score minus 11, from scoring the angina as exercise limiting, giving the high risk group.", "B": "Score minus 7 (7 minus 10 minus 4), placing him in the intermediate risk group.", "C": "Score plus 4, from leaving out the multipliers on ST depression and angina, giving low risk.", "D": "Score minus 14, from dropping the exercise time term, giving the high risk group."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Score minus 7 (7 minus 10 minus 4), placing him in the intermediate risk group.): The Duke score equals exercise minutes minus 5 times the ST deviation in mm minus 4 times the angina index (0 none, 1 nonlimiting, 2 limiting). Here: 7 minus 10 minus 4 equals minus 7, which lies in the intermediate range of minus 10 to plus 4. Scoring the angina as limiting gives minus 11, leaving out the multipliers gives plus 4, and omitting time gives minus 14."}, {"id": 121, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with long standing hypertension and concentric left ventricular hypertrophy has gated SPECT with an end diastolic volume of 38 mL and a reported EF of 82 percent. Echocardiography suggests mild dysfunction. What is the consequence of accepting the SPECT EF?", "options": {"A": "No consequence, because the ratio of volumes cancels any error from cavity size.", "B": "EF would be overestimated only in dilated ventricles, so the 82 percent can be trusted.", "C": "A mildly reduced EF may be missed, since gated SPECT overestimates EF in small ventricles.", "D": "EF would be underestimated, because small cavities lose counts through partial volume effects."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A mildly reduced EF may be missed, since gated SPECT overestimates EF in small ventricles.): In a small cavity with thick walls the endocardial borders are poorly resolved, and partial volume and limited frame resolution make the end systolic volume too small, which overestimates EF. Accepting 82 percent could hide mild dysfunction. Larger ventricles tend to give lower values, and the ratio does not cancel the error."}, {"id": 122, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A radiopharmacy delivers 30 mCi (1110 MBq) of N-13 ammonia calibrated for 09:00. The patient's IV fails and the stress setup is not ready until 09:20. The half-life of N-13 is 9.97 minutes. A colleague suggests injecting anyway. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Inject now, since 20 minutes is two half-lives and activity would still be about 15 mCi.", "B": "Inject the dose, since N-13 has a 110 minute half-life like F-18 and keeps 27 mCi.", "C": "Request a fresh dose, because activity has decayed to 7.5 mCi, too little for adequate counts.", "D": "Inject the 7.5 mCi and extend acquisition fourfold, since the reconstruction corrects for decay."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Request a fresh dose, because activity has decayed to 7.5 mCi, too little for adequate counts.): Twenty minutes is 2.006 half-lives, so the activity is 30 x 2^-2.006 = 7.5 mCi, about 25 percent of the intended dose. With a 10 minute half-life, activity continues to fall during any extended scan, so longer acquisition cannot fix the shortage. Two half-lives leave 7.5 mCi, not 15, and the 110 minute half-life belongs to F-18, so a replacement dose is requested."}, {"id": 123, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25 percent has a Tl-201 stress and 4 hour redistribution study showing a severe inferior defect with no change at 4 hours. Revascularization is being weighed. What explains the apparent fixed defect and what is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Poor extraction during stress caused the defect, so the stress injection is repeated tomorrow", "B": "Slow redistribution can understate viability, so reinject Tl-201 at rest and reimage for uptake", "C": "Diaphragmatic attenuation created the defect, so the study is repeated with the patient in prone position", "D": "The persistent defect proves transmural scar, so revascularization offers no benefit and further testing stops here"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slow redistribution can understate viability, so reinject Tl-201 at rest and reimage for uptake): Many segments that look fixed at 4 hours are hibernating and take up thallium after a resting reinjection or on 24 hour images, so reversal after reinjection identifies viable myocardium in up to a third or more of such segments. Declaring scar from the 4 hour images risks denying revascularization. Attenuation rarely produces a severe defect that is this focal, and repeating the stress adds nothing."}, {"id": 124, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man unable to exercise is scheduled for dobutamine stress SPECT for exertional dyspnea. The chart shows his resting blood pressures taken over 15 minutes in the stress room. He is asymptomatic and takes an ACE inhibitor. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Cancel all pharmacologic stress, since severe hypertension contraindicates vasodilator agents as well as dobutamine.", "B": "Give intravenous metoprolol first and then start dobutamine, since beta blockade makes hypertension safe.", "C": "Defer dobutamine and control blood pressure, or use vasodilator stress, since severe hypertension contraindicates it.", "D": "Start dobutamine and treat only if systolic pressure exceeds 220 mmHg during the infusion."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer dobutamine and control blood pressure, or use vasodilator stress, since severe hypertension contraindicates it.): Resting pressures near 195/110 mmHg meet the usual definition of severe hypertension, which is a contraindication to dobutamine because the infusion raises pressure further. Options are to treat and reschedule or to choose vasodilator stress, which does not raise blood pressure and is acceptable in hypertension. A beta blocker would also blunt the heart rate response the test depends on.", "image": "question_images/q_124.png"}, {"id": 125, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Two technologists draw the same number of Tc-99m syringes each day. One, right handed, wears her ring badge on the left hand with the chip facing outward. Her extremity reading is one quarter of her colleague's. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "The badge is on the less exposed hand and faces away, so it underestimates finger dose", "B": "Ring badges respond mainly to beta particles, so readings vary between workers", "C": "Her syringe shield covers the fingertips, so the ring cannot register any dose from the source", "D": "Her glove absorbs the 140 keV photons, so a ring badge under a glove reads low"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The badge is on the less exposed hand and faces away, so it underestimates finger dose): A ring badge should be worn on the dominant hand, usually the most exposed, with the chip facing the palm and the source. On the non-dominant hand and facing outward, it reads low and misses the true finger dose. Gloves stop beta particles, not 140 keV photons, and nothing in the scenario suggests a different technique."}, {"id": 126, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A diabetic patient undergoing F-18 FDG PET viability imaging received a glucose load, but the images show intense blood pool and skeletal muscle uptake with faint myocardial uptake. His blood glucose before injection was 310 mg/dL. What is the most likely cause, and what correction is best?", "options": {"A": "Excess FDG activity saturated myocyte transporters, fixed by halving the injected dose at the next study", "B": "Hyperglycemia and weak insulin effect, fixed by insulin titration to 100 to 140 mg/dL", "C": "Diabetic myocardium cannot take up FDG in any circumstance, so another agent is needed", "D": "A long fast shifted myocytes to fatty acids, corrected by an even longer fast"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hyperglycemia and weak insulin effect, fixed by insulin titration to 100 to 140 mg/dL): FDG competes with glucose for transport, and insulin drives glucose and FDG into myocytes. With glucose of 310 and poor insulin effect, uptake favors skeletal muscle and blood pool. A hyperinsulinemic or titrated insulin protocol restores uptake. A prolonged fast is for sarcoid imaging, and dose is not the limit."}, {"id": 127, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with chronic left bundle branch block needs stress SPECT. His age-predicted maximum heart rate is 156 beats per minute and he takes no rate-limiting drugs. Which plan is correct, and what would 85 percent of his predicted maximum be?", "options": {"A": "Vasodilator stress, since exercise causes false septal defects; 85 percent of 156 is about 133.", "B": "Use dobutamine to 133 beats per minute, since it matches exercise physiology in block.", "C": "Use treadmill exercise to 156 beats per minute, the full age-predicted maximum heart rate.", "D": "Use treadmill exercise to 133 beats per minute, since vasodilators are contraindicated in bundle branch block."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Vasodilator stress, since exercise causes false septal defects; 85 percent of 156 is about 133.): Rate-related septal perfusion defects appear with exercise or dobutamine in left bundle branch block, so vasodilator stress is preferred. The age-predicted maximum is 220 minus 64, or 156, and 85 percent equals 132.6, rounded to 133. Vasodilators are not contraindicated by bundle branch block, and dobutamine shares the tachycardia problem."}, {"id": 128, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man with exertional chest pressure walks 9 METs on the treadmill with no ST change. Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT is acquired after exercise and at rest, and gated EF is 58 percent on both. The stress and rest slices are displayed in the image. Which interpretation and recommendation fit best?", "options": {"A": "Soft tissue attenuation of the anterior wall; repeating the acquisition prone is the best next step.", "B": "Reversible anterior and anterolateral ischemia with preserved EF; symptom-guided medical therapy comes first.", "C": "Fixed anterior and anterolateral scar from prior infarction; FDG PET viability imaging is the next step.", "D": "Extensive left main equivalent ischemia; urgent revascularization referral is the best next step."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reversible anterior and anterolateral ischemia with preserved EF; symptom-guided medical therapy comes first.): The defect is present on the stress row and returns toward normal on the rest row in the basal and mid anterior and anterolateral segments, so it is reversible ischemia, with a summed stress score of 9, a summed rest score of 0 and a difference of 9, and the normal EF and negative ECG at a high workload point to a modest burden. Scar would persist at rest, attenuation would not change between stress and rest, and left main equivalent ischemia needs a much larger, balanced pattern with cavity dilation. Medical therapy first is consistent with stable ischemic heart disease guidance when burden is limited.", "image": "question_images/q_128.png"}, {"id": 129, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with COPD (FEV1 55 percent) and no baseline wheeze develops wheeze and dyspnea 90 seconds into an adenosine infusion at 140 mcg/kg/min. Oxygen saturation falls to 91 percent and the ECG shows sinus tachycardia. How should this finding be handled?", "options": {"A": "Give aminophylline immediately while the infusion runs, since the antagonist works best when given concurrently.", "B": "Stop the infusion and give aminophylline 50 to 250 mg intravenously if bronchospasm persists.", "C": "Continue to 6 minutes and give nebulized albuterol, since wheeze is an expected effect.", "D": "Switch to a regadenoson bolus right away, since an A2A-selective agonist cannot cause bronchospasm."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the infusion and give aminophylline 50 to 250 mg intravenously if bronchospasm persists.): Adenosine activates A1 receptors in the airway, and bronchospasm in a patient with airway disease is a reason to stop. Its half-life is under 10 seconds, so stopping the infusion often resolves symptoms, and aminophylline reverses persistent effects. Continuing risks worse bronchospasm, antagonist during infusion defeats the test, and regadenoson still carries a warning in airways disease."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A new SPECT camera is evaluated with the intrinsic Tc-99m flood in the exhibit. The vendor report states a useful field of view integral uniformity of 3.1 percent against a specification of 5 percent, and the physicist notes a thin ring of higher counts at the crystal border. Which interpretation and plan are best?", "options": {"A": "Photomultiplier overshoot from the outer tubes; request replacement of the outer ring of tubes immediately.", "B": "Collimator edge shadowing; reacquire with the collimator removed and then adopt the new image as baseline.", "C": "Edge packing; judge uniformity within the useful field of view and center the heart.", "D": "Window set too wide; narrow the energy window to 10 percent to flatten the border counts."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Edge packing; judge uniformity within the useful field of view and center the heart.): Counts are piled up in a ring at the crystal edge, an expected effect of position calculation near the border of the field. Specifications are measured over the useful field of view, which excludes this rim, and the measured 3.1 percent meets the 5 percent limit. Patients are positioned so the myocardium stays away from the rim. Tube replacement, collimator changes, and window narrowing do not address this effect.", "image": "question_images/q_130.png"}, {"id": 131, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 34-year-old woman breast-feeding her 4-month-old infant is referred for rest and stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT after an inconclusive treadmill test. She asks whether she must stop feeding. Applying current NRC guidance on breast-feeding interruption, which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Interruption is not required, but advise limiting close infant contact for several hours after each injection.", "B": "Interrupt feeding for 12 to 24 hours, the interval used for free Tc-99m pertechnetate.", "C": "Stop breast-feeding permanently, since any radioactive milk exposure carries unacceptable infant cancer risk.", "D": "Interrupt feeding and discard milk for two weeks, as is needed for long-lived radiopharmaceuticals."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interruption is not required, but advise limiting close infant contact for several hours after each injection.): NRC guidance on breast-feeding (Regulatory Guide 8.39) does not require interruption after Tc-99m sestamibi because the estimated infant dose stays well below 1 mSv, though sensible limits on close contact lower exposure from the mother's body. Longer interruptions are reserved for agents with higher milk excretion or long half-lives, and a permanent stop is never warranted by a diagnostic dose of this size."}, {"id": 132, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with BMI 43 kg/m2, left bundle branch block and exertional dyspnea cannot walk beyond 2 minutes on a treadmill. She has no bronchospasm or heart block. The laboratory offers Tc-99m SPECT without attenuation correction and Rb-82 PET with CT. Which protocol fits best?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson Tl-201 SPECT, since thallium photons penetrate soft tissue better than positron photons.", "B": "Treadmill Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT, since exercise gives prognostic data and bundle branch block spares the septum.", "C": "Dobutamine tetrofosmin SPECT, since dobutamine tachycardia does not produce septal defects in bundle branch block.", "D": "Regadenoson Rb-82 PET with CT correction, avoiding rate-related septal artifact and soft-tissue attenuation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson Rb-82 PET with CT correction, avoiding rate-related septal artifact and soft-tissue attenuation.): Left bundle branch block causes false septal defects when heart rate rises with exercise or dobutamine, and she cannot exercise adequately anyway, so vasodilator stress is preferred. Severe obesity degrades uncorrected SPECT through attenuation and scatter, and PET with CT correction is more specific in that setting. Thallium emits low-energy photons that attenuate more than the 511 keV photons used in PET."}, {"id": 133, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69 year old woman who could walk at a slow pace receives regadenoson while lying supine with no exercise, though low-level exercise was an option. Her blood pressure is normal at baseline. What is the likely consequence compared with adding low-level exercise?", "options": {"A": "Reduced sensitivity for left main disease, because exercise is required to activate the A2A receptor fully", "B": "Failure to reach adequate hyperemia, because regadenoson works only in patients with a high heart rate", "C": "Prolonged AV block lasting more than 30 minutes, because exercise normally shortens regadenoson action", "D": "More subdiaphragmatic tracer activity and more hypotension, because leg exercise would have redirected flow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (More subdiaphragmatic tracer activity and more hypotension, because leg exercise would have redirected flow): Adding low-level exercise to vasodilator stress raises heart rate and shifts blood flow toward working muscle, which lowers splanchnic and gut tracer activity, improves inferior wall image quality and reduces hypotension and side effects. Regadenoson itself works through the A2A receptor and does not require exercise or a high heart rate."}, {"id": 134, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man without symptoms has a coronary calcium score of 800 on screening. Exercise SPECT is normal with ejection fraction 60 percent and good functional capacity. His physician asks how to interpret the combination. What is the best statement?", "options": {"A": "Calcium of this size indicates left main disease, so angiography is required despite normal perfusion images", "B": "Low short-term risk from normal perfusion, yet high calcium still justifies intensive preventive therapy", "C": "A score of 800 means the scan is falsely normal, so repeat perfusion imaging with PET", "D": "Normal perfusion makes the calcium score irrelevant, so preventive therapy can be omitted for this patient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low short-term risk from normal perfusion, yet high calcium still justifies intensive preventive therapy): Perfusion imaging detects flow-limiting stenosis, and a normal result predicts a low near-term event rate. Calcium scoring measures plaque burden, which can be large without limiting flow. The two tests answer different questions, so preventive treatment follows the calcium result while angiography is not indicated by it.", "image": "question_images/q_134.png"}, {"id": 135, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A patient's record over 12 months shows a 3 mCi Tl-201 stress-redistribution study, a Rb-82 PET, a calcium score CT and a two day Tc-99m sestamibi study, totaling 29 mSv. Which single study contributed the largest share of her dose?", "options": {"A": "The Rb-82 PET study, since PET agents deliver a high dose per injection to the heart", "B": "The calcium score CT, since CT exceeds any nuclear study in effective dose", "C": "The Tl-201 study, since its long half-life gives the highest effective dose", "D": "The two day Tc-99m sestamibi study, since it uses two injections totaling 40 mCi"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The Tl-201 study, since its long half-life gives the highest effective dose): A 3 mCi Tl-201 study delivers roughly 15 to 20 mSv because of its long half-life and renal and myocardial retention. A two day Tc-99m protocol gives about 6 to 8 mSv, Rb-82 PET about 3 mSv, and a calcium score CT about 1 mSv. Activity alone is a poor guide to dose."}, {"id": 136, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory director explains to a referring cardiologist why a 29-year-old woman with low pretest probability and a technically adequate, normal stress-first Tc-99m study did not undergo rest imaging. Which single statement best expresses the ALARA principle behind that clinical decision?", "options": {"A": "Give the same activity to every patient so image quality remains standardized across studies", "B": "Use the least activity and fewest acquisitions that still answer the clinical question", "C": "Keep each patient's total exposure below the annual occupational dose limit of 50 mSv", "D": "Hold every patient dose under a fixed NRC per-study patient dose limit of 5 mSv"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the least activity and fewest acquisitions that still answer the clinical question): ALARA means keeping exposure as low as reasonably achievable while still obtaining a diagnostic answer, so skipping a rest study that cannot change management fits it. Occupational limits apply to workers, not patients. The NRC sets no per-study dose limit for patients, and a fixed activity ignores body habitus and the clinical question."}, {"id": 137, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient previously received dipyridamole for stress imaging and developed chest tightness that resolved after intravenous aminophylline. The fellow reviewing the chart asks the attending how dipyridamole produces coronary vasodilation and why aminophylline reverses its effect. Which mechanism is correct?", "options": {"A": "It blocks cellular reuptake and breakdown of adenosine, raising endogenous adenosine at coronary receptors.", "B": "It stimulates beta-1 receptors, raising heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand through inotropic stimulation.", "C": "It directly activates all four adenosine receptor subtypes and has a very short plasma half-life.", "D": "It directly stimulates A2A receptors as a selective agonist given as a fixed intravenous bolus."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It blocks cellular reuptake and breakdown of adenosine, raising endogenous adenosine at coronary receptors.): Dipyridamole is an indirect vasodilator that raises interstitial adenosine by inhibiting its uptake and deamination, and aminophylline reverses it by blocking adenosine receptors. Regadenoson is the selective A2A agonist, adenosine acts directly on all receptor subtypes, and dobutamine raises demand through beta-1 stimulation."}, {"id": 138, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old man on doxorubicin has serial gated blood pool studies. His baseline ejection fraction was 56 percent, and today it is 44 percent. List-mode data show frequent ventricular bigeminy with irregular beat lengths. What is the best step before reporting?", "options": {"A": "Remove the background correction, since background subtraction exaggerates the apparent drop in ejection fraction.", "B": "Average every beat including ectopic ones, since inclusion improves statistics and removes bias.", "C": "Reprocess with beat-length rejection to exclude ectopic and compensatory beats, verify the regions, then report.", "D": "Report 44 percent as confirmed cardiotoxicity and advise stopping chemotherapy, since the drop exceeds ten points."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reprocess with beat-length rejection to exclude ectopic and compensatory beats, verify the regions, then report.): Ectopic beats and the following long compensatory beats have different filling and volumes, so including them blurs the gated cycle and distorts the EF. Beat-length rejection windows or list-mode filtering and a check of the regions should come first. A treatment decision on an uncorrected drop is premature, and background correction should stay."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 74-year-old man with persistent atrial fibrillation and beat to beat variation has gated sestamibi SPECT. A narrow 20 percent beat acceptance window rejected about 45 percent of beats, and the software reports EF 31 percent although ungated images and visual wall motion look normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Keep the same window and use eight frames per cycle to reduce rejected beats.", "B": "Reacquire with a wider window or list mode, since rejected beats degraded counts.", "C": "Report the EF of 31 percent as measured, since the software output is the quantitative standard.", "D": "Discard gating and report an EF estimated visually from the ungated summed image."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reacquire with a wider window or list mode, since rejected beats degraded counts.): Irregular R to R intervals cause a narrow window to reject many beats, which lowers counts and distorts the time activity curve and the calculated volumes. Widening the window or using list mode recovers beats and gives a more reliable EF. Frame number does not change beat rejection, the displayed EF is not trustworthy, and an ungated visual estimate is not a quantitative substitute."}, {"id": 140, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory schedules a 31-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea for a dual-isotope protocol, with rest Tl-201 at 3 mCi followed by stress Tc-99m sestamibi at 25 mCi. What is the most likely consequence compared with a Tc-99m-only protocol on this camera?", "options": {"A": "Lower effective dose, because Tl-201 has a short half-life and clears quickly", "B": "Better inferior wall counts, because Tl-201 photons are lower in energy than Tc-99m photons", "C": "No effect on either image set, because the two isotopes use fully separate photopeaks", "D": "Higher radiation burden, plus Tl-201 photons spilling into the Tc-99m window"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Higher radiation burden, plus Tl-201 photons spilling into the Tc-99m window): Tl-201 has a physical half-life of about 73 hours and a larger effective dose than Tc-99m agents, so adding it raises the radiation burden, a concern in a young woman. Its 167 keV gamma and scatter also contaminate the Tc-99m window, which degrades quantitative comparison. Short half-life is not what Tl-201 has, lower energy worsens attenuation, and the energy windows are not independent."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 47-year-old woman with an implanted ICD and new sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia is suspected of having cardiac sarcoidosis. Cardiac MRI is considered but could be limited by device artifact. Which test is the best next choice for her workup?", "options": {"A": "FDG PET with rest perfusion after dietary preparation, because it is not limited by the ICD", "B": "Cardiac MRI with late gadolinium enhancement, because its tissue contrast is superior despite the device", "C": "Tc-99m pyrophosphate SPECT, because it identifies myocardial inflammatory tissue accurately", "D": "Regadenoson Rb-82 PET alone, because perfusion defects diagnose sarcoidosis reliably"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (FDG PET with rest perfusion after dietary preparation, because it is not limited by the ICD): FDG PET with a rest perfusion study shows active inflammation and scar patterns, and the images are not degraded by an ICD. MRI is a defensible test in general, but device artifact limits it here. Pyrophosphate targets amyloid, and perfusion defects alone are nonspecific for sarcoidosis."}, {"id": 142, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The chart shows monthly ring badge results for a PET technologist who prepares F-18 FDG syringes. The annual limit for the extremities is 50 rem (500 mSv). Doses are listed in millirem. What is the best action at the six month review?", "options": {"A": "Remove her from work now, since 4 rem a month exceeds the whole body limit", "B": "Take no action, since each monthly reading is far below the 50 rem annual extremity limit", "C": "Review her syringe handling now, since 6 months total 25 rem and project to the limit", "D": "Wait for the year end report, since ring doses act only above the lens limit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review her syringe handling now, since 6 months total 25 rem and project to the limit): The six readings sum to 24,900 mrem, about 24.9 rem, and continuing at this rate gives about 50 rem by year end, at the extremity limit. An ALARA program acts well before the limit: review technique, use tongs and shielded syringes, and rotate tasks. Monthly values must be summed against the annual limit, the whole body limit applies to another quantity, and the lens limit is not an action level for fingers.", "image": "question_images/q_142.png"}, {"id": 143, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with chronic heart failure and dyspnea on minimal exertion needs treadmill SPECT because she should not take a vasodilator and walks slowly with a cane. Her estimated functional capacity is about 2.5 METs. Which exercise protocol is best for her?", "options": {"A": "Bruce protocol with handrail support to reduce the effort needed at each stage", "B": "Standard Bruce protocol, because its 3 minute stages give the shortest and most reproducible test", "C": "Modified Bruce protocol, since its zero grade first stage best suits patients under 3 METs", "D": "Naughton protocol, whose small increments of about 1 MET allow a gradual workload rise"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Naughton protocol, whose small increments of about 1 MET allow a gradual workload rise): Standard Bruce starts at about 4.7 METs, far above this patient's capacity. Naughton starts near 1 MET and rises by about 1 MET per stage, so it lets a very low-capacity patient reach adequate stress. Modified Bruce is gentler than Bruce but its increments of about 1 to 2 METs are larger, and handrail support distorts the estimated workload."}, {"id": 144, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Tc-99m sestamibi images from a camera show reduced contrast and blurred myocardial borders. The quality control record shows the photopeak window centered at 128 keV with a 20 percent width, set after a software update, instead of the usual 140 keV. What best explains the degradation?", "options": {"A": "Photomultiplier gain is set too high, which narrows the pulse-height distribution and rejects scatter excessively.", "B": "Septal penetration from a low-energy collimator at 140 keV is causing the loss of contrast.", "C": "Crystal thickness limits intrinsic resolution, so the shifted window has no effect on contrast.", "D": "The shifted window spans about 115 to 141 keV, missing photopeak events and admitting extra scatter."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The shifted window spans about 115 to 141 keV, missing photopeak events and admitting extra scatter.): A 20 percent window centered at 128 keV is 25.6 keV wide and runs from about 115 to 141 keV. That discards much of the 140 keV photopeak and admits lower-energy Compton scatter, which lowers contrast and resolution. Crystal thickness is fixed, and a low-energy collimator is appropriate for Tc-99m."}, {"id": 145, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist drops a syringe in the dose room and 4 mCi of Tc-99m sestamibi spills on the floor near the door. Staff are notified, the area is cordoned off, and the spill is covered with absorbent paper and cleaned from the outside inward. Which step is most often omitted and most important to finish?", "options": {"A": "Wait ten half-lives and then release the room, since sixty hours of decay removes the activity", "B": "Survey the floor, shoes and hands, and take wipes to confirm no residual contamination", "C": "Wipe the area once with bleach to neutralize the radioactivity chemically before reopening it", "D": "Send the soiled absorbent paper to ordinary trash because Tc-99m has a short half-life"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Survey the floor, shoes and hands, and take wipes to confirm no residual contamination): Cleanup is not finished until a survey meter and wipe tests show the floor and the people involved are free of contamination, including shoes and hands where spread usually goes unnoticed. Decay alone leaves a busy doorway contaminated for days. Bleach does not alter radioactivity. Contaminated paper is radioactive waste and is held for decay and surveyed before disposal."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a SPECT acquisition, a patient slides 1.2 cm toward the feet at the midpoint of the scan, shown as an abrupt step on the sinogram. Software axial motion correction is applied. Which statement best describes the result and the strength or limitation of this algorithm?", "options": {"A": "It corrects a single rigid axial shift well but cannot repair non-rigid or lateral motion.", "B": "Axial correction also fixes continuous upward creep, which is the same artifact as a sudden step.", "C": "Correction restores full resolution for any displacement, so repeating the acquisition is rarely useful.", "D": "Software correction removes every motion type, so no review of the corrected images is required."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It corrects a single rigid axial shift well but cannot repair non-rigid or lateral motion.): Axial motion correction shifts projections back to a common position and handles abrupt rigid shifts well. It cannot fix lateral movement, rotation, respiratory or non-rigid motion, and a large or multiple shifts can leave residual blur that calls for repeating the acquisition. Review of the sinogram and corrected images is still needed."}, {"id": 147, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman has chest pain that resolved 6 hours ago, and the ED obtains rest sestamibi imaging after a nondiagnostic ECG and negative troponin. The injection was given after the pain had stopped. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "The study will show a fixed defect since uptake reflects flow at the time of imaging", "B": "A false positive result is likely because sestamibi concentrates in resting normal myocardium", "C": "A normal study may reassure falsely because defects can resolve after symptoms stop", "D": "Sestamibi will redistribute into the ischemic zone, so the study will still show it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A normal study may reassure falsely because defects can resolve after symptoms stop): Sestamibi uptake reflects flow at the time of injection and the tracer is retained afterward, so a pain-free injection may show normal perfusion even after a recent ischemic episode. The negative predictive value is highest when injection occurs during or near pain. Redistribution does not occur, and the likely error here is a false negative."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man without angina needs elective open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. He walks poorly because of claudication. Pharmacologic SPECT shows reversible anterior and lateral ischemia involving 20 percent of the left ventricle, with an LVEF of 40 percent. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Cancel the repair permanently because ischemia on SPECT is an absolute contraindication to surgery.", "B": "Proceed with optimized medical therapy and monitoring, since revascularization does not improve outcomes.", "C": "Stent the culprit vessels and delay surgery for twelve months to complete antiplatelet therapy.", "D": "Perform coronary bypass before the aneurysm repair, because large ischemia requires revascularization first."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Proceed with optimized medical therapy and monitoring, since revascularization does not improve outcomes.): Randomized data show that routine prophylactic revascularization before vascular surgery does not reduce death or infarction in stable patients, so coronary intervention is reserved for indications that exist regardless of the operation. This man has no angina, so medical therapy with careful perioperative management is reasonable. Ischemia raises risk but is not an absolute barrier, and stenting would impose a long antiplatelet delay."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A diabetic patient is being prepared for F-18 FDG PET viability imaging, and the table shows today's laboratory values from the morning. Which result creates the main pitfall for the myocardial study, and how should it be handled before injection?", "options": {"A": "Give a 75 g oral glucose load to boost insulin secretion before injecting FDG", "B": "Give heparin and a high-fat diet to suppress myocardial glucose use before FDG injection", "C": "Hyperglycemia competes with FDG and limits uptake, so lower glucose with intravenous insulin first", "D": "Proceed with injection because Rb-82 perfusion mismatch is unaffected by the blood glucose level"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hyperglycemia competes with FDG and limits uptake, so lower glucose with intravenous insulin first): FDG competes with glucose for uptake, and a glucose of 312 mg/dL with insulin resistance leaves little myocardial FDG uptake, so images would underestimate viability. Titrated intravenous insulin lowers glucose and promotes uptake. A glucose load worsens hyperglycemia, and suppressing glucose use is the opposite of what viability imaging needs.", "image": "question_images/q_149.png"}, {"id": 150, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with a left bundle branch block needs pharmacologic stress and cannot take regadenoson because of seizures treated with a medication list. She has severe aortic stenosis and a resting blood pressure of 100/60. A colleague suggests dobutamine instead. Which finding makes dobutamine a poor choice?", "options": {"A": "A seizure history, since dobutamine lowers the seizure threshold at standard infusion doses", "B": "Left bundle branch block, which prevents septal ischemia from being detected during dobutamine imaging", "C": "A resting systolic pressure of 100, which is too low for dobutamine to raise heart rate", "D": "Severe aortic stenosis with low pressure, since inotropic stress may provoke collapse"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Severe aortic stenosis with low pressure, since inotropic stress may provoke collapse): Dobutamine increases contractility and heart rate and is relatively contraindicated in severe outflow obstruction and hypotension. LBBB is better evaluated with vasodilator stress because of septal artifact, but that is a reason to avoid exercise or dobutamine rather than a block on detection. Seizures matter for regadenoson and aminophylline, not dobutamine."}, {"id": 151, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with BMI 38 has supine Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT showing lower inferior wall counts on both stress and rest. Gated motion is normal. The reader wants an acquisition change that best tests for diaphragmatic attenuation. Which change is most useful?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the acquisition prone, which usually moves inferior counts off the diaphragm", "B": "Switch to Tl-201 because its higher energy photons penetrate abdominal tissue better than Tc-99m", "C": "Widen the energy window to include more scattered photons from the abdomen and chest wall", "D": "Repeat stress with a higher dose of the stressor to increase inferior counts on the images"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the acquisition prone, which usually moves inferior counts off the diaphragm): Prone imaging lets the heart fall away from the diaphragm, and the inferior wall often normalizes if the cause was attenuation. Tl-201 has lower photon energy than Tc-99m and is more attenuated. A wider window adds scatter, and a stronger stressor changes flow, not photon absorption.", "image": "question_images/q_151.png"}, {"id": 152, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist reviews only the sinogram of a slice through the left ventricle and reports no motion. The raw cine, however, shows the heart rising about 3 pixels during the acquisition. What limitation of the sinogram explains this miss, and what is the appropriate practice?", "options": {"A": "A sinogram shows lateral shift but misses vertical shift, so review the cine.", "B": "A sinogram displays attenuation artifacts only, so it cannot be used to evaluate patient motion.", "C": "The linogram, not the sinogram, is the display required to detect lateral shift of the heart.", "D": "A sinogram shows lateral and vertical motion equally well, so cine review adds nothing."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A sinogram shows lateral shift but misses vertical shift, so review the cine.): A sinogram plots counts at one slice against angle, so lateral shift appears as a break in the sinusoidal trace. Vertical movement moves the heart out of the selected slice and may be missed. The cine, with a reference line, reveals it. The other options misstate what the sinogram and linogram show."}, {"id": 153, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist is planning a gated blood pool acquisition for ejection fraction in a patient with a prior anterior infarction, using in vitro red cell labeling and 24 frames per beat. Which projection best separates the left ventricle from the right ventricle and atria for the calculation?", "options": {"A": "A pure anterior projection, since it shows both ventricles without overlap from the atria.", "B": "A 30 degree right anterior oblique projection, since it puts the right ventricle behind the left.", "C": "The LAO projection at about 35 to 45 degrees, adjusted for the best septal separation.", "D": "A left lateral projection, since the left ventricle lies closest to the detector there."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The LAO projection at about 35 to 45 degrees, adjusted for the best septal separation.): The LAO view, adjusted until the septum is vertical and the ventricles are separate, is the standard acquisition for EF. Anterior and lateral projections overlap the right ventricle or atria with the left ventricle, and the right anterior oblique direction does not separate them. In vitro labeling gives high labeling efficiency and low background."}, {"id": 154, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Among 120 patients with at least 70 percent stenosis at angiography, perfusion imaging was positive in 96. Among 80 patients without significant stenosis, it was negative in 68. What are the sensitivity and the specificity of the imaging test in this group of patients?", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity 96 percent and specificity 68 percent, using the raw counts as percentages", "B": "Sensitivity 80 percent and specificity 15 percent, from 96 of 120 and 12 of 80", "C": "Sensitivity 85 percent and specificity 80 percent, from 68 of 80 and 96 of 120", "D": "Sensitivity 80 percent and specificity 85 percent, from 96 of 120 and 68 of 80"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sensitivity 80 percent and specificity 85 percent, from 96 of 120 and 68 of 80): Sensitivity is true positives over all diseased patients, 96 of 120 or 80 percent. Specificity is true negatives over all patients without disease, 68 of 80 or 85 percent. Swapping gives 85 and 80, and 15 percent is the false positive rate."}, {"id": 155, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Thirty seconds after regadenoson, tracer was injected in a patient with stable angina. Six minutes later she has chest pressure and 2.5 mm of horizontal ST depression, with blood pressure of 128/76 and no wheeze. Vasodilator-induced flow heterogeneity is suspected. What is the best action now?", "options": {"A": "Give metoprolol 5 mg IV to lower oxygen demand while the regadenoson effect continues", "B": "Start heparin and arrange emergent angiography for a presumed acute coronary syndrome in this patient", "C": "Observe without treatment, since vasodilator ST changes are nonspecific and usually resolve within a few minutes", "D": "Give aminophylline 75 to 125 mg IV slowly to reverse the A2A effect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give aminophylline 75 to 125 mg IV slowly to reverse the A2A effect): Regadenoson dilates normal arteries more than stenosed ones, so flow diverts from the territory supplied by a stenosis and can produce true ischemia with symptoms and ST depression. Tracer has been injected, so reversing the drug does not compromise the images. Aminophylline blocks the A2A receptor and is the first step. Treating as acute coronary syndrome or giving a beta blocker misses the cause."}, {"id": 156, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist's annual badge records show 380 mrem total effective dose, a 410 mrem lens estimate, and 4.2 rem on the ring badge. She asks whether she is near any limit. Which interpretation is correct against NRC annual occupational limits?", "options": {"A": "Her whole-body dose is 76 percent of the 0.5 rem limit, requiring a formal investigation", "B": "Her lens dose exceeds the 15 rem limit, so work restriction is required", "C": "All values are within limits, and whole-body and ring doses are each under 10 percent", "D": "Her ring dose exceeds the 5 rem whole-body limit, so the event must be reported"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (All values are within limits, and whole-body and ring doses are each under 10 percent): The annual limits are 5 rem (50 mSv) total effective dose, 15 rem to the lens and 50 rem to the extremities. 380 mrem is about 7.6 percent of 5 rem and 4.2 rem is about 8.4 percent of 50 rem, both under 10 percent, and the lens estimate is tiny against 15 rem. Applying the whole-body limit to the ring badge or the pregnancy limit to a general worker misuses the numbers."}, {"id": 157, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A referring cardiologist asks for F-18 FDG PET to diagnose transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis in a 77-year-old man with heart failure, thick ventricular walls and low-voltage ECG complexes, who has not yet had any serum protein testing. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Use Tc-99m sestamibi at rest, since amyloid shows a characteristic perfusion defect at one hour.", "B": "FDG does not target amyloid fibrils; use PYP, DPD or HMDP with SPECT and monoclonal screening.", "C": "Use I-123 MIBG, since a reduced heart-to-mediastinum ratio confirms the transthyretin type of amyloid.", "D": "Proceed with FDG, since intense myocardial uptake is specific for transthyretin deposits in the heart."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (FDG does not target amyloid fibrils; use PYP, DPD or HMDP with SPECT and monoclonal screening.): Bone-avid tracers such as Tc-99m pyrophosphate, DPD and HMDP bind transthyretin deposits and, with SPECT and exclusion of a monoclonal protein, permit noninvasive diagnosis. FDG tracks glucose metabolism and is not a validated amyloid test. Sestamibi shows perfusion, not a specific pattern, and MIBG denervation findings do not establish the amyloid type."}, {"id": 158, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The chart shows heart motion across the projections of a stress SPECT acquisition. The vertical centroid drifts upward steadily across projections, while the lateral centroid jumps abruptly by about 4 pixels near projection 21 and stays there. Which approach is best for this acquisition?", "options": {"A": "Reacquire for the vertical drift and use software for the lateral jump instead", "B": "Apply software to both motions, since the algorithm reliably corrects any rigid shift under five pixels", "C": "Report the data as is, since neither motion exceeds the clinical threshold of five pixels", "D": "Software corrects the gradual vertical drift, but the abrupt lateral jump needs reacquisition"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Software corrects the gradual vertical drift, but the abrupt lateral jump needs reacquisition): Motion correction handles slow vertical drift well, but abrupt lateral displacement changes the apparent heart position and is poorly corrected, so reacquisition is needed. The other choices overstate software performance, reverse the roles of the two motions, or rely on a made-up threshold.", "image": "question_images/q_158.png"}, {"id": 159, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman had a proximal left anterior descending stent four years ago and now has recurrent exertional angina. Regadenoson sestamibi SPECT shows a lateral defect at stress that normalizes at rest, with a summed stress score of 12 and a rest score of 0. Which interpretation of the likely culprit and next step fit best?", "options": {"A": "Right coronary artery disease, because the defect lies in the wall adjacent to the diaphragm.", "B": "Microvascular dysfunction, so ranolazine alone is the best next step without anatomic testing.", "C": "In-stent restenosis of the LAD, because recurrent symptoms must come from the previously stented vessel.", "D": "New circumflex disease rather than stent failure; angiography is reasonable given the ischemic extent."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (New circumflex disease rather than stent failure; angiography is reasonable given the ischemic extent.): The correct defect pattern is a lateral wall stress defect that fills in at rest, which is reversible ischemia in the circumflex territory with a difference score of 12, about 18 percent of the ventricle. The stented LAD supplies the anterior wall and septum, which look normal here, so stent failure does not explain the picture. The inferior wall is not involved, which argues against the right coronary artery. Ranolazine alone would skip testing a moderately large ischemic territory.", "image": "question_images/q_159.png"}, {"id": 160, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with known left bundle branch block had regadenoson SPECT combined with low-level treadmill walking. The exhibit shows a reversible septal defect with normal anterior and lateral walls. Coronary angiography is not planned. What is the best interpretation of the septal defect?", "options": {"A": "Breast attenuation artifact, because the defect sits in the septum on a woman's study.", "B": "Balanced three-vessel disease, because the other walls show normal uptake while only the septum looks abnormal.", "C": "A likely false positive from rate-related septal change in LBBB, so vasodilator alone is preferred.", "D": "Septal ischemia from a proximal LAD stenosis, because the defect reversed with stress."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A likely false positive from rate-related septal change in LBBB, so vasodilator alone is preferred.): In left bundle branch block, rising heart rate delays septal contraction and lowers septal tracer uptake, producing a false reversible septal defect. Exercise, even low-level, makes this worse, so vasodilator stress without exercise is preferred. Septal defects are not typical breast attenuation. Balanced disease causes diffuse reduced uptake rather than a single normal-looking set of walls.", "image": "question_images/q_160.png"}, {"id": 161, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Seconds after regadenoson injection, a 67-year-old man has severe chest pain with 3 mm inferior ST elevation. His blood pressure is 88/52 and heart rate is 52. Resuscitation equipment and the emergency drug cart are available in the room. Which is the best immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Give atropine 0.5 mg alone and observe the ST segments for a few minutes.", "B": "Give metoprolol 5 mg intravenously to reduce the heart rate and oxygen demand.", "C": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg first, because ischemic chest pain needs a nitrate.", "D": "Give aminophylline 125 mg slowly, monitor, and call for help if ST elevation persists."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give aminophylline 125 mg slowly, monitor, and call for help if ST elevation persists.): Vasodilator-induced ischemia with hypotension and ST elevation should be reversed with aminophylline, which antagonizes the adenosine receptor. Nitroglycerin lowers pressure further, and a beta blocker is dangerous with a pressure of 88 and a rate of 52. Atropine does not reverse the ischemic cause."}, {"id": 162, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with EF 28 percent and NYHA class III symptoms has I-123 MIBG imaging with a late heart to mediastinum ratio of 1.35 and washout of 42 percent. His physician asks whether this result changes ICD candidacy. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Higher risk is signaled, but ICD candidacy still follows guideline EF and symptom criteria.", "B": "Washout of 42 percent is normal, so only the late ratio matters for the risk estimate.", "C": "The MIBG result alone qualifies him for an ICD without regard to his EF and symptoms.", "D": "A ratio under 1.60 indicates low risk, so device therapy should be deferred pending repeat imaging."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Higher risk is signaled, but ICD candidacy still follows guideline EF and symptom criteria.): A late ratio of 1.35 and washout of 42 percent both show impaired sympathetic function and increased risk. However, guidelines base ICD candidacy on EF, symptoms and clinical context, and MIBG is not a stand alone indication. The other options reverse the meaning of the ratio or the washout, or overextend MIBG into a device qualification."}, {"id": 163, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A laboratory uses 30 mCi (1110 MBq) of Tc-99m for stress imaging with a 12 minute acquisition. To lower patient dose it will inject 15 mCi (555 MBq) and wants equivalent total myocardial counts. Ignoring decay and other factors, what acquisition time is required?", "options": {"A": "12 minutes, as image counts depend on injected activity alone rather than on time", "B": "24 minutes, since counts scale with dose times time, so half dose needs double time", "C": "18 minutes, because a 50 percent dose cut needs only half again as much time", "D": "6 minutes, since counts fall with dose so the time can shorten proportionally"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (24 minutes, since counts scale with dose times time, so half dose needs double time): Total counts are proportional to the product of administered activity and acquisition time. Halving the dose from 30 to 15 mCi therefore requires doubling the time from 12 to 24 minutes to hold counts constant. The other values come from inverting the ratio, adding an arbitrary increment, or leaving time unchanged."}, {"id": 164, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist is preparing a stress and rest Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT protocol on a conventional NaI gamma camera and must set the pulse height analyzer for the technetium acquisition. Which energy window setting is correct for this camera and tracer?", "options": {"A": "A 20 percent window centered on 159 keV, the main photon of iodine-123", "B": "A 20 percent window centered on the 140 keV photopeak", "C": "A 30 percent window centered on 70 keV, the mercury x-ray peak of thallium", "D": "A 20 percent window centered on 167 keV, the secondary thallium gamma peak"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 20 percent window centered on the 140 keV photopeak): Tc-99m emits a single 140 keV gamma ray, so the photopeak window is centered at 140 keV with a width of 15 to 20 percent. Thallium-201 is acquired at about 70 to 80 keV from mercury x-rays and, if used, 167 keV. Iodine-123 emits a 159 keV photon. Each wrong choice is a real window for a different tracer."}, {"id": 165, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 46-year-old man scheduled for a rest and stress Tc-99m sestamibi study asks whether radiation from the test might give him cancer, and he is considering declining. He has typical exertional angina. The referring physician did not discuss risk. What is the best action by the interpreting physician before injection?", "options": {"A": "Cancel the injection and ask the referring physician to obtain a signed consent form", "B": "Tell him nuclear doses are below natural background, so no risk discussion is needed before injection", "C": "Switch to Tl-201, because its lower effective dose removes his radiation concern", "D": "Explain the small radiation risk against the benefit, and document his informed decision"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the small radiation risk against the benefit, and document his informed decision): Shared decision making means explaining that the lifetime cancer risk from a typical study is small and that the benefit is large for a patient with typical angina. A frank discussion is respected even when brief. Tl-201 delivers a higher effective dose than Tc-99m agents, and the discussion cannot be delegated away."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A board member argues that because the linear no-threshold model assigns risk to every dose, a patient who received 12 mSv from Tc-99m MPI has a measurable cancer caused by that scan. Which statement best describes the limitation of applying the model this way?", "options": {"A": "The model describes only deterministic effects such as skin injury, so it cannot address cancer risk.", "B": "It estimates population risk for protection planning, not causation in one person's cancer.", "C": "The model shows no risk below 100 mSv, so 12 mSv carries no cancer probability.", "D": "The model is valid only for doses above the 50 mSv annual occupational dose limit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It estimates population risk for protection planning, not causation in one person's cancer.): The linear no-threshold model assumes cancer risk rises in proportion to dose with no safe threshold, and regulators use it to set limits and apply ALARA. It was never meant to prove that a given cancer in a given person was caused by a given low dose, because background cancer rates are high and effects at 12 mSv cannot be measured individually. It addresses stochastic, not deterministic, effects."}, {"id": 167, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old diabetic man with ejection fraction 24 percent, BMI 38 kg/m2 and multivessel disease is being evaluated for revascularization, and the surgeon wants to know whether the dysfunctional anterior wall is hibernating. A PET camera is available. Which viability protocol is best?", "options": {"A": "FDG PET alone without perfusion, since any FDG uptake proves viable myocardium that will recover surgically.", "B": "Dobutamine Tc-99m SPECT, since contractile reserve is measured directly and avoids insulin protocols in obesity.", "C": "Rest-redistribution Tl-201 SPECT, since it is the only protocol validated in diabetic patients with heart failure.", "D": "Rb-82 perfusion plus FDG PET with glucose-insulin loading, since mismatch identifies hibernating myocardium."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rb-82 perfusion plus FDG PET with glucose-insulin loading, since mismatch identifies hibernating myocardium.): Hibernating myocardium shows reduced perfusion with preserved glucose uptake, and the perfusion-metabolism mismatch is the core finding. Combining perfusion with FDG is best done with PET, which also suits large body size. FDG alone cannot tell mismatch from match, thallium protocols are one of several valid options, and diabetes is managed with insulin or a clamp rather than avoiding PET."}, {"id": 168, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A nuclear cardiology laboratory hires an experienced cardiologist who completed imaging fellowship but is not named on the facility's NRC or Agreement State license. The laboratory wants her to order and direct Tc-99m MPI doses starting Monday. Under 10 CFR Part 35, what is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Allow her to direct studies while a named technologist signs each written directive for her", "B": "Let the radiation safety officer supervise her doses, since the officer holds authority over all uses", "C": "Have her added as an authorized user through documented training and experience first", "D": "Allow her to order doses now because cardiology board certification meets the license requirement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Have her added as an authorized user through documented training and experience first): Only a physician who meets the training and experience requirements and is named as an authorized user may direct the use of byproduct material for imaging. The RSO oversees the radiation safety program but cannot stand in as the user physician. General board certification is not license status. A technologist works under the supervision of an authorized user and cannot replace one."}, {"id": 169, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Rb-82 PET/CT shows a reversible anterolateral defect, but the gated wall motion is normal. On fusion review, the lateral edge of the heart on the emission images sits inside the lung field of the CT attenuation map. Which step best addresses this finding before the study is interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the CT with a lower tube current to reduce the misregistration at the lateral wall", "B": "Re-reconstruct the study with a smoother filter to blend the lateral wall edge into lung", "C": "Review the fused emission and CT images and manually realign them before reconstruction", "D": "Read the study as is, since PET attenuation correction tolerates a few centimetres of shift"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the fused emission and CT images and manually realign them before reconstruction): Respiratory motion and breath level mismatch between CT and PET place the lateral wall over lung on the attenuation map, which causes falsely low corrected counts. The remedy is to check fusion and manually realign the data sets. Lower CT current does not change geometry, smoothing does not repair registration, and uncorrected misalignment produces artifactual defects."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Morning quality control on a Rb-82 generator shows 0.025 microcuries of Sr-82 per millicurie of Rb-82 (0.025 kBq per MBq) in the eluate, with Sr-85 within limits. The first patient is waiting for a regadenoson PET study. The Sr-82 expiration limit is 0.02 microcuries per millicurie. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Remove the generator from use, since Sr-82 exceeds expiration limits, and notify radiation safety.", "B": "Wait four hours for the Sr-82 to decay and elute again, since its half-life is short.", "C": "Proceed with the scan and retest after each patient, since only the alert limit prohibits use.", "D": "Proceed but halve the injected Rb-82 activity so the patient receives half the Sr-82 breakthrough."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the generator from use, since Sr-82 exceeds expiration limits, and notify radiation safety.): Sr-82 and Sr-85 breakthrough are tested daily, with an alert limit of 0.002 and 0.02 microcuries per millicurie, and expiration limits of 0.02 for Sr-82 and 0.2 for Sr-85. A value above the expiration limit means the generator must be taken out of service. Sr-82 has a 25 day half-life, so waiting hours changes nothing, and reducing the injected activity does not remove the bone and marrow dose hazard of the breakthrough."}, {"id": 171, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Annual dosimetry for a physician who performs nuclear cardiology studies shows a deep dose of 22 mSv, a lens dose of 160 mSv, and a ring badge dose of 410 mSv. Which NRC annual occupational limit, if any, was exceeded?", "options": {"A": "The lens limit of 150 mSv was exceeded; the whole body and extremity limits were not", "B": "No limit was exceeded, since the combined doses stay below 500 mSv", "C": "The extremity limit was exceeded, since 410 mSv is above the 150 mSv skin dose limit", "D": "The whole body limit of 20 mSv was exceeded, so the NRC must be notified"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The lens limit of 150 mSv was exceeded; the whole body and extremity limits were not): NRC annual limits are 50 mSv for whole body, 150 mSv for the lens, and 500 mSv for the skin and extremities. The lens result of 160 mSv is over its limit; 22 mSv and 410 mSv are under theirs. Each limit is judged separately and the doses are not summed."}, {"id": 172, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A cardiology lab uses a camera with a 3/8 inch NaI(Tl) crystal. A vendor says a 1/2 inch crystal would raise sensitivity for 140 keV Tc-99m photons. The lab director must decide what to weigh first before agreeing to the purchase. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Confirm fewer photomultiplier tubes will be needed, since a thicker crystal gathers light over larger areas.", "B": "Treat the gain as Tl-201 only, since 140 keV photons are already fully absorbed.", "C": "Assume resolution improves too, because extra photon interactions give more light and tighter event localization.", "D": "Weigh the sensitivity gain against poorer intrinsic resolution, since light spreads in thicker crystals."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weigh the sensitivity gain against poorer intrinsic resolution, since light spreads in thicker crystals.): A thicker NaI(Tl) crystal stops more photons, so sensitivity rises, but scintillation light disperses before reaching the tubes, which blurs the intrinsic resolution. A 3/8 inch crystal stops a high fraction of 140 keV photons but not all of them. Tube count depends on field size and tube diameter. The decision is therefore a trade between sensitivity and resolution."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A hospital with no cyclotron or generator on site plans to offer F-18 flurpiridaz PET perfusion imaging. The cardiology director asks about practical features of the agent. Practical details matter for scheduling and staffing. Which statement correctly describes its properties and role in this plan?", "options": {"A": "It requires an on-site generator eluted each morning, like rubidium-82, so none is practical.", "B": "Its 110-minute half-life allows regional cyclotron delivery, but doses must be scheduled around arrival times.", "C": "It is a Tc-99m labeled agent, so any existing gamma camera can acquire the images.", "D": "Its long positron range lowers spatial resolution compared with rubidium-82 images and blurs small defects."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Its 110-minute half-life allows regional cyclotron delivery, but doses must be scheduled around arrival times.): F-18 has a half-life of about 110 minutes, so it can be shipped from a cyclotron facility, yet the site needs a PET scanner and must fit studies into the delivery schedule. Its short positron range gives better resolution than rubidium-82. It is not generator produced and cannot be imaged on a gamma camera."}, {"id": 174, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A woman with large breasts has sestamibi SPECT. Stress images were acquired with the breasts positioned laterally and rest images with them uncontrolled. An anterior defect appears at stress and normalizes at rest. Wall thickening is normal. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Report reversible LAD ischemia, since reversibility is defined by a defect that improves at rest.", "B": "Suspect artifactual reversibility from breast positioning; review raw cine and rescan matched.", "C": "Apply attenuation correction only to the stress images to match the rest scan.", "D": "Smooth the stress images so the anterior wall matches the rest images in count."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspect artifactual reversibility from breast positioning; review raw cine and rescan matched.): Reversibility is only valid when stress and rest are acquired in comparable conditions. Different breast position can move attenuation between scans and mimic ischemia, and normal thickening supports artifact. Raw cine review and matched repeat positioning resolves it. Correcting only one scan or smoothing manipulates the data."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A Tc-99m sestamibi kit is prepared and tested by thin layer chromatography, giving a radiochemical purity of 86 percent. A second technologist repeats the strip and also reads 86 percent. The release limit is 90 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Use the vial for the rest injection only, since rest imaging tolerates lower purity", "B": "Heat the vial a second time and inject without retesting, since heating improves labeling", "C": "Inject a dose 10 percent larger to offset the 14 percent impurity", "D": "Reject the vial, prepare a new kit, and log the failed test"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reject the vial, prepare a new kit, and log the failed test): Two independent strips agree, so a technique error is unlikely and the preparation fails the 90 percent release limit. Impurities such as free pertechnetate and reduced hydrolyzed technetium raise background and give patients dose without useful signal, and extra activity does not fix that. The limit applies to every injection. Reheating may be tried only with retesting, and the failure should be documented."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with EF 25 percent and anterior akinesis has sestamibi SPECT showing resting anterior counts at 42 percent of peak. FDG PET with a clamp shows 70 percent uptake in the same area. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Stunned myocardium, which would not benefit from any revascularization strategy.", "B": "Mismatch showing hibernating viable myocardium; low sestamibi uptake understated viability.", "C": "A match pattern showing scar, since sestamibi uptake below 50 percent excludes viability.", "D": "Mismatch showing ischemia, so a repeat vasodilator stress is the next test."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mismatch showing hibernating viable myocardium; low sestamibi uptake understated viability.): Hibernating myocardium takes up FDG while perfusion is reduced, giving a perfusion metabolism mismatch. Sestamibi below about 50 percent of peak is a trap because FDG, being more sensitive, shows viability. A match would mean scar, and the mismatch here refers to metabolism, not stress ischemia."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A nursing mother had a Tc-99m sestamibi study and pumped milk right afterward, storing it in a labeled bottle for the lab to review. Tc-99m has a half-life of 6.0 hours. What fraction of the activity remains in the stored milk after 24 hours?", "options": {"A": "About 12 percent, from three half-lives of decay", "B": "About 6 percent, from four half-lives of decay", "C": "About 25 percent, from two half-lives of decay", "D": "About 50 percent, from a single half-life of decay"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 6 percent, from four half-lives of decay): Twenty four hours divided by 6 hours is four half-lives, and one half to the fourth power is 1/16, or 6.25 percent. Two half-lives leave 25 percent, one leaves 50 percent, and three leave 12.5 percent. Each wrong value comes from counting too few half-lives."}, {"id": 178, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During the morning quality control check before a cardiac SPECT list, a dual-head camera's center-of-rotation test shows an offset beyond the manufacturer's tolerance. The uniformity flood from the previous day was acceptable. What is the appropriate action before any myocardial perfusion patients are imaged on this camera?", "options": {"A": "Remove the camera from clinical use and recalibrate the center of rotation first.", "B": "Accept the camera because yesterday's flood uniformity was acceptable and covers rotational alignment.", "C": "Image patients with a 360-degree orbit so opposing views cancel the center-of-rotation error.", "D": "Image the list and shift the reconstruction axis afterward during processing to correct the offset."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the camera from clinical use and recalibrate the center of rotation first.): A center-of-rotation error means the camera's mechanical axis and the reconstruction axis disagree, which smears point-like features in every tomographic slice and can create false defects. It must be corrected by calibration before patients are scanned. A larger orbit does not cancel the error, retrospective shifts cannot restore lost resolution, and a normal flood tests detector uniformity, not rotational geometry."}, {"id": 179, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A cardiologist asks for the standard acquisition on a dual-head 90-degree camera for a Tc-99m sestamibi study in a patient of average build. The goal is the best counts and the least attenuation from the spine and posterior chest. Which orbit should the technologist choose?", "options": {"A": "A 180-degree arc centered on posterior projections, since the left ventricle lies close to the back.", "B": "A 180-degree arc from 45 degrees RAO to 45 degrees LPO, each head covering 90 degrees.", "C": "A 180-degree arc from 45 degrees LAO through posterior to 45 degrees RPO, including the spine.", "D": "A 360-degree orbit with each head covering 180 degrees, since full sampling improves contrast."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 180-degree arc from 45 degrees RAO to 45 degrees LPO, each head covering 90 degrees.): The 180-degree orbit from RAO through anterior to LPO samples the projections closest to the heart and avoids the posterior views, which have more attenuation and poorer resolution. Using two 90-degree heads in an L-mode configuration covers the arc in half the time. A 360-degree orbit or a posterior arc adds counts from distant, attenuated views."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 63-year-old man takes metoprolol 100 mg the morning of a treadmill sestamibi study. At stage 3 he reaches a heart rate of 104 against a target of 140 and has no symptoms or ST change. The ECG is nondiagnostic. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Convert to regadenoson stress, since beta blockade prevents reaching the target rate.", "B": "Cancel and reschedule after the beta blocker is held for 48 hours.", "C": "Give atropine 1 mg intravenously to raise the heart rate to the target.", "D": "Inject the tracer at the current heart rate and report the stress as adequate."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Convert to regadenoson stress, since beta blockade prevents reaching the target rate.): Beta blockers blunt heart rate response, so a patient cannot reach 85 percent of maximum predicted heart rate and the study is nondiagnostic if the tracer is injected submaximally. A vasodilator can be added since it does not depend on heart rate. Holding the drug for 48 hours is also possible, but the patient is already present."}, {"id": 181, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57 year old man arrives for a regadenoson study. He states his last coffee was 14 hours ago. He denies tea, chocolate or energy drinks since. He has a normal ECG and is not wheezing. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Give aminophylline before the study, since it prevents residual caffeine from affecting the study", "B": "Switch to dobutamine, since any caffeine exposure within 24 hours contraindicates vasodilators", "C": "Reschedule another 12 hours, since methylxanthines remain active for a full day", "D": "Proceed with the regadenoson study, since the usual 12 hour caffeine abstinence has been met"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed with the regadenoson study, since the usual 12 hour caffeine abstinence has been met): Guidelines call for withholding caffeine and other methylxanthines for at least 12 hours before vasodilator stress. Fourteen hours satisfies that, and the history excludes other methylxanthine sources. Delaying further, switching agents or giving aminophylline would be unnecessary, and aminophylline would itself block the vasodilator."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man, 80 kg, with serum creatinine 3.0 mg/dL needs ischemia evaluation before kidney transplant listing. Knee osteoarthritis prevents treadmill walking, and he has no known contrast reaction. Using Cockcroft-Gault, which choice gives the correct clearance and the best test?", "options": {"A": "Exercise SPECT; clearance is about 22 mL/min after the female correction, so contrast is avoided.", "B": "Regadenoson SPECT; clearance is about 26 mL/min, so iodinated contrast is best avoided.", "C": "Coronary CT angiography; clearance is about 78 mL/min, so iodinated contrast carries little kidney risk.", "D": "Regadenoson SPECT at half dose; clearance is about 26 mL/min and requires a renally reduced dose."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Regadenoson SPECT; clearance is about 26 mL/min, so iodinated contrast is best avoided.): Cockcroft-Gault: (140 - 70) x 80 / (72 x 3.0) = 5,600 / 216 = about 26 mL/min. With severe impairment, iodinated contrast adds risk, and he cannot walk on a treadmill, so a pharmacologic perfusion study is appropriate. Regadenoson needs no renal dose adjustment."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man with prior inferior infarction has a fixed inferior defect on sestamibi stress and rest, with akinesis. His glucose is 290 mg/dL and no insulin clamp is available. Revascularization is being considered. What is the best way to assess viability?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the stress study next day with a larger regadenoson dose to provoke more reversibility.", "B": "Label the fixed defect scar and proceed with medical therapy without further testing.", "C": "Repeat rest sestamibi after sublingual nitroglycerin, since a glucose of 290 degrades FDG.", "D": "Order FDG PET viability today, as it is the reference standard for hibernating myocardium."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat rest sestamibi after sublingual nitroglycerin, since a glucose of 290 degrades FDG.): A fixed sestamibi defect can still hold viable myocardium, and nitrate augmented rest imaging improves detection. FDG is often preferred but needs controlled glucose and insulin, which are unavailable here. Fixed does not mean scar. A larger vasodilator dose addresses ischemia, not rest viability."}, {"id": 184, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old diabetic woman has normal relative perfusion on Rb-82 PET. The chart shows rest and stress myocardial blood flow by coronary territory, with rest flow elevated. Her resting rate pressure product was 14,500, much higher than her usual baseline. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "The study is normal, because stress flow exceeds 2.0 mL/min/g in every territory.", "B": "A focal LAD stenosis is present, since the LAD shows the lowest flow reserve.", "C": "Diffusely reduced reserve proves balanced multivessel obstruction, so angiography should be recommended next.", "D": "Reserve is diffusely low with high rest flow; correct for rate pressure product first."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reserve is diffusely low with high rest flow; correct for rate pressure product first.): Flow reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow, about 1.5 in each territory here. A high resting rate pressure product raises rest flow and lowers reserve even when stress flow is adequate. Correcting rest flow for rate pressure product is part of the proper interpretation. Differences of 0.02 between territories do not indicate a focal lesion.", "image": "question_images/q_184.png"}, {"id": 185, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman has a normal stress PET and normal myocardial flow reserve. The referring physician asks the laboratory to add a coronary calcium CT to every study as standard practice. What is the best laboratory response to that request?", "options": {"A": "Add it only above age 70, because younger patients cannot have coronary calcium", "B": "Add it to every study, because the NRC requires it with PET perfusion imaging", "C": "Add it to every study, because calcium CT carries no measurable additional radiation", "D": "Add it only when the result would change management, and document the indication"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add it only when the result would change management, and document the indication): A calcium scan adds about 1 mSv, which is small but not zero, and it should be performed when the result could alter preventive therapy or interpretation. Documenting the indication supports ALARA. No regulation mandates it, age is not a rule for calcium, and the dose is not negligible when added to every study."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 44-year-old nonsmoker with atypical chest pain exercises on a beta blocker and stops at 78 percent of predicted heart rate because of leg fatigue. There are no ischemic ST changes. Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT gives an ejection fraction of 58 percent, and the summed stress and rest scores are both 0. What is the best interpretation and plan?", "options": {"A": "Normal perfusion, but submaximal stress raises risk to intermediate, so stress echocardiography should follow.", "B": "Normal study, and with low pretest probability a submaximal heart rate needs no repeat testing.", "C": "Normal perfusion, but a beta blocker masks defects, so coronary CT angiography is required.", "D": "Nondiagnostic study because heart rate was under 85 percent of predicted, so repeat now with regadenoson."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal study, and with low pretest probability a submaximal heart rate needs no repeat testing.): The polar maps show normal perfusion at stress and rest. A submaximal exercise test lowers sensitivity, but in a patient with a low pretest probability and a normal image a repeat study adds little. Beta blockers can limit heart rate, yet they do not hide flow-limiting disease to the degree that CT angiography becomes mandatory. Follow-up stress echocardiography would add cost without changing management.", "image": "question_images/q_186.png"}, {"id": 187, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old diabetic woman with known three vessel disease on prior angiography has symptoms. Rb-82 PET shows uniform relative perfusion, TID 1.3, an EF fall from 61 to 47 percent with stress and 2 mm ST depression. What is the consequence of calling the study normal?", "options": {"A": "Multivessel disease would be missed, since relative uptake looks normal if all territories fall.", "B": "Risk would be overestimated, because normal relative counts exclude ischemia in every territory.", "C": "Only mild disease would be missed, since TID and an EF fall are nonspecific findings.", "D": "Nothing important, since the EF fall is a gating artifact and the low risk reading stands."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multivessel disease would be missed, since relative uptake looks normal if all territories fall.): Relative perfusion compares each segment with the best segment, so equal reduction in all territories can look normal. Supporting findings such as TID, an EF fall and ST depression expose it. Calling it normal would therefore miss severe disease. Reading the EF fall as artifact discards a high risk sign."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist holds each syringe barrel unshielded for 20 seconds while dispensing. The fingertip dose rate is 150 mSv/h, she handles 10 syringes daily and works 250 days a year. If nothing changes, when does she reach the 500 mSv annual extremity limit?", "options": {"A": "After about 600 working days, because only one syringe per day counts toward finger exposure", "B": "After about 60 working days, because she accumulates roughly 8.3 mSv to her fingers each day", "C": "After about 120 working days, because the hand dose is half the fingertip dose rate", "D": "After about 6 working days, because the limit applied is 50 mSv"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (After about 60 working days, because she accumulates roughly 8.3 mSv to her fingers each day): Daily exposure time is 10 times 20 s, or 200 s, which is 0.0556 h. At 150 mSv/h that gives 8.3 mSv per day. Dividing 500 mSv by 8.3 gives about 60 days, far short of a year. Using 50 mSv, one syringe, or half the dose rate each produces a wrong date."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman wearing a size DD bra has atypical chest pain. Stress and rest SPECT images show slightly lower anterior counts on both rows, with summed scores of 0 and 0 against the normal database. No CT attenuation map is available. Which next step best resolves whether this is artifact or disease?", "options": {"A": "Order coronary angiography, since a fixed anterior defect indicates left anterior descending occlusion in this woman", "B": "Perform a 24 hour delayed rest image to assess anterior wall tracer redistribution", "C": "Review the gated wall motion and thickening and the raw projection images for a breast shadow", "D": "Repeat the same supine acquisition with doubled injected activity to raise anterior counts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the gated wall motion and thickening and the raw projection images for a breast shadow): Attenuation lowers counts without affecting contraction, so normal gated thickening with a breast shadow on the projection images identifies it without added radiation, and the scores of 0 already fit a normal perfusion study. More tracer does not change photon absorption. Delayed imaging addresses thallium redistribution, not attenuation, and angiography is premature.", "image": "question_images/q_189.png"}, {"id": 190, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A daily uniformity flood after a suspected photomultiplier tube dropout shows a maximum pixel count of 1,100 and a minimum of 700 in the useful field of view. Service has not been called yet. What is the integral uniformity, and what does it indicate?", "options": {"A": "About 36 percent, calculated by dividing the difference by the maximum count.", "B": "About 22 percent, indicating a significant dropout that needs service.", "C": "About 11 percent, calculated as half of the maximum-minimum difference over the maximum.", "D": "About 57 percent, calculated by dividing the difference by the minimum count."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 22 percent, indicating a significant dropout that needs service.): Integral uniformity is (max minus min) divided by (max plus min), or 400 divided by 1,800, about 22 percent. That is far above the few percent expected on a working camera, which points to the dropped tube. Dividing by max or min gives wrong values."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and a resting outflow gradient of 70 mm Hg has typical exertional angina. Echocardiography shows septal thickness of 22 mm. Regadenoson SPECT shows reversible septal and apical defects, and he asks if an artery is blocked. Which step is best next?", "options": {"A": "Add a long-acting nitrate for the reversible defects and reassess his symptoms in one month.", "B": "Repeat SPECT with thallium-201 to confirm that the defects are truly ischemic rather than artifactual.", "C": "Plan revascularization of the territories matching the defects without any further anatomic testing of the arteries.", "D": "Coronary CT or invasive angiography, since hypertrophy can cause perfusion defects with unobstructed epicardial arteries."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coronary CT or invasive angiography, since hypertrophy can cause perfusion defects with unobstructed epicardial arteries.): Hypertrophy, small vessel disease and outflow obstruction produce reversible defects without epicardial stenosis, so perfusion findings cannot decide the question. Anatomic imaging does. Nitrates and vasodilators can increase the outflow gradient, and another perfusion study repeats the same limitation."}, {"id": 192, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man with a known 60 percent proximal LAD stenosis and no symptoms has a regadenoson SPECT study. The anterior wall shows a reversible defect though he has no chest pain or ST change. Regadenoson activates A2A receptors. Which mechanism best explains the defect?", "options": {"A": "Normal arteries dilate several fold but the stenosed bed cannot, so relative uptake falls there.", "B": "Tracer extraction rises in the stenosed bed, lowering relative blood pool counts in that wall.", "C": "Regadenoson constricts the stenosed segment through A1 receptors, reducing its tracer delivery.", "D": "Heart rate rises sharply, which shortens diastole and reduces flow to the stenosed bed."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Normal arteries dilate several fold but the stenosed bed cannot, so relative uptake falls there.): Vasodilator stress produces heterogeneity: flow in normal territories can rise three to four times resting values, whereas a stenosis limits increase, so a relative perfusion difference appears without symptoms or ECG change. A1 stimulation is not the mechanism of the defect, and vasodilators raise heart rate only modestly."}, {"id": 193, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A sinogram from a stress SPECT study shows the band shifting sideways across successive projection angles beyond the expected sinusoidal path. A resident asks what this indicates about the patient. Which answer is correct? The acquisition lasted fifteen minutes with the arms raised overhead.", "options": {"A": "The detector orbit was elliptical, so the camera moved away from the chest", "B": "The patient drifted laterally during acquisition, moving the heart off its position", "C": "The patient coughed once, producing an abrupt single displacement of the heart position", "D": "The patient breathed deeply, so the diaphragm lifted the heart upward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The patient drifted laterally during acquisition, moving the heart off its position): A source that stays in place traces a fixed sinusoid on the sinogram. A steady sideways departure from that path means the heart moved laterally relative to the camera during the acquisition, usually from the patient slipping. An abrupt step would indicate a cough and a progressive upward climb would indicate creep from breathing and diaphragm recovery.", "image": "question_images/q_193.png"}, {"id": 194, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Filtered back projection SPECT in a 60-year-old man shows a dark band of reduced counts in the inferior wall next to intense subdiaphragmatic bowel activity. The raw projection cine shows normal-looking inferior wall counts and no motion. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Vertical patient motion, which causes a band-like artifact across the entire study in the reconstruction.", "B": "The ramp filter creates negative lobes near very intense activity, lowering adjacent inferior wall counts.", "C": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, which would also appear as reduced inferior counts in the raw projection cine.", "D": "A true inferior infarct that the raw projection cine happens to fail to show on review."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The ramp filter creates negative lobes near very intense activity, lowering adjacent inferior wall counts.): The ramp filter in filtered back projection overshoots near very bright sources and creates negative values around them, so counts next to a hot organ fall. Because the raw projections look normal, the cause is a reconstruction artifact. Attenuation or infarct would show in the cine, and motion would show a shift across all walls."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A laboratory with a dual-detector camera, detectors set at 90 degrees, is writing its standard protocol for Tc-99m SPECT myocardial perfusion imaging in supine adults. Which acquisition arc is standard for these studies, and what is the reason for choosing it over the alternatives?", "options": {"A": "180 degrees from 45 degrees right anterior oblique to left posterior oblique, avoiding attenuation", "B": "360 degrees, because full sampling doubles counts and reduces apical noise", "C": "180 degrees from 45 degrees left anterior to right posterior oblique, matching cardiac orientation", "D": "180 degrees from 90 degrees right anterior to left posterior oblique, sampling the lateral wall"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (180 degrees from 45 degrees right anterior oblique to left posterior oblique, avoiding attenuation): Cardiac SPECT is usually acquired over 180 degrees from 45 degrees right anterior oblique to 45 degrees left posterior oblique, because the heart lies closest to the camera in the anterior left side and the posterior views add attenuation and scatter. A reversed arc misses the closest views, and a full 360 degrees includes poor posterior projections."}, {"id": 196, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 41 and exertional dyspnea has regadenoson SPECT with Tc-99m sestamibi. Ejection fraction is 58 percent and the diaphragm sits high on the planar images. The inferior wall counts look mildly reduced and matched at stress and rest, with summed scores of 0. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Liver activity overlap, so repeat after a fatty meal to clear the bowel and liver.", "B": "Likely diaphragmatic attenuation artifact of the inferior wall, to check with prone images.", "C": "Reversible inferior ischemia, so refer for right coronary angiography.", "D": "Fixed inferior scar from silent right coronary infarction, so start secondary prevention."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Likely diaphragmatic attenuation artifact of the inferior wall, to check with prone images.): In an obese man with a high diaphragm, a matched inferior count reduction at stress and rest is the classic picture of diaphragmatic attenuation. Prone imaging moves the diaphragm away from the inferior wall, and normal thickening on gated images supports artifact. Scar would not thicken, and ischemia would be worse at stress than at rest. A fatty meal does not clear a matched artifact.", "image": "question_images/q_196.png"}, {"id": 197, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a body mass index of 34 kg/m2, large breasts, no known CAD and atypical exertional chest discomfort has the SPECT study summarized in the exhibit. How should the laboratory interpret these findings and manage her next steps?", "options": {"A": "Technically nondiagnostic study; repeat the entire test with a higher tracer activity next week", "B": "Prior anterior infarction with viability; arrange FDG PET viability imaging of anterior myocardium", "C": "True anterior ischemia confirmed by the fixed defect; refer her for invasive angiography", "D": "Breast attenuation artifact with normal perfusion; report as normal and avoid further testing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Breast attenuation artifact with normal perfusion; report as normal and avoid further testing): A fixed anterior and anterolateral defect that normalizes on prone imaging, with normal wall motion and thickening on gated images, fits breast attenuation rather than coronary disease. Preserved thickening argues against infarct, and a higher dose would not remove attenuation. Reporting normal perfusion and avoiding catheterization is the best use of the combined supine, prone and gated information.", "image": "question_images/q_197.png"}, {"id": 198, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A dose calibrator accuracy test uses a Cs-137 standard with a decay-corrected activity of 250 microcuries on today's test date. The calibrator reads 262 microcuries. What is the percent error, and does the result pass the regulatory 10 percent accuracy limit?", "options": {"A": "12 percent high, which exceeds the limit because the difference in microcuries is the error.", "B": "0.48 percent high, which passes by a very large margin.", "C": "4.8 percent high, but fails because the accepted accuracy tolerance is 2 percent.", "D": "4.8 percent high, within the 10 percent limit, so the calibrator passes."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (4.8 percent high, within the 10 percent limit, so the calibrator passes.): Percent error is (262 minus 250) divided by 250 times 100, which is 4.8 percent. The regulatory accuracy limit is plus or minus 10 percent, so the calibrator passes. The raw difference of 12 microcuries is not a percent."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 46 kg woman has a small heart with a cavity of about 2.5 cm and high counts. Gated SPECT processed with heavy low-pass smoothing gives an ejection fraction of 82 percent with a tiny cavity. Which processing change best reduces the error?", "options": {"A": "Lower the cutoff further to reduce noise given the high counts in the small cavity", "B": "Apply Chang attenuation correction because the ejection fraction calculation depends on attenuation", "C": "Use less smoothing or resolution-recovery reconstruction, and read the ejection fraction cautiously", "D": "Raise from 8 to 16 gated frames so that partial volume loss is corrected"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use less smoothing or resolution-recovery reconstruction, and read the ejection fraction cautiously): In small hearts, smoothing and limited resolution blur the cavity and overestimate the ejection fraction. Reducing smoothing or using resolution recovery sharpens the cavity boundary, though the value remains approximate. More smoothing worsens the bias, attenuation correction does not alter the cavity size, and more frames do not recover resolution."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man with hypertension, hyperlipidemia and diabetes, no known coronary stenosis, takes aspirin daily and has had three episodes of rest angina in 24 hours. His ECG shows 1.5 mm ST depression and troponin is elevated. What is his TIMI score for unstable angina, and what is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "A score of 6, high risk, so perform regadenoson imaging to choose the vessel to image", "B": "A score of 4, moderate risk, so obtain exercise imaging after 48 hours of medical therapy", "C": "Score of 6, high risk, so defer stress testing and proceed to early invasive angiography", "D": "A score of 3, intermediate risk, so obtain regadenoson imaging before invasive evaluation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Score of 6, high risk, so defer stress testing and proceed to early invasive angiography): Items scored: age 65 or older, three or more risk factors, aspirin in the last 7 days, two or more episodes in 24 hours, ST deviation, and positive marker, six points. Known stenosis of 50 percent or more is absent. With a high score and active ischemia, stress testing is not safe or useful, and early invasive care is chosen."}, {"id": 201, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man with apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and exertional chest pain has a reversible apical-septal defect with transient cavity dilation on SPECT. His coronary risk factors are modest and his resting ECG shows apical T wave inversion. What is the best next step in his further evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Treat as obstructive CAD and proceed to revascularization of the affected territory", "B": "Evaluate the coronary arteries with CT angiography, since defects are nonspecific in this disease", "C": "Accept the study as normal because hypertrophy cannot cause a perfusion defect", "D": "Repeat the SPECT study with a higher dose, since the defect is likely a count error"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Evaluate the coronary arteries with CT angiography, since defects are nonspecific in this disease): In HCM, reversible perfusion defects and transient dilation are common from microvascular dysfunction and hypertrophy even with normal epicardial arteries, so perfusion findings do not prove obstructive CAD. Anatomic imaging answers the question directly. Revascularizing on the defect, repeating with a higher dose or dismissing the abnormality would each be wrong."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A sestamibi vial calibrated at 08:00 holds 30 mCi in 3 mL. The technologist is preparing a rest dose and needs a 10 mCi dose drawn at 11:00 for the first patient. The half-life of Tc-99m is 6 hours. What volume should be drawn?", "options": {"A": "About 1.0 mL, taken from the original concentration without decay correction.", "B": "About 2.0 mL, treating the half-life as 3 hours.", "C": "About 0.7 mL, multiplying by the decay factor instead of dividing.", "D": "About 1.4 mL, since concentration has fallen to 7.1 mCi per mL."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 1.4 mL, since concentration has fallen to 7.1 mCi per mL.): After 3 hours the remaining fraction is 0.5 to the 0.5 power, or 0.707, so the vial holds 21.2 mCi in 3 mL, which is 7.07 mCi per mL. A 10 mCi dose then needs 10 divided by 7.07, about 1.4 mL. Ignoring decay or dividing the wrong way gives different volumes."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The exhibit summarizes a validation cohort of a perfusion test against angiography. A new patient, a 50-year-old man, has a pretest probability of coronary disease of 10 percent and now has an abnormal study. Using the positive likelihood ratio of the test, what is the best estimate of his probability of disease?", "options": {"A": "About 82 percent, the cohort positive predictive value measured at its own prevalence of 50 percent", "B": "About 45 percent, from the 10 percent probability multiplied by the likelihood ratio", "C": "About 90 percent, the sensitivity of the test in the validation cohort", "D": "About 33 percent, from pretest odds of 0.11 times a likelihood ratio of 4.5"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 33 percent, from pretest odds of 0.11 times a likelihood ratio of 4.5): Sensitivity is 90 of 100 and specificity 80 of 100, so the positive likelihood ratio is 0.90 divided by 0.20, or 4.5. A pretest probability of 10 percent is odds of 0.11; times 4.5 gives odds of 0.5, a probability of 33 percent. The cohort PPV of 82 percent holds only at the cohort prevalence of 50 percent.", "image": "question_images/q_203.png"}, {"id": 204, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A waste bin in the hot lab contains Tc-99m (half-life 6 hours) and Tl-201 (half-life 73 hours) syringes and vials. The technologist plans to survey the bin after 60 hours of decay and discard it if near background. What is the pitfall?", "options": {"A": "Longer-lived Tl-201 sets decay time: about 10 half-lives, roughly 30 days", "B": "The bin can be discarded now, since any waste containing Tc-99m decays as fast as Tc-99m", "C": "Tc-99m sets the decay time, so 60 hours is already enough for the mixed waste", "D": "Tl-201 sets the decay time at about 10 days, equal to ten times its biological half-life"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Longer-lived Tl-201 sets decay time: about 10 half-lives, roughly 30 days): Ten half-lives of Tc-99m is 60 hours, but for Tl-201 it is 10 times 73 hours, about 730 hours or 30 days. Mixed waste must be held for the longest-lived component and surveyed before disposal, with radioactive labels removed or defaced. Using the shorter half-life lets residual Tl-201 reach the ordinary trash."}, {"id": 205, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with exertional chest pain reports that her period is 12 days late. A urine pregnancy test in the clinic this morning is negative. A rest-stress Tc-99m study is scheduled for this afternoon. What is the best step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed using Rb-82 PET, since its 76 second half-life gives a fetal radiation dose of zero.", "B": "Proceed, since a negative urine test excludes pregnancy and the technetium fetal dose is negligible.", "C": "Obtain a serum hCG before injection, since urine tests miss very early pregnancy.", "D": "Repeat the urine test in 10 days and image today only if she lacks pregnancy symptoms."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain a serum hCG before injection, since urine tests miss very early pregnancy.): A delayed period raises real concern, and urine tests may be negative very early in pregnancy, so a sensitive serum hCG settles the question. If she is pregnant, the study should be postponed or an alternative without radiation chosen, with fetal dose documented if imaging is unavoidable. Short half-life lowers but does not eliminate dose, and symptoms cannot exclude pregnancy."}, {"id": 206, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man has stress-redistribution Tl-201 SPECT. An inferior defect is present on the stress images and is unchanged on the 4-hour images. What is the best next step before calling this defect scar? He has diabetes and prior angina.", "options": {"A": "Image again at 24 hours, which detects viability equally well as reinjection.", "B": "Report fixed scar, because persistence at 4 hours indicates nonviable myocardium.", "C": "Repeat the stress test to confirm the defect and reimage tomorrow.", "D": "Reinject Tl-201 at rest and reimage to look for viable myocardium."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reinject Tl-201 at rest and reimage to look for viable myocardium.): A defect that stays fixed at 4 hours may still contain viable but severely hypoperfused myocardium, because redistribution can be slow. Rest reinjection of Tl-201 and reimaging shows uptake in many apparently fixed defects. Calling scar at 4 hours misses viability, and 24-hour imaging is less sensitive than reinjection."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Rb-82 PET stress images show a new anterolateral defect that vanishes on the non-attenuation-corrected images. On the fusion display, the lateral wall of the emission data lies over lung on the CT attenuation map. The patient moved after the CT. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the stress injection after 10 minutes to confirm the defect with a second acquisition", "B": "Report lateral ischemia and recommend angiography, since the defect is on the corrected images", "C": "Reconstruct with fewer iterations and heavier smoothing to remove the lateral defect", "D": "Realign the emission data to the CT attenuation map manually and reprocess before reading"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Realign the emission data to the CT attenuation map manually and reprocess before reading): PET attenuation correction divides emission data by CT-derived factors, so if the heart is shifted onto low-density lung the lateral wall is overcorrected or undercorrected and an artifactual defect appears. Checking registration is a standard quality step, and manual realignment with reprocessing resolves it. A second injection or heavy smoothing does not address misregistration."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Anterior planar images after thallium-201 stress in a 59-year-old man with exertional dyspnea show a heart region of interest averaging 118 counts per pixel and a lung region averaging 71 counts per pixel. What is the lung to heart ratio, and how should it be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "0.60, above the usual upper limit of 0.5, suggesting transient dysfunction and extensive disease", "B": "1.66, the heart to lung ratio, which is high and indicates a normal study", "C": "0.38, within normal limits, if lung counts are divided by heart plus lung counts combined", "D": "0.40, within normal limits, if the count difference is divided by the heart counts alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (0.60, above the usual upper limit of 0.5, suggesting transient dysfunction and extensive disease): The lung to heart ratio is lung counts divided by heart counts, 71 over 118 equals 0.60. A value above about 0.5 on thallium-201 indicates increased lung uptake from stress-induced left ventricular dysfunction and raised filling pressure, a high-risk finding. The other values come from an inverted ratio, subtracting counts, or dividing by a sum."}, {"id": 209, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Quantitative analysis of a Tc-99m SPECT study reports a severe inferior defect with total perfusion deficit of 12 percent. On visual review, inferior counts look normal, but intense hepatic activity abuts the inferior wall, and the left ventricular contour extends into the liver. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, which the normal database already removes so the polar map cannot show it.", "B": "Contour misplacement into adjacent hepatic activity distorted sampling and created a false inferior defect.", "C": "Septal flash from left bundle branch block, which lowers counts only in the septal segments.", "D": "Wrong sex-specific normal limits, which would shift all segments equally and not only the inferior wall."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contour misplacement into adjacent hepatic activity distorted sampling and created a false inferior defect.): Automated programs depend on correct contour detection. When hepatic activity is adjacent and the contour includes it, the sampled counts do not represent myocardium and a false defect appears, even though visual counts are normal. Quantitative results should be checked against the images and contours. Normal databases do not correct attenuation, and bundle branch block affects the septum."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 48 year old woman receiving trastuzumab after anthracycline therapy needs left ventricular ejection fraction measured every 3 months. Her echo windows are poor, and her baseline ejection fraction was 58 percent. Treatment will be adjusted if it falls. Which method suits serial monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Left ventriculography each time, since it gives the most direct measure of ejection fraction", "B": "Echocardiography with a different sonographer each time, since it avoids radiation exposure altogether", "C": "Gated sestamibi SPECT at each visit, since it adds a perfusion assessment to the ejection fraction", "D": "Equilibrium radionuclide angiography, since count-based EF is reproducible and independent of acoustic windows"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Equilibrium radionuclide angiography, since count-based EF is reproducible and independent of acoustic windows): Cardiotoxicity monitoring depends on detecting small EF changes, such as a fall of 10 points to below 50 percent. Equilibrium gated blood pool imaging is count-based, uses no geometric assumptions and has low inter-study variability, about 3 to 5 percent. Variable operators and poor windows weaken echo, and gated SPECT or catheterization adds cost or risk without benefit."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 150 kg patient with a BMI of 44 is referred for Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The laboratory offers a same-day rest-stress protocol with a three hour slot or a two-day protocol. A large body habitus causes heavy scatter and low counts. Which plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Use the two-day protocol with a full weight-adjusted dose on each day to raise counts", "B": "Switch to same-day Tl-201 because its lower photon energy suits very large patients", "C": "Use same-day rest-stress with 10 and 30 mCi because a single visit avoids a second trip", "D": "Use same-day imaging with 30 mCi for each injection an hour apart to saturate counts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use the two-day protocol with a full weight-adjusted dose on each day to raise counts): In a very large patient, count-limited images benefit from the two-day protocol, which removes residual activity from the first dose and allows the full weight-adjusted dose for each study. Same-day imaging requires a smaller first dose and leaves background that contaminates the second, while a one-hour interval with equal doses worsens that contamination. Tl-201 has lower photon energy and suffers more attenuation, with a higher radiation burden."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man has no angina on stable medical therapy. SPECT shows a summed difference score of 10, an LVEF of 58 percent, and no left main concern. What percentage of the left ventricle is ischemic, and what is the best management?", "options": {"A": "About 59 percent ischemia; refer for bypass because most of the left ventricle is at risk.", "B": "About 15 percent ischemia; refer for routine angiography because ischemia above 10 percent requires revascularization.", "C": "About 10 percent ischemia; stop therapy because the score of ten equals ten percent of myocardium.", "D": "About 15 percent ischemia; continue guideline-directed medical therapy and revascularize only for refractory symptoms."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 15 percent ischemia; continue guideline-directed medical therapy and revascularize only for refractory symptoms.): The maximum score is 17 segments times 4, or 68, so 10 divided by 68 is about 15 percent. In stable patients on therapy, trials of a routine invasive strategy have not shown better survival than medical therapy with revascularization for symptoms. Treating the score as the percentage gives 10, and dividing by 17 segments instead of 68 gives an impossible 59."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man with left bundle branch block and exertional dyspnea has regadenoson SPECT. Septal and anteroseptal counts are reduced during stress and milder at rest (summed stress score 8, rest score 4, difference score 4), while the rest of the anterior descending territory looks normal. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Likely bundle branch pattern, so confirm with gated thickening and avoid labeling disease from septal counts", "B": "Septal defect despite left bundle branch block indicates proximal anterior descending stenosis, so refer for angiography", "C": "Fixed septal scar from prior infarction, since the defect is present on both rows", "D": "Reversible right coronary ischemia, since the septum receives its flow from that vessel in most hearts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Likely bundle branch pattern, so confirm with gated thickening and avoid labeling disease from septal counts): Left bundle branch block commonly causes an isolated septal count change on vasodilator studies without coronary stenosis. Because the rest of the anterior descending territory is normal and the difference score is small, the septal pattern alone does not support left anterior descending disease. Corroborate with thickening, ECG and clinical data before sending the patient for angiography.", "image": "question_images/q_213.png"}, {"id": 214, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man has stable moderate asthma on an inhaled steroid, with no wheezing today and a normal oxygen saturation. He cannot exercise because of severe knee pain and needs pharmacologic stress SPECT. His oxygen saturation is normal. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson with a bronchodilator available, since bronchospasm risk is lower than with adenosine.", "B": "Adenosine, because its half-life under ten seconds makes any bronchospasm brief.", "C": "Cancel vasodilator stress, since asthma is an absolute contraindication to every vasodilator.", "D": "Dipyridamole, because A2A selectivity spares airway receptors in asthma."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regadenoson with a bronchodilator available, since bronchospasm risk is lower than with adenosine.): Regadenoson is selective for A2A and has a lower rate of bronchospasm than adenosine, so it can be used in stable mild or moderate reactive airway disease with rescue medication ready. Active wheezing would change that. Dipyridamole acts through adenosine at nonselective receptors for a long period."}, {"id": 215, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Rb-82 PET/CT in a 70-year-old man with a right ventricular ICD lead shows intense focal uptake at the apical septum on attenuation-corrected images only. The uncorrected images look normal at that location. What is the cause of the hot spot?", "options": {"A": "PET and CT misregistration moving the heart into the lung field on the corrected attenuation map.", "B": "Rb-82 positron range blurring caused by the dense metal near the septum at the lead tip.", "C": "True hyperperfusion from regional hypertrophy around the lead tip at the apical septal wall segment.", "D": "Metal inflates CT values, so attenuation-corrected PET counts beside the lead are falsely high."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Metal inflates CT values, so attenuation-corrected PET counts beside the lead are falsely high.): Metal leads cause very high CT numbers, which convert to large attenuation correction factors for PET and create a false focal hot spot near the lead. The uncorrected images, which lack that error, look normal. Misregistration produces defects in the lateral wall, not a hot spot at the lead. Positron range is not changed by metal."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with a prior RCA stent and diffuse coronary calcification on an earlier CT has had 3 hours of chest pressure that resolved. The ECG is nondiagnostic, and serial high-sensitivity troponins at 0 and 3 hours are normal. She walks well. The ED physician wants one noninvasive test before discharge. What is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Coronary CT angiography, since it has the highest negative predictive value in emergency department chest pain", "B": "Repeat troponin at 24 hours and discharge if normal, with no imaging for a known stent", "C": "Exercise rest-stress perfusion imaging, since stent and calcium limit CT angiography", "D": "Urgent invasive angiography, since resolved pain with a prior stent calls for immediate catheterization"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exercise rest-stress perfusion imaging, since stent and calcium limit CT angiography): After serial negative troponins and no ongoing ischemia, a stress perfusion study can safely risk stratify. CT angiography loses accuracy with dense calcium and stents, which makes it a less valuable choice here even though it is useful in many ED patients. Immediate catheterization is for high risk features, which are absent."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old man exercises for 11 minutes, reaches 93 percent of age-predicted maximum heart rate and has no symptoms. Tc-99m stress images with attenuation correction show normal perfusion and normal gated ejection fraction. Which statement best explains why the rest study can be omitted?", "options": {"A": "Exercise at 93 percent of maximum lowers background activity, making rest comparison unnecessary in most patients.", "B": "Normal stress perfusion and function exclude ischemia and scar, so rest images add no information.", "C": "Normal stress perfusion excludes ischemia but not old scar, so rest images are needed to confirm.", "D": "Normal stress images exclude artifact, so rest imaging is needed only to repeat the ejection fraction."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal stress perfusion and function exclude ischemia and scar, so rest images add no information.): Stress-first imaging relies on a normal stress study. A perfusion defect from ischemia or scar would show on the stress images, so normal uptake and function leave no question that rest imaging could answer. This saves roughly 10 mSv of dose and an hour or more of time, and it is validated in patients with low pretest probability and no known infarction. Gated function comes from the stress acquisition, so rest data are not required for ejection fraction."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 48-year-old woman with a BMI of 33 had a Tl-201 rest-redistribution SPECT that showed a fixed anterior and lateral defect. A repeat study with Tc-99m sestamibi was normal. Which tracer property best explains the first result and supports using Tc-99m for her?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201 emits 69 to 80 keV photons, attenuated more by breast than 140 keV Tc-99m", "B": "Tl-201 has a short physical half-life of 73 hours that gives poor counts, which Tc-99m improves", "C": "Tl-201 redistributes too slowly in the anterior wall, so rapid Tc-99m washout corrects the defect", "D": "Tl-201 has low myocardial extraction in women, making anterior segments appear falsely fixed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tl-201 emits 69 to 80 keV photons, attenuated more by breast than 140 keV Tc-99m): Thallium photons (69 to 80 keV x rays and some 135 and 167 keV) are attenuated and scattered more than the 140 keV photon of Tc-99m, so soft tissue such as breast produces more artifact with thallium. The thallium half-life is about 73 hours, not short, and extraction and redistribution are not the cause here."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Rb-82 extraction is about 0.65 at a flow of 1.0 mL/min/g and about 0.40 at 3.0 mL/min/g, and net tracer uptake equals flow times extraction. When regadenoson triples flow from the resting value, by what factor does tracer uptake rise?", "options": {"A": "About 3.0-fold, because uptake rises in direct proportion to flow", "B": "About 1.8-fold, from 1.2 divided by 0.65, so uptake lags the flow rise", "C": "About 4.9-fold, because the extraction ratio is inverted before multiplying by flow", "D": "About 1.2-fold, because the hyperemic uptake value is compared with a resting value of one"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 1.8-fold, from 1.2 divided by 0.65, so uptake lags the flow rise): Rest uptake is 1.0 times 0.65, equal to 0.65, and hyperemic uptake is 3.0 times 0.40, equal to 1.20. The ratio is 1.20 divided by 0.65, about 1.8-fold, even though flow tripled. Ignoring roll-off gives 3.0, omitting the resting extraction gives 1.2, and inverting the ratio gives about 4.9, all wrong."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A nuclear cardiologist is explaining radiation units to a fellow before a case conference. Which quantity, expressed in sieverts, weights the absorbed dose by tissue sensitivity and radiation type to estimate overall biologic risk from a single perfusion study in an adult?", "options": {"A": "Activity, which counts disintegrations per second and is reported in sieverts for the tracer", "B": "Absorbed dose, which is energy deposited per unit mass and is reported in sieverts", "C": "Effective dose, which weights organ doses by tissue factors and is reported in sieverts", "D": "Exposure, which measures ionization in air and is reported in sieverts for each study"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Effective dose, which weights organ doses by tissue factors and is reported in sieverts): Absorbed dose is in gray (1 Gy = 100 rad), equivalent dose uses radiation weighting factors, and effective dose weights organ equivalent doses by tissue factors, both in sievert (1 Sv = 100 rem). Exposure applies to ionization in air and activity is measured in becquerel or curie."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The half-value layer of lead for the 140 keV photons of Tc-99m is about 0.3 mm. A syringe shield contains 0.9 mm of lead around a dose that would otherwise read 80 mR/h at the technologist's fingertip. What exposure rate is expected at the fingertip with the shield in place?", "options": {"A": "About 27 mR/h, found by dividing the unshielded rate by the number of half-value layers.", "B": "About 40 mR/h, found by applying a single half-value layer to the whole shield.", "C": "About 20 mR/h, found by counting only two half-value layers in the lead thickness.", "D": "About 10 mR/h, because three half-value layers reduce intensity to one eighth."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 10 mR/h, because three half-value layers reduce intensity to one eighth.): Thickness divided by the half-value layer gives 0.9 / 0.3 = 3 layers, and each layer halves the intensity: 80 x 1/2 x 1/2 x 1/2 = 10 mR/h. Dividing by 3 gives 27, two layers give 20 and one layer gives 40, each a distinct counting or arithmetic slip. The exponential rule means thickness reduces exposure by repeated halving, not by simple division."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a dobutamine SPECT study, a 69-year-old man on metoprolol that morning reaches 40 micrograms per kilogram per minute with a heart rate of 108 beats per minute, which is 65 percent of his predicted maximum. His blood pressure is stable and he has no arrhythmia. What is the safest next step?", "options": {"A": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin to improve filling, since lower afterload raises heart rate and lowers risk.", "B": "Stop the study as nondiagnostic, since beta blocker use means the target cannot be reached safely.", "C": "Increase the dobutamine to 60 micrograms per kilogram per minute, since dobutamine alone must reach target.", "D": "Add intravenous atropine in small increments while continuing the infusion, then inject at target rate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add intravenous atropine in small increments while continuing the infusion, then inject at target rate.): Beta blockade can blunt the chronotropic response to dobutamine. The standard approach is to add atropine in small increments of about 0.25 mg once the maximum dobutamine dose of 40 micrograms per kilogram per minute is reached. Exceeding that dose raises arrhythmia and hypotension risk. Nitroglycerin does not increase heart rate."}, {"id": 223, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man with exertional angina has angiographically proven three-vessel disease with 70 percent stenoses in each major artery, yet his Tc-99m SPECT shows homogeneous relative perfusion and normal wall motion on gating. Which explanation best accounts for the normal appearance?", "options": {"A": "Gated function cannot be normal in three-vessel disease, so the scan must be a technical failure.", "B": "Tracer extraction saturates in normal myocardium, so sestamibi cannot show any flow difference between regions.", "C": "Uptake is normalized to the highest-count region, so uniformly reduced flow can look normal.", "D": "Three-vessel stenoses of 70 percent are not hemodynamically significant, so normal perfusion is the correct finding."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Uptake is normalized to the highest-count region, so uniformly reduced flow can look normal.): SPECT displays counts relative to the hottest myocardium. When all three territories have similarly reduced flow reserve, no region stands out and the scan appears normal, which is balanced ischemia. Stenoses of 70 percent are significant by angiographic criteria, and gated function can be normal. Ancillary signs such as transient dilation or stunning can give a clue."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 34-year-old woman, eight months postpartum and breast-feeding her infant, receives 10 mCi (370 MBq) of F-18 FDG for a cardiac viability PET study. She lives with the infant and asks what to do when she gets home. What is the best instruction?", "options": {"A": "Limit close, prolonged infant contact for about 12 hours; breast-feeding itself may continue", "B": "Take potassium iodide before leaving to block uptake of free fluoride in the thyroid gland", "C": "Give no precautions, since F-18 has fully decayed within two hours of the injection", "D": "Discard all expressed milk for two weeks because F-18 concentrates in breast tissue and persists there"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Limit close, prolonged infant contact for about 12 hours; breast-feeding itself may continue): F-18 has a physical half-life of 110 minutes, so about 12 hours is roughly six half-lives, and the main exposure to the infant comes from close contact with the mother rather than from milk. Prolonged milk discarding fits long-lived I-131, not F-18. Potassium iodide blocks radioiodine uptake and does nothing for fluoride. Two hours is only about one half-life, so activity remains substantial."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a prior large infarct and an ejection fraction of 25 percent is being evaluated for revascularization. The team wants to know whether severely hypoperfused segments still contain viable myocardium. Which SPECT protocol is best for this question?", "options": {"A": "Thallium-201 rest and redistribution imaging, since uptake needs intact membranes and shows redistribution.", "B": "Thallium-201 stress imaging without delayed images, since it has the highest myocardial extraction fraction.", "C": "Tc-99m sestamibi stress imaging alone, since stress counts separate scar from viable tissue reliably.", "D": "Tc-99m tetrofosmin rest imaging alone, since its minimal redistribution reflects viability of the segments."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thallium-201 rest and redistribution imaging, since uptake needs intact membranes and shows redistribution.): Thallium-201 is a potassium analog that enters viable cells and redistributes, so rest-redistribution imaging identifies hibernating myocardium in severely hypoperfused segments. Tc-99m agents underestimate viability in these segments unless nitrates are used. Stress-only thallium without delayed images cannot show redistribution."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman with syncope and documented Mobitz type II AV block has no pacemaker. She is referred for stress MPI, and regadenoson has been ordered because she can walk only 3 minutes. Which action is safest given the pathophysiology of adenosine receptor stimulation?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with regadenoson and keep aminophylline ready, since reversal treats any block that follows.", "B": "Use dobutamine or wait for pacing, because adenosine agonists slow AV nodal conduction.", "C": "Give regadenoson at half dose, since A2A selectivity removes AV nodal slowing at low doses.", "D": "Switch to dipyridamole, since the indirect rise in adenosine spares the AV node."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use dobutamine or wait for pacing, because adenosine agonists slow AV nodal conduction.): Adenosine, regadenoson and dipyridamole all act through adenosine receptors and can worsen second or third degree AV block in patients without a pacemaker, so they are contraindicated. Dobutamine does not act on the AV node this way and is an alternative, or testing can wait until pacing is placed. Reversal with aminophylline is a rescue, not a plan."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A single-head camera acquires a 180 degree RAO 45 to LPO 45 orbit with 60 projections at 25 seconds each. The lab proposes a 360 degree orbit with 120 projections at the same time per stop. How much time does this change add?", "options": {"A": "It adds 50 minutes, since time doubles for the orbit and again for projections.", "B": "It adds 25 minutes, going from 25 to 50 minutes of acquisition time.", "C": "It adds no time, since total dwell is fixed by the detector radius.", "D": "It adds 12.5 minutes, since the extra projections share time with the existing ones."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It adds 25 minutes, going from 25 to 50 minutes of acquisition time.): The current scan takes 60 times 25 seconds, or 1,500 seconds, which is 25 minutes. The proposed scan takes 120 times 25 seconds, or 3,000 seconds, which is 50 minutes, so 25 more minutes are added. The projection count already includes the wider orbit, so doubling again would be a mistake. Longer scans raise motion risk."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 140 kg man with a BMI of 44 undergoes stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The injected stress dose was the laboratory's fixed 30 mCi. Raw projection images show no patient motion and quality control was normal, but the reconstructed slices are noisy with low count density. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Poor Tc-99m extraction in obesity, which requires conversion to thallium for adequate uptake", "B": "Patient motion during acquisition that blurred the wall and reduced apparent count density", "C": "Excess activity that saturated the detectors and caused dead time loss of myocardial counts", "D": "Fixed dose too low for his weight, so use weight-based dosing or PET"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fixed dose too low for his weight, so use weight-based dosing or PET): In a large patient photons are lost to attenuation and scatter, so a fixed standard dose gives noisy images. Weight-based dose scaling, a longer acquisition, or a switch to PET restores quality. Detector saturation occurs at much higher count rates than a 30 mCi injection produces, and the stem excludes motion and instrument faults."}, {"id": 229, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man with known sick sinus syndrome and no pacemaker is scheduled for adenosine MPI because he cannot exercise due to severe diabetic neuropathy and leg weakness. If the study is performed as planned, what is the most likely complication?", "options": {"A": "Severe bronchospasm that requires immediate aminophylline and ends the study", "B": "Prolonged sinus arrest or high-grade AV block requiring the infusion to stop", "C": "Coronary vasospasm with ST elevation that leads to emergency angiography", "D": "Sustained ventricular tachycardia that requires cardioversion in most patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prolonged sinus arrest or high-grade AV block requiring the infusion to stop): Adenosine slows sinoatrial and AV nodal conduction, so a patient with sick sinus syndrome and no pacemaker is at risk for long pauses or high-grade block. That is why the condition is a contraindication to adenosine and dipyridamole without pacing support. Bronchospasm occurs in a different susceptible group, and vasospasm and ventricular tachycardia are not the expected hazards."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man has gated SPECT showing a large fixed inferoposterior defect with an SRS of 11 and an SDS of 1, and an EF of 28 percent. A colleague says risk is low because ischemia is minimal. Which statement about prognosis is best?", "options": {"A": "Risk is low, because only ischemic extent predicts events and an SDS of 1 is minimal.", "B": "Risk is determined by the stress ECG, so the imaging variables should be set aside.", "C": "Risk is low, because the lack of TID shows the study carries no prognostic weight.", "D": "Risk is high, since a very low EF and large scar predict cardiac death without ischemia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Risk is high, since a very low EF and large scar predict cardiac death without ischemia.): Ischemic extent mainly predicts myocardial infarction and revascularization benefit, but cardiac death is driven by LV function and scar size. An EF of 28 percent with a large fixed defect is high risk. Minimal ischemia does not remove that risk, and the stress ECG does not outweigh the imaging."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "At 7:45 am the daily constancy check of the dose calibrator with Cs-137 reads 12 percent below the decay corrected reference, and a patient is due at 8:30 am. The tolerance is 10 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat only the annual accuracy test, since daily constancy has no tolerance of its own", "B": "Repeat the check, confirm the isotope setting and background, and pull the unit if error persists", "C": "Apply a 12 percent correction factor to each patient dose and continue the day's schedule", "D": "Run the linearity test first, since constancy readings cannot show a real calibrator fault"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the check, confirm the isotope setting and background, and pull the unit if error persists): Constancy is a daily check against a long lived source, and a result outside 10 percent means the instrument cannot be trusted. Repeat it, rule out a wrong setting or background, and take the unit out of service if the deviation persists. A blanket correction factor hides a fault and constancy does have a tolerance."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with a small hypertrophic left ventricle has gated Tc-99m SPECT showing an end-diastolic volume of 38 mL and a reported LVEF of 85 percent. How should the reader handle the reported EF in the final report? She has no chest pain.", "options": {"A": "Accept the value as exact, since gated SPECT is highly reproducible in very small hearts.", "B": "Repeat the acquisition with a higher dose to improve the camera's spatial resolution.", "C": "Report the EF as unreliable in a small cavity and confirm with echocardiography or MRI.", "D": "Reprocess with a tighter ventricular mask to correct the partial volume overestimation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the EF as unreliable in a small cavity and confirm with echocardiography or MRI.): In small ventricles, limited spatial resolution and partial volume effects shrink the apparent systolic cavity and push the gated SPECT EF too high. Confirmation with echocardiography or cardiac MRI is appropriate. Processing masks and extra dose do not overcome the resolution limit."}, {"id": 233, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Gated stress SPECT in a patient with frequent PVCs gives an ejection fraction of 31 percent, while echo last week gave 58 percent. The gated frames flicker with count loss in the late frames, and ungated perfusion is normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Increase from 8 to 16 gated frames to improve temporal resolution of the fraction", "B": "Report an ejection fraction of 31 percent with moderate dysfunction since gated SPECT is operator independent", "C": "Review the R-R histogram, reprocess with beat rejection or from list mode, then recompute the fraction", "D": "Widen the beat acceptance window to include all beats so no counts are discarded"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the R-R histogram, reprocess with beat rejection or from list mode, then recompute the fraction): Irregular rhythm causes gating errors, so late frames lose counts and the ejection fraction is underestimated. Reviewing the R-R histogram and tightening the acceptance window, or processing from list mode, removes short and long cycles and restores valid frames. Reporting the number accepts a rhythm artifact, a wider window adds ectopic beats, and more frames reduce counts per frame."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist is about to draw up F-18 FDG for a PET study using the 2 mm tungsten syringe shield normally used for Tc-99m. The exhibit lists the emissions and lead half-value layers for both tracers. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a shield rated for 511 keV, since annihilation photons pass the Tc-99m shield", "B": "Continue, because 2 mm is many half-value layers for Tc-99m and so protects here too", "C": "Continue, because the positron is the main hazard and 2 mm tungsten stops it", "D": "Wrap the syringe in plastic, because plastic stops 511 keV photons better than tungsten"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Switch to a shield rated for 511 keV, since annihilation photons pass the Tc-99m shield): The positron travels only millimeters in the syringe wall, but when it annihilates it produces two 511 keV photons that are far more penetrating than the 140 keV photon of Tc-99m. The exhibit shows a lead half-value layer of about 4 mm against about 0.3 mm, so a Tc-99m shield removes only a small fraction. A shield built for 511 keV is needed, and plastic does not attenuate photons well.", "image": "question_images/q_234.png"}, {"id": 235, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A stress SPECT study shows an isolated reversible defect in the basal and mid anterolateral wall, segments 6 and 12, with normal septal, inferior and apical segments. Angiography has not been performed. Which statement about the likely culprit vessel is best?", "options": {"A": "The left main artery only, since an isolated anterolateral defect cannot arise from a branch vessel.", "B": "Circumflex marginal branches most likely, with a diagonal LAD branch as a variant.", "C": "The distal LAD wrapping the apex, since the anterolateral wall is supplied by the apical vessel.", "D": "The proximal right coronary artery, since the anterolateral wall lies in the right coronary territory."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Circumflex marginal branches most likely, with a diagonal LAD branch as a variant.): Segments 6 and 12 belong to the anterolateral wall, supplied most often by obtuse marginal branches of the circumflex, though a diagonal of the LAD can supply it in some patients. The RCA supplies the inferior wall, apical wrap would involve the apex, and a branch vessel can cause an isolated lateral defect without left main disease."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A generator eluate assayed at 08:00 shows 0.10 microcuries of Mo-99 per millicurie of Tc-99m, within the 0.15 limit. The doses are not injected until 14:00. The Mo-99 half-life is 66 hours and Tc-99m is 6.02 hours. Why might the limit be exceeded at administration?", "options": {"A": "The generator column adds new Mo-99 to the vial during the six hours, increasing its content.", "B": "Tc-99m decays faster than Mo-99, so the ratio rises to about 0.19 and exceeds the limit.", "C": "The dose calibrator applies a six hour correction that lowers the true ratio below the limit.", "D": "Mo-99 decays faster than Tc-99m, so the ratio falls to about 0.05 and meets the limit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tc-99m decays faster than Mo-99, so the ratio rises to about 0.19 and exceeds the limit.): The limit is 0.15 microcuries of Mo-99 per millicurie of Tc-99m at the time of administration. In six hours the Tc-99m falls to 0.50 of its starting value while the Mo-99 falls only to 0.94, so the ratio is multiplied by about 1.87, giving 0.10 x 1.87 = 0.19. This exceeds the limit, so doses should be given earlier or a fresh elution used. Mo-99 is not added to an eluate vial after elution."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man had an inferior myocardial infarction with Q waves in leads II, III and aVF five years ago. He now has atypical chest discomfort. Regadenoson sestamibi SPECT is performed, and the stress, rest and reversibility polar maps are reviewed. What is the best interpretation of the inferior defect?", "options": {"A": "Hibernating myocardium, because uptake at rest remains reduced and function must therefore recover.", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, so the defect can be disregarded when planning any further treatment.", "C": "A fixed inferior and inferoseptal defect consistent with infarct scar, without ischemia there.", "D": "Reversible inferior ischemia, so the right coronary artery needs prompt revascularization now."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A fixed inferior and inferoseptal defect consistent with infarct scar, without ischemia there.): The same defect appears at stress and at rest, and the reversibility map is nearly dark, so the inferior and inferoseptal segments show scar rather than ischemia. That fits the patient's Q waves and prior infarct. Reversible ischemia would improve at rest. Attenuation cannot be assumed in a patient with known inferior infarction, and hibernation needs more than reduced rest counts.", "image": "question_images/q_237.png"}, {"id": 238, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A site enables resolution recovery iterative reconstruction using parameters for LEGP collimators, but the studies were acquired with LEHR collimators. Images look sharper than usual, with bright edges around the myocardial walls. What is the most likely consequence of this mismatch?", "options": {"A": "Counts scale down evenly across all walls, so only the absolute EF values change after reconstruction.", "B": "Attenuation is overcorrected by the mismatch, so inferior wall counts rise near the diaphragm.", "C": "Blur is overcorrected, creating edge overshoot and ringing that change counts in thin walls.", "D": "Scatter is no longer subtracted by the model, so liver counts bleed into the inferior wall."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blur is overcorrected, creating edge overshoot and ringing that change counts in thin walls.): Resolution recovery models the collimator blur as a function of distance. LEGP collimators blur more than LEHR, so applying LEGP parameters to LEHR data overcorrects, and the reverse mismatch undercorrects. Wrong parameters produce ringing, edge overshoot and spurious count changes in thin walls. The model does not handle attenuation or scatter."}, {"id": 239, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A nuclear cardiology laboratory adds F-18 FDG sarcoid studies. A technologist draws the dose with the thin tungsten syringe shield used for Tc-99m, and ring badge doses climb. The lab physicist is asked to review. Which statement best explains the problem and the correction?", "options": {"A": "Tc-99m photons at 140 keV penetrate more than 511 keV photons, so the shield is overbuilt.", "B": "Positron range, not photon energy, sets the shield thickness, so use a plastic shield.", "C": "Shielding is unnecessary because the 110-minute half-life lowers the dose rate within a few hours.", "D": "The 511 keV annihilation photons penetrate a Tc-99m shield, so thicker tungsten is needed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The 511 keV annihilation photons penetrate a Tc-99m shield, so thicker tungsten is needed.): F-18 decays by positron emission, and each annihilation produces two 511 keV photons that are far more penetrating than 140 keV Tc-99m photons. Shields designed for Tc-99m attenuate little, so thicker tungsten, distance and time minimization are required. Half-life does not remove the dose rate at the time of handling."}, {"id": 240, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A dose calibrator linearity test by the decay method starts with 200 mCi (7.4 GBq) of Tc-99m at 8:00 a.m. At 2:00 p.m., six hours later, the instrument reads 92 mCi. Using a 6.0 hour half life, what is the result?", "options": {"A": "Expected 50 mCi (1.85 GBq), deviation 84 percent, so linearity fails badly", "B": "Expected 100 mCi (3.7 GBq), deviation 8 percent, which exceeds a 5 percent limit and fails", "C": "Expected 119 mCi (4.4 GBq), deviation 23 percent, so the test fails", "D": "Expected 100 mCi (3.7 GBq), deviation 8 percent, inside the 10 percent limit, so it passes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Expected 100 mCi (3.7 GBq), deviation 8 percent, inside the 10 percent limit, so it passes): After one half life of 6.0 hours the expected activity is 100 mCi, and the reading of 92 mCi deviates by 8 percent. Required linearity tolerance is within 10 percent, so the test passes. Using two half lives, a 5 percent limit or an eight hour half life gives the wrong expected value or limit."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exhibit summarizes an event at a nuclear cardiology lab, where one patient received a dose prescribed for another. The physician must decide whether it is an NRC reportable medical event under 10 CFR 35.3045 and who must be told. Which determination is correct?", "options": {"A": "Not a reportable medical event, since the dose is under 5 rem effective dose", "B": "A medical event, because the activity differed from the prescription by more than 20 percent", "C": "Reportable within 30 days, since a dose under 5 rem needs only a written report", "D": "A medical event, because any administration to the wrong individual is reported by the next day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Not a reportable medical event, since the dose is under 5 rem effective dose): For a wrong individual, wrong drug, or wrong route, the event is a medical event only if the dose exceeds 5 rem (0.05 Sv) effective dose or 50 rem (0.5 Sv) to an organ or tissue. About 0.33 rem is far below both. The event should still be documented, the physician informed, and the process reviewed. The 20 percent deviation rule needs the dose threshold as well.", "image": "question_images/q_241.png"}, {"id": 242, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A new technologist asks why the laboratory acquires a high-count flood every morning, even though the camera is used mainly for tomographic myocardial perfusion studies. You explain that small sensitivity differences matter more in SPECT than in planar imaging. Which mechanism best explains why a few percent of nonuniformity is so damaging for SPECT?", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity defects alter the attenuation map, so attenuation correction overcompensates in the affected wall.", "B": "Flood nonuniformity degrades intrinsic spatial resolution, which blurs the reconstructed polar map uniformly.", "C": "Nonuniformity shifts the photopeak energy, so more counts fall into the scatter window and reduce contrast.", "D": "Back projection repeats a fixed defect at every angle and builds a ring artifact in slices."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Back projection repeats a fixed defect at every angle and builds a ring artifact in slices.): A detector defect stays at the same position in every projection, so during back projection it is smeared into an arc at every angle and sums to a ring, usually near the center of rotation. This is why SPECT tolerates far less nonuniformity than planar imaging. Energy shift and resolution loss are separate faults, and an uncorrected SPECT study has no CT attenuation map involved.", "image": "question_images/q_242.png"}, {"id": 243, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "In ischemic cardiomyopathy, rest-redistribution Tl-201 imaging shows a severe fixed inferior defect at 40 percent of peak activity on the 4 hour scan. Viability is the clinical question and no reinjection was done. What is the pitfall, and what is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Add nitroglycerin to the thallium study to promote redistribution of tracer into the defect", "B": "The defect is irreversible scar since 4 hours is sufficient, so no further imaging is needed", "C": "Four hour redistribution may be incomplete, so reinject Tl-201 or image at 24 hours", "D": "Repeat with Tc-99m sestamibi immediately because its redistribution is superior"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Four hour redistribution may be incomplete, so reinject Tl-201 or image at 24 hours): Thallium-201 redistribution can be slow in severely hypoperfused myocardium, so a fixed defect at 4 hours may still be viable. Reinjection or a 24 hour image uncovers delayed redistribution. Accepting scar underestimates viability, sestamibi has minimal redistribution, and nitrates belong to Tc-99m rest protocols."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A lab acquires stress cardiac SPECT in a 64 by 64 matrix with a pixel size of 6.4 mm. The physicist asks for the Nyquist frequency in order to set a Butterworth cutoff. Which value is correct for these acquisition parameters?", "options": {"A": "About 0.78 cycles per centimeter, or 0.5 cycles per pixel, from one over twice 0.64 cm", "B": "About 7.8 cycles per centimeter, found as one divided by twice the 0.064 cm pixel size", "C": "About 1.56 cycles per centimeter, found as one divided by the pixel size of 0.64 cm", "D": "About 0.39 cycles per centimeter, found as one divided by four times the pixel size"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 0.78 cycles per centimeter, or 0.5 cycles per pixel, from one over twice 0.64 cm): The Nyquist frequency is the highest frequency a sampling grid can represent, equal to one divided by twice the pixel size. With 0.64 cm pixels that gives 1 / 1.28 = 0.78 cycles per cm, or 0.5 cycles per pixel. Dropping the factor of two doubles the answer, using four samples halves it, and a decimal error in pixel size shifts the result tenfold. Cutoffs are usually quoted as a fraction of this value."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The exhibit summarizes serial gated blood pool studies in a 49-year-old woman receiving anthracycline chemotherapy for breast cancer, with no cardiac symptoms and a regular rhythm on both studies. The oncologist asks for the key interpretation. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A fall in right ventricular ejection fraction alone defines anthracycline cardiotoxicity in this setting.", "B": "The ejection fraction remains in the lower normal range, so therapy continues with usual surveillance.", "C": "The decline is under 5 points, within the inherent reproducibility of the technique.", "D": "Ejection fraction fell more than 10 points to below 50 percent, a cardiotoxicity threshold worth reporting."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ejection fraction fell more than 10 points to below 50 percent, a cardiotoxicity threshold worth reporting.): The exhibit shows a fall from 61 to 43 percent, an 18-point decline to below 50 percent, which meets the usual cardiotoxicity definition of a drop of more than 10 points to under 50 percent. The reproducibility of radionuclide ventriculography is about 5 points, so this change is real. Cardiotoxicity criteria are based on left ventricular ejection fraction.", "image": "question_images/q_245.png"}, {"id": 246, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A physicist sets a 20 percent window centered on 140 keV for Tc-99m SPECT, yet scattered photons still reduce image contrast in obese patients. A fellow asks which statement best explains why the window does not remove them. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Narrowing the window to 10 percent removes all scatter with no count penalty.", "B": "Scatter is absent for Tc-99m, because photoelectric absorption dominates at 140 keV in soft tissue.", "C": "Photons scattered through small angles lose little energy, so many still fall inside the window.", "D": "Compton scatter raises photon energy, which pushes scattered photons above the window into the image."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Photons scattered through small angles lose little energy, so many still fall inside the window.): At 140 keV, Compton scatter dominates in soft tissue and the scattered photon always loses energy, with small angle events losing only a few keV. Crystal energy resolution is roughly 9 to 10 percent, so these photons overlap the photopeak and cannot be separated. A narrower window trims some scatter but also removes primary photons."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "In a same-day Tc-99m sestamibi protocol, a 55-year-old woman received a rest dose of 9 mCi at 08:00. She is to receive the stress injection 60 minutes later. Residual rest activity will still be present in the myocardium. What stress dose best satisfies the usual dose ratio of at least 3 to 1?", "options": {"A": "About 9 mCi, the same activity as the first dose, since tracer decays over an hour", "B": "About 45 mCi, a five to one ratio chosen to remove all residual rest activity", "C": "About 18 mCi, a two to one ratio that suffices when both images are read together", "D": "About 27 mCi, which keeps stress counts roughly three times the residual rest activity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 27 mCi, which keeps stress counts roughly three times the residual rest activity): A same-day protocol uses a lower first dose of 8 to 10 mCi and a second dose of 24 to 30 mCi, a 3 to 1 ratio, so the stress counts swamp the residual first-study activity. Nine times three is 27 mCi. A 2 to 1 ratio leaves too much contamination, an equal dose gives no separation, and 45 mCi adds radiation with little gain."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A stress SPECT study shows a reversible defect in segments 3, 4, 9, 10 and 15, which are the basal inferoseptal, basal inferior, mid inferoseptal, mid inferior and apical inferior walls. Coronary angiography in this patient shows a left dominant system. Which vessel is the likely culprit, and what fraction of the 17 segment model is involved?", "options": {"A": "Distal left anterior descending artery wrapping the apex; 5 of 17 segments, about 29 percent.", "B": "Distal left circumflex via a posterior descending branch; 5 of 16 segments, about 31 percent.", "C": "Distal right coronary artery; 5 of 17 segments, about 29 percent of the myocardium.", "D": "Distal left circumflex via posterior descending; 5 of 17 segments, about 29 percent."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Distal left circumflex via posterior descending; 5 of 17 segments, about 29 percent.): In a left dominant circulation the circumflex gives rise to the posterior descending artery, so the inferior and inferoseptal walls fall in the circumflex territory. Five segments divided by 17 equals 0.294, or about 29 percent. A right coronary assignment is the default for a right dominant system and fails here, apical wrap would not produce basal and mid inferior involvement, and a 16 segment denominator belongs to the older model."}, {"id": 249, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The chart shows global myocardial blood flow from a Rb-82 dynamic PET in a 63-year-old diabetic patient with chest pain, measured at rest and during regadenoson stress. Based on the chart values, what is the coronary flow reserve and its meaning?", "options": {"A": "About 0.6, below 1.0, indicating coronary steal during regadenoson stress.", "B": "About 1.7, below the usual 2.0 threshold, indicating reduced flow reserve.", "C": "About 0.8, the stress minus rest difference, which is a normal flow reserve.", "D": "About 3.0, the sum of rest and stress flow, which is clearly normal."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 1.7, below the usual 2.0 threshold, indicating reduced flow reserve.): Coronary flow reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow, 1.9 divided by 1.1, about 1.7. Values below 2.0 suggest impaired reserve and are seen with diffuse epicardial disease or microvascular disease. The ratio is not a difference or a sum.", "image": "question_images/q_249.png"}, {"id": 250, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with exertional angina has a regadenoson SPECT with homogeneous perfusion, yet 2 mm of ST depression with hypotension, a post-stress ejection fraction fall from 56 to 44 percent and transient dilation of 1.3. Which protocol choice best addresses the concern?", "options": {"A": "Repeat SPECT with dobutamine, since vasodilators cannot uncover multivessel coronary disease.", "B": "Consider PET with flow quantification, which can unmask balanced ischemia that relative perfusion misses.", "C": "Call the study normal and attribute the ECG changes to a false positive response in men.", "D": "Add attenuation correction to the same study, since attenuation limited relative perfusion sensitivity."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Consider PET with flow quantification, which can unmask balanced ischemia that relative perfusion misses.): Relative perfusion images normalize to the highest-uptake region, so uniform reduction from multivessel disease can look normal. The ECG change, hypotension, stunning and dilation are warnings of balanced ischemia. PET with absolute flow and flow reserve can detect globally reduced flow. Attenuation correction and dobutamine do not remove the limitation of relative uptake."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit shows a same-day rest-stress sestamibi protocol with a laboratory policy of at least 9.0 million myocardial counts per study. Dose and decay for the rest study are fixed. What acquisition time per projection would bring the rest study to policy, and is the stress study acceptable?", "options": {"A": "72 seconds per projection, because the deficit in millions of counts equals extra seconds", "B": "30 seconds per projection, because a 50 percent time increase is needed for any low study", "C": "About 33 seconds per projection, since counts scale with time, and stress already meets policy", "D": "12 seconds per projection, since the ratio of recorded counts to policy sets the time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 33 seconds per projection, since counts scale with time, and stress already meets policy): The rest study recorded 5.4 million counts at 20 seconds, so reaching 9.0 million needs 20 times 9.0 over 5.4, about 33 seconds per projection. Stress counts of 14.6 million already exceed the policy. The other values invert the ratio, subtract counts, or use an arbitrary increase.", "image": "question_images/q_251.png"}, {"id": 252, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man with diabetes has exertional dyspnea. Vasodilator Tc-99m SPECT shows normal relative perfusion, but ejection fraction falls from 58 percent at rest to 44 percent after stress, and his calcium score is 1,420. Which statement best explains the apparent conflict?", "options": {"A": "A calcium score of 1,420 invalidates attenuation correction, so perfusion images must be repeated first.", "B": "Normal perfusion proves calcium is nonobstructive, so the function fall reflects gated imaging noise.", "C": "Diabetes causes false post-stress function decline, so function data are discarded when perfusion is normal.", "D": "Relative uptake can miss balanced multivessel disease, and falling function with calcium suggests extensive disease."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Relative uptake can miss balanced multivessel disease, and falling function with calcium suggests extensive disease.): Relative perfusion imaging normalizes to the hottest region, so balanced reduction of flow in all territories can give a normal appearance. A large fall in ejection fraction after stress and a heavy calcium burden point toward extensive coronary disease, and quantitative PET flow reserve or angiography should be considered. The other statements are not accepted principles."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old woman had dyspnea and a negative ECG. Regadenoson sestamibi SPECT is scored with 17 segments. The summed stress score is 13 and every rest segment scores 0. The reader needs the difference score and the burden as a share of the maximum of 68. What is the correct result?", "options": {"A": "The summed difference score is 13, about 19 percent of the myocardium, a large ischemic burden.", "B": "The summed difference score is 13, about 13 percent of the myocardium, a moderate burden.", "C": "The summed difference score is 0, because the rest score is zero, so ischemia is absent.", "D": "The summed difference score is 13, about 19 percent of the myocardium, a fixed scar burden."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The summed difference score is 13, about 19 percent of the myocardium, a large ischemic burden.): The summed difference score is the stress score minus the rest score, which is 13 minus 0, or 13. Divided by the maximum score of 68, 13 is about 19 percent, which exceeds the 10 percent threshold usually considered high risk. A zero rest score means the defect is fully reversible, so it is ischemia and not scar. Treating 13 as 13 percent confuses the score with a percentage, and using the rest score alone ignores the stress defect.", "image": "question_images/q_253.png"}, {"id": 254, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 47-year-old man on doxorubicin has a gated blood pool study; the time-activity curve in the exhibit is already background-corrected. The laboratory lower limit of normal is 50 percent. Raw counts are 19,550 at end-diastole and 8,467 at end-systole, with 1,325 background counts in both. Which EF should be reported?", "options": {"A": "An EF of 61 percent from background-corrected counts, which is normal for this laboratory.", "B": "A 39 percent EF from end-systolic divided by end-diastolic counts, which is below normal.", "C": "An EF of 44 percent using the summed counts as the denominator, below normal.", "D": "A 57 percent EF from the raw counts, which is normal but slightly underestimates function."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An EF of 61 percent from background-corrected counts, which is normal for this laboratory.): Corrected counts are 18,225 and 7,142, so the stroke counts are 11,083 and the EF is 11,083 divided by 18,225, or 61 percent. The raw calculation yields 57 percent because background inflates both counts, the ES to ED ratio is the residual fraction rather than the ejection fraction, and a summed denominator gives 44 percent.", "image": "question_images/q_254.png"}, {"id": 255, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A stress-first Tc-99m sestamibi study in a 54-year-old man with a normal EF shows a moderate fixed-appearing inferior defect on supine images. Prone imaging reduces but does not abolish it, and attenuation-corrected images are not available. What should the physician do next?", "options": {"A": "Report the study as normal because prone imaging reduced the defect, and skip the rest dose", "B": "Repeat the stress injection the same day at a higher dose to improve inferior wall counts", "C": "Report probable infarct without rest imaging because the defect looks fixed on the stress images", "D": "Proceed to rest imaging, since a defect persisting after prone imaging cannot be called normal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed to rest imaging, since a defect persisting after prone imaging cannot be called normal): A stress-first approach avoids the rest dose and its radiation only when stress images are clearly normal or the defect is confidently attributed to attenuation. A defect that persists after prone imaging needs a rest study to separate ischemia, scar and artifact. Calling the study normal or reporting infarct without rest images both exceed what the stress data support. A second stress injection adds radiation without answering the question."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT, and the slices show a small inferior defect that looks the same at stress and rest. Her summed stress score is 7 and the summed rest score is 7, so the difference score is 0. How should the difference score be handled in the report?", "options": {"A": "Report a zero difference as severe ischemia, since the inferior wall is abnormal on both rows.", "B": "Report the summed rest score of 7 as the ischemic burden of the inferior wall.", "C": "Treat a difference of 0 as no ischemia; report a fixed inferior defect.", "D": "Treat a zero difference as proof the rest study failed and repeat the rest acquisition."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat a difference of 0 as no ischemia; report a fixed inferior defect.): The difference score is the stress score minus the rest score, so 7 minus 7 is 0. A zero difference means no ischemia, and the matching small inferior defect is a fixed defect, consistent with scar. The rest score of 7 is the scar burden, not ischemia, and a matching rest study is an expected finding that needs no repeat.", "image": "question_images/q_256.png"}, {"id": 257, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old woman on trastuzumab has about 15 PVCs per minute and a poor echo window, and serial EF values will decide whether therapy continues. She is otherwise in sinus rhythm. Which MUGA acquisition approach best preserves the accuracy of the EF measurement?", "options": {"A": "Use list mode or beat length windowing that rejects ectopic and post-ectopic beats.", "B": "Use a first-pass study with a 5 mCi injection, because gating then becomes unnecessary.", "C": "Add 64 frames per beat, since more frames average out ectopy.", "D": "Accept all beats, since averaging every cycle gives the truest mean EF."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use list mode or beat length windowing that rejects ectopic and post-ectopic beats.): Ectopic and post-ectopic beats have abnormal filling and ejection, and including them smears the composite cycle and lowers the apparent EF. List mode or a beat length acceptance window keeps beats near the mean R-R interval and excludes the rest. More frames do not cure arrhythmia, and first pass requires a tight bolus rather than a gated cycle."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man with Mobitz type II second-degree AV block and no pacemaker has exertional dyspnea and needs ischemia evaluation. He cannot walk because of severe peripheral artery disease in both legs. He has no chest pain at rest today. Which pharmacologic stress choice is best?", "options": {"A": "Dipyridamole, because aminophylline can reverse AV block promptly when it appears during the infusion.", "B": "Dobutamine, because vasodilators carry risk of advanced AV block and he has no pacemaker.", "C": "Adenosine, because its ten-second half-life makes any block brief and fully reversible at the bedside.", "D": "Regadenoson, because A2A selectivity spares AV nodal conduction even in patients with advanced AV block."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dobutamine, because vasodilators carry risk of advanced AV block and he has no pacemaker.): Second or third degree AV block and sinus node dysfunction without a functioning pacemaker are labeled contraindications to regadenoson and adenosine, and dipyridamole shares the risk. Dobutamine does not act on adenosine receptors and is the better pharmacologic option, with attention to arrhythmia monitoring."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist draws the standard 32 mCi stress dose of Tc-99m sestamibi for a 48 kg woman with a BMI of 20. The technologist prepared it before checking the weight field in the chart. The laboratory has a weight-based protocol. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Give the standard dose and cut the stress acquisition time to reduce patient motion artifact.", "B": "Increase the dose for the small heart, since small hearts need extra counts to resolve defects.", "C": "Reduce the activity per the weight-based protocol, since counts stay adequate and dose falls.", "D": "Give the standard adult dose, since a larger dose gives better counts and shorter scans."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce the activity per the weight-based protocol, since counts stay adequate and dose falls.): A small patient has less attenuation, so adequate counts are reached with less activity. Scaling to weight lowers radiation dose, which is consistent with ALARA, and keeps image quality. A fixed standard dose gives needless exposure, and small heart size does not raise activity needs."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The chart shows estimated effective dose from Tc-99m sestamibi by total injected activity. A stress-first protocol uses 10 mCi for stress and 30 mCi for rest if needed. By how much does omitting the rest study lower the estimated dose?", "options": {"A": "About 3 mSv, the value read at the stress dose alone.", "B": "About 13 mSv, the entire dose of the full protocol.", "C": "About 10 mSv, roughly a 75 percent reduction from 13 mSv.", "D": "About 7 mSv, a 50 percent reduction from the full protocol."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 10 mSv, roughly a 75 percent reduction from 13 mSv.): The full protocol is 40 mCi, which the chart gives as about 13 mSv, and the stress dose alone at 10 mCi is about 3 mSv. Omitting rest therefore saves roughly 10 mSv, around 75 percent. This dose saving is a major reason to use stress-first imaging in patients with a normal stress result.", "image": "question_images/q_260.png"}, {"id": 261, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man with a left bundle branch block on his ECG and chest tightness is referred for repeat perfusion imaging. An outside study reported a mild septal defect, stress score 9 and rest score 5, with other walls normal, suspected to be a false positive. Which stress choice for the new study best avoids this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Use dobutamine infusion, because it mimics exercise and reliably leaves the septum unaffected.", "B": "Use a vasodilator such as regadenoson, because it raises heart rate little.", "C": "Perform treadmill exercise to a target heart rate, because exercise gives the best prognostic information.", "D": "Use exercise with a slow ramp protocol, because the lower rate removes any septal artifact."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a vasodilator such as regadenoson, because it raises heart rate little.): In left bundle branch block, septal perfusion defects arise mainly from abnormal septal activation, and they grow with faster heart rates. A mild isolated septal defect with partial rest improvement and normal other walls fits that artifact. Vasodilator stress raises the rate only modestly, so it produces fewer false positive septal defects than exercise or dobutamine. A slower exercise ramp still raises the rate substantially, and dobutamine is a strong chronotropic stressor. Exercise remains valuable for functional data, but it is the less specific choice here.", "image": "question_images/q_261.png"}, {"id": 262, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit shows today's dose calibrator constancy check, performed before the first Tc-99m sestamibi dose of the day in a busy nuclear cardiology laboratory. Four patient doses are scheduled within the next hour, and the physician is waiting to begin. What is the correct response to this result?", "options": {"A": "Raise each patient dose by the observed deviation and document it, with no repeat measurement.", "B": "Repeat the reading and, if confirmed, stop using the calibrator for patient doses until corrected.", "C": "Repeat the reading with the Tc-99m setting and average the two values as the correction.", "D": "Record the result and continue dispensing, since constancy deviations matter only above twenty percent."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the reading and, if confirmed, stop using the calibrator for patient doses until corrected.): The reading is about 12 percent below the decay-corrected reference, outside the usual plus or minus 10 percent tolerance. A repeat confirms the result and rules out a handling error. A confirmed failure means the instrument cannot be used for patient dosages until it is repaired, replaced or has a validated correction. Applying a quick offset or averaging is not acceptable.", "image": "question_images/q_262.png"}, {"id": 263, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with risk factors presents with new dyspnea, a dilated left ventricle and an echocardiographic ejection fraction of 28 percent. He has no angina and is otherwise stable. Which approach best fits appropriate use for a new cardiomyopathy of unknown cause?", "options": {"A": "Stress myocardial perfusion imaging to look for ischemic cause and extent of viable myocardium.", "B": "Repeat echocardiography in three months, since ejection fraction may recover without ischemia testing.", "C": "Resting FDG alone without a perfusion comparison, since glucose uptake identifies the cause.", "D": "No further ischemia testing, since heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is not an indication."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stress myocardial perfusion imaging to look for ischemic cause and extent of viable myocardium.): Newly diagnosed heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and risk factors makes ischemic evaluation appropriate, and perfusion imaging can also assess viability and prognosis. Skipping testing misses treatable disease. FDG interpretation requires a perfusion comparison. Waiting delays potentially revascularizable disease."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A new radiation safety officer finds the storage room full before an NRC inspection and must clear space. Files include dose calibrator constancy logs from five years ago, personnel dose monitoring records for former employees, and survey records from four years ago. What is the correct retention approach?", "options": {"A": "Keep every record indefinitely, because the NRC permits no disposal of any record at any time", "B": "Discard personnel exposure records after 5 years but keep calibrator logs for a full 10 years", "C": "Discard all records older than 3 years, including the personnel exposure records for former employees", "D": "Discard calibrator logs and surveys older than 3 years; keep personnel exposure records until license ends"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discard calibrator logs and surveys older than 3 years; keep personnel exposure records until license ends): Most routine quality and survey records, including dose calibrator checks and radiation surveys, need to be kept for 3 years. Records of individual occupational exposure must be retained until the NRC terminates the license. Discarding exposure records at 3 or 5 years violates this, and keeping everything forever is wasteful rather than required."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old woman on trastuzumab for breast cancer undergoes a gated blood pool study; the left ventricular time-activity curve in the exhibit is already background-corrected. The laboratory lower limit of normal is 50 percent. Raw counts are 19,200 at end-diastole and 9,828 at end-systole, with 1,557 background counts in each. What is the best reported result?", "options": {"A": "EF of 49 percent from the raw counts, which falls below normal and warrants holding therapy.", "B": "EF of 36 percent using the sum of both counts as the denominator, indicating cardiotoxicity.", "C": "EF of 47 percent from end-systolic divided by end-diastolic counts, indicating mildly reduced function.", "D": "EF of 53 percent from background-corrected counts, which is within the laboratory normal range."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (EF of 53 percent from background-corrected counts, which is within the laboratory normal range.): After subtracting 1,557 background counts, end-diastole is 17,643 and end-systole is 8,271. Stroke counts are 9,372, giving 9,372 divided by 17,643, about 53 percent, above the 50 percent limit. Using raw counts gives 49 percent, and the other two errors come from the wrong ratio or denominator.", "image": "question_images/q_265.png"}, {"id": 266, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 59-year-old man drank two cups of coffee 4 hours before a regadenoson SPECT and did not mention it when screened by the technologist. Regadenoson is given and the images are acquired on schedule without any other problem. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Prolonged AV block, because caffeine slows the metabolism of regadenoson.", "B": "Heightened bronchospasm, because caffeine increases adenosine receptor stimulation.", "C": "Blunted vasodilation from adenosine receptor blockade, risking a false negative study.", "D": "Increased tracer uptake in the liver, which interferes with inferior wall interpretation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blunted vasodilation from adenosine receptor blockade, risking a false negative study.): Caffeine and other methylxanthines block adenosine receptors, so regadenoson produces less coronary hyperemia and ischemia can be underestimated. Patients should avoid caffeine for at least 12 hours. Caffeine does not stimulate the receptor or slow regadenoson metabolism."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man with diabetes and atypical chest pain exercises for 9 METs and reaches 93 percent of predicted heart rate without ST changes. Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT gives an ejection fraction of 58 percent and summed stress and rest scores of 0. What does this study predict, and what is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Normal perfusion, but annual risk near 2 percent, so repeat SPECT imaging within six months.", "B": "Normal perfusion, but annual risk of 3 to 5 percent in diabetes, so refer for angiography.", "C": "A normal study excludes coronary atherosclerosis, so aspirin and statin therapy can be stopped now.", "D": "Normal perfusion predicts annual cardiac death or infarction below 1 percent, so no further testing."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Normal perfusion predicts annual cardiac death or infarction below 1 percent, so no further testing.): A normal stress perfusion study with summed scores of 0, a preserved ejection fraction and good exercise capacity carries a very low annual risk of cardiac death or myocardial infarction, below 1 percent. SPECT detects flow-limiting disease, not plaque, so risk factor treatment continues. Diabetes lowers the warranty period somewhat but does not justify angiography or repeat imaging within six months.", "image": "question_images/q_267.png"}, {"id": 268, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 28-year-old woman asks about harm after a same-day rest and stress Tc-99m sestamibi study, which delivered an effective dose of about 10 mSv. She is not pregnant, has no symptoms of illness and asks what to expect. What outcome should the physician describe?", "options": {"A": "No biologic effect, since doses below the 50 mSv occupational limit carry no risk to patients.", "B": "Transient bone marrow suppression requiring serial blood counts in the weeks after the study.", "C": "Skin redness and temporary hair loss in the weeks after the study from radiation injury.", "D": "A small stochastic rise in lifetime cancer risk and no deterministic tissue effects."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A small stochastic rise in lifetime cancer risk and no deterministic tissue effects.): At about 10 mSv, deterministic effects such as erythema or marrow suppression, which need doses in the gray range, are not expected. Stochastic effects, mainly cancer, are assumed to rise with dose without a threshold, so a small added risk is accepted when the study is justified. Occupational limits are not thresholds of safety."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man has vasodilator SPECT. Perfusion shows mild reversible anterior and lateral reductions. TID is 1.30 and EF falls from 58 to 44 percent after stress. The ECG shows 2 mm diffuse ST depression with hypotension. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for coronary angiography for probable left main or three vessel disease despite mild defects.", "B": "Repeat with exercise stress next week to confirm before any further decisions are made.", "C": "Report intermediate risk and add a calcium score to refine the decision on angiography.", "D": "Treat as separate LAD and circumflex ischemia and start medical therapy with outpatient follow up."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for coronary angiography for probable left main or three vessel disease despite mild defects.): Left main disease often produces modest or balanced defects across LAD and circumflex territories, but the high risk markers are TID, a post stress EF fall, diffuse ST depression and hypotension. Together they favor angiography. Mild defects alone would suggest otherwise, which is the trap. Repeat testing delays care and a calcium score cannot answer the question of flow limitation."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A medical physicist calculates that a cohabiting adult could receive an estimated effective dose of 2.1 mSv from a patient who has just completed Rb-82 and Tc-99m cardiac imaging. The patient asks whether she may go home. Under NRC patient release rules, what is the correct decision?", "options": {"A": "Hold her for admission because release needs a calculated dose to others below 0.5 mSv", "B": "Release her with written instructions, since the estimate exceeds 1 mSv but not 5 mSv", "C": "Hold her until the dose to others is under 1 mSv, since larger values bar release", "D": "Release her without instructions because 2.1 mSv is under the 5 mSv release limit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Release her with written instructions, since the estimate exceeds 1 mSv but not 5 mSv): The release criterion is that the dose to any other individual is unlikely to exceed 5 mSv (0.5 rem), and written instructions are required when the dose to another person is likely to exceed 1 mSv (0.1 rem). At 2.1 mSv both conditions apply, so she is released with written instructions. Treating 1 mSv as the barrier or skipping instructions misreads one of the two thresholds."}, {"id": 271, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A nuclear cardiology lab is considering replacing an aging NaI gamma camera with a dedicated cardiac CZT camera for myocardial perfusion imaging. Which statement describes a genuine benefit of CZT detectors for this purpose, compared with the conventional camera in use?", "options": {"A": "Whole-body field of view allows simultaneous imaging of the chest and abdomen in one acquisition", "B": "Higher sensitivity and better energy resolution allow shorter acquisitions or a lower administered activity", "C": "Lower cost per detector and a simpler energy calibration process than conventional cameras", "D": "A thicker crystal removes the need for attenuation correction in large patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Higher sensitivity and better energy resolution allow shorter acquisitions or a lower administered activity): Cadmium zinc telluride converts photons directly to charge, giving better energy resolution than NaI crystals with photomultiplier tubes, and dedicated cardiac designs collect many more counts per unit dose. That allows a shorter acquisition or reduced dose. Dedicated cardiac cameras have a small field of view, they still need attenuation assessment, and they are more costly than conventional detectors."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with hypertension has exertional dyspnea. Regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT shows an ejection fraction of 58 percent, with a summed stress score of 3, a summed rest score of 2 and a difference score of 1. Which statement best describes the findings and their clinical meaning?", "options": {"A": "A fixed mid lateral scar with no ischemic component, so no further testing is needed.", "B": "Lateral wall artifact from adjacent hepatic activity, so the study should be repeated after oral water.", "C": "A mild mid lateral defect partly improving at rest: residual scar, small ischemic component.", "D": "A mid lateral defect fully normalizing at rest, so pure right coronary ischemia."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A mild mid lateral defect partly improving at rest: residual scar, small ischemic component.): The mid lateral segments are mildly reduced at stress and modestly better at rest, so a small residual defect persists as scar and a small reversible part remains in the circumflex territory. Scores of 3 and 2 with a difference score of 1 describe a limited study. The right coronary artery does not supply the lateral wall. A fixed scar alone would show no change at all. Hepatic activity affects the inferior wall near the liver, not the lateral wall.", "image": "question_images/q_272.png"}, {"id": 273, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A SPECT acquisition shows upward creep of 14 mm in total across the sinogram. The detector pixel size is 6.4 mm. A technologist asks how much drift this is and what to do. Which answer is best? The study used a standard dual-detector camera.", "options": {"A": "About 90 pixels of drift, far too large, so cancel the study and reschedule", "B": "About two pixels of upward drift; apply motion correction and recheck slices", "C": "About two pixels of drift, so discard the data and repeat the full stress study", "D": "About half a pixel of drift, negligible, so report without any review of slices"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About two pixels of upward drift; apply motion correction and recheck slices): Drift in pixels equals distance divided by pixel size: 14 mm / 6.4 mm = about 2.2 pixels. That is a small gradual shift that correction software generally handles, followed by a check of the corrected slices. Multiplying gives 90, dividing the other way gives half a pixel, and repeating a full study for a correctable drift wastes radiation and time.", "image": "question_images/q_273.png"}, {"id": 274, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old woman has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT, and the slices show an inferior and inferoseptal defect that looks the same at stress and rest. The image is scored 0 to 4 across 17 segments. The summed stress score is 10 and the summed rest score is 10. Which statement about the difference score and what it means is correct?", "options": {"A": "The difference score is 10, indicating extensive right coronary ischemia that needs angiography.", "B": "Difference is 0, so the abnormality is fixed scar; risk follows infarct size and EF.", "C": "The difference score is 20, indicating severe ischemia because the stress and rest scores are combined.", "D": "The difference score is 0, so myocardial perfusion is normal and no follow-up is needed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Difference is 0, so the abnormality is fixed scar; risk follows infarct size and EF.): The difference score is the stress score minus the rest score, so 10 minus 10 is 0. A defect that is the same on both rows is fixed, which means scar, here in the right coronary territory. Prognosis then follows the size of the infarct, about 15 percent of the ventricle by the rest score, and the EF. Taking the stress score as ischemia confuses scar with ischemia, adding the scores has no meaning, and a score of 10 is not normal.", "image": "question_images/q_274.png"}, {"id": 275, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory runs a same-day dual-isotope protocol, injecting rest thallium-201 and imaging it, then giving Tc-99m sestamibi for stress. The director asks you to review the plan. Compared with a one-day Tc-99m rest and stress protocol, which is a recognized drawback of the dual-isotope approach?", "options": {"A": "A mandatory 4-hour wait between rest and stress studies because thallium-201 has a six-hour physical half-life.", "B": "Thallium-201 cannot show viable myocardium, so rest defects cannot be characterized as scar or hibernation.", "C": "Higher patient radiation dose and softer, lower-count thallium-201 images with more attenuation artifact.", "D": "Tc-99m photons downscatter into the thallium images, since rest imaging precedes the Tc-99m injection and persists."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Higher patient radiation dose and softer, lower-count thallium-201 images with more attenuation artifact.): Thallium-201 gives a higher effective dose per study and its low-energy photons cause lower count density and more attenuation and scatter. Because thallium is imaged before the Tc-99m dose, downscatter does not affect rest images in this order. The protocol is quick, and thallium is a classic viability agent."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A vial held 30 mCi of Tc-99m in 5 mL at 07:00, with decay shown in the chart. For the dosage record, what volume must be drawn at 11:30 to give a prescribed 8 mCi, using the activity at that time?", "options": {"A": "About 2.7 mL, taken from the 15 mCi value at the last point on the chart", "B": "About 0.45 mL, found by dividing 8 mCi by the vial activity at 11:30", "C": "About 1.3 mL, taken from the 6 mCi per milliliter concentration at 07:00", "D": "About 2.2 mL, since the vial held about 17.9 mCi in 5 mL at 11:30"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 2.2 mL, since the vial held about 17.9 mCi in 5 mL at 11:30): From 07:00 to 11:30 is 4.5 hours, and 30 mCi times 2 to the minus 4.5 over 6.01 is about 17.9 mCi, consistent with the chart between the 4 hour and 5 hour points. The concentration is 17.9 divided by 5, or 3.6 mCi/mL, so 8 mCi needs 8 divided by 3.6, or about 2.2 mL. Using the calibration time concentration gives 1.3 mL, reading the last chart point gives 2.7 mL, and ignoring the volume gives 0.45.", "image": "question_images/q_276.png"}, {"id": 277, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Six minutes into recovery from treadmill exercise, a patient has persistent 2 mm horizontal ST depression in V4 to V6, heart rate 96, blood pressure 140/80, and resolved chest discomfort. Tracer was injected at peak. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Continue ECG monitoring until the ST depression resolves, then review symptoms and images before discharge", "B": "Send the patient to the emergency department by ambulance without any further monitoring in the laboratory", "C": "Give aminophylline to reverse the exercise-related ST depression and then release the patient from the lab", "D": "Discharge now, since ST changes after exercise are expected and perfusion imaging will clarify them later"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue ECG monitoring until the ST depression resolves, then review symptoms and images before discharge): ST depression that persists in recovery reflects ischemia and is a high-risk finding, so the patient is kept on monitoring until the tracing returns to baseline. Symptoms and images are then reviewed. Early discharge ignores the finding, aminophylline antagonizes vasodilators rather than exercise, and immediate transfer is unnecessary when she is stable and symptom-free."}, {"id": 278, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "After a spill, a technologist surveys with a Geiger-Muller meter near a vial holding very high activity. The needle climbs, then falls to nearly zero as the probe is moved closer, and she concludes the vial is empty. What explains this finding?", "options": {"A": "Backscatter from the bench raises the reading at distance and lowers it up close", "B": "The lead pig is shielding the probe, so the reading reflects its shielding thickness", "C": "Decay runs faster at close range, so the reading drops as the probe nears the source", "D": "Saturation in an intense field can make a GM meter read low; use an ion chamber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Saturation in an intense field can make a GM meter read low; use an ion chamber): Geiger-Muller counters have a long dead time, and in a very intense field they can fail to register events and read falsely low or even zero. An ion chamber survey meter handles high exposure rates and should be used when a reading behaves like this. Decay does not depend on distance, and no shield was described between probe and vial."}, {"id": 279, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "An N-13 ammonia PET study in a 67-year-old woman shows a new anterior wall defect that is not seen on the registration check of her previous study. The fused image shows the anterior wall overlapping the CT lung field. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report a fixed anterior defect as scar, since a corrected image defect reflects true perfusion loss.", "B": "Repeat the study the next day with a second ammonia dose to obtain fresh images.", "C": "Realign the emission data to the CT attenuation map manually, then reprocess and reread.", "D": "Report the study using uncorrected images only, since attenuation correction is optional in PET."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Realign the emission data to the CT attenuation map manually, then reprocess and reread.): A new defect in a territory that overlaps lung on the fusion display points to misregistration between emission and CT. Manual realignment followed by reprocessing usually removes the artifact. Calling scar without checking registration risks a false diagnosis, a repeat study adds radiation, and uncorrected PET images are not an accepted routine alternative."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Rest SPECT was reconstructed by filtered backprojection with a ramp-Butterworth filter. It shows reduced inferior wall counts next to intense hepatic uptake, with radial streaks, while the projection images are clean and the patient did not move. Which reprocessing approach is best in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Reconstruct with the ramp filter alone, to keep quantitative count accuracy near the liver", "B": "Raise the Butterworth cutoff frequency so the sharper image shows the inferior wall better", "C": "Reconstruct with iterative OSEM, which limits streak spread from the hot focus, then reread", "D": "Apply uniform Chang attenuation correction to remove the streaks from the reconstructed slices"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reconstruct with iterative OSEM, which limits streak spread from the hot focus, then reread): Filtered backprojection with a ramp filter produces negative lobes and radial streaks around a very hot extracardiac focus, which depress adjacent wall counts. Iterative reconstruction models the data differently and limits streak spread. A sharper window amplifies streaks, Chang correction addresses attenuation rather than streaks, and the unwindowed ramp is the source of the artifact."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A dual-head camera passed its quarterly center of rotation test last week. This morning a technologist acquires an intrinsic Tc-99m flood on the head used for 90 degree cardiac orbits, and the exhibit shows the result. The next patient is a 58-year-old man for rest-stress tetrofosmin SPECT. What does this flood allow you to conclude about reconstructed patient images?", "options": {"A": "The flood is acceptable, but the uniformity correction map must be rebuilt before the patient scan.", "B": "No focal or global defect is present, so uniformity adds no ring artifact to reconstruction.", "C": "Texture indicates an off-peak energy window, so SPECT counts will be lost to scatter correction.", "D": "A mild gradient is present, so reconstructed slices will carry a ring artifact near the center."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No focal or global defect is present, so uniformity adds no ring artifact to reconstruction.): With a uniform flood, the detector sensitivity is even across the field, so back projection does not turn a local sensitivity difference into a ring in the slices. Apparent mild texture is Poisson noise and does not predict an artifact. An off-peak window would cause a global gradient or sensitivity change, which is not seen. Rebuilding a correction map is done on schedule or after a real change, not for a clean flood.", "image": "question_images/q_281.png"}, {"id": 282, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory uses Tl-201 for every stress study because of its high first-pass myocardial extraction. A 52-year-old woman with BMI 38 kg/m2 and large breasts is referred for vasodilator SPECT, and image quality is a concern. What is the main limitation of thallium for her?", "options": {"A": "Thallium has a 6 hour half-life, so high activity is forced and irradiates the myocardium.", "B": "Low-energy photons and limited allowed dose give poor counts and more attenuation in large patients.", "C": "Thallium shows redistribution only after 24 hours, so stress images cannot be acquired same session.", "D": "Thallium has low first-pass extraction, so flow in mild stenoses is underestimated at hyperemic flow rates."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low-energy photons and limited allowed dose give poor counts and more attenuation in large patients.): Thallium emits mercury x-rays of about 69 to 83 keV, which attenuate heavily in soft tissue, and its 73 hour half-life and dosimetry limit injected activity to about 3 mCi, so counts are low. Extraction is high, redistribution occurs over 3 to 4 hours, and those properties are not the problem here. Technetium agents or PET suit larger patients better."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old man with normal relative perfusion on Rb-82 PET has a global rest MBF of 0.85 mL/min/g and a global stress MBF of 2.55 mL/min/g after regadenoson. He has no known coronary disease. How should his flow reserve be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Reserve is 1.7, which is mildly reduced and borderline for the ischemia threshold in this setting.", "B": "Reserve is 0.33, which is severely reduced and abnormal for a man of this age.", "C": "Reserve cannot be interpreted with Rb-82, since reserve is valid only when measured with N-13 ammonia.", "D": "Reserve is 3.0, which is normal and argues against significant epicardial or microvascular disease."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reserve is 3.0, which is normal and argues against significant epicardial or microvascular disease.): Flow reserve equals stress flow divided by rest flow, 2.55 over 0.85, which is 3.0. Values above about 2.0 are generally normal, so the result is reassuring. Subtracting the two flows is not reserve, and reserve can be measured with Rb-82, N-13 ammonia or F-18 flurpiridaz."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A used Tc-99m generator is repackaged for return to the manufacturer. The surface dose rate is 0.2 mSv/h (20 mrem/h) and the dose rate at 1 m is 0.008 mSv/h (0.8 mrem/h). Which shipping label and transport index apply to this package?", "options": {"A": "Radioactive Yellow-II with a transport index of 0.2, taken from the surface reading in mSv/h", "B": "Radioactive White-I, because the transport index is below 1 at one meter", "C": "Radioactive Yellow-III, because the transport index equals the 20 mrem/h surface reading on the label", "D": "Radioactive Yellow-II with a transport index of 0.8, taken from the 1 meter reading in mrem/h"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Radioactive Yellow-II with a transport index of 0.8, taken from the 1 meter reading in mrem/h): The category depends on both numbers. A surface level above 0.005 mSv/h rules out White-I, while a surface level up to 0.5 mSv/h and a transport index of 1 or less gives Yellow-II. The transport index is the dose rate at 1 m in mrem/h, here 0.8. Yellow-III needs a surface level above 0.5 mSv/h or an index above 1."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old woman arrives for vasodilator SPECT for exertional dyspnea. Before the test her heart rate is 108 beats per minute, blood pressure 92/58, with bibasilar crackles, jugular venous distension and a new third heart sound. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with low-level treadmill exercise and stop at the first symptom or rise in dyspnea.", "B": "Proceed with regadenoson, since pharmacologic stress does not depend on patient effort or workload capacity.", "C": "Defer stress testing and treat the heart failure first, since decompensation contraindicates stress testing.", "D": "Proceed with dobutamine, since its inotropic effect will relieve the pulmonary congestion and improve cardiac output."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer stress testing and treat the heart failure first, since decompensation contraindicates stress testing.): Resting tachycardia, soft blood pressure, crackles, venous distension and an S3 indicate acute decompensated heart failure, which makes stress testing unsafe and unlikely to change management until she is stabilized. Neither vasodilators nor dobutamine remove that risk."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Stress images acquired 20 minutes after a 30 mCi (1110 MBq) Tc-99m injection are grainy with low myocardial counts. The pre-injection assay was 30 mCi, but the syringe and line still read 14.5 mCi afterward, and the IV site is swollen. The patient has left. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Lower the energy window to 15 percent of the photopeak to admit more counts", "B": "Reinject the full 30 mCi and repeat the pharmacologic stress to restore the original counts", "C": "Reconstruct with a sharper filter to compensate for the lower count density in the myocardium", "D": "Recognize that about half the dose was given and reacquire with longer time per projection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize that about half the dose was given and reacquire with longer time per projection): Residual activity of 14.5 mCi means the patient received roughly 15.5 mCi, about half the planned dose, probably because of infiltration. Tc-99m remains in tissue, so counts can be recovered by roughly doubling the acquisition time per projection. A new injection adds dose, a sharper filter amplifies noise, and narrowing the window lowers counts."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old man with chronic left bundle branch block can walk on a treadmill and is referred for perfusion SPECT to evaluate exertional chest pain, with no wheeze and normal blood pressure. Which stress modality is best for this patient, and why?", "options": {"A": "Dobutamine infusion with atropine because it raises the heart rate gradually and avoids early septal motion", "B": "Resting imaging only because septal defects are expected and cannot be separated from true coronary disease", "C": "Vasodilator stress, since exercise or dobutamine cause rate-related septal defects with this conduction pattern", "D": "Treadmill exercise to full target heart rate to maximize diagnostic sensitivity for multivessel disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Vasodilator stress, since exercise or dobutamine cause rate-related septal defects with this conduction pattern): With left bundle branch block, a fast heart rate causes dyssynchronous septal activation and false septal perfusion defects, so exercise and dobutamine reduce specificity. Vasodilator stress avoids a large heart rate rise and is preferred. Exercise and dobutamine both increase rate, and omitting stress throws away useful ischemia information."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 47-year-old woman with palpitations and vague chest discomfort cannot exercise because of knee arthritis and receives regadenoson with Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT. Ejection fraction is 58 percent. Scoring against a normal database gives a summed stress score of 0 and a summed rest score of 0. Which conclusion is best for the final report?", "options": {"A": "Reduced basal inferoseptal counts represent mild right coronary ischemia, so angiography is recommended.", "B": "Normal perfusion at stress and rest, with lower basal inferoseptal and apical counts being normal variants.", "C": "Equivocal study, so rest thallium reinjection should be added to assess viability in the septum.", "D": "Normal perfusion, but vasodilator stress cannot exclude ischemia, so the study should be repeated with exercise."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal perfusion at stress and rest, with lower basal inferoseptal and apical counts being normal variants.): The stress and rest maps look alike and scoring against a normal database gives zero in every segment, so there is no true perfusion defect. Slightly lower counts at the basal inferoseptum reflect the thin membranous septum, and mild apical thinning is also a recognized variant. Vasodilator stress is a validated method with equal diagnostic accuracy to exercise. Viability testing applies to fixed defects, not to a normal study.", "image": "question_images/q_288.png"}, {"id": 289, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old woman has a resting LVEF of 55 percent. On post-stress gated Tc-99m images 45 minutes after treadmill exercise, the LVEF is 43 percent with new anterior hypokinesis, and the rhythm was regular throughout. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Normal physiologic drop in EF after exercise, so report no abnormality.", "B": "Gating error from arrhythmia, so discard the gated result.", "C": "Post-stress ischemic stunning, indicating severe ischemia and a high-risk study.", "D": "Attenuation artifact, so repeat after attenuation correction before reporting."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Post-stress ischemic stunning, indicating severe ischemia and a high-risk study.): A drop in post-stress EF of about 5 points or more with new wall motion abnormality indicates stunning, which follows severe ischemia and carries a poor prognosis. The rhythm was regular, so gating failure is unlikely, and normal EF rises or stays the same after exercise. Attenuation affects counts, not wall motion."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During regadenoson Rb-82 PET, quantified flow is 3.2 mL/min/g in remote myocardium and 1.9 mL/min/g in the LAD territory, a large difference. Yet the relative uptake images show only about a 10 percent count difference between those regions. Which property of the tracer best explains the small count difference?", "options": {"A": "Positron range blurs the border between the two territories, smoothing the count difference", "B": "Rb-82 redistributes during acquisition, which fills the territory with the lower flow", "C": "The short half-life lowers count statistics in the territory with higher flow", "D": "Extraction falls as flow rises, so counts increase less than flow and compress the contrast"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extraction falls as flow rises, so counts increase less than flow and compress the contrast): Rb-82 first-pass extraction is high at rest but rolls off at hyperemic flows, so tissue uptake rises more slowly than flow. That compresses the count difference between a normal territory and a stenotic territory, which is why absolute flow quantification adds to relative images. Positron range does blur images but would not scale with flow. Rb-82 does not redistribute, and decay noise would not compress contrast systematically."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A reader compares OSEM with filtered back projection for sestamibi SPECT near intense bowel activity, and the protocol committee must set the iteration and subset parameters for the new software. Which statement correctly describes how iterative reconstruction behaves in this processing protocol?", "options": {"A": "Subsets speed convergence, so a few iterations suffice and extra iterations mostly add noise.", "B": "Every added iteration lowers noise and improves resolution, so more iterations are preferred.", "C": "Iterations replace the postfilter, so no smoothing is needed after reconstruction.", "D": "Added subsets raise the radiation dose delivered, so the protocol uses a single subset."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Subsets speed convergence, so a few iterations suffice and extra iterations mostly add noise.): OSEM divides the projections into ordered subsets and updates the image after each, which speeds convergence compared with maximum likelihood methods. Resolution recovers with iterations, but noise grows, so protocols fix a modest iteration count and apply a postfilter. Processing choices do not change the patient dose."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 55-year-old woman on trastuzumab has a rest equilibrium MUGA EF of 58% on processing, but echocardiography the same week gives 38%. The processing review shows the background ROI was drawn lateral to the left ventricle but overlapping the spleen. What is the most likely cause of the discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "Splenic activity inflated the background counts, and an overestimated background raises the calculated EF.", "B": "Background was underestimated, leaving extra counts in the end-systolic region and raising the calculated EF.", "C": "Free pertechnetate in the stomach leaves a high ventricular blood pool, which falsely raises the EF.", "D": "Spleen overlap lowers diastolic counts more than systolic counts, which falsely reduces the calculated EF."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Splenic activity inflated the background counts, and an overestimated background raises the calculated EF.): EF equals end-diastolic minus end-systolic counts divided by end-diastolic minus background counts. Background subtracts equally from the numerator terms, leaving the numerator unchanged, but a too-large background shrinks the denominator, so EF rises. Underestimating it has the opposite effect. Poor labeling raises background activity overall rather than altering one ROI."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman had a large anterior myocardial infarction years ago. Regadenoson sestamibi SPECT is scored with a summed stress score of 17 and a summed rest score of 16. The maximum possible score is 68. What do the scores show?", "options": {"A": "A difference score of 33, with extensive ischemia of about 49 percent of the myocardium.", "B": "A difference score of 17, with ischemia of about 25 percent of the myocardium.", "C": "A difference score of 1, with a large ischemic burden of about 24 percent.", "D": "A difference score of 1, no real ischemia, with fixed scar of about 24 percent."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A difference score of 1, no real ischemia, with fixed scar of about 24 percent.): The difference score is 17 minus 16, or 1, so almost no reversible change exists. The rest score of 16 reflects fixed scar, and 16 divided by 68 is about 24 percent of the myocardium. A large rest score is scar burden, not ischemic burden, and adding the two scores has no physiologic meaning.", "image": "question_images/q_293.png"}, {"id": 294, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "At month-end inventory, a 10 mCi (370 MBq) sealed Co-57 flood source used for gamma camera quality control cannot be found, and the log shows it was last seen two days ago. The technologist has already searched the camera room and the hot lab twice. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Tell the vendor about the loss and amend the log, leaving NRC notification to the vendor.", "B": "Notify the radiation safety officer immediately so the loss can be evaluated and reported if required.", "C": "Keep searching for another week and report only if the source is still missing at audit.", "D": "Order a replacement source and reconcile the record, since sealed sources carry no NRC reporting duty."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Notify the radiation safety officer immediately so the loss can be evaluated and reported if required.): A lost or unaccounted licensed source is a radiation safety event, and the radiation safety officer must be told promptly because that person judges whether the loss meets the reporting thresholds of 10 CFR 20 and notifies the NRC or Agreement State within the required time. Waiting for an audit delays a possibly urgent report. Sealed sources are not exempt from accountability, and the vendor cannot carry the licensee's reporting duty."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "An adult with transposition of the great arteries repaired by atrial switch in childhood has a rest SPECT showing the right-sided ventricle larger and with more intense tracer uptake than the left-sided ventricle, and no angina. How is this best explained?", "options": {"A": "Left ventricular scar makes the right ventricle appear brighter after normalization to the hottest region.", "B": "Right coronary stenosis with reperfusion hyperemia increases tracer uptake in the right ventricle.", "C": "Adaptive hypertrophy of the systemic right ventricle, which carries the systemic workload.", "D": "Extracardiac hepatic activity overlapping the right ventricle through scatter raises its apparent uptake."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Adaptive hypertrophy of the systemic right ventricle, which carries the systemic workload.): After an atrial switch, the morphologic right ventricle supports the systemic circulation, so it hypertrophies and takes up more tracer, much as the left ventricle does in a normal heart. This is expected anatomy and physiology, not necessarily ischemia. Reading the study requires knowing the anatomy, and scatter or normalization effects would not produce a dilated, hypertrophied ventricle."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist reconstructs a rest SPECT with OSEM using 8 iterations and 10 subsets and no post-filter, instead of the laboratory standard of 2 iterations, 10 subsets and a Gaussian post-filter. What is the most likely consequence for the myocardial images?", "options": {"A": "Attenuation correction fails with more iterations and inferior wall counts are then overestimated", "B": "Resolution falls and edges blur because more iterations converge slowly toward the final image", "C": "The images become uniformly smoother, with progressive loss of counts in the apex of the ventricle", "D": "Noise grows with iterations, giving patchy myocardial counts that can mimic mild perfusion defects"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Noise grows with iterations, giving patchy myocardial counts that can mimic mild perfusion defects): Iterative reconstruction converges toward higher resolution with more iterations, but noise also increases, and without post-filtering the myocardial counts become patchy. This heterogeneity can be mistaken for mild defects. The other outcomes reverse convergence, claim smoothing, or link iterations to attenuation correction."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A lactating mother of a 6-month-old needs F-18 FDG PET for viability assessment. She asks the nuclear cardiologist whether she must interrupt breast-feeding under NRC guidance. Staff recall conflicting rules from other agents. Which statement best avoids the common pitfall of applying the wrong agent's rule?", "options": {"A": "Interruption is not required for FDG, but limiting close infant contact for several hours is prudent.", "B": "Interrupt for 24 hours and discard milk, since positron emitters concentrate in breast tissue.", "C": "Interrupt for two weeks, matching the guidance used for Tl-201 and radioiodine.", "D": "Feed normally and skip counseling, since the injected activity is too small to give infant dose."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interruption is not required for FDG, but limiting close infant contact for several hours is prudent.): NRC Regulatory Guide 8.39 does not require interruption of breast-feeding after F-18 FDG, because milk excretion is low and the infant dose from milk is small. External exposure from close contact with the mother is the main concern, so counseling on limiting close contact for several hours is advised. Long interruptions belong to long-lived or highly excreted agents such as radioiodine."}, {"id": 298, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A rest-stress SPECT study with CT-based attenuation correction shows an inferior defect on non-corrected images that resolves on corrected images. Registration is verified, wall thickening on gated images is normal, and EF is 62%. The cardiologist hesitates to report the study as normal. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Add prone imaging every time because correction cannot fully resolve inferior attenuation", "B": "Report equivocal and recommend angiography because correction may overcorrect inferior wall counts", "C": "Report normal perfusion: registration verified, defect resolved by correction, thickening normal", "D": "Report a fixed inferior infarct, because the non-corrected images are the standard to follow"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report normal perfusion: registration verified, defect resolved by correction, thickening normal): Attenuation correction is intended to remove diaphragmatic attenuation. When registration is verified, the defect resolves after correction and wall thickening is normal, the findings agree that this is an attenuation artifact, and a normal report is appropriate. Both corrected and uncorrected images should be viewed and the decision documented. Extra imaging or angiography adds radiation and cost without new information."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old woman is scheduled for stress-rest sestamibi SPECT on a dual-head camera. The morning intrinsic flood for head 2 is shown in the exhibit, and a service technician says the defect near the middle of the detector is stable. If head 2 acquired her study uncorrected, which reconstruction artifact would be most likely?", "options": {"A": "A bright streak along the projection direction from patient motion between the stress and rest acquisitions.", "B": "A tuning-fork distortion of the left ventricle from a center of rotation offset in the orbit.", "C": "A ring or partial ring in the transaxial slices, centered near the center of rotation.", "D": "A truncation artifact at slice edges from the chest extending outside the field of view."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A ring or partial ring in the transaxial slices, centered near the center of rotation.): A cold spot near the middle of the detector sits close to the axis of rotation in every projection, so back projection stacks it into a small ring around the center of the slice. Truncation arises from the patient extending past the detector, motion produces streaks or shifted walls, and a center of rotation error gives blurring or a tuning-fork source, all separate from a tube defect.", "image": "question_images/q_299.png"}, {"id": 300, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "At stress, a woman is scanned with her left breast resting on the anterior chest. At rest, positioning is changed and the breast is pulled laterally and taped. The study shows an anterior defect on stress that normalizes at rest. What is the most likely interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Breast attenuation from a positioning change on stress images mimicking reversible ischemia", "B": "True reversible anterior ischemia in the LAD territory, since the defect fully normalized at rest", "C": "Hibernating myocardium, because a defect that improves at rest implies viable but underperfusing muscle", "D": "Fixed attenuation by the breast, since attenuation artifacts do not change between acquisitions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Breast attenuation from a positioning change on stress images mimicking reversible ischemia): Attenuation artifacts persist only when body position is identical between acquisitions. Moving the breast changes its shadow over the anterior wall, so the stress defect disappears at rest and looks like reversible ischemia. The change is a positioning error and the reversal cannot be trusted. Consistent positioning, supine or prone, with the same arm and breast position, avoids this problem. Hibernation is a physiologic state, not a positioning effect."}, {"id": 301, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with a body mass index of 33 and exertional dyspnea has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. The polar maps show reduced stress counts toward the bottom and left, normal at rest, with a stress score of 10 and a rest score of 0. The reader must name the territory and likely cause. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Reversible inferior and inferoseptal ischemia, usually right coronary territory, not attenuation.", "B": "Reversible ischemia of the anterior wall and apex in the left anterior descending artery territory.", "C": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, because the inferior wall is the most usual site of soft tissue artifact.", "D": "Reversible ischemia of the lateral wall in the left circumflex territory, with a likely proximal stenosis."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reversible inferior and inferoseptal ischemia, usually right coronary territory, not attenuation.): On a polar map the inferior wall is at the bottom and the septum at the left, so a defect there involves the inferior wall and inferoseptum, which the right coronary artery usually supplies. Because the defect resolves completely at rest, with a rest score of 0, it is ischemia rather than attenuation, which would remain fixed. Lateral and anterior walls are on the right and top of the map, so those territories do not fit this pattern.", "image": "question_images/q_301.png"}, {"id": 302, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A Tl-201 vial is 48 hours past calibration, and its concentration at calibration was 4.0 mCi/mL. To give a 3.0 mCi (111 MBq) stress dose, a technologist withdraws 0.75 mL using the calibration-day concentration. The Tl-201 half-life is 73 hours. What dose does the patient actually receive?", "options": {"A": "About 4.7 mCi, an overdose that raises patient exposure and blood pool background in the images.", "B": "About 3.0 mCi, since the drawn volume itself defines the dose that is delivered.", "C": "About 2.4 mCi, a slight underdose after correcting for only 24 hours of decay.", "D": "About 1.9 mCi, an underdose that lowers myocardial counts and degrades image quality."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 1.9 mCi, an underdose that lowers myocardial counts and degrades image quality.): The decay factor for 48 hours is 2^(-48/73) = 0.634. The drawn activity is 0.75 mL x 4.0 mCi/mL x 0.634 = 1.9 mCi, so the patient receives about 63 percent of the intended dose. An overdose of 4.7 mCi would result from dividing instead of multiplying, 3.0 mCi ignores decay, and 2.4 mCi uses 24 hours. The consequence is poor count density."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 63-year-old man with severe shoulder arthritis cannot raise his arms above his head, so his SPECT sestamibi study is acquired supine with both arms resting at his sides. Rest and stress images show matching reduced counts in the lateral wall, and gated wall motion is normal. What is the most likely consequence of this positioning?", "options": {"A": "Reduced lateral counts reflect true lateral infarction, since normal wall motion is common in nontransmural scar.", "B": "Arm tissue attenuates lateral projections, creating a matching lateral defect that mimics scar despite normal motion.", "C": "Arm position changes the ramp filter cutoff, thinning the apex on both image sets.", "D": "Scatter from the arms inflates septal counts, producing a falsely reversible septal defect on stress images."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arm tissue attenuates lateral projections, creating a matching lateral defect that mimics scar despite normal motion.): With arms down, the left arm and chest wall lie between the lateral myocardium and the detectors during the LPO views, so photons are attenuated and the lateral wall looks hypoperfused on both sets. Normal thickening in the same wall argues against scar. Scatter would not lower counts, and positioning does not alter the filter."}, {"id": 304, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man with exertional chest pain mentions that he drank two cups of coffee about 2 hours ago, just before regadenoson stress is to start. The technologist wants to proceed on schedule. What is the most likely consequence and the best action?", "options": {"A": "Caffeine potentiates regadenoson and risks prolonged heart block, so reschedule after a 24 hour period.", "B": "Regadenoson is A2A-selective so caffeine has no effect, and the study can proceed with normal reading.", "C": "Caffeine antagonizes A2A receptors and may hide ischemia, so reschedule after 12 hours without caffeine.", "D": "Caffeine increases coronary steal and produces false-positive defects, so proceed but read with caution."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Caffeine antagonizes A2A receptors and may hide ischemia, so reschedule after 12 hours without caffeine.): Caffeine and other methylxanthines compete for adenosine receptors, which reduces the vasodilator response and can lead to false-negative perfusion results. Standard instructions are to withhold caffeine at least 12 hours before testing. It does not potentiate regadenoson, and selectivity does not prevent competitive antagonism at the A2A receptor."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Automatic reorientation of a sestamibi SPECT in a patient with a dilated left ventricle places the long axis too far anterior. The short axis slices then show an apparent inferior defect. What is the effect of this processing limitation on interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Misaligned axes cut oblique slices that create artifactual defects, so review them manually.", "B": "The error is limited to the ejection fraction, with perfusion slices unaffected.", "C": "Attenuation correction will remove the apparent defect once it has been applied.", "D": "Axis placement affects only the display orientation and does not alter the count distribution."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Misaligned axes cut oblique slices that create artifactual defects, so review them manually.): An incorrect long axis produces oblique short axis slices, in which wall segments are sampled at wrong levels and appear as defects. Review of the axis is a routine check, especially in dilated or distorted ventricles. Attenuation correction cannot repair the geometry."}, {"id": 306, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61 year old woman unable to exercise arrives for regadenoson imaging at 10 AM. She reports two cups of coffee at 6 AM that day. She has no wheezing, a normal electrocardiogram and normal blood pressure. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Proceed after a 2 hour wait, since caffeine concentration falls quickly after coffee", "B": "Proceed using regadenoson 0.8 mg, since double the dose overcomes the caffeine effect", "C": "Proceed with regadenoson, since its A2A selectivity makes it unaffected by recent caffeine intake", "D": "Reschedule after at least 12 hours free of caffeine, since methylxanthines block adenosine receptors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reschedule after at least 12 hours free of caffeine, since methylxanthines block adenosine receptors): Caffeine is an adenosine receptor antagonist and can blunt vasodilator hyperemia and cause false-negative studies, so guidelines advise withholding it for at least 12 hours before regadenoson. Regadenoson comes only as a fixed 0.4 mg dose, so the dose cannot be raised. Receptor selectivity does not remove competitive blockade, and 4 hours is too short."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with large breasts and atypical chest pain undergoes stress and rest SPECT with gating. The reader notes slightly lower anterior counts alike on both rows, with summed stress and rest scores of 0 against the normal database. Ejection fraction and ECG are normal. Which description best fits breast attenuation on the paired images?", "options": {"A": "Lateral count reduction that appears only on the rest images and fades during stress acquisition", "B": "Apical count reduction that shifts position between sequential gated frames of the cardiac cycle", "C": "Inferior wall count reduction on stress images that normalizes on the rest images", "D": "Anterior count reduction on both rows, with normal wall thickening on the gated images"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anterior count reduction on both rows, with normal wall thickening on the gated images): Breast tissue absorbs photons from the anterior wall in both acquisitions, so the reduction is stable on stress and rest and scores 0 against the normal database. Because the myocardium is healthy, gated thickening remains normal. A stress-only inferior defect is ischemia, and a defect that moves between frames suggests motion.", "image": "question_images/q_307.png"}, {"id": 308, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 28 mCi Tc-99m sestamibi stress injection infiltrates the antecubital area. A region of interest over the site shows 20 percent of the total counts of the injected dose. The patient is otherwise stable. What is the estimated extravasated activity and the best action?", "options": {"A": "About 22.4 mCi (829 MBq) infiltrated; cancel imaging because most of the dose has been lost", "B": "About 0.56 mCi (21 MBq) infiltrated; no documentation is needed at this small level of activity", "C": "About 5.6 mCi (207 MBq) infiltrated; document it, elevate the arm, and extend the imaging time", "D": "About 5.6 mCi (207 MBq) infiltrated; repeat the full injection now to restore myocardial counts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 5.6 mCi (207 MBq) infiltrated; document it, elevate the arm, and extend the imaging time): Twenty percent of 28 mCi is 5.6 mCi, and 5.6 times 37 gives 207 MBq. The remaining 22.4 mCi still reaches the circulation, so imaging proceeds with documentation, elevation of the arm and longer acquisition for count statistics. Swapping the fractions inflates the loss, reinjecting adds dose unnecessarily, and a misplaced decimal understates the finding."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman completes 6 minutes of a Bruce protocol with 2 mm of horizontal ST depression and exertional angina that does not stop the test. Her sestamibi images show a limited inferior difference between rows, with summed stress score 6 and rest score 0. What are the Duke treadmill score and the combined risk conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Duke score is minus 8, but classed as high risk because ST depression exceeds 1 mm.", "B": "Duke score is plus 2, low risk, so the small inferior finding can be dismissed.", "C": "Duke score is minus 8, intermediate risk; the small reversible inferior defect supports medical therapy.", "D": "Duke score is minus 12, high risk, so the reversible inferior defect mandates urgent angiography."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Duke score is minus 8, intermediate risk; the small reversible inferior defect supports medical therapy.): The Duke score is minutes minus 5 times ST depression in millimeters minus 4 times the angina index. With 6 minutes, 2 mm and an index of 1 for non-limiting angina, the score is 6 minus 10 minus 4, which equals minus 8, in the intermediate band from minus 10 to plus 4. High risk starts at minus 11, and a limited reversible defect (difference score 6, under 10 percent of 68 points) does not raise the overall category to high.", "image": "question_images/q_309.png"}, {"id": 310, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man has a fixed perfusion defect confined to the basal inferolateral wall on Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Angiography has not been performed. A trainee reports left circumflex occlusion based on the defect alone. Which statement best describes the limitation of assigning a culprit vessel from perfusion territory?", "options": {"A": "Perfusion defects cannot be assigned to any vessel, so only angiography can name the culprit artery.", "B": "A wraparound left anterior descending artery supplies this wall when the apex is long.", "C": "The standard model assigns this wall to the circumflex territory, so the report is reliable.", "D": "Coronary dominance varies, so either the circumflex or right coronary may supply this wall."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coronary dominance varies, so either the circumflex or right coronary may supply this wall.): The circumflex usually supplies the lateral wall and the right coronary the inferior wall, but the basal inferolateral segments are shared, and left or right dominance moves the boundary. A defect therefore predicts a probable territory, not a certain vessel. Treating the standard map as fixed anatomy overstates certainty, while denying all territorial inference understates what the pattern offers. A wraparound left anterior descending artery affects the apical inferior wall, not the basal inferolateral segments."}, {"id": 311, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist shortens a 180 degree rest sestamibi SPECT on a dual-head camera by cutting from 32 to 16 projections per head, keeping 25 seconds per stop, because the patient cannot stay still. Reconstruction looks streaky with blurred walls. What is the most likely consequence of this change?", "options": {"A": "Truncation at the field of view edge, because the reconstruction matrix effectively shrank with fewer stops.", "B": "Gated and ungated perfusion mismatch, because the R-R acceptance window stayed too narrow for the rate.", "C": "Less attenuation artifact, because fewer projections mean fewer photon paths crossing the thick chest tissue.", "D": "Angular undersampling causes streaks and blur, and total counts fall because acquisition time halved."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Angular undersampling causes streaks and blur, and total counts fall because acquisition time halved.): Reconstruction needs enough angular samples, and halving the stops from 64 to 32 total leaves gaps that appear as streaks and lost detail. Counts also fall because the same 25 seconds are collected at half as many stops. Matrix size sets pixel size and was not changed. Attenuation depends on tissue thickness, not on the number of projections."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old woman had regadenoson Rb-82 PET for chest pain with normal relative perfusion. The exhibit lists her caffeine history, her preparation, hemodynamics and measured flows, including a low flow reserve. What is the most likely reason for the low flow reserve?", "options": {"A": "Balanced three-vessel disease is likely, so invasive angiography is the best next step for her.", "B": "Microvascular dysfunction is established, because a flow reserve below 2.0 defines that diagnosis in practice.", "C": "Caffeine antagonized adenosine receptors, blunting stress flow and lowering the measured reserve.", "D": "Rest flow rose from tachycardia, which inflated the denominator and falsely lowered the flow reserve."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Caffeine antagonized adenosine receptors, blunting stress flow and lowering the measured reserve.): The exhibit lists two coffees 2 hours before the study, and caffeine antagonizes adenosine receptors, reducing stress flow and thus the ratio. The resting rate pressure product is normal, so the rest flow was not inflated. Calling the result microvascular disease or multivessel disease overlooks a preparation error that must be repeated or corrected.", "image": "question_images/q_312.png"}, {"id": 313, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a C cup bra size has stress and rest sestamibi SPECT for atypical chest pain. Both scans show a mild anteroseptal and anterior count reduction of equal depth. Gated function shows normal wall thickening and an EF of 64 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Hibernating anterior myocardium, which explains the equal depth on stress and rest scans.", "B": "Likely breast attenuation with a normal anterior wall; confirm with attenuation correction or prone images.", "C": "Stress induced LAD ischemia, since any anterior reduction in a woman with chest pain is coronary.", "D": "Prior anterior infarct, so viability testing should be arranged before any revascularization decision is made."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Likely breast attenuation with a normal anterior wall; confirm with attenuation correction or prone images.): A fixed anterior defect of equal depth in a woman with normal wall thickening and normal EF fits soft tissue attenuation by breast. Scar would thin and hypokinetic segments, and hibernating myocardium would show abnormal motion. Attenuation correction or prone imaging can confirm. Labeling it ischemia ignores that nothing reverses between the two scans."}, {"id": 314, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man is referred for exercise SPECT for chest pain. In the intake interview he describes three episodes of chest pressure at rest lasting 15 to 20 minutes during the past 48 hours, the last one this morning. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Cancel outpatient stress testing and arrange urgent evaluation for suspected acute coronary syndrome.", "B": "Proceed with regadenoson SPECT today, since vasodilator stress carries little ischemic risk in acute syndromes.", "C": "Proceed with low-level exercise SPECT at half activity, since lower workload reduces risk of provoking events.", "D": "Schedule treadmill testing next week after a nitroglycerin trial, since nitrate response clarifies the diagnosis."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cancel outpatient stress testing and arrange urgent evaluation for suspected acute coronary syndrome.): Rest angina in the past 48 hours is a high-risk feature that meets criteria for unstable angina or acute coronary syndrome. Stress testing is contraindicated until that is evaluated, and the appropriate path is emergency assessment with ECG and troponin. Lower workload, vasodilator agents and waiting do not remove the risk and delay needed care."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient with right ventricular apical pacing has vasodilator SPECT. Five septal segments score 2 each at stress and rest, and the remaining stress scores add to 6 with rest scores of 2. Septal wall thickening is preserved. What stress score remains for coronary disease after discounting the paced septal pattern?", "options": {"A": "A stress score of 4, from subtracting the nonseptal rest score from the nonseptal stress score.", "B": "A stress score of 16, since the full summed score must be reported without discounting.", "C": "A stress score of 6 from nonseptal segments, with the paced septal score of 10 discounted.", "D": "A stress score of 10, which counts only the septal segments as the disease burden."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A stress score of 6 from nonseptal segments, with the paced septal score of 10 discounted.): Septal segments score 5 times 2, which is 10, at both stress and rest; with 6 from other segments the SSS is 16. Pacing causes an abnormal septal activation pattern that is fixed and not coronary, so the 10 is discounted, leaving 6. The SDS of 4 comes from the nonseptal segments and is unchanged. Reporting 16 counts artifact as disease."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man completes exercise SPECT with normal perfusion images. During exercise his systolic pressure fell 25 mmHg below baseline and he developed 3 mm of horizontal ST depression at 5 METs. What is the likely consequence of reporting only the normal images?", "options": {"A": "Wrongly cancelling a future stress study since today's perfusion images appear normal.", "B": "Overstating risk, because ST depression is meaningless whenever the perfusion images are normal.", "C": "Missing a high-risk exercise response that may reflect extensive ischemia and delay angiography.", "D": "No consequence, because the imaging report is complete without the hemodynamic response data."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Missing a high-risk exercise response that may reflect extensive ischemia and delay angiography.): Exertional hypotension and marked ST depression at a low workload are high-risk findings that can accompany left main or multivessel disease, even when balanced ischemia leaves perfusion looking uniform. Integrating the ECG, hemodynamics, and images prevents false reassurance. Normal images do not erase an abnormal hemodynamic response."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A woman with a body mass index of 31 and large breasts has a supine sestamibi SPECT showing a fixed anterior defect. Left ventricular size and ejection fraction are normal, and the stress was adequate. No CT attenuation correction is available. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Increase the stress injection dose in a repeat study to overcome the attenuation loss.", "B": "Repeat prone, since breast attenuation defects often resolve while scar persists.", "C": "Apply heavier smoothing to the supine data to reduce the apparent defect size.", "D": "Report an anterior infarct and recommend angiography, since the defect is fixed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat prone, since breast attenuation defects often resolve while scar persists.): Breast tissue over the anterior wall attenuates photons and produces a fixed anterior defect with normal function. Prone imaging moves the breast away, so an attenuation artifact improves while true scar persists. Raising dose and smoothing do not change tissue attenuation."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a dual-chamber ICD undergoes SPECT/CT sestamibi imaging. On the CT-corrected images there is intense focal activity at the right ventricular apex and septum, while the uncorrected images look nearly uniform. How should the study be handled before reading?", "options": {"A": "Accept the corrected set as final, since CT correction improves specificity and lead artifact is negligible.", "B": "Repeat the injection at a higher dose, since counts at the septum are too low.", "C": "Compare corrected and uncorrected images and inspect the CT for lead streak before interpreting.", "D": "Discard the uncorrected images and use a smaller filter width to suppress the focal activity."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Compare corrected and uncorrected images and inspect the CT for lead streak before interpreting.): High-density leads cause streaks and overcorrection on CT-derived attenuation maps, which creates false hot spots near the leads. Comparing corrected and uncorrected images, and checking registration and the CT itself, identifies the artifact. Repeat dosing, filter changes or discarding the uncorrected set do not address the cause."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The chart shows the highest hourly exposure rate recorded each day in the corridor outside the hot lab, an unrestricted area. Readings were between 0.3 and 0.5 mrem/h except on day 5. Under 10 CFR 20.1301, which limit did the day 5 reading exceed?", "options": {"A": "2 mrem (0.02 mSv) in any one hour, the hourly limit for an unrestricted area", "B": "5 rem (50 mSv) in a year, the total effective dose limit for occupational workers", "C": "100 mrem (1 mSv) in a year to the public, which one reading would breach", "D": "5 mrem (0.05 mSv) in one hour at 30 cm, the radiation area posting level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (2 mrem (0.02 mSv) in any one hour, the hourly limit for an unrestricted area): The corridor is unrestricted, so the hourly limit of 2 mrem (0.02 mSv) in any one hour applies. The day 5 reading of 3.2 mrem/h exceeds it. The annual public limit of 100 mrem is a cumulative value that one reading does not prove exceeded, the 5 rem limit applies to workers, and 3.2 mrem/h is below the 5 mrem/h threshold for posting a radiation area.", "image": "question_images/q_319.png"}, {"id": 320, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man with well-controlled diabetes, a normal resting ECG and no symptoms asks for a yearly stress MPI to screen for CAD. His pretest probability is about 5 percent. What is the most likely consequence of ordering routine screening MPI?", "options": {"A": "Insurance denial would be the only consequence, with no clinical effect on his care", "B": "Most normal results would be false negatives, leaving silent disease undetected and untreated", "C": "Most abnormal results would be false positives, prompting unneeded angiography and radiation", "D": "The test would show high sensitivity in this group and would justify annual repeats"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Most abnormal results would be false positives, prompting unneeded angiography and radiation): In a population with a pretest probability near 5 percent, positive predictive value is low, so most abnormal studies are false positives even with good specificity. These lead to catheterization, added radiation and incidental findings without outcome benefit in asymptomatic patients. Prevalence does not change sensitivity, and the main harm is clinical, not just financial."}, {"id": 321, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 135 kg man referred for rest-stress sestamibi SPECT is expected to give noisy low-count images on the standard one-day protocol because of his body habitus and the distance from his heart to the detector. Which adaptation best improves count statistics?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the acquisition to limit motion, since attenuation dominates the image anyway.", "B": "Use higher weight-adjusted doses and longer projection time, or a two-day protocol.", "C": "Switch to a high dose of Tl-201, since its lower energy photons are attenuated less.", "D": "Keep the usual dose and apply stronger postfiltering to smooth the noisy images."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use higher weight-adjusted doses and longer projection time, or a two-day protocol.): Large patients attenuate and scatter more, so counts drop. Adjusting dose to weight and lengthening acquisition raise counts, and a two-day protocol allows higher doses. Lower energy Tl-201 is attenuated more, not less, and smoothing blurs data without adding counts."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70-year-old woman has an echocardiographic EF of 45 percent estimated visually, and a gated blood pool study is ordered to settle the question, with the curve in the exhibit. After background subtraction, end-diastolic counts are 17,997 and end-systolic counts are 6,725. How should the laboratory report and handle the discordance?", "options": {"A": "Report 63 percent and review echo image quality and region placement for the discrepancy.", "B": "Report 45 percent from the echocardiogram and treat the counts as unreliable until a repeat study.", "C": "Average the two values and report 54 percent as the final EF for the clinical record.", "D": "Report 63 percent and state that the echocardiogram is wrong, so no echo follow-up is needed."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report 63 percent and review echo image quality and region placement for the discrepancy.): Stroke counts are 17,997 minus 6,725, or 11,272, and 11,272 divided by 17,997 is about 63 percent. The curve is smooth and complete, so the count-based value is reliable and highly reproducible. A difference this large still merits a look at echo image quality and region placement rather than averaging or silently discarding either test.", "image": "question_images/q_322.png"}, {"id": 323, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 58-year-old man has in vivo red cell labeling for gated blood pool imaging because the cardiologist wants an ejection fraction. The exhibit summarizes the labeling record, his concurrent medications and the first planar image. Review the exhibit. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report the study as normal because stomach and thyroid uptake shows that labeling was adequate", "B": "Reschedule and repeat with the in vitro kit method, which tolerates interfering drugs better", "C": "Double the Tc-99m activity and rescan now to improve the faint left ventricular blood pool signal", "D": "Give potassium perchlorate to block free pertechnetate in the stomach and thyroid, then proceed with gating"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reschedule and repeat with the in vitro kit method, which tolerates interfering drugs better): The record shows interfering drugs and poor labeling with free pertechnetate in stomach and thyroid. In vitro labeling gives higher and more reliable efficiency than in vivo labeling when interfering drugs such as heparin and hydralazine are present. Perchlorate lowers background but does not restore labeled red cells, a higher dose adds radiation without fixing the cause, and gastric uptake signals poor labeling, not adequate labeling.", "image": "question_images/q_323.png"}, {"id": 324, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader finishes a stress SPECT showing a moderate reversible anterior defect with summed difference score 5, left ventricular ejection fraction 58 percent, normal cavity size, and nonlimiting angina during exercise. The patient is a 66-year-old man. Which conclusion statement best fits a complete report?", "options": {"A": "Low risk study since ejection fraction is preserved, so ischemic extent need not be quantified.", "B": "Abnormal anterior defect, probably a left anterior descending stenosis of at least 70 percent, requiring stenting.", "C": "Abnormal: moderate reversible anterior defect, difference score 5, ejection fraction 58 percent, intermediate risk.", "D": "Positive for ischemia, recommend angiography, with no statement about defect extent, function or risk category."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Abnormal: moderate reversible anterior defect, difference score 5, ejection fraction 58 percent, intermediate risk.): A complete conclusion states whether the study is normal or abnormal and gives the location, extent, severity and reversibility of defects, summed scores, gated function, and a risk category, with relevant symptoms and ECG findings. It does not prescribe interventions or claim anatomic stenosis grades that perfusion cannot measure. Preserved ejection fraction does not remove ischemic extent from risk assessment."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist measures 160 µSv/h at 0.5 m from a shielded Tc-99m generator during elution. She plans to stand at 2 m from the generator for the rest of the elution. Treating the generator as a point source with no scatter, what dose rate should she expect at her new position?", "options": {"A": "About 80 µSv/h, because each doubling of distance halves the dose rate at that point", "B": "About 40 µSv/h, because dose rate falls in direct proportion to the distance", "C": "About 2.5 µSv/h, because dose rate falls with the cube of distance", "D": "About 10 µSv/h, because dose rate falls with the square of the distance ratio"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 10 µSv/h, because dose rate falls with the square of the distance ratio): Dose rate from a point source follows the inverse square law. Moving from 0.5 m to 2 m is a fourfold increase in distance, so the rate falls by 4 squared, which is 16: 160 divided by 16 equals 10 µSv/h. Dividing by 4 gives 40, halving gives 80, and dividing by 64 gives 2.5, each from a wrong exponent or step."}, {"id": 326, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with diabetes, prior myocardial infarction and typical exertional angina has a pretest probability of 90 percent. His stress perfusion study is read as normal. The test has sensitivity 85 percent and specificity 85 percent. What is the best interpretation of this normal result?", "options": {"A": "Probability is about 85 percent, equal to the specificity, so the normal result has no value", "B": "Probability stays near 60 percent, so a normal result does not exclude disease", "C": "Probability falls below 5 percent, so no further evaluation is needed and angina is noncardiac", "D": "Probability is about 15 percent, equal to one minus sensitivity, so medical therapy alone is safe"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Probability stays near 60 percent, so a normal result does not exclude disease): The negative likelihood ratio is 0.15 divided by 0.85, or 0.18. Pretest odds of 9 times 0.18 give odds near 1.6, a probability of about 61 percent. In a high pretest group a normal result leaves substantial risk, so balanced ischemia or microvascular disease should prompt PET flow quantification or angiography."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old man with diabetes has exertional chest tightness. He exercises 10 minutes on the Bruce protocol, reaching 92 percent of maximal predicted heart rate, and Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT shows an ejection fraction of 58 percent. Summed stress and rest scores are both 4. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "A small reversible mid lateral defect, so refer for circumflex angiography to treat inducible ischemia.", "B": "A fixed mid lateral defect proving hibernation, so FDG viability imaging should precede any other decision.", "C": "A mid lateral defect from apical thinning, so repeat with attenuation correction before reporting.", "D": "A small fixed mid lateral defect, likely scar, so continue medical therapy without angiography for ischemia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A small fixed mid lateral defect, likely scar, so continue medical therapy without angiography for ischemia.): The mid lateral segments show the same moderate reduction at stress and rest, with summed stress and rest scores of 4 and a difference score of 0, which indicates a small scar in the circumflex territory and no inducible ischemia. With a good workload and a preserved ejection fraction, risk is low and medical therapy is appropriate. Reversible ischemia is what would drive angiography. Apical thinning affects the apex, not the lateral wall. A small scar does not generally call for a viability study.", "image": "question_images/q_327.png"}, {"id": 328, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman on a treadmill reaches 105 bpm at 4 METs before stopping for leg fatigue. She has no ischemic symptoms or ECG changes, and there is no contraindication to vasodilators. What is the most appropriate protocol step before tracer injection?", "options": {"A": "Switch immediately to dobutamine and atropine because vasodilators require full exercise first", "B": "Inject the tracer at the current heart rate and image as a standard exercise study", "C": "Continue low-level exercise and add regadenoson or adenosine, then inject the tracer", "D": "Reschedule after holding all rate-slowing drugs for a week to reach target"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue low-level exercise and add regadenoson or adenosine, then inject the tracer): A patient who cannot reach an adequate workload should not have tracer injected at a low heart rate, because the study would be submaximal and insensitive. Combining low-level exercise with a vasodilator is an accepted approach that also reduces gut and liver activity. Rescheduling with a drug hold adds delay, and dobutamine is not needed when vasodilators are not contraindicated."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist moving from SPECT to PET dispenses 370 MBq (10 mCi) of F-18 FDG using the leaded glass syringe shield and lead apron she uses for Tc-99m. Her ring badge readings are triple her SPECT values. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Keep the current shields and draw doses earlier so the short half-life reduces the hand exposure", "B": "Wear a second lead apron layer, since it cuts the 511 keV exposure to her hands", "C": "Switch to a tungsten syringe shield, since 511 keV photons penetrate lead glass and thin aprons", "D": "Change to an acrylic syringe shield to stop the positrons that cause the hand dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Switch to a tungsten syringe shield, since 511 keV photons penetrate lead glass and thin aprons): Annihilation photons of 511 keV are far more penetrating than the 140 keV photons of Tc-99m, so a shield and apron sized for Tc-99m attenuate them poorly. Dense tungsten with adequate thickness is the standard answer for PET syringes. Extra apron layers add little, decay does not lower exposure during handling, and the positron range is short, so acrylic misses the real hazard."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with an ICD implanted 5 weeks ago has fever but is hemodynamically stable. FDG PET shows mild uptake around the generator pocket with no uptake along the leads, and blood cultures are pending. What is the most accurate interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Exclude infection, because low-grade pocket uptake is normal at any interval after implantation.", "B": "Repeat with a labeled leukocyte scan right away, because FDG cannot evaluate cardiac devices or leads.", "C": "Diagnose pocket infection and recommend complete device extraction based on the uptake alone, without cultures.", "D": "Low-grade pocket uptake can be normal healing, so weigh it against cultures and clinical findings."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low-grade pocket uptake can be normal healing, so weigh it against cultures and clinical findings.): In the first weeks after implantation, healing causes mild FDG uptake around the generator, so it is nonspecific. Lead uptake and intense, focal pocket uptake are more suggestive of infection, and results must be weighed with cultures, echocardiography and clinical course. Uptake alone should not drive extraction, and FDG is an accepted tool for device infection."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59 year old man with 15 years of diabetes and peripheral neuropathy denies chest pain during regadenoson imaging, but the scan shows a large reversible anterior defect in the left ventricle. How should the lack of symptoms during stress be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Likely a false positive, since a patient with true ischemia would feel chest pain during stress", "B": "Likely true ischemia, since diabetic neuropathy blunts anginal perception and causes silent ischemia", "C": "Likely breast attenuation artifact, since symptoms and imaging must agree before a defect is accepted", "D": "Equivocal, since absent symptoms lower specificity and the study needs to be repeated later"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Likely true ischemia, since diabetic neuropathy blunts anginal perception and causes silent ischemia): Autonomic and sensory neuropathy in diabetes impairs pain perception, so ischemia is often silent, and perfusion abnormality still carries full prognostic weight. A large reversible defect is not dismissed because the patient felt nothing. Requiring symptoms to confirm ischemia would miss many diabetic patients, and a male patient cannot have breast attenuation."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "After treadmill stress with Tc-99m sestamibi, the inferior wall appears to have reduced counts. The cine shows a tracer-containing hiatal hernia lying against the inferior wall, with intense activity in the herniated stomach. What is the best way to resolve the finding?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the acquisition after a delay and a drink of water, then compare the inferior wall.", "B": "Apply a stronger reconstruction filter to remove the herniated tracer from the image.", "C": "Inject a second full dose of tracer so heart counts exceed the hernia counts.", "D": "Report inferior ischemia in the right coronary territory and refer for angiography."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the acquisition after a delay and a drink of water, then compare the inferior wall.): Intense activity in an adjacent structure can raise the count normalization and produce a falsely low inferior wall on reconstruction. Clearing the stomach or hernia with delay and water lets the heart be reimaged without the interference. A filter cannot separate adjacent hot activity, a second injection adds radiation, and calling it ischemia would be a false positive."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old woman with atypical chest pain has normal stress and rest perfusion and an LVEF of 63 percent on SPECT/CT. The attenuation CT gives a coronary artery calcium score of 1,450. What is the best way to report and act on this combination?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal study and reassure her that no further cardiac evaluation or treatment is needed.", "B": "Report no ischemia but extensive atherosclerosis with high event risk, and advise preventive therapy.", "C": "Refer for urgent angiography because a score above 1,000 proves a flow-limiting stenosis.", "D": "Discard the calcium score because attenuation CT cannot be used for any plaque assessment in practice."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report no ischemia but extensive atherosclerosis with high event risk, and advise preventive therapy.): Normal perfusion shows no current inducible ischemia, but a calcium score above 1,000 signals a heavy plaque burden and high long-term risk, so statin and risk factor treatment are needed. Calcium does not prove obstruction, so angiography is not automatic. Even attenuation CT can flag heavy calcium, though dedicated gated CT is more accurate."}, {"id": 334, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "In a first-pass radionuclide angiogram, the time-activity curve over the superior vena cava is broad, and right and left ventricular phases overlap. The tracer was given as 2 mL through a small hand vein with a slow flush. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "An electrocardiogram gating error that shifts the R-wave trigger by a small amount", "B": "Insufficient dose of Tc-99m, which delays tracer arrival at the pulmonary circulation", "C": "A prolonged bolus from a small-vein, large-volume, slow-flush injection", "D": "Collimator septal penetration from using a low-energy collimator with Tc-99m"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A prolonged bolus from a small-vein, large-volume, slow-flush injection): First-pass studies need a compact bolus, given in a small volume into a large proximal vein and chased by a fast saline flush. A hand-vein injection with a larger volume and slow flush spreads the tracer in time, which widens the superior vena cava curve and makes the right and left ventricular phases overlap so that ejection fraction cannot be computed reliably. Septal penetration, dose and gating errors do not produce this timing pattern."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During regadenoson stress, a 68-year-old man without symptoms goes from heart rate 72 and blood pressure 138/80 to heart rate 96 and 124/74, with 1.5 mm of horizontal ST depression noted after injection. How should the ST depression be viewed?", "options": {"A": "ST depression with vasodilator stress suggests more severe coronary disease and is reported.", "B": "It is an expected nonspecific response from coronary steal, so it carries no clinical meaning here.", "C": "It indicates ineffective vasodilation, so aminophylline should be given and the study repeated.", "D": "A heart rate rise of 24 beats per minute signals a false negative study needing repeat."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (ST depression with vasodilator stress suggests more severe coronary disease and is reported.): ST depression during vasodilator stress is uncommon and, when it occurs, it is associated with more severe or extensive coronary disease and higher event rates, even in asymptomatic patients. The hemodynamic response shown is typical for regadenoson, with a modest heart rate rise and slight blood pressure fall. ST change is not a sign of inadequate vasodilation, and it is reported with the images."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 62-year-old with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 28 percent has thyroid blockade and I-123 MIBG planar imaging. The early heart to mediastinum ratio is 1.5, the late ratio at 4 hours is 1.2, and washout is 42 percent. How should this be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Normal innervation, because the early ratio is above 1.0 and confirms adequate uptake.", "B": "Nondiagnostic, because thyroid blockade reduces myocardial uptake of the tracer.", "C": "Reduced sympathetic drive with favorable prognosis, because washout is the main marker of good outcome.", "D": "Impaired sympathetic innervation: late ratio below 1.6 with high washout predicts higher event risk."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Impaired sympathetic innervation: late ratio below 1.6 with high washout predicts higher event risk.): A late heart to mediastinum ratio below about 1.6 with elevated washout indicates reduced cardiac sympathetic integrity and is associated with a higher risk of progression and events. Thyroid blockade protects the thyroid from free iodine and does not lower cardiac uptake. High washout suggests worse, not better, prognosis."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exhibit is the morning log for a Rb-82 generator. The technologist wrote 'within limits, proceed' and injected two patients from the same elution. Using the labeled values and the limits in the exhibit, what is the consequence of that decision?", "options": {"A": "None, because the Sr-85 value is under its limit and only that value governs release", "B": "Only image quality suffers from Sr-85 scatter, while patient dose stays as originally planned", "C": "The injected Rb-82 activity was overstated, so counts fall and one repeat injection will correct it", "D": "Both patients received excess Sr-82, adding bone and marrow dose; the generator must be withdrawn"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Both patients received excess Sr-82, adding bone and marrow dose; the generator must be withdrawn): Sr-82 per mCi of Rb-82 is 0.9 divided by 40, or 0.0225 µCi/mCi, above the 0.02 limit; Sr-85 at 0.4 divided by 40, or 0.01, is under its limit. Each limit is applied separately. Sr-82 has a 25 day half-life and localizes to bone and marrow, so both patients received extra dose and the generator must be removed from use.", "image": "question_images/q_337.png"}, {"id": 338, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with a BMI of 26 has no prior infarct, and exercised 10 METs with a normal ECG. Her attenuation-corrected stress Tc-99m images are normal with a gated ejection fraction of 62 percent. The laboratory uses stress-first imaging. What should be done next?", "options": {"A": "Perform rest imaging to rule out a fixed defect that stress images alone cannot show.", "B": "Repeat the stress study the next day with thallium-201 to confirm the normal result.", "C": "Cancel rest imaging, since a normal stress study in a low-risk patient is sufficient.", "D": "Perform rest imaging, since a stress-only protocol requires an abnormal stress study for comparison."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cancel rest imaging, since a normal stress study in a low-risk patient is sufficient.): Stress-first protocols allow rest imaging to be omitted when stress images, with attenuation correction and gating, are normal and the patient has low pretest risk, reducing radiation and time. Rest images are needed when stress images are abnormal or equivocal, or in higher-risk patients."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a six-minute adenosine infusion at 140 micrograms per kilogram per minute, a 70-year-old man develops transient second-degree Mobitz type I AV block at minute 3. He is asymptomatic with a pressure of 120/70 mmHg, and the block resolves within seconds. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "An expected A1 nodal effect, so continue monitoring and stop for Mobitz II or complete block.", "B": "This confirms inadequate dosing, so double the infusion rate to produce a full vasodilator response.", "C": "Stop the infusion and give aminophylline, since any second-degree block is an absolute contraindication to continuing.", "D": "This indicates severe left main disease, so inject the tracer and stop the infusion immediately."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An expected A1 nodal effect, so continue monitoring and stop for Mobitz II or complete block.): Adenosine acts on A1 receptors at the AV node, so transient first-degree or Mobitz I block is common, brief and benign. High-grade or Mobitz II block, symptomatic hypotension or severe bronchospasm are reasons to stop. The block is not a marker of left main disease, and doubling the dose would increase risk."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On review of the raw projection cine from an exercise Tc-99m SPECT, the heart drifts steadily upward in the field of view during the acquisition. The reconstructed images show a new inferior defect. What should the reader do before interpreting the defect?", "options": {"A": "Report inferior ischemia, because the polar map shows a clear abnormality in that wall.", "B": "Raise the filter cutoff to blur the motion and smooth the inferior wall.", "C": "Apply attenuation correction, which removes upward creep artifact from the reconstruction.", "D": "Reprocess with motion correction or repeat the acquisition, then reassess the defect."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reprocess with motion correction or repeat the acquisition, then reassess the defect.): Upward creep occurs after exercise as breathing and heart rate settle, and it smears counts and can produce a spurious inferior defect. The cine reveals it, and the fix is motion correction or reacquisition. Attenuation correction addresses soft tissue absorption, not movement, and filtering only blurs the problem."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man has normal exercise tolerance and gated SPECT with EF 63 percent and normal wall thickening. The chart plots percent of maximal counts for each of the 17 segments. Only segment 17 is reduced, with no other abnormality. What is the best interpretation of this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Distal LAD ischemia, since segment 17 is the most sensitive marker of left anterior descending disease.", "B": "Normal apical thinning, since the thin apex gives lower counts with normal thickening.", "C": "Attenuation by the left hemidiaphragm, requiring a repeat prone acquisition of the same segment.", "D": "Partial volume loss from marked hypertrophy, indicating likely hypertrophic cardiomyopathy of the apex."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal apical thinning, since the thin apex gives lower counts with normal thickening.): The apex is a thin tip of myocardium, so count density there is physiologically lower and a mild isolated reduction is a normal variant. Normal thickening and EF support this. Calling it LAD ischemia overreads a normal feature, diaphragmatic attenuation affects inferior segments and not the apex, and partial volume effects would need a thick wall and evidence of hypertrophy.", "image": "question_images/q_341.png"}, {"id": 342, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 31-year-old nursing mother receives a Tc-99m agent that, by the laboratory's calculation, would give her breast-feeding infant an effective dose of 3 mSv if feeding continued without interruption. She is otherwise well and wants to go home. What does the NRC release rule require?", "options": {"A": "Release her with written instructions on interrupting breast-feeding and the consequences of ignoring them", "B": "Release her with verbal advice only, because 3 mSv is below the 5 mSv release limit", "C": "Release her with no instructions because the rule covers only therapeutic radiopharmaceuticals", "D": "Hold her in the department until the infant dose estimate falls below 0.5 mSv"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Release her with written instructions on interrupting breast-feeding and the consequences of ignoring them): When the dose to a breast-fed infant could exceed 1 mSv (0.1 rem) without interruption, the licensee must give the patient written instructions on interrupting or discontinuing breast-feeding and the consequences of failure to follow them. The 5 mSv value governs release itself, but the lower infant threshold triggers the written instructions. The requirement is not limited to therapy."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with dyspnea has normal perfusion on gated SPECT. The end-diastolic volume is 210 mL, the end-systolic volume is 100 mL, and the LVEF is 52 percent. What is the best next step in his evaluation? He has no chest pain and a normal ECG.", "options": {"A": "Recognize a dilated left ventricle despite preserved EF, and recommend echocardiography for cardiomyopathy.", "B": "Report a normal study because an LVEF above 50 percent excludes significant ventricular disease.", "C": "Refer for angiography because normal perfusion with large volumes shows severe three-vessel coronary disease.", "D": "Repeat gating with a smaller zoom setting to shrink the volumes into the normal range."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recognize a dilated left ventricle despite preserved EF, and recommend echocardiography for cardiomyopathy.): Normal end-diastolic volume is roughly 120 to 150 mL in men, so 210 mL is clearly dilated even though the EF of 52 percent looks acceptable. Dyspnea with a dilated ventricle calls for evaluation for cardiomyopathy by echocardiography. Normal perfusion argues against multivessel ischemia as the cause, and zoom changes do not make a large ventricle small."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58 year old man exercises 9 minutes on the Bruce protocol and develops 2 mm of horizontal ST depression without angina. A colleague calls him low risk because he exercised well and had no chest pain. What is the Duke treadmill score and risk group?", "options": {"A": "Score +9, low risk, from exercise time alone since there was no angina", "B": "Score -1, intermediate risk, from 9 minus 10 for ST depression minus 0 for angina", "C": "Score -11, high risk, counting the ST depression in two subtraction terms of the formula", "D": "Score +5, low risk, using only 2 points for each millimeter of ST depression"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Score -1, intermediate risk, from 9 minus 10 for ST depression minus 0 for angina): The Duke score is exercise minutes minus 5 times the maximum ST deviation in millimeters minus 4 times the angina index. Here it is 9 minus 10 minus 0, or -1, which falls in the intermediate group of -10 to +4. Good exercise time does not offset 2 mm of ST depression."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old asymptomatic man has a study with defects in four segments, each scored 3 on stress and 3 on rest. A reader writes only that four of 17 segments are involved and gives no severity. What does this omission understate, using summed scores?", "options": {"A": "Reversibility, since four segments scored 3 give a summed difference score of 12.", "B": "Severity, since four segments scored 3 give a summed stress score of 12, severely abnormal.", "C": "Severity, since four segments scored 3 give a summed stress score of 12, moderately abnormal.", "D": "Severity, since four segments scored 3 give a summed stress score of 7, mildly abnormal."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Severity, since four segments scored 3 give a summed stress score of 12, moderately abnormal.): The summed stress score adds the score of each segment, so four segments at 3 equal 12, in the moderately abnormal range of 9 to 13. Rest scores are also 12, so the difference score is 0, meaning fixed defects. Listing extent alone hides severity. A score of 7 or a severe label would come from arithmetic or classification errors."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During treadmill stress, a 68-year-old man with baseline BP 142/84 reaches stage 2 with HR 118 and new 1.8 mm of horizontal ST depression. His SBP is now 128 and he reports leg fatigue and light-headedness. What should the supervising physician do?", "options": {"A": "Stop the test but skip the tracer, since symptoms make the study invalid", "B": "Continue to 85% of maximum predicted rate, 129, since he is below target", "C": "Continue, since a 14 mmHg fall is only meaningful when it drops below 100 mmHg", "D": "Terminate now: SBP fell over 10 mmHg with ST depression and symptoms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Terminate now: SBP fell over 10 mmHg with ST depression and symptoms): A sustained fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mmHg from baseline (142 to 128 is 14 mmHg), especially with evidence of ischemia such as ST depression and symptoms of poor perfusion, is an indication to stop. Maximum predicted rate is 220 minus 68 = 152, and 85% is about 129, but reaching target does not outrank an abnormal pressure response. The tracer should be injected at the peak and exercise continued about a minute if safe."}, {"id": 347, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During an adenosine infusion at 140 mcg/kg/min, a 55-year-old man feels flushing and chest tightness at minute 2. Telemetry shows transient second-degree AV block with Mobitz I pattern, heart rate 62, blood pressure 112/70, no wheezing and no ST change. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Stop and switch to dobutamine, since the block shows the patient cannot tolerate adenosine stress.", "B": "Stop the infusion and give atropine 0.5 mg intravenously, as for any symptomatic bradycardia.", "C": "Stop the infusion and give aminophylline 125 mg intravenously, because AV block signals toxicity.", "D": "Recognize an expected adenosine effect, continue under monitoring, and inject on schedule."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize an expected adenosine effect, continue under monitoring, and inject on schedule.): Adenosine acts on A1 receptors in the AV node, so transient first or second-degree block with flushing and chest tightness is common and short lived. With stable pressure, no wheezing and no ST change, the infusion can continue and tracer be injected. Reversal or a change of agent is reserved for severe block, hypotension, bronchospasm or persistent ischemia."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man with exertional angina and a prior smoking history has stress and rest Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT with an ejection fraction of 58 percent. Segment scoring gives a summed stress score of 10 and a summed rest score of 6. Which term best describes the perfusion pattern for the final report?", "options": {"A": "A fixed defect: pure scar and no residual ischemic myocardium in the anterior and anterolateral walls.", "B": "A reverse perfusion pattern: the rest defect is worse than the stress defect, anteriorly.", "C": "Partially reversible defect: scar plus residual ischemia in the anterior and anterolateral walls.", "D": "A fully reversible defect: pure ischemia and no scar component in the anterior and lateral walls."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Partially reversible defect: scar plus residual ischemia in the anterior and anterolateral walls.): The basal anterior and anterolateral defect is severe at stress and milder but still present at rest, with scores of 10 and 6 and a difference score of 4. Persistent defects represent scar and the improvement represents ischemia, so the pattern is partial reversibility. A fully reversible defect would normalize at rest. A fixed defect would look unchanged. Reverse perfusion would show the rest map worse than stress, the opposite of this study.", "image": "question_images/q_348.png"}, {"id": 349, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 140 kg patient has a low-count rest SPECT study, and the images look mottled and noisy. The current reconstruction uses filtered backprojection with a Butterworth filter at a high critical frequency. Which adjustment best matches the processing to this patient?", "options": {"A": "Increase the filter order to 20 while keeping the same critical frequency for smoothing", "B": "Raise the critical frequency to restore edge sharpness from the noisy projection data", "C": "Switch to a pure ramp filter without a window to reduce noise in the reconstruction", "D": "Lower the Butterworth cutoff, accepting some resolution loss to suppress noise in low counts"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower the Butterworth cutoff, accepting some resolution loss to suppress noise in low counts): Low-count studies need more smoothing, which is achieved by lowering the cutoff of the window filter at the cost of some resolution. A higher cutoff passes more noise, an unwindowed ramp amplifies it, and a higher Butterworth order only steepens the roll-off near the same cutoff rather than smoothing more."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old woman has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT scored with the 17-segment model. The reader notes reduced stress counts in the inferior wall only, normal at rest. The summed stress score is 5 and the summed rest score is 0. The maximum possible score is 68. What does the summed difference score show?", "options": {"A": "A difference score of 5, about 29 percent, found by dividing by the 17 segments.", "B": "A difference score of 0, because the rest score is zero, so no ischemic burden exists.", "C": "A difference score of 5, about 42 percent, found by dividing by only the inferior segments.", "D": "A difference score of 5, about 7 percent of the maximum, a small inferior ischemic burden."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A difference score of 5, about 7 percent of the maximum, a small inferior ischemic burden.): The difference score is the stress score minus the rest score, 5 minus 0, which is 5. Five divided by the maximum score of 68 is about 7 percent, well under the 10 percent line for higher risk. Dividing by the segment count or by the affected segments alone inflates the burden, and the zero rest score marks the defect as reversible rather than absent.", "image": "question_images/q_350.png"}, {"id": 351, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A woman is 168 cm tall and weighs 112 kg and has an indeterminate stress ECG. She needs perfusion imaging, and her physician calculates body mass index to decide between SPECT and PET. Which statement gives the correct BMI and its imaging consequence?", "options": {"A": "BMI is about 33.3 kg/m2, so SPECT remains adequate without attenuation correction.", "B": "BMI is about 39.7 kg/m2, so PET is favored because attenuation lowers SPECT specificity.", "C": "BMI is about 18.0 kg/m2, so imaging choice is unaffected by body habitus.", "D": "BMI is about 66.7 kg/m2, so PET is required because SPECT cannot be performed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (BMI is about 39.7 kg/m2, so PET is favored because attenuation lowers SPECT specificity.): BMI equals weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared: 112 divided by 1.68 squared (2.822) gives 39.7 kg/m2. Values of 35 to 39.9 define class II obesity. At this size, soft-tissue attenuation and scatter reduce SPECT counts and specificity, so PET with CT correction is favored when available. The other values come from not squaring the height, doubling it, or treating kilograms as pounds."}, {"id": 352, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A cyclotron site delivers N-13 ammonia for a PET study. The unit is assayed at 9:00, and the patient will be injected at 9:20 after IV placement and a pre-stress electrocardiogram. The protocol needs 10 mCi in the patient at injection. What activity should the syringe read at the 9:00 assay?", "options": {"A": "20 mCi, treating the full 20 minute delay as one half-life and doubling the dose", "B": "80 mCi, using a delay of 30 minutes for the decay correction instead of 20", "C": "30 mCi, adding half again to the target to cover a small loss during transport", "D": "40 mCi, since 20 minutes is two half-lives and activity falls to one quarter"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (40 mCi, since 20 minutes is two half-lives and activity falls to one quarter): N-13 has a physical half-life of about 10 minutes, so 20 minutes is two half-lives and activity falls to 25 percent. Working backward, 10 mCi divided by 0.25 gives 40 mCi. Doubling only to 20 mCi treats the delay as one half-life, 30 mCi is an arbitrary linear add-on, and 80 mCi would suit a 30 minute delay."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with severe COPD takes theophylline 400 mg daily, which his pulmonologist advises not to stop. He cannot exercise, has no arrhythmia, blood pressure is 128/76 mmHg, and the referring physician wants testing this week. Which stress is best?", "options": {"A": "Adenosine at 210 mcg/kg/min, since a higher rate overcomes receptor blockade and avoids stopping theophylline.", "B": "Regadenoson at a doubled dose, since higher agonist doses overcome competitive receptor blockade from theophylline.", "C": "Dobutamine stress, because theophylline antagonizes adenosine receptors and does not blunt beta-adrenergic stimulation.", "D": "Dipyridamole infusion, since it acts independently of adenosine receptors and is unaffected by methylxanthines."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dobutamine stress, because theophylline antagonizes adenosine receptors and does not blunt beta-adrenergic stimulation.): Theophylline blocks adenosine receptors, so regadenoson, adenosine and dipyridamole all lose effect. Dobutamine stimulates beta receptors and works regardless of methylxanthine therapy. Increasing doses of vasodilators is not validated and raises risk, and dipyridamole depends on endogenous adenosine, so it is also blunted."}, {"id": 354, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with known atrial fibrillation cannot walk on a treadmill. The chart shows his resting heart rate each minute before the planned pharmacologic stress. He is not on rate control, and an irregular rhythm is confirmed. Which agent is best?", "options": {"A": "Dobutamine at a low starting dose, since small doses are harmless in fast atrial fibrillation.", "B": "Dobutamine after intravenous metoprolol, since beta-blockade blunts the rapid rate.", "C": "Dobutamine, since its inotropic effect will slow the rate once contractility rises.", "D": "Regadenoson, because dobutamine would worsen an already uncontrolled ventricular rate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson, because dobutamine would worsen an already uncontrolled ventricular rate.): The chart shows resting rates of 122 to 141 beats per minute, an uncontrolled ventricular response. Dobutamine increases AV conduction and heart rate, risking symptomatic tachycardia and a nondiagnostic study. A vasodilator does not push the rate this way. Metoprolol blunts the dobutamine response and makes the study unreliable.", "image": "question_images/q_354.png"}, {"id": 355, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A referring physician asks what fetal absorbed dose is generally accepted as the level below which diagnostic radiation does not increase the risk of malformation in a pregnancy. Which value should the nuclear cardiologist give in response to this question?", "options": {"A": "Below 50 mGy, where no increase in malformation risk is expected", "B": "Below 20 mSv, the averaged annual limit for radiation workers in some systems", "C": "Below 1 mSv, the annual limit for members of the public", "D": "Below 5 mSv, the NRC limit for a declared pregnant worker"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Below 50 mGy, where no increase in malformation risk is expected): Deterministic effects on the embryo, including malformation, are not seen below about 50 mGy. The 1 mSv public limit and the 5 mSv occupational fetal limit are regulatory limits for planned exposure, not thresholds for harm to a patient's pregnancy, and 20 mSv is a worker average."}, {"id": 356, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old woman receiving trastuzumab for breast cancer has a baseline echocardiographic EF of 58 percent. Her blood pool software reports 85 percent from 17,984 counts in the first frame and 2,599 at the end of the curve. What should the interpreting physician do?", "options": {"A": "Call 85 percent an artifact, report limited quality, and repeat with arrhythmia rejection", "B": "Report an EF of 85 percent and note an increase relative to the echocardiographic baseline", "C": "Average the software value with the echocardiographic value and report 72 percent as the final EF", "D": "Lower the EF to the 70 percent upper limit of normal as the best estimate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Call 85 percent an artifact, report limited quality, and repeat with arrhythmia rejection): An EF of 85 percent is not physiologic, and the curve shows the cause: counts fall and do not return, so late frames are underfilled by short beats. The value cannot be corrected by averaging or capping, because both use a number that has no measurement behind it. The study should be reported as technically limited and repeated with a narrower acceptance window or list-mode acquisition.", "image": "question_images/q_356.png"}, {"id": 357, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old man is on a Bruce treadmill test with sestamibi ready for injection. The exercise record is shown in the table. He reports only mild fatigue and has no chest pain. Which is the best action for the stress laboratory?", "options": {"A": "Continue into stage 4 and give sublingual nitroglycerin to relieve the ST depression.", "B": "Stop exercise but withhold the tracer, because the stress was too short to be diagnostic.", "C": "Stop exercise and inject, since falling pressure with ST depression suggests ischemia.", "D": "Continue to 85 percent of predicted maximum heart rate, since the target has not been reached."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop exercise and inject, since falling pressure with ST depression suggests ischemia.): A systolic pressure that falls from 148 to 126 mm Hg while workload rises, together with 1.5 mm ST depression, is a termination criterion because it can signal severe multivessel disease with ventricular dysfunction. The tracer should be injected at that peak so that the images reflect the ischemia. Reaching the heart rate target is not required once an endpoint is met.", "image": "question_images/q_357.png"}, {"id": 358, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man completes treadmill testing before SPECT, and the exhibit lists his exercise variables. Using the Duke treadmill score formula, what are his score and risk category? He is on no beta blocker and has a history of hypertension and a resting ECG without ST changes.", "options": {"A": "Duke score of minus 11, which falls in the high-risk category.", "B": "Duke score of minus 3, which falls in the intermediate-risk category.", "C": "Duke score of minus 5, which falls in the intermediate-risk category.", "D": "Duke score of plus 5, which falls in the low-risk category."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Duke score of minus 11, which falls in the high-risk category.): The Duke score equals exercise minutes minus five times the maximal ST depression in millimeters minus four times the angina index. Here it is 7 minus 10 minus 8, or minus 11, which is in the high-risk range of minus 11 or lower. Counting angina once gives minus 5, leaving ST unweighted gives minus 3, and dropping the angina term gives plus 5.", "image": "question_images/q_358.png"}, {"id": 359, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman on doxorubicin has serial gated blood pool studies. Her EF appears to fall from 58 to 49 percent, but the rhythm recorded during acquisition is shown in the tracing. Before labeling this as cardiotoxicity, what is the most likely artifact to consider?", "options": {"A": "Positron range blur from the tracer, which enlarges the apparent end-systolic volume", "B": "Free pertechnetate in the injection, which lowers background activity around the ventricle", "C": "Irregular beat lengths in atrial fibrillation distort gated EF; use arrhythmia rejection", "D": "Photon attenuation by the breast, which lowers counts in the ventricle during every cardiac cycle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Irregular beat lengths in atrial fibrillation distort gated EF; use arrhythmia rejection): Gated blood pool EF assumes similar beat lengths, but atrial fibrillation creates variable R-R intervals so the summed cycle blurs end-diastole and end-systole and can bias EF. Arrhythmia rejection windows or list-mode acquisition reduce that error. Breast attenuation and positron range do not belong to this study, and poor labeling raises background rather than lowering it.", "image": "question_images/q_359.png"}, {"id": 360, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "After in vivo red cell labeling for a gated blood pool study, the image shows intense stomach and thyroid activity with only faint left ventricular blood pool. The technologist asks what accounts for this pattern on the first planar view. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Heat-damaged red cells, which are sequestered mainly by the spleen and spare the stomach", "B": "Colloid formation in the kit, which is taken up by the liver and spleen after injection", "C": "Poor labeling left free pertechnetate, which collects in the stomach, thyroid and salivary glands", "D": "Mo-99 breakthrough in the eluate, which concentrates in the stomach and thyroid of every patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Poor labeling left free pertechnetate, which collects in the stomach, thyroid and salivary glands): Pertechnetate that is not bound to red cells behaves like free pertechnetate and concentrates in the stomach, thyroid and salivary glands, leaving a weak blood pool signal. Colloid would localize in liver and spleen, and damaged cells would show splenic uptake. Mo-99 breakthrough affects radiation dose and image quality but does not produce this specific gastric and thyroid pattern."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A new technologist sets a Butterworth low-pass cutoff of 0.8 cycles per pixel, order 5, on 64 by 64 SPECT data, reasoning that a higher cutoff always preserves more detail. The slices look very grainy. What is the best explanation and correction?", "options": {"A": "The cutoff exceeds Nyquist and adds only noise; reprocess near 0.4 to 0.5", "B": "The matrix is too small, so rebin to 32 by 32 to gain finer detail", "C": "The cutoff is too low, so raising it to 1.2 will resolve the noise", "D": "The order is too high, so lowering it to 2 will restore detail and reduce graininess"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The cutoff exceeds Nyquist and adds only noise; reprocess near 0.4 to 0.5): No frequency above the Nyquist limit of 0.5 cycles per pixel exists in the sampled data, so a cutoff of 0.8 passes noise without gaining any resolution. Reprocessing with a cutoff in the range of roughly 0.4 to 0.5, depending on count density, balances noise and resolution. Lowering order changes how sharply the filter rolls off but not that the cutoff is meaningless. Rebinning loses resolution, and raising the cutoff worsens the noise."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with large breasts has exertional chest discomfort and undergoes regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. The polar maps show reduced stress counts in the anterior wall and apex that normalize on the rest map. The reader worries about breast attenuation. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Fixed anterior scar from a remote infarct, with no active ischemia in the anterior descending territory.", "B": "Breast attenuation artifact, so the study is falsely abnormal and no further testing is needed now.", "C": "Left bundle branch block septal artifact, which needs no further evaluation if heart rate was low.", "D": "Reversible anterior and apical ischemia in LAD territory; filling at rest argues against artifact."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reversible anterior and apical ischemia in LAD territory; filling at rest argues against artifact.): Breast tissue absorbs photons equally at stress and rest, so attenuation usually produces a defect that stays fixed. A defect that appears at stress and fills in at rest is better explained by true ischemia, here in the anterior wall and apex, which is left anterior descending territory. A fixed scar would persist at rest, and a bundle branch block artifact is septal, not anterior and apical.", "image": "question_images/q_362.png"}, {"id": 363, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader reports a stress defect in segment 11 of the 17-segment model as mid inferior wall. The study is used to plan revascularization. Segment 11 is actually the mid inferolateral segment. What is the most likely consequence of this mislabeling?", "options": {"A": "The culprit shifts from the circumflex to the right coronary artery, misdirecting revascularization.", "B": "The summed stress score changes, because segment 11 carries a higher weight than segment 10 does.", "C": "Nothing clinically changes, because the inferior and inferolateral walls share the same coronary supply.", "D": "The extent is miscalculated, because inferior segments are excluded from the 17-segment model."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The culprit shifts from the circumflex to the right coronary artery, misdirecting revascularization.): In the 17-segment model, segments 4 and 10 are inferior, and 5 and 11 are inferolateral. Inferior defects are typically attributed to the right or dominant artery, and inferolateral defects to the circumflex. Mislabeling therefore shifts the presumed culprit. All segments score equally, and all 17 are included, so the score does not change."}, {"id": 364, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient with high-grade AV block is referred for cardiac sarcoid FDG PET. The patient ate pasta the evening before and fasted 6 hours, and the laboratory gave heparin 50 IU/kg before FDG. Images show diffuse, uniform left ventricular uptake with normal Rb-82 rest perfusion. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse active sarcoid inflammation, so start immunosuppression based on the uniform myocardial glucose uptake", "B": "Suppression failed so the study is nondiagnostic; repeat after 24 hour high-fat, low-carbohydrate preparation", "C": "Heparin rescued suppression of glucose uptake, so report negative for active inflammation on this study", "D": "Physiologic uptake explains the pattern, so report no sarcoid because the resting perfusion is normal here"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suppression failed so the study is nondiagnostic; repeat after 24 hour high-fat, low-carbohydrate preparation): Uniform diffuse FDG uptake after a carbohydrate meal and a short fast indicates inadequate suppression of myocardial glucose metabolism, so inflammation cannot be judged. Preparation requires a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet and a prolonged fast, with heparin as an adjunct only. Calling it inflammation or negative are both unsupported, and normal perfusion does not exclude active inflammation."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Tl-201 was injected at peak stress, and the chart plots relative counts in a normal segment and an ischemic segment over time. A trainee reads only the first image and calls the study normal. What does the chart show about protocol timing?", "options": {"A": "The defect narrows as ischemic tissue washes out slowly, so 3 to 4 hour images help", "B": "The curves fall in parallel, so one early image is enough to separate ischemia from scar", "C": "Normal myocardium retains tracer longer, so late images show the defect more clearly", "D": "The ischemic segment rises above the normal one at once, so imaging at 30 minutes works"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The defect narrows as ischemic tissue washes out slowly, so 3 to 4 hour images help): Thallium redistributes: normal myocardium washes out faster than ischemic myocardium, so a defect seen soon after stress narrows or fills in on later images. Reading only the early image cannot separate reversible ischemia from fixed scar. Redistribution images at 3 to 4 hours, and sometimes reinjection, are therefore required.", "image": "question_images/q_365.png"}, {"id": 366, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist drops a syringe holding about 8 mCi (296 MBq) of Tc-99m sestamibi in the hot lab, wetting 40 cm of floor. She wipes the puddle with a paper towel, working from the center outward, then walks to the sink in contaminated shoes. What is the main consequence of this response?", "options": {"A": "Contamination spreads beyond the spill margin and onto shoes, enlarging the area needing survey and cleanup.", "B": "The towel removes all the activity from the floor, so a follow-up meter survey becomes unnecessary.", "C": "The spill becomes a major spill requiring telephone notification of the NRC the next day.", "D": "Sestamibi vaporizes during wiping and creates an inhalation hazard that forces evacuation of the whole department."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contamination spreads beyond the spill margin and onto shoes, enlarging the area needing survey and cleanup.): A small Tc-99m spill is a minor spill, handled by containing it, covering it with absorbent material and cleaning from the outside edge inward. Working outward drags activity beyond the margin, and walking away in contaminated shoes spreads it further, so more floor must be surveyed and cleaned. Wiping never replaces a survey, the NRC call applies to defined events and not to technique errors, and Tc-99m sestamibi is not volatile like free radioiodine."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A multidose vial contains 100 mCi of Tc-99m in 2.0 mL at 06:00. A 25 mCi dose is ordered for a patient scheduled at 10:00, and the half-life is 6 hours. The technologist must draw the correct volume. What volume should be drawn at 10:00?", "options": {"A": "1.00 mL, because four hours is treated as one half-life leaving 50 mCi", "B": "About 0.79 mL, because 63 mCi remains after four hours of decay", "C": "0.50 mL, because 25 of the original 100 mCi is one quarter of 2.0 mL", "D": "0.31 mL, because activity grows to 159 mCi after four hours"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 0.79 mL, because 63 mCi remains after four hours of decay): After 4 hours, which is two thirds of a half-life, the activity is 100 times 2 to the power of minus 2/3, about 63 mCi in 2.0 mL, or 31.5 mCi/mL. A 25 mCi dose needs 25 divided by 31.5, or 0.79 mL. Ignoring decay gives 0.50 mL, and treating four hours as a full half-life gives 1.00 mL. Treating activity as growing gives 0.31 mL."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old nondiabetic man exercises for 10 minutes, reaches 94 percent of predicted heart rate, and has no symptoms. His SPECT is normal with an EF of 60 percent. What annual rate of cardiac death or myocardial infarction should he be told to expect?", "options": {"A": "Under 0.1 percent per year, equal to someone with no risk factors.", "B": "About 2 to 3 percent per year, placing him in an intermediate risk category.", "C": "Under 1 percent per year, placing him in a low risk category.", "D": "About 5 percent per year, since any history of chest pain keeps risk high."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Under 1 percent per year, placing him in a low risk category.): A normal gated perfusion study with good exercise capacity is associated with an annual cardiac death or infarction rate below 1 percent. Intermediate risk applies when significant ischemia is present. A normal study does not make risk zero, so the 0.1 percent claim goes too far."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man with atypical chest pain and a 10-year risk of 12 percent has a normal exercise SPECT. Ejection fraction is 58 percent and he reached 10 METs. His physician asks what the result means for annual risk and what to advise. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Annual cardiac death or infarction risk is under 1 percent, so more ischemia testing is unnecessary", "B": "Yearly risk is near 3 percent, so repeat imaging is advised in six months", "C": "Low risk applies to pharmacologic studies, so an exercise result needs confirmation with another test", "D": "Normal perfusion excludes any coronary plaque, so preventive medication can be stopped by this patient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Annual cardiac death or infarction risk is under 1 percent, so more ischemia testing is unnecessary): A normal gated perfusion study in a patient who exercised adequately carries an annual hard event rate below 1 percent. That supports defer further testing and focus on risk factors. Perfusion imaging detects flow-limiting disease, not plaque, so prevention continues. The low-risk finding is not limited to pharmacologic stress.", "image": "question_images/q_369.png"}, {"id": 370, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old woman has stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The raw projection cine shows an abrupt vertical jump of the heart by about two pixels near projection 20, and the sinogram shows a matching discontinuity. Reconstruction shows an apical inferior defect, and motion correction leaves residual blurring. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reacquire the stress images, since the tracer is stable, rather than reporting disease.", "B": "Wait a day and repeat only the rest study, since motion defects fade with redistribution.", "C": "Report a fixed apical inferior defect and recommend coronary angiography based on the reconstructed slices.", "D": "Apply CT based attenuation correction to the same dataset to remove the motion related apical defect."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reacquire the stress images, since the tracer is stable, rather than reporting disease.): The cine and sinogram show a sudden patient shift, so the defect is a processing consequence of motion and not a flow abnormality. Software correction cannot repair a large abrupt shift, and Tc-99m sestamibi remains fixed in myocardium long enough to reacquire the stress images. Calling the defect fixed would send the patient to angiography unnecessarily, attenuation correction addresses photon loss and not movement, and rest redistribution does not apply to this tracer."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man has stable exertional angina despite two antianginal drugs. SPECT shows ischemia in 22 percent of the left ventricle across anterior and inferior territories, with an LVEF of 32 percent. What is the best next step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the stress study with PET in three months after adding a third drug.", "B": "Order viability imaging first, since viability must be shown before any anatomy is defined.", "C": "Refer for coronary angiography to define anatomy for surgical revascularization.", "D": "Order coronary CT angiography to rule out disease and discharge on current therapy."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for coronary angiography to define anatomy for surgical revascularization.): Large ischemic burden with a low EF in a symptomatic patient identifies a group in which bypass surgery can improve survival, so anatomy is needed. Viability testing can follow but is not a prerequisite for defining anatomy. Waiting for repeat imaging delays care, and noninvasive CT would not exclude disease in a patient this high risk."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with frequent premature ventricular contractions has a gated blood pool study reporting a left ventricular ejection fraction of 31 percent, with blurred ventricular edges and a flat time-activity curve. Echocardiography the same week showed 52 percent. Which explanation best accounts for the discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "Ectopic beats in a wide acceptance window blur the averaged cycle and lower the ejection fraction.", "B": "A background region drawn too near the ventricle oversubtracts counts and raises the ejection fraction.", "C": "Patient motion between frames shifts the ventricular region and falsely raises the calculated ejection fraction.", "D": "Incomplete red cell labeling leaves free pertechnetate and falsely lowers the measured ejection fraction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ectopic beats in a wide acceptance window blur the averaged cycle and lower the ejection fraction.): Frequent ectopy produces many short and long cycles. If the beat acceptance window is wide, these mixed beats are summed into one averaged cycle, which blurs the edges and flattens the time-activity curve, so stroke counts and the ejection fraction come out falsely low. Motion and background oversubtraction would tend to raise the value or not explain the flat curve. Poor labeling raises extracardiac activity and lowers contrast but does not change the shape of the cycle."}, {"id": 373, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has sharp, pleuritic left chest pain that occurs at rest and is unrelated to exertion. She has no diabetes, smokes no cigarettes, and her resting ECG is normal. Her primary physician requests a regadenoson PET study. Using pretest probability reasoning, what is the best response from the nuclear cardiologist?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with regadenoson PET, since a normal result excludes coronary disease in any chest pain patient", "B": "Add calcium scoring to the stress study, since a score turns low probability into testable", "C": "Convert to exercise SPECT, since exercise testing raises diagnostic yield in low risk women", "D": "Pretest probability is very low, so perfusion imaging would mostly yield false positives; no testing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pretest probability is very low, so perfusion imaging would mostly yield false positives; no testing): With atypical, nonexertional chest pain in a young woman, prevalence is very low, so a positive result is more likely false than true by Bayes theorem. Testing a low prevalence group yields mostly false positives and little added information. Exercise or calcium scoring does not change the prevalence in the patient and does not rescue the test."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man took his theophylline 300 mg tablet this morning and told no one. Regadenoson stress produced a heart rate rise of only 5 bpm with no symptoms, and the Tc-99m images are normal. How should the result be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Theophylline enhances coronary steal, so the normal result excludes ischemia even more strongly than usual.", "B": "Theophylline blocks adenosine receptors and blunts hyperemia, so the normal result may be falsely negative.", "C": "Regadenoson is selective for A2A receptors, so theophylline cannot antagonize it and the result stands.", "D": "Theophylline affects only dobutamine response, so the weak heart rate rise reflects a technical injection problem."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Theophylline blocks adenosine receptors and blunts hyperemia, so the normal result may be falsely negative.): Methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine competitively antagonize adenosine A1 and A2A receptors, including those targeted by regadenoson, which reduces coronary vasodilation. The small heart rate and symptom response supports blunted stimulation, so the normal result is not reliable. Rescheduling after the drug is held or choosing another stress is appropriate."}, {"id": 375, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 74-year-old man with heart failure and preserved ejection fraction has a Tc-99m PYP planar study at 1 hour with grade 2 cardiac uptake and a heart-to-contralateral ratio of 1.6. No SPECT was acquired. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Obtain SPECT to separate myocardial uptake from blood pool retention, and screen for monoclonal protein.", "B": "Repeat at 24 hours, since the 1-hour ratio is invalid and cannot guide any decision.", "C": "Diagnose transthyretin amyloidosis now, since grade 2 uptake with a ratio over 1.5 is definitive.", "D": "Report light chain amyloidosis, since PYP uptake grading separates it from transthyretin disease without blood tests."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Obtain SPECT to separate myocardial uptake from blood pool retention, and screen for monoclonal protein.): Planar uptake can reflect blood pool retention in the ventricular cavity rather than myocardial uptake, so SPECT is needed to confirm that the tracer sits in the myocardium. Before diagnosing transthyretin amyloidosis non-invasively, serum and urine immunofixation and free light chains must exclude a monoclonal protein. PYP does not distinguish amyloid types by uptake grade, and the 1-hour image remains usable."}, {"id": 376, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Ninety seconds after a regadenoson bolus and tracer injection, a 70-year-old patient develops new Mobitz II second-degree AV block with blood pressure 78/40 and dizziness. Heart rate is 38 per minute and the patient has no pacemaker. Which is the best first drug?", "options": {"A": "Atropine 1 mg IV, which overrides the vagal component of the block", "B": "Aminophylline 100 mg slow IV, which antagonizes the adenosine receptor effect on the AV node", "C": "Observe without drug because regadenoson effects resolve within 30 minutes and need no antagonist", "D": "Dobutamine infusion to raise the heart rate and blood pressure at once during the block"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aminophylline 100 mg slow IV, which antagonizes the adenosine receptor effect on the AV node): Regadenoson activates adenosine receptors, and severe AV block with hypotension is reversed by aminophylline, a competitive antagonist, given slowly. Atropine acts on vagal tone and may fail in a receptor-mediated block, dobutamine increases myocardial demand in a hypotensive patient, and watching alone is inappropriate when the patient is symptomatic with a heart rate of 38."}, {"id": 377, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 36 and atypical chest pain has supine sestamibi SPECT followed by prone images. The exhibit summarizes the findings, including gated function. What is the best interpretation of the inferior wall, taking all the information together?", "options": {"A": "Fixed ischemia in two territories, because the prone images show a new anterior change.", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation; the inferior wall is normal given normal thickening.", "C": "Hibernating myocardium requiring FDG, since the defect did not reverse with rest imaging.", "D": "Inferior scar, since a fixed defect is infarction and normal thickening is partial volume effect."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diaphragmatic attenuation; the inferior wall is normal given normal thickening.): A defect that resolves with prone positioning and shows normal thickening is attenuation, which is common in heavy patients. Scar would thin and hypokinetic segments. The new anterior reduction on prone images is a known positional artifact and does not indicate disease in another territory.", "image": "question_images/q_377.png"}, {"id": 378, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient reaches 90 percent of age-predicted maximum heart rate on a treadmill sestamibi study at stage 3, with mild chest discomfort and 1 mm ST depression. When should the tracer be injected, and what should the patient do afterward?", "options": {"A": "Inject once heart rate has returned within 10 beats of baseline, so images are stable.", "B": "Inject 3 minutes into recovery, when the ST depression is largest on the monitor.", "C": "Inject at peak exercise, then continue exercising about 1 minute for tracer extraction.", "D": "Inject immediately and stop the treadmill at that moment to limit the duration of ischemia."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inject at peak exercise, then continue exercising about 1 minute for tracer extraction.): The tracer is extracted by the myocardium within about a minute, so the stress state must be maintained after injection. Stopping at once or injecting in recovery lets blood flow change before uptake is complete and reduces defect size. Waiting for baseline heart rate loses the stress image entirely."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A lab manager is writing the policy for a technologist who has declared her pregnancy in writing. She works daily with Tc-99m and F-18 syringes. Which dose limit applies to her embryo and fetus for the entire gestation period under 10 CFR Part 20?", "options": {"A": "1.5 rem (15 mSv), the limit set for the lens of the eye", "B": "5 rem (50 mSv), the annual total effective dose limit for adult radiation workers", "C": "0.1 rem (1 mSv), the annual limit that applies to members of the public", "D": "0.5 rem (5 mSv), counted from the estimated date of conception"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (0.5 rem (5 mSv), counted from the estimated date of conception): The dose limit to the embryo and fetus of a declared pregnant worker is 0.5 rem (5 mSv) for the entire gestation period, with efforts to avoid substantial variation above a uniform monthly rate. The public limit is 0.1 rem per year, the adult occupational total effective dose limit is 5 rem per year, and the lens limit is 15 rem per year."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A hot lab entrance is posted 'Caution Radioactive Materials'. Inside, staff can reach a lead-lined decay bin holding Tc-99m syringes. A survey finds 0.08 mSv/h (8 mrem/h) at 30 cm from the bin surface. What posting is required for the area around the bin?", "options": {"A": "No added posting, since Tc-99m has a six hour half-life and the level will fall quickly", "B": "A 'Caution High Radiation Area' sign, since the reading is above the 5 mrem/h threshold", "C": "A 'Caution Radiation Area' sign, since the level at 30 cm exceeds 0.05 mSv/h (5 mrem/h)", "D": "The existing radioactive materials sign on the lab entrance is enough for any level inside"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A 'Caution Radiation Area' sign, since the level at 30 cm exceeds 0.05 mSv/h (5 mrem/h)): Under 10 CFR 20.1902, a radiation area is one where an individual could receive more than 0.05 mSv (5 mrem) in an hour at 30 cm from the source. At 8 mrem/h the bin area qualifies and needs its own sign. A high radiation area starts at 1 mSv (100 mrem) per hour, and decay does not remove the posting requirement while the level exists."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During minute 3 of adenosine at 140 mcg/kg/min combined with slow walking, a patient develops complete heart block with a rate of 38, blood pressure of 78/40 and lightheadedness. Tracer has not been injected and the infusion is still running. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Give atropine 1 mg IV and continue the infusion for the full six minutes.", "B": "Give aminophylline 100 mg IV before stopping the infusion, to reverse the receptor effect.", "C": "Keep the patient walking to raise heart rate and overcome the block.", "D": "Stop the adenosine, stop walking and lay the patient down; effects usually resolve within seconds."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the adenosine, stop walking and lay the patient down; effects usually resolve within seconds.): Adenosine has a half-life under 10 seconds, so stopping the infusion removes the cause fastest. Stopping exercise and lying down supports blood pressure. Aminophylline is given only if the block or hypotension persists after stopping. Continuing the infusion or exercise keeps the stimulus on the AV node and risks asystole."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 48-year-old with unexplained high-grade AV block has FDG PET for cardiac sarcoidosis after only a 4 hour fast and no dietary restriction. The images show diffuse myocardial FDG uptake with normal perfusion. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Normal fasting pattern, because the myocardium takes up FDG in any fasted patient.", "B": "Suppression was inadequate, so repeat after proper dietary preparation and a prolonged fast.", "C": "Active diffuse cardiac sarcoidosis is confirmed, so start corticosteroid treatment now.", "D": "Cardiac amyloidosis, so proceed to pyrophosphate imaging and withhold steroids."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suppression was inadequate, so repeat after proper dietary preparation and a prolonged fast.): Myocardial FDG uptake is physiologic when glucose is the fuel, so preparation with a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet and a prolonged fast is needed to suppress it. Diffuse uptake after a short fast is nonspecific and should not be read as active sarcoid. The study should be repeated with proper preparation."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 76-year-old woman in rehabilitation after a hip fracture has limited exercise tolerance but can walk slowly on level ground. Her physician wants an exercise stress SPECT study if it can be done safely, and she has no ECG abnormality at rest. Which exercise protocol best suits her situation?", "options": {"A": "Standard Bruce started at stage 2, to shorten the test and reduce total exercise time.", "B": "The Naughton protocol, which starts at a low workload and rises about one MET per stage.", "C": "Standard Bruce from stage 1, since its three-minute stages are the most widely validated protocol.", "D": "Standard Bruce with handrail support, to offset the large step increases in workload."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Naughton protocol, which starts at a low workload and rises about one MET per stage.): Standard Bruce increases workload in large steps and begins near 4.6 METs, which is difficult for older or deconditioned patients. Naughton starts around 2 METs and rises about 1 MET per stage. Handrail support inflates the apparent capacity and reduces ischemic stress, so it should be avoided."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist injects 25 mCi of Tc-99m sestamibi meant for patient A into patient B, who was scheduled for a different test. Patient B's estimated effective dose is 8 mSv and no harm is expected. How should the event be classified under NRC rules?", "options": {"A": "Not a medical event, since Tc-99m diagnostic doses need no written directive", "B": "Reportable because the dose differs from the prescribed dose by over 20 percent", "C": "A medical event only if the effective dose to patient B exceeded 50 mSv", "D": "A medical event because the wrong patient received it, needing an NRC call next day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Not a medical event, since Tc-99m diagnostic doses need no written directive): A medical event under 10 CFR 35.3045 is defined for administrations that require a written directive, such as therapeutic I-131 or diagnostic I-131 above 30 µCi. Tc-99m sestamibi does not need one, so the error is not a medical event. It still belongs in the facility's quality program with records and corrective action, and the patient and referring physician should be told."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with known CAD, prior stenting and CABG presents to the ED this evening with chest discomfort that has since resolved, and the exhibit summarizes his workup. Which next test best fits appropriate use criteria for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Exercise SPECT MPI, because stress imaging localizes ischemia in known CAD", "B": "Treadmill ECG without imaging, because a normal resting ECG makes imaging unnecessary", "C": "Immediate invasive angiography, because prior CABG makes every chest pain presentation high risk", "D": "Coronary CT angiography, because it replaces functional testing after bypass surgery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exercise SPECT MPI, because stress imaging localizes ischemia in known CAD): With two negative serial troponins, a nonischemic ECG, resolved pain and good exercise capacity, he is low risk for ongoing acute coronary syndrome. In known CAD with stents and grafts, stress imaging identifies the territory and extent of ischemia. Treadmill ECG alone cannot localize it, CT angiography is limited by stents and calcium, and catheterization is not needed on these findings.", "image": "question_images/q_385.png"}, {"id": 386, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man has a lateral wall defect on stress SPECT. The processing screen lists a summed stress score of 13 and a summed rest score of 9, both confined to the lateral wall, so counts increase at rest but stay below the level of normal walls. Which term describes this pattern, and what does it imply about the myocardium?", "options": {"A": "Reverse redistribution, implying rest counts fall below stress counts in the lateral wall", "B": "Partially reversible defect, implying infarcted tissue with residual ischemic myocardium", "C": "Reversible defect, implying counts return to the level of the normal walls at rest", "D": "Fixed defect, implying scar with no viable myocardium remaining in the lateral wall of the ventricle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Partially reversible defect, implying infarcted tissue with residual ischemic myocardium): A defect that improves on rest images is reversible. If the improvement stops short of normal counts, the region holds both scar and ischemic tissue. Fixed means no meaningful change, and reverse redistribution means worse counts at rest, the opposite of what is described.", "image": "question_images/q_386.png"}, {"id": 387, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man with BMI 27 kg/m2, no prior infarction and a normal resting ECG exercises to 11 METs and 92 percent of predicted rate with no symptoms. Tc-99m sestamibi stress images look normal with normal gated function. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Confirm image quality and gated function, then end the study without rest imaging.", "B": "Repeat stress with Tl-201 the next day, since a second tracer confirms a normal technetium study.", "C": "Inject a rest dose after 45 minutes, since rest comparison is needed to confirm normal perfusion.", "D": "Acquire prone stress images and then perform rest imaging, since supine images cannot be trusted alone."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirm image quality and gated function, then end the study without rest imaging.): Stress-first imaging is designed for low-risk patients, and a normal stress study with normal function and good image quality reliably excludes significant disease. Rest imaging would add dose, time and cost without altering the result. The conditions are no known infarct, adequate stress and technically clean images, all of which are met here. A second tracer or extra positioning does not add information."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63 year old man with 20 years of diabetes and neuropathy has no chest pain but reports new dyspnea after climbing one flight of stairs. His rest ECG is normal and he cannot exercise beyond a slow walk. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Resting echocardiography, since the absence of chest pain makes ischemia unlikely in this patient", "B": "Coronary calcium scoring alone, since a score of zero would exclude any functional ischemia", "C": "Defer testing until typical angina appears, since symptoms are needed to justify stress imaging", "D": "Regadenoson perfusion imaging, since dyspnea may be an anginal equivalent and neuropathy limits exercise"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson perfusion imaging, since dyspnea may be an anginal equivalent and neuropathy limits exercise): Diabetic patients often present with dyspnea or fatigue instead of chest pain, so exertional dyspnea is an anginal equivalent and justifies stress imaging. A slow-walking patient is unlikely to exercise adequately, so a vasodilator is appropriate. Normal rest echo and a zero calcium score do not exclude flow-limiting disease."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Two minutes after a 0.4 mg regadenoson bolus, a 72-year-old man with known coronary disease has a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Tc-99m sestamibi was already injected 40 seconds after the bolus, and his blood pressure is 150/90. Which treatment is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Give intravenous metoprolol 5 mg to lower the heart rate and reduce cerebral oxygen demand", "B": "Give aminophylline 100 mg slowly intravenously to reverse the adenosine receptor agonist effect", "C": "Give a benzodiazepine such as lorazepam, protect the airway, and avoid aminophylline", "D": "Observe for five minutes and give sublingual nitroglycerin if the pressure stays above 140"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give a benzodiazepine such as lorazepam, protect the airway, and avoid aminophylline): Aminophylline normally reverses regadenoson effects, but it lowers the seizure threshold and can prolong or worsen seizures, so it is avoided when the adverse event is a seizure. Standard anticonvulsant treatment with a benzodiazepine and airway protection is appropriate. A beta-blocker does nothing for the seizure, and waiting while giving nitroglycerin treats the wrong problem and delays definitive control."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman receiving trastuzumab for breast cancer has no symptoms. Her baseline MUGA EF was 62 percent and the repeat is 38 percent on two separate acquisitions with tight beat rejection, and no ectopy or breathlessness. Which management is best?", "options": {"A": "Continue trastuzumab unchanged and repeat the MUGA at the next scheduled cycle in three weeks.", "B": "Start a beta blocker alone and recheck the EF in six months while treatment continues.", "C": "Hold trastuzumab, start heart failure therapy, repeat EF in 2 to 4 weeks, resume if recovered.", "D": "Discontinue trastuzumab permanently, since an EF below 40 percent is irreversible in all patients."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold trastuzumab, start heart failure therapy, repeat EF in 2 to 4 weeks, resume if recovered.): An EF fall of 10 points or more to below 40 percent, confirmed on repeat, is severe cardiotoxicity. Standard practice holds the drug, starts heart failure therapy and rechecks within weeks, because trastuzumab related dysfunction often recovers and rechallenge may be possible. Unchanged treatment risks harm, permanent cessation presumes irreversibility, and a six month gap is too long."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 100 kg man had a supine SPECT showing a fixed inferior defect, and prone imaging is added to separate attenuation from infarct. Both acquisitions use a dual-head 90 degree camera with 32 stops per head at 25 seconds per stop. What is the approximate total acquisition time?", "options": {"A": "About 7 minutes, since the two heads divide the time again.", "B": "About 53 minutes, since both heads are counted separately for each position.", "C": "About 13 minutes, because only one of the two acquisitions is actually counted.", "D": "About 27 minutes, since each position takes 32 stops at 25 seconds."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 27 minutes, since each position takes 32 stops at 25 seconds.): Each head covers 90 degrees with 32 stops, so one position takes 32 times 25 seconds, or 800 seconds, about 13 minutes, because the heads acquire at the same time. Two positions give about 27 minutes. Dividing again by two heads or counting both heads in series gives incorrect times."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man has a regadenoson SPECT study. Segment scoring on the 17-segment model gives a summed stress score of 11 and a summed rest score of 8. The ejection fraction is 58 percent. What is the summed difference score, and what does it indicate about this patient?", "options": {"A": "Difference score is 8, the rest score, showing infarct scar only and no ischemia.", "B": "Difference score is minus 3, rest minus stress, showing reverse redistribution from partial volume.", "C": "Difference score is 3, stress minus rest, showing mild ischemia on top of scar.", "D": "Difference score is 19, stress plus rest, showing extensive ischemia needing urgent revascularization."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Difference score is 3, stress minus rest, showing mild ischemia on top of scar.): The difference score is the summed stress score minus the summed rest score, 11 minus 8 equals 3. A value of 1 to 3 is mild ischemia, and the rest score of 8 represents the fixed, scarred component. Adding the scores gives a meaningless 19. The rest score alone ignores the reversible part. Reversing the subtraction gives a negative number that would imply reverse redistribution, not this finding.", "image": "question_images/q_392.png"}, {"id": 393, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man undergoes rest-stress SPECT. The reader records a summed stress score of 16 and a summed rest score of 4 on the 17-segment model. What is the summed difference score, and what is the best interpretation of the study?", "options": {"A": "Summed difference score of 12, indicating predominantly ischemia with a small fixed component.", "B": "Summed difference score of 20, indicating a large infarct because the sums are combined.", "C": "Summed difference score of 12, indicating predominantly fixed infarct with minimal ischemia.", "D": "Summed difference score of 4, indicating mostly infarct because rest defects define the difference."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Summed difference score of 12, indicating predominantly ischemia with a small fixed component.): The difference score is the stress sum minus the rest sum, so 16 minus 4 is 12. A large difference compared with the rest sum means most of the abnormality reverses at rest, which is ischemia, while the rest sum of 4 shows a small fixed, infarct-like component. Adding the sums or reading the rest sum alone gives the wrong value, and reading 12 as fixed inverts its meaning."}, {"id": 394, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a BMI of 33 has a fixed inferior defect on supine SPECT sestamibi imaging, with normal wall motion on gated images and no transient ischemic dilation. The camera has no CT attenuation correction. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the rest images with arms at his sides, since arm position improves inferior wall visualization.", "B": "Repeat the stress acquisition prone, which moves the diaphragm and bowel away from the heart.", "C": "Refer him directly to angiography, since a fixed inferior defect in an obese man indicates infarction.", "D": "Add four-hour redistribution images, since delayed uptake typically resolves attenuation in the inferior wall."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the stress acquisition prone, which moves the diaphragm and bowel away from the heart.): A fixed inferior defect with normal thickening in a large patient suggests diaphragmatic attenuation. Prone imaging displaces the diaphragm and can separate artifact from scar. Direct angiography is premature, arm position does not help the inferior wall, and delayed imaging with sestamibi does not resolve attenuation."}, {"id": 395, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A non-contrast gated cardiac CT for calcium scoring shows two coronary lesions on one slice. Lesion one has an area of 6 mm2 with a peak density of 220 HU, and lesion two has an area of 10 mm2 with a peak of 410 HU. What is the Agatston score for this slice?", "options": {"A": "64, from 16 mm2 multiplied by the highest density factor of 4", "B": "52, from 6 x 2 plus 10 x 4", "C": "44, from 6 x 4 plus 10 x 2 with factors swapped", "D": "16, from the sum of the two lesion areas"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (52, from 6 x 2 plus 10 x 4): The Agatston score multiplies each lesion area by a density factor set by its peak attenuation: 1 for 130 to 199 HU, 2 for 200 to 299, 3 for 300 to 399 and 4 for 400 or more. Lesion one at 220 HU has factor 2 and gives 12, lesion two at 410 HU has factor 4 and gives 40, for a total of 52. The wrong values come from one factor for both lesions, swapped factors or area alone. A low-dose free-breathing attenuation CT is not suitable for scoring."}, {"id": 396, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist gave a 118 kg patient with BMI 40 kg/m2 the same 8 and 24 mCi Tc-99m doses used for a 70 kg patient, and the images show low counts and a noisy inferior wall. Which statement identifies the cause and the correction?", "options": {"A": "The rest dose scattered into the stress image, so injections should be 3 hours apart.", "B": "Hepatic clearance in obesity removes tracer before imaging, so images must be acquired within 5 minutes.", "C": "Tc-99m extraction falls in obese patients, so thallium must replace technetium for large patients.", "D": "Dose was not scaled to body size, so counts fell; weight-adjusted activity is needed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dose was not scaled to body size, so counts fell; weight-adjusted activity is needed.): Photon attenuation and scatter in larger patients reduce count density, so activity should be adjusted for weight or body habitus, with longer acquisition or a two-day protocol as options. Technetium extraction is not reduced in obesity, hepatic clearance is not the cause, and residual rest activity would add counts, not lower them."}, {"id": 397, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A lab's brochure lists a one day Tc-99m sestamibi study as 9 mSv, based on 30 mCi total. A 140 kg patient receives 45 mCi total at this lab. What is the most defensible estimate of her effective dose now?", "options": {"A": "About 20 mSv, because dose rises with the square of the administered activity", "B": "About 9 mSv, because the brochure value applies to the protocol whatever the activity", "C": "About 13.5 mSv, because effective dose scales in proportion to administered activity", "D": "About 6 mSv, because a larger body attenuates more and lowers the internal dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 13.5 mSv, because effective dose scales in proportion to administered activity): For the same radiopharmaceutical and biodistribution, effective dose scales directly with administered activity: 9 mSv times 45 over 30 is 13.5 mSv. A table value reflects its standard activity, and body size does not lower the internal dose in this simple model. A squared relation is not used."}, {"id": 398, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old woman drank two cups of coffee 6 hours ago. She receives regadenoson 0.4 mg, and her heart rate rises by only 4 bpm with no flushing or dyspnea. The technologist asks whether to inject the tracer. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Inject the tracer after giving aminophylline, which restores the response of the coronary vessels", "B": "Caffeine can block the A2A receptor and blunt hyperemia; reschedule after 12 caffeine-free hours", "C": "Inject the tracer, since regadenoson is selective and caffeine cannot affect its coronary vasodilation", "D": "Inject the tracer and add 0.4 mg of regadenoson to overcome the competitive blockade by caffeine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Caffeine can block the A2A receptor and blunt hyperemia; reschedule after 12 caffeine-free hours): Methylxanthines such as caffeine compete at adenosine receptors, including the A2A receptor, and can blunt the hyperemic response to regadenoson, increasing the chance of a falsely negative study. A heart rate rise of only 4 bpm without symptoms suggests inadequate response. Caffeine should be held at least 12 hours, so rescheduling is best. A second bolus is not recommended, and aminophylline is itself a blocker."}, {"id": 399, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old obese man with no known coronary disease has a fixed mild inferior defect on both stress and rest sestamibi images. Gated function shows normal inferior wall thickening and an ejection fraction of 60 percent. What is the most likely consequence if the defect is read as an infarct?", "options": {"A": "A false ischemia call raises the dilation ratio and produces a spurious post-stress ejection fraction drop.", "B": "Transmural infarction is missed and urgent revascularization is delayed because the defect is underreported.", "C": "The patient receives excess radiation because attenuation is corrected by repeating injection at higher dose.", "D": "A false infarct diagnosis triggers needless angiography; normal inferior thickening suggests attenuation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A false infarct diagnosis triggers needless angiography; normal inferior thickening suggests attenuation.): A fixed inferior defect with normal wall thickening in an obese man is typical of diaphragmatic attenuation. Reading it as infarct creates a false diagnosis of prior myocardial infarction, prompts needless invasive testing and distorts prognosis. Gating, prone imaging or attenuation correction can clarify the finding. Attenuation does not alter the dilation ratio, and correction does not require repeat dosing."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "An 80 kg man with no bronchospasm, a systolic pressure of 126 mmHg and no high-grade AV block is cleared for adenosine stress at 140 mcg/kg/min over 6 minutes, using a 3 mg/mL syringe. At what pump rate should the infusion run?", "options": {"A": "3.7 mL/min, from 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg divided by 3 mg/mL", "B": "8.2 mL/min, from the dose times 176 pounds instead of kilograms", "C": "0.047 mL/min, from 140 mcg/min divided by 3 mg/mL with no weight applied", "D": "0.37 mL/min, from the dose divided by 30 mg/mL instead of 3 mg/mL"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3.7 mL/min, from 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg divided by 3 mg/mL): The dose is 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg, equal to 11,200 mcg/min or 11.2 mg/min. Dividing by 3 mg/mL gives 3.7 mL/min, and over 6 minutes the patient receives about 67 mg. Leaving out the weight, entering pounds, or misreading the concentration each shifts the result by a factor that would under- or overdose him."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "On a gated blood pool study, the uncorrected end-diastolic left ventricular counts are 52,000 and the uncorrected end-systolic counts are 24,000. Background counts measured in the same region are 6,000. What is the background-corrected left ventricular ejection fraction that should be reported for this patient?", "options": {"A": "46 percent, calculated as end-systolic counts over end-diastolic counts after correction.", "B": "61 percent, from 28,000 net stroke counts divided by 46,000 corrected end-diastolic counts.", "C": "65 percent, calculated by correcting only the end-systolic count before dividing.", "D": "54 percent, calculated without any background subtraction from either count."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (61 percent, from 28,000 net stroke counts divided by 46,000 corrected end-diastolic counts.): Ejection fraction is (ED counts minus ES counts) divided by (ED counts minus background). Subtracting background gives 46,000 and 18,000, so the stroke counts are 28,000 and the EF is 28,000 divided by 46,000, which is 61 percent. Without correction the value is 54 percent, and correcting only one count gives 65 percent."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 46 year old woman of normal weight with low pretest probability is booked for rest and stress Tc-99m sestamibi on a conventional camera. The physician wants to lower her radiation dose without losing diagnostic value. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Rest and redistribution Tl-201 imaging, which gives a lower effective dose than sestamibi", "B": "Half the activity for both injections with acquisition time unchanged to save dose", "C": "A stress first protocol with weight based dosing, omitting rest imaging if stress is normal", "D": "A two day protocol, which lowers the total dose by splitting it across two visits"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A stress first protocol with weight based dosing, omitting rest imaging if stress is normal): In a patient with low pretest probability, stress first imaging allows the rest study and its tracer dose to be omitted when the stress images are normal, and weight based dosing avoids excess activity. Thallium gives a higher effective dose than sestamibi, halving activity without longer acquisition degrades counts, and a two day protocol delivers the same total activity."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy, an EF of 28 percent and NYHA class III symptoms despite 4 months of optimal therapy has a late I-123 MIBG heart-to-mediastinum ratio of 1.31. What does that result mean for his further management?", "options": {"A": "A ratio below 1.60 indicates low risk, so the ICD can be deferred despite his EF", "B": "A ratio below 1.60 means ischemia, so coronary angiography is needed before any other step", "C": "A ratio below 1.60 is itself an indication for ICD implantation even with preserved EF", "D": "Late ratio below 1.60 marks higher risk but does not replace EF-based criteria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Late ratio below 1.60 marks higher risk but does not replace EF-based criteria): A late heart-to-mediastinum ratio below about 1.60 reflects impaired cardiac sympathetic innervation and predicts higher risk of death and arrhythmic events in heart failure. It informs risk stratification, but device decisions remain anchored on EF and clinical criteria, which this man already meets. It does not diagnose ischemia, and a low ratio signals higher, not lower, risk."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory manager proposes a same-day dual-isotope protocol for stable outpatients, using rest Tl-201 followed immediately by stress Tc-99m sestamibi, to shorten patient time in a busy department. Which statement best describes one advantage and one drawback of this protocol?", "options": {"A": "It lowers total radiation below single-isotope Tc-99m protocols, but is less sensitive for viability", "B": "It allows simultaneous acquisition of rest and stress images, but needs both tracers at peak exercise", "C": "It shortens the visit to about two hours, but raises dose and adds Tc-99m crosstalk", "D": "It removes attenuation artifacts by using lower energy thallium photons, but the visit lasts all day"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It shortens the visit to about two hours, but raises dose and adds Tc-99m crosstalk): Rest thallium followed immediately by stress Tc-99m takes about 1 to 2 hours but delivers a higher effective dose because of Tl-201, and the higher energy Tc-99m photons scatter into the thallium window (downscatter), degrading rest images. Thallium has more, not less, attenuation, and the two tracers are not injected together."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has atypical chest pain with a pretest probability of obstructive coronary disease of 5 percent. Her SPECT study is abnormal. The laboratory reports sensitivity 87 percent and specificity 73 percent. Which interpretation of the result is best?", "options": {"A": "About 73 percent chance of obstructive disease, matching the specificity reported by the laboratory.", "B": "About 15 percent chance of obstructive disease, since most positives at this prevalence are false.", "C": "About 87 percent chance of obstructive disease, matching the sensitivity reported by the laboratory.", "D": "About 50 percent chance of obstructive disease, since abnormal results are equally likely true or false."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 15 percent chance of obstructive disease, since most positives at this prevalence are false.): With a prevalence of 5 percent, true positives are 0.05 x 0.87 = 0.0435 and false positives are 0.95 x 0.27 = 0.2565. Positive predictive value is 0.0435 divided by 0.300, or about 14.5 percent. Sensitivity and specificity describe the test, not the probability in this patient."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist infiltrates part of a 3.0 mCi (111 MBq) Tc-99m sestamibi injection in the forearm. Serial counting over the site gave the decay-corrected curve in the chart. For the dose record, which value best states the activity still present at the site 3 hours after injection?", "options": {"A": "About 2.1 mCi, because only the 6 hour physical half-life governs tracer trapped in tissue", "B": "About 0.75 mCi, since physical and biological clearance give a 1.5 hour effective half-life", "C": "About 2.3 mCi, because the 6 hour and 2 hour half-lives add to 8 hours", "D": "About 1.1 mCi, because the 2 hour biological half-life alone determines the retained activity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 0.75 mCi, since physical and biological clearance give a 1.5 hour effective half-life): The chart is decay corrected, so it shows biological clearance alone: 100 percent falls to 50 percent at 2 hours, a biological half-life of 2 hours. The effective half-life is 1 divided by (1/6 plus 1/2), which is 1.5 hours. In 3 hours there are 2 effective half-lives, so 3.0 mCi becomes 0.75 mCi. Physical decay alone would leave 2.1 mCi, biological clearance alone 1.1 mCi, and adding half-lives gives 2.3 mCi, each of which ignores one of the two processes.", "image": "question_images/q_406.png"}, {"id": 407, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A woman has regadenoson stress without exercise and sestamibi. Images begin 20 minutes after injection and show intense hepatobiliary and gut activity abutting the inferior wall, with an apparent inferior defect that was not expected from her history. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report a fixed inferior scar, since the defect persists on the early images.", "B": "Switch to prone positioning only, since that fully removes subdiaphragmatic activity.", "C": "Repeat with a higher dose to overcome the neighboring gut activity.", "D": "Re-image after 45 to 60 minutes with water so abdominal activity clears."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Re-image after 45 to 60 minutes with water so abdominal activity clears.): Without exercise, splanchnic blood flow stays high and hepatobiliary clearance is slow, so 20 minutes is too early. A delay of 45 to 60 minutes with water allows clearance. Increasing dose also increases the extracardiac signal, and a defect near hot gut is often artifact."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A nuclear laboratory is troubled by false anterior defects in women with large breasts on supine SPECT with Tc-99m sestamibi. The ejection fraction in the latest patient is 60 percent. Which technical change best reduces this artifact on the next acquisition?", "options": {"A": "Add CT-based or line-source attenuation correction and compare with prone images.", "B": "Widen the energy window to 40 percent to collect more scattered photons from the breast.", "C": "Use a high resolution collimator and double the acquisition time at each stop.", "D": "Switch to thallium-201, because its lower energy photons penetrate breast tissue more easily."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Add CT-based or line-source attenuation correction and compare with prone images.): Attenuation correction models photon loss through breast tissue, and prone imaging moves the breast away from the anterior wall, so the pair separates artifact from true defects. Thallium-201 has lower energy than technetium agents and is attenuated more. A wider window adds scatter, which degrades contrast. A different collimator or longer time improves counts or resolution but not attenuation.", "image": "question_images/q_408.png"}, {"id": 409, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The physicist's note on the intrinsic flood in the exhibit, for a camera that passed acceptance, describes a thin ring of higher counts at the border. A technologist then positions a 74-year-old woman for a 180 degree sestamibi SPECT, and her heart lies toward one side of the detector. Which positioning change best takes account of the flood?", "options": {"A": "Center the heart in the field of view, away from the bright rim.", "B": "Narrow the energy window to 15 percent so border counts are rejected from the heart region.", "C": "Use the minimum orbit radius so the heart sits near the edge of the field.", "D": "Increase zoom so the heart reaches the detector rim, since the bright border adds wall counts."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Center the heart in the field of view, away from the bright rim.): The flood shows a brighter ring at the border, which reflects distorted position calculation near the edge of the crystal, so counts there are not quantitatively reliable. Centering the heart keeps it in the well-behaved central field. Zooming toward the rim or using minimal orbit radius pushes the heart toward the problem region, and the window does not change position accuracy.", "image": "question_images/q_409.png"}, {"id": 410, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient with an ejection fraction of 25 percent has a thallium-201 study. For the anteroseptal segment, normalized to the peak segment, activity is 28 percent at stress, 41 percent at four-hour redistribution and 54 percent after reinjection. What is the best conclusion about this segment?", "options": {"A": "Nonviable, because viability requires uptake above 80 percent of peak at some point in the protocol.", "B": "Indeterminate, because only a gain of 30 points from stress to reinjection establishes viability.", "C": "Nonviable, because the four-hour redistribution value of 41 percent falls below threshold and defines scar.", "D": "Viable, since reinjection uptake of 54 percent of peak exceeds the roughly 50 percent threshold."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Viable, since reinjection uptake of 54 percent of peak exceeds the roughly 50 percent threshold.): With thallium, a segment with uptake of about 50 to 60 percent of peak or more on late redistribution or reinjection images is considered viable. Reinjection recovers uptake in segments that redistribute slowly, so 54 percent counts as viable. Stopping at 41 percent understates viability. The 80 percent value and a 30-point gain are not accepted criteria."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist realizes she has injected Tc-99m MAA instead of the ordered Tc-99m sestamibi into a patient lying on the stress table. The patient reports no symptoms and the stress test is waiting to begin. What should she do next?", "options": {"A": "Record the dose as sestamibi so that the dose log matches the original order", "B": "Ask the patient to return in a week without telling the authorized user", "C": "Proceed with imaging and correct the record after the study to avoid any delay", "D": "Notify the authorized user and radiation safety staff immediately so she can be assessed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Notify the authorized user and radiation safety staff immediately so she can be assessed): A wrong radiopharmaceutical is an error that the authorized user must know about at once to decide on patient assessment, documentation and corrective steps. Quietly continuing, rescheduling or relabeling the record conceals the event and prevents review. Honest, prompt reporting is the first step even when the patient has no symptoms."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man has a 60 percent LAD stenosis on CT angiography, and the physician needs to know if it limits flow. A Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT read as mildly reduced anterior uptake. The reader notes that technetium extraction plateaus at high flow. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Quantitative PET flow reserve, since Tc-99m roll-off compresses flow differences from moderate stenosis", "B": "Repeat SPECT with a doubled Tc-99m dose, so myocardial uptake becomes proportional to hyperemic flow", "C": "Switch to exercise stress, since lower flow rates avoid roll-off and exaggerate differences", "D": "Accept the mild defect as insignificant, since a 60 percent stenosis cannot limit hyperemic flow"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quantitative PET flow reserve, since Tc-99m roll-off compresses flow differences from moderate stenosis): Extraction of Tc-99m falls as flow rises, so uptake underestimates the flow difference between normal and stenotic territories, and the mild SPECT result may understate its significance. PET with absolute flow quantification gives flow reserve directly. More counts do not correct physiology, and a 60 percent stenosis may still impair hyperemic flow."}, {"id": 413, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader scores 17 segments on a stress and rest SPECT, using scores from 0 for normal to 4 for absent uptake. The chart lists each segment's stress and rest score. What is the summed difference score for this study?", "options": {"A": "Summed difference score of 2, because the rest score alone defines reversible ischemia in these segments.", "B": "Summed difference score of 10, because the stress score alone defines the ischemic difference here.", "C": "Summed difference score of 12, found by adding the stress and rest sums together across segments.", "D": "Summed difference score of 8, reflecting reversible ischemia in the left anterior descending territory."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Summed difference score of 8, reflecting reversible ischemia in the left anterior descending territory.): The stress sum is 2 plus 3 plus 3 plus 2, or 10, and the rest sum is 1 plus 1, or 2. The difference score is stress minus rest, so 10 minus 2 equals 8. Segments 7, 8, 13, and 14 are anterior and apical, the left anterior descending territory. Adding the sums gives 12 and quoting either sum alone gives 10 or 2, all wrong.", "image": "question_images/q_413.png"}, {"id": 414, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A dry, intact Tl-201 shipment is delivered to the security desk at 9:30 pm Thursday. The lab opens at 8:00 am Friday and the technologist surveys the package at 2:00 pm that day. What is the consequence of that timing?", "options": {"A": "A medical event, since a late survey exposes patients to an unmonitored radiopharmaceutical", "B": "The survey was late: monitoring was due by 11:00 am Friday, so a regulatory violation occurred", "C": "No violation, since a dry intact package needs only a wipe within 24 hours of delivery", "D": "The survey was late, since monitoring was required by 12:30 am Friday, three hours after delivery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The survey was late: monitoring was due by 11:00 am Friday, so a regulatory violation occurred): Under 10 CFR 20.1906, a package received after hours must be monitored within 3 hours of the start of the next working day, here 11:00 am. A 2:00 pm survey missed it. The 3 hours from delivery rule applies only to packages received during working hours, and a late survey is a violation, not a medical event."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70-year-old man with prior inferior infarction has a severe fixed inferior defect on 4-hour Tl-201 redistribution images, and the question is whether it contains viable myocardium in that territory. Which next step is best for determining viability with the same tracer?", "options": {"A": "Reinject about 1 mCi of Tl-201, then image 30 to 60 minutes later", "B": "Repeat 4-hour imaging the next morning without a new injection to look for more redistribution", "C": "Inject Tc-99m sestamibi at rest and image 10 minutes later, since it redistributes like thallium", "D": "Image again after dipyridamole infusion, since vasodilator stress reveals viable but fixed defects"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reinject about 1 mCi of Tl-201, then image 30 to 60 minutes later): Thallium redistributes slowly, so a defect that looks fixed at 4 hours can still contain viable myocardium. A small reinjection of about 1 mCi, imaged after 30 to 60 minutes, improves uptake in viable regions and is a standard viability technique. Waiting for 24-hour images without reinjection gives low counts, sestamibi does not redistribute, and vasodilator stress tests flow reserve, not viability."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A cardiologist is discussing how pretest probability alters the meaning of an abnormal perfusion study result. For a test with fixed sensitivity and specificity, which change in the tested population most directly raises the positive predictive value of an abnormal result?", "options": {"A": "A lower prevalence of obstructive coronary disease, which concentrates true positive results among abnormal studies", "B": "A higher prevalence of obstructive coronary disease in the tested population", "C": "A longer acquisition time for each stress imaging sequence, which improves count statistics", "D": "More normal studies being read as normal by the same reader in this population"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A higher prevalence of obstructive coronary disease in the tested population): Positive predictive value depends on prevalence as well as sensitivity and specificity. As prevalence rises, a larger share of abnormal results are true positives. Lower prevalence reduces it. Acquisition time and the count of normal reads change neither sensitivity nor specificity."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man presented to the emergency department with 40 minutes of chest pain that resolved 3 hours ago. Troponin is negative twice and the ECG is nonspecific. Rest sestamibi was injected only after the pain had resolved, and the rest SPECT is normal. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Gated EF of 60 percent confirms absence of ischemia, so stress testing is unnecessary.", "B": "Normal rest perfusion excludes acute coronary syndrome, so the patient may go home untested.", "C": "Repeat the rest imaging with Tl-201 redistribution to confirm the normal result before stress testing proceeds.", "D": "Late injection lowers sensitivity, so normal rest imaging does not exclude ischemia; proceed to stress imaging."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Late injection lowers sensitivity, so normal rest imaging does not exclude ischemia; proceed to stress imaging.): The high negative predictive value of rest imaging in acute chest pain depends on injection during pain or very soon after. Tracer given 3 hours after resolution may reflect recovered flow, so a normal study is less reassuring. Stress imaging is the logical next step. Redistribution imaging does not fix the timing problem, and a normal EF does not exclude inducible ischemia."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 62-year-old man with frequent premature ventricular contractions has a blood pool study. Software reports an EF near 85 percent from 17,992 counts in the first frame and 2,680 at the lowest frame. Which finding on the curve shows that this value cannot be trusted?", "options": {"A": "The curve declines during the first third of the cycle, indicating normal systolic ejection", "B": "The curve does not return to its starting count, so late frames are underfilled", "C": "The curve starts at its highest count, which is expected for a true end-diastolic frame", "D": "The first frame count exceeds 17,000, indicating more than enough injected activity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The curve does not return to its starting count, so late frames are underfilled): A valid cycle ends near its starting count because the cardiac cycle repeats. Here the last frames sit around 2,700 against about 18,000, so the late frames lost counts, a sign that short ectopic-coupled beats were included. The high first-frame count, the early decline from ejection and a good count density are all normal and do not undermine the result. The 85 percent EF is an artifact of poor gating.", "image": "question_images/q_418.png"}, {"id": 419, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old diabetic man has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. Perfusion shows a modest reversible inferior defect with an SSS of 7 and SDS of 5. The TID ratio is 1.38 and the stress EF is 52 percent. What does the TID finding most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "A normal variant in diabetic patients that carries no added risk beyond the SSS.", "B": "Nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy from diabetes, which explains the larger stress cavity.", "C": "A technical artifact from different count levels, so the ratio should be ignored in risk grading.", "D": "Severe extensive disease, such as multivessel ischemia, that the modest defect may understate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Severe extensive disease, such as multivessel ischemia, that the modest defect may understate.): TID above the normal upper limit for sestamibi, about 1.2, reflects diffuse subendocardial hypoperfusion after stress, which makes the cavity look larger. It signals severe extensive disease even when the visible defect is small. Technical causes exist but are not the default. A resting cardiomyopathy would not enlarge the cavity only on stress, and TID adds risk beyond the SSS."}, {"id": 420, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 47-year-old man with nonanginal chest pain has a normal exercise SPECT. The reader notes slightly lower counts at the basal septum and apex on both rows, and the processing screen lists summed stress and rest scores of zero. Ejection fraction is normal. Which explanation accounts for these mild reductions?", "options": {"A": "Normal anatomy, with the membranous septum and thin apex yielding slightly lower counts in healthy hearts", "B": "Early small vessel disease in the anterior descending territory affecting both walls of the left ventricle", "C": "Scatter from bowel activity in the subdiaphragmatic region reducing counts at the base on both rows", "D": "Patient motion causing a vertical shift of the heart during acquisition of the two datasets"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Normal anatomy, with the membranous septum and thin apex yielding slightly lower counts in healthy hearts): The basal septum includes the thin membranous portion, and the apex is naturally thin, so both show slightly lower counts on normal studies. The finding is similar on stress and rest and mild. Subdiaphragmatic activity affects the inferior wall, and motion artifacts vary between acquisitions and appear on the raw projection images.", "image": "question_images/q_420.png"}, {"id": 421, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Rb-82 PET in a 120 kg man with suspected multivessel disease and a prior normal SPECT gives global rest flow of 1.10 mL/min/g and stress flow of 1.87 mL/min/g during regadenoson. What are the myocardial flow reserve and its meaning?", "options": {"A": "0.59, since rest flow divided by stress flow is lower than 1, showing severe impairment.", "B": "1.7, below normal near 2.0, consistent with diffuse disease that SPECT can miss.", "C": "0.77, taken as stress flow minus rest flow, which is normal because it is under 1.", "D": "2.97, from adding rest and stress flow, which is normal and excludes multivessel disease."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1.7, below normal near 2.0, consistent with diffuse disease that SPECT can miss.): Myocardial flow reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow: 1.87 divided by 1.10 is 1.7. Values below about 2.0 suggest impaired reserve, and global reduction can occur with balanced multivessel disease that relative perfusion images miss. This is a strength of PET with absolute flow, particularly in obese patients."}, {"id": 422, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with intermediate pretest probability cannot exercise because of severe knee arthritis. She takes an inhaled steroid and bronchodilator daily and is audibly wheezing loudly in the waiting room today. Which stressor is best for her MPI study?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson at the standard bolus, because it is selective and safe in any asthma", "B": "Dipyridamole infusion at 0.14 mg/kg/min, because it is gentler than adenosine in airway disease", "C": "Treadmill exercise with a slow protocol, because arthritis does not affect heart rate response", "D": "Dobutamine with atropine if needed, since active wheezing argues against vasodilators"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dobutamine with atropine if needed, since active wheezing argues against vasodilators): Active wheezing makes adenosine, dipyridamole and, in practice, regadenoson unattractive because they can provoke bronchospasm. Dobutamine raises demand through beta stimulation and does not act on adenosine receptors, so it is the preferred alternative. Dipyridamole acts by raising adenosine, so it shares the airway risk, and arthritis prevents an adequate treadmill workload."}, {"id": 423, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman receiving anthracycline has serial echocardiograms with poor windows: 58 percent at baseline and 49 percent on repeat by a different sonographer. She has a recently implanted pacemaker that is not MR conditional. Oncology may stop therapy. Which test best prevents a wrong decision?", "options": {"A": "Equilibrium radionuclide ventriculography, since count-based ejection fraction is reproducible without acoustic windows.", "B": "Repeat echocardiography in one week by the same sonographer, accepting the poor windows and operator variability.", "C": "Cardiac magnetic resonance with gadolinium, because it is the reference standard for ejection fraction measurement.", "D": "Gated Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT, because perfusion imaging provides the most reliable serial ejection fraction values."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Equilibrium radionuclide ventriculography, since count-based ejection fraction is reproducible without acoustic windows.): Interstudy variability of echo ejection fraction can exceed the 10-point change that triggers a decision, so acting on these two values risks stopping or continuing therapy wrongly. Blood pool counts give a geometry-free, reproducible measure for serial comparison. MR would normally compete, but the non-conditional device removes it. Gated perfusion SPECT is less reproducible in small hearts."}, {"id": 424, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During the weekly intrinsic flood on a dual-head camera, with collimators removed and a Tc-99m point source placed 2 meters from the detector, the technologist sees the exhibit for head 1. The camera passed the same test last week. A stress SPECT patient is waiting. What is the most likely cause and the best next action?", "options": {"A": "Cracked crystal producing a thin cold line; schedule a crystal check after the current patient.", "B": "Photomultiplier tube failure at that location; take the head out of clinical use and request service.", "C": "Decay of the flood source since the morning; repeat with a more active source and continue.", "D": "Damaged collimator septa at that location; replace the collimator and then resume studies today."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Photomultiplier tube failure at that location; take the head out of clinical use and request service.): The exhibit shows a round, well-defined cold spot in an otherwise even field, the pattern of a failed or drifted photomultiplier tube. It appears with the collimator off, so collimator damage cannot be the cause. Low source activity lowers counts across the whole field rather than in one spot, and a crystal fracture gives a line. Software cannot restore the lost signal, so the head should be withdrawn and serviced.", "image": "question_images/q_424.png"}, {"id": 425, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man has NYHA class II heart failure and EF 30 percent despite guideline directed therapy, and his team asks about prognosis. Planar I-123 MIBG imaging is performed, and the exhibit lists the results at 15 minutes and 4 hours. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Normal MIBG uptake, because the early heart to mediastinum ratio of 1.52 exceeds the 1.40 cutoff.", "B": "Late ratio under 1.60 with fast washout shows impaired sympathetic function and higher risk.", "C": "The low late ratio reflects infarct scar, so perfusion imaging must be repeated before any conclusion.", "D": "Elevated washout means improved neuronal function and a lower risk of heart failure events overall."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Late ratio under 1.60 with fast washout shows impaired sympathetic function and higher risk.): The late ratio of 1.38 is below the commonly used 1.60 threshold and the washout of 41 percent is well above the normal range of roughly 10 to 20 percent. Both indicate reduced neuronal retention and sympathetic overactivity, linked with a higher risk of progression and arrhythmic death. The early ratio is not the prognostic marker, faster washout is adverse, and the ratio is a global measurement.", "image": "question_images/q_425.png"}, {"id": 426, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman receiving trastuzumab after anthracycline has a gated blood pool study with EF 46 percent, down from 60 percent. The tracing shows frequent premature ventricular beats and the beat rejection window was set wide open. Which cause best explains the low value before therapy is changed?", "options": {"A": "Transient trastuzumab stunning that will recover, so the value can be disregarded for monitoring.", "B": "Established anthracycline cardiotoxicity, so trastuzumab should be stopped now based on this value alone.", "C": "Ectopic beats admitted by the wide window blur end systole; repeat with tight rejection.", "D": "Poor left anterior oblique angle merging the ventricles, which lowers EF by 14 points."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ectopic beats admitted by the wide window blur end systole; repeat with tight rejection.): Gated blood pool EF depends on summing beats of similar length. A wide rejection window lets premature, short beats into the acquisition, which blurs the end systolic frame and lowers the calculated EF. A drop with frequent ectopy should be rechecked with tight rejection before any cardio-oncology decision. Real cardiotoxicity is plausible but unproven, the stunning idea has no basis here, and an angle error does not lower EF by a fixed amount."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with a BMI of 41 kg/m2 and exertional dyspnea has an intermediate pretest probability of coronary disease and cannot walk on a treadmill because of knee arthritis. A prior Tc-99m SPECT study elsewhere was nondiagnostic from heavy anterior and inferior attenuation. Which test should be ordered now?", "options": {"A": "Repeat sestamibi SPECT with a doubled injected dose and a longer acquisition per projection angle", "B": "Dobutamine SPECT with prone imaging alone, since prone positioning removes soft tissue attenuation", "C": "Tl-201 stress redistribution SPECT, since its lower photon energy penetrates thick tissue more easily", "D": "Regadenoson Rb-82 PET with CT attenuation correction, which handles obesity better than SPECT photons"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson Rb-82 PET with CT attenuation correction, which handles obesity better than SPECT photons): Rb-82 has a half-life of 76 seconds, so rest and stress images are acquired in minutes with a total study time near 30 minutes, and routine CT attenuation correction preserves quality in obesity. PET photons are 511 keV, higher in energy, not lower. The very short half-life is the speed advantage, and tracer cost is not lower than SPECT."}, {"id": 428, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The chart shows measured Tc-99m sestamibi activities for 8 patients prescribed 10 mCi each. Patient 4 received 14 mCi, about 40 percent over the prescription, with an estimated effective dose of 0.46 rem. How should the lab classify this deviation under 10 CFR 35.3045?", "options": {"A": "Not a medical event, since it is under 5 rem effective dose; document and review it", "B": "A medical event only if tissue effects appear within 24 hours, since reporting follows injury", "C": "A medical event, since any activity above 12 mCi is reported by the next calendar day", "D": "A medical event, since a deviation of 20 percent or more is sufficient alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Not a medical event, since it is under 5 rem effective dose; document and review it): A medical event from an unsealed source needs both a deviation of 20 percent or more and a dose that differs from the prescribed dose by more than 5 rem effective dose or 50 rem to an organ or tissue. A 40 percent deviation with 0.46 rem meets the first only. It should still be recorded and reviewed internally, and the referring physician informed. The rule does not depend on injury.", "image": "question_images/q_428.png"}, {"id": 429, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old woman on a Bruce treadmill protocol has a resting systolic pressure of 132 mmHg, which rises to 156 mmHg at stage 1. At stage 2 her pressure falls to 118 mmHg with increasing workload, and she reports new chest tightness with 2 mm of ST depression. Which finding most clearly requires termination and tracer injection now?", "options": {"A": "Horizontal ST depression of 1 mm during stage 2 in a patient with no chest discomfort.", "B": "Heart rate reaching 85 percent of age-predicted maximum with no symptoms and a stable blood pressure.", "C": "A systolic pressure of 220 mmHg with diastolic 100 mmHg during stage 3 and mild fatigue.", "D": "A systolic fall over 10 mmHg despite rising workload, with new angina and ST depression."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A systolic fall over 10 mmHg despite rising workload, with new angina and ST depression.): A drop in systolic pressure of more than 10 mmHg from baseline despite increasing workload, with other evidence of ischemia, is an absolute indication to stop and suggests extensive ischemia with falling output. Reaching target rate is not a stop requirement, blood pressure limits for stopping are about 250 and 115, and 1 mm ST change without symptoms is not an endpoint."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "An extrinsic Tc-99m flood on a SPECT camera with its low-energy collimators in place shows the exhibit. The same detector passed an intrinsic flood two weeks ago. The collimator was dropped during a cleaning last month and was never inspected. Which step best separates the likely causes before anyone orders parts?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the extrinsic flood with 100 million counts to see whether the defect fills in.", "B": "Replace the collimator immediately, since septal damage causes round defects on extrinsic floods.", "C": "Repeat peaking for the 140 keV photopeak, because a failed tune produces round cold areas.", "D": "Remove the collimator and repeat an intrinsic flood to see if the defect persists."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove the collimator and repeat an intrinsic flood to see if the defect persists.): Both a damaged collimator and a failed tube can produce a round cold spot on an extrinsic flood. An intrinsic flood without the collimator isolates the detector: if the spot persists the tube is at fault, and if it disappears the collimator is. Replacing parts first is a guess, extra counts cannot fill a physical defect, and peaking corrects global energy settings.", "image": "question_images/q_430.png"}, {"id": 431, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man on metoprolol, taken this morning, begins treadmill exercise for stress perfusion imaging. After 3 minutes of the Bruce protocol he is breathless, with a heart rate that plateaus at 96 beats per minute, blood pressure 140/80 and no ST change. His predicted maximum is 154. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Stop exercise and give regadenoson with low level activity, since beta blockade blunts heart rate", "B": "Inject now and image with a dobutamine protocol to compensate for the low heart rate", "C": "Inject now, since exertional breathlessness confirms that he reached a diagnostic level of effort", "D": "Raise treadmill grade and speed for 5 more minutes to push the rate above 131"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop exercise and give regadenoson with low level activity, since beta blockade blunts heart rate): Beta blockers prevent the expected rise in heart rate, so the 85 percent target of about 131 beats per minute is unlikely to be reached, and a tracer injected at 96 would give a nondiagnostic study. Regadenoson with low level exercise provides vasodilation independent of rate. Dyspnea alone does not define an adequate test."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man is reviewing his stress and rest SPECT report with a trainee who asks about segment assignment. The image shows a stress-only defect confined to the inferior wall and inferoseptum. Which statement about coronary artery assignment for the mid inferoseptal segment is correct?", "options": {"A": "It goes to the first diagonal branch because it lies next to the anterior wall segments.", "B": "It is assigned to the obtuse marginal branch of the circumflex whatever the coronary dominance.", "C": "It goes to the right coronary in right dominance, or the circumflex in left dominance.", "D": "It is assigned to the septal perforators of the left anterior descending artery in every patient."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It goes to the right coronary in right dominance, or the circumflex in left dominance.): The 17-segment model assigns the inferior and inferoseptal segments to the artery that gives the posterior descending branch, which is the right coronary in about 85 percent of patients and the circumflex in left dominance. The anterior septum comes from LAD septal branches, and the obtuse marginal serves the lateral wall. Diagonals serve the anterolateral wall rather than the inferior septum.", "image": "question_images/q_432.png"}, {"id": 433, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist handling F-18 FDG wears a standard 0.5 mm lead apron and believes it makes syringe handling safe. The department also stocks a tungsten syringe shield for positron emitters. What is the best interpretation of the apron at the photon energy of F-18?", "options": {"A": "The apron protects only against beta particles, so it adds nothing against gamma rays", "B": "The apron works as well at 511 keV as at 140 keV, so handling is safe", "C": "The apron stops little of the 511 keV radiation, so tungsten shielding and distance matter more", "D": "The apron blocks most 511 keV photons, so a tungsten syringe shield is an optional extra"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The apron stops little of the 511 keV radiation, so tungsten shielding and distance matter more): A thin lead apron is designed for low-energy photons like the 140 keV of Tc-99m, but the 511 keV annihilation photons penetrate it with only modest attenuation. Syringe shields of thick tungsten, quick handling and distance are the effective controls. Aprons also do not exist to stop beta particles, and shielding effectiveness falls sharply with higher photon energy."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The morning extrinsic flood image on a SPECT camera shows a round cold area over one photomultiplier tube, and integral uniformity exceeds the vendor tolerance. Six cardiac perfusion patients are scheduled for the day on this camera. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Scan the patients with doubled acquisition time so that the cold area is averaged out", "B": "Remove the camera from use, have service retune it, then repeat the flood and document", "C": "Proceed, because the uniformity correction map will remove the defect from the images", "D": "Run a center-of-rotation test instead and proceed if it passes, since it covers uniformity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the camera from use, have service retune it, then repeat the flood and document): Daily uniformity is a core camera test, and a result beyond tolerance means the camera can produce artifacts such as ring patterns in SPECT. The camera should be taken out of service, serviced, retested and documented before patient scanning. A correction map handles only small nonuniformity, the center-of-rotation test checks a different parameter, and longer acquisitions do not remove a fixed defect."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 72-year-old woman receives dipyridamole at 0.142 mg/kg/min over four minutes. Two minutes after the tracer injection she develops wheezing, chest pain and 2 mm of ST depression, and her saturation falls to 91 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin and wait fifteen minutes before reversal, since the effect fades quickly.", "B": "Give an albuterol nebulizer and observe, since bronchodilators alone reverse dipyridamole effects.", "C": "Give aminophylline slowly intravenously to reverse the dipyridamole effect, with monitoring and supportive care.", "D": "Give a second tracer injection to ensure the ischemic state is captured on stress images."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give aminophylline slowly intravenously to reverse the dipyridamole effect, with monitoring and supportive care.): Dipyridamole raises adenosine levels, so wheezing with ischemia is treated by aminophylline, an adenosine antagonist, given slowly intravenously. The tracer was already injected, so repeat dosing adds only radiation. Albuterol does not antagonize adenosine, and nitroglycerin lowers blood pressure while the effect persists."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with prior stroke takes extended-release dipyridamole 200 mg with aspirin twice daily and is scheduled for an adenosine infusion stress SPECT because he cannot exercise due to arthritis. What is the best management and the reason for it?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with standard adenosine, since dipyridamole acts on platelets and has no coronary vasodilator effect.", "B": "Double adenosine to 280 mcg/kg/min, since the combination is needed to overcome dipyridamole inhibition.", "C": "Hold dipyridamole at least 24 hours, since blocked adenosine reuptake would potentiate the infusion.", "D": "Hold dipyridamole 24 hours, since receptor blockade would blunt hyperemia and give a falsely negative scan."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold dipyridamole at least 24 hours, since blocked adenosine reuptake would potentiate the infusion.): Dipyridamole blocks cellular reuptake and breakdown of adenosine, which raises endogenous and infused adenosine levels and exaggerates its effects. Holding it for at least 24 hours restores a predictable response, and some laboratories reduce the adenosine dose if it cannot be held. Receptor blockade is the effect of caffeine and theophylline, not dipyridamole, and raising the dose would worsen the risk."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A cardiac Rb-82 PET/CT shows a new lateral wall defect on attenuation corrected images, but the nonattenuation corrected images look uniform. The table lists the registration check performed by the technologist. What is the most likely consequence of the finding in the table on the corrected images?", "options": {"A": "Uniform loss of counts across all walls, with relative myocardial distribution fully preserved.", "B": "Falsely increased lateral wall uptake, because the wall is overcorrected with dense liver attenuation values.", "C": "A falsely low ejection fraction, because gated frames lose temporal alignment with the CT scan.", "D": "Falsely reduced lateral wall uptake that mimics a perfusion defect, from low lung attenuation values."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Falsely reduced lateral wall uptake that mimics a perfusion defect, from low lung attenuation values.): A 12 mm lateral offset places the lateral wall on the CT lung field, which has low density, so the map undercorrects that wall and the corrected image shows an artifactual defect. Matching uniform uncorrected images support this. Overcorrection by liver occurs when the heart slips toward the diaphragm, and gating is not involved.", "image": "question_images/q_437.png"}, {"id": 438, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A Tc-99m vial shield made of lead has a half-value layer of 0.3 mm and is 3.0 mm thick. The unshielded dose rate at the vial surface is 400 mSv/h. What dose rate is expected behind the shield, ignoring scatter?", "options": {"A": "About 0.4 mSv/h, since ten half-value layers cut the rate by a factor of 1,024", "B": "About 40 mSv/h, since ten half-value layers divide the rate by ten", "C": "About 12.5 mSv/h, since five half-value layers divide the rate by thirty two", "D": "About 133 mSv/h, since the rate is divided by the 3 mm thickness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 0.4 mSv/h, since ten half-value layers cut the rate by a factor of 1,024): Thickness divided by HVL is 3.0 over 0.3, or 10 half-value layers. Transmission is one half to the tenth power, which is 1/1,024, so 400 divided by 1,024 is about 0.39 mSv/h. The wrong values come from dividing by 10 or 3, or counting five layers."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A nondiabetic patient with ischemic cardiomyopathy has fasted for six hours and is referred for FDG PET viability imaging before a revascularization decision. The goal is to maximize myocardial glucose uptake on the study. Which patient preparation is the best choice for this nondiabetic patient?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous dextrose infusion with no insulin, since glucose delivery alone drives uptake into myocytes.", "B": "An oral glucose load of 50 to 75 grams, with insulin titrated if needed before injection.", "C": "A fast of twenty-four hours, so that myocardial glucose uptake is driven by free fatty acids.", "D": "A high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet with heparin, which maximizes glucose uptake in viable myocardium."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An oral glucose load of 50 to 75 grams, with insulin titrated if needed before injection.): Viability FDG studies need the myocardium to use glucose, so a carbohydrate load stimulates endogenous insulin and shifts metabolism toward glucose, with additional insulin if glucose stays high. Prolonged fasting or a high-fat diet favors fatty acid use and suppresses FDG uptake, which is the sarcoid approach."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist draws up about 40 F-18 FDG doses each week. Her body badge shows 0.2 mSv a month, but her ring dosimeter, worn chip-side palmward under the glove, shows 25 mSv a month. Her annual extremity limit is 500 mSv. Which action best fits this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Move the ring to the back of the hand to lower the reading by increasing distance.", "B": "Stop FDG work, since 25 mSv a month already exceeds the annual 500 mSv extremity limit.", "C": "Add tungsten syringe shields and forceps or automated dispensing, since fingers receive most dose.", "D": "Take no action, since 300 mSv yearly is under the limit and the badge is low."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add tungsten syringe shields and forceps or automated dispensing, since fingers receive most dose.): The hands are nearest the source during syringe handling, so ring readings run far above the chest badge. At 25 mSv a month the annual extremity dose is about 300 mSv, below the 500 mSv limit, but ALARA calls for reducing a high dose with tungsten shields, forceps or automated dispensing. Moving the ring would hide, not reduce, the dose, and 300 mSv does not exceed the limit."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 61-year-old man who had Tl-201 stress-redistribution imaging with 3.5 mCi six days ago triggers a radiation portal monitor at an airport. He is healthy, and the security officer asks for an explanation. What is the best way to interpret this finding and respond?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201 clears within 24 hours, so the alarm means he was contaminated somewhere else", "B": "Tl-201 alarms last about 30 days, so he should avoid public transport for a month", "C": "Residual Tl-201 can still alarm sensitive monitors, so he should carry a dated letter", "D": "He has internal contamination needing decontamination and a report made to the licensee"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Residual Tl-201 can still alarm sensitive monitors, so he should carry a dated letter): Tl-201 has a 73-hour physical half-life, so six days is about two half-lives and the remaining activity can still alarm sensitive border monitors. The best practice is to give patients a dated note or wallet card stating the tracer and study date. It is not contamination, and a month of travel avoidance is unwarranted."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old diabetic woman has typical exertional angina and a normal resting ECG. Rb-82 PET shows uniform relative perfusion and normal wall motion on gated images, but global myocardial flow reserve is 1.4. Which interpretation and action are best for this report?", "options": {"A": "Report abnormal flow reserve as a high-risk marker despite uniform perfusion, and consider angiography.", "B": "Report isolated right coronary disease and recommend stenting of that vessel based on the flow value.", "C": "Report mild artifact from rest-stress misalignment and repeat the PET acquisition on another day.", "D": "Report a normal study because relative perfusion is uniform and wall motion is preserved."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report abnormal flow reserve as a high-risk marker despite uniform perfusion, and consider angiography.): Relative perfusion can look uniform when disease is balanced or diffuse, but a global flow reserve below about 2.0 signals extensive epicardial or microvascular disease and carries a worse prognosis. With typical angina, the abnormal value should be reported and weighed toward further evaluation. A global value cannot identify a single vessel, and nothing in the exhibit supports a misalignment artifact."}, {"id": 443, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A nuclear cardiology lab turns off scatter correction on its Tc-99m SPECT protocol to save processing time. Compared with scatter-corrected data, what is the expected effect on the reconstructed perfusion images, for example in a patient with normal anatomy and a low-count study?", "options": {"A": "Lower contrast from scattered photons in the photopeak window, understating defects", "B": "No change in the images because scatter photons are rejected by the collimator", "C": "Higher spatial resolution because the scattered photons add counts to the photopeak window", "D": "Greater defect contrast because the scatter counts add signal in regions of low uptake"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower contrast from scattered photons in the photopeak window, understating defects): Compton-scattered photons that lose a little energy can still fall inside the photopeak window and are assigned to the wrong location, which adds a haze that lowers contrast and fills in defects. Scatter correction, such as a lower-window subtraction, removes much of this. Scatter does not improve resolution, and the collimator cannot reject scattered photons that arrive along acceptable paths."}, {"id": 444, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman treated medically for chest pain four years ago now has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. The slices show an anterior, septal and apical defect that looks the same at stress and rest, with a summed stress score of 14 and a summed rest score of 14 across 17 segments. What fraction of the left ventricle is fixed defect?", "options": {"A": "About 41 percent, from a rest score of 14 divided by a maximum of 34.", "B": "About 14 percent, taking the rest score directly as a percentage of the ventricle.", "C": "About 21 percent, from a rest score of 14 divided by 68.", "D": "About 35 percent, from six abnormal segments divided by the 17 segments."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 21 percent, from a rest score of 14 divided by 68.): The fixed defect is measured by the summed rest score, because scar remains at rest. That is 14 divided by the maximum of 17 segments times 4, or 68, which is about 21 percent. The difference score is 0, so none of it is ischemic. A maximum of 34 halves the denominator, counting segments ignores how severe each one is, and the raw score is not a percentage.", "image": "question_images/q_444.png"}, {"id": 445, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader finalizes an outpatient SPECT showing a summed difference score of 14, a transient ischemic dilation ratio of 1.4 and a stress ejection fraction of 38 percent versus 58 at rest. The patient has already gone home. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Contact the patient directly to schedule angiography, bypassing the referring clinician who ordered the study.", "B": "Telephone the referring clinician the same day with the high risk findings and document it.", "C": "Add an addendum after the next clinic visit, once the referring clinician has reviewed the images.", "D": "Send the routine report in the next batch, since the patient is asymptomatic."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Telephone the referring clinician the same day with the high risk findings and document it.): A difference score of 14 is an ischemic burden of about 21 percent, with transient dilation and a large post-stress fall in ejection fraction. These are high risk findings, and critical results should be communicated directly to the referring clinician promptly, with the communication documented. Waiting for a batch or a clinic visit delays care, and patient care decisions remain with the treating team."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 63-year-old man has ischemic cardiomyopathy, ejection fraction 22 percent, and a Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT study showing an anterior and apical defect at 28 percent of peak activity. He is being considered for revascularization. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Rb-82 rest perfusion with F-18 FDG imaging, to look for perfusion metabolism mismatch", "B": "Treadmill exercise Tc-99m imaging to look for new reversible defects in the infarcted territory now", "C": "Stress Tl-201 imaging with a 4 hour redistribution scan to assess viability in the scarred wall", "D": "Repeat Tc-99m sestamibi rest imaging after sublingual nitroglycerin to improve flow to the territory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rb-82 rest perfusion with F-18 FDG imaging, to look for perfusion metabolism mismatch): Severely reduced Tc-99m activity cannot reliably separate scar from hibernating myocardium, and sestamibi may underestimate viability. PET perfusion with FDG identifies preserved glucose metabolism in hypoperfused segments, the mismatch pattern that predicts benefit from revascularization. Nitrate augmentation helps when uptake is only moderately reduced, redistribution Tl-201 may be incomplete, and exercise imaging asks an ischemia rather than a viability question."}, {"id": 447, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man with chest pain was sent from the emergency department for treadmill SPECT. The table lists his laboratory results drawn that morning and repeated 3 hours later. Based on the table, what is the most appropriate action for the stress laboratory?", "options": {"A": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin and proceed with imaging, because troponin elevation does not alter stress safety.", "B": "Proceed with a low-level modified Bruce protocol, stopping at 85 percent of maximum predicted heart rate.", "C": "Cancel the stress test and return him to the treating team for acute coronary syndrome management.", "D": "Switch to regadenoson, since vasodilator stress places less demand on the heart than exercise does."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cancel the stress test and return him to the treating team for acute coronary syndrome management.): A rising troponin from 0.84 to 1.9 ng/mL indicates evolving myocardial injury, so stress testing is contraindicated until an acute coronary syndrome is evaluated. A submaximal exercise protocol still raises oxygen demand, and vasodilator agents are also avoided in the first days after a recent infarction. Nitroglycerin does not remove the hazard.", "image": "question_images/q_447.png"}, {"id": 448, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A radiation safety officer explains the units used in dose reports to new staff and notes that sieverts appear in several different forms. Which quantity multiplies the dose to each organ by a tissue weighting factor and sums the results to estimate overall stochastic risk?", "options": {"A": "Equivalent dose, which scales absorbed dose by a radiation weighting factor for the type of radiation", "B": "Effective dose, which sums weighted organ equivalent doses and is expressed in sieverts", "C": "Exposure, which is the ionization produced in air and is expressed in roentgens", "D": "Absorbed dose, which is the energy deposited per unit mass and is expressed in grays"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Effective dose, which sums weighted organ equivalent doses and is expressed in sieverts): Effective dose takes each organ equivalent dose, multiplies by its tissue weighting factor, and sums over organs to give one value in sieverts for stochastic risk. Equivalent dose applies a radiation weighting factor, absorbed dose is energy per mass in grays, and exposure measures ionization in air in roentgens."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with exertional chest pain is scheduled for Tc-99m sestamibi stress imaging. At check-in she says her last menstrual period was 6 weeks ago and she is unsure whether she could be pregnant. Under radiation safety practice, what is the best action before injection?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with half the Tc-99m dose and shield her abdomen with a lead apron during imaging", "B": "Proceed at full dose, since fetal exposure from sestamibi is too small to matter", "C": "Obtain a pregnancy test before injection, and use a nonradiating test if it is positive", "D": "Proceed with Tl-201 instead, since its long physical half-life limits placental transfer of radiation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain a pregnancy test before injection, and use a nonradiating test if it is positive): A patient who may be pregnant must be screened and tested before any radiopharmaceutical is given. If pregnancy is confirmed, a stress echocardiogram or deferral is chosen unless the benefit clearly justifies fetal exposure. Reduced doses and aprons do not remove the need for testing, and thallium has a higher dose."}, {"id": 450, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old diabetic woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease is being listed for kidney transplant. She has no angina. A regadenoson SPECT is normal with an ejection fraction of 60 percent. What is the best way to report the result?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal study with the standard multi-year warranty used for nondiabetic patients without kidney disease.", "B": "Report normal perfusion, state the shorter warranty in diabetes with kidney disease, advise reassessment.", "C": "Repeat with dobutamine, because a normal vasodilator study cannot be trusted in renal disease.", "D": "Recommend immediate coronary angiography, because diabetes with kidney disease makes a normal study unreliable."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report normal perfusion, state the shorter warranty in diabetes with kidney disease, advise reassessment.): Diabetes, kidney disease, women and older age all shorten the period during which a normal perfusion study predicts low event risk. The result remains reassuring, and angiography is not indicated without symptoms or other abnormal findings. Vasodilator stress is valid in kidney disease. The report should still carry a shorter warranty and a plan for reassessment."}, {"id": 451, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy, an EF of 25 percent and multivessel disease is being considered for bypass surgery. An echocardiogram shows akinetic anterior and apical walls. He is a poor candidate for any study requiring a prolonged fast because of insulin-treated diabetes. Which viability study is best?", "options": {"A": "Rest Tl-201 with redistribution or reinjection, which needs no fasting and tests membrane integrity", "B": "Stress-only Tc-99m SPECT, since normal stress uptake alone identifies hibernating myocardium", "C": "Regadenoson Rb-82 PET stress images alone, since hyperemia unmasks viable dysfunctional tissue", "D": "FDG PET after a 12 hour fast without insulin, so glucose uptake is maximal in diabetics"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rest Tl-201 with redistribution or reinjection, which needs no fasting and tests membrane integrity): Thallium redistribution or reinjection demonstrates membrane integrity and viable tissue without fasting, so it suits a diabetic. FDG PET needs glucose clamping or insulin protocols in diabetes, and a prolonged fast cannot simply be used. Stress-only Tc-99m or regadenoson perfusion alone does not separate hibernating from scarred myocardium."}, {"id": 452, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62 year old woman has relatively uniform Rb-82 perfusion images, but PET flow quantification shows a stress flow of 1.1 mL/min/g and a myocardial flow reserve of 1.4. The ECG during stress was normal. How should this be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Technical failure, since coronary flow reserve below 2 is invalid unless patient motion was documented", "B": "Diffusely reduced flow reserve, suggesting balanced multivessel disease or microvascular dysfunction that relative images miss", "C": "Normal study, since uniform relative perfusion proves that flow is normal in every segment", "D": "Isolated proximal LAD stenosis, since flow reserve below 2 localizes the lesion to one vessel"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diffusely reduced flow reserve, suggesting balanced multivessel disease or microvascular dysfunction that relative images miss): Relative perfusion images compare each segment with the best segment, so uniform diffuse reduction in flow looks normal. PET quantification gives absolute flow, and a reserve of 1.4, below about 2.0, indicates diffusely impaired flow from balanced multivessel disease or microvascular dysfunction. A single stenosis would produce a regional rather than global change."}, {"id": 453, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A PET technologist sets the coincidence energy window for an F-18 FDG study. The positrons have a maximum kinetic energy of 0.634 MeV, slow in tissue, and annihilate nearly at rest. A trainee proposes centering the window above 1 MeV. What is the energy of each detected annihilation photon?", "options": {"A": "Each photon carries 634 keV, because the positron's maximum kinetic energy is shared by the photons.", "B": "Each photon carries 511 keV, the rest energy of one electron, so the window belongs there.", "C": "Each photon carries 1.145 MeV, because kinetic energy adds to the rest energy of the pair.", "D": "Each photon carries 1.022 MeV, because the pair's total rest energy goes to one detected photon."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Each photon carries 511 keV, the rest energy of one electron, so the window belongs there.): A positron that has lost its kinetic energy annihilates with an electron, converting the two rest masses (2 x 511 keV = 1.022 MeV) into two photons emitted nearly 180 degrees apart, each 511 keV. The 0.634 MeV kinetic energy is deposited in tissue before annihilation and does not appear in the photons. Coincidence windows are therefore centered near 511 keV."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man with left bundle branch block and a resting heart rate of 70 is referred for routine myocardial perfusion SPECT stress imaging. The referring physician orders a standard treadmill study. Which stress choice is best for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Treadmill exercise, since the highest heart rate gives the strongest hyperemia for septal imaging", "B": "Dobutamine stress, since inotropic stress avoids heart-rate-related septal defects with left bundle branch block", "C": "Treadmill exercise using a low-level protocol, since slower rates avoid septal defects in bundle branch block", "D": "Regadenoson vasodilator stress, since rate-related septal defects with left bundle branch block are less likely"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson vasodilator stress, since rate-related septal defects with left bundle branch block are less likely): With left bundle branch block, abnormal septal activation causes a heart-rate-dependent septal perfusion abnormality, which can appear as a false defect with exercise and with dobutamine as the rate rises. Vasodilator stress raises rate much less, so the septum stays normal and the study is more specific for coronary disease. A low-level treadmill study lowers the problem but vasodilator remains preferred."}, {"id": 455, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader is localizing an anterior wall defect on a standard SPECT display of reoriented slices. The reader opens the vertical long-axis slice and must name the walls shown. Which walls appear at the top and at the bottom of that image?", "options": {"A": "Inferior wall at the top and anterior wall at the bottom.", "B": "Septal wall at the top and lateral wall at the bottom.", "C": "Lateral wall at the top and septal wall at the bottom.", "D": "Anterior wall at the top and inferior wall at the bottom."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anterior wall at the top and inferior wall at the bottom.): The vertical long-axis slice cuts from anterior to inferior, so the anterior wall lies at the top and the inferior wall at the bottom, with the apex pointing to the right. The horizontal long-axis slice displays the septum and lateral wall. Mixing these views is the commonest localization mistake."}, {"id": 456, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A laboratory found the center of rotation error shown in the exhibit and plans to recalibrate. Clinical cardiac studies use a 180 degree orbit, a 64 by 64 matrix, and low-energy high-resolution collimators, but the last calibration was done with a 360 degree orbit on a 128 matrix. How should the new calibration be done?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the point source acquisition with the clinical orbit, matrix, and collimators, then store that calibration.", "B": "Rebuild the uniformity correction map, since a stale map is the usual source of the arc.", "C": "Shorten the orbit radius until the arc disappears and then keep that radius for patients.", "D": "Apply a fixed two pixel shift to every projection before reconstruction to cancel the error."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the point source acquisition with the clinical orbit, matrix, and collimators, then store that calibration.): The offset can depend on orbit type, matrix, and collimator, so calibrating under the same conditions as clinical use is what makes the correction valid for patient studies. A fixed shift ignores the measured offset, uniformity maps do not alter the geometry, and changing orbit radius does not calibrate the electronic center.", "image": "question_images/q_456.png"}, {"id": 457, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "For a treadmill SPECT study the technologist places the limb electrodes on the wrists and ankles instead of on the torso, as torso placement is the laboratory standard. The patient is walking briskly at stage 3 of the protocol. What is the most likely consequence for the stress ECG?", "options": {"A": "The frontal axis shifts rightward and inferior Q waves disappear compared with the supine tracing", "B": "The heart rate display becomes unreliable, though ST segment measurements remain fully interpretable", "C": "Motion artifact and baseline wander degrade ST analysis, risking a nondiagnostic or falsely abnormal tracing", "D": "Precordial leads record falsely low voltage, so exercise ST depression is systematically underestimated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Motion artifact and baseline wander degrade ST analysis, risking a nondiagnostic or falsely abnormal tracing): Exercise ECG recording uses torso placement of the limb leads, such as the shoulders and lower abdomen, to avoid limb muscle and motion artifact. Distal limb electrodes pick up this noise and make ST segments hard to measure, so the tracing may be uninterpretable or falsely positive. Axis shift is a feature of torso lead placement itself, and precordial voltage is not affected by limb electrode position."}, {"id": 458, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The daily Cs-137 constancy check on the dose calibrator reads 12 percent above its decay-corrected expected value. Quarterly linearity passed last month and the annual accuracy test passed in January. The technologist wants to scale future readings by a correction factor and continue. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Continue clinical use, because the earlier linearity and accuracy tests have already passed this year", "B": "Switch to a survey meter for dose assay until the next quarterly linearity test is done", "C": "Remove it from patient use until the deviation is investigated, repeated and resolved by service", "D": "Apply a fixed correction factor to all patient doses, since the error looks consistent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove it from patient use until the deviation is investigated, repeated and resolved by service): Constancy is a daily test that detects changes in instrument response, and a 12 percent deviation exceeds usual action limits. Earlier passing tests do not cover a response that changed today. The instrument should not assay patient doses until the cause is found and verified by repeat testing and, if needed, service and accuracy checks. A survey meter cannot assay millicurie doses accurately."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old man with intermittent chest tightness reaches 11 METs and 94 percent of maximal predicted heart rate on the treadmill, with no angina and no ST change. Sestamibi SPECT slices at stress and rest are in the image, and EF is 58 percent. Which conclusion should the report give?", "options": {"A": "Normal study, since a negative stress ECG at a high workload excludes lateral wall ischemia.", "B": "Fixed lateral defect from old infarction that is stable and needs no ischemia statement.", "C": "Indeterminate study because lateral motion could produce the defect, so a repeat scan is needed.", "D": "Abnormal study with reversible lateral ischemia in the circumflex territory despite the negative ECG."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Abnormal study with reversible lateral ischemia in the circumflex territory despite the negative ECG.): ECG response and perfusion imaging are independent measures, and the lateral wall can be ischemic with a silent ECG, so the images decide the report. Lateral counts are lower at stress than at rest across contiguous slices in one coronary territory, which is reversible ischemia (summed stress score 10, rest score 0). Scar would not change, and motion artifact does not follow a single territory or differ between stress and rest in that pattern.", "image": "question_images/q_459.png"}, {"id": 460, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a BMI of 31 reports exertional jaw discomfort. Supine Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT is done with no attenuation correction, using regadenoson stress and a rest injection on the same day. Compare the stress and rest slices in the image. Which interpretation best fits?", "options": {"A": "Reversible inferior and inferoseptal ischemia in the right coronary territory, since counts recover at rest.", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, because inferior defects in obese women on supine imaging need no further workup.", "C": "Fixed inferior scar from an unrecognized infarct, so stress induced ischemia is excluded with confidence here.", "D": "Normal basal inferior thinning near the membranous septum, a variant needing no follow-up testing or review."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reversible inferior and inferoseptal ischemia in the right coronary territory, since counts recover at rest.): Attenuation by the diaphragm is a strong tell in an obese patient, but it produces a fixed defect that looks the same at stress and rest. Here the inferior and inferoseptal counts are lower at stress and recover at rest, which is the signature of reversible ischemia in the right coronary territory, with a summed stress score of 10 and a rest score of 0. Scar would persist at rest, and normal thinning is confined to a small basal region rather than the whole inferior wall.", "image": "question_images/q_460.png"}, {"id": 461, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man with a prior stent has stress and rest Tc-99m SPECT with an ejection fraction of 58 percent. Segment scoring gives a summed stress score of 17 and a summed rest score of 10. On the polar maps, which coronary artery supplies the abnormal territory, and how should the report describe the defect?", "options": {"A": "The right coronary artery, with a partly reversible defect across the septum and apex.", "B": "A diagonal branch alone, with a partly reversible defect limited to the anterior segments.", "C": "Left anterior descending artery, with a partly reversible anterior, septal and apical defect.", "D": "The left circumflex artery, with a partly reversible defect across the anterior and lateral walls."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left anterior descending artery, with a partly reversible anterior, septal and apical defect.): Reduced counts in the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex fall in the left anterior descending territory. The defect is more severe at stress than at rest, with scores of 17 and 10 and a difference score of 7, so it is partly reversible and mixes scar with ischemia. The right coronary artery mostly supplies the inferior wall. The circumflex supplies the lateral wall. A diagonal branch would spare the septum and the apex.", "image": "question_images/q_461.png"}, {"id": 462, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A hospital radiation safety committee considers dropping the ALARA program because one member says there is no proven harm from occupational doses below 20 mSv in nuclear medicine. Which statement best describes the role of the linear no-threshold model in regulation?", "options": {"A": "It has been disproved, so regulators set limits only at thresholds for tissue reactions", "B": "It is the prudent assumption behind ALARA and dose limits, not proof of harm", "C": "It describes only deterministic effects, so ALARA is unnecessary for stochastic ones", "D": "It proves every 1 mSv causes measurable cancers, so the only acceptable limit is zero"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is the prudent assumption behind ALARA and dose limits, not proof of harm): The linear no-threshold model assumes cancer risk rises in proportion to dose with no safe level. Regulators adopt it as a conservative basis for ALARA and dose limits, while accepting that low dose risk cannot be measured directly. It is not proof of harm and it is not a model for tissue reactions."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 12 mCi (444 MBq) Tc-99m spill occurs on a corridor floor at 06:00. A technologist suggests posting the area and waiting 12 hours, when activity will be about 3.0 mCi, before cleaning it. The half-life is 6.02 hours. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Wait 24 hours, which reduces the activity below 1 mCi and avoids any cleaning.", "B": "Wait 6 hours until about 6 mCi remains, since one half-life is enough before cleaning.", "C": "Cordon the area and decontaminate now, since decay does not remove the spreading risk.", "D": "Wait 12 hours, since decay to about 3 mCi downgrades the spill to a minor one."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cordon the area and decontaminate now, since decay does not remove the spreading risk.): Twelve hours is 1.99 half-lives, so 12 x 0.25 = 3.0 mCi remains, but in the meantime staff and patients walk through a corridor and track the contamination. Decay is not a substitute for containing, posting and cleaning, which should begin promptly with the radiation safety officer informed. At 24 hours about 0.76 mCi would remain, and one half-life gives 6 mCi, but waiting does not address spreading."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man who smokes has stress sestamibi SPECT. The raw projections show a focal region of intense uptake in the right upper lung, separate from the heart, while myocardial perfusion and gated function are normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report the extracardiac finding and recommend chest CT to evaluate a possible pulmonary mass.", "B": "Repeat the sestamibi study at rest to determine whether the lung uptake redistributes.", "C": "Disregard the lung uptake as nonspecific tracer behavior and report only the normal perfusion.", "D": "Recommend repeat stress imaging with Tl-201, since the extracardiac finding invalidates the sestamibi study."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the extracardiac finding and recommend chest CT to evaluate a possible pulmonary mass.): Focal intense extracardiac uptake in a smoker may reflect a tumor or other pathology, and it is the reader's duty to report it and suggest definitive imaging. Perfusion interpretation remains valid. Disregarding it risks a missed cancer, redistribution is not a feature of this tracer, and changing tracers will not characterize a lung lesion."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A SPECT camera passes its intrinsic uniformity flood with the collimator removed. After the low-energy high-resolution collimator is mounted, an extrinsic flood shows a straight linear cold streak across the field of view. The first patient is waiting. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat energy peaking and photomultiplier tuning, since uniformity problems originate in the detector crystal.", "B": "Image the patient, since the intrinsic flood is the only uniformity test required before clinical use.", "C": "Apply the stored uniformity correction map to patient data, since software can compensate for the streak.", "D": "Take the collimator out of service and inspect for septal damage, since intrinsic flood passed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take the collimator out of service and inspect for septal damage, since intrinsic flood passed.): A normal intrinsic flood places the detector in good order, so a new streak that appears only with the collimator mounted points to collimator damage such as bent septa. Correction maps and retuning cannot fix a collimator and would hide the defect in patient data. The collimator should be replaced or repaired."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 35-year-old patient who had two nuclear studies last year asks whether a third is acceptable for new exertional symptoms. Her physician explains the dose-response model that regulators apply to diagnostic radiation and what it implies. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Threshold model: below about 100 mSv no risk is expected, so added studies need no justification.", "B": "Linear-quadratic model with a 1 to 2 Gy threshold, so risk begins only above deterministic limits.", "C": "Linear no-threshold: risk is assumed proportional to dose, so justification and ALARA apply to each study.", "D": "Hormetic model: low doses are protective, so added studies reduce long-term cancer risk."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Linear no-threshold: risk is assumed proportional to dose, so justification and ALARA apply to each study.): Radiation protection regulation, including the NRC framework, assumes a linear no-threshold relationship for stochastic effects, so each exposure adds a small proportional risk. That supports justifying each study by benefit and keeping dose as low as reasonably achievable, without barring appropriate imaging. Thresholds of 1 to 2 Gy apply to deterministic injury, and a protective low-dose effect is not accepted for regulatory use."}, {"id": 467, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with diabetes and stage 3 kidney disease is scheduled for elective open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair in three weeks. She has no angina and a normal resting ECG, but cannot climb one flight of stairs because of arthritis. What is the best next step before surgery?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a resting echocardiogram alone, since resting ejection fraction predicts perioperative ischemia.", "B": "Proceed to surgery without testing, because she has no angina and a normal resting ECG.", "C": "Pharmacologic stress perfusion imaging, since risk is high, capacity poor and results could alter management.", "D": "Send her directly to invasive coronary angiography to define the anatomy before any noninvasive testing."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pharmacologic stress perfusion imaging, since risk is high, capacity poor and results could alter management.): Elevated-risk vascular surgery, poor functional capacity (under 4 METs) and several clinical risk factors make stress imaging reasonable when the result would alter care. Skipping testing fits low-risk surgery or good capacity. Angiography without an ischemia signal or acute syndrome is premature. Resting ejection fraction does not detect inducible ischemia."}, {"id": 468, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A lab keeps its monthly badge reports and annual occupational dose summaries for 3 years and then shreds them, as it does with survey records. A technologist from a former employer later requests her lifetime dose history. What is the consequence of the lab's practice?", "options": {"A": "A violation, since occupational dose records are kept until the license is terminated", "B": "No violation, because the badge vendor keeps the reports for the licensee", "C": "A violation, because occupational dose records must be kept for 10 years", "D": "No violation, because 3 years is the standard period for survey and dose records"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A violation, since occupational dose records are kept until the license is terminated): Under 10 CFR 20.2106, records of occupational dose must be kept until the Commission terminates the license, a much longer period than the 3 years for survey records and dosage determinations. The licensee remains responsible even when a vendor keeps copies. A fixed 10 year period is not what the rule states."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit summarizes an 8-frame gated sestamibi SPECT in a patient with frequent PVCs. The reader sees normal wall motion visually on the cine display, yet the software gives an ejection fraction of 38 percent and a normal volume. Which is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Increase to 16 frames per cycle to improve temporal resolution of the ejection fraction.", "B": "Tighten ectopic beat rejection or reprocess from list mode, noting ejection fraction may be low.", "C": "Widen the R-R acceptance window to 40 percent so that more beats contribute.", "D": "Report 38 percent as accurate, because most of the beats were accepted for the study."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tighten ectopic beat rejection or reprocess from list mode, noting ejection fraction may be low.): Premature beats shorten the cycle, so the accepted beats blur end-systole and the software underestimates the ejection fraction. Rejection should be tightened or the data reprocessed from list mode, and visual wall motion given weight. More frames add noise, and a wide window admits abnormal beats.", "image": "question_images/q_469.png"}, {"id": 470, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 17-segment stress scan scores 19 in total and the rest scan scores 11 in total, giving an SDS of 8. A reader leaves two rest segments scoring 3 points together out of the rest sum but includes them in the stress sum. What does the reported result show?", "options": {"A": "SSS reads 16 and SDS reads 8, because SDS is derived from stress segments alone.", "B": "SSS and SRS both fall by 3 points, so SDS stays at 8.", "C": "SRS reads 8 and SDS reads 11, overstating ischemia by 3 points.", "D": "SRS reads 14 and SDS reads 5, understating ischemia because rest scores are added."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (SRS reads 8 and SDS reads 11, overstating ischemia by 3 points.): SSS is the stress sum (19), SRS is the rest sum, and SDS equals SSS minus SRS. Correct SRS is 11 and SDS is 8. Dropping 3 points from the rest sum gives SRS 8 and SDS 19 minus 8, which is 11, so ischemia is overstated by 3 while SSS is untouched. Adding the lost points would move SDS the other way, and SDS depends on both sums, so the error cannot cancel."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Because of a camera fault, stress Tl-201 imaging in a 57-year-old man began 40 minutes after peak exercise injection. A 4 hour redistribution scan shows no defect anywhere. He has typical exertional angina. What is the best interpretation of these normal images?", "options": {"A": "The study excludes obstructive disease, because stress and redistribution images both show uniform uptake.", "B": "Reverse redistribution is present, because tracer uptake improved in normal myocardium over time.", "C": "Normal images cannot exclude ischemia, because redistribution began before the stress images were acquired.", "D": "Hibernating myocardium is proven, because uptake improved between the two image sets."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Normal images cannot exclude ischemia, because redistribution began before the stress images were acquired.): Tl-201 redistributes from ischemic regions during the hours after injection, and stress imaging should begin within 5 to 10 minutes. A 40 minute delay lets early redistribution fill the defect, so stress images can look normal despite ischemia. The normal study in a patient with typical angina therefore does not exclude disease and the test is limited."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59 year old woman takes long-acting theophylline for severe asthma and cannot stop it. She cannot exercise, has a resting blood pressure of 138/82, no arrhythmia and no beta blocker use. Stress perfusion imaging is needed for chest pain. Which stress strategy fits best?", "options": {"A": "Adenosine at 210 mcg/kg/min, to override the antagonism with a higher infusion rate", "B": "Regadenoson at a doubled dose, to overcome the receptor blockade caused by the theophylline she takes", "C": "Dipyridamole infusion, because it raises endogenous adenosine and works despite theophylline", "D": "Dobutamine with atropine as needed, since it acts through beta receptors and ignores theophylline"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dobutamine with atropine as needed, since it acts through beta receptors and ignores theophylline): Theophylline antagonizes the adenosine receptors that regadenoson, adenosine and dipyridamole depend on, so vasodilator stress is unreliable unless the drug is held for at least 12 to 24 hours. When it cannot be held, dobutamine, which stimulates beta-1 receptors, avoids the interaction. Higher doses or indirect agents cannot reliably overcome the competitive blockade and add side effects."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a rest Tc-99m sestamibi injection in a 59-year-old woman, the flush meets resistance and the dorsal hand swells. A survey meter shows intense activity at the site, and the heart count rate is a quarter of expected. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Image anyway with longer time per projection to make up for the low counts.", "B": "Flush with 20 mL saline through the same cannula to move tracer into the vein.", "C": "Document it and repeat the rest injection through a new IV in the other arm.", "D": "Cover the site and image after a 2 hour delay to allow tissue washout."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document it and repeat the rest injection through a new IV in the other arm.): When a dose infiltrates, the heart receives only a fraction of the tracer and the study will not represent perfusion. The injection should be documented, a new IV placed in the other arm, and the injection repeated. Tracer in soft tissue clears slowly and flushing does not retrieve it. Longer imaging adds noise rather than information."}, {"id": 474, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old woman stops a treadmill test at 5 minutes because of knee pain. Heart rate is 98 bpm (64 percent of predicted), she has no angina, and the ST segments are flat. What is the correct action and its rationale?", "options": {"A": "Inject now and read as negative, since absent angina at 64 percent of maximum excludes ischemia.", "B": "Reschedule a dobutamine study later, since pharmacologic conversion after exercise is not permitted.", "C": "Give aminophylline to raise her heart rate, then inject tracer once she reaches the target.", "D": "Add regadenoson with low-level walking, since submaximal exercise leaves hyperemia inadequate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add regadenoson with low-level walking, since submaximal exercise leaves hyperemia inadequate.): A rate of 98 bpm is well under the 85 percent target, so the test has low sensitivity and the ECG cannot exclude ischemia. Converting to vasodilator stress with continued low-level exercise is accepted and gives flow heterogeneity. Aminophylline is a reversal drug and does not increase exercise capacity."}, {"id": 475, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Two minutes after regadenoson and tracer injection, a 66-year-old man has severe chest pain with 3 mm ST depression, blood pressure 88/50 and no wheeze. Oxygen saturation is 97 percent. The pain persists three minutes later despite rest. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Give atropine 0.5 mg IV, since adenosine agonists cause vagal slowing and a low blood pressure.", "B": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg now, since this picture is vasospastic angina in a hypotensive man.", "C": "Give atropine 0.5 mg IV, since adenosine agonists cause vagal slowing and low pressure.", "D": "Give aminophylline 50 to 250 mg IV slowly, antagonizing the adenosine receptor effect."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give aminophylline 50 to 250 mg IV slowly, antagonizing the adenosine receptor effect.): Regadenoson effects usually fade in minutes, but persistent ischemic symptoms with hypotension call for the antagonist aminophylline, 50 to 250 mg IV slowly. Nitroglycerin lowers blood pressure further and can worsen the hypotension. Atropine does not reverse the vasodilation that produced the pain, and the low pressure here is from vasodilation rather than a vagal effect."}, {"id": 476, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 134 kg man (BMI 44 kg/m2) had a one-day rest-stress Tc-99m sestamibi study with 10 and 30 mCi, and the images were noisy with low counts and poor inferior wall detail. He needs repeat imaging. Which protocol adjustment is best?", "options": {"A": "Keep the one-day 10 and 30 mCi doses and double acquisition time for each study.", "B": "Use a two-day protocol with weight-adjusted activities of about 25 to 30 mCi each day.", "C": "Reduce technetium to 15 mCi each day to lower scatter from abdominal fat.", "D": "Switch to Tl-201 at 3 mCi, since its lower photon energy improves penetration in large patients."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a two-day protocol with weight-adjusted activities of about 25 to 30 mCi each day.): Large body size attenuates and scatters photons, so counts fall unless administered activity is increased. A two-day protocol removes the residual activity problem of one-day studies and allows higher weight-based doses. Longer acquisition helps only partly and raises motion risk, lowering dose worsens count statistics, and thallium has low energy photons that attenuate more."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The tracing shows radiochemical purity of Tc-99m sestamibi from one kit tested at several times after preparation. The kit's acceptance limit is 90 percent radiochemical purity. A cardiologist asks for the last tested time at which doses from this kit remain acceptable. What does the tracing show?", "options": {"A": "Four hours after preparation, because purity has already started to decline by that point", "B": "Eight hours after preparation, because purity there is the nearest point to the 90 percent limit", "C": "Zero hours after preparation, because only freshly prepared doses can ever meet the 90 percent limit", "D": "Six hours after preparation, the last tested point at or above 90 percent"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Six hours after preparation, the last tested point at or above 90 percent): Radiochemical purity is acceptable when it is at least 90 percent. The tracing shows 91 percent at 6 hours and 87 percent at 8 hours, so 6 hours is the last tested time that still meets the limit. Doses at 8 hours fall below the limit, while earlier points are acceptable but not the last one.", "image": "question_images/q_477.png"}, {"id": 478, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with controlled hypertension is scheduled for an elective cholecystectomy, a low risk operation. He has no cardiac symptoms, climbs two flights of stairs without difficulty, and has a normal ECG. The surgeon asks whether stress perfusion imaging is needed. What is the best answer under appropriate use criteria?", "options": {"A": "No imaging is needed, since low surgical risk and good functional capacity make testing inappropriate", "B": "Yes, since age over 65 alone makes regadenoson perfusion imaging appropriate before elective surgery", "C": "Yes, since controlled hypertension is a sufficient reason for stress perfusion imaging before elective surgery", "D": "Yes, obtain a coronary calcium score first, then stress imaging for any score above zero"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No imaging is needed, since low surgical risk and good functional capacity make testing inappropriate): For low risk surgery in a patient who exceeds 4 METs without symptoms, preoperative stress testing does not change management, and appropriate use criteria rate it inappropriate. Age and controlled hypertension alone are not indications, and calcium scoring is not a validated preoperative screen."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an EF of 30 percent has a fixed anterior defect on sestamibi at 40 percent of peak, with akinesis. A reader calls it scar without viability testing. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Hibernating myocardium may be missed, so he could be denied beneficial revascularization.", "B": "Prognosis would be overestimated because scar is generally associated with low event risk.", "C": "Scar would be mistaken for ischemia, inflating the reversible defect burden in the report.", "D": "No consequence follows, since resting sestamibi below 50 percent of peak proves necrosis."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hibernating myocardium may be missed, so he could be denied beneficial revascularization.): A fixed defect with reduced counts can hold hibernating myocardium that recovers after revascularization. Calling it scar without testing, such as with FDG or nitrate augmented imaging, risks withholding benefit. Fixed defects do not add ischemic burden, and a count threshold on sestamibi does not prove necrosis."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with diabetes and no symptoms has sestamibi SPECT before starting a vigorous exercise program. Gated EF is 58 percent. The slices show a small mid lateral defect that looks the same at stress and rest, with summed stress and rest scores of 3 each. Which interpretation and recommendation fit best?", "options": {"A": "Reversible lateral ischemia that justifies coronary angiography before he starts the vigorous exercise program.", "B": "Stunned lateral myocardium that will recover within weeks, so repeat imaging after a short delay.", "C": "Small fixed lateral defect from an unrecognized infarct with no inducible ischemia; begin secondary prevention only.", "D": "Lateral wall attenuation that disappears with prone imaging, so no further action or follow-up is needed."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Small fixed lateral defect from an unrecognized infarct with no inducible ischemia; begin secondary prevention only.): A defect that is the same at stress and rest, with a summed stress score of 3, a summed rest score of 3 and a difference score of 0, is a small fixed defect, meaning scar from a prior unrecognized infarct. There is no ischemia to treat by revascularization, and EF is preserved, so risk is low and secondary prevention is the answer. Stunning would not appear identically on both rows, and attenuation of a lateral wall is not typical and would not be fixed in a way that needs no follow-up.", "image": "question_images/q_480.png"}, {"id": 481, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 74 year old woman has shortness of breath after one flight of stairs and walks slowly with a rolling walker. Her Duke Activity Status Index estimates under 4 METs, and her resting electrocardiogram is normal. Stress perfusion imaging is requested for dyspnea. Which stress mode fits?", "options": {"A": "Standard Bruce treadmill, because the test can be stopped when she tires and still be diagnostic", "B": "Exercise with injection at the level reached, since peak effort exceeds the requirement for sensitivity", "C": "Pharmacologic vasodilator stress, because she cannot reach adequate workload or target heart rate by walking", "D": "Dobutamine with atropine, because it is preferred over vasodilators in all patients unable to exercise"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pharmacologic vasodilator stress, because she cannot reach adequate workload or target heart rate by walking): Patients who cannot climb a flight of stairs are likely to manage under 4 to 5 METs and will not reach 85 percent of maximum heart rate. Vasodilator stress does not depend on effort and is the usual choice for them. A Bruce test would probably end submaximal, and dobutamine is generally reserved for those who cannot take a vasodilator."}, {"id": 482, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 29 year old woman at 10 weeks gestation has stable atypical chest pain and a low pretest probability of disease. Her cardiologist is choosing a stress test, and she asks to avoid any avoidable radiation. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rb-82 PET, since its 76 second half-life means no radiation reaches the fetus", "B": "Tl-201 SPECT, since thallium does not cross the placenta and spares the fetus", "C": "Half dose Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT, since any dose under 5 mSv is safe at this stage", "D": "Stress echocardiography, since it avoids fetal radiation and her risk does not need a nuclear study"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stress echocardiography, since it avoids fetal radiation and her risk does not need a nuclear study): A low risk pregnant patient should have a test without ionizing radiation, such as stress echocardiography, unless the information is essential and cannot be obtained otherwise. Reduced dose SPECT still exposes the fetus, Rb-82 and its decay products still give maternal dose with some placental exposure, and thallium crosses the placenta."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient receives a resting Tc-99m sestamibi injection and asks to be imaged after 15 minutes so that she can finish and leave the department early. What is the correct standard rest waiting time, and what helps clear the hepatobiliary activity?", "options": {"A": "Wait 15 minutes, since sestamibi leaves the liver rapidly in all resting patients", "B": "Wait 45 to 60 minutes; a fatty meal or water may help clear hepatobiliary activity", "C": "Wait 4 hours, since sestamibi needs redistribution before the images can be taken", "D": "Wait 5 minutes and give a diuretic to clear the activity from the hepatobiliary system"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wait 45 to 60 minutes; a fatty meal or water may help clear hepatobiliary activity): Sestamibi clears from the liver slowly at rest, so imaging after a rest injection should wait about 45 to 60 minutes, with a light fatty meal or water to promote gallbladder emptying. Early imaging leaves bright subdiaphragmatic activity that can alter the inferior wall. Redistribution times belong to thallium, and diuretics act on the kidneys."}, {"id": 484, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with a BMI of 34 has supine sestamibi SPECT with no attenuation correction. The image shows an inferior and inferoseptal defect on both stress and rest rows, with summed stress and rest scores of 12 and 11. She has no known infarction. Which additional finding would best help to decide between scar and diaphragmatic attenuation?", "options": {"A": "A preserved global EF of 58 percent, which excludes infarction in the inferior wall.", "B": "Lower inferior counts at stress than at rest on the reversibility map, favoring scar over attenuation.", "C": "Normalization of inferior counts on prone rest images, which confirms infarcted myocardium in that wall.", "D": "Absent inferior wall thickening on gated images, which points to scar rather than attenuation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Absent inferior wall thickening on gated images, which points to scar rather than attenuation.): Absent inferior wall thickening on gated images points to scar, because attenuation lowers counts but the muscle still thickens normally. The defect is the same on both rows, with a difference score of 1, so reversibility cannot separate the two causes. A preserved global EF does not exclude a small infarct. Normal inferior counts on prone images would favor attenuation, not confirm infarction.", "image": "question_images/q_484.png"}, {"id": 485, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 58 year old woman has finished rest and regadenoson stress Tc-99m sestamibi imaging. She asks what to do at home to reduce radiation to her bladder. She has no other medical issues and normal kidney function. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Take a potassium iodide tablet to block tracer uptake in the bladder wall", "B": "Avoid caffeine and theophylline for 24 hours so the tracer clears from the body", "C": "Drink extra fluids and void frequently for the rest of the day", "D": "Collect her urine in a shielded container for 48 hours before discarding it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Drink extra fluids and void frequently for the rest of the day): Sestamibi is cleared largely through the kidneys and hepatobiliary system, so hydration and frequent voiding shorten bladder residence time and reduce bladder wall dose. Potassium iodide protects the thyroid from radioiodine and does nothing for Tc-99m. Urine collection is not needed after a diagnostic study, and caffeine avoidance applies before vasodilator stress, not after it."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old diabetic woman has Rb-82 PET with regadenoson. Heart rate and blood pressure responses were appropriate. Relative perfusion is uniform in all 17 segments. Quantified flow is 0.95 mL per minute per gram at rest and 1.45 at stress. Which report is best?", "options": {"A": "Normal study, since relative perfusion is uniform in every segment and no regional defect exists.", "B": "Inadequate vasodilator stress; repeat the whole study with dipyridamole on another day before any interpretation.", "C": "Reduced global flow reserve of 1.5 despite uniform uptake, suggesting balanced multivessel or microvascular disease.", "D": "Isolated circumflex disease, since a single territory must account for the reduced global flow reserve."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduced global flow reserve of 1.5 despite uniform uptake, suggesting balanced multivessel or microvascular disease.): Flow reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow, here 1.45 divided by 0.95, or about 1.5, below the usual 2.0 threshold. A global reduction with uniform relative uptake points to balanced multivessel disease or microvascular dysfunction, which relative imaging alone would miss. Hemodynamics were appropriate, so the stress was adequate, and a single vessel would give a regional defect."}, {"id": 487, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old man with intermediate pretest probability chest pain has an Agatston calcium score of 1,600. His clinician orders coronary CT angiography as the first test. He has no known contrast allergy. Which statement best predicts the consequence of that choice for management?", "options": {"A": "Heavy calcification improves vessel wall visibility, so accuracy rises for severe stenoses in proximal segments.", "B": "Beam hardening from dense coronary calcium hides stenoses, giving false negatives that delay the diagnosis.", "C": "Contrast injection lowers the calcium score, making risk appear lower than it is on the report.", "D": "Calcium blooming may overcall stenoses and cause unnecessary angiography, so stress perfusion is better first."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calcium blooming may overcall stenoses and cause unnecessary angiography, so stress perfusion is better first.): A very high calcium score reduces CT angiography specificity because blooming artifact makes lesions look more severe, which can drive needless catheterization. Stress perfusion imaging is less affected by calcium. Calcium does not improve accuracy."}, {"id": 488, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man with poor functional capacity, under 4 METs, is scheduled for intermediate risk abdominal surgery. Vasodilator SPECT shows a mild reversible defect in one segment, 6 percent of the myocardium, with normal EF. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Cancel surgery and revascularize first, since any reversible defect carries prohibitive perioperative risk.", "B": "Delay surgery for one year of surveillance, because a mild defect predicts perioperative infarction.", "C": "Proceed with surgery and optimize medical therapy, since mild limited ischemia carries low perioperative risk.", "D": "Repeat the study with dobutamine to confirm the defect before deciding about surgery."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Proceed with surgery and optimize medical therapy, since mild limited ischemia carries low perioperative risk.): Perioperative risk rises mainly with ischemia affecting more than about 10 percent of the myocardium and with depressed EF. One segment of mild ischemia with normal function is low risk, and revascularization for it offers no benefit before surgery. Further testing or delay would not change management."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The table summarizes quality control items reviewed before a nuclear cardiology lab inspection, listing the time since each was last done. Several entries look current, but one is out of compliance with NRC requirements for the interval stated in the regulations. Which entry is out of compliance, and what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Survey meter calibration is overdue because it is required every 6 months, so send it out.", "B": "Dose calibrator accuracy is overdue because the NRC requires it every 6 months, so repeat today.", "C": "Dose calibrator linearity is overdue past the 3 month interval, so test it now.", "D": "Intrinsic flood is overdue because the NRC requires a weekly check, so repeat it."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dose calibrator linearity is overdue past the 3 month interval, so test it now.): NRC rules require dose calibrator linearity at intervals not exceeding 3 months, and the table shows 13 months, so it is out of compliance. Accuracy and survey meter calibration are annual and are within limits at 10 and 9 months. Camera uniformity checks are good practice, but the NRC does not set their frequency.", "image": "question_images/q_489.png"}, {"id": 490, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist stands beside a patient who was just infused with Rb-82 for a PET stress study. She wears a 0.5 mm lead-equivalent apron, assuming it protects as well as it does during Tc-99m injections. Which statement correctly describes her protection?", "options": {"A": "The apron stops little of the 511 keV photons, so time and distance protect her.", "B": "Lead attenuates higher-energy photons better, so the apron protects more against Rb-82 than Tc-99m.", "C": "The apron attenuates 511 keV photons as well as 140 keV photons, both being gamma radiation.", "D": "Rb-82 emits beta particles that the apron absorbs, making it the correct primary shield."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The apron stops little of the 511 keV photons, so time and distance protect her.): Positron annihilation produces two 511 keV photons, which pass through a thin apron with little loss, while 140 keV Tc-99m photons are attenuated well. Rb-82 has a 76 second half-life, so decay is rapid and limiting time near the patient and keeping distance are the practical measures. Thick tungsten or lead shields are used for the generator and for syringes, not thin aprons for personnel."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 60-year-old man received regadenoson stress with no exercise using Tc-99m sestamibi. The technologist wants to begin imaging 15 minutes after injection, as is done after treadmill exercise, though the scout shows intense subdiaphragmatic activity. What is the best timing?", "options": {"A": "Wait 3 hours, since sestamibi redistributes and delayed images give the best contrast.", "B": "Wait 45 to 60 minutes, because vasodilator stress alone leaves more splanchnic activity to clear.", "C": "Image at 15 minutes, since sestamibi retention is fixed and gut activity is irrelevant.", "D": "Image at 5 minutes, before hepatobiliary excretion starts and while blood levels are highest."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wait 45 to 60 minutes, because vasodilator stress alone leaves more splanchnic activity to clear.): Exercise shunts blood from the splanchnic bed, so imaging can start 15 to 30 minutes after injection. Vasodilator stress alone leaves more liver and gut tracer, so imaging usually waits 45 to 60 minutes. Sestamibi does not redistribute, so a 3 hour delay adds only decay. Very early imaging has high blood pool background."}, {"id": 492, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old diabetic man with a prior inferior infarction and an EF of 32% had stress and 4 hour redistribution Tl-201 SPECT before possible bypass surgery. The table summarizes inferior wall counts. Viability must be established. What is the best next protocol step?", "options": {"A": "Reinject about 1 mCi (37 MBq) Tl-201 and reimage shortly to detect viable hibernating myocardium.", "B": "Call the inferior wall scar because the defect persists at 4 hours, and stop imaging here.", "C": "Reinject a full 3 mCi dose and acquire immediately with only a 140 keV window setting.", "D": "Repeat the redistribution images at 8 hours using the same dose, energy window and acquisition time."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reinject about 1 mCi (37 MBq) Tl-201 and reimage shortly to detect viable hibernating myocardium.): Tl-201 redistribution can be slow in diabetic patients and in regions with severe ischemia, so a 4 hour defect often still contains viable muscle. A reinjection of about 1 mCi boosts blood levels and uptake in viable tissue, and imaging follows 15 to 60 minutes later. Tl-201 uses its 70 to 80 keV x-ray peaks, not 140 keV. A full stress dose adds needless exposure.", "image": "question_images/q_492.png"}, {"id": 493, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man has Tc-99m SPECT showing a stress and rest defect in the entire inferior wall and basal inferolateral wall. Prior angiography documented a left-dominant system with the posterior descending artery from the circumflex. Which artery most likely supplies the abnormal territory?", "options": {"A": "The left circumflex artery, because in a left-dominant system it also supplies the inferior wall.", "B": "The left anterior descending artery, because its wraparound branch runs along the inferior wall.", "C": "The right coronary artery, because the basal inferolateral wall is shared with the posterior descending artery.", "D": "The right coronary artery, because the inferior wall belongs to it in the typical right-dominant pattern."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The left circumflex artery, because in a left-dominant system it also supplies the inferior wall.): In about 85 percent of patients the right coronary gives off the posterior descending artery and supplies the inferior wall, but this patient is left dominant, so the circumflex supplies that wall. The imaging pattern should be matched to documented anatomy rather than the textbook default. A wraparound left anterior descending artery reaches only the apical inferior segments."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A nuclear cardiology laboratory acquires its daily Tc-99m extrinsic flood on a dual-head SPECT camera at 7:30 AM, collecting 30 million counts per head before any patient arrives. The flood image for one head is in the exhibit. A stress-rest sestamibi study is booked for 8:15 AM. Based on this flood, what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Call service to retune the photomultiplier tubes because the speckled texture suggests tube gain drift.", "B": "Accept the flood as uniform within statistical noise and start the scheduled patient studies on time.", "C": "Run a center of rotation acquisition first to confirm head alignment before the first patient.", "D": "Repeat the flood with a fresh source to exclude collimator contamination before scanning any patient."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accept the flood as uniform within statistical noise and start the scheduled patient studies on time.): The exhibit shows an even count density with only random fluctuation, which is expected at 30 million counts, so the head meets daily uniformity expectations and patients can be scanned. Retuning is for global drift or a focal defect, neither of which is present, so a service call would waste time. Contamination would produce a focal hot or cold artifact, not diffuse texture. Center of rotation checks alignment of the orbit and does not assess detector uniformity.", "image": "question_images/q_494.png"}, {"id": 495, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "To shorten a rest SPECT acquisition, a technologist keeps 25 seconds per stop but reduces a 180-degree orbit from 60 projections to 16 projections. The reconstructed slices show streaks radiating from the intense liver. What is the cause and the best response?", "options": {"A": "Excess time per projection saturating the crystal; reacquire with 5 seconds per stop and 16 stops", "B": "Insufficient zoom around the heart; reacquire with a larger zoom and the same 16 stops", "C": "Angular undersampling from too few projections; reacquire with 32 or more stops", "D": "A matrix that is too large; reacquire in 32 by 32 to reduce noise"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Angular undersampling from too few projections; reacquire with 32 or more stops): Filtered back projection needs enough angular samples, and too few projections leave streaks that radiate from hot structures such as the liver. Roughly 32 to 60 projections over 180 degrees is typical for cardiac SPECT with a 64 by 64 matrix. The time per stop affects noise and not the streak pattern, a smaller matrix lowers resolution, and zoom changes pixel size and not the number of views."}, {"id": 496, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old woman walks slowly with a walker and can manage about 3 METs. Her heart rate is 76 bpm at rest. A colleague sends her for treadmill SPECT rather than a pharmacologic study. What is the most likely consequence of that choice?", "options": {"A": "Reduced radiotracer uptake that forces doubling the Tc-99m dose.", "B": "A falsely low Duke score because of her low capacity, with a normal perfusion result.", "C": "Excessive tachycardia that produces false reversible defects in the inferior wall.", "D": "A submaximal heart rate response that leaves the study nondiagnostic and may miss ischemia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A submaximal heart rate response that leaves the study nondiagnostic and may miss ischemia.): A patient who cannot reach 85 percent of predicted maximum heart rate or adequate workload gives a nondiagnostic or falsely reassuring result, because ischemia needs stress adequate to unmask it. Low capacity is a reason to choose a pharmacologic study. Low effort does not change the dose needed."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Quantitative analysis of a SPECT study in a 67-year-old man with prior inferior infarction shows stress total perfusion deficit of 14 percent, rest deficit of 12 percent, and an ischemic deficit of 2 percent. EF is 40 percent. What does this imply for management?", "options": {"A": "Normal study, since deficits below 15 percent are within normal limits.", "B": "Large ischemic burden, since a stress deficit of 14 percent indicates extensive reversible disease.", "C": "Unreliable quantification, so the study should be repeated before any conclusion is drawn.", "D": "Mostly fixed scar with small ischemic burden; risk follows scar and EF, not ischemia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mostly fixed scar with small ischemic burden; risk follows scar and EF, not ischemia.): The ischemic deficit is the stress deficit minus the rest deficit, about 2 percent, so most of the 14 percent is fixed scar. Prognosis then follows scar and EF, and revascularization targeted at ischemia is unlikely to help. The stress total alone overstates ischemia, and 14 percent is abnormal, not normal."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A same-day rest-stress sestamibi protocol gives 10 mCi at rest, then the stress injection is given 2.5 hours later. To simplify the dose schedule and save tracer, the technologist gives 10 mCi again for stress. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Residual rest activity dilutes stress counts, so ischemia is underestimated.", "B": "Stress images become count rich, so the acquisition time can be safely shortened.", "C": "Rest activity has fully decayed by 2.5 hours, so the ratio of doses does not matter.", "D": "Reversibility is overestimated, because rest counts dominate and exaggerate the stress defect."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Residual rest activity dilutes stress counts, so ischemia is underestimated.): After 2.5 hours about 75 percent of the Tc-99m rest dose remains, since the half-life is 6 hours. Same-day protocols therefore give the second dose at about three times the first so stress counts dominate. With equal doses, rest activity dilutes the stress contrast and ischemia is underestimated."}, {"id": 499, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with stable exertional angina takes atenolol and isosorbide mononitrate every morning, including this morning, before a planned treadmill exercise MPI ordered to look for ischemia. What is the most likely consequence of these medications for the study?", "options": {"A": "Increased false positive anterior defects, because nitrates cause coronary steal", "B": "Larger reversible defects, because nitrates increase flow to ischemic myocardium during stress", "C": "Reduced sensitivity, so small or mild ischemic defects may be missed", "D": "Higher counts in normal myocardium, because beta blockers increase tracer extraction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduced sensitivity, so small or mild ischemic defects may be missed): Beta blockers limit heart rate and contractility and nitrates dilate epicardial vessels, so both blunt ischemia during stress. The result is a lower sensitivity for detecting mild disease, which is why guidelines recommend holding them when the aim is diagnosis. Neither produces coronary steal, bigger defects or higher extraction."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Raw projection review of a 17-minute SPECT study begun 12 minutes after treadmill exercise shows the heart edge drifting steadily upward by about 1.8 cm across the acquisition. The reconstructed images show apical blur, and the patient did not move on the table. What is the most likely cause of this finding?", "options": {"A": "Upward myocardial creep as ventilation settles after exercise, common when imaging begins early after stress.", "B": "Detector-to-patient distance set too large, which produces uniform blur without any drift.", "C": "A gating window that is too narrow, which shifts the apparent position of the left ventricle.", "D": "Gantry sag at the detector bearings, which would require mechanical service before imaging continues."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Upward myocardial creep as ventilation settles after exercise, common when imaging begins early after stress.): Creep is a gradual upward displacement of the heart as the diaphragm and lung volumes settle after exercise, typically when imaging begins soon after stress. Delaying acquisition or using creep correction reduces the effect. Mechanical sag or orbit distance would not produce a progressive drift of the myocardium within a scan."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with atypical chest pain has a stress and rest Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT study with gated ejection fraction of 60 percent. Segment scoring gives a summed stress score of 10, a summed rest score of 10 and a difference score of 0. Which perfusion interpretation and coronary territory fit the polar maps best?", "options": {"A": "A fully reversible anterior and apical defect, representing ischemia, in the left anterior descending territory.", "B": "A fixed anterior and apical defect, representing scar, in the right coronary territory.", "C": "Breast attenuation artifact with normal perfusion, so no coronary territory is involved.", "D": "A fixed anterior and apical defect, representing scar, in the left anterior descending territory."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A fixed anterior and apical defect, representing scar, in the left anterior descending territory.): The defect lies in the mid and distal anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex and looks the same on stress and rest, with summed scores of 10 on both and a difference score of 0, so it is fixed and indicates scar. The anterior wall and apex are supplied by the left anterior descending artery. Ischemia would improve on the rest map. The right coronary artery supplies the inferior wall. Breast attenuation is usually milder, and a dense matched defect points to scar.", "image": "question_images/q_501.png"}, {"id": 502, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "After dipyridamole 0.142 mg/kg/min over 4 minutes, a 66-year-old man reports headache and mild flushing. His heart rate rises from 70 to 92, blood pressure is stable and there are no ST changes on the monitor. How should this response be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Expected effects of adenosine accumulation, so no reversal is needed and injection proceeds.", "B": "Early anaphylaxis, so give epinephrine and stop the study.", "C": "Inadequate response, because heart rate rose by less than 40 beats.", "D": "Coronary steal ischemia, so give aminophylline before imaging."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Expected effects of adenosine accumulation, so no reversal is needed and injection proceeds.): Dipyridamole increases adenosine levels, and headache, flushing and a modest heart rate rise are common and expected. With stable pressure and no ST change there is no sign of ischemia or anaphylaxis. Heart rate is not the endpoint of vasodilator stress."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old man has a fixed inferior perfusion defect on SPECT after a Tc-99m sestamibi study. The sinogram shows a smooth continuous band. He is obese, and the physician is deciding whether motion could explain the defect. What does the sinogram suggest?", "options": {"A": "The smooth band means attenuation is absent, so no further correction or imaging is needed", "B": "The smooth band confirms true scar, so report infarct in the right coronary territory", "C": "Motion is unlikely to explain it, so consider attenuation and correct it before calling scar", "D": "Motion created the defect, so repeat the acquisition with the patient restrained"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Motion is unlikely to explain it, so consider attenuation and correct it before calling scar): A smooth sinogram excludes meaningful motion, but it says nothing about attenuation, which is a common cause of a fixed inferior defect in obese men from the diaphragm. Next steps include attenuation correction, prone imaging or gated wall thickening. Reporting scar from the pattern alone, or assuming no attenuation, skips those steps.", "image": "question_images/q_503.png"}, {"id": 504, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Transaxial slices of a rest Tc-99m SPECT study show a faint ring around the center of the slice, and the daily extrinsic flood image that morning showed one small round cold area. The center-of-rotation test was normal. Which cause best explains the ring?", "options": {"A": "A drifting photomultiplier tube causing a sensitivity defect the uniformity map misses", "B": "A damaged collimator septum producing a linear streak that is parallel to the table", "C": "Excessive hepatic uptake next to the inferior wall producing a scatter and reconstruction streak", "D": "A center-of-rotation offset of the detector heads causing blur of each point source"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A drifting photomultiplier tube causing a sensitivity defect the uniformity map misses): A localized sensitivity defect fixed at one detector position rotates around the center during acquisition, and after back projection it forms a ring artifact. The cold area on the flood image points to a drifting photomultiplier tube that needs retuning and a new uniformity map. A center-of-rotation error produces a tuning-fork shape from point sources and was normal here. Liver activity causes streaks, not a ring centered on the axis."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient waits for a 180 degree dual-head stress SPECT. The daily intrinsic flood on detector 1 shows a round cold spot about 6 cm across, while the flood on detector 2 is uniform. The technologist asks what to do before imaging. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Widen the energy window to 30 percent to recover counts across the cold area.", "B": "Hold imaging until service repairs the tube; uniformity correction cannot restore a dead area.", "C": "Reacquire the flood with a hotter source to see whether the cold spot fills in.", "D": "Proceed and apply the current uniformity map, because that correction fills a cold spot this size."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold imaging until service repairs the tube; uniformity correction cannot restore a dead area.): A round cold spot on a flood image is the typical sign of a photomultiplier tube that is off or out of tune. Uniformity correction maps adjust modest variations but cannot create counts where the detector is blind. A wider window or a hotter source does not change a dead tube. Service must repair or retune it before patient studies."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During rest SPECT, a patient coughs and the heart shifts abruptly by about 3 cm laterally and upward at the midpoint of acquisition, then stays in the new position. The sinogram shows a clear step. Software correction produces a persistent inferolateral distortion. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report the corrected images with a note, since software correction restores quality for any displacement", "B": "Interpret only the first half of the projections after discarding the later projections", "C": "Reprocess with a lower cutoff filter to smooth the discontinuity between the two halves", "D": "Repeat the acquisition, since a large abrupt shift exceeds software correction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat the acquisition, since a large abrupt shift exceeds software correction): Software correction works best for small smooth drift or modest single shifts. A large abrupt step of about 3 cm, with a residual distortion after correction, means the data cannot be trusted, and the better course is to repeat the acquisition if the patient can cooperate. Using half the projections undersamples the orbit, and smoothing does not align projections."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A Tc-99m sestamibi syringe is labeled 30 mCi (1110 MBq) at 06:00. At 09:00 the technologist measures it in the dose calibrator with the isotope setting on Tc-99m and reads 30 mCi. The half-life is 6.02 hours. What does this finding suggest, and what should be done?", "options": {"A": "The syringe gained activity from generator ingrowth, so record the higher value and dispense it.", "B": "The reading is acceptable, since a dose calibrator reports activity at the label time after delay.", "C": "The reading exceeds the expected 21 mCi, so check label, time and isotope setting before dispensing.", "D": "The calibrator linearity has failed, so use the reading but correct it by 0.71 decay factor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The reading exceeds the expected 21 mCi, so check label, time and isotope setting before dispensing.): Three hours is 0.498 half-lives, so a 30 mCi dose at 06:00 should read 30 x 0.708 = 21.2 mCi at 09:00. A reading of 30 mCi means the label, the calibration time or the isotope setting is wrong, or the syringe is not what it is labeled, so it must be resolved before any patient is dosed. A calibrator measures current activity, and a syringe cannot gain activity."}, {"id": 508, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with diabetes and exertional dyspnea has regadenoson SPECT. The stress images show a larger cavity than the rest images and reduced anterior and anteroseptal counts, with summed stress score 12, rest score 0 and difference score 12. Rest images look normal and ejection fraction is 56 percent. Which next step is best?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the study with a different tracer to resolve a suspected artifact", "B": "Refer for angiography, since dilation with a large reversible defect suggests high risk coronary disease", "C": "Reassure the patient, since a defect resolving at rest means a normal study with low risk", "D": "Continue medical therapy alone and repeat imaging in one year to track change in the cavity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for angiography, since dilation with a large reversible defect suggests high risk coronary disease): Transient ischemic dilation with a large reversible anterior and anteroseptal defect and a normal rest study indicates severe ischemia, possibly with more disease than the defect alone shows. That is a high risk imaging profile where angiography is appropriate. A normal study would show no cavity change, and a different tracer would not remove a genuine physiologic change.", "image": "question_images/q_508.png"}, {"id": 509, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "After a building power outage, a dual-head camera was restarted and the technologist acquired an intrinsic flood on head 2, shown in the exhibit. A colleague says re-peaking the photopeak restores detector performance after an outage, and a stress SPECT patient is checked in. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Wait thirty minutes for crystal temperature to stabilize after the restart, then image the patient.", "B": "Reload last week's uniformity correction map, since a stale map causes localized cold areas.", "C": "Re-peak the 140 keV photopeak, since tuning restores tube gain and clears such a spot.", "D": "Peaking cannot restore a failed tube, so keep the head out of use for service."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Peaking cannot restore a failed tube, so keep the head out of use for service.): The exhibit shows a localized round cold spot, not an overall shift, so the energy tune is not the problem and re-peaking leaves it unchanged. Loading an old map cannot create or remove a hardware defect, and thermal settling after power loss affects the whole crystal gradually. A tube that dropped out after the outage needs service before clinical use.", "image": "question_images/q_509.png"}, {"id": 510, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old man with frequent premature ventricular beats has a gated blood pool study acquired with a 20 percent beat rejection window, and the curve is in the exhibit. Background-corrected counts are 17,886 at end-diastole and 6,298 at end-systole. Which statement best describes the validity of the study and the EF?", "options": {"A": "Valid only if the beat window is widened to 50 percent, so repeat before reporting.", "B": "Valid study, since the curve returns to its starting counts; the EF is 65 percent.", "C": "Valid study, but the EF is 35 percent, since end-systolic over end-diastolic counts gives it.", "D": "Invalid study, since ectopy distorts the curve, so the 65 percent EF cannot be used."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Valid study, since the curve returns to its starting counts; the EF is 65 percent.): A complete, smooth curve that returns to roughly its starting count shows the accepted beats covered the full cardiac cycle, so ectopy was effectively rejected. Stroke counts are 17,886 minus 6,298, or 11,588, and 11,588 divided by 17,886 is 65 percent. A wider window would admit short ectopic beats and lose end-diastolic counts, and ES over ED gives the residual fraction of 35 percent.", "image": "question_images/q_510.png"}, {"id": 511, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a nuclear cardiology lab audit, the dose calibrator log shows daily constancy checks and an annual accuracy test, but the last linearity test was done 14 months ago. Under US NRC rules for dose calibrators, what finding is correct?", "options": {"A": "Linearity is required daily, together with constancy, using the long-lived reference source", "B": "Linearity is required annually, along with accuracy, so the lab is within the allowed interval", "C": "Linearity must be tested at least every three months, so the lab is out of compliance", "D": "Linearity is required only at installation, so no repeat test is due after the first one"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Linearity must be tested at least every three months, so the lab is out of compliance): NRC rules require dose calibrator constancy each day of use, accuracy at installation and annually, linearity at installation and at least quarterly, and geometry dependence at installation. A linearity test 14 months ago breaks the quarterly interval. The annual interval belongs to accuracy, a daily check is constancy with a long-lived source, and installation-only testing applies to geometry."}, {"id": 512, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has Rb-82 perfusion imaging and FDG PET for suspected cardiac sarcoidosis. She ate a sandwich the morning of the scan. FDG images show diffuse intense uptake throughout the left ventricle. What is the most likely cause of this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Widespread active myocardial sarcoidosis involving the entire left ventricle wall, so treatment should begin immediately.", "B": "Excess FDG dose causing blooming of the myocardial signal on attenuation-corrected images.", "C": "Hibernating myocardium throughout the septum, which characteristically takes up glucose avidly on these images.", "D": "Incomplete suppression of normal myocardial glucose use from poor diet preparation, making uptake nondiagnostic."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Incomplete suppression of normal myocardial glucose use from poor diet preparation, making uptake nondiagnostic.): Sarcoid FDG protocols require suppression of physiologic myocardial glucose use, typically a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet for at least 24 hours followed by a prolonged fast. Eating carbohydrate shortly before the scan leaves the normal myocardium glucose-avid, producing diffuse uptake that cannot be read for inflammation. Diffuse uptake of this kind is a preparation failure, not proof of disease, and dose does not produce it."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with diabetes and a long smoking history presents with new dyspnea and an echocardiographic EF of 30 percent. He has no angina, no left ventricular hypertrophy, normal ECG voltage and no conduction disease. Coronary anatomy is unknown, and he prefers a noninvasive first step. Which test is most appropriate for establishing the cause?", "options": {"A": "I-123 MIBG scintigraphy, since the heart to mediastinum ratio defines the cause of cardiomyopathy", "B": "Tc-99m pyrophosphate imaging, since amyloid is the leading cause of new heart failure at his age", "C": "Rest and stress perfusion imaging, to assess ischemic cause when coronary status is unknown", "D": "Repeat echocardiography in 3 months, since a newly depressed EF should be reassessed before any testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rest and stress perfusion imaging, to assess ischemic cause when coronary status is unknown): In new heart failure with reduced EF and unknown coronary anatomy, an ischemic cause must be sought, and perfusion imaging is an appropriate noninvasive option when the patient prefers it. Pyrophosphate needs red flags such as LVH, low voltage or heart block, which are absent. MIBG gives prognosis rather than etiology, and waiting delays treatable disease."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 44-year-old man with atypical chest pain, a normal resting ECG and a good exercise capacity is scheduled for Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. His estimated pretest probability is low to intermediate. The laboratory is choosing between stress first and rest first sequencing. Which sequence is best and why?", "options": {"A": "Rest first, since a baseline rest study identifies artifacts before stress and lowers total dose", "B": "Stress first, since stress images require a rest comparison to be interpreted in any patient", "C": "Rest first, since rest images must precede exercise so attenuation correction can be calculated", "D": "Stress first, since normal stress images let the rest study be cancelled, saving radiation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stress first, since normal stress images let the rest study be cancelled, saving radiation): In a patient likely to have normal perfusion, stress-first imaging lets a normal stress scan end the study, a stress-only approach that cuts time and exposure. Rest-first suits patients with known large infarcts or suspected artifacts. Attenuation correction does not depend on the sequence, and a clearly normal stress study can stand alone."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old obese man has SPECT showing a large reversible anterior apical defect with an SDS of 9, a mild fixed inferior defect with normal thickening, TID 1.05 and a rest EF of 33 percent. Which statement best identifies the basis for high risk?", "options": {"A": "High risk comes from TID, so a ratio near 1.05 supports the label on its own.", "B": "The fixed inferior defect indicates infarct, which defines high risk, while the anterior defect is secondary.", "C": "No finding is high risk, since that label needs TID above 1.2 plus a fixed defect.", "D": "Risk rests on the reversible anterior defect and low EF; the inferior defect is probably attenuation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Risk rests on the reversible anterior defect and low EF; the inferior defect is probably attenuation.): A large reversible territory with an SDS of 9 and an EF of 33 percent are accepted high risk markers. The mild inferior defect with preserved thickening in an obese patient most likely reflects attenuation. A ratio of 1.05 is normal and carries no risk, and no rule demands TID plus a fixed defect."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "For an in vivo red cell labeling procedure, a technologist injects stannous pyrophosphate and gives Tc-99m pertechnetate only 3 minutes later because the schedule is tight. The blood pool image is poor with high stomach and thyroid uptake. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Pyrophosphate is not needed for labeling, so the injection simply added unbound tin to blood", "B": "The Tc-99m dose was too high, saturating all the available red cell binding sites", "C": "Too short an interval after pyrophosphate left too little stannous ion in cells", "D": "Excess time after pyrophosphate caused the stannous ion to leave the red cells before labeling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Too short an interval after pyrophosphate left too little stannous ion in cells): In vivo labeling needs about 15 to 30 minutes for stannous pyrophosphate to enter red cells and be present for reducing pertechnetate before Tc-99m is given. At 3 minutes too little tin is inside the cells, so much pertechnetate stays free and collects in the stomach and thyroid. Waiting longer would help, the dose is far from saturating sites, and the tin is the active component."}, {"id": 517, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On stress SPECT, four segments in the anterior and apical walls each score 2 for moderate reduction, and rest images are normal. Which statement best combines extent, severity, and the summed stress score for this study? He is a 60-year-old man.", "options": {"A": "Four of 17 segments, about 24 percent of the ventricle, moderate, summed stress score 8.", "B": "Four segments of moderate severity, summed stress score 4, because segments count once.", "C": "Four segments of severe reduction, summed stress score 16, because each segment scores four.", "D": "Four of 17 segments, about 12 percent, because 8 divided by 68 gives extent."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Four of 17 segments, about 24 percent of the ventricle, moderate, summed stress score 8.): Extent is the fraction of segments involved, 4 of 17 or about 24 percent. Severity is the average depth, here a score of 2 meaning moderate reduction. The summed stress score is the total of all segment scores, 4 times 2, or 8. Dividing 8 by 68 gives a score fraction near 12 percent that is not extent, and scoring each segment 4 would mean absent uptake."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist begins imaging 5 minutes after an exercise injection of Tc-99m sestamibi, as logged in the exhibit. The first processed polar map shows intense subdiaphragmatic hepatic activity touching the inferior wall and a falsely reduced inferior count density. The patient is stable. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Change to prone positioning now, since it moves the inferior wall away from the liver", "B": "Restart after at least 30 minutes, with water or a fatty meal to speed clearance", "C": "Reinject a second 20 mCi dose, since higher myocardial counts will overcome the liver contribution", "D": "Continue acquiring and rely on CT attenuation correction to remove liver activity from the inferior wall"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Restart after at least 30 minutes, with water or a fatty meal to speed clearance): Sestamibi clears from the liver and gut slowly, and after exercise typically 15 to 30 minutes is needed, longer after vasodilator stress alone. Imaging too early lets high subdiaphragmatic activity overlap the inferior wall and distort count normalization. Attenuation correction and extra dose do not remove extracardiac activity, and prone positioning will not clear it.", "image": "question_images/q_518.png"}, {"id": 519, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A busy laboratory wants the shortest practical interval between exercise injection and imaging in a patient without contraindications. Past sestamibi studies here showed hepatobiliary activity near the inferior wall. Which tracer choice is best, and what feature of the tracer justifies it?", "options": {"A": "Sestamibi, because its hepatobiliary clearance in the first 15 minutes is faster than the alternative", "B": "Tetrofosmin, whose faster hepatic clearance permits earlier imaging with less inferior interference", "C": "Either Tc-99m agent, since liver clearance rates are identical though extraction fractions differ", "D": "Thallium-201, since its hepatic clearance within minutes allows immediate imaging at low radiation dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tetrofosmin, whose faster hepatic clearance permits earlier imaging with less inferior interference): Tetrofosmin clears from liver and lung faster than sestamibi, so images can be acquired earlier after exercise with less inferior wall contamination. Sestamibi has slower hepatic clearance, thallium-201 delivers a higher effective dose with worse image quality, and the two Tc-99m agents do differ in hepatic clearance."}, {"id": 520, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man had coronary artery bypass surgery three years ago. He has recurrent exertional chest discomfort, walks 7 METs, has a normal resting ECG and had anaphylaxis to iodinated contrast. He asks which test is best. Which test best explains his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Exercise perfusion imaging, since it localizes ischemia by territory and avoids contrast.", "B": "Coronary artery calcium scoring, since calcium predicts graft failure after bypass surgery.", "C": "Coronary CT angiography of the grafts, since it avoids stress and is allowed with contrast allergy.", "D": "Exercise ECG alone, since it localizes ischemia well after bypass surgery."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exercise perfusion imaging, since it localizes ischemia by territory and avoids contrast.): For symptomatic patients after revascularization, stress imaging is appropriate, and exercise is preferred when capacity is good. Perfusion imaging localizes ischemia to graft or native territories. ECG alone localizes poorly, calcium scoring does not assess graft function, and CT angiography needs iodinated contrast."}, {"id": 521, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 160 kg man receives a standard 10 mCi Tc-99m sestamibi rest dose, and the projections are acquired for 15 seconds each. The image counts are very low and the gated study is noisy. What is the most likely consequence for interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Contrast improves, since lower counts reduce scatter in the myocardial region and clarify the borders.", "B": "Gated ejection fraction is systematically overestimated, while perfusion defects remain fully reliable.", "C": "High statistical noise creates false heterogeneity and unreliable gated function, so dose or time should rise.", "D": "Resolution is unaffected, so only the dose record needs correction and the study is readable."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High statistical noise creates false heterogeneity and unreliable gated function, so dose or time should rise.): Low counts increase Poisson noise, so uptake looks patchy and false defects can appear, and gated function becomes unreliable because the edges are poorly defined. Larger patients attenuate more, so adding dose, time or both is appropriate. Contrast and resolution do not improve with fewer counts."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On projection images of a gated SPECT of a 66-year-old man, the heart moves vertically at frame 12 of 32, shifting 4 pixels on the sinogram. Pixel size is 6.4 mm. What displacement does this represent and what is the best response?", "options": {"A": "A 4 mm shift, too small to matter, so only the polar map needs checking.", "B": "A 25.6 mm shift; apply motion correction or repeat the acquisition before interpreting.", "C": "A 1.6 mm shift, which is below any useful threshold, so interpretation can go ahead.", "D": "A 10.2 mm shift, which needs only a change of the reconstruction filter."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 25.6 mm shift; apply motion correction or repeat the acquisition before interpreting.): Displacement equals pixels times pixel size: 4 times 6.4 is 25.6 mm. A discontinuity of that size in the sinogram can create false defects, so motion correction or reacquisition is needed. The conversion cannot be skipped, and a filter does not fix a mid acquisition shift."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man has ongoing rest angina, 2 mm anterior ST depression, a rising troponin and recurrent episodes despite medical therapy. A colleague suggests regadenoson Rb-82 PET today to define his ischemia. What is the best next step in his care?", "options": {"A": "Perform regadenoson Rb-82 PET today to define the culprit territory before angiography", "B": "Perform rest sestamibi SPECT tomorrow after pain stops to confirm the diagnosis", "C": "Proceed to urgent coronary angiography, because he has high-risk acute coronary syndrome", "D": "Perform dobutamine echocardiography once pain resolves to assess wall motion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Proceed to urgent coronary angiography, because he has high-risk acute coronary syndrome): Ongoing rest angina with dynamic ST changes and rising troponin is a high-risk acute coronary syndrome, where stress testing is contraindicated and early invasive evaluation is indicated. Imaging adds delay and risk without changing the need for angiography. Rest imaging tomorrow or dobutamine echo later would postpone treatment."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman wearing a size DD bra has no angina and a normal ECG. Her SPECT images show the anterior wall slightly dimmer on both rows, and summed stress and rest scores against the normal database are 0 and 0. Ejection fraction is 60 percent. What is the most likely explanation and best step?", "options": {"A": "Anterior infarct from left anterior descending occlusion, so refer for coronary angiography", "B": "Breast attenuation artifact, so review gated wall thickening and the raw projection images before calling disease", "C": "Hibernating anterior myocardium, so order FDG PET to assess anterior wall viability", "D": "Liver uptake overlap lowering anterior counts, so repeat imaging after a fatty meal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Breast attenuation artifact, so review gated wall thickening and the raw projection images before calling disease): Breast attenuation lowers anterior counts equally at stress and rest, and the scores of 0 against the normal database show the counts fall within the range expected for women with breast tissue. Checking gated thickening and the projection images shows the breast shadow and confirms the heart is normal. Liver activity affects the inferior wall, and infarct or hibernation would be accompanied by abnormal contraction.", "image": "question_images/q_524.png"}, {"id": 525, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 59-year-old man had his left arm slip from the overhead support during stress SPECT. The sinogram shows a lateral shift of the band across angles. Raw images show the heart remains inside the field of view. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Check the heart stays in view on raw projections; correct if so, repeat if not", "B": "Accept as is because sideways movement cannot degrade tomographic reconstruction", "C": "Repeat only the CT attenuation scan since motion affects only the transmission data here", "D": "Increase matrix size and filter cutoff to compensate for the blur in reconstruction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check the heart stays in view on raw projections; correct if so, repeat if not): Lateral movement degrades reconstruction just as any displacement does. If the heart stays in the field of view on every projection, software motion correction can realign the data, and the corrected slices should be checked. If the heart leaves the field, the data are incomplete and the study should be repeated. Filter changes cannot fix misregistration, and the CT scan is not the problem.", "image": "question_images/q_525.png"}, {"id": 526, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist declares her pregnancy in writing at the start of month 4 of a 9 month gestation. Her dosimetry records show 0.10 rem (1.0 mSv) to the embryo and fetus from conception to the declaration date. Under 10 CFR 20.1208, how much more dose may the embryo and fetus receive for the remainder of the pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "0.40 rem (4 mSv) in total, which is about 0.07 rem (0.7 mSv) per remaining month", "B": "0.33 rem (3.3 mSv), by prorating 0.5 rem across the 6 months remaining out of 9", "C": "0.50 rem (5 mSv), because the gestation limit starts afresh on the day of declaration", "D": "0.05 rem (0.5 mSv), because the dose already exceeded 0.45 rem before she declared"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (0.40 rem (4 mSv) in total, which is about 0.07 rem (0.7 mSv) per remaining month): The limit is 0.5 rem for the whole gestation, so dose already received counts against it: 0.50 minus 0.10 leaves 0.40 rem. Spread over 6 remaining months that is about 0.07 rem per month, matching the requirement to avoid substantial variation above a uniform monthly rate. The 0.05 rem remainder rule applies only when 0.45 rem has already been exceeded at declaration. Prorating by months gives 0.33 rem and ignores the actual dose history."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A generator is eluted with a theoretical maximum Tc-99m activity of 500 mCi, calculated from its Mo-99 content and ingrowth time. The dose calibrator reads 380 mCi in the eluate vial. The lead technologist asks for the elution yield and a decision. What is the correct yield and decision?", "options": {"A": "24 percent yield, indicating generator failure that requires immediate return of the unit to the vendor", "B": "76 percent yield, a normal elution efficiency, so the eluate can be used after breakthrough testing", "C": "120 mCi yield, which is the lost activity and therefore the amount available for kit preparation", "D": "132 percent yield, indicating generator overproduction that should be investigated before the eluate is used"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (76 percent yield, a normal elution efficiency, so the eluate can be used after breakthrough testing): Yield is measured activity divided by theoretical activity: 380 divided by 500 is 0.76, or 76 percent, a normal elution efficiency, so the eluate is acceptable once Mo-99 breakthrough and radiochemical purity are checked. Inverting the ratio gives 132 percent, which is physically implausible. The 24 percent figure and the 120 mCi value describe what was not eluted, not the yield."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with atypical chest pain exercises for 11 minutes by the Bruce protocol with no ST deviation and no angina, and his perfusion images are normal with an EF of 60 percent. What is his Duke treadmill score and how does it fit?", "options": {"A": "The score is minus 5, an intermediate result that conflicts with the normal perfusion images.", "B": "The score is plus 4, an intermediate result that agrees with the normal perfusion images.", "C": "The score is zero, which needs the perfusion result to define the risk group.", "D": "The score is plus 11, a low risk result that agrees with the normal perfusion images."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The score is plus 11, a low risk result that agrees with the normal perfusion images.): The Duke score equals exercise minutes minus 5 times maximum ST deviation in mm minus 4 times the angina index. With 11 minutes and no ST change or angina it is 11, in the low risk range of plus 5 or above. That fits normal perfusion. Penalties apply only when ST change or angina occurred."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man took his metoprolol this morning and reached a peak heart rate of 118 bpm on the treadmill without symptoms or ECG changes. Predicted maximum heart rate is 220 minus age. What percentage of predicted maximum did he reach, and how does it affect interpretation?", "options": {"A": "About 74 percent, below 85 percent, so a normal image cannot exclude ischemia reliably", "B": "About 74 percent, so a nitrate should now be added to raise the test's sensitivity", "C": "About 74 percent, but a normal image still excludes ischemia because beta blockade lowers demand", "D": "About 87 percent, which is adequate, so a normal image excludes ischemia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 74 percent, below 85 percent, so a normal image cannot exclude ischemia reliably): Predicted maximum is 160 bpm, so 118 divided by 160 equals 74 percent, which is under the 85 percent threshold for an adequate exercise test. Beta blockers limit heart rate and ischemia, lowering sensitivity, so a normal image in this setting is less reassuring. Dividing by the target gives 87 percent, and nitrates also reduce sensitivity rather than increasing it."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old diabetic patient with severe anterior dysfunction is scheduled for FDG viability imaging before revascularization. His fasting glucose is 255 mg/dL and he took no insulin this morning. Which action best achieves a diagnostic study while keeping him safe?", "options": {"A": "Give titrated intravenous insulin with frequent glucose checks, injecting FDG near 100 to 140 mg/dL.", "B": "Cancel the study and recommend perfusion only imaging, since glucose above 200 prevents FDG use.", "C": "Give a single large insulin bolus of 20 units and inject FDG without further glucose checks.", "D": "Inject FDG immediately, because hyperglycemia increases myocardial uptake, and image at 30 minutes."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give titrated intravenous insulin with frequent glucose checks, injecting FDG near 100 to 140 mg/dL.): FDG viability needs the myocardium to use glucose, so glucose and insulin are managed, usually with titrated intravenous insulin and glucose measurements every 15 minutes or so, injecting when glucose is within a target range. An unmonitored large bolus risks hypoglycemia. Hyperglycemia lowers myocardial FDG uptake and does not cancel the study, since protocols exist to correct it."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist places a syringe of unused Tc-99m sestamibi, which has a half-life of 6 hours, into decay-in-storage after the clinic day ends. What is the minimum holding period before it can be surveyed and discarded as ordinary trash in this laboratory?", "options": {"A": "At least 4 half-lives, or 24 hours, then a survey must show background", "B": "At least 10 half-lives of Tl-201, or about 30 days, then a survey", "C": "At least 2 half-lives, or 12 hours, then relabeling the syringe as nonradioactive", "D": "At least 10 half-lives, or 60 hours, then a survey at background"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At least 10 half-lives, or 60 hours, then a survey at background): NRC decay-in-storage rules require holding material for at least 10 half-lives, which is 60 hours for Tc-99m with a 6 hour half-life. The waste must then be surveyed with an appropriate meter and show no detectable activity above background before radiation labels are removed. Four or two half-lives are too short, and Tl-201 has a different half-life."}, {"id": 532, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 74-year-old man with heart failure, increased wall thickness and low ECG voltage has a Tc-99m pyrophosphate scan with grade 3 uptake, but no serum free light chain or protein electrophoresis was done. What is the consequence of this omission?", "options": {"A": "A positive scan may be attributed to transthyretin disease when AL is present, delaying urgent treatment.", "B": "The scan is falsely negative in AL amyloidosis, since light chain deposits cannot bind bone tracers.", "C": "Genetic testing would no longer be needed, since uptake with grade 3 proves wild-type disease directly.", "D": "The uptake grade becomes unreliable, since monoclonal proteins interfere with tracer binding in the myocardium."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A positive scan may be attributed to transthyretin disease when AL is present, delaying urgent treatment.): Bone tracers bind transthyretin deposits strongly but can also show uptake in some AL cases. The diagnosis of ATTR cardiac amyloidosis by scintigraphy requires excluding a monoclonal protein with serum and urine immunofixation and free light chains. Without that, AL may be missed, and AL needs prompt chemotherapy. Genetic testing is still needed to classify hereditary versus wild-type ATTR."}, {"id": 533, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist who draws up about 30 Tc-99m syringes daily has a quarterly ring badge below the reporting level. A colleague with the same workload reads 600 mrem. Her ring is on the dominant hand holding the syringe, with the detector label facing the back of the finger. What explains the difference?", "options": {"A": "The ring sat on the dominant hand, which is further from the vial", "B": "The control badge stored near the hot lab was subtracted, which wiped out her reading", "C": "Tc-99m photons are too low in energy for a ring dosimeter to register on any person", "D": "The detector faced away from the source, so her finger shielded the photons from the chip"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The detector faced away from the source, so her finger shielded the photons from the chip): For syringe work the source is in the palm side of the hand, so the ring detector should face the palm. Worn with the label to the back of the finger, the finger tissue attenuates the 140 keV photons before they reach the chip and the reading is falsely low. Ring dosimeters do detect Tc-99m, a control badge removes only background, and the hand holding the syringe is usually the higher dose hand."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A heart failure patient has I-123 MIBG planar imaging with thyroid blockade given before the dose. The early heart-to-mediastinum ratio is markedly low at 1.3. His medication list was entered after the scan. Which agent most plausibly caused a falsely low ratio?", "options": {"A": "Use of a medium-energy collimator, which reduces septal penetration and lowers the measured ratio.", "B": "Labetalol, which inhibits norepinephrine reuptake and lowers cardiac MIBG uptake without nerve damage.", "C": "Potassium iodide given for thyroid blockade, which also blocks neuronal uptake in the myocardium.", "D": "A four-hour fast before injection, which depletes catecholamine stores in cardiac sympathetic neurons."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Labetalol, which inhibits norepinephrine reuptake and lowers cardiac MIBG uptake without nerve damage.): MIBG enters sympathetic nerve terminals by the uptake-1 transporter. Drugs such as labetalol, tricyclic antidepressants, reserpine and some calcium channel blockers interfere and lower the measured ratio. Potassium iodide protects the thyroid only. A medium-energy collimator is preferred for I-123 and does not falsely depress uptake."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A flood image on head 2 of a dual-head camera is shown in the exhibit. A new uniformity correction map was applied after last week's service visit, and the technologist notes that the gradient looks the same with the map on or off. The next study is a sestamibi SPECT. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Replace the photomultiplier tube at the brightest edge, since a gradient indicates a failing tube there.", "B": "Accept the image, since correction maps are designed to flatten gradients of this size during reconstruction.", "C": "Increase the flood to 100 million counts so the gradient averages out and the study proceeds.", "D": "Repeat tuning and photopeak calibration on all detectors, then reacquire the flood and a new map."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat tuning and photopeak calibration on all detectors, then reacquire the flood and a new map.): A smooth, systematic gradient that persists with the map on points to a tuning or energy window problem rather than a local defect, and maps handle small residual differences only. Tuning first, then a fresh flood and map, restores uniform response. More counts reduce noise but not a systematic gradient, and a single failed tube produces a localized spot.", "image": "question_images/q_535.png"}, {"id": 536, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man with a lateral Q wave from a remote circumflex infarction now reports atypical chest tightness at moderate exertion. EF is 58 percent. Stress and rest sestamibi slices show a small mid lateral defect that looks the same on both rows, with scores of 3 and 3. Which conclusion best guides management of his symptoms at this point?", "options": {"A": "Patient motion between the two acquisitions, so repeat both studies before reporting anything about perfusion.", "B": "Stress induced lateral ischemia added to the old infarct, so add a nitrate and plan angiography.", "C": "Hibernating lateral myocardium that bypass surgery would improve, so referral for surgery is the next step.", "D": "Lateral scar without inducible ischemia; seek other causes of the symptoms and continue secondary prevention."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lateral scar without inducible ischemia; seek other causes of the symptoms and continue secondary prevention.): A small defect that is the same at stress and rest, with a difference score of 0, is scar from the old circumflex infarct without inducible ischemia. His symptoms are therefore unlikely to be ischemic, so other causes should be sought while secondary prevention continues. Nothing on the slices suggests added ischemia, hibernation, which would show a fill-in or viable counts and would not be assumed, or motion, since both rows match.", "image": "question_images/q_536.png"}, {"id": 537, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A laboratory reviews the chart of soft tissue linear attenuation coefficients at the photon energies of Tl-201 (70 keV), Tc-99m (140 keV) and Rb-82 (511 keV) before choosing correction methods for its SPECT and PET systems. Based on the chart, which conclusion about attenuation correction is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201 photons are attenuated more per centimeter than 511 keV photons, so artifacts are larger.", "B": "Rb-82 photons at 511 keV are attenuated most per centimeter, so PET needs the largest correction.", "C": "CT based correction is valid only at 511 keV, so SPECT cannot use a CT map.", "D": "Tc-99m photons at 140 keV pass through soft tissue with negligible loss, so correction is unnecessary."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tl-201 photons are attenuated more per centimeter than 511 keV photons, so artifacts are larger.): The curve falls as photon energy rises, with about 0.19 per cm at 70 keV, 0.15 at 140 keV and 0.10 at 511 keV. Lower energy photons are therefore attenuated more per centimeter of soft tissue, so Tl-201 shows larger attenuation effects than the PET photons. Attenuation is not negligible at 140 keV, and CT maps are scaled to the SPECT photon energy for SPECT/CT correction.", "image": "question_images/q_537.png"}, {"id": 538, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old diabetic man has exertional angina and takes a beta blocker. Regadenoson stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT shows mild to moderate reduced counts in the anterior, septal, apical and lateral segments at stress, all normal at rest. The summed stress score is 12 and the summed rest score is 0. Which interpretation and plan fit best?", "options": {"A": "Single vessel ischemia limited to one territory, so continue medical therapy and recheck in a year.", "B": "Artifact from a lateral wall attenuation, so reimage after a delay of one hour.", "C": "Balanced three vessel ischemia that makes the study falsely normal, so repeat imaging with PET.", "D": "Reversible anterior descending and circumflex defects mean multivessel ischemia; refer for angiography."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reversible anterior descending and circumflex defects mean multivessel ischemia; refer for angiography.): Reduced counts in the anterior, septal, apical and lateral segments that normalize at rest span at least the left anterior descending and circumflex distributions, so two territories show inducible ischemia. The summed difference score of 12 is about 18 percent of the maximum of 68, a substantial burden even though most single segments score only 1. Balanced ischemia would erase the defects, which is not the case. Attenuation tends to be fixed and does not reverse at rest. Because this is multiterritory ischemia with symptoms, angiography is the reasonable next step to define anatomy for revascularization.", "image": "question_images/q_538.png"}, {"id": 539, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Slices from a gated SPECT study look noisy, and a small inferior defect is hard to evaluate. A physicist lowers the Butterworth cutoff from 0.50 to 0.30 cycles per pixel at the same order. What effect should the reader expect?", "options": {"A": "Less noise and higher resolution, since the filter now removes the statistical fluctuation only", "B": "Less noise and smoother images, but lower resolution that can reduce the contrast of small defects", "C": "Unchanged noise but a steeper roll-off, because the cutoff only controls how fast the filter falls", "D": "More noise and sharper images, since a lower cutoff admits additional high-frequency information"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Less noise and smoother images, but lower resolution that can reduce the contrast of small defects): In a Butterworth filter, the cutoff sets the frequency where response begins to fall and the order sets the steepness of the fall. Lowering the cutoff removes more high-frequency content, so noise falls and the image is smoother, but fine detail is lost and small defects may show less contrast. The opposite result describes raising the cutoff. Order governs the roll-off, and noise suppression always costs some resolution."}, {"id": 540, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea has Rb-82 PET/CT for risk assessment. The attenuation correction CT shows a 14 mm spiculated nodule in the right upper lobe, and perfusion is normal with an EF of 58 percent. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Mention it only in the technical section, since perfusion is normal and EF preserved.", "B": "Disregard it, since low dose attenuation CT has no diagnostic role in PET.", "C": "Report the nodule to the referring physician and recommend dedicated chest imaging.", "D": "Order FDG PET immediately without notifying the physician, to save the patient time."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the nodule to the referring physician and recommend dedicated chest imaging.): Attenuation CT is not diagnostic quality, yet a spiculated nodule is a clinically important extracardiac finding that must be reported with a recommendation for dedicated imaging. Ignoring it or burying it risks delay in diagnosis, and ordering tests without telling the clinician bypasses care."}, {"id": 541, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with left bundle branch block and exertional dyspnea receives regadenoson with Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Ejection fraction is 58 percent. The summed stress score is 8, the summed rest score is 4 and the difference score is 4, all in septal segments, while the anterior, lateral and inferior walls score 0. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Septal and anteroseptal ischemia from a proximal left anterior descending stenosis, so angiography is needed.", "B": "Inferoseptal liver attenuation, so the study should be repeated in the prone position.", "C": "A fixed septal scar from a prior anteroseptal infarct, so no ischemia is present.", "D": "Septal defect typical of left bundle branch block, not diagnostic of left anterior descending disease."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Septal defect typical of left bundle branch block, not diagnostic of left anterior descending disease.): Left bundle branch block causes abnormal septal activation and a septal perfusion defect that is present at rest and only partly reversible, without disease in the left anterior descending artery. Confinement of every score to the septum, with the anterior, apical and lateral walls normal, supports this pattern, and vasodilator stress minimizes it. Reading the defect as ischemia or scar would send the patient for needless angiography or viability work.", "image": "question_images/q_541.png"}, {"id": 542, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man had Tl-201 injected at peak treadmill stress. The exhibit shows imaging times and an inferior fixed defect on both sets of images. The referring physician asks whether this defect represents scar or something else. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Imaging began after 95 minutes, so redistribution may hide ischemia and reinjection is needed.", "B": "A repeat study at 24 hours is more accurate than reinjection, since redistribution is complete.", "C": "The defect is fixed on both studies, so it is nonviable scar and revascularization is futile.", "D": "Delay does not alter thallium images because uptake is permanent, so the fixed defect is infarction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Imaging began after 95 minutes, so redistribution may hide ischemia and reinjection is needed.): Thallium redistributes beginning within minutes, and stress images should be acquired within about 10 to 15 minutes. After 95 minutes, an ischemic territory can partly refill and look fixed, which underestimates ischemia. Fixed defects on thallium may still contain viable myocardium, and reinjection or other viability testing should be used before calling it scar.", "image": "question_images/q_542.png"}, {"id": 543, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with NYHA class III heart failure and ejection fraction 28 percent has an I-123 MIBG study before a decision about defibrillator therapy. The late heart-to-mediastinum ratio is 1.28 on planar imaging. What does this low ratio most directly reflect?", "options": {"A": "Increased washout through extraneuronal uptake, indicating that the tracer was injected with poor technique.", "B": "Impaired presynaptic norepinephrine uptake and storage, meaning cardiac sympathetic denervation and higher event risk.", "C": "Reduced resting myocardial blood flow, indicating hibernating myocardium that would improve after revascularization.", "D": "Reduced beta-adrenergic receptor density on myocytes, indicating postsynaptic downregulation as the measured process."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired presynaptic norepinephrine uptake and storage, meaning cardiac sympathetic denervation and higher event risk.): MIBG is a norepinephrine analog taken up and stored by presynaptic sympathetic neurons. A late heart-to-mediastinum ratio below about 1.6 and faster washout indicate impaired uptake and are linked to worse outcomes in heart failure. It does not measure perfusion or beta receptor density, and low values are not explained by injection technique."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist places a Tc-99m point source 1.2 m from the bare crystal of a camera with a 54 cm useful field of view and acquires an intrinsic flood. The image shows a smooth bright center fading to the edges, with no focal defects. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Move the source to 30 cm so photons strike the crystal more perpendicularly.", "B": "Repeat with the source at least five field widths away, about 2.7 m.", "C": "Call service to retune the photomultiplier tubes, since a smooth central gradient signals gain drift.", "D": "Build the uniformity correction map from this flood and use it for patients."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat with the source at least five field widths away, about 2.7 m.): An intrinsic flood needs a point source at least five times the useful field of view away so photon flux is nearly uniform across the crystal, here about 2.7 m. At 1.2 m, inverse square falloff makes the center brighter than the edge. Using this flood for a correction map would imprint that gradient on patient images. Tube drift gives focal or irregular patterns."}, {"id": 545, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with hypertension has chest pain at rest for 40 minutes that is still present, a nondiagnostic ECG and two normal troponin values drawn 3 hours apart. She has no known coronary disease or contrast allergy. Which perfusion imaging approach best fits?", "options": {"A": "Symptom limited treadmill SPECT immediately, to reproduce the pain under controlled conditions.", "B": "Delay imaging for 24 hours until she is pain free, then perform a rest only study.", "C": "Rest SPECT with Tc-99m injected during pain or soon after, to compare with later images.", "D": "Regadenoson stress SPECT immediately, since pharmacologic stress is safe during unstable symptoms."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rest SPECT with Tc-99m injected during pain or soon after, to compare with later images.): Ongoing rest pain with a nondiagnostic ECG and negative troponin suggests possible acute ischemia, and a rest injection during or soon after pain captures perfusion at the time of symptoms. Stress testing is inappropriate in suspected acute coronary syndrome, and a delayed study can miss a defect that resolves quickly."}, {"id": 546, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A rubidium-82 PET study in a 59-year-old man with no known coronary disease shows a new anterior and lateral defect on both rest and stress images. CT attenuation images were acquired in a different respiratory phase, and on the fused display the lateral wall projects over lung. What best explains the defect?", "options": {"A": "Breast attenuation on the CT map, since overlying soft tissue reduces anterior counts in rubidium studies.", "B": "Emission and CT misregistration puts the lateral wall over lung, so attenuation is undercorrected there.", "C": "A fixed anterolateral infarct, since a matching rest and stress defect on corrected images represents scar.", "D": "Interstitial tracer infiltration at the injection site, which lowers delivered activity in the anterolateral territory."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Emission and CT misregistration puts the lateral wall over lung, so attenuation is undercorrected there.): When the heart on the CT lies partly over lung, the attenuation map assigns lung density to myocardium, so the correction is too small there and the wall looks falsely hypoperfused. The pattern is the same on rest and stress and the defect shifts with realignment. Reviewing registration comes before calling scar."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old woman moved suddenly late in a stress SPECT acquisition, and the sinogram shows an offset in the band at that point. The reader considers the reconstruction uncorrected. Which finding is most expected on the short-axis slices? The study was a one day Tc-99m protocol.", "options": {"A": "Misregistration of early and late projections, giving blurred walls and apparent defects", "B": "A complete ring of activity around the myocardium from detector nonuniformity in the camera", "C": "A hot spot in the inferior wall from subdiaphragmatic bowel activity near the heart", "D": "Uniformly reduced counts in every wall from soft tissue attenuation by the chest"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Misregistration of early and late projections, giving blurred walls and apparent defects): Projections acquired before the step see the heart in one position and later ones see it in another, so reconstruction blends two positions. The result is blurring, distorted walls and focal apparent defects, usually where the heart edge crossed. Diffuse count loss suggests attenuation, a hot spot suggests extracardiac activity, and a ring suggests uniformity problems; none of these fit a sudden offset.", "image": "question_images/q_547.png"}, {"id": 548, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory audits its myocardial perfusion imaging against invasive angiography. It reports sensitivity of 88 percent and specificity of 74 percent for obstructive coronary disease. The medical director asks the fellow to calculate the positive likelihood ratio from these figures. What is the value?", "options": {"A": "About 1.2, calculated as sensitivity divided by specificity.", "B": "About 0.16, calculated as one minus sensitivity divided by specificity.", "C": "About 6.2, calculated as specificity divided by one minus sensitivity.", "D": "About 3.4, calculated as sensitivity divided by one minus specificity."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 3.4, calculated as sensitivity divided by one minus specificity.): Positive likelihood ratio equals sensitivity divided by the false positive rate: 0.88 / (1 - 0.74) = 0.88 / 0.26 = 3.4. The other values come from using the wrong terms: 0.88/0.74 = 1.2, the negative ratio 0.12/0.74 = 0.16, and 0.74/0.12 = 6.2."}, {"id": 549, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old nondiabetic man had a normal stress sestamibi study with summed stress score 0, EF 62 percent and 9 METs achieved. His physician orders repeat stress imaging every year for surveillance. What is the most likely consequence of this practice?", "options": {"A": "Low yield and added radiation, since event rates stay under 1 percent for two years.", "B": "Higher risk of events because the repeat stress study itself raises the rate of ischemia.", "C": "Missed progression, since risk after a normal study rises to 5 percent by year two.", "D": "Reduced accuracy of repeat studies from residual tracer retained after the earlier injection."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low yield and added radiation, since event rates stay under 1 percent for two years.): A normal perfusion study with preserved EF and good exercise capacity carries an annual cardiac event rate under 1 percent, often called the warranty period, lasting about two years in a nondiabetic patient. Annual repeat imaging in the absence of new symptoms adds radiation and cost with very low yield. The other statements are not supported by the data."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 57-year-old man has stress SPECT with Tc-99m sestamibi. Before reconstruction, the technologist reviews the sinogram. Which description of the sinogram would support accepting the acquisition without motion correction? He has no known arrhythmia and, per the technologist, held still throughout a 15 minute scan.", "options": {"A": "A band that is smooth except for one sharp offset near the middle", "B": "A band that climbs steadily from the first projection to the last projection", "C": "A band whose center wanders sideways across successive projection angles of the scan", "D": "A smooth continuous sinusoidal band with no breaks or steps across projection angles"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A smooth continuous sinusoidal band with no breaks or steps across projection angles): A stationary source traces a smooth sinusoid across projection angles, so a continuous unbroken band means the heart stayed in place and correction is not needed. A steady climb indicates upward creep, a sideways wander indicates lateral drift, and a single offset indicates a sudden movement. Each of those breaks the expected path and warrants review or correction.", "image": "question_images/q_550.png"}, {"id": 551, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 29-year-old nuclear technologist tells her supervisor in the hallway that she is pregnant and asks about her dose limit. The supervisor proposes to cap her at one twelfth of the annual 5 rem occupational limit each month. What is the correct regulatory response?", "options": {"A": "Invite a written declaration, then limit fetal dose to 0.5 rem (5 mSv) for the gestation", "B": "Treat the verbal statement as binding and cap her dose at 0.5 rem for each month", "C": "Keep the 5 rem annual limit because the NRC sets no separate limit for pregnancy", "D": "Remove her from all radiation work immediately since pregnant workers may not enter a laboratory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Invite a written declaration, then limit fetal dose to 0.5 rem (5 mSv) for the gestation): The NRC embryo and fetus limit is 0.5 rem (5 mSv) for the entire gestation, and it applies once the worker declares the pregnancy in writing. A verbal remark prompts the employer to offer the declaration, not to invent a monthly cap. Pregnant workers are not barred from radiation work, and the standard annual limit does not remain the only limit after declaration."}, {"id": 552, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "While setting up the morning studies, a nuclear cardiologist notices that the lab's survey meter calibration sticker is 14 months old. The battery check passes and the meter reads the long-lived reference source normally. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Keep using it, since a passed battery check and normal source reading satisfy the requirement.", "B": "Wait for the 24 month point, since survey meters need only biennial calibration.", "C": "Use it only for dose calibrator work until a service visit is booked.", "D": "Remove the meter from use until calibrated, since the interval cannot exceed 12 months."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove the meter from use until calibrated, since the interval cannot exceed 12 months.): NRC rules require survey instruments to be calibrated before first use, at intervals not exceeding 12 months, and after servicing. A battery or reference source check confirms function but is not a calibration. There is no exception that lets an out-of-date meter continue in service for partial duties."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with an RCRI score of 1 is scheduled for elective knee arthroplasty. She has no angina, dyspnea or active cardiac condition, and she climbs two flights of stairs without symptoms. Her surgeon requests a stress MPI. Which action best fits appropriate use criteria?", "options": {"A": "Order a coronary calcium score so that revascularization can be arranged beforehand", "B": "Order regadenoson MPI because any RCRI point above zero triggers testing", "C": "Order dobutamine stress imaging because exercise testing cannot predict perioperative events", "D": "Proceed to surgery without stress imaging, since capacity exceeds 4 METs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed to surgery without stress imaging, since capacity exceeds 4 METs): For elective noncardiac surgery, testing is reserved for patients with poor or unknown functional capacity whose result would change management. This woman exceeds 4 METs and has a single risk factor, so imaging is low value. Coronary revascularization before surgery in stable disease has not improved outcomes, and an exercise-capable patient does not need a pharmacologic test."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70 kg patient receives adenosine 140 mcg/kg/min. At minute 3 she develops wheezing and high-grade AV block with systolic pressure 104 mm Hg. The infusion is stopped, but symptoms persist 2 minutes later. What total adenosine was delivered, and what is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "About 9.8 mg delivered; give aminophylline 125 mg slowly, since the dose was low.", "B": "About 29 mg delivered; give atropine 1 mg intravenously because the block is vagally mediated.", "C": "About 29 mg delivered; give aminophylline 125 mg slowly, since symptoms persist after stopping.", "D": "About 29 mg delivered; observe another 10 minutes because adenosine clears within seconds."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 29 mg delivered; give aminophylline 125 mg slowly, since symptoms persist after stopping.): The dose is 140 mcg x 70 kg = 9,800 mcg per minute, and 3 minutes gives 29.4 mg. Adenosine has a half-life under 10 seconds, so symptoms still present 2 minutes after stopping justify methylxanthine reversal. Aminophylline competitively blocks the adenosine receptor, whereas atropine does not reverse bronchospasm or nodal block caused by this agent. Waiting only delays treatment of a patient with persistent bronchospasm."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "SPECT/CT in a woman with large breasts shows a fixed anterior defect on uncorrected images that normalizes on attenuation-corrected images. CT and emission registration was verified, gated wall motion and thickening are normal, and the stress ECG was nondiagnostic. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Normal perfusion with breast attenuation artifact, read from corrected, uncorrected and gated data together", "B": "Report a fixed anterior defect as scar since corrected images add noise and are unvalidated here", "C": "Repeat the study prone before reporting because attenuation corrected images cannot be trusted in women", "D": "Report anterior ischemia because attenuation correction cannot remove a true perfusion defect from the wall"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Normal perfusion with breast attenuation artifact, read from corrected, uncorrected and gated data together): When a defect normalizes after correction with verified registration and wall motion and thickening are preserved, breast attenuation is the likely cause. Reading corrected, uncorrected and gated images together gives a robust conclusion. Calling it scar contradicts normal motion, calling it ischemia ignores the correction, and a repeat prone study adds little."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 28 percent has a severe anterior perfusion defect on rest rubidium-82 PET. FDG PET with proper glucose loading shows preserved uptake in the same segments. How should this perfusion-metabolism pattern be interpreted for his revascularization decision?", "options": {"A": "Stunned myocardium with normal resting flow, which requires no revascularization.", "B": "An FDG artifact from inadequate fasting, which makes the viability pattern unreliable.", "C": "A matched defect, indicating nonviable scar that will not benefit from revascularization.", "D": "A perfusion-metabolism mismatch, indicating hibernating, viable myocardium that may improve after revascularization."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A perfusion-metabolism mismatch, indicating hibernating, viable myocardium that may improve after revascularization.): Reduced flow with preserved glucose uptake means the tissue is alive but down-regulated, the definition of hibernating myocardium, and function may recover after revascularization. A matched reduction in both indicates scar. Stunning has preserved resting flow, and the study was prepared with glucose loading."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old man with heart block and suspected cardiac sarcoidosis has an F-18 FDG PET study after a pasta dinner and a 6-hour fast. Images show diffuse intense myocardial FDG uptake. What is the main consequence and best next step?", "options": {"A": "Acceptable if images are repeated after oral glucose loading to improve myocardial signal", "B": "Diagnostic for active inflammation, since diffuse uptake is the typical pattern of active sarcoidosis", "C": "Uptake was not suppressed, so it is nondiagnostic; repeat after a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet", "D": "Diagnostic for hibernating myocardium, since glucose uptake implies viable but underperfused muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Uptake was not suppressed, so it is nondiagnostic; repeat after a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet): Normal myocardium takes up FDG when glucose and insulin are available. To detect inflammation, physiologic uptake must be suppressed with a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet for 12 to 24 hours and a fast of at least 4 to 12 hours, sometimes with heparin. After a carbohydrate meal, diffuse uptake cannot be separated from disease, so the scan is nondiagnostic. Glucose loading does the opposite of what is needed."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A nuclear cardiology laboratory performs a daily flood before Tc-99m cardiac imaging and wants the test to evaluate the crystal, tubes and the collimator actually used on patients, with a Co-57 sheet source. Which quality control test fits this purpose?", "options": {"A": "A bar phantom acquisition that measures intrinsic resolution, since resolution loss reflects uniformity loss.", "B": "An extrinsic flood acquired with the patient collimator in place, using a uniform sheet source.", "C": "A center-of-rotation acquisition with a point source, which reveals nonuniformity as ring artifacts on reconstruction.", "D": "An intrinsic flood acquired with the collimator removed and a distant Tc-99m point source."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An extrinsic flood acquired with the patient collimator in place, using a uniform sheet source.): An extrinsic flood is acquired with the collimator mounted, so it assesses the detector and the collimator together and can show damage to septa. An intrinsic flood uses a distant point source with the collimator removed and tests only the crystal and electronics. The other options are separate tests with different purposes."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Rb-82 dynamic PET in a 61-year-old man with chest pain shows normal relative perfusion on both stress and rest images. Global rest myocardial blood flow is 1.10 mL/min/g and regadenoson stress flow is 1.98 mL/min/g. What is the coronary flow reserve, and what is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "1.8, reduced, suggesting balanced multivessel or microvascular disease despite normal relative perfusion", "B": "3.1, well above 2.0, so coronary flow reserve is normal and no further testing is needed", "C": "0.56, flow falling during vasodilation, indicating steal physiology in the left main coronary territory", "D": "0.88, an increment below 1 mL/min/g, showing that coronary reserve is preserved at this level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1.8, reduced, suggesting balanced multivessel or microvascular disease despite normal relative perfusion): Coronary flow reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow, so 1.98 divided by 1.10 equals 1.8. A value under about 2.0 is reduced, and with normal relative perfusion it points to globally limited flow from balanced multivessel or microvascular disease that relative images cannot show. The other values come from an inverted ratio, subtraction or addition."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man had vasodilator Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Findings are a summed stress score of 16, summed rest score of 4, summed difference score of 12, resting EF 34 percent, and TID 1.45. Which risk statement is best supported by these data?", "options": {"A": "Intermediate risk, because only the EF is abnormal while the perfusion extent is moderate.", "B": "High risk: low EF, large reversible defects and high TID give over 3 percent yearly mortality.", "C": "Risk cannot be stratified until a coronary calcium score is added to the nuclear findings.", "D": "Low risk, because the resting score of 4 shows mostly preserved uptake at rest."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (High risk: low EF, large reversible defects and high TID give over 3 percent yearly mortality.): A summed stress score of 16 is severely abnormal, the difference score of 12 reflects large inducible ischemia, resting EF is markedly reduced and TID is elevated. Together these place the annual cardiac death rate above 3 percent. The rest score alone is the scar component and does not offset ischemia, and calcium scoring is not required to assign risk."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A shipper packs Tl-201 vials in a box labeled Radioactive White-I. On receipt, the survey shows 8 mrem/h (0.08 mSv/h) at the surface and 0.4 mrem/h at one meter. Which statement describes the consequence of this labeling error and the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Handlers are over-warned, since White-I is the highest category for any package above 5 mrem/h.", "B": "Handlers are under-warned since it needs a Yellow-II label; relabel it and report the discrepancy.", "C": "The package must be refused and returned, since only White-I packages may enter a nuclear laboratory.", "D": "No error exists, since the category follows the one meter reading of 0.4 mrem/h."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Handlers are under-warned since it needs a Yellow-II label; relabel it and report the discrepancy.): Radioactive White-I packages may not exceed 0.5 mrem/h at the surface. At 8 mrem/h at the surface and a transport index of 0.4 at one meter, the package belongs in Yellow-II, which allows up to 50 mrem/h at the surface and a transport index up to 1. The mislabel under-informs carriers and handlers, so the receiver documents it, corrects the label and notifies the shipper and radiation safety officer."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 48-year-old man with new high-grade AV block has cardiac FDG PET after a high-fat, low-carbohydrate preparation. Resting perfusion shows a septal defect, and FDG shows focal intense uptake in the same septal segments. Which interpretation fits this pattern best?", "options": {"A": "Transmural scar, because a perfusion defect matched by no metabolic activity indicates nonviable tissue.", "B": "Hibernating myocardium with preserved metabolic viability in a chronically underperfused septum.", "C": "Active inflammation, a mismatch pattern consistent with cardiac sarcoidosis.", "D": "Physiologic glucose uptake from inadequate dietary preparation in normal septal myocardium."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Active inflammation, a mismatch pattern consistent with cardiac sarcoidosis.): A perfusion defect with focal FDG uptake in the same segments after proper suppression of normal glucose use indicates active inflammation, which fits sarcoidosis in a young patient with AV block. The same mismatch pattern means viability in an ischemia setting, but the preparation and clinical picture point away from that. Inadequate preparation tends to give diffuse uptake, and scar would show no FDG."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A gated SPECT shows an end-diastolic volume of 120 mL and an end-systolic volume of 72 mL, with an akinetic apex and anterior wall. What are the LVEF and the most likely vessel territory for the abnormal wall? The patient is a 67-year-old with exertional dyspnea.", "options": {"A": "LVEF of 48 percent, with the akinetic wall in left anterior descending territory.", "B": "LVEF of 60 percent, with the akinetic wall in left anterior descending territory.", "C": "LVEF of 40 percent, with the akinetic wall in left anterior descending territory.", "D": "LVEF of 40 percent, with the akinetic wall in right coronary territory."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (LVEF of 40 percent, with the akinetic wall in left anterior descending territory.): Ejection fraction is stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, so (120 minus 72) divided by 120 is 48 divided by 120, or 40 percent. The anterior wall and apex belong to the left anterior descending artery. Dividing the end-systolic by the end-diastolic volume gives 60 percent, and quoting the 48 mL stroke volume as the fraction is a unit confusion."}, {"id": 564, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man exercises 7 minutes on a treadmill and stops with limiting angina and 2 mm of horizontal ST depression. A resident scores him by omitting the angina term. Using the Duke treadmill score, which statement describes the correct score and its consequence?", "options": {"A": "Score is minus 3, intermediate risk, so adding stress imaging is enough before deciding on management.", "B": "Score is plus 3, intermediate risk, so adding imaging after a medical therapy trial is reasonable.", "C": "Score is minus 7, intermediate risk, so medical therapy and observation are sufficient for this patient.", "D": "Score is minus 11, high risk with about 5 percent annual mortality, so angiography is appropriate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Score is minus 11, high risk with about 5 percent annual mortality, so angiography is appropriate.): Duke score = minutes - 5 x ST deviation (mm) - 4 x angina index. With limiting angina the index is 2: 7 - 10 - 8 = minus 11. A score of minus 11 or lower is high risk, with annual mortality about 5 percent. Omitting angina gives minus 3, dropping multipliers gives 3, and an index of 1 gives minus 7."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man took his metoprolol the morning of treadmill testing. He exercised 6 minutes and reached a peak heart rate of 98 beats per minute, with no angina and no ST changes. SPECT perfusion is normal. What is the most likely consequence of reporting this as an adequate negative study?", "options": {"A": "False reassurance, because a submaximal heart rate lowers sensitivity and the study should be called limited.", "B": "Correct reassurance, because normal perfusion overrides the heart rate response in every patient.", "C": "No effect, because a heart rate above 85 percent of predicted is unnecessary with Tc-99m tracers.", "D": "Overdiagnosis of ischemia, because beta blockers produce spurious defects on perfusion imaging."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (False reassurance, because a submaximal heart rate lowers sensitivity and the study should be called limited.): His age-predicted maximum is about 154, so 98 is only about 64 percent, short of the 85 percent target. Submaximal stress may fail to provoke a flow disparity, reducing sensitivity, and the report should state the limitation and consider a pharmacologic study. Beta blockade does not create defects, and the heart rate target applies regardless of the tracer used."}, {"id": 566, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with hypertension completes 7 minutes of Bruce exercise at 148 beats per minute with chest tightness. Ejection fraction is 58 percent. The processing screen lists a summed stress score of 12 and a summed rest score of 7, confined to the inferior and inferoseptal walls. Which wording belongs in the final impression?", "options": {"A": "Extensive reversible anterior ischemia in the left anterior descending territory with preserved ejection fraction values", "B": "Severe fixed inferior scar without viable myocardium, so no stress-induced ischemia is present in that wall", "C": "Normal perfusion with an inferior attenuation artifact, so no ischemia is reported on this stress study", "D": "Moderate partially reversible inferior and inferoseptal defect in right coronary territory, preserved ejection fraction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Moderate partially reversible inferior and inferoseptal defect in right coronary territory, preserved ejection fraction): The impression should name the walls, the severity, the reversibility and the vessel territory. A stress score of 12 falling to 7 at rest is a moderate defect that improves without normalizing, and inferior and inferoseptal segments are right coronary territory. A fixed defect would not change, an anterior description names the wrong walls, and attenuation would not show a stress-to-rest change.", "image": "question_images/q_566.png"}, {"id": 567, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with diabetes and an eGFR of 18 mL/min, listed in the exhibit, is being evaluated for kidney transplant and cannot exercise. The transplant team wants to cancel imaging because of his kidney function. What is the best response from the nuclear cardiologist?", "options": {"A": "Cancel all cardiac testing, since the transplant itself will treat his cardiovascular risk", "B": "Halve the regadenoson dose, since the drug is cleared mainly by the kidneys", "C": "Switch to coronary CT angiography with iodinated contrast, which is safe at this kidney function", "D": "Proceed with regadenoson imaging, since it needs no renal dose adjustment or nephrotoxic contrast"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed with regadenoson imaging, since it needs no renal dose adjustment or nephrotoxic contrast): Radionuclide perfusion agents are given in trace amounts and are not nephrotoxic, and regadenoson, a vasodilator with a fixed 0.4 mg dose, needs no renal adjustment. Contrast CT could harm the remaining kidney function, and omitting cardiac evaluation before transplant risks missing high-risk coronary disease that a surgeon needs to know about.", "image": "question_images/q_567.png"}, {"id": 568, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A medical event is discovered at 4:30 pm on Friday, October 2, by the radiation safety officer. Under 10 CFR 35.3045, what are the latest allowed time for telephone notice to the NRC Operations Center and the date for the written report?", "options": {"A": "Telephone by end of Saturday, October 3, and written report by October 17", "B": "Telephone by Monday, October 5, the next business day, and written report by October 16", "C": "Telephone by end of Sunday, October 4, and written report by October 17", "D": "Telephone by 4:30 pm Saturday, October 3, and written report by November 1"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Telephone by end of Saturday, October 3, and written report by October 17): The telephone notice is due no later than the next calendar day after discovery, which includes weekends, so Saturday, October 3. The written report is due within 15 days of discovery, which is October 17. Business days are not used, the 30 day period belongs to other reports, and the telephone notice allows no second day."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man has exertional angina that is controlled on metoprolol and amlodipine. Rest-stress SPECT shows reversible ischemia limited to the inferior wall, about 5 percent of the left ventricle, with an LVEF of 60 percent. He asks whether stenting would prolong his life. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Refer for stenting of the right coronary artery, because any reversible defect predicts death without revascularization.", "B": "Perform coronary bypass surgery, because inferior ischemia signals disease of all three coronary vessels.", "C": "Repeat stress imaging with PET to see whether the ischemic burden grows before choosing treatment.", "D": "Continue medical therapy, because small ischemic burden with controlled symptoms gives no survival gain from revascularization."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue medical therapy, because small ischemic burden with controlled symptoms gives no survival gain from revascularization.): Survival benefit from revascularization is tied to a large ischemic burden, roughly above 10 percent of the myocardium, together with reduced EF or left main disease. This patient has about 5 percent ischemia, normal EF, and symptoms controlled on drugs, so symptom relief rather than survival is the only possible gain and medical therapy is appropriate. Stenting for survival and bypass for presumed multivessel disease both lack support, and repeating imaging adds nothing."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 40-year-old woman asks whether the 9 mSv from her rest-stress SPECT could cause skin burns or hair loss, because she read about radiation injuries after therapy procedures. The physician explains which type of radiation effect is relevant. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Effective dose in mSv equals absorbed skin dose in Gy, so 9 mSv means 9 mGy.", "B": "Skin injury and hair loss are deterministic effects needing about 2 Gy; stochastic risk matters.", "C": "Cancer risk is a threshold effect above roughly 100 mSv, so nothing more needs consideration.", "D": "Skin injury occurs at any dose, so 9 mSv carries a small chance of hair loss."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Skin injury and hair loss are deterministic effects needing about 2 Gy; stochastic risk matters.): Deterministic effects such as erythema and epilation appear only above thresholds of about 2 Gy absorbed dose, far above diagnostic nuclear exposures. Stochastic effects such as cancer are modeled as having probability proportional to dose without a threshold, and that is what justification and dose optimization address. Effective dose is a risk-weighted quantity, not the absorbed dose to skin."}, {"id": 571, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory acquires gated SPECT with a 180 degree orbit, from 45 degrees right anterior oblique to 45 degrees left posterior oblique, instead of a full 360 degree orbit. The change saves time and keeps the detectors close to the chest. Compared with a full orbit, what is the main consequence?", "options": {"A": "Lower spatial resolution, because the detectors stay farther from the heart during the shortened arc", "B": "Higher contrast and resolution from detector proximity, but more distortion from incomplete sampling", "C": "Acquisition time doubles, because each projection needs twice the counts to make up for missing angles", "D": "No change in image quality, because the heart lies in the span and all views exist"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Higher contrast and resolution from detector proximity, but more distortion from incomplete sampling): The 180 degree orbit keeps the camera close to the left ventricle, improving contrast and resolution, and it shortens acquisition. But sampling is incomplete and attenuation and depth effects become nonuniform, so artifacts and distortion rise. Resolution does not fall, time does not double and image quality is not unchanged."}, {"id": 572, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 47-year-old woman with BMI 46 kg/m2 and atypical chest pain cannot exercise. An outside SPECT showed a fixed anterior defect that she was told might be breast artifact. The laboratory has gated Tc-99m SPECT without attenuation correction and Rb-82 PET. Which choice is best?", "options": {"A": "Prone-only Tc-99m imaging, since position change fixes anterior attenuation and removes further testing needs.", "B": "Regadenoson Tl-201 SPECT, since thallium photons have higher energy and penetrate breast tissue better.", "C": "Repeat gated Tc-99m SPECT, since normal wall thickening alone separates attenuation from scar adequately.", "D": "Regadenoson Rb-82 PET with CT correction, which separates attenuation from true defects better here."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regadenoson Rb-82 PET with CT correction, which separates attenuation from true defects better here.): Obesity and breast tissue produce fixed anterior defects on uncorrected SPECT, and the outside result cannot tell attenuation from scar. Rb-82 PET with CT correction has higher count density and better specificity in obese patients. Gating helps but cannot settle every case, thallium photons are lower in energy and attenuate more, and prone imaging is supplementary and does not replace corrected PET."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "After a Tc-99m vial spill the technologist uses the survey meter described in the exhibit to confirm the cleanup, and plans to release the room for patient use that same afternoon for the next scheduled patient. What is the problem with this plan?", "options": {"A": "None, because a check source response proves the meter is fit to release the room", "B": "None, because calibration within 24 months is acceptable for survey meters at a nuclear laboratory", "C": "The meter is overdue for annual calibration; re-survey with a calibrated meter and wipe the area", "D": "The room should be locked for 10 half-lives and then released without further surveys"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The meter is overdue for annual calibration; re-survey with a calibrated meter and wipe the area): Survey instruments must be calibrated before first use, at intervals not exceeding 12 months, and after repair. A reading from a meter 20 months past calibration cannot document that the room is clean. A check source response confirms only that the meter is working, not that it reads accurately. The area should be wiped for removable contamination, and decay time does not replace a release survey.", "image": "question_images/q_573.png"}, {"id": 574, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient is scheduled for regadenoson SPECT at 8:00 a.m. The patient drank a large cup of coffee at 6:00 p.m. the evening before, about 14 hours earlier, and takes no theophylline. No other caffeinated products were taken. Vital signs and baseline ECG are acceptable. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with regadenoson, since 14 hours exceeds the commonly recommended 12 hour abstinence", "B": "Convert to dobutamine because residual caffeine will prevent adequate coronary vasodilation today", "C": "Draw a serum caffeine level and delay stress until the level is undetectable", "D": "Postpone the study 24 hours because xanthine intake in the prior day will block adenosine receptors"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Proceed with regadenoson, since 14 hours exceeds the commonly recommended 12 hour abstinence): Caffeine antagonizes adenosine A2A receptors, and most laboratories require at least 12 hours of abstinence before vasodilator stress. Fourteen hours satisfies that interval, so the planned study may go ahead. Delaying a full day, measuring serum caffeine or changing to dobutamine all add burden or risk without a clinical reason in this patient."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old man with atypical chest pain exercised on a treadmill, stopping at minute 8 for leg fatigue with no angina or ST change. Perfusion imaging is normal. The chart shows his heart rate during exercise. How should the normal scan be interpreted in light of the chart?", "options": {"A": "The scan overestimates risk, because a low heart rate lowers demand and creates false defects", "B": "Perfusion findings are valid only when more than 10 METs were reached during the treadmill protocol", "C": "Sensitivity is reduced, because peak rate fell well below 85 percent of age-predicted maximum", "D": "The study is fully reliable, because absent symptoms and ST changes confirm stress was adequate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sensitivity is reduced, because peak rate fell well below 85 percent of age-predicted maximum): Age-predicted maximum is 220 minus 66, or 154 beats per minute, so 85 percent is about 131. The chart shows a peak near 112, well below target, and the patient stopped for a noncardiac reason. A submaximal exercise study has lower sensitivity for ischemia, so normal images do not exclude disease and conversion to vasodilator stress would have been appropriate.", "image": "question_images/q_575.png"}, {"id": 576, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old woman stops a treadmill test at 5 minutes with leg fatigue at 78% of her maximum predicted heart rate. The technologist, delayed by an IV problem, injects Tc-99m sestamibi 3 minutes into recovery. Stress ECG showed 1.5 mm of horizontal ST depression at peak. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Stress images will show falsely severe defects, since late tracer concentrates in the ischemic territory", "B": "The study becomes a rest study with identical counts, so the rest injection can be cancelled", "C": "Stress images reflect recovery flow, so defect size can be underestimated and ischemia may look normal", "D": "Image quality improves because liver and gut clearance have begun before the tracer reaches the heart"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stress images reflect recovery flow, so defect size can be underestimated and ischemia may look normal): Sestamibi uptake reflects myocardial blood flow at the moment of injection and then stays fixed. Flow heterogeneity is greatest at peak stress and falls quickly in recovery, so late injection narrows the difference between normal and ischemic regions and can make ischemia look smaller or absent. The ST depression suggests ischemia was present, so a normal-looking study would be falsely reassuring. A late dose does not replace a rest dose, and liver clearance is unrelated."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The exhibit shows a protocol planned for a 34-year-old woman with a moderate pretest probability of CAD, normal BMI and a rest ECG within normal limits. The laboratory has SPECT only. Which change best fits the radiation goals of the study?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the Tl-201 stress dose to 1 mCi while keeping the 4 hour redistribution", "B": "Switch to a Tc-99m stress-first protocol, omitting rest imaging if stress is normal", "C": "Keep the Tl-201 stress and add a Tc-99m rest study for dual-isotope comparison", "D": "Keep the Tl-201 protocol but add rest reinjection to improve viability detection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Switch to a Tc-99m stress-first protocol, omitting rest imaging if stress is normal): Tl-201 gives a higher effective dose than Tc-99m agents, which matters in a 34-year-old woman with a normal body habitus. A stress-first Tc-99m protocol can end after a normal stress study, lowering the dose. Reinjection and dual-isotope add radiation, and cutting Tl-201 to 1 mCi would leave too few counts.", "image": "question_images/q_577.png"}, {"id": 578, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman with a large bra cup size has stress and rest SPECT with a mild, matched anterior wall count reduction, summed stress and rest scores of 0, and an ejection fraction of 60 percent. The interpreting physician is deciding between artifact and scar. Which additional step best separates the two?", "options": {"A": "Double the injected sestamibi dose to improve anterior wall counts.", "B": "Switch to dobutamine stress, because the defect will change with a different stress agent.", "C": "Repeat acquisition after four hours with thallium redistribution, since redistribution resolves attenuation artifacts.", "D": "Review gated wall thickening in the affected wall, since normal thickening favors attenuation artifact over scar."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review gated wall thickening in the affected wall, since normal thickening favors attenuation artifact over scar.): Attenuation artifact lowers counts but leaves the muscle contracting and thickening normally, whereas scar thins and fails to thicken. Reviewing gated thickening in the affected wall is therefore the most useful additional step. Attenuation is a fixed physical effect, so a higher dose, a longer redistribution period or a different stress agent will not remove it.", "image": "question_images/q_578.png"}, {"id": 579, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman has progressive exertional dyspnea. Regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT has an ejection fraction of 58 percent. The summed stress score is 15, the rest score is 0, and the inferior wall scores 0. Defects involve anterior, septal, apical and lateral walls. Which vessel pattern should the report raise, and how urgent is the follow-up?", "options": {"A": "Single vessel left anterior descending disease, because septal and apical defects are largest, so elective angiography.", "B": "Breast attenuation artifact across the anterior wall, so repeat with attenuation correction before naming vessels.", "C": "Isolated proximal right coronary disease, because the inferior wall is spared, so outpatient medical therapy only.", "D": "Extensive anterior and lateral reversible defects raise concern for left main disease, so prompt angiography."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extensive anterior and lateral reversible defects raise concern for left main disease, so prompt angiography.): Reversible defects across the anterior, septal, apical and lateral walls, with a difference score of 15, cover both the left anterior descending and circumflex territories while the inferior wall is spared. That pattern raises concern for left main or proximal multivessel disease and calls for prompt angiography. A normal inferior wall does not point to the right coronary artery. Attenuation artifact would be matched at rest.", "image": "question_images/q_579.png"}, {"id": 580, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old man on a Bruce protocol stops at 7 minutes because of leg fatigue, reaching 72 percent of his predicted maximum heart rate with no symptoms or ECG change. Radiotracer is injected and imaging proceeds. What is the most likely consequence of this approach?", "options": {"A": "Submaximal stress may not provoke a flow difference, lowering sensitivity and risking a falsely normal study.", "B": "Specificity rises because severe stenoses alone produce a defect, so the normal result is more reliable.", "C": "Only the gated ejection fraction is affected, while perfusion analysis remains fully reliable.", "D": "Hepatic and gut uptake of sestamibi increases, leaving interfering inferior activity on the images."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Submaximal stress may not provoke a flow difference, lowering sensitivity and risking a falsely normal study.): A study at less than 85 percent of predicted maximum heart rate, without ischemic ECG or symptoms, is considered inadequate. Flow differences between normal and narrowed arteries may be small, so sensitivity falls and false negatives are more likely. Many laboratories add a vasodilator in this situation. Exercise reduces rather than raises splanchnic uptake."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exposure rate is 40 mrem/h at 20 cm from a small F-18 syringe, which may be treated as a point source. A technologist stands 1 m from it during a 10 minute wait. What exposure rate should she expect at her position?", "options": {"A": "8 mrem/h, from dividing the 20 cm rate by the distance ratio of 5", "B": "About 1.6 mrem/h, from 40 mrem/h times the squared ratio of 20 cm to 100 cm", "C": "0.32 mrem/h, from dividing the 20 cm rate by the cubed distance ratio", "D": "0.1 mrem/h, from using 20 and 1 without converting centimeters to meters"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 1.6 mrem/h, from 40 mrem/h times the squared ratio of 20 cm to 100 cm): The inverse square law gives rate at 100 cm equal to 40 times (20 over 100) squared, which is 40 times 0.04, or 1.6 mrem/h. Dividing by 5 gives 8 mrem/h and treats the falloff as linear. Cubing gives 0.32 mrem/h. Squaring the ratio of 1 to 20 without converting units gives 0.1 mrem/h."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man with exertional dyspnea has regadenoson SPECT. The stress left ventricular cavity looks about 20 percent larger in linear size than on rest. Anterior and anteroseptal stress counts are reduced (summed stress score 12), while rest images are normal (summed rest score 0). Which interpretation and risk statement fit best?", "options": {"A": "Dilated cardiomyopathy, which would enlarge both cavities equally on stress and rest, a chronic finding", "B": "A normal variant from heart rate changes, a low risk finding needing no follow up testing", "C": "Transient ischemic dilation, a high risk marker suggesting severe coronary disease", "D": "Patient shift between acquisitions causing a falsely larger stress cavity, a low risk finding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transient ischemic dilation, a high risk marker suggesting severe coronary disease): A cavity that appears larger at stress than at rest together with a sizable reversible anterior and anteroseptal defect (summed difference score 12) is transient ischemic dilation. It reflects subendocardial hypoperfusion after stress and carries a worse prognosis. A true dilated ventricle would be large on both rows, and positioning or heart rate do not explain it.", "image": "question_images/q_582.png"}, {"id": 583, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A radiologist tells a patient that the 9 mSv effective dose of her SPECT study is her personal cancer risk and multiplies it by a population risk factor. Which limitation of effective dose does this approach ignore in her case?", "options": {"A": "It uses population averaged weights, so it cannot predict one patient's risk by age and sex", "B": "Effective dose cannot be added across studies, so a lifetime total is meaningless", "C": "Effective dose measures energy per kilogram, so it must first be converted back into gray", "D": "Effective dose describes only deterministic effects, so it has no use for cancer risk"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It uses population averaged weights, so it cannot predict one patient's risk by age and sex): Effective dose weights organ doses with tissue and radiation weighting factors averaged over a reference population, and it is meant for protection planning and comparing procedures. It was not built to give an individual's risk, which varies with age and sex. It can be summed across studies and is a stochastic risk measure."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "After a stress sestamibi study shows an inferior defect, a technologist proposes imaging again at 4 hours to look for reversibility, as is done with thallium. The tracer has a 6 hour half-life and 140 keV photons. What is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with 4 hour images, because sestamibi's higher extraction than thallium produces strong redistribution", "B": "Delayed images will not show reversibility, since sestamibi barely redistributes and a rest injection is needed", "C": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin before delayed imaging, because it converts sestamibi into a redistributing tracer", "D": "Wait 24 hours instead, because blood pool clearance of sestamibi takes a full day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delayed images will not show reversibility, since sestamibi barely redistributes and a rest injection is needed): Sestamibi is retained in mitochondria and washes out very slowly, so a delayed image closely resembles the stress image. Reversibility is judged against a separate rest injection, in the same-day or two-day protocol. Higher extraction does not imply redistribution, and nitroglycerin changes blood flow before injection, not the tracer's kinetics afterward."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with severe obesity, intermediate pretest probability and a prior nondiagnostic SPECT has a PET study planned. The laboratory has just received F-18 flurpiridaz from a regional radiopharmacy and asks which property supports its use for stress imaging. Which statement best fits this tracer?", "options": {"A": "High first-pass extraction and a 110 minute half-life, allowing exercise stress and distribution", "B": "A 6 hour half-life with low extraction, allowing same-day imaging with the lowest radiation", "C": "A 10 minute half-life demanding an on-site cyclotron, with extraction low enough to limit counts", "D": "A 76 second half-life requiring an on-site generator, which rules out exercise protocols and regional shipping"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (High first-pass extraction and a 110 minute half-life, allowing exercise stress and distribution): F-18 flurpiridaz is a mitochondrial complex I binder with a half-life of 109 to 110 minutes and high first-pass extraction with little roll-off, so it can be distributed from regional cyclotrons and used after treadmill exercise. Rb-82 has a 76 second half-life from a generator, N-13 ammonia about 10 minutes, and Tc-99m 6 hours with lower extraction."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The exhibit shows the delivery record for an F-18 flurpiridaz unit dose for a 09:40 rest-stress PET appointment. Before injection the physician must assay and document the activity as required by radiation safety rules. About what activity should the dose calibrator read at 09:40?", "options": {"A": "About 4.5 mCi (166 MBq), after two half-lives of 109.8 minutes have elapsed.", "B": "About 9.0 mCi (333 MBq), after only a single half-life has elapsed.", "C": "About 18 mCi (666 MBq), since the calibration activity applies at the time of injection.", "D": "About 11.8 mCi (437 MBq), using a 6 hour half-life as for technetium."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 4.5 mCi (166 MBq), after two half-lives of 109.8 minutes have elapsed.): F-18 has a physical half-life of 109.8 minutes. From 06:00 to 09:40 is 220 minutes, about two half-lives, so activity falls to roughly 25 percent of 18 mCi, giving 4.5 mCi or 166 MBq. The assayed value must be recorded before administration. Forgetting decay, using a technetium half-life or counting one half-life give the other values.", "image": "question_images/q_586.png"}, {"id": 587, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman in atrial fibrillation with ventricular rates from 55 to 140 had gated SPECT with 8 frames. The software reports an EF of 74% with noisy end-systolic frames. Before the report is released, what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Average three reprocessings using different filters and report the mean as the EF.", "B": "Reprocess with 16 frames, which recovers accuracy by adding more points to the curve.", "C": "Report the 74 percent EF as measured, since the contour algorithm corrects R-R variability.", "D": "State that arrhythmia limits gated data, and do not present the EF as precise."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (State that arrhythmia limits gated data, and do not present the EF as precise.): Gating needs a regular R-R interval, and irregular rhythms smear the cycle and lower counts per frame, so EF values and volumes become unreliable. More frames reduce counts per frame and worsen the noise. Repeating processing of the same data cannot fix an acquisition problem. The report should explain the limitation and avoid false precision."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man has angina despite three antianginal drugs. His ECG shows a normal QRS duration of 90 ms. Regadenoson sestamibi SPECT shows moderately reduced stress counts over the anterior wall, septum and apex that normalize at rest. The summed difference score is 12. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat stress imaging with dobutamine, because the septal findings may reflect conduction artifact alone.", "B": "Continue current medical therapy and repeat imaging in one year, because the defect is reversible.", "C": "Coronary angiography, because substantial anterior descending ischemia persists despite three drugs.", "D": "Cardiac MRI to quantify scar, because normal rest counts may hide an old subendocardial infarction."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Coronary angiography, because substantial anterior descending ischemia persists despite three drugs.): A normal QRS excludes left bundle branch block as the cause of the septal defect, so the anterior, septal and apical findings are a true left anterior descending territory pattern. Difference score 12 equals about 18 percent of the myocardium, and symptoms persist despite three drugs. That supports invasive evaluation. Reversibility does not mean low risk, and the other options address artifact or scar, which this pattern does not suggest.", "image": "question_images/q_588.png"}, {"id": 589, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with an old anterolateral infarction has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. The reader reports that the basal anterior and anterolateral segments show the same reduced counts at stress and rest, at about 20 to 35 percent of peak. The surgeon asks whether revascularization would help those segments. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "The defect shows inducible ischemia, so revascularization will probably relieve her angina.", "B": "Counts at 20 to 35 percent of peak mean nonviable scar; recovery after revascularization is unlikely.", "C": "Reduced rest counts indicate hibernating myocardium, so revascularization will probably restore function in those segments.", "D": "Sestamibi cannot assess viability, so FDG PET is mandatory before any further consideration."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Counts at 20 to 35 percent of peak mean nonviable scar; recovery after revascularization is unlikely.): Rest uptake above roughly 50 to 60 percent of peak suggests viable tissue, while counts of 20 to 35 percent suggest transmural scar with little recoverable myocardium. The defect is fixed, so there is no inducible ischemia. FDG PET can refine viability in borderline cases, but sestamibi at this severity already gives useful information, so declaring it mandatory is not necessary here.", "image": "question_images/q_589.png"}, {"id": 590, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a normal resting ECG exercises on the Bruce protocol for 5 minutes and stops for leg fatigue. Peak heart rate is 128 beats per minute and blood pressure 150/80. He has no symptoms or ST changes. His maximal age-predicted heart rate is 162. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Inject now, because a systolic pressure of 150 confirms that the stress was adequate for diagnosis", "B": "Inject now, because a peak rate above 120 is the usual threshold for diagnostic exercise", "C": "Repeat the exercise after 20 minutes of rest, using the same workload to improve peak rate", "D": "Convert to pharmacologic stress, since 79 percent of predicted maximum misses the 85 percent target"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Convert to pharmacologic stress, since 79 percent of predicted maximum misses the 85 percent target): 85 percent of 162 is about 138 beats per minute, and he reached 128, which is 79 percent of predicted. Submaximal exercise lowers sensitivity, so a pharmacologic stressor (regadenoson or adenosine, with low level exercise if tolerated) is better. Blood pressure alone does not define adequacy, and repeating the exercise is unlikely to succeed."}, {"id": 591, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During Tc-99m SPECT the projection images show an abrupt 3 cm lateral shift of the heart partway through the acquisition. Vertical motion software was applied, yet short-axis slices still show blurred septal and lateral walls. Perfusion otherwise looks normal and the patient remains in the department. What is the best conclusion and action?", "options": {"A": "Software handles lateral jumps poorly, so repeat the stress acquisition with the patient repositioned", "B": "The correction overshot, so rerun it with a stronger smoothing filter to remove the residual blur", "C": "The blur reflects true septal and lateral scar, so report fixed defects with moderate size", "D": "Reacquire only after a second tracer injection because count loss prevents any repeat imaging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Software handles lateral jumps poorly, so repeat the stress acquisition with the patient repositioned): Motion correction algorithms work best for gradual vertical drift and perform poorly for abrupt lateral displacement, so blur persists after correction. Because Tc-99m activity remains in the myocardium, the stress images can simply be repeated without a new injection. A stronger filter only masks the problem, and labeling the blur as scar misreads an artifact."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with BMI 27 and an old inferior Q wave has new exertional dyspnea. After adenosine, the processing screen lists a summed stress score of 12 and a summed rest score of 7, confined to the inferior and inferoseptal walls. Ejection fraction is normal. Which territory and pattern fit best?", "options": {"A": "Left anterior descending territory with partially reversible defect, because the anterior descending artery perfuses the inferoseptum", "B": "Right coronary territory with partially reversible defect, meaning infarct plus residual peri-infarct ischemia", "C": "Right coronary territory with fully reversible ischemia only, since rest counts are higher than stress counts", "D": "Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact that should look identical on stress and rest images"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right coronary territory with partially reversible defect, meaning infarct plus residual peri-infarct ischemia): The inferior wall and inferoseptum are supplied by the right coronary artery in a right dominant heart. Rest counts improve yet remain reduced, so scar and ischemia coexist, consistent with the old inferior Q wave. The anterior descending artery supplies the anterior and anteroseptal walls instead. Artifact would not change between acquisitions.", "image": "question_images/q_592.png"}, {"id": 593, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "During a vasodilator stress test, the patient develops second-degree AV block with symptomatic hypotension while adenosine runs at 140 mcg/kg/min. Staff reach for aminophylline immediately, but the nuclear cardiologist hesitates. Which first action reflects the usual teaching, and what must be documented afterward?", "options": {"A": "Stop the adenosine infusion first, given its half-life under ten seconds, and record the event.", "B": "Give atropine 1 mg IV before stopping the infusion, since vagal tone caused the block.", "C": "Halve the infusion rate and keep imaging, documenting the event only if symptoms persist afterward.", "D": "Give aminophylline 100 mg IV immediately, since stopping the infusion cannot reverse the conduction block."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the adenosine infusion first, given its half-life under ten seconds, and record the event.): Adenosine is cleared in seconds, so stopping the infusion usually resolves transient block and hypotension within a minute or so; aminophylline, a competitive antagonist, is reserved for persistent or severe effects. The adverse event, the actions taken, the vital signs and the patient's response belong in the stress record. Reducing the rate leaves the drug running, and atropine does not address an adenosine receptor effect."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. The three polar maps are labeled stress, rest and reversibility. Apex is at the center, base at the rim, anterior at the top and septum at the left. Reduced stress counts lie toward the bottom and left. Where is the defect located?", "options": {"A": "The anterior wall and apex, which fall in the left anterior descending territory.", "B": "The lateral wall, which falls in the left circumflex territory of supply.", "C": "The inferior wall and inferoseptum, which fall in the right coronary territory.", "D": "The apex alone, which falls in distal left anterior descending territory."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The inferior wall and inferoseptum, which fall in the right coronary territory.): With anterior at the top and septum at the left, the bottom of the map is inferior and the lower left is the inferoseptal region. Those segments are normally right coronary territory. The lateral wall lies on the right side of the map, the anterior wall at the top, and the apex at the center, so those answers do not match the picture.", "image": "question_images/q_594.png"}, {"id": 595, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. The stress slices show a lateral wall defect that fills in at rest. Segments are scored 0 to 4 across 17 segments, with a summed stress score of 13 and a summed rest score of 0. What percentage of left ventricular myocardium is ischemic?", "options": {"A": "About 0 percent, from the summed rest score of 0 divided by the maximum of 68.", "B": "About 19 percent, from a difference of 13 divided by 68, above the 10 percent threshold.", "C": "About 13 percent, taking the summed stress score directly as a percentage of the ventricle.", "D": "About 38 percent, from a difference of 13 divided by a maximum of 34."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 19 percent, from a difference of 13 divided by 68, above the 10 percent threshold.): Ischemia equals the stress score minus the rest score, so 13 minus 0 is 13. The maximum total is 17 segments times 4, or 68, and 13 divided by 68 is about 19 percent. That is above the 10 percent threshold for substantial ischemia. A maximum of 34 halves the denominator, the rest score of 0 measures scar and not ischemia, and the raw score is not a percentage.", "image": "question_images/q_595.png"}, {"id": 596, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man has stress sestamibi SPECT reconstructed by filtered back projection with a ramp filter. Intense subdiaphragmatic bowel activity lies next to the inferior wall, which appears reduced in count. If this is reported without comment, what is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "A spurious inferior defect read as right coronary disease, leading to unnecessary angiography.", "B": "A falsely elevated EF, since hot extracardiac counts increase the calculated stroke volume measurement.", "C": "A falsely reversible lateral defect, since the artifact would move to the opposite wall at rest.", "D": "A falsely normal inferior wall, since bowel counts add to the myocardium and mask real disease."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A spurious inferior defect read as right coronary disease, leading to unnecessary angiography.): Hot activity adjacent to the heart causes negative streaks with a ramp filter and count normalization to the hottest region, so the neighboring inferior wall appears falsely reduced. Reporting it as RCA disease may lead to angiography. The other options describe effects that do not follow from the artifact, or that go in the wrong direction."}, {"id": 597, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist processes a gated blood pool study from a patient receiving serial trastuzumab monitoring, and the left ventricular ejection fraction must be comparable with prior studies. Where should the background region of interest be placed to correct the counts?", "options": {"A": "Over the center of the spleen, since its stable activity represents tissue background for the thorax.", "B": "Over the aortic arch, where blood pool activity is uniform and reproducible between studies.", "C": "Just lateral to the end-systolic LV border, away from the spleen, stomach and aorta.", "D": "Within the LV cavity at end-diastole, which samples the same blood pool being corrected."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Just lateral to the end-systolic LV border, away from the spleen, stomach and aorta.): Background correction removes counts from tissue behind and in front of the ventricle, so the region sits beside the end-systolic LV edge in a spot free of other blood pool structures, usually lateral and slightly inferior. Splenic, aortic or cavity regions contain blood pool counts that distort the EF and break comparability with earlier studies."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "In the chart, hepatobiliary-to-heart count ratios at rest and stress are plotted against the delay after Tc-99m sestamibi injection. A rest study began 25 minutes after injection, and the inferior wall appears reduced beside bright liver. What is the best action for the technologist?", "options": {"A": "Increase the time per projection, since longer acquisition averages away liver activity near the heart.", "B": "Image now and apply a scatter correction, since liver counts can be removed by software.", "C": "Wait until about 60 minutes after injection, with water, so the liver-to-heart ratio falls.", "D": "Image now at one frame per minute, which separates myocardial from hepatic activity by timing."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wait until about 60 minutes after injection, with water, so the liver-to-heart ratio falls.): At rest, sestamibi clears from the liver slowly, so images are typically taken 45 to 60 minutes after injection, longer than after stress. The chart shows that the ratio at 25 minutes remains above 1. Intense liver activity next to the inferior wall with ramp filtering can create falsely low inferior counts, so waiting is the correct remedy.", "image": "question_images/q_598.png"}, {"id": 599, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man has stable exertional angina despite a beta blocker, a long-acting nitrate and amlodipine at full doses. Regadenoson sestamibi SPECT shows reduced stress uptake in the anterior, septal, apical and lateral segments, normal at rest. The summed stress score is 14 and the summed rest score is 0. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Add a fourth antianginal agent and repeat perfusion imaging in six months to judge the response.", "B": "Order FDG PET viability imaging to look for hibernating myocardium in the reduced segments.", "C": "Perform cardiac MRI for late gadolinium enhancement to exclude anterior wall myocardial scar.", "D": "Refer for angiography because extensive ischemia persists despite maximal antianginal therapy."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer for angiography because extensive ischemia persists despite maximal antianginal therapy.): A summed difference score of 14, about 21 percent of the maximum of 68, involves more than one territory and persists despite three antianginal drugs. That combination supports invasive evaluation for revascularization. Viability studies address a different question, since segments with normal rest uptake are already viable. Scar imaging adds little when rest perfusion is normal, and a fourth drug delays definition of anatomy in a patient already failing therapy.", "image": "question_images/q_599.png"}, {"id": 600, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "On a Rb-82 PET/CT, the corrected images show a new anterolateral defect that the uncorrected images do not. The fused display shows the anterolateral wall projecting over the left lung field on CT. The interpreter suspects misregistration. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the rest injection with a larger Rb-82 dose to improve count statistics in the defect.", "B": "Report a fixed anterolateral scar, since the corrected images are the reliable standard for diagnosis.", "C": "Apply a lung density correction factor to the emission data and report the existing images.", "D": "Manually realign the CT attenuation map to the emission data and reprocess before interpreting."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Manually realign the CT attenuation map to the emission data and reprocess before interpreting.): Wall projection onto lung on the CT map assigns low attenuation values, so the corrected images show an artifactual defect. The fix is to realign the map to the emission data and reprocess, then confirm that the defect resolves. Added dose and extra emission corrections do not address the cause."}, {"id": 601, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with diabetes and exertional dyspnea has regadenoson SPECT with normal ejection fraction. The processing screen lists a summed stress score of 7, a summed rest score of 4 and a summed difference score of 3, all within the inferior wall. Stress and rest slices appear in the exhibit. Which interpretation fits best?", "options": {"A": "Mixed inferior defect with scar plus residual ischemia, since rest counts improve without normalizing", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact, since the inferior wall is reduced on both stress and rest images alike", "C": "Fully reversible inferior ischemia, since stress counts are lower than rest counts in the same wall", "D": "Fixed inferior scar only, since any residual defect on the rest images means infarcted myocardium present"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mixed inferior defect with scar plus residual ischemia, since rest counts improve without normalizing): The inferior wall is the right coronary territory, and the summed score falls from 7 to 4 at rest but stays abnormal. That combination is a partially reversible defect: some nonviable or scarred tissue plus tissue that is ischemic at stress. Full reversibility would require rest counts to match the normal walls, and a purely fixed defect would look the same on both rows. Attenuation tends to be unchanged between rows, so it does not explain a defect that changes.", "image": "question_images/q_601.png"}, {"id": 602, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A sealed 200 µCi Cs-137 dose calibrator reference source cannot be found during the annual inventory, even after a two hour search by the RSO. The Appendix C quantity for Cs-137 is 10 µCi. What is the correct regulatory action?", "options": {"A": "Telephone the NRC by the next calendar day, as is required for a medical event", "B": "Make no report unless a member of the public is later found to be exposed", "C": "Keep searching and send the NRC a written report within 30 days", "D": "Telephone the NRC Operations Center immediately, since any lost source above Appendix C needs it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep searching and send the NRC a written report within 30 days): Under 10 CFR 20.2201, loss of material above 1,000 times the Appendix C quantity needs immediate telephone notice, while loss above 10 times needs a written report within 30 days. At 20 times the Appendix C quantity (200 µCi against 10 µCi) the source falls between the two. The search continues, and medical event timing does not apply to a lost sealed source."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A cardiologist defers a clinically indicated repeat stress SPECT in a 66 year old patient because the patient has already received 6 rem (60 mSv) of imaging dose this year, citing the 5 rem occupational limit. What is the consequence of this reasoning?", "options": {"A": "None, since the 5 rem limit covers patients and 6 rem makes the repeat study unlawful", "B": "The limit does not apply to patients, so the deferral may delay a needed diagnosis", "C": "The repeat study is allowed only with a written NRC exception for the patient", "D": "None, since the 0.1 rem public limit makes any further imaging unlawful this year"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The limit does not apply to patients, so the deferral may delay a needed diagnosis): The annual limits in 10 CFR Part 20 apply to occupational and public exposure, not to patients undergoing medically indicated procedures. Medical exposure is governed by justification and optimization. Applying a limit to the patient may delay a diagnosis. Cumulative dose should be weighed with the benefit, but no NRC exception applies."}, {"id": 604, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The exhibit summarizes the preoperative assessment of a 71-year-old man scheduled for elective open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair next month. His surgeon wants noninvasive ischemia testing only if it could change perioperative management, and no cardiac testing has been done before. Which test is best?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson SPECT, since poor capacity and bundle branch block favor vasodilator stress.", "B": "Exercise treadmill SPECT, since exercise adds prognostic data and bundle branch block spares septal perfusion.", "C": "Dobutamine SPECT, since regadenoson is renally cleared and accumulates when eGFR falls below 30.", "D": "Coronary CT angiography first, since contrast at eGFR 22 is acceptable before major surgery."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regadenoson SPECT, since poor capacity and bundle branch block favor vasodilator stress.): He has several clinical risk factors, cannot climb a flight of stairs and faces a high-risk operation, so testing is reasonable if it will change management. Left bundle branch block makes exercise and dobutamine prone to false septal defects, so vasodilator stress is preferred. Regadenoson needs no renal adjustment and radiotracers carry no nephrotoxic burden, whereas iodinated contrast could worsen his kidney function.", "image": "question_images/q_604.png"}, {"id": 605, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with diabetes and hypertension has exertional dyspnea. Regadenoson SPECT images appear normal with uniform counts. Ejection fraction is 61 percent, the transient ischemic dilation ratio is 1.01, and heart rate rose by 12 beats per minute. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Normal perfusion without high risk markers, so balanced multivessel ischemia is unlikely", "B": "Balanced three vessel ischemia, so angiography should follow because counts are uniform in every wall", "C": "Normal study but diabetic patients require repeat stress imaging within six months to be certain", "D": "Nondiagnostic study, since regadenoson requires a large heart rate rise to be valid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Normal perfusion without high risk markers, so balanced multivessel ischemia is unlikely): Relative uniform counts can hide balanced ischemia, but that is usually revealed by transient ischemic dilation, a drop in ejection fraction or ECG changes, none of which is present here. Vasodilator studies depend on the vasodilator response, not on a large heart rate rise. No retest rule applies to diabetes alone.", "image": "question_images/q_605.png"}, {"id": 606, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man with severe emphysema needs a pharmacologic stress study. He takes theophylline daily, and his last dose was this morning. His resting heart rate is 78 and he has no AV block. The laboratory plans regadenoson. Which finding is the key reason to reconsider this agent or delay the test?", "options": {"A": "A resting heart rate of 78, which is too slow to permit adequate pharmacologic stress", "B": "Severe emphysema, which is an absolute contraindication to every vasodilator including regadenoson", "C": "Recent theophylline intake, which blocks adenosine receptors and may cause a falsely negative study", "D": "Absent AV block, since regadenoson requires some conduction delay to produce coronary vasodilation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recent theophylline intake, which blocks adenosine receptors and may cause a falsely negative study): Methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine antagonize adenosine A2A receptors, so regadenoson may fail to produce hyperemia and a false negative can result. The drug should be held or another approach chosen. Emphysema without active wheeze is not an absolute contraindication to regadenoson, and no conduction delay is needed."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A laboratory plans to buy a new camera for a practice that images mostly Tc-99m sestamibi and Tl-201 cardiac SPECT patients, and the vendor supplied two detector options. The table lists the two detectors. Based on the table, which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Either camera, because only the collimator sets resolution and the crystal has no effect.", "B": "Camera 2, because thicker crystals are required to detect Tl-201 and Tc-99m photons.", "C": "Camera 2, because a thicker crystal improves both intrinsic resolution and sensitivity at these energies.", "D": "Camera 1, because a thinner crystal gives better intrinsic resolution at these energies."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Camera 1, because a thinner crystal gives better intrinsic resolution at these energies.): Intrinsic resolution worsens as crystal thickness increases because light spreads more before reaching the tubes. At 70 to 140 keV, a 9.5 mm crystal absorbs most photons, so a thick crystal adds little sensitivity. Thick crystals benefit higher energies such as 511 keV.", "image": "question_images/q_607.png"}, {"id": 608, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old nonsmoker has exercise Tl-201 imaging with a lung-to-heart ratio of 0.58 on early images. He stopped at 5 minutes for dyspnea and has stress perfusion defects in two coronary territories. What best explains the increased lung uptake? Heart rate peaked at 140.", "options": {"A": "Transient left ventricular dysfunction with raised filling pressure from extensive ischemia.", "B": "Delayed imaging that allowed tracer to redistribute into the lung parenchyma over several hours.", "C": "Chronic cigarette smoke effect on lung tracer uptake, which is a benign explanation here.", "D": "Normal lung uptake, because values up to 0.8 are expected on early Tl-201 images."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transient left ventricular dysfunction with raised filling pressure from extensive ischemia.): A lung-to-heart ratio above about 0.5 on early Tl-201 images reflects pulmonary congestion from elevated left ventricular filling pressure, usually due to extensive ischemia and left ventricular dysfunction. In a nonsmoker with multiple-territory defects, this is a high-risk finding. Smokers can show increased lung uptake, but that does not apply here, and redistribution refers to myocardial rather than lung tracer."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A fellow preparing for the board asks which elements a complete nuclear cardiology report should contain according to the ASNC reporting guidelines, because the department template seems to omit several. The attending wants the full list stated. Which set of elements is correct?", "options": {"A": "Stress hemodynamics and ECG findings, since imaging findings are reported in a separate document.", "B": "Indication, camera serial number, tracer lot number, collimator type and the technologist's initials.", "C": "Indication, stress response, tracer doses, perfusion extent and severity, gated function and an impression.", "D": "Perfusion extent, leaving EF to the clinical record and the impression to the referrer."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Indication, stress response, tracer doses, perfusion extent and severity, gated function and an impression.): A complete report includes the indication, the stress type and response including symptoms and ECG changes, the tracer and doses, the perfusion findings with extent and severity, gated function and an overall impression that integrates them. Equipment details are not clinical content, and the interpreting physician, not the technologist, reports the imaging findings."}, {"id": 610, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A syringe of Tc-99m measures 11.4 mCi in the dose calibrator at 07:30. It will be given to a patient at 09:30 for a prescribed dosage of 10 mCi. Dosages must be within 20 percent of the prescription. What should the dosage record state?", "options": {"A": "About 11.4 mCi, the measured value, which is 14 percent above and within tolerance", "B": "About 5.7 mCi, which is 43 percent below the prescription and must be rejected", "C": "About 9.1 mCi at 09:30, 9 percent below the prescription and within tolerance", "D": "About 14.4 mCi, which is 44 percent above the prescription and must be rejected"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 9.1 mCi at 09:30, 9 percent below the prescription and within tolerance): In 2 hours Tc-99m decays by 2 to the minus 2 over 6.01, a factor of 0.79, so 11.4 mCi becomes about 9.1 mCi. That is 9 percent below 10 mCi and inside the 20 percent tolerance. The record must show activity at administration time. Reversing the correction gives 14.4 mCi and using a full half-life gives 5.7 mCi."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The table shows quality data from a first-pass radionuclide angiogram performed to measure right ventricular function in a 54-year-old patient with suspected pulmonary hypertension, injected by an inexperienced technologist this morning. Based on the table, what is the likely consequence for the study?", "options": {"A": "Truncated count rates from detector dead time, which only affects the absolute count totals.", "B": "Right and left ventricular phases overlap, making the ejection fraction unreliable.", "C": "An overestimated ejection fraction, since extra lung transit counts are subtracted incorrectly.", "D": "Thyroid dose increases because free pertechnetate persists after a delayed bolus."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right and left ventricular phases overlap, making the ejection fraction unreliable.): A wide bolus at the superior vena cava means the tracer arrives in the right ventricle over several seconds, so the right and left ventricular phases overlap and the time-activity curves cannot be separated. Ejection fraction is then unreliable. A tight bolus is needed, injected rapidly in a large vein.", "image": "question_images/q_611.png"}, {"id": 612, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man has presyncope and is referred for stress imaging. The tracing from the clinic shows regular P waves with no consistent relation to a slow regular ventricular rhythm, and no pacemaker is present. The scheduler plans adenosine. What is the most likely consequence of proceeding, and the best action?", "options": {"A": "Brisk sinus tachycardia and a false positive study, so lower the infusion rate and proceed", "B": "Profound hypertension from alpha receptor stimulation, so give a beta blocker and proceed", "C": "Prolonged asystole from worsened AV block, so defer vasodilator stress and evaluate for pacing", "D": "Severe bronchospasm from airway receptor stimulation, so premedicate with a bronchodilator and proceed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prolonged asystole from worsened AV block, so defer vasodilator stress and evaluate for pacing): Adenosine slows AV nodal conduction through A1 receptors. Third degree AV block without a pacemaker is a contraindication, because the escape rhythm can fail and asystole may be prolonged. Dipyridamole shares this risk. The patient needs evaluation for pacing before any vasodilator study.", "image": "question_images/q_612.png"}, {"id": 613, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "At 7:30 a.m. a technologist runs the daily dose calibrator constancy check with a Cs-137 reference source. The reading is 6 percent below the decay-corrected expected value. The last quarterly linearity test passed, and a Tc-99m sestamibi syringe is due for assay in 30 minutes. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Log the result and continue, since the reading is within the 10 percent tolerance", "B": "Remove the calibrator from service and send it for repair before assaying the syringe", "C": "Repeat the linearity test by the decay method before any patient dose is assayed today", "D": "Apply a 6 percent correction factor to every assay performed on the instrument for the day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Log the result and continue, since the reading is within the 10 percent tolerance): Daily constancy with a long-lived reference source must agree with the decay-corrected expected value within 10 percent, and 6 percent meets that, so the result is logged and use continues. Repeating linearity addresses a different question, linearity over a range of activities, and is performed quarterly. A correction factor is only justified when results fall outside tolerance and cannot be corrected by repair. Removing the unit from service would be right only for a deviation above 10 percent."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old man who took metoprolol 100 mg this morning has dobutamine stress SPECT. At 40 mcg/kg/min plus 1 mg atropine his heart rate is 112 bpm, with no symptoms or ECG changes. The tracer is injected, and images appear normal. What is the main concern with this result?", "options": {"A": "The atropine dose exceeded the allowed maximum, so images will show diffuse reduced tracer uptake", "B": "The target heart rate was not reached, so the normal study may be falsely negative", "C": "Metoprolol raises myocardial uptake of sestamibi, so the study will show falsely severe defects", "D": "Dobutamine at 40 mcg/kg/min lowers coronary flow, so the images will show false reversible defects"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The target heart rate was not reached, so the normal study may be falsely negative): For a 61-year-old, the maximum predicted heart rate is 159 and 85% is about 135 bpm. A rate of 112 on maximal dobutamine and 1 mg atropine means the beta-blocker blunted the response and stress was inadequate, so a normal study may miss ischemia. Atropine is allowed up to about 2 mg total, and dobutamine increases myocardial demand and flow rather than lowering it. Beta-blockers do not change tracer uptake directly."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient with ischemic cardiomyopathy and ejection fraction of 25 percent has an FDG and perfusion PET showing a large anterior mismatch in seven segments. The reader calls the anterior wall scar based on the perfusion defect alone. What is the most likely consequence of this error?", "options": {"A": "Defibrillator implantation is prohibited because viable anterior wall myocardium is present.", "B": "Revascularization is withheld, forgoing the function and survival gains of treating hibernating myocardium.", "C": "Medical therapy fails because scarred segments cannot respond to beta blockers or other heart failure drugs.", "D": "Unneeded revascularization of scar proceeds, exposing the patient to procedural risk without functional benefit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Revascularization is withheld, forgoing the function and survival gains of treating hibernating myocardium.): A mismatch pattern, with preserved FDG uptake in a perfusion defect, indicates hibernating, viable myocardium. Calling it scar from perfusion alone leads clinicians to deny revascularization, and a large amount of viable myocardium is associated with improved function and survival after revascularization. The reverse error would subject patients with scar to unnecessary procedures. Viability does not contraindicate a defibrillator."}, {"id": 616, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A draft report reads, small to moderate reversible posterior defect in the LAD territory, with no segment numbers, no summed scores and no gated results. The attending asks the fellow to revise it to follow standardized ASNC nomenclature. Which revision is best?", "options": {"A": "Keep the posterior and territory wording, which is standard nomenclature, and add an EF.", "B": "Replace the description with one global statement of abnormal, naming no segments.", "C": "Name the defect by the 20 segment model and state the territory as definite.", "D": "Use 17 segment names, give extent and severity as summed scores, and call territory probable."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use 17 segment names, give extent and severity as summed scores, and call territory probable.): The ASNC standard uses the 17 segment model with names such as basal inferolateral, and expresses extent and severity with summed scores. Territory assignment is probable, since coronary anatomy varies. A posterior label is outdated, a global statement discards useful regional information, and the 20 segment model is not the accepted standard."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a remote anterior infarct now has exertional chest pressure. He reaches 9 METs with ST depression, and ejection fraction is normal. The processing screen lists a summed stress score of 8 and rest score of 4, confined to anterior, anteroseptal and apical segments. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Infarct with peri-infarct ischemia in the anterior descending territory, so consider angiography and medical therapy", "B": "Pure reversible ischemia in that territory with no scar, so intensify medication and skip further evaluation", "C": "Apical thinning as a normal variant, so reassure the patient and repeat testing in two years", "D": "Left circumflex territory ischemia, so angiography should target the lateral wall branches first and foremost"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Infarct with peri-infarct ischemia in the anterior descending territory, so consider angiography and medical therapy): Anterior, anteroseptal and apical segments belong to the left anterior descending artery. The stress score of 8 falls to 4 at rest, so the defect improves but does not normalize, which indicates scar plus jeopardized myocardium. With symptoms and ST depression, a limited but ischemic defect still justifies revascularization assessment. A normal variant would not involve several walls, and the lateral wall is spared.", "image": "question_images/q_617.png"}, {"id": 618, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with exertional chest pain is scheduled for MPI. Her period is 10 days late, and she says she cannot be pregnant because she has an intrauterine device. What is the appropriate action before any radiopharmaceutical is given?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with Rb-82 PET since its 76 second half-life eliminates fetal exposure", "B": "Proceed with stress-only imaging to halve her dose and then inform her afterward", "C": "Proceed with a Tc-99m study since the fetal dose is below any regulatory concern", "D": "Obtain an hCG test first, since contraception history does not exclude pregnancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Obtain an hCG test first, since contraception history does not exclude pregnancy): A late period is a reason to confirm pregnancy objectively with an hCG test before any radiation exposure, because intrauterine devices lower but do not eliminate the chance of pregnancy. If positive, a nonradiation test would usually be preferred. A low tracer dose, a short half-life or a stress-only protocol each reduce exposure but do not replace screening."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "At 07:00 a Mo-99/Tc-99m generator eluate has 0.10 microcurie of Mo-99 per millicurie of Tc-99m. A kit prepared from this eluate is scheduled for a patient at 13:00. The limit is 0.15 microcurie per millicurie at administration. What is the correct decision for this kit?", "options": {"A": "Do not administer it, since by 13:00 the ratio rises to about 0.19", "B": "Administer it after diluting the eluate with saline to bring the ratio below 0.15", "C": "Administer it, since Mo-99 decays more slowly so the ratio falls as time passes", "D": "Administer it, since the 0.10 ratio measured at elution is below the 0.15 limit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not administer it, since by 13:00 the ratio rises to about 0.19): The Mo-99 limit applies at the time of administration. In 6 hours Tc-99m falls to about half while Mo-99, with a 66 hour half-life, loses only about 6 percent, so the ratio becomes 0.10 times 0.94 divided by 0.5, which is about 0.19. The ratio therefore rises with time. Adding saline dilutes both nuclides equally and leaves the ratio unchanged."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A patient just injected with Tc-99m emits 6 mrem/h at 1 m. A transport aide stands 3 m away for 30 minutes, treating the patient as a point source. A colleague says the dose rate drops to one third at 3 m. What is the correct dose to the aide?", "options": {"A": "About 0.33 mrem, because the rate falls to 0.67 mrem/h at 3 m", "B": "About 3 mrem, because the rate stays at 6 mrem/h for the whole thirty minutes", "C": "About 0.11 mrem, because the rate falls to 0.22 mrem/h at a distance of 3 m", "D": "About 1 mrem, because the rate falls to 2 mrem/h at a distance of 3 m"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 0.33 mrem, because the rate falls to 0.67 mrem/h at 3 m): By the inverse square law, tripling distance reduces the rate by nine: 6 divided by 9 is 0.67 mrem/h. Over 0.5 hour the dose is 0.33 mrem. Dividing by three, as the colleague suggested, gives 2 mrem/h and 1 mrem. Dividing by 27 or ignoring distance gives other wrong values."}, {"id": 621, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist preparing regadenoson for a 142 kg patient wants to scale the dose by weight, as is done with adenosine at 140 mcg/kg/min. The vial contains 0.4 mg in 5 mL. The patient has no contraindications. What should be given?", "options": {"A": "A 10 mL (0.8 mg) bolus, since 142 kg is about twice a 70 kg patient.", "B": "A 5 mL dose infused slowly over 60 seconds, as is done for adenosine.", "C": "A reduced 2.5 mL dose, to limit the heart rate rise expected in larger patients.", "D": "A fixed 0.4 mg bolus over 10 seconds followed by a 5 mL saline flush."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A fixed 0.4 mg bolus over 10 seconds followed by a 5 mL saline flush.): Regadenoson is dosed at a fixed 0.4 mg regardless of weight, delivered as a rapid 10 second injection and followed by a 5 mL saline flush. Tracer is injected 10 to 20 seconds later. Unlike adenosine, it is not an infusion and no body weight calculation applies. Dose reduction lowers the hyperemic effect without clear safety benefit."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A trainee scores a SPECT study. Stress scores: four anterior segments score 3 each, two apical segments 2 each, and one lateral segment 1. Rest scores: four anterior segments score 1 each, all others 0. The trainee reports a difference score of 21. What is the correct value and the error?", "options": {"A": "The difference score is 8, because only the four anterior segments count toward the difference.", "B": "The difference score is 17, because the stress sum already contains the whole reversible component.", "C": "It is 13 (stress sum 17 minus rest sum 4); the trainee added the two sums.", "D": "The difference score is 4, because the rest sum alone measures the reversible component."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is 13 (stress sum 17 minus rest sum 4); the trainee added the two sums.): The stress sum is 12 plus 4 plus 1, which is 17. The rest sum is 4. The difference score is stress minus rest, 13. The trainee added the sums to reach 21. Scoring uses all 17 segments, so restricting the calculation to anterior segments is wrong. The rest sum reflects fixed defects, not reversibility."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A resting Rb-82 PET in a patient with an ejection fraction of 25 percent shows intense blood pool activity and poor myocardial contrast on the static images. Acquisition began 70 seconds after the start of the infusion. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Recent caffeine intake displaced tracer uptake from the myocardium into the blood pool.", "B": "Strontium breakthrough from the generator raised background counts in the blood pool.", "C": "Imaging began too late, after most activity had decayed given the 76 second half-life.", "D": "Imaging began before blood pool clearance, which is slower with low ejection fraction."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Imaging began before blood pool clearance, which is slower with low ejection fraction.): Rb-82 half-life is 76 seconds, so images are acquired after blood pool clearance, usually 90 to 120 seconds, and longer with low ejection fraction. Starting at 70 seconds catches tracer still in the cavity. Strontium breakthrough affects quality control and dose, not this pattern."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man has rest chest pain that recurred an hour ago despite aspirin and heparin, rising troponin and new ST depression. The emergency physician asks for an urgent stress SPECT study to risk stratify. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Defer stress imaging and proceed to early invasive angiography, given high-risk acute coronary syndrome features.", "B": "Perform adenosine SPECT today, since vasodilator stress is considered safe within hours of recent unstable angina.", "C": "Perform resting Tc-99m sestamibi imaging during pain, which can replace angiography in this setting.", "D": "Observe for 72 hours on medical therapy, then perform a symptom-limited treadmill test before deciding."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Defer stress imaging and proceed to early invasive angiography, given high-risk acute coronary syndrome features.): Refractory pain, rising troponin and dynamic ST changes define a high-risk acute coronary syndrome, which calls for early invasive management rather than stress testing. Stress is reserved for stabilized, low or intermediate risk patients. Rest imaging can help triage low-risk presentations but does not replace angiography here."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A SPECT study on a 72-year-old man shows EF 28 percent, a large reversible anterior and apical defect and TID 1.35. He left the department, is reached by phone, and reports mild breathlessness. The ordering physician is available by phone. What is the best action in reporting?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the stress study the next day to confirm the findings before any communication occurs.", "B": "Telephone the ordering physician promptly, document the call, then issue the finalized report.", "C": "Finalize the report with a flagged impression and rely on routine electronic delivery within 24 hours.", "D": "Advise the patient to seek angiography directly and bypass the ordering physician."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Telephone the ordering physician promptly, document the call, then issue the finalized report.): Findings that suggest high risk, such as a low EF, a large reversible defect and TID, with new symptoms are critical results needing direct communication with the responsible clinician, documented with time and content. Routine delivery adds delay. Instructing the patient alone skips the clinician accountable for care, and repeating the study postpones action when risk is already evident."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 63-year-old woman reaches 80 percent of her predicted maximum heart rate at stage 2 of a Bruce protocol. She now has exertional chest tightness with 2.5 mm of downsloping ST depression, and her blood pressure is rising appropriately. She is still able to walk. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Stop the treadmill immediately and inject the radiotracer during recovery after ST depression has resolved.", "B": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin first to relieve the discomfort, then inject once the ST segments recover.", "C": "Continue exercise without tracer until she reaches 85 percent of maximum rate, then inject.", "D": "Inject the radiotracer now at near-peak stress and continue exercise about one more minute if tolerated."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inject the radiotracer now at near-peak stress and continue exercise about one more minute if tolerated.): Angina with 2 mm or more of ST depression is a valid reason to end exercise, but the tracer must be given at peak stress so that it reflects the ischemic state. It is injected and exercise continues about 30 to 60 seconds so that the tracer distributes. Waiting for recovery or giving nitrates risks missing the ischemic territory."}, {"id": 627, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 44-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and a septal thickness of 24 mm has exertional chest pain. Regadenoson SPECT shows a reversible apical and septal defect. Coronary CT angiography shows no stenosis. What is the best explanation for the defect?", "options": {"A": "Partial volume loss from thin walls, which makes the defect an imaging artifact of count loss.", "B": "Microvascular dysfunction and demand mismatch from hypertrophy, not obstructive epicardial disease.", "C": "Diaphragmatic attenuation of the septum, which will resolve if the study is repeated prone.", "D": "Severe proximal LAD stenosis, which should be treated by angiography and revascularization of the vessel."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Microvascular dysfunction and demand mismatch from hypertrophy, not obstructive epicardial disease.): In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, perfusion defects occur from small vessel disease, high wall stress and demand mismatch even when the epicardial arteries are normal. The CT result argues against obstructive disease. Partial volume loss affects thin walls and not thick ones, and the diaphragm lies beneath the inferior wall, not the septum."}, {"id": 628, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient returns for rest sestamibi imaging after a stress acquisition that was done earlier the same day. The rest study will be compared with the stress images, and the interpreter wants differences to reflect physiology. What step best prevents positional artifacts from causing false reversibility?", "options": {"A": "Acquire rest images with a 360-degree orbit to compensate for any stress positioning differences.", "B": "Reduce the rest acquisition time, since shorter scans reduce the effect of positional changes.", "C": "Record arm position, orbit start angle, table height and pillow placement at stress, then repeat them.", "D": "Use whichever arm position the patient finds comfortable, since reversibility reflects tracer and not setup."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record arm position, orbit start angle, table height and pillow placement at stress, then repeat them.): Differences in arm position, bed height, orbit or detector distance change attenuation and geometry between studies and can produce a false difference between stress and rest. Recording and duplicating the setup limits this. Free positioning or a different orbit introduces artifacts, and scan time does not solve it."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A small electrical fire starts near shielded waste containers in the hot lab. Mo-99 generators and unsealed Tc-99m are stored in the room, and two staff are present nearby right now. A technologist asks what comes first. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Sound the alarm and evacuate, call the fire department and tell them radioactive materials are present", "B": "Wait for the RSO to survey the room before calling the fire department, to avoid contamination", "C": "Use the extinguisher first and evacuate only if the fire persists, then notify the RSO", "D": "Lock the generator in its shield first, since radioactive material takes priority over people"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sound the alarm and evacuate, call the fire department and tell them radioactive materials are present): Life safety comes first: alarm, evacuate, and call the fire department, and tell responders that radioactive materials are in the room so they can plan. The RSO is notified next and surveys for contamination afterward. Securing materials or delaying the call puts people at risk, and firefighting should be left to trained responders."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 79-year-old man with a thick left ventricle and heart failure has Tc-99m pyrophosphate planar imaging at 1 hour with a heart to contralateral ratio of 1.7. No SPECT was acquired, and the study was reported positive for ATTR cardiac amyloidosis. What is the main risk of this approach?", "options": {"A": "A false positive, since blood pool activity can mimic myocardial uptake without SPECT confirmation.", "B": "Excess radiation dose, because planar acquisition delivers more exposure than a SPECT acquisition.", "C": "Missing AL amyloidosis, because pyrophosphate cannot separate light chain disease without monoclonal protein testing.", "D": "A falsely low ratio, because renal excretion removes most pyrophosphate before the 1 hour images."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A false positive, since blood pool activity can mimic myocardial uptake without SPECT confirmation.): A planar ratio of 1.7 exceeds 1.5, but retained blood pool tracer can raise the ratio without bone-tracer uptake in the myocardium. SPECT separates myocardial uptake from the blood pool and is required before calling the study positive. Excluding AL amyloid does need monoclonal protein testing, but that is not the risk of skipping SPECT."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with exertional chest pain and hypertension has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT with an ejection fraction of 58 percent. Segment scoring gives a summed stress score of 11, a summed rest score of 8 and a difference score of 3. Which interpretation of the polar maps and coronary disease is best?", "options": {"A": "A fully reversible inferior defect: pure ischemia without scar, in the left circumflex territory distribution.", "B": "Partly reversible inferior and inferoseptal defect: scar plus residual ischemia, right coronary territory.", "C": "A fixed inferior defect: scar alone, so medical therapy fits and no ischemic myocardium is present.", "D": "Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact in a normal heart, so repeat imaging in the prone position."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Partly reversible inferior and inferoseptal defect: scar plus residual ischemia, right coronary territory.): The inferior and inferoseptal defect is moderate to severe at stress and still moderate at rest, with scores of 11 and 8 and a difference score of 3. Persistent rest reduction indicates scar, while the improvement indicates a peri-infarct ischemic component. These segments belong to the right coronary territory in a right dominant heart. Pure ischemia would normalize at rest, pure scar would not change, and attenuation artifact does not improve between stress and rest.", "image": "question_images/q_631.png"}, {"id": 632, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Two patients with the same last name, Garcia, are scheduled for nuclear imaging on the same morning. The stress agent and the tracer dose meant for one Garcia are given to the other. What is the most likely root cause of this error?", "options": {"A": "The dose calibrator was out of calibration and misread the dose in the syringe", "B": "The generator eluate had excess Mo-99 breakthrough, changing which patient received it", "C": "Staff did not confirm two unique patient identifiers against the order before administration", "D": "The stress agent was prepared in advance, which changes how the tracer distributes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Staff did not confirm two unique patient identifiers against the order before administration): Same-surname mix-ups are classic identification failures prevented by checking two unique identifiers, such as full name and date of birth, against the order immediately before administration. An instrument error, breakthrough or early preparation could not send a dose to the wrong person. The cause is a process failure, not a physical or chemical one."}, {"id": 633, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A report lists absorbed dose to the heart as 25 mGy after a stress sestamibi study, and a resident tells the patient that his whole-body risk equals 25 mSv. The patient asks whether that is right. What is the problem with that statement?", "options": {"A": "Effective dose weights organ absorbed doses by tissue factors, so heart mGy is not whole-body mSv", "B": "Equivalent dose is measured in grays, so the figure must be converted by dividing by 100", "C": "Gamma emitters carry a radiation weighting factor of 20, so the dose is really 500 mSv", "D": "Absorbed dose already includes tissue weighting, so 25 mGy equals 25 mSv and is correct"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Effective dose weights organ absorbed doses by tissue factors, so heart mGy is not whole-body mSv): Absorbed dose, in grays, is energy per unit mass in one organ. Equivalent dose applies a radiation weighting factor, which is 1 for gamma and beta, and effective dose sums organ equivalent doses with tissue weighting factors, so it describes whole-body risk. A heart dose of 25 mGy cannot be read directly as 25 mSv."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a prior lateral infarct has regadenoson SPECT after a negative exercise attempt limited by knee pain. The processing screen lists a summed stress score of 14 and a summed rest score of 10, all in the lateral wall. Ejection fraction is 58 percent. Which vessel and defect pattern are most consistent?", "options": {"A": "Left circumflex territory partly reversible defect, indicating scar plus residual jeopardized myocardium", "B": "Left anterior descending territory fixed defect, indicating transmural anterior scar and no ischemia present", "C": "Right coronary territory reversible defect, since the lateral wall receives the posterior descending artery", "D": "Reversible lateral ischemia without scar, since rest counts exceed stress counts in that wall"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left circumflex territory partly reversible defect, indicating scar plus residual jeopardized myocardium): Lateral segments belong to the left circumflex artery. Rest counts improve but do not reach the level of normal walls, which is the signature of a partially reversible defect with scar plus ischemia. The anterior descending and right coronary arteries supply other walls. Calling the defect fully reversible ignores the residual rest abnormality.", "image": "question_images/q_634.png"}, {"id": 635, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Tc-99m waste was held for 60 hours (ten half-lives) in a lead lined bin. The technologist surveyed it with a GM meter inside the closed bin, read background, and plans to discard it as ordinary trash. What is the flaw in this approach?", "options": {"A": "Decay in storage applies only to materials with half-lives longer than 120 days", "B": "A GM meter cannot detect Tc-99m photons, so an ion chamber survey must be used instead", "C": "Ten half-lives is too short, and twenty are needed before decay in storage disposal", "D": "Waste must be surveyed unshielded and unlabeled, since lead can hide residual activity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Waste must be surveyed unshielded and unlabeled, since lead can hide residual activity): Decay in storage applies to material with a half-life under 120 days, held at least ten half-lives. Before disposal the waste must be surveyed unshielded at the surface with a suitable meter, show no activity above background, and have radioactive labels removed. A reading taken inside a lead bin can miss residual activity."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man has typical angina and a pretest probability of obstructive coronary disease of 80 percent. His stress SPECT study is normal. The test has sensitivity 88 percent and specificity 74 percent. He asks whether he is clear. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Less than 5 percent residual probability, which effectively excludes obstructive disease after a normal test.", "B": "About 12 percent residual probability, which equals one minus the sensitivity of the test used.", "C": "About 26 percent residual probability, which equals one minus the specificity of the test used.", "D": "About a 39 percent residual probability of disease remains, so further evaluation is reasonable."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About a 39 percent residual probability of disease remains, so further evaluation is reasonable.): False negatives are 0.80 x 0.12 = 0.096 and true negatives are 0.20 x 0.74 = 0.148. Residual probability is 0.096 / 0.244, about 39 percent. When pretest probability is high, a normal result lowers risk but does not exclude disease. One minus sensitivity or specificity are test properties."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man with angina on maximal medical therapy has a stress SPECT with summed stress score 18 and summed rest score 8. The maximum possible score is 68. Both studies used the same 17-segment scoring. Which calculation and interpretation of his ischemic burden is correct?", "options": {"A": "Ischemic burden 59 percent, from dividing the difference score by 17 segments instead of 68.", "B": "Ischemic burden 12 percent, from the rest score divided by 68, which measures scar not ischemia.", "C": "Ischemic burden about 15 percent (10 divided by 68), a moderate to large extent.", "D": "Ischemic burden 26 percent, from the stress score divided by 68, which includes fixed scar."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ischemic burden about 15 percent (10 divided by 68), a moderate to large extent.): The difference score is 18 minus 8, which is 10. Ischemic burden as a percentage of the myocardium is 10 divided by 68, about 15 percent. A burden above roughly 10 percent is considered moderate to large and, with ongoing angina despite therapy, supports referral for angiography. The stress score includes scar, the divisor is the maximal score, and the rest score represents fixed defects."}, {"id": 638, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52 year old man with atypical chest pain has a normal rest ECG, walks 3 miles daily, takes no rate-limiting medicines and has no musculoskeletal limits to exercise. Stress perfusion imaging is planned. Which stress mode is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Adenosine, since it avoids the need for patient effort and shortens the appointment slot", "B": "Dobutamine, since an inotropic stressor gives the best image quality with the fewest side effects", "C": "Treadmill exercise, since he can reach target rate and it adds functional and prognostic data", "D": "Regadenoson, since vasodilator hyperemia is more sensitive than exercise for detecting coronary stenoses in all patients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treadmill exercise, since he can reach target rate and it adds functional and prognostic data): Exercise is the preferred stress for patients who can perform adequate work because it provides functional capacity, heart rate recovery, ST response and symptoms in addition to perfusion. Vasodilator and dobutamine stress are reserved for those who cannot exercise adequately or have an uninterpretable ECG or conduction disease. Convenience is not an indication."}, {"id": 639, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Weekly SPECT quality control uses a point source in air. After reconstruction, the point source appears as a ring or tuning-fork shape across the transaxial slices instead of a single point, though the daily flood image is normal. Which fault does this finding most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "An energy peak drift, which produces cold areas at the edges of the field of view", "B": "A center of rotation error, which misaligns projections and smears a point into an arc", "C": "Poor detector uniformity, which produces concentric rings across slices of a uniform phantom", "D": "A dead photomultiplier tube, which creates a round cold area on the intrinsic flood image"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A center of rotation error, which misaligns projections and smears a point into an arc): A center of rotation error shifts projections relative to the reconstruction axis, so a point source is smeared into an arc or tuning-fork shape. A failed tube appears as a cold area on a flood field, uniformity problems give ring artifacts in a uniform phantom, and an off-peak window reduces counts and alters edges. Each belongs to a different quality control test."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man with prior coronary artery bypass surgery and two stents has new exertional chest pressure. The requisition in the exhibit asks for a rest and regadenoson stress perfusion study plus a noncontrast coronary calcium scan in the same visit. As the interpreting physician reviewing the order for radiation safety, what should you do?", "options": {"A": "Perform the perfusion study and omit the calcium scan, which cannot alter management here", "B": "Add the calcium scan and base revascularization decisions on the Agatston score", "C": "Perform both studies, since the calcium score refines pretest probability of obstructive disease", "D": "Replace perfusion imaging with the calcium scan, since it uses less radiation and is quicker"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perform the perfusion study and omit the calcium scan, which cannot alter management here): Calcium scoring helps in estimating risk in patients without known coronary disease. With prior surgery and stents, atherosclerosis is already established, so the score will not alter the plan and the extra CT dose is unjustified. Perfusion imaging answers the question of ischemia and extent and guides further management.", "image": "question_images/q_640.png"}, {"id": 641, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an echocardiographic EF of 40 percent has a gated blood pool study to guide a primary prevention defibrillator decision. The curve is smooth, with 18,010 counts in the first frame and 11,578 at the lowest frame after background correction. How should the result be reported?", "options": {"A": "EF cannot be reported because the curve fails to return to its starting count", "B": "EF is about 64 percent, normal, taking end-systolic counts over end-diastolic counts", "C": "EF is about 36 percent, borderline; report the number with its variability", "D": "EF is about 55 percent, preserved, matching the typical normal value for a smooth curve"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (EF is about 36 percent, borderline; report the number with its variability): The calculation is (18,010 - 11,578) / 18,010 = 6,432 / 18,010, about 36 percent. That sits just above the usual 35 percent device threshold, within the repeat measurement variability of a few EF points, so the number should be reported with that caveat and the decision left to the clinician with other data. The 64 percent figure is the remaining fraction. A smooth curve indicates good gating and says nothing about normal function.", "image": "question_images/q_641.png"}, {"id": 642, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman has exertional chest pressure and a regadenoson SPECT study with an ejection fraction of 58 percent. The summed stress score is 5 and the rest score is 0, with mild reversible defects in basal and mid anterior and anterolateral segments. The nuclear physician must name the coronary territories involved. Which pair is best?", "options": {"A": "The left anterior descending alone, because the lateral defect is only spillover from anterior wall activity.", "B": "Left anterior descending and right coronary, because the right artery supplies the lateral wall.", "C": "Left circumflex and right coronary, because the anterior wall sits at the polar map bottom.", "D": "Left anterior descending and left circumflex territories, indicating mild ischemia in two vessels."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left anterior descending and left circumflex territories, indicating mild ischemia in two vessels.): The basal and mid anterior segments belong to the left anterior descending artery, and the anterolateral segments belong to the left circumflex, so two territories are involved, although the burden is mild with a difference score of 5. The lateral wall is not supplied by the right coronary artery. On a polar map the anterior wall is at the top and the inferior wall at the bottom. A lateral defect needs its own territory.", "image": "question_images/q_642.png"}, {"id": 643, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64 year old man had bypass grafting 6 years ago and two circumflex stents placed afterward. He now has exertional dyspnea and walks 6 METs on the treadmill at home. Native vessels are heavily calcified on a prior scan. Which test is best for the evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Coronary calcium scoring, since a score above 400 would confirm recurrent obstructive disease after bypass", "B": "Exercise myocardial perfusion imaging, since it localizes ischemia despite calcium or stents", "C": "Exercise treadmill ECG alone, since it can detect new ischemia in any vascular territory reliably", "D": "Coronary CT angiography, since it can display grafts and native vessels together in one single scan"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Exercise myocardial perfusion imaging, since it localizes ischemia despite calcium or stents): After bypass and stenting, stress perfusion imaging shows ischemic burden and location, which guides whether angiography is needed. CT angiography is degraded by stents and heavy calcium in native vessels. Calcium scoring gives no functional information and is not useful in revascularized patients, and ECG alone cannot localize ischemia."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with diabetes and exertional dyspnea has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The image shows stress and rest slices scored with the 17-segment, 0 to 4 system, giving a summed stress score of 16 and a summed rest score of 0. Which statement about the summed difference score and its meaning is correct?", "options": {"A": "The difference score is 16, indicating extensive reversible LAD ischemia needing angiography planning.", "B": "The difference score is 16, indicating fixed LAD scar because the stress score is elevated.", "C": "The difference score is 8, indicating mild ischemia that is managed with medical therapy alone.", "D": "The difference score is 0, indicating no ischemia because the rest score is normal."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The difference score is 16, indicating extensive reversible LAD ischemia needing angiography planning.): The summed difference score is the stress score minus the rest score, 16 minus 0, which is 16. That is about 24 percent of the 68 possible points, well above the 10 percent ischemic threshold that identifies patients who may benefit from revascularization. The segments involved are anterior, anteroseptal and apical, the LAD territory. A normal rest score means the defect is reversible, not scar, and halving the score has no basis.", "image": "question_images/q_644.png"}, {"id": 645, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory manager compares perfusion PET tracers for a new program and asks which fits N-13 ammonia best. The program has a cyclotron on site and a department wants a tracer with high extraction at rest and a physical half-life long enough for a brief rest and stress sequence. Which description is correct for N-13 ammonia?", "options": {"A": "A 10 minute half-life from an on-site cyclotron, with extraction near 80 percent", "B": "A 6 hour half-life from a molybdenum generator, with extraction near 60 percent and flow plateau", "C": "A 76 second half-life from a strontium generator, with extraction that stays high across flows", "D": "A 110 minute half-life allowing regional distribution, with extraction near 95 percent and little roll-off"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A 10 minute half-life from an on-site cyclotron, with extraction near 80 percent): N-13 ammonia has a half-life of about 10 minutes and must be made by a cyclotron on site. It is extracted at about 80 percent at rest and about 60 percent at high flow, then trapped as glutamine. The 76 second, 110 minute, and 6 hour half-lives belong to Rb-82, F-18 flurpiridaz and Tc-99m respectively."}, {"id": 646, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Gated SPECT rest results for a 66-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and ankle swelling appear in the exhibit, including volumes, perfusion and regional wall motion. The reader needs the EF and the meaning of the inferior finding. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "EF is 40 percent, and akinesis with a fixed defect suggests attenuation, not scar.", "B": "EF is 67 percent, found by dividing stroke volume by ESV.", "C": "EF is 60 percent, since ESV divided by EDV gives the fraction ejected.", "D": "EF is 40 percent, and akinesis with a fixed defect favors scar over attenuation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (EF is 40 percent, and akinesis with a fixed defect favors scar over attenuation.): EF equals EDV minus ESV divided by EDV: 160 minus 96 is 64, and 64 divided by 160 is 0.40. A fixed defect with akinesis, meaning absent thickening, indicates scar, whereas attenuation preserves thickening. Dividing ESV by EDV gives 60 percent, the fraction remaining.", "image": "question_images/q_646.png"}, {"id": 647, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 73-year-old man shifted suddenly during the middle of a stress SPECT acquisition. The sinogram shows an abrupt step of about 1.5 cm, larger than two pixels at this detector setting. Radiopharmaceutical activity remains adequate. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the acquisition, since a step this large is not reliably corrected by software", "B": "Discard the projections after the step and reconstruct from the earlier half of the data", "C": "Apply motion correction and accept the reconstruction without further review", "D": "Accept the study because a single step affects only one projection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the acquisition, since a step this large is not reliably corrected by software): After a sudden displacement every later projection is registered to a heart in a different place, not just one. Correction algorithms handle small shifts, roughly under two pixels, but a larger abrupt step leaves residual blur and defects. The practical choice is to repeat while activity permits. Dropping half the angles leaves too little sampling for a valid tomogram.", "image": "question_images/q_647.png"}, {"id": 648, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old diabetic man with new exertional angina and BMI 41 kg/m2 had a normal SPECT study 2 years ago and can walk only 3 minutes on a treadmill. Balanced multivessel disease is now suspected. Which test is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Resting gated SPECT only, since function at rest would show balanced ischemia directly.", "B": "Tl-201 SPECT with reinjection, since thallium redistribution exposes diffuse flow-limiting disease on delayed images.", "C": "Rb-82 PET with absolute flow and flow reserve quantification, since it can detect globally reduced flow.", "D": "Repeat dual-isotope SPECT, since a second tracer would reveal the balanced disease that sestamibi missed."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rb-82 PET with absolute flow and flow reserve quantification, since it can detect globally reduced flow.): Relative perfusion imaging compares regions, so diffuse reduction can look normal. PET provides absolute myocardial blood flow and flow reserve, which reveal global impairment. A second SPECT tracer or thallium reinjection stays relative, and resting function seldom shows exertional ischemia."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory runs a one-day Tc-99m sestamibi stress-rest protocol for a 55-year-old man with exertional chest pain and intermediate pretest probability, using standard activities for his body size. Which dose plan is correct for the two injections, and why is it used?", "options": {"A": "Give a higher first dose, then a lower second dose to protect later image quality", "B": "Give a lower first dose, then a second dose three times larger", "C": "Give equal doses of 20 mCi each so that counting statistics match across both studies", "D": "Give a low first dose and an equal second dose, then subtract the first image"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give a lower first dose, then a second dose three times larger): In one-day protocols the first injection leaves residual activity in the heart during the second study, so the second dose is about three times the first, typically 8 to 10 mCi then 25 to 30 mCi. That ratio keeps first-study contamination small. A larger first dose would swamp the lower second dose, and equal doses or subtraction do not solve the contamination."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "While reorienting a rest SPECT study in a woman with suspected lateral wall ischemia, a reader needs, before signing the report, a slice set that shows the apex plus both the septal and lateral walls together. Which reoriented slice set shows these walls?", "options": {"A": "Mid ventricular short axis slices, which show only the apex and base.", "B": "Vertical long axis slices, which show the anterior wall, apex and inferior wall.", "C": "Unreoriented coronal slices, since reorientation is not required to see septal and lateral walls.", "D": "Horizontal long axis slices, showing septum and lateral wall along the long axis."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Horizontal long axis slices, showing septum and lateral wall along the long axis.): Vertical long axis slices show the anterior wall, apex and inferior wall, while horizontal long axis slices show the septum on one side and the lateral wall on the other. Short axis slices cut perpendicular to the long axis and display all walls as a ring. Unreoriented slices are not aligned to the heart, so wall assignment would be unreliable."}, {"id": 651, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old man with frequent nonsustained ventricular tachycardia and blood pressure of 190/110 on medication has severe hip arthritis and cannot walk on a treadmill. A perfusion study for chest pain is requested. Which stress agent is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson, since dobutamine can provoke ventricular arrhythmias and raise pressure further", "B": "Arm crank ergometry, since upper body exercise avoids the hip and reaches target heart rates", "C": "Treadmill exercise at a reduced speed, since low workloads suit patients with joint disease", "D": "Dobutamine with atropine, since inotropic stress is the most sensitive for stable coronary disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regadenoson, since dobutamine can provoke ventricular arrhythmias and raise pressure further): Dobutamine increases heart rate, contractility and blood pressure and can provoke ventricular arrhythmias, so ventricular ectopy and severe hypertension argue against it. A vasodilator such as regadenoson does not depend on joint function. Arm ergometry seldom reaches adequate workload, and treadmill testing is impossible here."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A wipe sample from a package carrying Tc-99m was taken over 300 cm2 and counted in a well counter with 20 percent efficiency, giving 480 net counts per minute. The facility's action level is 2,000 dpm. What is the activity in dpm, and what action follows?", "options": {"A": "It is 2,400 dpm, but generator packages are exempt from wipe testing, so no action follows.", "B": "It is 960 dpm, below the action level, since counter efficiency is taken as 50 percent.", "C": "It is 96 dpm, below the action level, so the package may be accepted and opened.", "D": "It is 2,400 dpm, above the action level, so notify radiation safety and apply procedure."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is 2,400 dpm, above the action level, so notify radiation safety and apply procedure.): Disintegrations per minute equal net counts per minute divided by efficiency: 480 / 0.20 = 2,400 dpm. This exceeds the 2,000 dpm facility action level, so the radiation safety officer is notified and the package is handled under the contamination procedure. Multiplying by efficiency gives 96 dpm, assuming 50 percent gives 960 dpm, and receiving rules apply to all radioactive packages including generators."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old diabetic man has typical exertional angina. Regadenoson SPECT shows homogeneous uptake in all segments, with TID 1.0. Gated EF falls from 58 percent at rest to 41 percent after stress, and a prior angiogram showed severe three vessel disease. What is the most likely consequence of reporting the perfusion images alone as normal?", "options": {"A": "Underestimated infarct size, because the images would reflect only resting uptake in scarred segments.", "B": "Overestimated ischemic burden, leading to unnecessary angiography in a patient with only microvascular disease.", "C": "A falsely low EF from gating error, reclassifying the patient into a lower risk group.", "D": "Missed extensive balanced ischemia, labeled low risk, delaying revascularization despite high risk markers."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Missed extensive balanced ischemia, labeled low risk, delaying revascularization despite high risk markers.): Relative perfusion imaging normalizes each segment to the one with the highest counts, so uniformly reduced flow from three vessel disease can look normal. The post stress EF fall and known anatomy mark this as balanced ischemia. Calling the study normal would mislabel the patient as low risk. The other options describe overcalls or errors that the data do not support."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Rest-gated SPECT in a 61-year-old man shows akinesis of the mid and apical anterior wall, the apex and the distal septum, while the basal inferior and lateral walls contract normally. A cardiologist asks which coronary artery most likely supplies the abnormal region. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "The left main trunk, which causes equal involvement of both the anterior and lateral walls.", "B": "The left circumflex artery through the obtuse marginal branches, which supply the anterior and apical walls.", "C": "The left anterior descending artery, supplying the anterior wall, apex and septum.", "D": "The right coronary artery through its posterior descending branch, which supplies the septum and apex."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The left anterior descending artery, supplying the anterior wall, apex and septum.): Anterior, septal and apical wall motion abnormalities map to the left anterior descending artery. The lateral wall belongs to the circumflex and the inferior wall to the right or dominant artery, and both are normal here, which argues against left main disease or a circumflex lesion. The posterior descending artery supplies the inferior septum, not the anterior wall."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man with a known prior inferior infarction has a stress-only SPECT that shows an inferior defect, and no rest images were acquired because the laboratory routinely uses stress-first protocols. What is the main consequence of omitting rest imaging?", "options": {"A": "Ejection fraction cannot be measured, since gating requires a rest acquisition for the end-diastolic reference.", "B": "The stress image is invalid because attenuation correction requires matched rest data for each segment.", "C": "Defect severity is underestimated because the stress-only dose is lower, which reduces counts in every segment.", "D": "Reversibility cannot be assigned, so ischemia cannot be separated from scar and rest imaging is needed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reversibility cannot be assigned, so ischemia cannot be separated from scar and rest imaging is needed.): A defect on stress images may be ischemia or old scar, and only a rest comparison separates them. Stress-first omission is intended for patients without known infarction, so a defect should trigger rest imaging the same day or by protocol. Gated function and attenuation correction come from the stress acquisition itself and do not depend on rest data."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A department lead says the 12 mSv from a one-day Tc-99m study sits below the threshold for harm, so cancer risk is nil. The lead cites the skin erythema threshold of about 2 Gy as proof. Which consequence follows from confusing stochastic and deterministic effects?", "options": {"A": "Cancer severity is understated, since stochastic effects become more severe as the dose rises.", "B": "Heritable risk is eliminated, because stochastic effects arise only in the exposed person's own cells.", "C": "Skin injury risk is understated, because deterministic effects occur at any dose and scale with probability.", "D": "Cancer risk is understated, because stochastic effects have no threshold and their probability rises with dose."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cancer risk is understated, because stochastic effects have no threshold and their probability rises with dose.): Deterministic effects such as erythema or cataract need a threshold dose and become more severe with dose; stochastic effects such as cancer have a probability that rises with dose, with no threshold assumed, while severity does not depend on dose. A dose far below 2 Gy avoids skin injury but does not make cancer risk zero. Stochastic effects can also be heritable when germ cells are exposed."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Low-count SPECT of a 150 kg patient looks very noisy after filtered back projection with a Butterworth filter at cutoff 0.75 of Nyquist and order 5. The reader accepts slightly softer images, and the raw data cannot be reacquired. Which adjustment suits this patient?", "options": {"A": "Use a ramp filter alone with no window, since a ramp suppresses noise.", "B": "Raise the order to 15, so the filter blurs more broadly across the image.", "C": "Lower the cutoff toward 0.4 of Nyquist, accepting some resolution loss for less noise.", "D": "Raise the cutoff to 1.0 of Nyquist to pass more frequencies and average out noise."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lower the cutoff toward 0.4 of Nyquist, accepting some resolution loss for less noise.): The Butterworth cutoff sets the frequency above which data are suppressed, so lowering it smooths more and reduces noise at the cost of detail. Order only sets how sharply the filter rolls off around that cutoff. A ramp filter with no window amplifies high frequency noise, and a higher cutoff passes more noise into a count-poor study."}, {"id": 658, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 44-year-old man with syncope has the rhythm recorded in the exhibit, and echo shows an EF of 40 percent with basal septal thinning. He has no coronary risk factors and a normal coronary angiogram. Which imaging test and preparation is the best next step for suspected cardiac sarcoidosis?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson Rb-82 PET to quantify myocardial flow reserve in the septum", "B": "FDG PET with rest perfusion after a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet and prolonged fasting", "C": "FDG PET after an oral glucose load, as used for viability imaging", "D": "Tc-99m pyrophosphate SPECT at 3 hours to look for transthyretin amyloid deposits"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (FDG PET with rest perfusion after a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet and prolonged fasting): Unexplained high-grade AV block with septal thinning in a young adult with normal coronaries strongly suggests cardiac sarcoidosis. FDG PET shows active inflammation only when physiologic myocardial glucose uptake is suppressed by a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet and a prolonged fast. Glucose loading does the opposite, pyrophosphate targets amyloid in older patients, and flow reserve does not show inflammation.", "image": "question_images/q_658.png"}, {"id": 659, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "While eluting a Tc-99m generator, a technologist sees fluid leaking from the column onto the bench. The authorized user is reading studies in another room, and the department chief asks her to continue because patients are waiting. Under 10 CFR 35, what should she do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the authorized user to finish reading, since only that physician may stop an elution.", "B": "Continue under the chief's instruction, since the department chief holds final authority over radioactive materials use.", "C": "Stop and call the NRC first, since only the NRC may order licensed work halted.", "D": "Stop and notify the radiation safety officer, who can halt unsafe operations and direct cleanup."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop and notify the radiation safety officer, who can halt unsafe operations and direct cleanup.): Under NRC rules, the radiation safety officer is responsible for the radiation safety program and has the authority to identify problems and stop unsafe operations. A leaking generator column is an unsafe condition, so any worker should stop and notify the radiation safety officer, who directs containment and cleanup. A department chief's wishes cannot override safety, and the NRC need not be called before a local stop."}, {"id": 660, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Review of raw projection images from a rest SPECT study shows the heart drifting upward by about one pixel over the last third of the acquisition, with a smooth sinogram trace. No abrupt shifts are seen. How should the study be handled?", "options": {"A": "Report the study as nondiagnostic, noting that motion cannot be corrected in software", "B": "Apply software motion correction, then review corrected slices and accept if artifact clears", "C": "Apply a lower cutoff filter to blur the shifted wall and hide the creep", "D": "Discard the study and reacquire immediately, since any vertical drift makes the data unusable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply software motion correction, then review corrected slices and accept if artifact clears): Smooth upward creep of a pixel or so is the typical case in which vertical motion correction works well, provided the corrected projections and slices are reviewed to confirm that the artifact resolved. Software handles lateral and vertical rigid motion better than abrupt or nonrigid motion. Reacquiring is unnecessary here, and calling the data nondiagnostic ignores an available fix. Smoothing hides but does not correct displacement."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with atypical chest pain is evaluated with a two-day Tc-99m sestamibi protocol of 25 mCi on each day. Stress images on day one are normal with normal gated function and no attenuation. The lab cancels the rest study. By what percentage does cancelling reduce her total injected activity?", "options": {"A": "33 percent, since one of three injected doses is avoided in a typical protocol", "B": "25 percent, since a same-day rest dose is one quarter of the total activity", "C": "50 percent, since 25 mCi is given instead of 50 mCi, so dose halves", "D": "75 percent, since the stress dose is also cut when no rest study follows"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (50 percent, since 25 mCi is given instead of 50 mCi, so dose halves): The planned total was 25 plus 25, or 50 mCi. A normal stress-only study stops after the first 25 mCi, a 50 percent reduction, and the effective dose falls by roughly the same fraction. Stress-only imaging is appropriate when stress images are normal, gated function is normal and attenuation is excluded."}, {"id": 662, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old woman with large breasts has a fixed anterior defect on Tl-201 stress and redistribution SPECT. A colleague suggests the defect is less likely to be attenuation than on a Tc-99m study because Tl-201 has a longer half-life. Which statement is best?", "options": {"A": "Wrong; both tracers have the same energy and breast tissue affects neither", "B": "The colleague is right, since lower photon energy means the photons penetrate breast tissue more easily", "C": "Wrong; Tl-201 photons have lower energy and attenuate more, so breast artifacts are greater", "D": "The colleague is right, since a longer half-life gives more counts and reduces soft-tissue artifacts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wrong; Tl-201 photons have lower energy and attenuate more, so breast artifacts are greater): Tl-201 emits mercury x-rays at about 70 to 80 keV, much lower than the 140 keV photon of Tc-99m, so more of its signal is absorbed and scattered in soft tissue. Breast and diaphragmatic attenuation artifacts are therefore generally worse with thallium. Half-life affects dose and imaging window, not attenuation. Lower energy means less penetration, and the two tracers do not share the same energy."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with known coronary disease arrives for treadmill sestamibi imaging. She reports chest pressure at rest for the past 3 hours, and her resting ECG shows new 2 mm ST depression in V4 to V6 compared with last month. The technologist proceeds with the Bruce protocol. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Nondiagnostic images only, because the target heart rate will be reached too early in exercise.", "B": "Falsely large reversible defects, because stress tracer redistributes into the rest images afterward.", "C": "A falsely negative perfusion study, because resting ischemia blunts the heart rate response to exercise.", "D": "Provoking infarction or arrhythmia, since unstable angina absolutely contraindicates exercise."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Provoking infarction or arrhythmia, since unstable angina absolutely contraindicates exercise.): Rest pain with new ST depression indicates an acute coronary syndrome in evolution, and exercise stress is absolutely contraindicated until the syndrome is excluded or stabilized. Raising myocardial oxygen demand on an unstable plaque can cause infarction or ventricular fibrillation. A blunted heart rate response, early target heart rate and redistribution are image quality issues, not the main hazard here."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 33-year-old woman has Tc-99m sestamibi MPI, about 10 mSv effective dose, and learns two days later that she was about 6 weeks pregnant at the time. She is frightened and asks about her fetus. What is the best counseling?", "options": {"A": "Fetal dose is far below 50 mGy, so no deterministic effects are expected", "B": "The fetal dose likely exceeds 50 mGy, so termination should be offered to her promptly", "C": "Any exposure in the first trimester causes major malformations, so detailed ultrasound is urgent now", "D": "No dose reaches the fetus because Tc-99m does not cross the placenta from the mother"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fetal dose is far below 50 mGy, so no deterministic effects are expected): Fetal doses from standard Tc-99m cardiac studies are on the order of a few mGy, well under the roughly 50 mGy level below which deterministic effects and malformations are not expected. Termination is not recommended on radiation grounds. Tc-99m agents can reach the fetus through maternal circulation, but at a small dose, so zero dose is wrong."}, {"id": 665, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A radiation safety officer reviews the NRC annual dose limit for a member of the public, which is 1 mSv (0.1 rem). A new technologist asks for the limit in mrem to compare with a monitor's readout. Which conversion is correct?", "options": {"A": "10 mrem per year, treating 1 mSv as 10 mrem", "B": "100 mrem per year, because 1 mSv equals 100 mrem", "C": "500 mrem per year, because the public limit equals the occupational skin limit", "D": "1,000 mrem per year, treating 1 mSv as equal to 1 rem"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (100 mrem per year, because 1 mSv equals 100 mrem): One sievert equals 100 rem, so 1 mSv equals 0.1 rem, or 100 mrem. The NRC public limit is therefore 100 mrem (1 mSv) per year total effective dose equivalent. Treating mSv as rem or shifting the factor by ten gives the wrong readout comparison."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "An 80 kg patient with left bundle branch block can walk, but exercise would likely produce a false septal defect, so vasodilator stress is chosen with adenosine at 140 mcg/kg/min for 6 minutes. What total adenosine dose will be infused over the full 6 minutes?", "options": {"A": "About 45 mg total, from 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg for 4 minutes", "B": "About 67 mg total, from 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg times 6 minutes", "C": "About 101 mg total, from 210 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg for 6 minutes", "D": "About 11 mg total, from 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg for a single minute"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 67 mg total, from 140 mcg/kg/min times 80 kg times 6 minutes): Left bundle branch block causes septal perfusion defects with exercise because of rate-related septal dyssynchrony, so vasodilator stress is preferred. The rate is 140 times 80, or 11,200 mcg per minute (11.2 mg). Over 6 minutes the total is 67.2 mg. Using one minute, a 4 minute run or the 210 mcg/kg/min dose gives the other values."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58 year old woman with ischemic cardiomyopathy, an ejection fraction of 20 percent, a body mass index of 41 and poor echo windows has insulin-treated diabetes. Fasting glucose is 210 mg/dL. Viability will decide on bypass surgery. Which protocol fits best?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201 rest redistribution SPECT, since thallium viability data hold up well in markedly obese patients", "B": "Tc-99m sestamibi rest imaging without nitrate, since it separates scar from hibernation reliably in this setting", "C": "FDG PET with insulin titration to lower glucose before injection, since obesity limits other methods", "D": "Low-dose dobutamine echocardiography, since contractile reserve is the most direct viability marker available to us"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (FDG PET with insulin titration to lower glucose before injection, since obesity limits other methods): With severe dysfunction, obesity and poor acoustic windows, FDG PET gives the highest count quality and allows a metabolic readout of hibernating tissue. In insulin resistance, glucose must be lowered and insulin titrated, often with an IV protocol, so that myocardium takes up FDG. Thallium and sestamibi suffer attenuation and underestimate viability, and dobutamine echo is limited by windows."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A sestamibi vial with 82 percent radiochemical purity is injected by mistake, and the impurity is free pertechnetate. After the study, the physician reviews the images and tries to predict the expected pattern in this patient. Which finding best fits free pertechnetate?", "options": {"A": "Intense liver and gallbladder activity from hydrolyzed colloid, which obscures the inferior wall", "B": "Higher soft tissue background with thyroid and gastric uptake, lowering the heart to background ratio", "C": "Faster myocardial washout from the impurity, which makes defects appear smaller than they are", "D": "Focal lung uptake from large particle aggregates, which resembles pulmonary perfusion imaging"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Higher soft tissue background with thyroid and gastric uptake, lowering the heart to background ratio): Free pertechnetate behaves as an ion and is not taken up by myocardium. It circulates and concentrates in the thyroid, salivary glands and stomach, raising background and lowering the heart to background ratio. Hepatic uptake would suggest colloid, lung uptake would suggest particles, and a free impurity does not change myocardial washout kinetics."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT for exertional chest pain. Resting counts are even around the ventricle, while the stress row in the image shows relatively lower counts in one region. Which mechanism best explains why a flow-limiting stenosis produces that stress finding?", "options": {"A": "Tracer washes out faster from ischemic myocytes within minutes, so the stress row loses counts there.", "B": "Adenosine A2A receptors are missing downstream of a stenosis, so those vessels cannot dilate.", "C": "Hyperemic flow rises less beyond the stenosis than in normal myocardium, so tracer delivery lags there.", "D": "Regadenoson constricts the stenotic artery directly, which cuts tracer delivery to its territory during stress."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hyperemic flow rises less beyond the stenosis than in normal myocardium, so tracer delivery lags there.): Resting flow is preserved by autoregulation distal to a stenosis, so rest images look normal. Vasodilator stress raises flow two to four fold in normal vessels but much less beyond a flow-limiting lesion, and tracer uptake follows flow, so relative counts are lower there. Regadenoson does not constrict arteries, sestamibi washes out slowly with little redistribution, and receptors remain present.", "image": "question_images/q_669.png"}, {"id": 670, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman had vasodilator Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT without exercise, and intense hepatobiliary activity adjacent to the heart obscured the inferior wall. She needs repeat imaging next week and the laboratory has several agents. Which agent and reason are best?", "options": {"A": "Tetrofosmin, since faster hepatobiliary clearance allows earlier imaging with less inferior wall interference.", "B": "Tetrofosmin imaged at 5 minutes, since this agent has no liver uptake after pharmacologic stress.", "C": "Tl-201, since its redistribution removes hepatic activity and the inferior wall improves on the delayed images.", "D": "Sestamibi again, imaged at 15 minutes, since its hepatic clearance is faster than that of tetrofosmin."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tetrofosmin, since faster hepatobiliary clearance allows earlier imaging with less inferior wall interference.): Both Tc-99m agents are cleared through the liver and gut, and tetrofosmin clears from the liver somewhat faster, which lets imaging begin earlier. Vasodilator stress without exercise increases splanchnic activity. Thallium has worse photon characteristics and its redistribution does not target liver activity, and no agent is free of hepatic uptake."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A man has stress Tc-99m SPECT images with an apparent inferior defect next to intense subdiaphragmatic activity. Thirty minutes before imaging he ate a large fatty meal as instructed to promote gallbladder emptying. Which is the most likely cause of the apparent defect?", "options": {"A": "Patient motion from bowel peristalsis, which creates a downward shift of the heart", "B": "Caffeine from the same morning blocking tracer uptake in the inferior wall segments", "C": "Hot bowel activity from fatty-meal gallbladder emptying lies near the inferior wall and distorts its counts", "D": "Diaphragmatic attenuation from the supine position, which lowers inferior counts whatever the tracer clearance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hot bowel activity from fatty-meal gallbladder emptying lies near the inferior wall and distorts its counts): A fatty meal prompts gallbladder contraction that delivers hepatobiliary tracer into the bowel adjacent to the inferior wall. Intense nearby activity distorts the reconstruction and lowers relative inferior counts. Diaphragmatic attenuation is real but does not explain this timing, caffeine affects vasodilators, and bowel peristalsis does not move the heart."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "An upward heart shift during a rest SPECT is reported by processing software as an abrupt 3 pixel shift starting at projection 41 of 64, with later projections realigned. Pixel size is 6.4 mm. What is the physical shift, and what limits this correction?", "options": {"A": "About 0.5 mm; correction recovers nearly all motion because it works on every frame.", "B": "About 19 mm; correction realigns rigid shifts but cannot fix motion within a single projection.", "C": "About 38 mm; correction fails because upward motion is counted from both edges.", "D": "About 9 mm; correction cannot address vertical movement and works only on horizontal shifts."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 19 mm; correction realigns rigid shifts but cannot fix motion within a single projection.): Distance equals pixels times pixel size, so 3 times 6.4 mm gives about 19 mm. Motion correction shifts whole projections back into line, and it works for vertical shifts that persist between frames. Motion that happens during a single projection, or that is nonrigid, remains blurred and may need repeat acquisition."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old nondiabetic man with an EF of 22 percent and a dilated ventricle has an akinetic anterior wall. A Tl-201 rest, 4 hour redistribution and reinjection study gave uptake of 45 percent of peak in the anterior segments, a borderline value. Surgeons need a clear decision. What is the best next test?", "options": {"A": "Sestamibi SPECT with sublingual nitroglycerin, since nitrates convert scarred myocardium into viable tissue", "B": "Repeat Tl-201 reinjection, which gives a more reliable estimate of membrane integrity than before", "C": "Regadenoson stress perfusion alone, since a persistent defect identifies scarred anterior wall segments", "D": "F-18 FDG PET, since metabolic uptake detects hibernation in borderline dysfunctional segments"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (F-18 FDG PET, since metabolic uptake detects hibernation in borderline dysfunctional segments): Uptake near 50 percent of peak is a gray zone, and in a dilated left ventricle with an EF of 22 percent, FDG PET is the more sensitive study for hibernating myocardium. Another thallium study would give the same limited answer, nitrate sestamibi is also uptake based and offers no clear gain, and a perfusion defect alone cannot separate hibernation from scar."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old woman has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT. Scores are given as a summed stress score of 10 and a summed rest score of 10, with basal anterior and anterolateral defects reported on both polar maps. The summed difference score is calculated. What does it show about ischemia?", "options": {"A": "A difference score of 20 means extensive ischemia, because the two scores are added together.", "B": "A difference score of 0 means no ischemia; the unchanged defect is fixed scar.", "C": "A difference score of 0 means a normal study, because no change excludes any defect.", "D": "A difference score of 10 means moderate ischemia, because the rest score is carried forward."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A difference score of 0 means no ischemia; the unchanged defect is fixed scar.): The difference score is stress minus rest, 10 minus 10, which is 0. With defects present on both maps and no change between them, the finding is fixed scar rather than reversible ischemia. Adding the scores has no physiologic meaning, carrying the rest score forward confuses scar with ischemia, and a zero difference does not make a study normal because both scores are abnormal.", "image": "question_images/q_674.png"}, {"id": 675, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with atypical chest pain has a pretest probability of obstructive CAD of 15 percent. Her MPI has a sensitivity of 90 percent and specificity of 80 percent, and the study is abnormal. Using Bayes theorem, what is her post-test probability and what does it mean?", "options": {"A": "About 68 percent, because the positive likelihood ratio multiplies the pretest probability directly", "B": "About 44 percent, which is intermediate and short of diagnostic certainty for angiography alone", "C": "About 35 percent, because the false positive rate adds to the pretest probability", "D": "About 90 percent, because a positive result carries the test's sensitivity as its probability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 44 percent, which is intermediate and short of diagnostic certainty for angiography alone): The positive likelihood ratio is 0.90 divided by 0.20, or 4.5. Pretest odds are 0.15 over 0.85, about 0.176; multiplied by 4.5 gives 0.794, which is a probability of 0.794 over 1.794, about 44 percent. Multiplying the probability by the ratio, quoting the sensitivity, or adding the false positive rate all skip the odds conversion that Bayes theorem requires."}, {"id": 676, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71 year old man with chest pain walks on the treadmill, stops at 4 METs from knee pain at 98 beats per minute, about 66 percent of maximum, with no ST change or angina. No tracer is injected yet. What limitation applies if tracer is given now, and what should be done?", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity falls with submaximal exercise, so convert to regadenoson during low-level exercise", "B": "Inject now and add regadenoson only if ST depression appears on the recording made later", "C": "Rest 15 minutes, then repeat a full Bruce protocol, since knee pain recovers quickly with rest", "D": "Inject now, since any exercise raises flow enough to reveal moderate stenoses in the images"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sensitivity falls with submaximal exercise, so convert to regadenoson during low-level exercise): Perfusion defects need adequate stress, and exercise below 85 percent of maximum rate with no ischemic signs reduces sensitivity. Non-cardiac limitation with a submaximal rate is an accepted indication to convert, injecting the vasodilator during low-level exercise before tracer. Delaying injection until ST change appears makes no sense, and repeating treadmill testing does not fix the limitation."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man who takes no rate-limiting drugs and walks well is sent for treadmill SPECT. The laboratory requires 85 percent of age-predicted maximum heart rate (220 minus age) as the target. What target should the technologist record on the requisition?", "options": {"A": "Exercise to 162 beats per minute, the full age-predicted maximum, before the injection.", "B": "At least 138 beats per minute, calculated as 85 percent of 162.", "C": "Exercise to 122 beats per minute, taking 75 percent of the age-predicted maximum.", "D": "Exercise to 129 beats per minute, taking 85 percent of 220 and then subtracting age."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (At least 138 beats per minute, calculated as 85 percent of 162.): Age-predicted maximum is 220 minus 58 = 162. Multiplying by 0.85 gives 137.7, rounded to 138. Using 100 percent gives 162, 75 percent gives 121.5, and computing 0.85 x 220 = 187 minus 58 gives 129, which is the wrong order of operations."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with intermediate pretest probability undergoes treadmill stress for myocardial perfusion imaging, and the laboratory wants a negative result to be reliable. Which peak heart rate satisfies the standard target of at least 85 percent of age-predicted maximum, and what calculation produces it?", "options": {"A": "148 bpm, from 220 minus age and then subtracting 15 beats rather than taking 85 percent.", "B": "163 bpm, from 220 minus age, with no further adjustment for the target fraction.", "C": "187 bpm, from 85 percent of 220 without subtracting age before applying the fraction.", "D": "139 bpm, from 85 percent of 163, where 163 equals 220 minus age."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (139 bpm, from 85 percent of 163, where 163 equals 220 minus age.): Age-predicted maximum heart rate is 220 minus 57, which is 163 bpm. The adequacy target is 85 percent of that, 163 times 0.85, or about 139 bpm. Reaching it makes a negative result reliable. Subtracting 15 beats, reporting the full maximum, or applying 85 percent to 220 produces other values that are not the standard target."}, {"id": 679, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A patient is undergoing a rubidium-82 PET perfusion study, and the referring physician also requests a coronary calcium score for risk stratification. The low-dose, free-breathing CT used for attenuation correction has already been acquired during the study. Which approach provides the best calcium quantification for this request?", "options": {"A": "Acquire the CT at 80 kVp to lower dose, since lower voltage improves calcium conspicuity.", "B": "Score the free-breathing attenuation CT, since its calcium is quantitatively equivalent to a gated scan.", "C": "Acquire a separate ECG-gated, breath-hold noncontrast CT at 120 kVp with thin slices.", "D": "Acquire a gated contrast-enhanced CT, since iodinated contrast sharpens calcified plaque for scoring."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acquire a separate ECG-gated, breath-hold noncontrast CT at 120 kVp with thin slices.): Agatston scoring is validated on ECG-gated, breath-hold, noncontrast CT at 120 kVp with 2.5 to 3 mm slices. The free-breathing attenuation map is acquired at low dose and cannot give a standardized score, although calcium may be commented on qualitatively. Contrast obscures calcium thresholds and a different kVp changes calibration."}, {"id": 680, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A camera's daily check shows the Tc-99m photopeak has drifted to 146 keV, but the acquisition window is still centered at 140 keV with a 15 percent width. A patient is scheduled in 20 minutes. Which is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Adjust the pixel size calibration to compensate for the displaced energy peak.", "B": "Widen the window to 25 percent to capture the shifted peak and keep imaging.", "C": "Repeat energy peaking and uniformity floods, since an off-center window loses primary photons.", "D": "Proceed because the shifted peak still lies inside the window, then use a software uniformity map."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat energy peaking and uniformity floods, since an off-center window loses primary photons.): Even when the shifted peak sits inside the window, it is off-center and the edge of the peak is cut away, which loses primary photons and worsens uniformity. The camera should be repeaked and tested again. Widening admits scatter, and pixel size is a spatial parameter."}, {"id": 681, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Two minutes after a regadenoson injection for SPECT stress, a 66-year-old man has a 40-second generalized tonic-clonic seizure in the stress room. He is now postictal and breathing, with a blood pressure of 130/80 mmHg and a normal oxygen saturation on the monitor. What is the best management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Protect the airway, give a benzodiazepine if seizure recurs, and avoid aminophylline, which may prolong seizures.", "B": "Give intravenous metoprolol to slow the reflex tachycardia that is likely causing the seizure.", "C": "Give aminophylline intravenously immediately, since it reverses regadenoson and should terminate the seizure.", "D": "Give sublingual nitroglycerin, since the seizure most likely reflects coronary spasm that nitrates resolve."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Protect the airway, give a benzodiazepine if seizure recurs, and avoid aminophylline, which may prolong seizures.): Seizures are a rare regadenoson effect. Aminophylline can lower the seizure threshold and prolong or provoke seizures, so it should be avoided. Management is supportive with airway protection and an intravenous benzodiazepine for recurrence or prolonged activity. The seizure is neurologic, not an ischemic or rate-related event."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Two prepared syringes sit in tungsten shields on a hot lab counter, one of Tc-99m sestamibi for a rest study and one of Tc-99m tetrofosmin for another patient. Neither shield is labeled. Which labeling and storage practice best matches each dose to its patient and meets NRC requirements?", "options": {"A": "Label each syringe and shield with drug name and radiation symbol, matched to paperwork.", "B": "Label only the vial, since whoever drew the syringe already knows what the contents are.", "C": "Label the syringe with patient initials only, leaving the drug identity to the dose record.", "D": "Color-code the shields without printed names, since color identifies each agent for all staff."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Label each syringe and shield with drug name and radiation symbol, matched to paperwork.): NRC rules (10 CFR 35.69) require each syringe and vial of unsealed radioactive material to be labeled to identify the drug, and syringe shields likewise unless the syringe label is visible. Pairing each labeled syringe with its patient's paperwork prevents a mix-up between two similar technetium agents. A vial label alone leaves the syringe unidentified, and a color or initials scheme does not identify the drug."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with dyspnea has a rest-stress SPECT. Left ventricular perfusion is normal, but the right ventricle shows intense tracer uptake approaching the left ventricle, with a dilated right cavity and flattened septum. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal study since left ventricular perfusion is normal and right uptake is not assessed.", "B": "Recommend coronary angiography for right coronary ischemia because increased right ventricular uptake indicates ischemia.", "C": "Repeat the stress study with a higher vasodilator dose to saturate the right ventricular uptake.", "D": "Report right ventricular hypertrophy and dilation, suggest pulmonary hypertension, and recommend echocardiography."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report right ventricular hypertrophy and dilation, suggest pulmonary hypertension, and recommend echocardiography.): Normally the right ventricle is faintly visible. Marked right ventricular uptake means hypertrophy from pressure overload, and a dilated cavity with septal flattening supports pulmonary hypertension. These findings belong in the report with a recommendation for echocardiography and right heart catheterization. Increased uptake is not ischemia, and the right ventricle must be commented on whatever the left ventricle shows."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A department receives a sealed pure beta emitting check source with a maximum beta energy of 2.3 MeV. The RSO designs a storage container for it in the hot lab. Which design best limits both the beta dose and the bremsstrahlung photons?", "options": {"A": "A thick lead wall placed directly against the source, because lead stops betas most efficiently", "B": "An inner lead layer followed by an outer acrylic layer to absorb the scatter", "C": "A single aluminum foil wrapper, since beta particles produce no penetrating photons", "D": "An inner acrylic layer to absorb betas, then a thin outer lead layer for bremsstrahlung"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An inner acrylic layer to absorb betas, then a thin outer lead layer for bremsstrahlung): High energy betas are stopped best by low atomic number material such as acrylic, which makes little bremsstrahlung. The photons that do form are then attenuated by a thin lead layer outside it. Lead placed first generates more bremsstrahlung, and reversing the order or using foil alone leaves a photon hazard."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 148 kg patient with a BMI of 46 received a standard 30 mCi stress sestamibi dose. Images are noisy with low count density and an apparent fixed inferior defect. What is the most likely cause of the poor image quality?", "options": {"A": "Liver shine-through from imaging too late, which would be fixed by imaging at 10 minutes.", "B": "Attenuation and scatter in thick tissue lower counts, so weight-adjusted dose or longer time.", "C": "An orbit set too wide, which is fixed by narrowing the arc to 90 degrees.", "D": "A collimator that is too sensitive, so switching to LEHR would lower noise."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Attenuation and scatter in thick tissue lower counts, so weight-adjusted dose or longer time.): In very large patients, photons are absorbed and scattered in thick tissue, so fewer reach the camera and the study looks noisy with apparent defects. Raising the dose by weight, lengthening time per projection and using attenuation correction help. LEHR collimators reduce counts further. Narrowing the orbit and imaging earlier do not address the low count density."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 42 kg/m2 had a prior SPECT study with an inferior defect that could not be separated from attenuation. A cardiac PET scanner with CT attenuation correction is available. Which test is the best next choice, and why?", "options": {"A": "Repeat sestamibi SPECT with a higher injected activity and longer acquisition time", "B": "Dobutamine stress echocardiography, because image quality does not depend on body habitus", "C": "Rb-82 PET, because higher counts and attenuation correction reduce artifacts in obesity", "D": "Tl-201 SPECT, because its lower photon energy penetrates tissue better than Tc-99m"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rb-82 PET, because higher counts and attenuation correction reduce artifacts in obesity): PET detects coincidence photons at 511 keV with attenuation correction applied routinely, so it gives higher resolution and fewer attenuation artifacts than SPECT in very large patients. Raising SPECT activity does not remove attenuation. Tl-201 emits lower energy photons that attenuate even more, and obesity commonly degrades echo windows as well."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old woman had a lateral myocardial infarction eight years ago. She has atypical chest discomfort, and regadenoson sestamibi SPECT is done. The stress and rest polar maps show the same reduced counts at the right side of the map. Which interpretation and implication are best?", "options": {"A": "Reversible lateral ischemia in the left circumflex territory, so urgent angiography is needed now.", "B": "Reversible anterior ischemia in the left anterior descending territory, so medical therapy is needed.", "C": "A fixed lateral defect in circumflex territory, consistent with scar, without inducible ischemia.", "D": "A left bundle branch block septal artifact, so no clinical relevance can be assigned here."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A fixed lateral defect in circumflex territory, consistent with scar, without inducible ischemia.): The right side of the polar map is lateral, and the same defect at stress and rest identifies a fixed lateral scar, which suits the old lateral infarction and the circumflex territory. No ischemia is inducible in that territory, so urgent angiography is not driven by this study. A septal bundle branch block artifact does not apply to the lateral wall.", "image": "question_images/q_687.png"}, {"id": 688, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A trainee asks why a laterally drifting band on a sinogram is a useful display for technologists compared with paging through every projection image. Which answer is correct? The technologist notes that scans are reviewed this way before every reconstruction at the laboratory.", "options": {"A": "It stacks counts by projection angle, so any movement breaks the expected smooth band", "B": "It plots lung to heart ratios, so tracer distribution errors are seen quickly by readers", "C": "It shows reconstructed slices, so wall motion and perfusion appear together", "D": "It displays gated volumes, so ejection fraction can be read at a glance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It stacks counts by projection angle, so any movement breaks the expected smooth band): A sinogram arranges one profile of counts per projection angle into a single picture. A stationary heart produces a smooth sinusoidal band, so a drift or step stands out immediately without reviewing each projection. It does not show reconstructed slices, quality ratios or gated volumes, which are separate displays used for other decisions.", "image": "question_images/q_688.png"}, {"id": 689, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT scored in 17 segments. Six segments score 1 or 2 at stress, giving a summed stress score of 11, and every rest segment scores 0. The reader reports ischemia as a percentage of the maximum score of 68. Which result is correct?", "options": {"A": "The difference score is 11, about 16 percent of myocardium, above the high-risk threshold.", "B": "The difference score is 6, about 9 percent of myocardium, which is low risk.", "C": "The difference score is 0, because the rest score is zero, so no ischemia is present.", "D": "The difference score is 11, about 11 percent of myocardium, which is moderate risk."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The difference score is 11, about 16 percent of myocardium, above the high-risk threshold.): The difference score is 11 minus 0, or 11. Dividing 11 by the maximum of 68 gives about 16 percent, which is above 10 percent and counts as high risk. A rest score of 0 means the defect is fully reversible, so it is ischemia. Treating 11 as 11 percent confuses the score with a percentage, and using the rest score alone does not measure reversible defect size.", "image": "question_images/q_689.png"}, {"id": 690, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old asymptomatic man has a stress SPECT study showing a small, mild, reversible inferior defect involving 2 of 17 segments, with normal EF and an unremarkable exercise ECG at 10 METs. Which impression best communicates the significance of this result to the referring physician?", "options": {"A": "Normal study; the small defect has no clinical consequence and needs no report comment.", "B": "Mildly abnormal study with limited mild inferior ischemia, normal function and low cardiac event risk.", "C": "Positive study for inferior ischemia, and urgent coronary angiography is recommended for this patient.", "D": "Technically limited study with probable artifact; clinical correlation is suggested before any firm conclusion is drawn."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mildly abnormal study with limited mild inferior ischemia, normal function and low cardiac event risk.): A report impression should state the finding, its extent and severity, the function and the risk meaning. Two mildly affected segments with normal EF and strong exercise capacity indicate limited ischemia and a low event risk. Urgent angiography overstates it, calling it normal drops a true finding, and a vague technical caveat gives the clinician no usable conclusion."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old woman had exercise Tl-201 SPECT two years ago and now has regadenoson sestamibi SPECT with attenuation correction. The new study shows a fixed inferior defect that was absent before. Gated wall motion is normal. What is the best approach to the comparison?", "options": {"A": "Defer the comparison to the ordering physician, since it is outside the reader's usual duties.", "B": "Call the earlier study a false negative, since the defect probably existed two years ago.", "C": "Report a new inferior infarct, since the defect was not present on the earlier exercise study.", "D": "Do not call interval infarction until both studies match for tracer, stress and processing."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do not call interval infarction until both studies match for tracer, stress and processing.): Tracer, stress modality, camera and attenuation correction all change the apparent distribution of counts, so a new defect between unmatched studies may be technical. The reader should compare like with like, review non-corrected images and gated function, and then decide. Calling infarct or false negative is premature, and comparison with prior studies is part of the interpreting physician's duty."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist looking at a morning flood image from a SPECT camera notes a smooth side-to-side change in count density across the field rather than any isolated spot, and asks which kind of camera drift most often produces this pattern. Which parameter drift is the most common cause?", "options": {"A": "Loss of alignment between the collimator holes and the detector, which shifts counts toward one side.", "B": "Offset of the detector from its mechanical center during orbit, which tilts the count density.", "C": "Drift of photomultiplier gain or the energy window, so the photopeak is no longer centered.", "D": "Insufficient flood counts causing Poisson variation that is stronger on one side of the field."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Drift of photomultiplier gain or the energy window, so the photopeak is no longer centered.): A global gradient arises when tube gains or the window setting drift so that the photopeak is sampled differently across the crystal. Collimator alignment and orbit offset do not change planar count density in this way, and Poisson noise is random, not directional. This is why the daily flood is paired with peaking.", "image": "question_images/q_692.png"}, {"id": 693, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60 year old woman with poorly controlled diabetes has a fasting glucose of 240 mg/dL when FDG is injected for viability imaging. Myocardial uptake is faint and blood pool activity is high on the images. What limitation does this create for interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Glucose competes only with Rb-82, so FDG image quality is unaffected by glucose values", "B": "High blood pool activity makes lung uptake appear as a lateral perfusion defect", "C": "Insulin resistance lowers myocardial FDG uptake, so viable segments may be misread as scar", "D": "High glucose raises myocardial FDG uptake, so scar may be misread as viable myocardium on images"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Insulin resistance lowers myocardial FDG uptake, so viable segments may be misread as scar): FDG competes with glucose for transport, and without insulin action the myocardium takes up little tracer. Poor uptake and high blood pool make viable tissue look like scar. Glucose and insulin management before injection, using oral loading or IV insulin, is needed to restore uptake."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "While processing an LAO equilibrium MUGA in a 47-year-old patient on chemotherapy, a technologist must place the background ROI for the EF calculation. Splenic activity is prominent lateral to the left ventricle, and the aorta lies superior to it. Which background placement is best?", "options": {"A": "Over the aortic arch, to sample true blood pool background.", "B": "Beside the left ventricle at the lateral wall, away from spleen and great vessels.", "C": "Over the center of the left lung, which is free of organs.", "D": "Over the splenic area, where counts represent the same blood pool activity as the ventricle."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Beside the left ventricle at the lateral wall, away from spleen and great vessels.): The background ROI estimates activity from tissue overlying and surrounding the ventricle, so it belongs beside the left ventricle, usually at the lateral wall near end systole. The spleen, stomach and great vessels are avoided. The spleen has high counts that overstate background. Lung is too low, and the aorta contains blood pool activity rather than background tissue."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The exhibit describes a 63-year-old man with left bundle branch block and atypical chest pain whose exercise SPECT shows a septal defect. He has no known coronary disease. The referring physician asks how to proceed. Which approach best matches the protocol to the patient?", "options": {"A": "Switch to dobutamine, which raises heart rate more reliably and should clear the septal defect.", "B": "Probable bundle branch block artifact, so repeat with vasodilator stress or PET instead.", "C": "Repeat exercise at a lower workload to separate artifact from ischemia in the same patient.", "D": "True septal ischemia, so proceed to left anterior descending angiography on the exercise result."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Probable bundle branch block artifact, so repeat with vasodilator stress or PET instead.): In left bundle branch block, rate-related abnormal septal motion and perfusion produce a septal defect at higher heart rates, especially with exercise or dobutamine. Vasodilator stress raises the rate less and avoids the artifact, and PET adds accuracy. Treating the septal defect as proof of left anterior descending stenosis, or repeating with another rate-raising stressor, risks a false positive.", "image": "question_images/q_695.png"}, {"id": 696, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A gated SPECT study of a 63-year-old man with dyspnea on exertion reports a stress LV cavity volume of 142 mL and a rest volume of 105 mL for TID. The upper limit of normal for this sestamibi protocol is about 1.2. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The ratio is 1.35, above the upper limit, suggesting severe extensive ischemia.", "B": "The ratio is 0.74, which lies below 1.2 and is therefore normal for TID.", "C": "The difference of 37 mL is below an absolute cutoff, so the finding is normal.", "D": "The ratio is 1.35, but 1.5 is the upper limit, so the finding is normal."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The ratio is 1.35, above the upper limit, suggesting severe extensive ischemia.): TID is stress volume divided by rest volume: 142 divided by 105 is 1.35. That exceeds about 1.2 for this protocol, so it is abnormal and points to severe extensive ischemia, although technical causes should be excluded. Dividing the other way gives 0.74 and misses it, and TID is judged as a ratio with a protocol specific limit."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old man has Tc-99m sestamibi imaging started 10 minutes after peak treadmill exercise while he is still breathing hard. A stress-only inferior defect is present, and the motion display shows the heart drifting upward during acquisition. Rest images are normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat stress immediately with a second tracer dose to wash out the apparent inferior defect.", "B": "Report fixed inferior scar and recommend viability testing since the rest images look normal.", "C": "Review the cine for upward drift, apply motion correction, and reacquire stress images later if needed.", "D": "Report reversible inferior ischemia and recommend angiography because the stress-only defect is reproducible."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the cine for upward drift, apply motion correction, and reacquire stress images later if needed.): Upward creep occurs when the diaphragm and heart settle as hyperventilation subsides after exercise, and early acquisition can produce false inferior or posterior defects. The motion display provides the clue. Correcting for motion and, if necessary, reimaging after the patient is calm is appropriate. A stress-only defect is not a fixed scar, and a second injection adds dose without addressing the cause."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A Tc-99m agent has a physical half-life of 6.0 hours and a biological half-life of 6.0 hours in the target tissue, giving an effective half-life of 3.0 hours. The laboratory is setting the hold time for unused syringes under decay-in-storage. Which half-life governs the hold time, and why?", "options": {"A": "The physical half-life of 6.0 hours governs, because stored syringes decay physically with no biological clearance.", "B": "An average of 4.5 hours governs, because it approximates the activity retained in the patient.", "C": "The biological half-life of 6.0 hours governs, because patient excretion determines how long material stays radioactive.", "D": "The effective half-life of 3.0 hours governs, because it combines both clearance routes and describes decay."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The physical half-life of 6.0 hours governs, because stored syringes decay physically with no biological clearance.): The effective half-life follows 1/Te = 1/Tp + 1/Tb = 1/6 + 1/6, giving 3.0 hours, and it applies to activity inside a living patient. Stored syringes and waste have no biological removal, so only physical decay reduces them and the physical half-life of 6.0 hours sets the hold time. The arithmetic mean has no physical meaning here."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation, ventricular rates of 70 to 110 per minute, has a blood pool study before trastuzumab. The curve starts near 17,930 counts and falls to 2,720 in the final frames. Software reports an EF of 85 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Do not report the EF; repeat with a narrower R-R acceptance window to reject short beats", "B": "Report the EF from the mid-cycle frames only and discard the final frames", "C": "Increase the dose of Tc-99m labeled red cells and repeat to raise count density", "D": "Report an EF of 85 percent as hyperdynamic function and recommend continuing trastuzumab"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not report the EF; repeat with a narrower R-R acceptance window to reject short beats): Counts should return to near the first-frame value at the end of a cardiac cycle. Here they keep falling because short beats, common in atrial fibrillation, contribute nothing to late frames. The computed 85 percent comes from (17,930 - 2,720) / 17,930 and is an artifact. A narrower acceptance window, or list-mode acquisition, rejects short beats. Truncating frames or adding activity does not repair the timing problem.", "image": "question_images/q_699.png"}, {"id": 700, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man has an inferior wall defect with summed stress score 6 on the non-corrected sestamibi images. After CT based attenuation correction the summed stress score is 0, and gated wall motion and thickening are normal. Which statement best describes what this comparison supports?", "options": {"A": "An unreliable study, since attenuation correction can only be applied to the stress images alone.", "B": "Attenuation artifact, since the defect resolved after correction with normal wall thickening.", "C": "True ischemia that attenuation correction has masked by overcorrecting the counts in the inferior wall.", "D": "A fixed scar was present, since the corrected study still showed a reduced summed score."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Attenuation artifact, since the defect resolved after correction with normal wall thickening.): Attenuation correction restores counts lost to soft tissue overlying the inferior wall, so a defect that disappears with normal thickening is most consistent with attenuation. Real scar or ischemia persists after correction. The score fell from 6 to 0, which a scar would not do, and correction is applicable to both rest and stress images."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exhibit shows the receiving record for a Mo-99/Tc-99m generator delivered to a busy nuclear cardiology department whose working day starts at 08:00. Under the NRC rule for monitoring labeled packages, by when exactly must the package be monitored for contamination?", "options": {"A": "By 17:50 Monday, which is 24 hours after the next working day begins", "B": "By 11:00 Monday, three hours after the start of the next working day", "C": "By 20:50 Friday, which is three hours after the delivery time", "D": "By 08:00 Monday, which is the moment the next working day begins"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (By 11:00 Monday, three hours after the start of the next working day): For a labeled package received after normal hours, the NRC requires monitoring no later than 3 hours from the beginning of the next working day. With a Friday evening delivery and an 08:00 Monday start, that deadline is 11:00 Monday. Counting from the delivery time or from 24 hours misstates the allowance, and starting at 08:00 omits the grace period.", "image": "question_images/q_701.png"}, {"id": 702, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea receives regadenoson, and Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT is acquired at stress and again at rest. The short-axis and long-axis slices are in the image. Which coronary artery most likely supplies the myocardium whose uptake differs between the two rows?", "options": {"A": "The left circumflex artery, which supplies the lateral wall where counts fall at stress.", "B": "The septal perforator branches of the left anterior descending artery, supplying the basal septum.", "C": "The posterior descending artery in a right dominant system, supplying the inferior wall and inferoseptum.", "D": "The left anterior descending artery, which supplies the anterior wall and the apex of the heart."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The left circumflex artery, which supplies the lateral wall where counts fall at stress.): On short-axis slices the lateral wall sits on the right side of each ring, and the long-axis views show the same wall, so the stress-only drop in counts lies in the lateral segments (summed stress score 14, rest score 0). The left circumflex supplies the basal and mid anterolateral and inferolateral segments and the lateral apex. The LAD feeds anterior and septal segments, the posterior descending artery feeds the inferior wall, and the septal perforators feed the septum, so each of those fails to match the location.", "image": "question_images/q_702.png"}, {"id": 703, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman receiving trastuzumab had a baseline LVEF of 62 percent by gated blood pool imaging. At follow-up, biplane echocardiography reports 55 percent, and her oncologist plans to stop therapy for a seven point fall. What is the most likely consequence of comparing these two values?", "options": {"A": "Therapy may stop because of a method difference, not myocardial injury", "B": "Cumulative anthracycline exposure will be underestimated in her treatment record", "C": "Her radiation exposure from the blood pool study will rise above the echo exposure", "D": "A true decline will be missed because blood pool imaging is insensitive to small changes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Therapy may stop because of a method difference, not myocardial injury): Different techniques give systematically different LVEF values, and gated blood pool EF is typically a few points higher than echo. A seven point difference across methods can therefore reflect method variance, so serial monitoring should use the same modality and laboratory. The risk of mixing methods is a spurious fall that interrupts effective cancer treatment, not missed injury, mistaken dosing records or a radiation change."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A laboratory plans stress PET with N-13 ammonia delivered by a regional cyclotron facility, as recorded in the exhibit. Injection is scheduled 2 hours after the order leaves the supplier on a 120 mile route. What is the problem with this plan?", "options": {"A": "Delivery is acceptable, since the half-life of N-13 ammonia is about 110 minutes", "B": "Delivery is acceptable if the order is tripled to compensate for decay during the transit time", "C": "Two hours spans 12 half-lives, leaving no usable activity, so N-13 must be made on site", "D": "The problem is that Rb-82 generators cannot be used, because Rb-82 comes from regional cyclotrons"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Two hours spans 12 half-lives, leaving no usable activity, so N-13 must be made on site): N-13 has a half-life of about 10 minutes, so after 120 minutes only about 1/4000 of the activity remains. It must be produced on site with a cyclotron. Tripling the order cannot restore it. Rb-82 comes from an on-site Sr-82 generator, a different logistic from N-13.", "image": "question_images/q_704.png"}, {"id": 705, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "After adenosine stress without exercise in a 63-year-old woman, a Tc-99m sestamibi image shows an apparent inferior defect adjacent to intense bright activity just below the heart. Which property of the tracer explains this finding, and what is the appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Renal excretion of sestamibi hides the inferior wall; switch to Tl-201 for stress imaging", "B": "Sestamibi redistributes into the inferior wall from the blood pool; reinject the tracer", "C": "Slow lung washout lowers inferior counts; shorten the delay to 15 minutes", "D": "Hepatobiliary clearance leaves bright activity beside the inferior wall; wait longer and reacquire"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hepatobiliary clearance leaves bright activity beside the inferior wall; wait longer and reacquire): Sestamibi clears through the liver and bowel, and without exercise there is little splanchnic flow reduction, so subdiaphragmatic activity stays high. That activity can create a false inferior defect through filtered-backprojection ramp effects or overlap. Waiting longer, often with water or a light meal, lets clearance occur before reacquisition. Redistribution does not occur with sestamibi, and a shorter delay would worsen the problem."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old woman has severe COPD with wheezing on daily bronchodilators, a left bundle branch block on her resting ECG, and cannot walk on a treadmill. She is referred for SPECT myocardial perfusion imaging. Which stress protocol best matches her conditions?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson, since selective A2A action lowers bronchospasm risk and avoids heart-rate-related septal defects.", "B": "Low-level treadmill exercise to a modest heart rate, since slow increments reduce the septal artifact.", "C": "Dobutamine with atropine to reach target rate, since it spares the airways and avoids vasodilators.", "D": "Dipyridamole infusion over four minutes, since its indirect adenosine effect is gentler on airways."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regadenoson, since selective A2A action lowers bronchospasm risk and avoids heart-rate-related septal defects.): Left bundle branch block makes exercise and dobutamine unreliable because rising heart rate produces septal and anterior defects without coronary disease, so a vasodilator is preferred. Regadenoson is A2A selective and has the lowest bronchospasm risk, though caution is still needed. Dipyridamole raises endogenous adenosine at all receptors and carries bronchospasm risk."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man scheduled for adenosine SPECT in two days takes extended-release dipyridamole with aspirin twice daily for stroke prevention. He phones the nuclear cardiology lab to ask what to do about his medicines before the test. Which instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Continue it as usual, since dipyridamole does not change the response to adenosine", "B": "Hold dipyridamole at least 24 hours before the study, after confirming with the prescriber", "C": "Hold it for 4 hours, because a short gap is enough to clear the interaction effect", "D": "Double the morning dose to enhance the effect of the stress agent on the coronary arteries"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold dipyridamole at least 24 hours before the study, after confirming with the prescriber): Dipyridamole blocks adenosine reuptake and potentiates it, which can cause severe bradycardia or heart block, and it should be held at least 24 hours before adenosine. It also can alter other vasodilator tests. Instructions should include prescriber approval when the drug is for secondary stroke prevention. Doubling the dose raises risk, and four hours is too brief."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader ignores a calcium score of 1,800 on the attenuation CT of a 60-year-old man with diabetes and a normal SPECT, and tells him his low risk lasts 5 years. What is the most likely consequence of this error?", "options": {"A": "His risk is underestimated, so intensified preventive therapy and earlier retesting are missed.", "B": "Ischemic burden is overestimated, because calcium shifts counts into the inferior wall of the heart.", "C": "Perfusion images become uninterpretable, because dense calcium blocks tracer from entering the myocardium.", "D": "Radiation dose is overestimated, because calcium adds to the reported effective dose of the study."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (His risk is underestimated, so intensified preventive therapy and earlier retesting are missed.): A calcium score above 1,000 indicates a large plaque burden and shortens the warranty of a normal perfusion study. Ignoring it leads to false reassurance, with missed statin intensification and earlier follow-up. Calcium on the attenuation CT does not change tracer uptake, effective dose reporting or interpretability of the perfusion images."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old alert woman who speaks only Vietnamese presents for treadmill SPECT. Her adult daughter offers to translate the risks and sign for her. The patient is competent and able to give consent. What is the best way to obtain consent?", "options": {"A": "Proceed under implied consent because the patient arrived for the scheduled appointment with the daughter", "B": "Accept the daughter as translator and have her co-sign the consent form as the patient's proxy", "C": "Use a qualified medical interpreter, explain risks and alternatives, and obtain the patient's own consent", "D": "Obtain verbal consent only from the patient because stress testing is routine and carries low risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a qualified medical interpreter, explain risks and alternatives, and obtain the patient's own consent): A competent patient must give her own informed consent, and when a language barrier exists a qualified interpreter should convey the risks and alternatives accurately. A family member may not be able to translate technical content neutrally, and a proxy signature is not valid for a competent patient. Verbal consent without documentation and implied consent do not meet the standard for stress testing."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "Rest and stress short axis slices from a Tc-99m sestamibi study appear to show a new lateral wall defect on stress, with normal gated wall motion. The exhibit lists the processing parameters used for each set. Which explanation fits best, and what is the correct next step?", "options": {"A": "Upward creep during the stress acquisition; apply motion correction and keep the current reorientation.", "B": "Different long axis angles were used; reprocess both sets with identical axes and compare again.", "C": "Reversible lateral ischemia in the circumflex territory; recommend coronary angiography without further processing.", "D": "Lower stress count density in the lateral wall; extend acquisition time on the next study."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Different long axis angles were used; reprocess both sets with identical axes and compare again.): The exhibit shows horizontal and vertical long axis angles that differ by about 11 and 8 degrees between sets, so the slices sampled different wall planes. This produces an apparent regional difference with normal wall motion and thickening. Reprocessing both sets with the same axes removes the false change. Creep and count density are not suggested by the parameters, and ischemia would be called prematurely.", "image": "question_images/q_710.png"}, {"id": 711, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and a known left bundle branch block undergoes regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT with no chest pain. The septal and anteroseptal segments are reduced at stress, partly improved at rest, with a summed stress score of 8 and a summed rest score of 3. The other walls are normal. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Large reversible left anterior descending territory ischemia, so refer directly for coronary angiography and revascularization planning.", "B": "Inferior wall attenuation from the diaphragm, so repeat the whole study with prone imaging.", "C": "Fixed septal scar from a remote septal infarction, so order viability testing next.", "D": "Septal pattern typical of left bundle branch block; do not assign anterior descending stenosis from septum."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Septal pattern typical of left bundle branch block; do not assign anterior descending stenosis from septum.): The septal and anteroseptal defect, milder at rest, with a normal anterior wall, apex and lateral wall is the classic left bundle branch block septal pattern, which appears with vasodilator stress less often but still occurs. A summed stress score of 8 and rest score of 3 describe a limited defect confined to the septum. It reflects abnormal septal contraction and perfusion timing, not a flow-limiting stenosis, so territory-wide LAD ischemia is not shown. Reading it as LAD disease prompts needless angiography. A fixed scar does not improve at rest, and diaphragmatic attenuation affects the inferior wall, not the septum.", "image": "question_images/q_711.png"}, {"id": 712, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and no known coronary disease has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Gated ejection fraction is 60 percent. Segment scoring gives a summed stress score of 13, a summed rest score of 13 and a difference score of 0. Which interpretation and management implication best fit the polar maps?", "options": {"A": "A reversible lateral defect from circumflex ischemia, so coronary angiography is the best next step.", "B": "A fixed defect that proves viable hibernating myocardium, so revascularization is mandatory to restore function.", "C": "Diaphragmatic attenuation artifact in the lateral wall, so prone imaging should be repeated.", "D": "A fixed lateral wall defect consistent with scar, with no inducible ischemia in that territory."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A fixed lateral wall defect consistent with scar, with no inducible ischemia in that territory.): The lateral wall defect has the same extent and severity on the stress and rest maps, the summed stress and rest scores are both 13, and the difference score is 0, so there is no inducible ischemia and the pattern is scar in the circumflex territory. Angiography would be driven by ischemia, which is absent here. Diaphragm attenuation affects the inferior wall, not the lateral wall. A severe fixed defect suggests nonviable scar, and viability would need a separate test before any revascularization decision.", "image": "question_images/q_712.png"}, {"id": 713, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A nuclear cardiology lab begins drawing Tc-99m into 1 mL syringes and 10 mL vials. Assays of the same activity in the two containers differ by 8 percent in the dose calibrator. Which required quality control test checks reading variation with sample volume and container geometry?", "options": {"A": "Linearity test, assaying a decaying high activity Tc-99m source across the full range of activities", "B": "Constancy test, reading a long-lived reference source on every day of use against its expected value", "C": "Geometry test, assaying one Tc-99m source in stepwise larger volumes and applying correction factors", "D": "Accuracy test, comparing Cs-137 and Co-57 standard sources against their certified activities each year"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Geometry test, assaying one Tc-99m source in stepwise larger volumes and applying correction factors): Geometry testing is done at installation and after repair or relocation, and it measures how the reading changes as the same activity is spread over different volumes, so correction factors can be applied. Constancy checks a reference source each day of use, accuracy uses certified standards, and linearity covers the range of activity. Each of these answers a different question from the volume dependence in this lab."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 150 kg man with a BMI of 47 arrives for same-day Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The scanner table accepts 205 kg and the lab uses 10 and 30 mCi fixed doses. A technologist asks how to proceed. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Scale activity to body weight under the lab's protocol and lengthen acquisition time per projection.", "B": "Give the standard fixed doses and shorten the number of projections to limit patient motion artifact.", "C": "Switch to thallium-201 stress and redistribution, since its higher photon energy attenuates less in tissue.", "D": "Give the standard rest dose and double the stress injection to correct for abdominal scatter."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Scale activity to body weight under the lab's protocol and lengthen acquisition time per projection.): Large patients attenuate photons and lose count density, so weight-based activity and longer acquisition preserve image quality. Fixed doses leave noisy images. Thallium photons are lower energy and attenuate more. Doubling only the stress dose is not a validated scaling scheme, and PET could also be considered if available."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with prior anterior infarction and EF 28 percent has a severe fixed anterior and apical defect on a stress and rest sestamibi study, and the rest images were acquired 45 minutes after injection. Revascularization is being considered. What is the likely consequence if the defect is simply reported as scar?", "options": {"A": "Scar may be labeled viable, leading to futile revascularization of segments that cannot recover.", "B": "The defect would be reclassified as reversible, which changes only the choice of stress agent used.", "C": "Partial reversibility would be missed, which affects only the reported summed difference score value.", "D": "Hibernating myocardium may be labeled scar, denying revascularization to salvageable segments."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hibernating myocardium may be labeled scar, denying revascularization to salvageable segments.): A severe fixed defect on sestamibi can still contain hibernating myocardium, because the tracer underestimates viability in low flow segments. Reporting scar without further viability testing, such as late Tl-201 redistribution or FDG PET, may withhold revascularization from a patient who could improve in function. The other options reverse the error or understate its clinical consequence."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A generator eluate is assayed at 07:00, showing 0.9 uCi of Mo-99 in 8.0 mCi of Tc-99m, a ratio of 0.11 uCi per mCi. The limit is 0.15 uCi per mCi at the time of administration. The laboratory plans to give doses from this eluate until 13:00. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Use it until 19:00, because Mo-99 decay lowers the ratio with every hour that passes", "B": "Use it all day, because 0.11 is below 0.15 and breakthrough is tested only at elution", "C": "Use it only until about 09:30, since the ratio reaches 0.15 as Tc-99m decays", "D": "Reject it now, because 0.9 uCi is greater than the 0.15 uCi limit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use it only until about 09:30, since the ratio reaches 0.15 as Tc-99m decays): Tc-99m decays with a 6-hour half-life while Mo-99 barely changes, so the ratio rises over time. The ratio hits 0.15 when Tc-99m falls to 0.9 divided by 0.15, or 6.0 mCi, which takes 6 times log base 2 of 8/6, about 2.5 hours. By 13:00 the ratio would be about 0.225, above the limit. Using 0.9 as the limit confuses an absolute amount with a ratio."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old woman develops chest pain after the sudden death of her husband, with ST elevation and mildly raised troponin. Coronary angiography is normal. Rest perfusion SPECT shows an apical defect with basal hyperkinesis on gating. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Acute LAD occlusion, since an apical defect with ST elevation requires immediate reperfusion therapy now.", "B": "Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, where a fixed apical defect would persist on all later follow up studies.", "C": "Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, with an apical perfusion defect expected to improve on repeat imaging.", "D": "Acute myocarditis, which causes a basal perfusion defect with apical hypercontractility on gated imaging."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, with an apical perfusion defect expected to improve on repeat imaging.): The emotional trigger, normal coronary arteries and apical hypoperfusion with basal hyperkinesis are typical of Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, a non-coronary cause of defects that usually recovers within weeks. LAD occlusion is excluded by angiography, apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is a fixed structural condition, and myocarditis does not typically show this regional pattern."}, {"id": 718, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A published study reports 92 percent sensitivity and 60 percent specificity for MPI detecting angiographic stenosis of at least 50 percent. Only patients with abnormal MPI results were sent to angiography. Which statement best describes the limitation of these figures?", "options": {"A": "Prevalence differences alter sensitivity and specificity directly, so the values cannot be used", "B": "Post-test referral bias inflates sensitivity and deflates specificity of the reported figures", "C": "Random sampling error is the only issue, since sensitivity and specificity are fixed properties", "D": "Spectrum bias from healthy volunteers inflates both, so accuracy is overstated in routine patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Post-test referral bias inflates sensitivity and deflates specificity of the reported figures): When mainly abnormal tests are verified by angiography, true negatives are undercounted, so sensitivity rises and specificity falls, a form of verification or post-test referral bias. Spectrum bias is a different problem that arises when healthy and severely diseased groups are compared. Prevalence affects predictive values, not sensitivity and specificity, and design flaws are more than random error."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man has exertional chest pressure and a nondiagnostic ECG. Treadmill sestamibi polar maps are reviewed. Reduced counts appear toward the top and left of the stress map, normal at rest, with a summed stress score of 9 and a rest score of 0. Which statement is the best reading?", "options": {"A": "Reversible inferior wall ischemia in the right coronary territory, so repeat the study with prone imaging.", "B": "Reversible anterior, septal and apical anterior descending ischemia; angiography is reasonable.", "C": "Reversible ischemia of the lateral wall in the circumflex territory, so adjust medical therapy first.", "D": "Fixed anterior scar from a prior infarct, so no further ischemia testing is needed at present."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reversible anterior, septal and apical anterior descending ischemia; angiography is reasonable.): On the polar map the anterior wall lies at the top, the septum at the left and the apex at the center, so reduced stress counts in that region that normalize at rest are reversible and sit in the mid and distal left anterior descending distribution. The summed difference score of 9 is about 13 percent of the maximum of 68. The lateral wall is on the right and the inferior wall at the bottom, so those readings fail the orientation. A fixed scar would persist at rest. Symptoms plus this ischemia justify angiography.", "image": "question_images/q_719.png"}, {"id": 720, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with no symptoms, LDL cholesterol 112 mg/dL, blood pressure 122/76 mmHg, no diabetes and a normal resting ECG asks for a stress perfusion scan after a friend died suddenly. She jogs daily without limitation. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Decline imaging, estimate ten-year risk with a pooled cohort tool, and treat risk factors.", "B": "Order a treadmill ECG every year without imaging, since repeated screening finds silent ischemia early.", "C": "Order exercise SPECT, since a normal result would reassure her and justify skipping statins.", "D": "Order regadenoson Rb-82 PET, since its higher accuracy suits screening of anxious asymptomatic women."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline imaging, estimate ten-year risk with a pooled cohort tool, and treat risk factors.): Routine stress imaging in low-risk asymptomatic adults is rated rarely appropriate in the appropriate use criteria, because pretest probability is low and positive results are mostly false or lead to needless angiography. Risk estimation and risk-factor treatment change outcomes here. A normal scan would not remove the need for preventive therapy, PET accuracy does not fix a low pretest probability, and serial treadmill testing adds cost without benefit."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma, treated with an inhaled steroid and a rescue inhaler, has intermediate risk chest pain and cannot exercise. She has had mild wheezing twice this month. Which pharmacologic agent is most appropriate for her stress study?", "options": {"A": "Dipyridamole over 4 minutes, since its indirect action spares adenosine receptors in the bronchi", "B": "Regadenoson, since A2A selectivity carries less bronchospasm risk and aminophylline reverses it", "C": "Adenosine at 140 micrograms per kilogram per minute, since its very short half-life spares the airways", "D": "Dobutamine at 40 micrograms per kilogram per minute, since it has no bronchial or rhythm effects"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Regadenoson, since A2A selectivity carries less bronchospasm risk and aminophylline reverses it): Adenosine and dipyridamole (which raises endogenous adenosine) act on A1, A2B and A3 receptors that can provoke bronchospasm. Regadenoson is A2A selective and is used cautiously in mild to moderate reactive airway disease. Dobutamine avoids adenosine receptors but is less suited to someone with unstable wheeze and may provoke tachyarrhythmia."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During dipyridamole stress for a patient with newly recognized severe asthma, he develops wheezing and chest pain with marked ST depression after the infusion ends. He is hypotensive but alert. The nuclear cardiologist plans to reverse the effect. Which choice best reflects the mechanism and the correct reversal?", "options": {"A": "Give intravenous atropine, since dipyridamole acts by cholinergic stimulation of the AV node and bronchi", "B": "Give aminophylline, an adenosine receptor antagonist, since dipyridamole raises adenosine by blocking its uptake", "C": "Give intravenous adenosine, to compete with the dipyridamole already bound at the receptors in the vessels", "D": "Give intravenous esmolol, since symptoms result from direct beta receptor stimulation by the infusion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give aminophylline, an adenosine receptor antagonist, since dipyridamole raises adenosine by blocking its uptake): Dipyridamole inhibits adenosine reuptake and deamination, raising interstitial adenosine, which then acts on A1, A2A, A2B and A3 receptors. Aminophylline (50 to 250 mg slowly) antagonizes these receptors and reverses vasodilation, bronchospasm and AV block. Adenosine would worsen the effects, and atropine and esmolol act on other receptors."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A clinic screens 1,000 asymptomatic low-risk adults, of whom 5 percent have significant coronary disease. The perfusion test has 88 percent sensitivity and 80 percent specificity. A colleague wants to start treatment for every patient with a positive result. Which statement about the positive results is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 80 percent of positives are true, since predictive value of a test equals its specificity", "B": "About 20 percent of positives are false, since 20 percent test positive without disease", "C": "About four of five positives are false, since low prevalence drives down positive predictive value", "D": "Most positives are true, since 88 percent sensitivity means 88 percent of positive tests are real"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About four of five positives are false, since low prevalence drives down positive predictive value): With 50 diseased patients, 44 are detected, and of 950 healthy patients, 190 test positive falsely. Positive predictive value is 44 divided by 234, or about 19 percent. Sensitivity and specificity describe the test, not the probability that a given positive is true, which depends on prevalence."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A lab compares two parallel-hole collimators for Tc-99m SPECT using a point source at increasing distance. The chart shows system resolution for Collimator X and Collimator Y. Collimator X gives the smaller full width at half maximum at every distance. Which statement correctly identifies the collimators and the cost of the better resolution?", "options": {"A": "Y is the general-purpose type, and its sensitivity is lower because its septa are thicker", "B": "X is the general-purpose type, and its thick septa raise sensitivity but degrade energy resolution", "C": "X is the high-resolution type, and its narrow, long holes pass fewer photons per dose", "D": "Y is the high-resolution type, and its wider holes raise sensitivity at identical resolution"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (X is the high-resolution type, and its narrow, long holes pass fewer photons per dose): Resolution worsens with distance for both collimators, and the curve that stays lower belongs to the high-resolution design. That design uses longer, narrower channels, which accept fewer photons and so reduce sensitivity, forcing longer acquisitions or more dose. The general-purpose collimator trades resolution for roughly more counts through wider holes. The tempting alternatives swap the labels or reverse the sensitivity trade.", "image": "question_images/q_724.png"}, {"id": 725, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A technologist calibrates a vial at 08:00 with 33 mCi of Tc-99m in 3 mL. At 11:00 she draws 0.91 mL, the volume that would give 10 mCi at 08:00, without correcting for decay. About how much does the patient receive, and what is the likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "About 10 mCi, because 3 hours is under one half-life so decay does not matter", "B": "About 7.1 mCi, nearly 30 percent below the prescription, which risks a count-poor study", "C": "About 5 mCi, half of the prescription, because the activity has halved since 08:00", "D": "About 14.1 mCi, 41 percent above the prescription, because decay raises the activity per milliliter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 7.1 mCi, nearly 30 percent below the prescription, which risks a count-poor study): After 3 hours the activity per milliliter has fallen by 2 to the power of minus 3 over 6.01, a factor of 0.71. The concentration is 33 times 0.71 divided by 3, or about 7.8 mCi/mL, so 0.91 mL gives about 7.1 mCi. That is 29 percent low, outside the 20 percent tolerance, and the study may be count poor. The other values come from reversing the correction, ignoring decay, or using a full half-life."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man with progressive dyspnea has a stress SPECT with normal LV perfusion and an LVEF of 61 percent. Tracer uptake in the right ventricle approaches the intensity of the left ventricle on both stress and rest images. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal variant because the right ventricle is commonly visible on stress studies.", "B": "Report right ventricular infarction from right coronary occlusion and arrange urgent coronary angiography.", "C": "Repeat imaging at half the dose to reduce the apparent right ventricular tracer uptake.", "D": "Recommend echocardiography with estimated pulmonary pressures to assess right ventricular overload."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recommend echocardiography with estimated pulmonary pressures to assess right ventricular overload.): Right ventricular uptake is usually much fainter than left ventricular uptake, so an intensity approaching the left side suggests hypertrophy from pressure or volume load, such as pulmonary hypertension. An infarcted right ventricle would show reduced, not increased, uptake. Echocardiography can confirm the cause and estimate pulmonary pressure. Changing the dose does not alter physiologic uptake ratios."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old diabetic woman with angina has Rb-82 PET showing uniform relative perfusion and a global flow reserve of 1.6. Coronary CT angiography showed smooth arteries without stenosis. What best explains the findings on PET? Her LVEF is normal. She takes no nitrates.", "options": {"A": "Left main stenosis causing global hypoperfusion despite smooth arteries seen on CT angiography.", "B": "Balanced three-vessel ischemia from severe epicardial stenoses in all three coronary arteries.", "C": "Misregistration of stress and rest frames lowering the computed myocardial flow reserve value reported.", "D": "Coronary microvascular dysfunction causing reduced flow reserve despite smooth epicardial arteries."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coronary microvascular dysfunction causing reduced flow reserve despite smooth epicardial arteries.): Diabetes and angina with smooth arteries and a low flow reserve point to microvascular disease, where small vessels fail to dilate normally. Balanced multivessel or left main disease would have appeared on CT angiography. Misregistration is a pitfall but needs evidence of motion between frames."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a morning check on a dual-head camera, the intrinsic Tc-99m flood on head 1 is shown in the exhibit. The camera log shows the photopeak window was found centered at 128 keV with a 20 percent width. Stress SPECT with sestamibi is scheduled in thirty minutes. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Recenter the window on 140 keV, repeat the peaking, and reacquire the flood before scanning patients.", "B": "Call for photomultiplier tube replacement, since one failed tube produces a smooth gradient across the detector.", "C": "Run a center of rotation test, since an orbit offset makes count density uneven.", "D": "Clean the collimator face on the bright side, since contamination there causes the gradient."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recenter the window on 140 keV, repeat the peaking, and reacquire the flood before scanning patients.): The flood shows a smooth change in count density across the field, and the window is centered well below the 140 keV photopeak, so tube responses are sampled unevenly. Recentering and repeating the peaking is the logical correction. A failed tube would make a round spot, a center of rotation error affects reconstruction rather than a planar flood, and contamination gives a focal artifact.", "image": "question_images/q_728.png"}, {"id": 729, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist selects a 20 percent energy window centered at 70 keV for a Tc-99m sestamibi myocardial SPECT acquisition because the previous patient was a thallium study. The photopeak should have been centered at 140 keV. What is the expected result on the images?", "options": {"A": "Sharper images, because accepting more low-energy photons improves spatial resolution.", "B": "Higher contrast with half the counts, because the narrow window rejects most scatter.", "C": "No change, because both windows include a share of the Tc-99m primary photons.", "D": "Mostly scatter is accepted, giving count-poor, low contrast images."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mostly scatter is accepted, giving count-poor, low contrast images.): A window at 63 to 77 keV excludes the 140 keV photopeak, so it accepts mainly scattered photons and some lead x-rays rather than primary photons. The result is low counts and poor contrast with blurred borders. Resolution improves only when scatter is rejected, not accepted."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man had a drug-eluting stent in the LAD 18 months ago and has been free of symptoms since, with good exercise tolerance. His cardiologist requests an annual regadenoson perfusion study. What is the most likely consequence of ordering routine annual imaging in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Few useful findings, with false positives that can prompt unnecessary invasive procedures and radiation", "B": "Specific identification of stent thrombosis, since stents produce perfusion defects specific to that event", "C": "Lower radiation in future years, since prior images allow later studies at reduced dose", "D": "Reliable detection of silent restenosis, which improves survival in asymptomatic drug-eluting stent patients"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Few useful findings, with false positives that can prompt unnecessary invasive procedures and radiation): For an asymptomatic patient less than 2 years after PCI, routine stress imaging is rated inappropriate by appropriate use criteria. Surveillance does not improve outcomes and can generate false positives and unneeded catheterization, with added radiation. Prior images do not reduce later doses."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man with an LVEF of 25% and a dilated left ventricle is undergoing rest Rb-82 PET. The technologist wants to start the static image 70 seconds after the infusion begins. What is the best choice for the start time?", "options": {"A": "Start at 70 seconds, since Rb-82 decays quickly with a 76 second half-life.", "B": "Wait 8 to 10 minutes for the blood pool to clear.", "C": "Start at about 2 to 3 minutes, because poor EF slows blood pool clearance.", "D": "Start at 30 seconds with gating off to catch peak myocardial uptake."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start at about 2 to 3 minutes, because poor EF slows blood pool clearance.): Rb-82 has a half-life of 76 seconds, so imaging cannot wait long. A low EF slows clearance of tracer from the blood pool, so imaging starts at about 2 to 3 minutes rather than the usual 90 seconds. Starting at 30 seconds images blood pool. Waiting 8 to 10 minutes leaves almost no counts."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old woman with large breasts and no coronary history has Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT with mildly reduced anterior and anterolateral counts at stress and rest. Gated thickening and motion are normal, and the LVEF is 66 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Probable breast attenuation, so report a normal study without ischemia or scar.", "B": "Reversible left anterior descending ischemia, so refer for angiography.", "C": "Fixed anterior scar from prior infarction, so start secondary prevention and cardiac rehabilitation.", "D": "Technical failure from low tracer dose, so repeat with a higher dose next week."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Probable breast attenuation, so report a normal study without ischemia or scar.): A mild anterior defect that is identical at stress and rest, with normal thickening and a normal EF in a woman with no coronary history, fits breast attenuation. Scar would typically reduce thickening and motion, and ischemia would change between stress and rest. A larger dose does not remove soft tissue absorption."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A nuclear technologist's annual total effective dose equivalent from her badge records is 5.3 rem (53 mSv) for the calendar year, and no declared pregnancy applies. The laboratory manager asks what this means under NRC occupational limits. What is the correct statement?", "options": {"A": "It exceeds the 5 rem annual limit, so the exposure must be reported to the NRC", "B": "It exceeds the 0.5 rem limit, so she must stop working with radiation permanently", "C": "It is within limits, because the occupational limit for the whole body is 50 rem", "D": "It is within limits, because the occupational limit for the whole body is 15 rem"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It exceeds the 5 rem annual limit, so the exposure must be reported to the NRC): The NRC annual limit for total effective dose equivalent is 5 rem (50 mSv), so 5.3 rem exceeds it, and the licensee must report the overexposure to the NRC. The 15 rem value is the lens limit and 50 rem is the extremity or skin limit. The 0.5 rem value is the declared pregnancy limit and does not apply here."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A reader judges a stress perfusion defect as narrow because it spans only a small arc on the mid short-axis slice. The vertical long-axis slice shows the same anterior wall abnormality running from base to apex. Which method best determines true defect extent?", "options": {"A": "Use the lowest pixel count on the polar map as a percentage of the left ventricle.", "B": "Count involved segments across all slices using the 17-segment model and long axis.", "C": "Measure the widest angular arc on the single mid short-axis slice and report that as extent.", "D": "Count only segments with the lowest score of four, since those define the infarcted zone."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Count involved segments across all slices using the 17-segment model and long axis.): Extent is the amount of myocardium involved, so it is counted as segments across short-axis and long-axis views and expressed against the 17-segment total. A single slice can understate a defect that runs base to apex. Counting only severely reduced segments mixes severity into extent, and a single low pixel value describes depth rather than area."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old diabetic man has a vasodilator SPECT study whose perfusion appears nearly normal. Gated EF is 56 percent at rest but 38 percent post stress, and TID is 1.38. He had no side effects beyond mild flushing. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Low risk, since normal relative perfusion indicates an annual cardiac event rate under 1 percent overall.", "B": "A gating error, so the resting EF should be reported and the post stress value discarded.", "C": "A transient vasodilator effect, so no further action is needed beyond routine outpatient clinical follow up.", "D": "High risk features despite subtle perfusion, suggesting extensive balanced ischemia that warrants angiography."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (High risk features despite subtle perfusion, suggesting extensive balanced ischemia that warrants angiography.): A fall of 18 EF points after stress and an elevated TID point to post stress stunning and transient cavity dilation, which are signs of severe, extensive ischemia that may be masked by balanced flow reduction. These are high risk markers and justify angiography. Normal relative images do not give a low risk label, and flushing does not cause stunning."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A cardiac SPECT with Tc-99m sestamibi shows lower resolution and blurred wall borders than usual. Count rate is higher than expected. The technologist followed the protocol for 180 degree orbit, 64 projections and 64 by 64 matrix. Which setup error is most likely?", "options": {"A": "The orbit was set to 360 degrees, which adds redundant views to every projection.", "B": "A 128 by 128 matrix was used, which spreads counts into too many pixels.", "C": "A medium-energy collimator was mounted instead of the low-energy high-resolution collimator.", "D": "The energy window was set too narrow around 140 keV, so wall borders blurred."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A medium-energy collimator was mounted instead of the low-energy high-resolution collimator.): A medium-energy or general-purpose collimator has thicker septa and wider holes, which raise sensitivity but blur resolution, matching higher counts with poorer detail. A narrow window lowers counts. A larger matrix or a longer orbit does not blur borders at a higher count rate."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During morning quality control, an Rb-82 generator shows Sr-82 breakthrough of 0.025 microcuries per millicurie of Rb-82, while Sr-85 breakthrough is within its limit. The label expiration limit for Sr-82 is 0.02 microcuries per millicurie. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue patient studies because the Sr-85 breakthrough result is within its own limit", "B": "Remove the generator from patient use and notify the radiation safety officer", "C": "Limit use to patients over 70 so lifetime bone dose risk stays low", "D": "Elute a large saline volume as waste and retest, then resume use if the value falls"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the generator from patient use and notify the radiation safety officer): Sr-82 breakthrough above the expiration limit means the generator can deliver long-lived strontium to bone marrow, so it must be removed from patient use. Notifying the radiation safety officer and vendor documents the event and arranges disposal. Passing the Sr-85 check does not offset the Sr-82 failure, and flushing or age restrictions do not restore an expired generator."}, {"id": 738, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A 62 year old woman receiving doxorubicin for breast cancer needs a baseline MUGA. Her last study showed gastric and thyroid activity from poor red cell labeling. Which labeling method is the best choice to give reliable blood pool imaging this time?", "options": {"A": "In vitro labeling with a commercial kit, which gives labeling efficiency above 95 percent", "B": "In vivo labeling with a doubled pyrophosphate dose, since more tin offsets interfering drugs", "C": "Modified in vivo labeling with a 15 minute pyrophosphate delay, which is as reliable here", "D": "Standard in vivo labeling with pyrophosphate, since doxorubicin improves tin uptake in cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (In vitro labeling with a commercial kit, which gives labeling efficiency above 95 percent): In vitro labeling removes red cells from the plasma and from circulating drugs before technetium is added, with efficiency above 95 percent, whereas in vivo labeling gives about 75 to 80 percent and modified in vivo about 85 to 90 percent. Doxorubicin is among the drugs that reduce in vivo labeling. A longer delay, a larger pyrophosphate dose, or assuming the drug helps does not remove the interference."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman has stress SPECT. On short axis slices normalized to each slice's own maximum, an inferior defect looks severe. A polar map with whole heart normalization places the same segments at about 65 percent of peak. How should severity be graded?", "options": {"A": "Grade it as severe, since visual contrast on the slices is the primary severity measure.", "B": "Grade it as normal, since counts above 50 percent of peak cannot be abnormal on SPECT.", "C": "Grade it as absent uptake, because any dark slice is below 10 percent of peak.", "D": "Grade it as mildly reduced using the polar map, since slice normalization exaggerates defects."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grade it as mildly reduced using the polar map, since slice normalization exaggerates defects.): Normalizing each slice to its own maximum scales a segment to a different reference on every image and can make a mild reduction look severe. Severity belongs to count relative to a normal reference on the whole heart. At about 65 percent it is a mild reduction, not normal and not severe."}, {"id": 740, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old woman with stable exertional chest pain exercises for 7 minutes on the Bruce protocol, with 2 mm of horizontal ST depression and exercise limiting angina. Use the Duke treadmill score formula, minutes minus 5 times ST depression minus 4 times angina index. What is the score and its best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Score of minus 7, intermediate risk, favoring medical therapy with later reassessment.", "B": "Score of plus 9, low risk, favoring discharge on current medical therapy.", "C": "Score of minus 11, high risk, favoring early coronary evaluation rather than reassurance.", "D": "Score of minus 3, intermediate risk, favoring imaging with no urgent referral."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Score of minus 11, high risk, favoring early coronary evaluation rather than reassurance.): Exercise limiting angina has an index of 2. The score is 7 minus 5 times 2 minus 4 times 2, which equals 7 minus 10 minus 8, or minus 11. A score at or below minus 11 is high risk. Omitting the angina term gives minus 3, using angina index 1 gives minus 7, and adding the ST depression gives plus 9, so each wrong value has a nameable arithmetic cause."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man has polar maps after regadenoson sestamibi SPECT for dyspnea. The anterior wall is at the top and the septum at the left. Reduced stress counts occupy the top and left and normalize at rest, with a stress score of 12 and a rest score of 0. Which coronary territory best matches this reversible pattern?", "options": {"A": "Posterior descending artery from a left dominant system, supplying the entire inferior wall.", "B": "Left circumflex artery, supplying the lateral wall and part of the inferolateral wall.", "C": "Right coronary artery, supplying the inferior wall and the posterior septum.", "D": "Left anterior descending artery, supplying the anterior wall, anterior septum and apex."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left anterior descending artery, supplying the anterior wall, anterior septum and apex.): The top of the polar map is anterior and the left side is septal, so a reversible defect there involves the anterior wall, anterior septum and apex, which the left anterior descending artery supplies. The summed difference score of 12 is about 18 percent of the maximum of 68. The inferior wall is at the bottom of the map and belongs to the right coronary or posterior descending artery, while the lateral wall sits on the right and is the circumflex territory.", "image": "question_images/q_741.png"}, {"id": 742, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A laboratory injects the same 30 mCi (1110 MBq) Tc-99m sestamibi stress dose into every patient, from a 55 kg woman to a 130 kg man, with unchanged imaging time. Which change most reduces patient radiation dose while keeping count density acceptable?", "options": {"A": "Replace Tc-99m sestamibi with Tl-201 in all patients, since thallium delivers a lower effective dose.", "B": "Inject half the dose into everyone with unchanged acquisition time, since counts scale with detector size.", "C": "Scale injected activity to body weight, using longer acquisition or sensitive cameras to preserve counts.", "D": "Raise the dose to 40 mCi for every patient, which shortens acquisition and lowers motion artifact."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Scale injected activity to body weight, using longer acquisition or sensitive cameras to preserve counts.): Fixed dosing overexposes small patients while large patients still get count-poor images. Weight-based activity, combined with longer acquisition or high-sensitivity cadmium-zinc-telluride cameras, keeps counts adequate while lowering dose where possible, and stress-first imaging can omit the rest dose. Raising the dose increases exposure, Tl-201 gives a higher effective dose than Tc-99m agents, and halving activity with the same time halves counts."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A ring badge report shows 85 mrem for the month for a nuclear technologist. The department's trending sheet is kept in mSv and uses a monthly action level of 0.50 mSv for review and corrective action. Which entry and decision are correct?", "options": {"A": "85 mSv, which is well above the action level and exceeds the monthly extremity limit", "B": "0.85 mSv, which is above the action level of 0.50 mSv and needs review", "C": "0.085 mSv, which is below the action level and needs no review", "D": "8.5 mSv, which is far above the action level and needs immediate NRC notification"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0.85 mSv, which is above the action level of 0.50 mSv and needs review): One mSv equals 100 mrem, so 85 mrem is 0.85 mSv. That is above the 0.50 mSv action level, so it is reviewed internally; it is far below any NRC limit and needs no notification. The other values come from wrong powers of ten."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with left bundle branch block has an exercise Tc-99m SPECT showing an isolated septal defect on stress that partly normalizes at rest. The LAD territory is otherwise normal and he has no angina. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Septal infarct, because the defect did not resolve fully at rest.", "B": "Probable LBBB-related septal artifact, so report cautiously and consider pharmacologic stress in future testing.", "C": "Definite left anterior descending ischemia, because any reversible septal defect indicates proximal stenosis.", "D": "Normal study, because bundle branch block does not affect perfusion images at fast heart rates."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Probable LBBB-related septal artifact, so report cautiously and consider pharmacologic stress in future testing.): Left bundle branch block can cause abnormal septal activation and relative septal hypoperfusion at fast heart rates during exercise, producing a reversible septal defect with a normal left anterior descending territory. A vasodilator study avoids the heart rate effect. A true stenosis would typically involve the anterior and apical walls, and scar would be fixed."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "During a regadenoson study, a patient develops wheezing and a seizure risk is noted. The nurse pushes 250 mg of aminophylline IV over 20 seconds and charts only 'aminophylline given'. What is the most likely consequence of this administration error?", "options": {"A": "Ventricular arrhythmia or seizure from a rapid methylxanthine level rise, with no dose or time charted", "B": "Respiratory depression from central adenosine receptor stimulation after the rapid push of the drug", "C": "Persistent hypotension because regadenoson vasodilation continues after the antagonist is given", "D": "Prolonged high grade AV block from added adenosine receptor stimulation by aminophylline"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ventricular arrhythmia or seizure from a rapid methylxanthine level rise, with no dose or time charted): Aminophylline is a methylxanthine that blocks adenosine receptors and reverses regadenoson, but it should be given slowly, usually 50 to 250 mg over 30 to 60 seconds. A rapid bolus can cause tachyarrhythmia, hypotension and seizures, especially when seizure risk exists. The record also lacks dose, route and time. The other options reverse the drug's mechanism."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 78-year-old man with heart failure and a negative serum and urine immunofixation and normal free light chains has a one-hour Tc-99m pyrophosphate planar study. Heart region counts are 7,200 and contralateral chest counts are 4,000. SPECT confirms myocardial uptake. What is the interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Ratio is 1.8, indeterminate, since the positive cutoff for transthyretin amyloid at one hour is 2.0.", "B": "Ratio is 1.8, above the 1.5 cutoff, consistent with transthyretin amyloid after excluding monoclonal protein.", "C": "Ratio is 0.56, below 1.0, so the study is negative for cardiac amyloid at one hour.", "D": "Ratio is 1.8, negative, since the positive cutoff for transthyretin amyloid is 3.0 at one hour."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ratio is 1.8, above the 1.5 cutoff, consistent with transthyretin amyloid after excluding monoclonal protein.): The heart to contralateral ratio is 7,200 / 4,000 = 1.8. At one hour a ratio of 1.5 or more with grade 2 or 3 myocardial uptake confirmed by SPECT supports transthyretin cardiac amyloid when light chain disease is excluded. Inverting the ratio gives 0.56, and the other cutoffs are wrong."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "After an outpatient Tc-99m sestamibi stress study, a patient who lives with a newborn and a pregnant sister asks what precautions to follow at home that evening and the next day. Which advice is appropriate for her to follow today?", "options": {"A": "Take no precautions, because Tc-99m emits only low energy particles with no photons", "B": "Avoid contact with anyone for 3 days, since Sr-82 impurity remains in the body", "C": "Stay in isolation for seven days, as is advised after I-131 therapy", "D": "Limit prolonged close contact with infants and pregnant women on the day of the study"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Limit prolonged close contact with infants and pregnant women on the day of the study): Tc-99m has a 6 hour half-life and emits 140 keV photons, so practical advice is brief: limit prolonged close contact with infants and pregnant women that day. Isolation rules belong to I-131 therapy, Tc-99m does emit photons, and Sr-82 is a Rb-82 generator issue, not a Tc-99m one."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "On a CT-corrected SPECT study in a patient with an ICD, a focal area of increased uptake appears at the septal apex on the corrected images. The uncorrected images show normal uptake there. What is the best interpretation of this finding?", "options": {"A": "Focal inflammation around the lead tip, supported by the finding on corrected images", "B": "Liver scatter into the septal apex, which is removed by choosing a lower filter cutoff", "C": "Photon scatter from the ICD generator into the septal apex, which scatter correction removes", "D": "A metal-lead artifact causing CT overcorrection, so the apparent hot spot is not real uptake"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A metal-lead artifact causing CT overcorrection, so the apparent hot spot is not real uptake): Metal leads produce very high CT numbers that are converted into overestimated attenuation coefficients, so counts near the lead are overcorrected and create a false hot spot. The uncorrected images are normal there, which confirms the artifact. Reviewing both image sets and the CT slices avoids misreading it. Hyperemia and inflammation would not appear only after correction, and liver scatter is not focal at the apical septum."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A SPECT/CT laboratory wants to add coronary artery calcium scoring to a stress study. A technologist proposes scoring from the free-breathing attenuation correction CT already acquired. What is the best protocol for an accurate Agatston score? The patient is 58 years old.", "options": {"A": "Acquire a contrast-enhanced CT angiogram and calculate the Agatston score from it.", "B": "Acquire a dedicated ECG-gated noncontrast CT at 120 kVp with 3 mm slices and Agatston scoring.", "C": "Use the free-breathing attenuation correction CT because its slice thickness gives equal accuracy.", "D": "Acquire the scan at 80 kVp with 5 mm slices to reduce radiation exposure."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acquire a dedicated ECG-gated noncontrast CT at 120 kVp with 3 mm slices and Agatston scoring.): The Agatston score is validated for noncontrast, ECG-gated acquisitions at 120 kVp with 3 mm slices. Free-breathing, low-dose, thick-slice attenuation scans blur and misplace calcium and can underestimate the score. Contrast attenuation obscures calcium scoring, and changing voltage or thickness alters the density weighting."}, {"id": 750, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A busy clinic swaps its low-energy high-resolution collimator for a low-energy general-purpose collimator on Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT to shorten scan time per patient. A referring physician asks how image quality will change as a result of the swap. What is the correct statement about the change?", "options": {"A": "Counts rise from higher sensitivity, but spatial resolution is worse and myocardial borders are blurrier.", "B": "Sensitivity stays the same and resolution improves, since larger holes accept photons from fewer angles.", "C": "Septal penetration is reduced for Tc-99m, so contrast improves despite the higher count rate.", "D": "Counts and spatial resolution both improve, since larger holes and fewer septa admit more useful photons."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Counts rise from higher sensitivity, but spatial resolution is worse and myocardial borders are blurrier.): General-purpose collimators have shorter or wider holes, which accept more photons and raise sensitivity but widen the response function and lower spatial resolution. High-resolution collimators accept fewer photons and give sharper images. The trade-off is fundamental, and Tc-99m has little septal penetration with either design."}, {"id": 751, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with a large bra cup size and atypical chest pain has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT with an ejection fraction of 60 percent. The anterior wall counts look mildly reduced to the same degree on stress and rest maps, and the summed stress and rest scores are both 0. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Reversible anterior ischemia, so refer directly for left anterior descending angiography.", "B": "Normal apical thinning, so no further assessment or comment in the report is needed.", "C": "Probable breast attenuation artifact, matched at stress and rest, to confirm with gated thickening.", "D": "Fixed anterior scar in the left anterior descending territory, so plan secondary prevention and revascularization."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Probable breast attenuation artifact, matched at stress and rest, to confirm with gated thickening.): A mild anterior count reduction that is the same at stress and rest, in a woman with large breasts, is the classic setting for breast attenuation artifact. The summed scores of 0, a normal ejection fraction and normal wall thickening support an artifact, and attenuation correction or prone images can confirm it. Calling it scar or ischemia risks needless angiography. Apical thinning would not explain a reduced anterior wall.", "image": "question_images/q_751.png"}, {"id": 752, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with a known occluded LAD and prior anterior infarction is being assessed for revascularization. Stress Tl-201 shows an anterior defect that, as the chart shows, stays nearly unchanged at 4 hours. He lives far away and cannot return tomorrow. What is the best step before he leaves?", "options": {"A": "Reinject a small Tl-201 dose at rest and image again to assess residual viability", "B": "Double the 4 hour acquisition time to increase anterior wall counts", "C": "Image again at 8 hours since redistribution continues at the same rate without reinjection", "D": "Report the anterior wall as nonviable scar because the defect did not redistribute"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reinject a small Tl-201 dose at rest and image again to assess residual viability): A fixed defect on 4 hour Tl-201 images can still be viable myocardium with severely reduced resting flow and slow redistribution. A rest reinjection of about 1 mCi raises blood-pool thallium and often fills defects that are viable, and it can be done the same day. Calling the defect scar, imaging longer, or waiting 8 hours would not answer the viability question for this patient.", "image": "question_images/q_752.png"}, {"id": 753, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man took metoprolol succinate 100 mg this morning before a treadmill study. He reached 78 percent of age-predicted maximal heart rate with no symptoms or ST change, and the SPECT perfusion images are normal. How should the result be reported?", "options": {"A": "Normal perfusion with a nondiagnostic stress ECG, since beta blockade limited the heart rate", "B": "Nondiagnostic perfusion study because the target of 85 percent of maximal heart rate was missed", "C": "Fully diagnostic study, since perfusion imaging is independent of the heart rate the patient achieved", "D": "Falsely negative study that must be repeated off beta blockers before any report is issued"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Normal perfusion with a nondiagnostic stress ECG, since beta blockade limited the heart rate): Beta blockers blunt heart rate and may lower sensitivity of exercise perfusion imaging, and failure to reach 85 percent of predicted rate makes the ECG nondiagnostic for ischemia. The images themselves remain interpretable, so normal perfusion is reported with that limitation stated. Calling the study fully diagnostic hides the limitation, and labeling the images nondiagnostic or demanding a repeat overreaches."}, {"id": 754, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70 year old man is about to receive regadenoson. The chart shows his systolic pressure after standing from supine. He reports mild lightheadedness on standing. If regadenoson is given without addressing this finding, what is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "Severe bronchospasm, since A2B receptor activation is the dominant response in older men", "B": "Seizure, since low standing pressure lowers the threshold for A2A related neurologic events", "C": "Symptomatic hypotension or syncope, since vasodilators lower pressure further", "D": "Complete heart block, since regadenoson acts mainly on the AV node at this blood pressure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Symptomatic hypotension or syncope, since vasodilators lower pressure further): The chart shows a fall in systolic pressure of more than 40 mm Hg after standing, which suggests orthostatic hypotension or volume depletion. Regadenoson causes systemic vasodilation and can lower pressure by 5 to 10 mm Hg or more, so a patient already near the limit may faint or become hypotensive. Bronchospasm, block and seizure are possible but unrelated to this finding.", "image": "question_images/q_754.png"}, {"id": 755, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On a stress sestamibi study the inferior wall looks severely reduced, while a bright loop of bowel lies directly against the inferior wall. Gated thickening is normal and the rest study, with little bowel activity, is normal. Reconstruction used filtered backprojection. Which cause best explains the inferior defect?", "options": {"A": "Ramp filter over subtraction beside intense bowel activity; delayed imaging may resolve it.", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, since inferior counts fall when the diaphragm overlies the inferior wall.", "C": "Reversible RCA ischemia, because a defect present only on stress must be flow limiting.", "D": "Upward creep of the heart during the acquisition, which shifts counts toward the abdomen."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ramp filter over subtraction beside intense bowel activity; delayed imaging may resolve it.): Filtered backprojection uses a ramp filter that creates negative lobes around very hot structures, subtracting counts from neighboring myocardium. Intense bowel beside the inferior wall can produce a dark halo that mimics a defect, and the rest study with little bowel activity looks normal. Attenuation would persist on both scans, ischemia would reduce thickening, and creep shows on raw projection images as a vertical shift."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with left bundle branch block has treadmill sestamibi SPECT, reaching a heart rate of 145. There is an isolated reversible septal defect, no angina and a normal EF. If this is reported as LAD ischemia, what error has occurred?", "options": {"A": "An attenuation error, since LBBB moves the septum away from the detector during the exercise.", "B": "A false negative result, since LBBB masks real septal ischemia at faster exercise heart rates.", "C": "A correct report, because a reversible septal defect indicates a stenosis in the LAD.", "D": "A false positive, since LBBB causes septal defects with exercise; use vasodilator stress."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A false positive, since LBBB causes septal defects with exercise; use vasodilator stress.): In LBBB, abnormal septal activation and relaxation reduce septal counts, mainly with higher exercise heart rates, giving a reversible septal defect without LAD disease. Vasodilator stress avoids this because it does not raise heart rate much. Calling the finding LAD ischemia produces a false positive."}, {"id": 757, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A cardiology laboratory adds F-18 FDG viability imaging to its Tc-99m practice and keeps using its 3 mm lead syringe shields, which stop 140 keV photons well. After two weeks, a technologist's ring badge reads three times its usual value. What is the most appropriate corrective action?", "options": {"A": "Stack a 3 mm lead layer on every shield to restore the attenuation seen with Tc-99m.", "B": "Replace lead with plastic shields, since plastic blocks positrons and therefore stops the annihilation photons.", "C": "Report the tripled ring reading to the NRC as an overexposure of the technologist.", "D": "Switch to tungsten syringe shields and injection shielding built for 511 keV photons, then recheck rings."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Switch to tungsten syringe shields and injection shielding built for 511 keV photons, then recheck rings.): The half-value layer of lead is about 0.3 mm at 140 keV but about 4 mm at 511 keV, so 3 mm of lead gives under one half-value layer for FDG photons and offers little protection. Tungsten syringe shields and PET-rated injection stations raise attenuation, and ring readings are then rechecked. Plastic stops positrons but not the penetrating annihilation photons, and a tripled reading far below the 500 mSv limit is not a reportable overexposure."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The processing exhibit shows a reorientation record for a rest Tc-99m SPECT study. A reviewing cardiologist notes that the short-axis slices look oval and the apical slice shows a crescent shape. What is the best next step before the study is sent for final interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Apply a lower Butterworth cutoff so the oval slices become round after additional smoothing", "B": "Redo the reorientation along the apex to mitral valve axis, then confirm round slices", "C": "Accept the manual axis because operator edits override automated detection and the slices are usable", "D": "Reacquire the study with a larger matrix so that the apical slice contains more counts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Redo the reorientation along the apex to mitral valve axis, then confirm round slices): Short-axis slices should be round or donut shaped when the long axis is aligned along the true LV axis from apex to mitral valve center. Oval slices and a crescent apex mean the axis was set at the wrong angle, which can create artificial septal or lateral differences. Filtering cannot repair geometry and a new acquisition is unnecessary, because the raw data are intact. Reorient and verify the slices.", "image": "question_images/q_758.png"}, {"id": 759, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "After a service visit, the daily constancy check on a dose calibrator reads 14% above the decay-corrected expected value for the reference source. A resting Tc-99m dose of 10 mCi is scheduled in 20 minutes. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the dose and recheck constancy weekly, since a 14% deviation is a minor drift", "B": "Dispense the dose after subtracting 14% from every reading to correct the displayed value", "C": "Remove it from use until repaired or replaced and retested; use a backup calibrator", "D": "Dispense the dose after repeating accuracy testing with a second source next month"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove it from use until repaired or replaced and retested; use a backup calibrator): A constancy error well beyond the allowed 10% shows that dose readings cannot be trusted, and patient doses must be measured on an instrument that works. The unit should be taken out of service, repaired or replaced and retested, with a backup calibrator used meanwhile. Stretching the check interval or applying a guessed offset does not meet the requirement, and deferring a retest to next month keeps an unreliable device in use."}, {"id": 760, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with BMI 43, controlled mild asthma and knee arthritis cannot exercise and needs stress testing for dyspnea evaluation. A cardiac PET scanner and a SPECT camera are both available on site, and she is not claustrophobic. Which strategy is best?", "options": {"A": "Dobutamine SPECT, since regadenoson cannot be given safely to a patient who has asthma.", "B": "Same day Tc-99m SPECT with a larger dose, accepting the attenuation limits of the camera.", "C": "Exercise sestamibi SPECT after a short walking trial, since exercise is the first stress choice.", "D": "Rb-82 PET with regadenoson, since attenuation correction and count quality outperform SPECT in obesity."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rb-82 PET with regadenoson, since attenuation correction and count quality outperform SPECT in obesity.): In severe obesity, PET gives better attenuation correction, higher counts and lower radiation than SPECT. Regadenoson is acceptable in mild controlled asthma. She cannot exercise, and increasing SPECT dose does not remove attenuation. Dobutamine is less well tolerated and is not required, since regadenoson is not contraindicated by controlled asthma."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient with severely reduced EF has rest sestamibi counts of 38 percent of peak in the anterior wall but FDG uptake of 80 percent of peak in the same segments. How should this perfusion and metabolism pattern be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "A perfusion FDG match indicating transmural scar with no expected recovery of regional function.", "B": "A mismatch indicating stunning only, which would exclude any benefit from revascularization.", "C": "A reverse mismatch typical of left bundle branch block, which has no viability meaning here.", "D": "A perfusion FDG mismatch showing hibernating viable myocardium that sestamibi counts alone understated."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A perfusion FDG mismatch showing hibernating viable myocardium that sestamibi counts alone understated.): Reduced perfusion with preserved glucose metabolism defines a mismatch, which represents hibernating myocardium that may recover after revascularization. Sestamibi uptake near 38 percent understates viability in this setting. A match signals scar, stunning implies normal flow, and a reverse mismatch has preserved perfusion with reduced FDG, which is not this pattern."}, {"id": 762, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with new high-grade AV block and an echocardiogram showing basal septal thinning is suspected to have cardiac sarcoidosis. She is referred for F-18 FDG PET with a rest perfusion scan. The ordering physician asks how to prepare her. Which preparation is best?", "options": {"A": "A high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet for two meals, then a 12 to 18 hour fast", "B": "A clear liquid diet for 24 hours with an insulin infusion just before the scan", "C": "A high-carbohydrate meal one hour before injection, to increase myocardial glucose uptake", "D": "A normal diet with a 4 hour fast and a glucose load just before FDG injection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet for two meals, then a 12 to 18 hour fast): Inflammatory imaging requires suppression of normal myocyte FDG uptake, which is done by a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet plus a prolonged fast so myocardium switches to fatty acids. Glucose loading and insulin enhance myocyte uptake and are used for viability, where uptake is the goal, so they would hide focal inflammatory uptake."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "During a stress study, the dose calibrator reads 32.0 mCi in the syringe before injection and 2.0 mCi in the empty syringe after injection of Tc-99m sestamibi. Staff must document the administered activity in both units. What is the administered activity?", "options": {"A": "30.0 mCi, which equals 1,110 MBq administered to the patient", "B": "34.0 mCi, which equals 1,258 MBq, found by adding the residual to the pre-injection reading", "C": "30.0 mCi, which equals 810 MBq using a conversion of 27 MBq per mCi", "D": "32.0 mCi, which equals 1,184 MBq, since the pre-injection assay is the recorded dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (30.0 mCi, which equals 1,110 MBq administered to the patient): Administered activity is the pre-injection assay minus the residual: 32.0 minus 2.0 is 30.0 mCi. Converting with 37 MBq per mCi gives 1,110 MBq. Adding the residual overstates the dose, using only the pre-injection value ignores retained activity, and 27 MBq per mCi is the wrong conversion factor."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A patient has no symptoms 14 months after PCI and asks for routine surveillance perfusion imaging because a friend had a silent restenosis. A colleague says insurance will cover it. What does the appropriate use approach say about this request?", "options": {"A": "It is appropriate if nuclear imaging is chosen over treadmill ECG, since imaging has higher yield", "B": "It is appropriate yearly, since silent in-stent restenosis is common and should be screened for", "C": "Routine stress imaging soon after PCI in an asymptomatic patient is rarely appropriate", "D": "It is rarely appropriate until 5 years after PCI, since that interval applies to any stent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Routine stress imaging soon after PCI in an asymptomatic patient is rarely appropriate): Appropriate use criteria rate routine stress testing in asymptomatic patients within 2 years of PCI as rarely appropriate. The 5 year interval applies to bypass surgery. Imaging a person without new symptoms rarely changes management, and screening for silent restenosis has not improved outcomes."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The annual calibration sticker on a Geiger-Mueller survey meter is dated 5 months ago. At the start of the day, a long-lived reference source reads 40% below its expected value on the same geometry, and the battery check passes. A technologist wants to survey a Tc-99m elution area. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Do not use the meter; tag it out of service and have it repaired and recalibrated", "B": "Use it, since the annual calibration is current and the source reading is a nuisance check", "C": "Replace the battery, then use it for the survey if the source reads above 50%", "D": "Use it and multiply each reading by 1.67 to compensate for the low response"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not use the meter; tag it out of service and have it repaired and recalibrated): Survey meters must be calibrated annually and after repair, and a daily operational check with a reference source confirms they still respond. A 40% deficit shows the instrument is not working properly even though the calibration is nominally current, so it cannot be trusted for exposure or contamination surveys. A made-up correction factor is not a calibration. The battery already passed its check, so changing it does not address a response error."}, {"id": 766, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "Under the linear no-threshold model, a nominal cancer risk coefficient of 5 percent per sievert is applied to a patient who receives a 20 mSv myocardial perfusion study. What lifetime cancer risk does the model estimate for this single study alone?", "options": {"A": "No increased risk, since doses below 100 mSv carry zero risk under the model", "B": "About 1 percent, or roughly 1 in 100", "C": "About 0.01 percent, or roughly 1 in 10,000", "D": "About 0.1 percent, or roughly 1 in 1,000"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 0.1 percent, or roughly 1 in 1,000): Twenty millisieverts is 0.02 Sv, and 0.02 times 5 percent per Sv is 0.1 percent, about 1 in 1,000. The linear no-threshold model assumes risk is proportional to dose with no threshold, so low doses still carry a small estimated risk. A one in 100 answer uses a 0.2 Sv dose, and one in 10,000 uses a coefficient ten times too small."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and risk factors has Rb-82 PET/CT showing normal relative perfusion and normal flow reserve, but a coronary calcium score of 1,240 Agatston on the attenuation CT. His LDL cholesterol is 150 mg/dL. How should this discordance be explained?", "options": {"A": "Calcium blooming on the attenuation CT falsely inflates the score, so the score can be ignored.", "B": "CT and PET misregistration causes a falsely normal perfusion result, so the study must be repeated.", "C": "Balanced three vessel ischemia is the only explanation, so angiography should be scheduled.", "D": "Extensive nonobstructive atherosclerosis can calcify without flow limitation, so risk stays high."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extensive nonobstructive atherosclerosis can calcify without flow limitation, so risk stays high.): Calcium score reflects total atherosclerotic burden, not flow limitation. A high score with normal perfusion means extensive disease that has not yet narrowed lumens enough to limit flow, which still carries elevated risk and justifies intensive prevention. Balanced ischemia needs other clues, and misregistration produces defects, not falsely normal perfusion."}, {"id": 768, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with atypical chest discomfort has a normal rest and regadenoson stress SPECT study. A noncontrast CT used for attenuation correction gives an Agatston calcium score of 1,200. His LDL cholesterol is 150 mg/dL. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Start high-intensity statin therapy, since normal perfusion does not exclude extensive coronary plaque.", "B": "Reassure him and defer lipid treatment, since normal perfusion shows his coronary arteries are healthy.", "C": "Repeat the study with Rb-82 PET to look for balanced ischemia in all three coronary territories.", "D": "Refer for invasive angiography, because a calcium score above 400 mandates catheterization despite normal perfusion."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start high-intensity statin therapy, since normal perfusion does not exclude extensive coronary plaque.): Perfusion imaging detects flow-limiting disease, while calcium score measures total atherosclerotic burden. A score of 1,200 is very high risk for events even with normal perfusion, so preventive therapy is the key response. No guideline requires angiography for a score alone, and nothing in the stem suggests balanced ischemia."}, {"id": 769, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During treadmill stress without a vasodilator, a 57-year-old woman develops typical angina and 2 mm of horizontal ST depression at 3 minutes, and stops at 5 minutes. Her SPECT perfusion images are normal. The reader is finalizing the report. Which approach is correct?", "options": {"A": "Call the study abnormal on ECG criteria alone and recommend revascularization from the ST depression.", "B": "Omit the ECG findings from the report, because perfusion is definitive and overrides the stress ECG.", "C": "Reclassify the normal perfusion as an artifact and recommend repeating the whole study tomorrow.", "D": "Report the ischemic ECG response with normal perfusion, noting balanced ischemia or false positive."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the ischemic ECG response with normal perfusion, noting balanced ischemia or false positive.): The stress ECG and perfusion images answer different questions, and discordance must be stated, not resolved by ignoring one. ST depression with typical symptoms carries prognostic weight and can occur with balanced multivessel ischemia or microvascular disease, yet it is also frequently a false positive in women. A complete report presents both and recommends clinical correlation rather than discarding either."}, {"id": 770, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "The exhibit shows a requisition for Tc-99m SPECT myocardial perfusion imaging. The referring cardiologist asks that the study be done this week. Her pretest probability of obstructive coronary disease is low and she can exercise to an adequate workload. Which course best matches the protocol to this patient?", "options": {"A": "Perform a rest-only Tl-201 study, because thallium crosses the placenta less than technetium agents.", "B": "Proceed with a stress-first Tc-99m sestamibi protocol, since the fetal dose is below any harmful threshold.", "C": "Defer radionuclide imaging for stress echocardiography; the findings do not justify fetal exposure.", "D": "Obtain a Rb-82 PET study with a lead apron over the abdomen against 511 keV photons."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer radionuclide imaging for stress echocardiography; the findings do not justify fetal exposure.): With a positive pregnancy test and a low-risk presentation, the benefit of a radionuclide study does not outweigh avoidable fetal exposure, so a non-ionizing test such as stress echocardiography is preferred. Technetium doses are small but not zero, and Tl-201 has a long half-life and gives a higher dose. A standard apron does not meaningfully attenuate 511 keV photons, so it provides no fetal protection in PET.", "image": "question_images/q_770.png"}, {"id": 771, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man is referred for exercise SPECT for exertional dizziness. Examination shows a late-peaking systolic ejection murmur radiating to both carotids, a soft second heart sound and slow carotid upstrokes. He reports near fainting while climbing stairs last week. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with a gentle Naughton exercise protocol, stopping at the first symptom or blood pressure drop.", "B": "Cancel the stress study today and obtain an echocardiogram to assess aortic valve severity.", "C": "Switch to regadenoson, since pharmacologic stress does not depend on exertion or workload capacity.", "D": "Proceed with low-dose dobutamine, since inotropy is easier to titrate than treadmill effort in this patient."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cancel the stress study today and obtain an echocardiogram to assess aortic valve severity.): The examination suggests severe aortic stenosis, and exertional presyncope makes exercise hazardous. Vasodilators and dobutamine can also cause hypotension or arrhythmia when outflow is fixed. Defining valve severity first answers the real question, and stress testing can be reconsidered afterward if still needed."}, {"id": 772, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A reader reviews gated SPECT in a 74-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation and an irregular ventricular rate averaging 95, acquired with a standard 20 percent R-R window and 8 frames per cycle. Which statement best describes how gating handles the irregular rhythm?", "options": {"A": "Beats outside the window are rejected, lowering counts and reliability of ejection fraction.", "B": "A wide window eliminates the effect of irregularity on the reported ejection fraction.", "C": "Gating is disabled automatically, and ejection fraction is derived from ungated images.", "D": "All beats are summed whatever their length, so accuracy equals that of sinus rhythm."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Beats outside the window are rejected, lowering counts and reliability of ejection fraction.): Gating sorts beats into frames according to the R-R interval, and beats outside the window are rejected. In atrial fibrillation many beats are rejected, counts fall and the ejection fraction is less reliable. Wide windows admit irregular beats and blur end-systole."}, {"id": 773, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On a Rb-82 PET/CT study of a 66-year-old woman with atypical chest pain, a new lateral wall defect appears at both rest and stress. On the fused display the lateral left ventricular edge extends 12 mm into the lung field of the CT. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Check the fusion, manually realign CT and emission data, and reprocess before reading the lateral wall.", "B": "Lower the CT tube current on a repeat scan to reduce lung overcorrection of counts.", "C": "Discard the emission data and repeat the injection and acquisition after a one hour delay.", "D": "Report lateral wall infarction, since matched rest and stress defects indicate fixed scar on both scans."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check the fusion, manually realign CT and emission data, and reprocess before reading the lateral wall.): When the heart overlaps low density lung on the CT, attenuation correction undercorrects that wall and produces a false defect, usually anterior or lateral. Review of the fusion and manual realignment fixes it without more injected activity or radiation. Matched defects can still be artifact, and tube current is not the cause."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A cardiac PET laboratory's Rb-82 generator delivers a calibrated bolus of 1,480 MBq (40 mCi) at the end of elution. A tubing kink delays the patient injection until 150 seconds after that calibration time. Using a half-life of 76 seconds, what activity does the patient actually receive?", "options": {"A": "About 740 MBq (20 mCi), because one half-life has elapsed during the delay", "B": "About 370 MBq (10 mCi), because about two half-lives of 76 seconds each have elapsed", "C": "About 185 MBq (5 mCi), because three half-lives have elapsed during the delay", "D": "About 1,110 MBq (30 mCi), because only 25 percent decays per half-life"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 370 MBq (10 mCi), because about two half-lives of 76 seconds each have elapsed): Rb-82 has a half-life of about 76 seconds, so 150 seconds is about two half-lives (1.97). Activity falls to one half and then one quarter: 1,480 times 0.25 equals 370 MBq (10 mCi). A single half-life gives 740 MBq, three half-lives gives 185 MBq, and removing 25 percent per half-life gives the wrong 1,110 MBq. Each wrong value comes from misusing the half-life, which is why the delay is a real dose and image-quality problem."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The exhibit summarizes a Rb-82 PET stress and rest study reconstructed with CT-based attenuation correction. The lateral wall shows a mild to moderate defect on AC images that is absent on non-AC images. What is the best explanation and the next step?", "options": {"A": "Patient motion during the PET emission scan; discard the stress data and repeat the stress study", "B": "True lateral ischemia unmasked by correction; report an LCx territory defect and recommend angiography", "C": "CT-emission misregistration placing the lateral wall over lung; realign the CT and reconstruct", "D": "Under-dosing of Rb-82 from generator breakthrough; repeat the study with a freshly eluted generator"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (CT-emission misregistration placing the lateral wall over lung; realign the CT and reconstruct): When the emission lateral wall overlaps lung on the CT map, it receives the low attenuation coefficient of lung and is undercorrected, producing an artifactual defect. The registration note and the defect appearing only after correction point to misregistration. The fix is to realign the CT with the emission images and reconstruct again. A real defect would also appear without correction, and neither motion nor generator problems would produce this pattern.", "image": "question_images/q_775.png"}, {"id": 776, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "After a software update on a dual-head camera, the reconstructed point source from the center of rotation test is shown in the exhibit. A 61-year-old man with exertional chest pain is next on the schedule for rest-stress SPECT. If the camera were used without correction, what would most likely appear on his images?", "options": {"A": "A single sharp reversible defect confined to the inferior wall from diaphragmatic attenuation.", "B": "Blurred myocardium with distorted or artifactual wall defects that could be misread as perfusion abnormality.", "C": "A ring-shaped hot band around the left ventricle caused by an uneven detector response.", "D": "Extracardiac uptake superimposed on the inferior wall caused by hepatic activity near the heart."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Blurred myocardium with distorted or artifactual wall defects that could be misread as perfusion abnormality.): A center of rotation error makes projections from opposing angles misregister, so the reconstruction blurs and distorts the left ventricle and can leave artifactual thinning or defects that mimic disease. Diaphragmatic attenuation, detector nonuniformity, and liver activity are real sources of artifacts, but each has a distinct appearance and none is the result of the exhibit.", "image": "question_images/q_776.png"}, {"id": 777, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist asks why the final frames of a gated blood pool curve fall to about 2,665 counts from 17,962 in the first frame instead of returning to the starting level. Which explanation is correct? Heart rate varied between 60 and 100 per minute during the acquisition.", "options": {"A": "Background activity from the spleen is subtracted twice during late frames, removing ventricular counts", "B": "Tracer redistribution out of the blood pool lowers counts progressively during the acquisition", "C": "Normal ventricular emptying continues until the end of the cycle, so counts keep falling", "D": "Beats shorter than average contribute nothing to late frames, so counts fall"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Beats shorter than average contribute nothing to late frames, so counts fall): In a normal curve, counts reach a minimum at end-systole and rise again as the ventricle fills, ending near the starting value. When beats shorter than the accepted interval are included, they never reach the last frames, so late frames receive too few counts and the curve falls away. Tracer loss, ongoing emptying and background subtraction do not produce a curve that fails to return in this way.", "image": "question_images/q_777.png"}, {"id": 778, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man has angina despite three antianginal drugs. Regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT shows a stress ejection fraction of 58 percent. Across the stress, rest and reversibility polar maps, the summed stress score is 19, the summed rest score is 0 and the summed difference score is 19. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Reversible defects in two coronary territories show multivessel ischemia, so angiography is reasonable.", "B": "Reversible ischemia in the left anterior descending territory alone, so intensify medical therapy only now.", "C": "Low risk study because rest perfusion is normal, so reassess with repeat testing in two years.", "D": "Fixed defects from old infarcts in two coronary territories, so viability imaging should come first."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reversible defects in two coronary territories show multivessel ischemia, so angiography is reasonable.): Rest perfusion is normal and the stress defects fully reverse, so the difference score of 19 equals the stress score and reflects ischemia, not scar. Anterior, septal, apical and inferior segments are involved, covering the left anterior descending and right coronary territories. This large ischemic burden in a symptomatic patient on maximal medical therapy makes angiography reasonable. A normal rest study does not mean low risk when the stress study is this abnormal.", "image": "question_images/q_778.png"}, {"id": 779, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man had a prior treadmill sestamibi study at 92 percent of maximal predicted heart rate showing a large reversible LAD defect, then received a stent. Repeat exercise stopped at 76 percent for leg fatigue and shows a smaller defect. A reader reports ischemia resolved. What is the consequence of that error?", "options": {"A": "Overcalling of worsening disease, because lower heart rate enlarges defects relative to the prior study.", "B": "No consequence, because a smaller SDS on any later study proves successful revascularization.", "C": "False reassurance, since submaximal stress underestimates ischemic extent and the studies differ.", "D": "Underestimated EF, because a smaller defect on stress signals a fall in resting function."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (False reassurance, since submaximal stress underestimates ischemic extent and the studies differ.): Defect size depends on how much flow heterogeneity the stress produces. Reaching only 76 percent of the target rate lowers the stimulus, so residual ischemia can look smaller. Declaring it resolved risks missing in stent or distal disease. Comparison needs similar stress, tracer and processing. Submaximal stress shrinks defects rather than enlarging them, and defect size says nothing about resting EF."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "During regadenoson stress, a 57-year-old man's heart rate rose by 4 beats per minute, he had no symptoms, and the images were normal. He later admitted drinking three cups of coffee that morning. What is the most likely cause of the blunted response?", "options": {"A": "Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, blunting hyperemia and risking a falsely normal study.", "B": "Regadenoson was underdosed because it should have been adjusted for body weight.", "C": "A long-acting nitrate had already dilated all normal arteries and removed heterogeneity.", "D": "A beta-blocker prevented the vasodilator from lowering coronary resistance."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, blunting hyperemia and risking a falsely normal study.): Methylxanthines such as caffeine and theophylline are competitive antagonists at adenosine receptors, so they reduce the hemodynamic and hyperemic response to regadenoson, adenosine and dipyridamole, creating false negatives. Patients should avoid caffeine for at least 12 hours. Regadenoson is a fixed dose."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea cannot exercise because of severe osteoarthritis. Her history reveals an ECG diagnosis of Mobitz type II block with no pacemaker, and she has no wheezing or tachyarrhythmia and normal blood pressure. Which stress is best?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson SPECT, since its A2A selectivity avoids AV nodal effects seen with adenosine.", "B": "Dobutamine stress with Tc-99m, because high-grade AV block without pacing contraindicates vasodilator agents.", "C": "Adenosine infusion with atropine on standby, since atropine reverses AV block during vasodilator stress.", "D": "Treadmill exercise at low workload, since osteoarthritis only limits maximal capacity and not low-level testing."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dobutamine stress with Tc-99m, because high-grade AV block without pacing contraindicates vasodilator agents.): Second- or third-degree AV block without a pacemaker is a contraindication to adenosine and regadenoson because they slow AV conduction. Dobutamine does not suppress AV conduction and is the better pharmacologic choice if no arrhythmia or severe hypertension is present. Atropine standby is not accepted as a workaround, and she cannot reach adequate exercise."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old woman has regadenoson SPECT with a larger left ventricular cavity on the stress images than on the rest images, and reduced anterior and anteroseptal stress counts with a summed stress score of 13 and rest score of 0. Before reporting transient ischemic dilation as a high risk finding, which check adds the most value?", "options": {"A": "Confirm that the patient fasted four hours, since food intake enlarges the left ventricular cavity measured", "B": "Confirm that the stress dose was lower than the rest dose, since extra dose enlarges cavities", "C": "Confirm that both datasets share orientation, masking and processing, and check for motion", "D": "Confirm heart rate rose by 20 beats per minute, since dilation needs a heart rate response"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Confirm that both datasets share orientation, masking and processing, and check for motion): Apparent dilation can result from different processing masks, orientation or motion between datasets, so the first step is to verify the comparison is valid. Tracer dose, fasting status and heart rate rise do not change cavity size. Only after a technical explanation is excluded should the finding be called high risk, alongside the summed difference score of 13.", "image": "question_images/q_782.png"}, {"id": 783, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man with stable exertional angina has a pacemaker with ventricular pacing nearly 100 percent of the time and can walk about 5 METs. He needs perfusion imaging for stable ischemic heart disease. Which protocol is the best match?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson SPECT or PET, because ventricular pacing makes the ECG uninterpretable and promotes septal defects.", "B": "Treadmill ECG alone without imaging, since angina reproduced at 5 METs confirms obstructive disease by itself.", "C": "Dobutamine SPECT with atropine, since pacing does not alter septal perfusion during heart rate rise.", "D": "Treadmill SPECT with Duke score, since five METs of capacity makes exercise preferred for all patients."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regadenoson SPECT or PET, because ventricular pacing makes the ECG uninterpretable and promotes septal defects.): Paced ventricular activation causes septal perfusion defects that increase with heart rate, and it prevents ST-segment analysis. Vasodilator stress avoids the rate-related artifact and provides reliable perfusion data. Exercise and dobutamine would both raise rate, and an ECG-only test cannot be read in a paced rhythm. Capacity of 5 METs makes exercise possible, yet it is not the best fit."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 54-year-old man drank two cups of coffee about four hours before his regadenoson stress test. He reports no flushing, his heart rate rose only 8 beats per minute, and perfusion images are normal. What is the key limitation when interpreting this normal study?", "options": {"A": "The small heart rate rise shows a strong regadenoson effect, so sensitivity is higher.", "B": "The normal images are reliable, since caffeine raises baseline coronary flow and makes ischemia more visible.", "C": "Absent flushing means the dose was low, so a repeat tracer dose validates it.", "D": "Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, so hyperemia may be blunted and the normal images falsely negative."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, so hyperemia may be blunted and the normal images falsely negative.): Methylxanthines such as caffeine competitively block adenosine receptors, reducing the vasodilator response, so a flow difference between normal and stenosed arteries may not develop and ischemia can be missed. Guidelines advise withholding caffeine for at least 12 hours. A weak rate response and no flushing support blunted stress, not extra sensitivity."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a dobutamine stress SPECT at 30 mcg/kg/min, a 64-year-old man develops sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at 150 per minute. His blood pressure is 118/72, and he has no symptoms. The infusion is still running and the target heart rate has been reached. Which is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Stop dobutamine, and give intravenous esmolol or metoprolol if the arrhythmia persists.", "B": "Reduce the infusion and continue to reach the target heart rate, since blood pressure is stable.", "C": "Give adenosine 6 mg intravenously to terminate the tachycardia.", "D": "Give aminophylline 125 mg intravenously, because the dobutamine effect should be reversed with a methylxanthine."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop dobutamine, and give intravenous esmolol or metoprolol if the arrhythmia persists.): Sustained ventricular tachycardia during dobutamine requires stopping the infusion; dobutamine has a half-life of about 2 minutes, and a short-acting beta blocker is added if the rhythm persists, with cardioversion available if instability develops. Aminophylline is for adenosine-type agents, and adenosine is not a ventricular tachycardia therapy."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A PET laboratory teaching conference reviews attenuation physics. About what fraction of 511 keV photons from the myocardium pass unattenuated through 10 cm of soft tissue, assuming an attenuation coefficient of 0.096 per cm along the entire path of the photon?", "options": {"A": "About 38 percent, the transmitted fraction given by e to the power minus 0.96.", "B": "About 15 percent, squaring the single photon transmission for the coincident pair.", "C": "About 90 percent, calculated as one minus the attenuation coefficient.", "D": "About 62 percent, the fraction that is attenuated rather than transmitted."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 38 percent, the transmitted fraction given by e to the power minus 0.96.): Transmission is e raised to minus mu times thickness, so e to minus 0.96 is about 0.38. The coincidence pair has the same total path along the line of response, so the pair transmission is also 0.38 rather than squared. The 62 percent value is the attenuated share."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea and no obstructive coronary disease on CT angiography has Rb-82 PET with regadenoson, uniform relative perfusion and a global myocardial flow reserve of 1.4 on quantification. Which statement about this reserve value is correct?", "options": {"A": "A reduced reserve in the absence of coronary stenosis excludes any added event risk.", "B": "Reserve of 1.4 matters only when relative images also show a regional defect.", "C": "Reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow, and under 2.0 predicts higher adverse event risk.", "D": "Reserve is rest flow divided by stress flow, so 1.4 indicates normal endothelial function."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reserve is stress flow divided by rest flow, and under 2.0 predicts higher adverse event risk.): Flow reserve is the ratio of stress to rest myocardial blood flow. Values under about 2.0 carry increased cardiac event risk even without obstructive epicardial disease, reflecting diffuse disease or microvascular dysfunction. The inverted ratio gives a number below 1 for normal subjects, and reserve has independent prognostic value without a regional defect."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with hypertension and no prior infarction has stable angina, Canadian class I on two antianginal drugs. SPECT shows 2 of 17 segments with mild reversible reduction, an SDS of 3, and a normal EF. Which strategy is best?", "options": {"A": "Refer for CABG, because a mildly reversible defect indicates multivessel disease needing surgery.", "B": "Continue medical therapy, since a small ischemic burden with controlled symptoms offers little gain.", "C": "Refer for PCI, because any reversible defect predicts a benefit from revascularization.", "D": "Repeat the study with a different stress agent to confirm the small defect."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue medical therapy, since a small ischemic burden with controlled symptoms offers little gain.): Revascularization benefit rises with ischemic burden. A small defect with controlled symptoms and normal EF leaves little to gain from added procedures over medical therapy. Small defects do not indicate extensive disease, and repeating the study would not change management."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man says he walks about two blocks on level ground at a slow pace with a cane, then stops for hip pain, and cannot climb one flight of stairs. How should his exercise capacity affect the choice of stress test?", "options": {"A": "Capacity is adequate because he walks two blocks, so a standard treadmill study is appropriate", "B": "Capacity is borderline, so a Bruce protocol with a slowed first stage will reach target", "C": "Capacity is poor, so dobutamine should be preferred over vasodilators as first-line stress", "D": "Capacity is under 4 METs, so reaching target heart rate is unlikely; plan vasodilator stress"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Capacity is under 4 METs, so reaching target heart rate is unlikely; plan vasodilator stress): Slow level walking with a cane and inability to climb a flight of stairs corresponds to roughly 3 METs, well below the 5 METs or more usually needed to reach 85 percent of predicted maximum heart rate. A vasodilator is the standard first-line alternative when exercise is inadequate and there is no contraindication. Dobutamine is second line, and a slowed Bruce protocol will still be limited by hip pain."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On the third polar map of a sestamibi study, labeled rest minus stress, a bright region appears over the anterior wall and septum. The first two maps show stress and rest counts, with the apex at the center and the base at the rim. What does the bright region represent?", "options": {"A": "Segments where the stress count is lower than the rest count, meaning reversible ischemia.", "B": "Segments where the stress count equals the rest count, meaning fixed scar.", "C": "Segments where the rest count is lower than the stress count, meaning reversed redistribution.", "D": "Segments with the highest absolute counts on stress, meaning normal myocardium."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Segments where the stress count is lower than the rest count, meaning reversible ischemia.): The reversibility map subtracts the stress image from the rest image, so it is bright where rest counts exceed stress counts, which is the signature of reversible ischemia. Equal counts cancel out and appear dark, which is the pattern of fixed scar or normal tissue. Reverse redistribution is a different pattern with lower rest counts.", "image": "question_images/q_790.png"}, {"id": 791, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man finishes a treadmill test with Tc-99m sestamibi. Gated images obtained 45 minutes later show a left ventricular ejection fraction of 38 percent, versus 56 percent at rest. Perfusion shows only a small reversible inferior defect. What is the best interpretation of the ejection fraction change?", "options": {"A": "Post-stress stunning from severe ischemia, implying more extensive disease than the small defect suggests.", "B": "Gating error from ectopy, so the stress ejection fraction should be discarded as unreliable and omitted.", "C": "Normal variation, since ejection fraction typically falls about ten points after any exercise stress.", "D": "Dilated cardiomyopathy unmasked by exercise, establishing a nonischemic cause for the reduced function."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Post-stress stunning from severe ischemia, implying more extensive disease than the small defect suggests.): A fall of about 5 percentage points or more from rest to post-stress, with normal rest function, reflects transient stunning after severe ischemia. It points to more extensive disease, including multivessel or left main disease, and is a high-risk marker even when the visible defect is small. Normal physiology does not lower ejection fraction after exercise, and a nonischemic cardiomyopathy would show reduced rest function."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A daily intrinsic flood field shows a round 6 cm cold area at the same detector position every day. On patient projections the cold area stays fixed on the detector while the heart moves. The uniformity correction map was refreshed yesterday. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat energy peaking only and return the detector to clinical use after the peak is reset", "B": "Take the camera head out of service, call for tube replacement, and use another detector", "C": "Update the uniformity correction map again and resume clinical imaging with this same detector today", "D": "Rotate the patient prone and repeat on the same detector to shift the apparent defect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take the camera head out of service, call for tube replacement, and use another detector): A cold area fixed to the detector that persists across patients and flood images indicates a failed or dropped-out photomultiplier tube. Correction maps cannot restore a tube that has failed, and energy peaking does not restore its gain. The camera should come out of service for repair, and clinical imaging should shift to a working detector."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "The daily Tc-99m flood field QC shows the photopeak spectrum centered at 133 keV, while the acquisition window is still centered at 140 keV. The first patient is waiting and the uniformity image looks acceptable. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Widen the window to 25 percent so that the shifted photopeak still falls inside it", "B": "Image the patient because the flood uniformity appears acceptable to the technologist", "C": "Shift the patient acquisition window manually to 133 keV and begin imaging at once", "D": "Repeat energy peaking and confirm uniformity before imaging any patient on this camera"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat energy peaking and confirm uniformity before imaging any patient on this camera): The photopeak must be centered at 140 keV, so a peak at 133 keV indicates drift that must be corrected by peaking before clinical use. Moving the window or widening it works around the problem, degrades scatter rejection and does not restore calibration of the energy and linearity corrections. Visual uniformity does not substitute for a passed energy check."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "Dynamic Rb-82 PET after regadenoson gives rest flow 0.95 and stress flow 1.05 mL/min/g, a flow reserve of 1.1. Heart rate rose by 4 beats per minute, there were no symptoms, and the patient admits two cups of coffee that morning. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Lower the flow reserve threshold so that a value of 1.1 is considered normal", "B": "Recognize inadequate hyperemia rather than disease, and repeat the study after a caffeine washout", "C": "Report severely reduced flow reserve and recommend invasive angiography for multivessel disease", "D": "Apply motion correction to the dynamic frames and quantify flow again"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize inadequate hyperemia rather than disease, and repeat the study after a caffeine washout): A minimal heart rate rise, no symptoms and recent caffeine suggest adenosine receptor blockade and an inadequate vasodilator response, so stress flow does not reflect true reserve. The study should be flagged and repeated after caffeine washout rather than read as disease. Motion correction does not explain the flat response, and changing the threshold would hide a technical failure."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man has treadmill stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT started 10 minutes after peak exercise. The raw cine shows the heart drifting upward about 3 pixels across the acquisition, and reconstruction shows a new inferior defect. Rest images are normal. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Lateral patient motion, which should be corrected by applying attenuation correction to the stress set.", "B": "Stress induced LV dilation, which should be quantified using the TID ratio before further review.", "C": "True inferior ischemia confined to the right coronary territory, requiring angiography as the next step.", "D": "Upward creep from early imaging caused a spurious inferior defect; confirm and reprocess."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Upward creep from early imaging caused a spurious inferior defect; confirm and reprocess.): Early imaging after exercise lets the diaphragm and heart settle upward as ventilation and heart rate fall, so counts in different projections come from a heart in different positions, causing spurious inferior defects. The cine shows the drift directly. Creep correction or delayed imaging solves it. Ischemia and TID are not supported by the cine, and attenuation correction does not fix movement."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 150 kg (330 lb) man with a BMI of 46 needs stress myocardial perfusion imaging for chest pain evaluation. PET is not available at the site, and the camera table limit is 227 kg. Which protocol and dosing approach is best?", "options": {"A": "A one-day low-dose protocol using 8 mCi at rest, 25 mCi at stress, standard times", "B": "A two-day Tc-99m protocol with weight-adjusted doses and longer acquisitions", "C": "A one-day Tc-99m protocol with standard 10 and 30 mCi doses and shorter acquisition", "D": "A planar anterior view with a standard dose, since tomography is unreliable in heavy patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A two-day Tc-99m protocol with weight-adjusted doses and longer acquisitions): In large patients, photon attenuation and scatter reduce counts, so higher weight-based Tc-99m doses and longer acquisitions are used, and a two-day protocol avoids rest activity contaminating stress counts and stays within dose limits per injection. Thallium has lower energy and worse attenuation. Planar imaging gives no localization, and standard low doses give noisy images in a patient of this size."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "At peak stage 3 of a Bruce protocol, a 63-year-old man reaches 91% of predicted maximum heart rate with 2.5 mm downsloping ST depression and mild chest tightness, and blood pressure is 168/84. Tracer is drawn up. What is the best action for tracer injection and exercise?", "options": {"A": "Continue exercise to 100 percent of predicted heart rate before injecting the tracer.", "B": "Stop at once and inject after 5 minutes of recovery, since this is an absolute criterion.", "C": "Inject the tracer now and keep the patient exercising about one more minute, then stop.", "D": "Stop exercise, then inject tracer after a nitroglycerin dose to confirm the response."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inject the tracer now and keep the patient exercising about one more minute, then stop.): The target heart rate is already reached, and marked ST depression with chest tightness justifies ending soon, but tracer must be injected at peak stress so it reflects flow at that moment. Sestamibi or tetrofosmin should be given with exercise continuing for 1 to 2 minutes afterward. Injecting after recovery would miss the ischemic flow pattern."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "selection_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56 year old woman with a body mass index of 46 cannot exercise. A prior SPECT study was equivocal because of soft tissue attenuation. Both SPECT and Rb-82 PET are available at the center. Her renal function and rhythm are normal. Which is the best study?", "options": {"A": "Regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT, since uncorrected SPECT remains accurate in large patients", "B": "Exercise SPECT with prone imaging only, since prone imaging resolves attenuation in every habitus", "C": "Rb-82 PET with regadenoson, since CT attenuation correction and higher photon energy suit large patients", "D": "Dobutamine SPECT with thallium, since the low-energy photons of thallium penetrate large bodies better"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rb-82 PET with regadenoson, since CT attenuation correction and higher photon energy suit large patients): Rb-82 PET uses 511 keV photons and CT attenuation correction, giving higher counts, better resolution and higher diagnostic accuracy in obese patients, with a short protocol. Thallium emits low-energy photons that attenuate more, and prone imaging helps only inferior wall artifact. She cannot exercise, so vasodilator stress fits."}, {"id": 799, "domain": "radiation_safety_and_management_of_radiopharmaceuticals", "question": "A cardiologist counsels a patient choosing between a 1-day Tc-99m sestamibi study using about 30 mCi total and a Tl-201 stress-redistribution study using 3.5 mCi. He reasons that the smaller number of millicuries means the smaller radiation dose. Which statement corrects the reasoning?", "options": {"A": "Tl-201 gives under 1 mSv because its low photon energy delivers little dose to tissue", "B": "Tl-201 at 3.5 mCi usually gives the higher dose, roughly 15 to 20 mSv", "C": "The Tc-99m study gives about 30 mSv because effective dose in mSv equals activity in mCi", "D": "Both studies give about 3 mSv, roughly equal to one year of natural background exposure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tl-201 at 3.5 mCi usually gives the higher dose, roughly 15 to 20 mSv): Effective dose depends on the tracer's half-life, biodistribution and emissions, not on millicuries alone. Tl-201 has a long half-life and gives about 15 to 20 mSv for a 3.5 mCi stress-redistribution study, higher than a typical 1-day Tc-99m sestamibi study near 10 mSv. Background in the United States is roughly 3 mSv per year from natural sources, and mCi and mSv are not interchangeable units."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man who had an inferior infarction five years ago is scheduled for elective open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. He is asymptomatic and walks one flight of stairs. Gated sestamibi slices show an inferior defect at stress and rest, with summed scores of 11 and 12 and a difference of minus 1. Which recommendation fits the findings best?", "options": {"A": "Cancel surgery, since an inferior defect mandates coronary stenting before elective vascular surgery.", "B": "Delay surgery and refer for angiography, because a perfusion defect of any kind predicts perioperative infarction.", "C": "Report inferior scar without inducible ischemia, then proceed with surgery on optimized medical therapy.", "D": "Repeat the study with dobutamine, because regadenoson cannot unmask ischemia in the inferior wall."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report inferior scar without inducible ischemia, then proceed with surgery on optimized medical therapy.): The inferior defect is the same at stress and rest, so it is scar without inducible ischemia, and the difference score of minus 1 is effectively zero. Without ischemia or symptoms, surgery can proceed on optimized medical therapy. Scar alone does not mandate stenting or angiography. Regadenoson unmasks ischemia in the inferior wall as well as any other territory, so a dobutamine repeat adds nothing.", "image": "question_images/q_800.png"}, {"id": 801, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man had a stress-only gated SPECT study because of chest tightness. The post-stress ejection fraction is 25 percent with global hypokinesis and no focal defect, and an echocardiogram three weeks ago was not available. He asks the reader how to separate transient from baseline dysfunction. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Calculate the ischemic burden from the polar map, since it measures baseline function without rest data.", "B": "Report baseline cardiomyopathy, since global hypokinesis cannot be stress induced when there is no focal defect.", "C": "Acquire rest gated images and compare ejection fractions, since a larger post-stress drop indicates ischemic stunning.", "D": "Repeat the post-stress acquisition after a longer delay, since transient dysfunction shows on repeated stress images."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses a perfusion score for function", "B": "excludes stress-induced global dysfunction", "D": "repeats stress instead of acquiring rest"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-011_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acquire rest gated images and compare ejection fractions, since a larger post-stress drop indicates ischemic stunning.): Post-stress function that is lower than resting function suggests ischemic stunning and high risk disease, but this needs a resting comparison to show. A gated rest acquisition or an existing echocardiogram gives the baseline ejection fraction. A perfusion score measures perfusion rather than function, and repeating stress images cannot reveal which state is baseline."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man weighing 150 kg underwent a same day rest and stress Tc-99m protocol. The second injection was meant to be three times the first, yet the projections of both studies are noisy and dim with no motion. For a repeat, which protocol best fixes the low counts?", "options": {"A": "A one day protocol with the first injection reduced further to limit residual activity", "B": "The same one day protocol, performed with shorter acquisitions to limit motion risk", "C": "A two day protocol with a full weight based dose for each study", "D": "The same one day protocol with a stress dose of twice the rest dose"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cutting the first dose and reducing rest counts further", "B": "Trading counts for time when counts are the problem", "D": "Keeping the same limited rest to stress ratio"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-117_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A two day protocol with a full weight based dose for each study): In a same day protocol the first dose is kept small so it does not contaminate the second, which leaves the first study short of counts in a large patient. A two day protocol allows a full weight adjusted dose for both, with no carryover. Smaller first doses and shorter acquisitions deepen the count deficit."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old woman has gated stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The rotating raw data show an abrupt change in heart position partway through, and the software reports a gated ejection fraction of 60 percent. How should the gated results be used?", "options": {"A": "Report the ejection fraction as normal because 60 percent is within the reference range.", "B": "Hold the gated values as unreliable until a motion free acquisition is available.", "C": "Average the gated values with the rest study to cancel the motion effect.", "D": "Use the gated wall motion to override the perfusion findings in the report."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "trusts a number from motion degraded data", "C": "believes averaging repairs motion artifact", "D": "overweights gated data from a flawed acquisition"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-104_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the gated values as unreliable until a motion free acquisition is available.): Gating relies on the same projection data, so misregistration blurs edges and can alter wall motion and volumes even when the number looks normal. Waiting for a repeat acquisition protects the report. A normal looking value from a flawed acquisition can mislead, gated data cannot overrule perfusion here, and averaging does not remove motion error."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman presents two days after a severe emotional shock with chest pain and elevated troponin. Coronary angiography showed no obstructive disease. Gated resting SPECT shows akinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex with preserved resting counts and a reduced ejection fraction of 37 percent. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute anterior infarction from a distal anterior descending occlusion, so repeat angiography is needed now.", "B": "Healed anterior scar from an old infarct, so the ejection fraction will stay at 37 percent.", "C": "Hibernating myocardium from chronic stenosis, so revascularization of the anterior descending artery should follow.", "D": "Stress cardiomyopathy, which typically recovers within weeks, so repeat imaging in four to eight weeks."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "ignores the angiogram without obstruction", "B": "calls preserved counts scar", "C": "claims stenosis despite a clean angiogram"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-021_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stress cardiomyopathy, which typically recovers within weeks, so repeat imaging in four to eight weeks.): Akinesis with preserved perfusion after an emotional trigger, a normal angiogram and troponin leak fit stress cardiomyopathy. Function usually recovers over days to weeks, so a follow-up study confirms the diagnosis. Infarct, scar and hibernation all require an obstructive lesion or reduced resting counts, which are not present."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "On a rubidium-82 PET study, the technologist reports that the CT attenuation map lies about 10 mm to the patient's left of the heart on the emission data, and the lateral wall counts look lower than the other walls. Wall motion looks normal. Which quality control action is standard before the study is interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Check the fused CT and emission images in all three planes and reprocess after realignment", "B": "Switch off attenuation correction and interpret the uncorrected images for all segments", "C": "Increase the injected activity so that the emission counts overcome the attenuation mismatch", "D": "Accept the study if the left ventricle sits inside the CT field of view at stress"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "drops correction instead of fixing alignment", "C": "believes more dose corrects a registration error", "D": "equates field of view coverage with correct alignment"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-067_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check the fused CT and emission images in all three planes and reprocess after realignment): Registration of the CT attenuation map and the emission data should be checked on fused images in all planes for every study, and any misalignment should be corrected before reprocessing. More dose does not change the alignment. Uncorrected images have their own known artifacts. Being inside the field of view says nothing about whether the maps overlay correctly."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man has Tc-99m tetrofosmin imaging started 8 minutes after maximal treadmill exercise. On the rotating raw projections the heart slowly rises relative to the horizontal line across the whole acquisition. The reader must choose the explanation. Which response fits best?", "options": {"A": "Creep from settling heart rate and breathing, so use motion correction.", "B": "Hot gut activity beside the heart, so give water and image again after delay.", "C": "Failure of the center of rotation, so recalibrate the gantry and reacquire the acquisition.", "D": "A sudden body shift on the table, so repeat the acquisition with arms supported."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "mistakes motion for extracardiac neighbor", "C": "blames the gantry for patient physiology", "D": "mistakes gradual drift for an abrupt shift"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-105_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Creep from settling heart rate and breathing, so use motion correction.): After exercise, heart rate and lung volumes fall toward baseline, and the heart gradually rises in the chest during an early acquisition. Delaying the start lets this settle, and motion correction handles a gradual drift well. An abrupt shift would be a step, gut activity changes counts and not position, and center of rotation faults produce blur at a point."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man has a history of an old anterior myocardial infarction. Resting gated SPECT shows abnormal wall motion at the apex, apical anterior and apical septal walls, read with the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 45 percent. Which vessel and finding fit?", "options": {"A": "Left circumflex territory with matched perfusion and motion abnormality, indicating scar of the lateral wall.", "B": "Right coronary territory with matched perfusion and motion abnormality, indicating scar of the inferior wall.", "C": "Distal left anterior descending territory with matched perfusion and motion abnormality, indicating scar.", "D": "Distal left anterior descending territory with preserved perfusion, indicating stunning of the apex after recent ischemia."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning apical segments to the circumflex", "B": "Assigning apical segments to the right coronary", "D": "Misreading matched loss as preserved perfusion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-054_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Distal left anterior descending territory with matched perfusion and motion abnormality, indicating scar.): The apical anterior, apical septal and apex segments are supplied by the distal left anterior descending artery. Reduced resting counts matched with severely reduced motion indicate scar. Stunning requires preserved counts, and neither the right coronary nor the circumflex supplies the apex."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49 year old man with exertional chest pain has a rest gated SPECT. The cine shows a region with absent inward motion and thickening, while counts in the same region are preserved. In the standard 17 segment model, which vessel territory does the affected region belong to?", "options": {"A": "Posterior descending branch of a right dominant right coronary artery, supplying apex and septum.", "B": "Obtuse marginal branch of the circumflex, supplying the mid anterior and anteroseptal walls.", "C": "Left circumflex artery, through its obtuse marginal branches to the anterior wall.", "D": "Mid to distal left anterior descending artery, through its septal and diagonal branches."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assigning the apex to the right coronary artery", "B": "confusing marginal territory with anteroseptal wall", "C": "confusing circumflex branches with anterior wall supply"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-033_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mid to distal left anterior descending artery, through its septal and diagonal branches.): On the cine the akinetic region sits in the anterior wall, septum and apex. In the 17 segment model these segments (7, 8, 13, 14 and 17) are supplied by the left anterior descending artery. The circumflex serves the lateral wall (5, 6, 11, 12, 16), and the right coronary serves the inferior wall and inferoseptum."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man has stress Tc-99m sestamibi imaging with abrupt movement noted on the raw data, and rest imaging 3 hours later is motion free. The reconstructed stress images show an anterior defect that fills in at rest. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Reversibility may reflect stress motion artifact, so repeat the stress acquisition first.", "B": "Hibernating myocardium, because the anterior segment recovers counts between the stress and rest acquisitions.", "C": "A normal study, because rest imaging was motion free and overrides the stress data.", "D": "Reversible anterior ischemia, because the defect improves at rest on a motion free study."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "applies a viability concept to artifact", "C": "discards stress data in favor of rest", "D": "treats a reversible pattern as proof of ischemia"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-102_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reversibility may reflect stress motion artifact, so repeat the stress acquisition first.): A reversible looking defect is only meaningful when both acquisitions are technically sound. Motion on the stress data can create a defect that disappears on a clean rest study, producing false ischemia, so the stress acquisition should be repeated. Hibernation is a physiologic state and not a conclusion from technical artifact, and rest data cannot cancel a stress abnormality."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with exertional angina and a known chronic right coronary stenosis undergoes resting gated Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The short-axis cine is read with the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 40 percent. Which finding best explains the regional function and what is the best next direction?", "options": {"A": "Inferior scar with dead tissue; continue medical therapy alone because revascularization cannot improve akinetic segments.", "B": "Chronic hibernation of the inferior wall; plan revascularization because preserved counts predict improved function.", "C": "Inferior attenuation artifact; repeat imaging with attenuation correction before drawing conclusions about regional function.", "D": "Acute stunning after a missed infarct; repeat resting imaging in six weeks without revascularization planned."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating akinesis with infarction", "C": "Applying attenuation to a motion finding", "D": "Using stunning for chronic angina"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-053_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chronic hibernation of the inferior wall; plan revascularization because preserved counts predict improved function.): Akinetic inferior segments with preserved counts in a patient with chronic flow-limiting disease fit hibernation, in which myocardium downregulates function to match reduced flow. Revascularization often restores function and angina is an indication. Scar would reduce counts, and attenuation changes counts, not wall motion."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist asks which feature on a gated SPECT cine most strongly suggests beat-length variation rather than low administered activity alone. The patient is a 63-year-old man with frequent ectopic beats on the monitor, and the software reads LVEF 55 percent. What should the technologist look for?", "options": {"A": "Count density falls progressively across the later frames, with the last frames of the cycle noisiest.", "B": "Counts drop equally in every frame, giving a uniformly grainy cine from first frame to last.", "C": "The septum looks brighter than the lateral wall in every frame, with the same ratio throughout.", "D": "The cavity looks larger in early frames than late frames, with brightness unchanged between frames."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Describing uniform low-count noise instead", "C": "Describing a fixed regional count pattern", "D": "Describing a volume change, not a count change"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-011_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Count density falls progressively across the later frames, with the last frames of the cycle noisiest.): Low administered activity or short acquisition makes every frame grainy by a similar amount. Beat-length variation starves late frames specifically, so noise and dimness grow with frame number. A fixed septal-to-lateral ratio points to attenuation or true regional perfusion differences, and cavity size changes with systole and diastole are normal motion."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55 year old man undergoes stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. On review of the rotating raw projection images, the heart stays near the center of the field, no vertical jump appears between frames, and the liver and bowel sit below the heart without overlapping it. What should the physician decide about the acquisition?", "options": {"A": "The raw data are adequate, so reconstruct and read without repeating.", "B": "Delay reconstruction until motion correction has been applied to the vertical shift between frames.", "C": "Repeat it because liver appears in the field and must be absent from every frame.", "D": "Repeat with a smaller orbit because the heart must fill the detector in each frame."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "corrects motion that is not present", "C": "treats normal liver visibility as a defect", "D": "misjudges where the heart should sit"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-076_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The raw data are adequate, so reconstruct and read without repeating.): Normal raw data show a heart near the center of the field, smooth motion across the cine, and extracardiac organs that sit below the myocardium without overlapping it. Liver and bowel are always in the field of view, and a heart filling the detector is not a goal. With no vertical jump, motion correction is unnecessary, so the study can be reconstructed and read."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70-year-old man with frequent premature ventricular beats has a gated blood-pool acquisition in which the late frames of the cycle look noisy and dim. The processed LVEF is 45 percent. The technologist asks how to improve the next acquisition. Which change is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Increase the number of frames per cycle to 64 so each frame has more counts", "B": "Apply a beat-length acceptance window to reject ectopic beats and acquire for longer", "C": "Move the heart to the edge of the field of view to reduce attenuation", "D": "Lower the energy window to include more scattered photons in every acquired frame of the cycle"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "splits counts across more frames and worsens noise", "C": "changes positioning that does not address the gating problem", "D": "adds scatter instead of fixing the beat-length problem"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-045_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply a beat-length acceptance window to reject ectopic beats and acquire for longer): Premature beats shorten the cycle, so the data collected after the early beat cut off before the late frames fill, leaving them dim and noisy. Rejecting beats outside an acceptance window around the average R-R interval removes these, and a longer acquisition restores counts. More frames spread the counts thinner, a lower energy window adds scatter, and repositioning has no effect on the gating error."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old man with hypertension has exertional dyspnea and a gated blood-pool LVEF of 46 percent. Earlier studies showed an LVEF of 60 percent, never at or below 40 percent. Under the current heart failure classification, which category best describes his ejection fraction?", "options": {"A": "Improved ejection fraction, defined by a rise from a prior low value", "B": "Preserved ejection fraction, defined as 50 percent or higher", "C": "Mildly reduced ejection fraction, in the 41 to 49 percent range", "D": "Reduced ejection fraction, defined as 40 percent or lower"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses the improved category without a prior value at or below 40 percent", "B": "applies the preserved category to a fraction below 50 percent", "D": "applies the reduced category to a fraction above 40 percent"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-041_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mildly reduced ejection fraction, in the 41 to 49 percent range): The universal classification defines reduced ejection fraction as 40 percent or lower, mildly reduced as 41 to 49 percent and preserved as 50 percent or higher. An LVEF of 46 percent falls in the mildly reduced group. The improved category requires a prior value of 40 percent or lower that later rose above 40 percent, which his earlier fraction of 60 percent excludes."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old woman with a prior inferior infarct and no current symptoms has gated SPECT before elective hip surgery. Resting inferior wall counts are about 70 percent of peak, the cine shows mild inferior hypokinesis with brisk motion elsewhere, and LVEF is 56 percent. Which statement is the best summary of the study?", "options": {"A": "Small nontransmural scar in the right coronary territory, with matched mild perfusion and motion loss.", "B": "Normal ejection fraction excludes scar, so this is a normal variant of regional thickening.", "C": "Perfusion and motion mismatch indicates hibernating inferior myocardium needing revascularization before surgery.", "D": "Inferior attenuation explains the low counts, so the motion abnormality can be disregarded."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "assuming normal global EF excludes regional scar", "C": "calling a matched defect a mismatch", "D": "dismissing a motion abnormality as attenuation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-024_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Small nontransmural scar in the right coronary territory, with matched mild perfusion and motion loss.): Both uptake and motion are mildly reduced in the same inferior segments (basal, mid and apical inferior), which is a matched pattern and points to partial thickness scar in the right coronary territory. Attenuation can lower counts but does not change wall motion on the cine. A mismatch would need preserved counts with abnormal motion, and a normal global EF of 56 percent does not exclude a small regional scar."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old woman has exertional dyspnea and atypical chest tightness. A gated blood-pool cine in the left anterior oblique view is processed with an LVEF of 48 percent. The reader notes reduced thickening of the lateral wall, while septal and apical motion look brisk. Which summary of the regional wall motion belongs in the report?", "options": {"A": "Hypokinesis of the lateral wall with preserved motion of the remaining walls", "B": "Hypokinesis of the inferior wall with preserved motion of the lateral wall", "C": "Global hypokinesis of all walls with a uniformly dilated ventricle", "D": "Dyskinesis of the septum with preserved motion of the lateral wall"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "swaps inferior and lateral wall positions on the left anterior oblique view", "C": "reads a regional defect as global dysfunction", "D": "reads septal motion as abnormal, as in conduction delay"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-030_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hypokinesis of the lateral wall with preserved motion of the remaining walls): The cine shows reduced thickening limited to the lateral wall, which lies on the right of the left ventricle in the left anterior oblique display, while the septum and the remaining walls contract normally. This gives a modestly reduced LVEF of 48 percent. Inferior wall disease would appear in a different position, septal dyskinesis would show paradoxical motion, and global hypokinesis would give a much lower EF."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55 year old man with atypical chest pain and a normal resting ECG has a rest Tc-99m sestamibi gated SPECT. Counts are uniform, but the cine shows mildly reduced thickening of the lateral wall, and LVEF is 53 percent. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Normal ejection fraction means regional motion should be reported as normal without further comment.", "B": "Regional dysfunction without perfusion loss argues against scar; correlate with stress images and clinical context.", "C": "Circumflex infarct scar, since any wall motion abnormality proves myocardial necrosis in that territory.", "D": "Soft tissue attenuation lowering lateral thickening, so repeat imaging with different arm positioning."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "letting global EF override a regional finding", "C": "equating any motion abnormality with necrosis", "D": "blaming attenuation for a wall motion finding"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-027_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Regional dysfunction without perfusion loss argues against scar; correlate with stress images and clinical context.): Preserved resting uptake argues against scar, yet mild lateral hypokinesis (segments 5, 6, 11, 12, 16) is real and should be reported. It can reflect stunning, hibernation or a nonischemic cause, so stress data and clinical context decide what it means. Global EF can stay normal with a small regional abnormality, and attenuation changes counts, not motion."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58 year old man has recurrent chest pain four days after stenting of a circumflex marginal branch. Rest Tc-99m sestamibi gated SPECT is acquired. On the gated cine the lateral wall does not thicken or move inward, yet resting counts in that wall are preserved and LVEF is 43 percent. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Left bundle branch block motion artifact reflecting abnormal activation rather than myocardial disease.", "B": "Transmural lateral scar, because absent wall motion alone proves irreversible myocyte loss.", "C": "Left hemidiaphragm attenuation causing apparent lateral hypokinesis and falsely reduced lateral counts on the gated images.", "D": "Viable stunned or hibernating lateral myocardium in the circumflex territory, recovering after reperfusion."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confusing conduction motion artifact with regional disease", "B": "equating akinesis with irreversible scar", "C": "attributing lateral findings to diaphragmatic attenuation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-023_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Viable stunned or hibernating lateral myocardium in the circumflex territory, recovering after reperfusion.): Akinesis with preserved resting counts means the myocytes are still taking up tracer, which fits stunning after recent ischemia or chronic hibernation in the circumflex territory (basal and mid inferolateral and anterolateral segments plus the lateral apex). Scar would show reduced counts matched to the motion loss. Diaphragmatic attenuation affects inferior counts, not lateral wall motion, and a left bundle branch block artifact is septal and is not the finding on this cine."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 59-year-old man has a gated rubidium-82 PET. Uptake in the basal, mid and apical inferior walls is reduced to about half of peak, with reduced wall motion in the same region, and the remaining walls look normal. In which coronary territory does this pattern fit best?", "options": {"A": "Diagonal branch territory, because basal inferior segments are fed through septal perforators of that vessel", "B": "Right coronary artery territory in a right-dominant system, supplying the entire inferior wall", "C": "Left circumflex territory, because the inferior wall lies beside the inferolateral segments it supplies", "D": "Left anterior descending territory, because mid and apical inferior segments are shared with its distal branches"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assigns the inferior wall to a diagonal branch", "C": "mixes the inferior wall with the inferolateral wall", "D": "extends the left anterior descending into the inferior wall"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-060_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right coronary artery territory in a right-dominant system, supplying the entire inferior wall): The basal, mid and apical inferior segments are supplied by the right coronary artery in a right-dominant system through the posterior descending branch. The distal left anterior descending can wrap around the apex but does not supply the entire inferior wall. The circumflex supplies the inferolateral and anterolateral walls, and the diagonal branches supply anterior and anterolateral segments."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A woman has stress sestamibi SPECT, and the anterior wall looks dimmer than the other walls on the raw projections. The technologist notes about 30 mm of added soft tissue over her left anterior chest. Which factor determines how much the anterior wall counts fall on the raw projections and reconstructed images?", "options": {"A": "Tissue thickness between heart and camera, since absorption rises with path length.", "B": "Cup size alone, since breast tissue has the same density as muscle in all patients.", "C": "Heart rate during acquisition, since faster rates cause anterior dimming from motion blur.", "D": "The injected activity in megabecquerels, since a higher dose lowers the fraction absorbed."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "ignores tissue path length", "C": "blames heart rate for attenuation", "D": "thinks dose changes absorption fraction"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-085_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tissue thickness between heart and camera, since absorption rises with path length.): Photon absorption follows the path length through tissue, so a thicker breast removes more photons headed toward the anterior wall, and the attenuation artifact is bigger. Injected dose changes total counts but not the fraction absorbed, and heart rate affects gating rather than soft tissue loss."}, {"id": 821, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67 year old man with prior anterior infarction and angina has gated SPECT. The mid anterior, anteroseptal and apical segments are moderately hypokinetic with counts near 50 percent of peak, and LVEF is 50 percent. What is the best next step to guide revascularization?", "options": {"A": "Proceed directly to bypass surgery, since every hypokinetic segment with measurable uptake will recover.", "B": "Classify the segments as scar, since counts below 60 percent of peak exclude recovery.", "C": "Repeat gated sestamibi in six months, since uptake of this degree resolves spontaneously.", "D": "FDG PET viability study, since metabolism separates hibernating myocardium from scar at indeterminate counts."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treating any measurable uptake as viable", "B": "using too high a threshold to exclude viability", "C": "expecting spontaneous resolution of a chronic count reduction"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-042_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (FDG PET viability study, since metabolism separates hibernating myocardium from scar at indeterminate counts.): Counts around 50 percent of peak with moderate hypokinesis in the left anterior descending territory mean a mix of viable and scarred myocardium, so SPECT is indeterminate. FDG PET adds metabolic information and clarifies whether revascularization is likely to help. Measurable uptake alone does not prove recovery, and viability thresholds sit near 50 percent, not 60."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old woman on trastuzumab for breast cancer had a baseline blood-pool LVEF of 58 percent and is asymptomatic with a normal examination. Today the study shows a dilated LV with globally reduced contraction and LVEF near 35 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue trastuzumab with weekly blood-pool studies, since she is asymptomatic and tolerating the drug.", "B": "Halve the trastuzumab dose and continue, since dose reduction usually restores EF to normal.", "C": "Place a primary-prevention ICD now, since LVEF has reached the 35 percent device threshold.", "D": "Hold trastuzumab, start heart failure therapy, and arrange cardio-oncology review with repeat EF."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Continuing because of absent symptoms", "B": "Assuming dose reduction reverses a large EF fall", "C": "Applying device criteria to new cardiotoxicity"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-024_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold trastuzumab, start heart failure therapy, and arrange cardio-oncology review with repeat EF.): A 23-point fall to 35 percent meets the definition of cardiotoxicity even without symptoms. Trastuzumab-related dysfunction is often at least partly reversible after holding the drug and treating heart failure. Defibrillators require sustained low EF on optimized therapy, and dose halving lacks evidence of restoring function."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist reviewing the rotating raw projections of a gated rest SPECT reports that the heart slides slowly sideways about 14 mm with no up and down shift. She asks how much concern this deserves compared with an abrupt upward heart shift of similar size midway through acquisition. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Both motions are equally harmful, so either one requires immediate repeat acquisition", "B": "Neither needs review, because gating removes patient motion from the reconstructed slices", "C": "Sideways drift distorts slices less than an abrupt vertical shift, but still needs correction", "D": "Sideways drift is worse because it shifts the heart across every tomographic plane of the slices"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating all motion direction as equal in effect", "B": "Assuming gating corrects patient motion", "D": "Reversing which direction causes more damage"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-114_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sideways drift distorts slices less than an abrupt vertical shift, but still needs correction): Vertical shifts move the heart between transaxial planes and tend to produce larger and more visible artifacts than a gradual sideways drift of similar size. The lateral drift still blurs counts, so it should be reviewed on the projection images and corrected. Gating handles cardiac motion, not patient movement, and not every motion requires repeat acquisition."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man has new class III dyspnea despite three months of guideline-directed therapy for nonischemic cardiomyopathy. He is in sinus rhythm with a left bundle branch block and QRS duration of 168 ms. Gated blood-pool imaging gives LVEF 22 percent with a dilated cavity. Which device is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Cardiac resynchronization defibrillator, since LVEF is 35 percent or less with left bundle branch block", "B": "No device now, repeating the study after another year of therapy", "C": "Dual-chamber pacemaker, since the conduction delay is the main problem to correct", "D": "Single-chamber defibrillator alone, since LVEF is low and QRS prolongation adds nothing to the overall benefit"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "waits though criteria are already met", "C": "treats a conduction delay with bradycardia pacing logic", "D": "ignores the benefit of resynchronization in wide left bundle branch block"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-029_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cardiac resynchronization defibrillator, since LVEF is 35 percent or less with left bundle branch block): Resynchronization therapy is indicated in sinus rhythm with LVEF 35 percent or less, left bundle branch block, QRS 150 ms or longer and persistent class II to IV symptoms on therapy. Adding defibrillator capability covers the sudden-death risk of an EF of 22 percent. A defibrillator without resynchronization leaves the dyssynchrony untreated, a standard pacemaker does not resynchronize, and waiting is unjustified once criteria are met."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man has Tc-99m stress SPECT imaging after treadmill exercise. During review of the rotating raw projection images, the technologist uses the fixed horizontal line shown on the display. What is the main purpose of that reference line during this review?", "options": {"A": "To align the camera for the center of rotation correction.", "B": "To measure the left ventricular ejection fraction from the raw image set.", "C": "To judge change in heart position from one projection to the next.", "D": "To mark the boundary between normal and abnormal counts in the myocardium."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confuses position check with calibration", "B": "assigns a gated function task to the reference line", "D": "assigns a count threshold role to the reference line"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-103_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To judge change in heart position from one projection to the next.): A fixed line across the cine lets the reviewer see whether the heart sits at the same height in every projection, which is how motion is spotted. Ejection fraction comes from gated reconstructed data, count thresholds are set in processing, and center of rotation is a separate quality control test."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man has a Duke treadmill score of +6 and no angina during exercise. Gated Tc-99m sestamibi clips follow: Clip 1 is post-stress and Clip 2 is rest. Anterior and apical walls are akinetic after stress and normal at rest, with LVEF about 50 percent and 60 percent. How should this affect his risk assessment?", "options": {"A": "The low risk treadmill score settles the question, so no further evaluation is needed now", "B": "A 10 point fall at stress is expected in all patients and carries no added risk", "C": "The imaging pair is unreliable because he had no angina during stress", "D": "Stunning overrides the low risk treadmill score; consider angiography"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Letting the exercise score overrule imaging findings", "B": "Treating stunning as a normal response", "C": "Requiring symptoms for a valid imaging finding"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-129_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-129_2.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stunning overrides the low risk treadmill score; consider angiography): A Duke score of +5 or higher is low risk, yet post-stress stunning in the anterior descending territory with a 10 point LVEF fall indicates severe ischemia. Imaging evidence of high risk takes priority, so referral for angiography is reasonable. Absence of angina does not make the gated findings invalid."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52 year old man 3 months after a lateral NSTEMI with a 90 percent obtuse marginal stenosis has gated SPECT. The mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral walls are akinetic with preserved counts, and LVEF is 43 percent. Which management statement is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Implant a primary prevention defibrillator now, since akinetic myocardium predicts sudden death at any ejection fraction.", "B": "Repeat with attenuation correction, because normal counts cannot coexist with akinesis in one wall.", "C": "Revascularize the obtuse marginal territory; LVEF above 35 percent does not support a defibrillator.", "D": "Order late gadolinium MRI first, since SPECT counts cannot establish scar in the lateral wall."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "using akinesis rather than EF to select ICD therapy", "B": "denying that akinesis with preserved counts exists", "D": "claiming SPECT cannot establish viability or scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-039_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Revascularize the obtuse marginal territory; LVEF above 35 percent does not support a defibrillator.): Preserved counts in akinetic segments 11 and 12 indicate hibernation in the circumflex territory, which supports revascularization alongside medical therapy. Primary prevention ICD criteria rely on LVEF at or below 35 percent after adequate therapy, and here it is 43 percent. Akinesis with normal counts is a recognized pattern, and SPECT already addresses viability."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 65 year old man has a large anterior infarct, with a persistent apical aneurysm and severely reduced systolic function on his gated SPECT cine. He has no new symptoms today. Which complication is an apical aneurysm of this kind most directly associated with in the long term?", "options": {"A": "Aortic stenosis progression, since the dilated apex raises systolic pressure on the aortic valve.", "B": "Papillary muscle rupture, since the aneurysm weakens the wall that anchors the mitral valve.", "C": "Pericardial constriction, since the thin scar triggers calcified thickening of the pericardium.", "D": "Left ventricular mural thrombus, since slow flow within the aneurysmal cavity favors clot formation."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "invents a valve consequence", "B": "assigns an acute complication to a chronic aneurysm", "C": "confuses aneurysm with constrictive disease"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-075_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left ventricular mural thrombus, since slow flow within the aneurysmal cavity favors clot formation.): A dyskinetic apex has stagnant flow that favors mural thrombus, and embolization can follow. Papillary muscle rupture is an acute event within days of infarction, aneurysm does not worsen aortic stenosis, and constriction is a pericardial disease. Thrombus is therefore the complication tied to aneurysm, and it needs echocardiography or MRI to detect, since SPECT cannot show it."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A nuclear laboratory switches its gated SPECT acquisition from 16 frames to 8 frames per cardiac cycle to shorten processing. A 55-year-old man with a true ejection fraction near 58 percent is rescanned. Which effect on the gated results is most expected from the 8-frame protocol?", "options": {"A": "The ejection fraction is unreliable unless beat rejection discards over half of the acquired cycles.", "B": "The ejection fraction is slightly overestimated, since fewer frames sharpen end-diastole and shrink end-systolic volume.", "C": "The ejection fraction is slightly underestimated, since the true end-systolic frame is blurred with neighboring phases.", "D": "The ejection fraction is unchanged, since frame number alters only count density, not the volume curve."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confuses gating frames with beat rejection", "B": "reverses the direction of the frame effect", "D": "believes frame number has no effect"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-007_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The ejection fraction is slightly underestimated, since the true end-systolic frame is blurred with neighboring phases.): With fewer frames per R to R interval, each frame covers a longer slice of the cycle, so the true end-systolic minimum is blurred into adjacent phases. The measured end-systolic volume is therefore too large and the ejection fraction falls a few points compared with 16 frames. Count density per frame rises but this does not offset the temporal averaging, and beat rejection is a separate issue."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old woman in sinus rhythm with occasional atrial premature beats has gated SPECT acquired with a fixed 20 percent window. The technologist reports that frames 5 to 8 of the full cine are sparse and noisy, though the key frames shown look adequate, and the department stores list-mode data. What is the best salvage step?", "options": {"A": "Rebin the list-mode data with a narrower window excluding short and long beats, then recompute EF.", "B": "Reorient the short-axis slices, since misplaced axes starve the late frames of counts.", "C": "Apply scatter correction to the late-frame images and then recompute the EF.", "D": "Smooth the summed images with a heavier Butterworth filter to restore the sparse late frames."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Blaming reorientation for frame count loss", "C": "Applying a physics correction to a timing problem", "D": "Using filtering to fix a sampling defect"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-007_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rebin the list-mode data with a narrower window excluding short and long beats, then recompute EF.): List mode stores each event with its time stamp, so frames can be rebuilt after the fact with a different acceptance window. That lets the physician drop outlying beats and restore even sampling. Filtering, reorientation and scatter correction all work on counts already binned into frames and cannot rebuild a frame that was left unfilled."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 57-year-old man with frequent premature beats has a gated SPECT cine with fewer counts in the later frames, while perfusion images look adequate and the heart rate during acquisition was 72. The physicist asks which mechanism explains the declining count density across the cardiac cycle in this study.", "options": {"A": "Patient motion accumulates during acquisition, so later frames show more blur and apparently lower counts.", "B": "Tracer washout from myocardium during acquisition lowers counts progressively across the cardiac cycle.", "C": "Septal motion toward the cavity lowers diastolic counts because the wall thickens less in late frames.", "D": "Short beats end before the final frames fill, so late frames draw on fewer beats."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing a timing artifact to patient motion", "B": "Mixing up washout with within-cycle timing", "C": "Confusing wall motion with frame sampling"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-008_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Short beats end before the final frames fill, so late frames draw on fewer beats.): Each frame is a fixed slice of the average R-R interval. When a beat is shorter than average, the last frames of that cycle do not occur, so those frames collect counts from fewer beats. Myocardial washout over minutes is negligible within one cycle, wall thickening does not change count sampling, and patient motion does not track frame number."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has been on optimal medical therapy for four months. His echocardiogram last month gave an ejection fraction of 33 percent, while gated SPECT now gives 36 percent with a dilated cavity. His physician asks whether the SPECT alone changes the primary prevention ICD decision. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Yes, a value above 35 percent removes the indication, so the ICD is no longer considered.", "B": "Yes, the larger cavity volume on SPECT qualifies him whatever the measured ejection fraction is.", "C": "No, because SPECT ejection fraction is considered invalid and echocardiography must override it in the decision.", "D": "No, the values straddle 35 percent within variability, so all data guide the decision."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats one value above threshold as decisive", "B": "uses cavity size as an ICD criterion", "C": "declares SPECT invalid compared with echocardiography"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-013_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, the values straddle 35 percent within variability, so all data guide the decision.): Primary prevention ICD criteria use an ejection fraction of 35 percent or less on optimal therapy, and different modalities disagree by several points. A SPECT value of 36 and an echocardiographic value of 33 straddle the line within expected variability, so the decision should rest on the whole clinical picture and possibly a confirmatory measurement. No modality is invalid, and cavity volume is not a criterion."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old woman has chest pain and a mild troponin rise two days after her husband's sudden death. Resting Tc-99m sestamibi gated SPECT is reviewed, including the gated short-axis and vertical long-axis cine and the resting perfusion images. Calculated LVEF is 42 percent. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Normal study because the LVEF is above 40 percent; no follow-up imaging is needed.", "B": "Breast attenuation over the anterior wall; repeat with attenuation correction before reporting abnormality.", "C": "Apical stunning with preserved perfusion, as in stress cardiomyopathy; repeat imaging in several weeks.", "D": "Completed anterior infarction with apical scar; start secondary prevention and report left anterior descending scar."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring regional motion when global EF is borderline", "B": "Blaming attenuation for a motion abnormality", "D": "Calling wall motion loss scar despite preserved perfusion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-045_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apical stunning with preserved perfusion, as in stress cardiomyopathy; repeat imaging in several weeks.): Preserved resting counts with severely reduced apical motion is a perfusion-function mismatch, which fits stunning such as stress cardiomyopathy and predicts recovery over weeks, so repeat imaging is the rational follow-up. Scar would show matched count loss. Attenuation changes counts, not motion or thickening. A global EF of 42 percent is reduced and the regional abnormality still matters."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old man had exercise Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The first clip shows the post-stress gated study and the second shows the rest gated study. The circumflex territory moves poorly after stress, with LVEF about 52 percent after stress and about 62 percent at rest. What does the change in LVEF indicate?", "options": {"A": "A fall below a 15 point threshold is too small to carry any prognostic value", "B": "A 10 point fall exceeds the 5 point stunning threshold and signals high risk", "C": "A 10 point fall is a normal variation between two acquisitions on the same day", "D": "An LVEF of 52 percent is normal, so the pair of studies shows no abnormality"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a wrong, larger threshold for stunning", "C": "Believing variation reaches 10 points", "D": "Judging each value alone without comparing studies"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-125_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-125_2.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 10 point fall exceeds the 5 point stunning threshold and signals high risk): Post-stress LVEF falling 5 or more points below rest LVEF is accepted as evidence of stunning, an indicator of severe ischemia and higher risk. Here the fall is 10 points. Day to day variation is small, and an LVEF of 52 percent looks acceptable alone but is abnormal when compared with the 62 percent at rest."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with obesity and chest pressure is referred for rest and regadenoson stress imaging. The cardiology fellow asks why the gated Rb-82 images look sharper and less noisy than the SPECT sestamibi study from last year. Which property of the PET acquisition best explains the difference?", "options": {"A": "Coincidence detection with CT attenuation correction gives higher sensitivity and resolution than SPECT", "B": "PET gating collects more cardiac cycles because the acquisition lasts longer than a typical SPECT study", "C": "Positron emission produces a higher energy photon that scatters less inside the patient's tissue", "D": "Rubidium has a much longer physical half-life, so more counts accumulate during the gated acquisition window"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "assumes PET gated scans are longer than SPECT scans", "C": "confuses 511 keV energy with scatter behavior", "D": "inverts the 76-second half-life of rubidium"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-051_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Coincidence detection with CT attenuation correction gives higher sensitivity and resolution than SPECT): PET detects the two 511 keV annihilation photons in coincidence, which removes the need for a lead collimator and raises sensitivity severalfold. Measured attenuation correction and about 4 to 6 mm resolution give crisp wall edges. Rb-82 has a half-life of 76 seconds, so counts do not accumulate for long. The higher photon energy does not reduce scatter in a useful way, and PET acquisitions are shorter, not longer."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68 year old man has exertional angina and a known severe obtuse marginal stenosis. Rest gated SPECT shows severe hypokinesis of the mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral walls with preserved counts, and LVEF is 48 percent. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Viability cannot be judged from sestamibi counts, so FDG PET is required first.", "B": "Viable hibernating mid lateral myocardium likely to improve after revascularization of the obtuse marginal.", "C": "Right coronary disease, because the inferolateral wall is perfused by the posterior descending artery.", "D": "Nonviable transmural scar, because severe hypokinesis with near normal ejection fraction signals necrosis."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "believing sestamibi uptake cannot indicate viability", "C": "assigning lateral segments to the right coronary artery", "D": "reading hypokinesis as scar despite preserved counts"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-030_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Viable hibernating mid lateral myocardium likely to improve after revascularization of the obtuse marginal.): Sestamibi uptake requires intact membranes and mitochondria, so preserved resting counts in dysfunctional lateral segments (11 and 12) support viability, and the known circumflex marginal stenosis fits hibernation. PET is an option when SPECT is indeterminate but is not needed here. The lateral wall is not a right coronary territory, and scar would lower counts."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 47-year-old woman presents with three weeks of orthopnea. She completed anthracycline chemotherapy eight months ago and has no angina or vascular risk factors. Coronary CT angiography last month showed normal arteries. The gated blood-pool cine shows a dilated ventricle contracting poorly throughout, with LVEF 25 percent. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the blood-pool study in a week to confirm the reading before any treatment is started", "B": "Plan invasive coronary angiography now to exclude an ischemic cause of the dysfunction", "C": "Start heart failure therapy, stop further cardiotoxic agents and involve cardio-oncology for presumed cardiomyopathy", "D": "Implant a primary-prevention defibrillator now because LVEF is below 35 percent on this single study alone"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "delays therapy for a clearly reduced EF", "B": "repeats coronary testing already shown normal", "D": "skips the required period of medical therapy"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-027_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start heart failure therapy, stop further cardiotoxic agents and involve cardio-oncology for presumed cardiomyopathy): A dilated ventricle with global hypokinesis, recent anthracycline exposure and normal coronary arteries fits anthracycline cardiomyopathy. Treatment begins with guideline therapy and removal of further cardiotoxic exposure with oncology input. Repeat coronary testing adds little after a normal angiogram. A defibrillator is considered only after about three months of therapy if LVEF stays at or below 35 percent, and a one-week repeat would only postpone treatment."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman has a rest Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. In the raw cine a bright area lies below and behind the heart, reported as separate from the myocardium, and there is no patient motion. Reconstructed slices show a reduced inferior wall appearance. What is the best way to handle this finding?", "options": {"A": "Review the raw cine, read the inferior wall cautiously, and report the activity", "B": "Report a fixed inferior infarct, since reduced inferior wall counts on the slices mean scar", "C": "Call it a left ventricular aneurysm because it lies behind the heart on the cine", "D": "Delete the bright area during processing and report only the heart without mention of it"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reading an extracardiac artifact as infarction", "C": "Confusing an extracardiac structure with a cardiac one", "D": "Masking an extracardiac finding the clinician needs"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-121_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Review the raw cine, read the inferior wall cautiously, and report the activity): Intense activity adjacent to the inferior wall degrades slices through reconstruction filter effects and scatter, which can lower apparent counts in that wall. The raw cine shows the source is separate from the heart. The reader should interpret the inferior wall with caution, use other data such as thickening, and report the finding so it can be followed up."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A cardiology program wants to limit breast attenuation artifact in women referred for SPECT, and is choosing between Tl-201, which emits photons near 70 to 80 keV, and Tc-99m agents, which emit 140 keV photons. Which statement correctly applies photon energy to that choice?", "options": {"A": "Both are attenuated equally, since absorption does not depend on photon energy clinically.", "B": "Tl-201 is attenuated less than Tc-99m, since lower energy photons pass soft tissue more easily.", "C": "Tc-99m is attenuated less, since higher energy photons penetrate soft tissue better.", "D": "Tl-201 removes breast artifact, since its long half-life lets redistribution correct attenuated areas."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "ignores energy dependence of absorption", "B": "inverts the energy relationship", "D": "confuses half life with attenuation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-086_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tc-99m is attenuated less, since higher energy photons penetrate soft tissue better.): Attenuation falls as photon energy rises, so the 140 keV photons of Tc-99m are absorbed less than the 70 to 80 keV photons of Tl-201, and breast artifacts are usually milder with technetium agents. The other statements invert the energy effect, deny it, or invoke redistribution, which is a physiologic process and not a physical one."}, {"id": 840, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with a prior anthracycline course undergoes a gated rubidium-82 PET. The gated display shows a large cavity with uniformly reduced wall thickening, and the LVEF measures 40 percent. The report needs one sentence on prognostic meaning. Which statement is best supported by this finding?", "options": {"A": "A PET LVEF of 40 percent can be ignored because function is graded only on SPECT", "B": "Reduced LVEF with a dilated cavity indicates higher heart failure risk, whatever the perfusion", "C": "Normal perfusion means her prognosis is excellent whatever the measured LVEF value turns out to be", "D": "The risk of cardiac death is determined mainly by the stress blood pressure response"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "dismisses PET function as unimportant", "C": "uses perfusion alone to estimate prognosis", "D": "replaces ventricular function with a stress variable"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-057_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced LVEF with a dilated cavity indicates higher heart failure risk, whatever the perfusion): LVEF and cavity size are strong independent predictors of heart failure events and cardiac mortality, so they carry prognostic weight even when perfusion is normal. Normal perfusion lowers ischemia risk but does not cancel low function. PET gating gives reliable function data. Stress hemodynamics add information but do not replace LV function as a determinant of outcome."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old woman had exercise Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Clip 1 is post-stress and Clip 2 is rest. LVEF is about 43 percent after stress and 58 percent at rest. After stress the lateral and inferolateral walls barely move, while the septum and anterior wall keep moving. Which vessel territory is involved?", "options": {"A": "Left circumflex territory, since the lateral and inferolateral walls lose motion", "B": "A diffuse global process, since global EF fell after stress in the paired clips", "C": "Left anterior descending territory, because a larger EF drop implicates this artery", "D": "Right coronary territory, because the inferior wall is involved along with the lateral wall"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reading a regional wall motion change as global disease", "C": "Assigning territory from EF change rather than wall location", "D": "Treating inferolateral as the same as inferior"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-124_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-124_2.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left circumflex territory, since the lateral and inferolateral walls lose motion): The territory follows the segments that lose motion, not the size of the EF drop. Lateral and inferolateral walls belong to the circumflex distribution, while the septum and anterior wall belong to the anterior descending artery. A regional change with preserved motion elsewhere argues against a global cause."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with diabetes and atypical chest pain has regadenoson stress with Tc-99m sestamibi, and acquisition began 30 minutes after injection. The technologist reports an intense bright structure just below the heart on the rotating raw projections, abutting the inferior wall like bowel activity. Which action is most appropriate before the inferior wall is interpreted on the reconstructed slices?", "options": {"A": "Give several glasses of water, wait for tracer clearance, then reacquire before reading the inferior wall.", "B": "Report a fixed inferior defect and recommend angiography to evaluate for right coronary artery disease now.", "C": "Apply attenuation correction to the same data set and read the inferior wall as normal.", "D": "Reprocess with a sharper ramp cutoff so the extracardiac signal drops out of the slices."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "reads artifact as infarction", "C": "confuses attenuation correction with hot neighbor artifact", "D": "believes filter choice removes adjacent counts"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-089_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give several glasses of water, wait for tracer clearance, then reacquire before reading the inferior wall.): Bright activity beside the heart on the raw images makes the ramp filter subtract counts from the neighboring inferior wall, so the reconstruction cannot be trusted there. Water and a longer wait let bowel and liver activity clear, and reacquisition fixes the data itself. Calling an infarct treats artifact as disease, a sharper filter cannot remove counts already acquired, and attenuation correction addresses soft tissue loss, not a hot neighbor."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old woman with dyspnea has a normal-appearing gated SPECT with a small ventricle and an LVEF of 78 percent. A colleague says high EF with vigorous contraction means the heart is truly hyperdynamic. Which mechanism makes that conclusion unreliable?", "options": {"A": "Hyperdynamic function accompanies hypertrophy, so the EF is accurate for ventricles of any size.", "B": "Excess diastolic counts in the cavity exaggerate the stroke volume at end diastole in small hearts.", "C": "Partial volume effect makes a small end-systolic cavity look empty as wall counts spill into it.", "D": "Attenuation correction raises end-systolic counts and lowers the apparent systolic cavity volume."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming EF accuracy independent of size", "B": "Misattributing the bias to diastolic counts", "D": "Blaming attenuation correction for the EF bias"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-066_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Partial volume effect makes a small end-systolic cavity look empty as wall counts spill into it.): In small ventricles the wall is thick compared with the resolution of the camera, so wall counts spill into the cavity at end systole and the cavity seems to vanish, falsely raising EF. The error arises at end systole, not end diastole, and it is not caused by attenuation correction or independent of heart size."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman of small stature with hypertension and chest pain has resting and stress gated sestamibi SPECT. On the gated short-axis cine the end-diastolic volume measures 53 mL and the LVEF is 78 percent. How should this LVEF be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "The LVEF is a precise value proving hyperdynamic function and can be reported without qualification.", "B": "The LVEF above 70 percent indicates hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and requires cardiac magnetic resonance referral.", "C": "The LVEF is probably overestimated because partial volume effects distort small cavities; report that caveat.", "D": "The LVEF is falsely low in small hearts, so the true value exceeds 78 percent."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the number as precise in a small heart", "B": "Diagnosing cardiomyopathy from EF alone", "D": "Reversing the direction of the small cavity bias"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-064_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The LVEF is probably overestimated because partial volume effects distort small cavities; report that caveat.): In small cavities, partial volume effects and count spill make the end-systolic cavity look nearly empty, which inflates the gated SPECT EF. The value therefore should not be accepted as precise or used as a diagnosis. The bias is toward overestimation, and an EF number alone does not establish cardiomyopathy."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 55-year-old man had two hours of chest pain last week and has Q waves in leads III and aVF. A gated blood-pool cine is processed to LVEF 48 percent. The lateral and anterior walls contract well, while the inferior and inferoseptal walls move less. Which coronary artery supplies the abnormal region in a right-dominant system?", "options": {"A": "Left anterior descending artery, since the septum is involved in the abnormal motion", "B": "Right coronary artery, which supplies the inferior and inferoseptal walls", "C": "Left main coronary artery, since more than one wall shows reduced motion", "D": "Left circumflex artery, since inferior wall motion is reduced in the left ventricle"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assigns the septum to the left anterior descending artery regardless of segment", "C": "presumes multiple walls imply left main disease", "D": "assigns inferior wall to circumflex in a right-dominant system"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-031_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right coronary artery, which supplies the inferior and inferoseptal walls): In a right-dominant circulation the right coronary artery supplies the inferior and inferoseptal segments, which are the basal and mid inferior, inferoseptal and apical inferior segments. Q waves in III and aVF agree. The anterior and lateral walls contract normally, which argues against the left anterior descending and left circumflex territories, and left main disease would involve anterior and lateral walls together."}, {"id": 846, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man with prior anterior infarction has resting gated SPECT. The mid anterior, mid anteroseptal, apical anterior, apical septal and apical segments show reduced but present inward motion and thickening. Resting counts there are normal. Which description of the wall motion is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Dyskinesis in the circumflex territory, since reduced motion of the anterior wall means outward bulging.", "B": "Mild hypokinesis in the right coronary territory, since the apical and septal segments belong there.", "C": "Mild hypokinesis in the anterior descending territory, since the walls move inward but less than normal.", "D": "Akinesis in the anterior descending territory, since the abnormal segments have lost their inward motion."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses the wrong grade and vessel", "B": "assigns segments to the wrong vessel", "D": "uses the grade for absent motion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-020_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mild hypokinesis in the anterior descending territory, since the walls move inward but less than normal.): Hypokinesis means inward motion and thickening are reduced but still present, while akinesis means none and dyskinesis means outward motion. The affected mid anterior, anteroseptal, apical anterior, apical septal and apex segments are supplied by the left anterior descending artery. The right coronary and circumflex arteries do not supply this region."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man arrives six hours after primary PCI of the right coronary artery for an inferior STEMI. Gated Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT is acquired the next day and read with the long-axis and short-axis cine and resting perfusion images. LVEF is 37 percent. Which interpretation and plan are best supported?", "options": {"A": "Stunned inferior myocardium in the right coronary territory; function should recover within several weeks.", "B": "Diaphragmatic attenuation, which also produces akinesis; repeat imaging prone before reporting a wall motion abnormality.", "C": "Circumflex hibernation; refer for angiography of the lateral wall before this patient leaves the hospital.", "D": "Completed inferior infarct; plan a primary prevention ICD now because the LVEF is 37 percent."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing attenuation causes akinesis", "C": "Assigning inferior segments to the circumflex", "D": "Judging EF for ICD too soon after infarction"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-050_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stunned inferior myocardium in the right coronary territory; function should recover within several weeks.): Preserved resting counts with akinetic inferior segments in the territory of a just-reperfused right coronary artery is stunning, which usually recovers, so EF should be reassessed later; ICD decisions are made after waiting at least 40 days after infarction. Attenuation reduces counts but not motion, and the inferior wall is not the circumflex territory."}, {"id": 848, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 77-year-old man with atrial fibrillation and a ventricular rate of 120 is scheduled for gated SPECT because of exertional dyspnea. His first attempt a week ago, done at the same rate, gave a poor cine with sparse late frames. What is the best strategy for the repeat study?", "options": {"A": "Repeat at the same rate using 16 frames, since finer sampling offsets irregular R-R intervals.", "B": "Cancel gating and report an EF estimated from the perfusion defect size on summed images.", "C": "Inject a larger dose and extend acquisition, since low counts explain the poor late-frame quality.", "D": "Control the rate if safe, then reacquire with beat rejection, keeping ungated perfusion if gating fails."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using more frames to offset irregular beats", "B": "Estimating EF from perfusion alone", "C": "Attributing poor gating to dose rather than timing"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-012_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Control the rate if safe, then reacquire with beat rejection, keeping ungated perfusion if gating fails.): Irregular and rapid beats are the main enemy of gating. Rate control narrows the spread of cycle lengths, and rejection further removes outliers. If gating remains inadequate, perfusion from ungated images is still valid and should be reported with that limit. Frame number, dose and acquisition time do not repair irregular cycle length, and EF cannot be inferred from defect size."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 56-year-old woman has a rubidium-82 PET for atypical chest pain. Uptake is markedly reduced, near one quarter of peak, in the mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral walls with reduced thickening, and the other walls are normal. Which coronary artery is the most likely culprit?", "options": {"A": "The left anterior descending artery, because the anterolateral wall is fed by its diagonal branches", "B": "The right coronary artery, because the inferolateral wall is supplied by its posterolateral branches", "C": "The left circumflex artery, because the lateral segments lie in its distribution", "D": "The posterior descending artery, because inferior wall segments below the base are in its field"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats the anterolateral wall as anterior descending territory", "B": "gives a lateral wall to the right coronary", "D": "mislocates the inferior wall in a lateral defect"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-063_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The left circumflex artery, because the lateral segments lie in its distribution): The mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral segments are in the left circumflex territory. A diagonal branch of the left anterior descending can supply the anterolateral wall in some anatomies, but inferolateral involvement together with it points to the circumflex. The right coronary and posterior descending supply the inferior wall and are normal here."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "When screening a rotating raw projection cine from a patient with chest pain before reconstruction, a physician looks for features of an adequate study. Which pair of observations best shows that the raw data are good enough to process without repeating anything?", "options": {"A": "The heart stays centered and steady across projections, with no sudden position shifts between neighbouring frames.", "B": "The heart changes position between frames, and the rotation is slowed for extra averaging.", "C": "The heart is brighter than the liver in every projection, and lung uptake is heavy throughout.", "D": "The bowel is brighter than the heart in all views, with intense uptake above the apex."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "accepts movement as normal", "C": "uses lung uptake as a quality sign", "D": "accepts extracardiac uptake as normal"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-077_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The heart stays centered and steady across projections, with no sudden position shifts between neighbouring frames.): A good raw cine keeps the myocardium at a fixed position, with no abrupt shifts from patient movement. Heavy lung uptake, drifting position, or bright adjacent bowel all signal problems. Only the centered, smooth cine supports proceeding to reconstruction without repeating the acquisition."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man with chest pain and a large abdomen undergoes stress SPECT showing an inferior and inferoseptal count reduction. The gated cine is reviewed. LVEF is 60 percent. Which gated finding best separates attenuation from infarction in these segments?", "options": {"A": "A mildly enlarged cavity at stress compared with rest, which indicates a true infarct.", "B": "Preserved systolic thickening and motion of the inferior wall, which a transmural infarct would lose.", "C": "Counts below half of peak in the inferior wall, which distinguishes scar from artifact.", "D": "Resting counts equally reduced as at stress, which occurs in both attenuation artifact and scar."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Citing cavity size as a discriminator", "C": "Using count severity as a discriminator", "D": "Using a nondiscriminating fixed defect finding"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-061_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Preserved systolic thickening and motion of the inferior wall, which a transmural infarct would lose.): Gated motion and thickening discriminate: attenuation lowers counts but not wall thickening, while a transmural infarct lowers both. A fixed defect appears in both conditions, so it cannot separate them, and defect severity overlaps heavily with attenuation in obese patients."}, {"id": 852, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man with atrial fibrillation and frequent ventricular ectopy has a gated equilibrium blood pool study before anthracycline therapy. In the gated cine, the last third of the cardiac cycle looks dim and grainy compared with early frames, and the processed LVEF is 45 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Review the R-R histogram, apply bad-beat rejection, and reacquire with a longer acquisition time", "B": "Widen the energy window to thirty percent to recover counts lost from the late frames", "C": "Report the LVEF of 45 percent as acquired, since the early and mid-cycle frames define volumes", "D": "Repeat the study in the left anterior oblique view after repositioning to improve septal separation"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "uses an energy window change to fix R-R variability", "C": "assumes late-frame count loss cannot affect the result", "D": "treats a gating problem as a positioning problem"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-049_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Review the R-R histogram, apply bad-beat rejection, and reacquire with a longer acquisition time): Dim, noisy late frames mean fewer counts were collected there, which happens when irregular R-R intervals are mixed into one cycle. The R-R histogram shows the spread, and bad-beat rejection with a tighter acceptance window, plus a longer acquisition to rebuild counts, gives a trustworthy curve. Reporting the number as acquired accepts a distorted time-activity curve. Repositioning addresses septal overlap, not trigger quality. Energy window width changes scatter acceptance, not the fixed number of frames collected per beat."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old man finished treadmill exercise and Tc-99m sestamibi injection 12 minutes ago. The software reports an abrupt 20 mm upward shift during the acquisition just completed, and the technologist asks how best to proceed. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Wait four hours for the tracer to clear, then repeat the stress acquisition.", "B": "Reconstruct the same data with a stronger filter, and report the study as adequate.", "C": "Repeat the stress acquisition now, since the injected tracer remains in the myocardium.", "D": "Re-stress the patient the next day with a new injection before any repeat imaging is done."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "applies a rest waiting period to a repeat", "B": "uses a filter to repair motion", "D": "believes the tracer must be reinjected"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-100_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the stress acquisition now, since the injected tracer remains in the myocardium.): Technetium agents trap in the myocardium after injection and do not redistribute quickly, so a repeat acquisition shortly afterward records the same stress perfusion pattern without another stress. Re-stressing wastes time and dose, a four hour wait loses image quality and delays the rest study, and no filter can repair misaligned projections."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman completes treadmill stress with Tc-99m tetrofosmin, and the technologist reviews the rotating raw projection images for quality, comparing the heart with the fixed horizontal line. Which display best confirms the quality problem, and what would it show?", "options": {"A": "The gated cine, showing reduced thickening limited to the apex.", "B": "The reversibility map, showing paired reduction of counts in the septum.", "C": "The sinogram, showing an abrupt step in the sinusoidal trace at one projection.", "D": "The polar map, showing a uniform fall in counts across all segments of the myocardium."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses function display for a positional artifact", "B": "confuses stress rest comparison with motion screen", "D": "uses a processed display that cannot show projection level motion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-097_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The sinogram, showing an abrupt step in the sinusoidal trace at one projection.): Patient motion is checked on the raw data, and the sinogram displays one detector row against projection angle, so a sudden shift appears as a step or break in the smooth sinusoidal trace. Polar maps, gated function and reversibility maps are processed displays that cannot tell motion from disease reliably, so they cannot confirm the quality problem."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with persistent atrial fibrillation, ventricular rate near 95, has rest Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT with 8-frame gating and a 100 percent R-R acceptance window. Cine quality varies across the cardiac cycle. Which change would most improve a repeat gated acquisition?", "options": {"A": "Switch to 16-frame gating so end-diastolic frames are sampled more finely despite the irregular beats.", "B": "Narrow the acceptance window with arrhythmia rejection, or acquire in list mode and rebin beats later.", "C": "Double the time per projection with the same window so late frames gain counts.", "D": "Move to prone positioning to separate inferior wall from bowel activity and improve cine."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Thinking more frames fix irregular beat lengths", "C": "Treating a trigger problem as a count problem", "D": "Applying a position change for a gating fault"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-002_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Narrow the acceptance window with arrhythmia rejection, or acquire in list mode and rebin beats later.): A 100 percent window accepts every beat, so long and short beats in atrial fibrillation are all binned together and late frames become sparse. Restricting acceptance to beats near the mean length, or storing list-mode data for later rebinning, targets that problem. More frames split counts further, longer projections keep the same unevenness, and prone imaging addresses subdiaphragmatic attenuation instead."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man has a rest Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. In the rotating raw projection images the heart slides gradually toward one side of the field over the acquisition, with no up and down shift. What is the expected consequence of this lateral drift on the reconstructed myocardium if it is left uncorrected?", "options": {"A": "Blurred myocardial walls with artifactual count loss that can mimic a perfusion defect", "B": "A uniform rise in counts across every wall because the heart stays in view", "C": "No visible change in the slices because sideways shifts cancel between opposing projections", "D": "A fixed inferior wall defect produced by attenuation of photons passing through the diaphragm"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing motion adds counts rather than smearing them", "C": "Assuming lateral shifts average out across the orbit", "D": "Confusing motion with soft tissue attenuation artifact"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-111_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Blurred myocardial walls with artifactual count loss that can mimic a perfusion defect): During reconstruction each projection is assumed to show the heart at the same position. A sideways drift places the myocardium at different positions in different projections, so counts are smeared across the walls and some segments lose apparent counts, which can look like a perfusion defect. Attenuation artifact has a fixed location tied to anatomy, and shifts do not cancel or add counts."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A cardiologist reviews a gated rubidium-82 PET with normal perfusion and an LVEF of 60 percent in a 48-year-old man. Compared with SPECT, which characteristic of this study is a direct advantage for the reader when judging perfusion in the inferior and lateral walls?", "options": {"A": "Routine CT-based attenuation correction and higher resolution reduce soft tissue artifact in those walls", "B": "The tracer redistributes fully over four hours, so delayed images can separate scar from ischemia", "C": "Cardiac gating is unnecessary because PET captures perfusion and function in one single frame", "D": "Image acquisition relies on a lead collimator that improves septal and apical count uniformity over SPECT"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "attributes thallium-like redistribution to rubidium", "C": "confuses PET timing with the need for gating", "D": "assigns SPECT hardware to a PET system"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-053_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Routine CT-based attenuation correction and higher resolution reduce soft tissue artifact in those walls): PET corrects attenuation with measured transmission or CT data and has higher resolution, so inferior and lateral soft tissue shadowing is less of a problem than on SPECT. Rubidium does not redistribute; stress and rest are separate injections. PET has no lead collimator because it uses coincidence detection. Gating remains necessary to measure function and wall motion."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist asks how to resolve an equivocal inferior finding on a 51-year-old man's sestamibi SPECT. He has BMI 38 and the gated cine looks brisk. LVEF is 64 percent. Which acquisition change would best address a soft tissue artifact?", "options": {"A": "Switch to Tl-201 because its lower photon energy is attenuated less by the diaphragm.", "B": "Repeat the acquisition prone, which shifts the diaphragm and abdominal contents away from the inferior wall.", "C": "Increase acquisition time per projection to improve count statistics and eliminate the inferior defect.", "D": "Add a second pharmacologic stress agent to unmask ischemia in the inferior wall."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the energy and attenuation relationship", "C": "Treating attenuation as a count statistics problem", "D": "Using stress to fix an artifact"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-058_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the acquisition prone, which shifts the diaphragm and abdominal contents away from the inferior wall.): Prone positioning moves the inferior wall away from the diaphragm and abdominal organs, which reduces attenuation. More counts do not remove absorption, and Tl-201 emits lower energy photons that are attenuated more, not less. Additional stress addresses ischemia, not an artifact."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman has been newly diagnosed with nonischemic cardiomyopathy. Gated SPECT shows a markedly dilated cavity with an ejection fraction of 25 percent and no perfusion defect. She started guideline directed medical therapy two weeks ago and is in NYHA class III. What is the best next step regarding defibrillator therapy?", "options": {"A": "Implant a primary prevention ICD now, since an ejection fraction of 25 percent already meets criteria.", "B": "Continue medical therapy and repeat the ejection fraction at three months, deferring ICD.", "C": "Start amiodarone for sudden death prevention, since a drug is preferred with a dilated cavity.", "D": "Repeat the gated SPECT in two weeks, since ICD decisions need two consecutive low ejection fractions."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "ignores the three month therapy period", "C": "substitutes an antiarrhythmic for ICD criteria", "D": "invents a repeat interval rule"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-010_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue medical therapy and repeat the ejection fraction at three months, deferring ICD.): Primary prevention ICD in nonischemic cardiomyopathy is considered when the ejection fraction stays at or below 35 percent after at least three months of optimal medical therapy. Many patients improve with treatment, so a study taken two weeks into therapy is too early to decide. Amiodarone has no survival benefit for this purpose, and repeating imaging after two weeks does not answer the question."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old woman receiving trastuzumab has serial gated blood pool studies. Today the rotating cine shows later frames visibly noisier than frames at the start of the cycle, and the R-R histogram is broad. The technologist asks what most likely explains the appearance. Which explanation and response is best?", "options": {"A": "Patient motion occurred during the study, so strap the arms and rerun the same acquisition", "B": "Dead time from excess activity occurred, so reduce the injected dose before the next attempt", "C": "Red cell labeling efficiency was poor, so repeat the in vivo labeling with more stannous pyrophosphate", "D": "Variable beat lengths leave fewer counts in late frames; narrow the acceptance window and acquire longer"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "mistakes motion for a late-cycle gating pattern", "B": "attributes a gating defect to count-rate saturation", "C": "blames labeling for a frame-specific count loss"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-050_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Variable beat lengths leave fewer counts in late frames; narrow the acceptance window and acquire longer): With a broad histogram, short and long beats are mixed. Frames at the end of the cycle exist only in the longer beats, so they collect fewer counts and look noisy. Narrowing the acceptance window rejects extreme beats and a longer acquisition restores statistics. Poor labeling lowers blood pool contrast in all frames equally. Motion blurs every frame rather than dimming the late ones. Dead time lowers counts across the whole cycle, not selectively at the end."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old man with exertional chest pain has a rubidium-82 PET showing a severe reduction to about one quarter of peak in the apical anterior wall, apical septum and apex, with reduced thickening. The remaining walls are normal. Which finding best explains why the apex is involved in this territory?", "options": {"A": "The apex has no dominant vessel, so its count loss is usually an attenuation artifact", "B": "The distal left anterior descending artery wraps around the apex and supplies the apical segments", "C": "The left circumflex artery supplies the apex through its obtuse marginals in most hearts", "D": "The right coronary artery wraps around the apex and supplies the apical segments in most hearts"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "dismisses apical count loss as an artifact", "C": "assigns the apex to the circumflex", "D": "gives the apex to the right coronary in all anatomy"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-064_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The distal left anterior descending artery wraps around the apex and supplies the apical segments): The left anterior descending artery commonly reaches and wraps around the apex, so a severe apical anterior, apical septal and apical defect fits a stenosis or scar in that vessel. The right coronary supplies the inferior wall and the circumflex the lateral wall. A severe, territorial defect with reduced thickening is not explained by apical attenuation."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A gated SPECT study on a 60-year-old man reports a left ventricular ejection fraction of 60 percent and an end-diastolic volume of 104 mL. A cardiology fellow is asked to quote the end-systolic volume for the report. Which value is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 62 mL, the stroke volume obtained by multiplying the end-diastolic volume by the ejection fraction.", "B": "About 173 mL, found by dividing the end-diastolic volume by the ejection fraction.", "C": "About 42 mL, since 40 percent of the end-diastolic volume remains in the cavity after systole.", "D": "About 44 mL, found by subtracting the ejection fraction percentage directly from the end-diastolic volume."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "reports stroke volume as end-systolic volume", "B": "inverts the ejection fraction relation", "D": "subtracts percent directly from milliliters"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-005_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 42 mL, since 40 percent of the end-diastolic volume remains in the cavity after systole.): Ejection fraction equals stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, so stroke volume is 0.60 times 104, about 62 mL. End-systolic volume is the end-diastolic volume minus the stroke volume, 104 minus 62, about 42 mL, which equals 40 percent of 104. The 62 mL value is the stroke volume, the 44 mL value mixes percent with milliliters, and 173 mL comes from dividing instead of multiplying."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70 year old man with a right dominant coronary system had an inferior infarction eight months ago. Gated SPECT shows akinesis of the basal, mid and apical inferior segments with counts near 25 percent of peak, and LVEF is 40 percent. Which conclusion about the inferior wall is best?", "options": {"A": "Diaphragmatic attenuation lowers inferior counts and also explains the akinetic inferior wall on cine.", "B": "Circumflex scar, because the circumflex supplies the entire inferior wall in right dominance.", "C": "Hibernating inferior myocardium, since akinesis with any residual counts predicts recovery after revascularization.", "D": "Nonviable inferior scar; revascularizing the right coronary artery would not recover this wall."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "invoking attenuation to explain a true motion abnormality", "B": "assigning the inferior wall to the circumflex artery", "C": "treating minimal residual counts as proof of viability"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-031_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nonviable inferior scar; revascularizing the right coronary artery would not recover this wall.): Segments 4, 10 and 15 are the inferior wall, supplied by the right coronary artery in right dominance. Counts near 25 percent of peak alongside akinesis mean transmural scar, and recovery is unlikely. Attenuation changes counts but cannot make a wall stop moving, and a trace of uptake is not the same as viability."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with severe shoulder arthritis has Tc-99m tetrofosmin imaging with her arms overhead. During review of the rotating raw projections, the technologist sees the heart change position relative to the fixed line partway through. Which explanation and plan are best?", "options": {"A": "A sudden body shift from arm discomfort, so repeat with the arms well supported.", "B": "Gut activity beside the heart, so give water and delay imaging again.", "C": "Gradual post exercise creep, so wait fifteen minutes and image again from the start.", "D": "A detector alignment fault, so recalibrate the center of rotation and reconstruct."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "mistakes motion for extracardiac activity", "C": "mistakes abrupt change for physiologic creep", "D": "blames the gantry rather than the patient"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-101_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A sudden body shift from arm discomfort, so repeat with the arms well supported.): An abrupt change in position fits a patient shift, and arm discomfort in a patient with shoulder arthritis is a plausible trigger, so the repeat should address comfort and support. Creep is gradual and physiologic, hot neighbor activity changes counts rather than heart position, and a center of rotation fault would show a ring or point source blur, not a one time jump."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a prior lateral myocardial infarction has gated SPECT. In the horizontal long-axis panel of the cine, with the septum at the top, the lower lateral wall moves outward during systole while resting counts there remain preserved. Which description of the abnormal region best fits?", "options": {"A": "Hypokinesis of the basal and mid inferior segments, which lie in the right coronary territory.", "B": "Akinesis of the mid anteroseptal and anterior segments, which lie in the left anterior descending territory.", "C": "Dyskinesis of the apical anterior and apical septal segments, which lie in the anterior descending territory.", "D": "Dyskinesis of the mid inferolateral and anterolateral segments, which lie in the left circumflex territory."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "names the wrong grade and RCA territory", "B": "names the wrong motion grade and territory", "C": "names the right grade at wrong location"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-018_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dyskinesis of the mid inferolateral and anterolateral segments, which lie in the left circumflex territory.): In the horizontal long axis the septum is at the top and the lateral wall at the bottom, so the abnormal wall is lateral. Outward paradoxical systolic motion is dyskinesis, which is a different grade from akinesis or hypokinesis. The mid inferolateral and anterolateral segments are supplied by the left circumflex artery, not by the left anterior descending or the right coronary artery."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with persistent atrial fibrillation and a variable ventricular rate needs a gated blood-pool study. A trial acquisition has noisy, dim late frames in the cycle. The scanner offers frame mode and list mode. Which approach gives the most reliable data?", "options": {"A": "Use frame mode with a wide acceptance window so no beats are discarded from the average", "B": "Acquire in list mode, then reject beats outside a narrow R-R window during reconstruction", "C": "Cancel the study because gating is impossible in atrial fibrillation", "D": "Use frame mode and double the number of frames per cycle"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "accepts irregular beats and corrupts the late frames", "C": "assumes gating cannot work in irregular rhythm", "D": "raises frame count without addressing irregular cycle lengths"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-046_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acquire in list mode, then reject beats outside a narrow R-R window during reconstruction): List mode records every event with its timing, so beats can be sorted and rejected after the study using a narrow R-R window, which gives a clean average cycle even in an irregular rhythm. Frame mode fixes the framing in real time and cannot be reprocessed, and a wide window or more frames leaves the late frames degraded. Gating is possible in atrial fibrillation with list mode and selective rejection."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man, BMI 33, has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi imaging for dyspnea. The reconstructed stress images show reduced inferior wall counts. A reader suspects diaphragmatic attenuation as the cause. Which observation on the rotating raw projections would best support a different explanation?", "options": {"A": "Bright lung uptake above the heart on the first anterior projection.", "B": "Activity brighter than the myocardium lying directly against the inferior wall in many projections.", "C": "A heart that stays at the same height against the horizontal reference line throughout.", "D": "A soft tissue shadow over the inferior wall on lateral projections."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "describes lung uptake rather than the inferior neighbor", "C": "describes motion instead of extracardiac activity", "D": "describes true attenuation instead of hot neighbor"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-093_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Activity brighter than the myocardium lying directly against the inferior wall in many projections.): Diaphragmatic attenuation is a dimming of counts by tissue and does not require any bright structure nearby. A very bright structure touching the inferior wall suggests the filter artifact, since the ramp filter removes counts from the adjacent myocardium. Motion appears as a shift in position, and lung uptake sits above the heart, not against the inferior wall."}, {"id": 868, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old woman receiving adjuvant chemotherapy had a baseline gated blood-pool LVEF of 54 percent. After the first cycle the study is repeated with the same camera, view and processing, and LVEF is reported as 50 percent. She is asymptomatic and the cine shows symmetric contraction. Which interpretation and plan are best?", "options": {"A": "The change is unreliable, so repeat the injection that same day to average the results", "B": "An ejection fraction of 50 percent is abnormal, so stop the drug and refer to cardiology", "C": "A 4-point change is within reproducibility, so continue treatment with scheduled monitoring", "D": "This meets cardiotoxicity criteria, so withhold the drug and begin heart failure therapy now"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "repeats the study to average values that are already reproducible", "B": "applies an absolute value as a stop rule", "D": "reads a small change as a clinically significant decline"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-038_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A 4-point change is within reproducibility, so continue treatment with scheduled monitoring): Serial radionuclide ejection fraction varies by roughly 3 to 5 points with identical technique, so a fall from 54 to 50 percent is not a real decline. Cardiotoxicity decisions usually require a fall of 10 points or more, particularly to a low value, and a symmetric cine in an asymptomatic patient supports continuing with planned monitoring. Withholding drug or stopping permanently is not justified, and same-day repeat adds radiation without new information."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57 year old woman with large breasts has a fixed anterior defect on a one day sestamibi study. Thickening on the gated study is borderline and she is at intermediate risk. Her laboratory has no CT attenuation correction. Which step best resolves whether the defect is real?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a thallium redistribution study at 24 hours to separate soft tissue loss from scar.", "B": "Repeat supine at a slower rotation speed, since poor resolution is the likely cause.", "C": "Obtain a prone acquisition, since artifact should resolve and true scar persists.", "D": "Report scar and refer for revascularization planning, since no other test is possible without CT."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses redistribution for an artifact question", "B": "blames camera resolution for tissue loss", "D": "treats limited equipment as proof of scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-083_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain a prone acquisition, since artifact should resolve and true scar persists.): Without CT attenuation correction, repositioning the patient is the practical way to test an attenuation artifact: prone imaging moves the breast off the anterior wall, so an artifact disappears and scar remains. Slower rotation does not change tissue absorption, and a redistribution study answers a viability question, not an attenuation one."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 47 year old woman with large breasts has a fixed anterior defect on sestamibi SPECT, normal thickening, and no coronary risk factors. A referring surgeon wants wording that prevents needless invasive testing. Which statement for the report best achieves that without overcalling or undercalling disease?", "options": {"A": "Anterior ischemia cannot be excluded, so angiography is advised for all women with this finding.", "B": "Fixed anterior defect representing prior infarction, with high risk for cardiac death.", "C": "Nondiagnostic study because the defect is anterior, with no conclusion on perfusion or function.", "D": "Fixed anterior defect with normal thickening most likely reflects attenuation, so risk is low."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "recommends reflex angiography for every finding", "B": "labels artifact as infarct", "C": "calls the whole study nondiagnostic"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-084_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fixed anterior defect with normal thickening most likely reflects attenuation, so risk is low.): A report should convey a confident, evidence based reading: a fixed defect with preserved thickening in a woman with large breasts and low pretest probability is an attenuation artifact, and the risk is low. Calling it infarction or advising reflex angiography contradicts the gated data, and calling the study nondiagnostic discards usable perfusion and function results."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old man with diabetes and hypertension has a SPECT study read with the gated short-axis and long-axis cine and the perfusion images. The inferior and inferoseptal count reduction is read with the cine, and LVEF is 60 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Right coronary infarction, because count reduction in that territory is evidence of myocardial necrosis here.", "B": "Mixed scar and ischemia, requiring a stress-only repeat study after one week of high-dose statin therapy.", "C": "Probable inferior attenuation; normal motion and thickening argue against infarction, so do not report scar.", "D": "Hibernating inferior myocardium, because reduced counts with normal function is a recognized hibernation pattern."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating inferior count loss with necrosis", "B": "Inventing scar from normal function", "D": "Reversing the perfusion-function mismatch"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-059_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Probable inferior attenuation; normal motion and thickening argue against infarction, so do not report scar.): Reduced counts with normal wall motion and thickening point to a fixed artifact such as diaphragmatic attenuation, not scar, because infarcted tissue stops thickening. Hibernation is the opposite mismatch, with preserved counts and reduced motion. Scar would show reduced motion."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman with atypical chest discomfort has gated Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT. The vertical long-axis cine shows the extreme apical tip is dimmer and thinner than the walls beside it, yet it still moves toward the base in systole. How should this apical appearance be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Apical hypokinesis from stress cardiomyopathy, so repeat imaging with a different tracer should be recommended.", "B": "A fixed apical defect from a distal anterior descending infarct, so angiography should be recommended.", "C": "A normal variant from apical thinning and partial volume effect, not reported as abnormal.", "D": "A tracer extraction problem at the apex, so the entire study should be called technically nondiagnostic."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "calls preserved apical motion hypokinesis", "B": "reads normal apical thinning as infarct", "D": "calls a normal variant a nondiagnostic study"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-002_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A normal variant from apical thinning and partial volume effect, not reported as abnormal.): The true apex is only a few millimeters thick, so partial volume effect lowers its apparent counts even in healthy hearts. Preserved inward motion and brightening of the same tip show that it contracts normally, which separates a normal variant from scar or stunning. Calling it infarct or technical failure would send a normal patient to unnecessary testing."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old woman had a stress study acceptable after exercise, and her rest Tc-99m sestamibi images show intense extracardiac activity beside the inferior wall on the rotating raw projections. The technologist started acquiring 20 minutes after injection. Which change for the repeat rest acquisition is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Repeat at 20 minutes again but rotate the camera over a larger arc.", "B": "Start imaging 10 minutes after injection so less tracer has time to reach the bowel region.", "C": "Reduce the injected rest dose so the extracardiac activity falls below the heart.", "D": "Wait about 60 minutes after injection with water, then repeat the rest acquisition."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "changes geometry rather than clearance", "B": "applies exercise timing to a rest study", "C": "thinks lower dose changes the ratio"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-095_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wait about 60 minutes after injection with water, then repeat the rest acquisition.): Rest injections have no exercise to divert blood from the splanchnic bed, so hepatobiliary clearance needs a longer wait, commonly 45 to 60 minutes with Tc-99m agents. Earlier imaging brings more extracardiac activity, a different arc does not change what is in the body, and a smaller dose lowers heart and gut counts together."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man with hypertension had a left anterior descending stent placed for unstable angina three days ago. Resting Tc-99m sestamibi gated SPECT is read using the short-axis and vertical long-axis cine and the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 59 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Normal study, because an LVEF of 59 percent excludes a regional wall motion abnormality here.", "B": "Established anterior scar; report an infarct because wall motion loss in a vascular territory means necrosis.", "C": "Mild stunning with preserved perfusion in the anterior territory; expect recovery and report no infarct.", "D": "Hibernating myocardium needing urgent repeat revascularization despite the recent stent and normal global function."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusting global EF to exclude regional disease", "B": "Equating motion loss with necrosis", "D": "Confusing stunning with hibernation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-048_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mild stunning with preserved perfusion in the anterior territory; expect recovery and report no infarct.): Preserved resting counts with mildly reduced anterior and apical motion fits stunning after a recent ischemic event and reperfusion; recovery usually follows over days to weeks. Necrosis would show reduced counts. Hibernation implies chronic flow limitation, which a freshly stented vessel argues against. A normal global EF does not exclude a regional abnormality."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with right-dominant coronary anatomy and a chronic occlusion of the right coronary artery has dyspnea. Resting gated SPECT is acquired and the short-axis and long-axis cine are read with the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 43 percent. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Inferior attenuation; repeat the acquisition prone to improve the apparent count loss and wall motion.", "B": "Viable inferior myocardium with preserved counts; consider revascularization of the right coronary artery territory.", "C": "Viable inferior myocardium in the circumflex territory, because the inferior wall is supplied by the circumflex.", "D": "Nonviable inferior scar; offer medical therapy because akinetic segments cannot recover after revascularization is performed."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing attenuation causes akinesis", "C": "Applying left dominance to a right-dominant system", "D": "Treating akinesis as proof of scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-052_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Viable inferior myocardium with preserved counts; consider revascularization of the right coronary artery territory.): The inferior wall is right coronary territory when the system is right dominant. Akinesis with preserved resting counts is a mismatch that supports viable, stunned or hibernating tissue, which can improve with revascularization. Scar would reduce counts. Attenuation reduces counts but never abolishes thickening."}, {"id": 876, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70 year old man had a large anterior infarction years ago. His gated cine shows dyskinesis of segments 13 through 17, which are the apical anterior, apical septal, apical inferior, apical lateral and apex segments. Which coronary lesion most likely explains scar that wraps around the whole apex?", "options": {"A": "Left anterior descending occlusion, since it wraps around the apex.", "B": "Posterior descending artery occlusion in a right dominant system, supplying the apex through the septum.", "C": "Proximal right coronary artery occlusion, since the apical inferior segment belongs to its territory.", "D": "Left circumflex occlusion, since the apical lateral segment lies in its territory."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "picks a branch that stops short of the apex", "C": "assigns the whole apex to the right coronary", "D": "assigns the whole apex to the circumflex"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-074_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left anterior descending occlusion, since it wraps around the apex.): The left anterior descending artery supplies the anterior and septal walls and usually wraps around the apex to reach the apical inferior wall, so a single large infarct there can involve segments 13 through 17. The right and circumflex arteries each supply at most a single apical segment. Scar across all five apical segments therefore points to the left anterior descending artery."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with a BMI of 34 and a remote anterior infarction has resting SPECT. The anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex show mildly reduced inward motion, and resting counts in those same segments are modestly lower than in other walls. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Stunned myocardium, since lower counts with reduced motion in the same walls are typical of stunning.", "B": "Matched mild motion and count loss indicate partial thickness scar, more likely than attenuation.", "C": "Breast attenuation artifact, since an anterior count reduction with high BMI is artifactual despite abnormal motion.", "D": "Hibernating myocardium, since hibernation is the usual diagnosis for mildly lowered counts in a remote infarct."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "applies stunning to matched count loss", "C": "attributes a motion abnormality to attenuation", "D": "applies hibernation to a remote infarct"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-022_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Matched mild motion and count loss indicate partial thickness scar, more likely than attenuation.): A true perfusion defect with matching hypokinesis points to scar, here partial thickness because the changes are mild. Attenuation lowers counts without altering wall motion or thickening, so abnormal motion argues against artifact. Stunning and hibernation are expected to show preserved resting counts or a flow-limited stenosis, and neither fits a remote infarct with matched loss."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with dyspnea undergoes gated SPECT. After normalization to the brightest segment, the mid and basal lateral walls show the highest counts, while the basal septum and apex are the dimmest. Wall motion and thickening are normal everywhere. What is the best interpretation of this count distribution?", "options": {"A": "A normal pattern, since the lateral wall is often brightest and the septum and apex dimmer.", "B": "A hot lateral wall from hibernating circumflex myocardium, so fluorodeoxyglucose PET viability imaging should be added.", "C": "A septal defect in the anterior descending territory, so angiography of that vessel is next.", "D": "A fixed apical and septal defect from prior infarction, so the study should be reported abnormal."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "reads normal brightest wall as hibernation", "C": "reads normal septal count as an LAD defect", "D": "reads normal dim segments as scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-008_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A normal pattern, since the lateral wall is often brightest and the septum and apex dimmer.): Normalization sets the brightest region to 100 percent, and in healthy hearts the lateral wall is commonly that region. The membranous septum and thin apex normally read lower, so lower counts there are not a perfusion defect when motion and thickening are preserved. A true defect or hibernating segment would show an abnormal motion pattern or a count drop below the normal limits."}, {"id": 879, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 59-year-old man in sinus rhythm has gated stress SPECT after treadmill exercise. The technologist saw the R-wave trigger indicator flicker during acquisition, and the cine's last frames hold few counts. He asks whether the images can be reprocessed. Before reprocessing, which check best explains the problem?", "options": {"A": "Confirm the collimator is high-resolution, since general-purpose collimation removes counts from the late frames.", "B": "Review ECG lead placement and R-wave amplitude, since a weak signal misses or double-counts triggers.", "C": "Check the center of rotation file, since misalignment deletes late frames from the gated cine.", "D": "Confirm the post-stress heart rate fell below 60, since faster rates drop late frames."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blaming collimator type for frame-specific loss", "C": "Confusing a tomographic alignment fault with gating", "D": "Misattributing the fault to heart rate alone"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-003_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Review ECG lead placement and R-wave amplitude, since a weak signal misses or double-counts triggers.): The scanner builds frames by timing from each detected R wave. A flickering indicator means beats were missed or counted twice, which scrambles frame assignment and leaves late frames sparse. Checking lead position and R-wave height is the first step. Collimator choice affects resolution, a rapid rate shortens frames without losing them, and center-of-rotation errors blur slices rather than remove frames."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 35-year-old woman with no cardiac risk factors has three months of progressive dyspnea and leg swelling. Gated SPECT shows a dilated cavity, diffuse hypokinesis in all walls and no focal perfusion defect. The software reports a left ventricular ejection fraction near 25 percent. Which interpretation best fits?", "options": {"A": "Hibernating myocardium in all territories, since preserved counts with reduced motion indicate a hibernating state.", "B": "A nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy pattern, since dysfunction is global with a dilated cavity and uniform perfusion.", "C": "Stress-induced stunning, since global hypokinesis after regadenoson is the typical finding of an acute ischemic event.", "D": "Ischemic cardiomyopathy from three-vessel disease, since an ejection fraction near 25 percent requires revascularization planning."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "applies hibernation to a diffuse low-risk pattern", "C": "calls resting global dysfunction pharmacologic stunning", "D": "assumes low ejection fraction means ischemic disease"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-009_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy pattern, since dysfunction is global with a dilated cavity and uniform perfusion.): Ischemic cardiomyopathy typically leaves territorial defects or regional wall motion abnormalities that follow coronary distributions. A young woman with no risk factors, a dilated cavity, uniform counts and global hypokinesis fits a primary myocardial disease. Hibernation and stunning are regional phenomena tied to a stenosis, so applying them to every wall here ignores the pretest probability and the pattern."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 56-year-old man with BMI 36, diabetes and atypical chest pain has Tc-99m sestamibi regadenoson SPECT. Images are read using the gated short-axis and long-axis cine alongside the perfusion data. LVEF is 60 percent. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Inferior infarct in the right coronary territory; refer for angiography because fixed count loss means scar.", "B": "Hibernating inferior wall; plan revascularization because perfusion and motion are discordant in this territory.", "C": "Inferior ischemia; recommend catheterization because count reduction in the right coronary territory means flow limitation.", "D": "Probable diaphragmatic attenuation; normal inferior thickening argues against infarction, so report low likelihood of scar."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating fixed count loss as scar without function", "B": "Reversing the direction of the mismatch", "C": "Equating inferior count loss with ischemia"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-057_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Probable diaphragmatic attenuation; normal inferior thickening argues against infarction, so report low likelihood of scar.): Reduced inferior counts with normal motion and thickening is the reverse of hibernation and is characteristic of diaphragmatic attenuation, more likely in obese or male patients. A transmural infarct would abolish thickening. Gated function therefore lowers the probability of disease and angiography is not justified on this finding alone."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69-year-old man had a lateral myocardial infarction six years ago. Resting gated SPECT shows moderately abnormal wall motion in the mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral walls, with resting counts reduced to about half of peak. Which vessel and interpretation are best?", "options": {"A": "Right coronary artery, with matched reduction in counts and motion indicating scar of the inferior wall.", "B": "Left circumflex artery, with matched reduction in counts and motion suggesting partially scarred myocardium.", "C": "Left anterior descending artery, with reduced counts from breast attenuation and preserved lateral thickening.", "D": "Left circumflex artery, with preserved counts and reduced motion indicating pure stunning of the lateral wall."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning lateral segments to the right coronary", "C": "Citing attenuation for abnormal motion", "D": "Ignoring reduced resting counts"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-056_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Left circumflex artery, with matched reduction in counts and motion suggesting partially scarred myocardium.): The mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral segments are left circumflex territory. Moderately reduced counts matched with reduced motion suggest partial-thickness scar. Pure stunning requires normal counts, and attenuation does not change motion or thickening."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 55-year-old man with no cardiac history has a rest-stress rubidium-82 PET. The attenuation correction CT is displaced about 10 mm to the patient's left compared with the emission data. The reader notes reduced lateral wall counts on both rest and stress images, and wall motion is normal. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "Misregistration artifact from the shifted attenuation map, with normal myocardium and preserved wall motion", "B": "Balanced three-vessel ischemia producing a global count reduction, most obvious in the lateral wall", "C": "Reversible lateral ischemia from a circumflex stenosis, expected to improve on the rest images", "D": "Fixed lateral scar from prior circumflex infarction, shown by matching count reduction on both image sets"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "confuses global attenuation error with balanced ischemia", "C": "misses that rest images match stress images", "D": "reads artifact as fixed scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-065_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Misregistration artifact from the shifted attenuation map, with normal myocardium and preserved wall motion): When the CT attenuation map and emission data do not overlay, the heart is corrected with the wrong tissue density in one region, often producing falsely low counts in the lateral wall. Wall motion stays normal, which separates artifact from disease. A true scar would show reduced thickening, and ischemia would not look the same on rest and stress."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman finishes a stress Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT. The raw projections show the heart drifting about 14 mm sideways across the acquisition, with no vertical shift. The technologist reports slight blurring of the reconstructed slices. The patient can return for repeat imaging if needed. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Inject a second tracer dose now to raise counts and reacquire at the same position", "B": "Turn on attenuation correction, because it also removes motion related smearing of the walls", "C": "Apply motion correction, compare slices, and repeat acquisition if blurring persists", "D": "Reconstruct with a much smoother filter to hide the blurring and report the study"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding radiation dose to fix a positioning problem", "B": "Confusing attenuation correction with motion correction", "D": "Using a filter to mask motion instead of correcting it"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-112_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply motion correction, compare slices, and repeat acquisition if blurring persists): Lateral drift is handled by reviewing the projections or sinogram, applying the motion correction program, and checking that the corrected slices look sharper and agree with the clinical picture. If blurring remains the study is repeated, ideally with a comfortable position. Heavier smoothing hides the problem, extra tracer adds dose without fixing motion, and attenuation correction does not realign projections."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man had an ejection fraction of 28 percent on gated SPECT a year ago. After therapy, a repeat study shows a dilated cavity with global hypokinesis and an ejection fraction of 38 percent. The cardiology note asks how to classify his heart failure now. Which classification is correct?", "options": {"A": "Heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction, since the value lies between 35 and 49 percent.", "B": "Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, since 38 percent is not above 40 despite the rise.", "C": "Heart failure with improved ejection fraction, since it rose 10 points above the previous category.", "D": "Heart failure with recovered ejection fraction, since any rise after therapy resolves the original category."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses the wrong lower limit for the category", "C": "ignores the requirement of above 40 percent", "D": "treats any rise as recovery"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-015_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, since 38 percent is not above 40 despite the rise.): Current heart failure definitions classify reduced ejection fraction as 40 percent or below, mildly reduced as 41 to 49, and improved ejection fraction as a baseline of 40 or below with a rise of at least 10 points to a value above 40. A value of 38 meets the rise but not the threshold, so the patient remains in the reduced category. The mildly reduced label starts at 41 percent."}, {"id": 886, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62 year old man had an anterior myocardial infarction two years ago and now has exertional dyspnea. Rest gated SPECT shows a dilated ventricle with an ejection fraction near 32 percent, and the apical segments bulge outward during systole on the cine. Besides the ejection fraction, which additional concern should the physician raise in the report?", "options": {"A": "Right ventricular failure from pulmonary hypertension, so advise right heart catheterization before further imaging.", "B": "Normal apical function with a motion artifact, so no thrombus comment is needed.", "C": "Possible apical aneurysm with thrombus risk, so advise contrast echocardiography or cardiac MRI.", "D": "Stunned but viable apical myocardium, so advise repeating the study after a dobutamine infusion."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "misreads left apical motion as right sided disease", "B": "dismisses paradoxical motion as artifact", "D": "treats chronic scar like stunning"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-070_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Possible apical aneurysm with thrombus risk, so advise contrast echocardiography or cardiac MRI.): Outward systolic bulging of the apex in a dilated ventricle two years after an anterior infarction is dyskinesis, the pattern of an apical aneurysm. Aneurysms promote stasis and mural thrombus, which SPECT cannot show, so contrast echocardiography or MRI is the right follow up. Stunning does not persist two years after infarction, and nothing points to a right sided cause or artifact."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man has a stress and rest SPECT. The technologist reports a focal intense retrocardiac area on the raw cine, consistent with a hiatal hernia, and the reader notes the inferior wall looks mildly reduced on both stress and rest slices. His gated thickening in that wall is normal. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Inferior wall ischemia, since extracardiac activity signals an underlying coronary artery lesion", "B": "Likely artifact from adjacent activity, supported by normal wall thickening", "C": "Technical failure of the camera that requires a repeat of both the stress and rest studies", "D": "Fixed inferior scar, since the reduction looks the same on both stress and rest slices"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Linking an extracardiac finding with coronary disease", "C": "Blaming equipment for a recognizable patient related artifact", "D": "Applying the fixed defect rule without checking thickening"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-122_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Likely artifact from adjacent activity, supported by normal wall thickening): A fixed inferior reduction could be scar, but scar usually shows reduced wall thickening and motion. Normal thickening plus an adjacent hot structure on the raw cine points to a processing artifact from the neighboring activity. A camera fault would not appear as a focal extracardiac source, and the hernia is not evidence of coronary disease."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 38-year-old man in sinus rhythm is referred for a resting equilibrium gated blood-pool study to measure LVEF after viral myocarditis. The technologist must separate the two ventricles for background and region selection. Which camera position is best for this study?", "options": {"A": "A left anterior oblique view with best septal separation and a 10 to 15 degree tilt.", "B": "A right anterior oblique view at about 30 degrees, as used for first-pass right ventricular imaging.", "C": "An upright anterior view with the patient seated, which separates the atria from the ventricles.", "D": "A left lateral view that places the lateral wall in profile and maximizes lateral wall counts."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using the first-pass angle for equilibrium imaging", "C": "Using a view that does not split the septum", "D": "Choosing a view that overlaps both ventricles"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-017_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A left anterior oblique view with best septal separation and a 10 to 15 degree tilt.): The left anterior oblique projection is angled so the septum appears edge-on, separating left ventricle from right ventricle and allowing a clean background region. A small caudal tilt separates the left atrium from the left ventricle. Right anterior oblique, left lateral and anterior views overlap chambers and do not define the septum."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63 year old woman with hypertension has a normal Tc-99m tetrofosmin perfusion study. The gated cine shows a small, tightly contracting ventricle, with a calculated end-diastolic volume of 53 mL and an ejection fraction of 75 percent. Her cardiologist asks whether this proves hyperdynamic function from long standing hypertension. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "The small volume makes it unreliable, so correlate with echocardiographic wall thickness first.", "B": "Values above 70 percent are accurate on gated SPECT and confirm hyperdynamic function from hypertension.", "C": "Apply attenuation correction first, since soft tissue loss lowers the end-systolic volume.", "D": "Repeat the study after stopping her antihypertensive drugs, since they probably caused the high figure."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "accepts any gated value as exact", "C": "confuses attenuation with partial volume effect", "D": "attributes a volume artifact to medication"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-068_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The small volume makes it unreliable, so correlate with echocardiographic wall thickness first.): With an end-diastolic volume near 53 mL the stroke volume is about 40 mL and the end-systolic volume only about 13 mL, a size at which gated SPECT underestimates the end-systolic cavity. Echocardiography or cardiac MRI measures wall thickness and volume more reliably in small hearts. Stopping drugs and attenuation correction do not address the resolution limit behind the high number."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist asks how to label the motion of the apical wall in a gated SPECT cine of a patient with a large old infarct. The apical wall moves outward while the rest of the ventricle contracts inward. For the written report, which term correctly describes that abnormal wall motion?", "options": {"A": "Akinesis, a segment with no inward motion or thickening in systole that stays still.", "B": "Hypokinesis, a segment that contracts inward but less than normal neighboring segments do.", "C": "Dyskinesis, paradoxical systolic expansion of a segment while the remaining walls contract.", "D": "Hyperkinesis, increased compensatory inward motion of a segment next to infarcted muscle."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confuses akinesis with dyskinesis", "B": "confuses hypokinesis with dyskinesis", "D": "picks compensatory motion as the label"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-073_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dyskinesis, paradoxical systolic expansion of a segment while the remaining walls contract.): Dyskinesis is outward bulging of a segment during systole while the rest of the wall shortens. Akinesis lacks both inward motion and thickening but does not move outward, hypokinesis is weak contraction, and hyperkinesis is increased contraction, often seen in remote walls. Only dyskinesis fits an apical segment moving paradoxically outward on the cine."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52 year old woman with very large breasts has a stress Tc-99m sestamibi study. The anterior wall counts are lower than the other walls on the stress tomograms. Which acquisition change is most likely to separate breast attenuation from true anterior ischemia?", "options": {"A": "Repeat with a longer time per projection, since extra counts correct soft tissue loss.", "B": "Repeat supine with the arms lowered beside the chest, which restores normal anterior wall counts.", "C": "Repeat with Tl-201, since its lower photon energy penetrates breast tissue better than technetium.", "D": "Repeat prone, which shifts breast tissue away from the anterior wall."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses counts to fix attenuation", "B": "changes arm position for a breast problem", "C": "reverses the effect of photon energy"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-081_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat prone, which shifts breast tissue away from the anterior wall.): Breast tissue lies over the anterior and anterolateral walls and absorbs photons, so counts fall in those regions. Prone imaging lets the breast fall away from the chest, which usually restores anterior counts, though the inferior wall may then be attenuated by the diaphragm. Thallium has lower energy and attenuates more, and extra counts do not correct tissue absorption."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49 year old woman with large breasts has a fixed mild anterior perfusion defect on sestamibi SPECT. Gated images show normal anterior wall thickening and an ejection fraction of 62 percent. Her primary physician asks about angiography. What interpretation should the report favor?", "options": {"A": "Anterior infarction with preserved function, since a fixed defect is scar whatever the thickening.", "B": "Nondiagnostic study, since any anterior defect in a woman needs a full repeat another day.", "C": "Severe left anterior descending ischemia, so urgent angiography is arranged for this defect.", "D": "Probable breast attenuation, since thickening is normal and angiography is unsupported."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "calls every fixed defect scar", "B": "declares a usable study unreadable", "C": "treats a fixed defect as ischemia"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-082_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Probable breast attenuation, since thickening is normal and angiography is unsupported.): A fixed anterior defect in a woman with large breasts, with normal thickening, is the classic attenuation pattern, because scarred muscle would not thicken normally. The report should say so and relate the finding to clinical risk. Scar needs reduced thickening, ischemia needs a reversible defect, and the study remains readable."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman receiving anthracycline chemotherapy for breast cancer has a baseline ejection fraction of 58 percent. After a cumulative dose of 300 mg/m2 she has dyspnea. Gated SPECT shows a dilated cavity, global hypokinesis and no perfusion defect, with an ejection fraction of 25 percent. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Treat as cardiotoxicity, hold the anthracycline and start cardioprotective therapy with cardio-oncology.", "B": "Regard the fall as usual gated SPECT repeat variability and recheck the ejection fraction later.", "C": "Attribute the low value to breast attenuation and repeat with attenuation correction before acting on it.", "D": "Continue chemotherapy unchanged, since normal perfusion shows the fall is not clinically meaningful for her."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "calls a large drop normal variability", "C": "blames attenuation for a global change", "D": "uses normal perfusion to dismiss dysfunction"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-012_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat as cardiotoxicity, hold the anthracycline and start cardioprotective therapy with cardio-oncology.): A fall of more than 10 points to below 50 percent defines cardiotoxicity, and a drop from 58 to 25 percent far exceeds the roughly 5 point repeat variability of gating. Global hypokinesis with a normal perfusion pattern is typical of drug-related myocardial injury, not coronary disease. Attenuation changes counts in a region rather than ejection fraction globally, so repeating the test would only delay treatment."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70-year-old man is referred for rest and regadenoson N-13 ammonia PET for exertional dyspnea. The processed images show sharp wall edges, uniform uptake throughout all segments, and a gated LVEF of 65 percent. He has no known coronary disease. Which statement about reporting this study is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Report normal perfusion and normal function; low image noise adds confidence", "B": "Recommend delayed images to exclude redistribution because normal PET uptake may still hide old scar", "C": "Report mild apical ischemia because the lowest segment count is about one third of maximum", "D": "Downgrade confidence because an LVEF of 65 percent usually reflects overestimation by ammonia gating"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "applies SPECT redistribution logic to a PET study", "C": "reads the normal apical thinning as a defect", "D": "treats high normal EF as a method error"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-054_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report normal perfusion and normal function; low image noise adds confidence): A uniform study with sharp edges and a normal EF supports a normal report. Normal apical thinning and a low relative count at the true apex are expected and are not ischemia. Redistribution imaging belongs to thallium SPECT, not to ammonia PET. An EF of 65 percent is within the normal range and is not a reason to doubt the study."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman, 155 cm tall, will start anthracycline chemotherapy. Her baseline gated SPECT, read with the cine, shows an end-diastolic volume of 53 mL and an LVEF of 78 percent. How should this baseline be used to monitor for cardiotoxicity?", "options": {"A": "Switch to Tl-201 gating, which gives larger cavity counts and a more accurate EF measurement.", "B": "Repeat gated SPECT with 16 frames, which eliminates the small cavity bias in the calculation.", "C": "Treat it as an overestimate and follow cardiotoxicity with echocardiography or blood-pool imaging instead.", "D": "Accept the value as exact and treat a later fall below 60 percent as the trigger."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming tracer choice fixes cavity size bias", "B": "Assuming frame count removes size bias", "D": "Accepting an inflated small heart EF"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-065_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat it as an overestimate and follow cardiotoxicity with echocardiography or blood-pool imaging instead.): Small cavities inflate the gated SPECT EF through partial volume effects, so a baseline of 78 percent may overstate true function and later falls could be hard to compare. A method that does not depend on small geometric edges, such as echocardiography or blood-pool counting, is more reliable. Frame count and tracer change do not remove size bias."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 72-year-old man with diabetes and chest pain has a rubidium-82 PET at regadenoson stress. Segments in the basal, mid and apical inferior wall show reduced uptake near half of peak with reduced thickening. His electrocardiogram shows an old inferior Q wave. Which next step is best?", "options": {"A": "Conclude the defect is attenuation and no further assessment is needed because the heart is normal", "B": "Review function and perfusion together and consider FDG viability imaging before deciding on revascularization", "C": "Recommend right coronary revascularization immediately because uptake at half of peak counts as viable myocardium", "D": "Repeat regadenoson stress with aminophylline reversal and compare images for transient cavity dilation"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "labels a true territorial defect an artifact", "C": "treats any reduced uptake as viable myocardium", "D": "uses reversal agents as a diagnostic maneuver"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-062_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Review function and perfusion together and consider FDG viability imaging before deciding on revascularization): Half of peak uptake with reduced thickening in the right coronary territory and an old Q wave may represent scar, hibernating myocardium or a mix. Viability decisions need an answer to whether metabolism persists, and FDG imaging or a rest-versus-stress comparison provides it. Immediate revascularization without that is premature. A territorial defect with matching wall motion abnormality is not attenuation. Aminophylline reverses the stress agent, not the diagnosis."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 57-year-old woman on doxorubicin, cumulative dose 300 mg/m2, had a baseline blood-pool LVEF of 67 percent. Today both ventricles contract normally with LVEF near 56 percent, and she has no symptoms, edema or rhythm complaints. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Switch to gated SPECT to confirm the EF, since MUGA cannot guide chemotherapy decisions.", "B": "Declare cardiotoxicity from the 11-point fall alone and end anthracycline therapy permanently for this patient.", "C": "Disregard the change because MUGA variability between studies commonly exceeds 15 points for the same patient.", "D": "The EF stays above the lower limit of normal, so continue closer surveillance and discuss cardioprotection."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Doubting MUGA's role in chemotherapy monitoring", "B": "Applying the fall criterion without the threshold", "C": "Overstating test-to-test variability in this clinical setting"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-020_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The EF stays above the lower limit of normal, so continue closer surveillance and discuss cardioprotection.): A fall of 10 points or more is concerning, but the standard definition of cardiotoxicity also requires a value below the lower limit of normal, which 56 percent is not. At 300 mg/m2 closer monitoring is sensible, and cardioprotective drugs can be discussed. Radionuclide variability is about 5 points, and MUGA is the reference for serial monitoring."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A trainee asks why the interpreting physician reviews the rotating raw projection cine before looking at the reconstructed short-axis and long-axis slices of a SPECT study, when the reconstructed slices are what the report is built on. What is the main reason?", "options": {"A": "It reveals motion, attenuation, and extracardiac uptake that reconstructed slices can hide.", "B": "It gives ejection fraction and wall thickening directly, which reconstructed slices cannot provide.", "C": "It replaces daily quality control, since a normal cine shows the camera is working.", "D": "It lets the physician measure the injected dose used to judge image quality."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "assigns gated function to the raw cine", "C": "treats the cine as camera quality control", "D": "confuses image review with dose assay"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-079_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It reveals motion, attenuation, and extracardiac uptake that reconstructed slices can hide.): Reconstruction smooths over problems that are easy to see in the raw frames: movement shows as a vertical jump, soft tissue shows as dimming in some views, and extracardiac activity shows as bright foci near the heart. The cine does not provide ejection fraction or dose information, and it cannot replace daily camera quality control."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man with nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy, NYHA class II and QRS 104 ms, has had optimized guideline-directed therapy for 4 months. Blood-pool imaging shows a dilated LV with globally reduced contraction and LVEF near 30 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for cardiac resynchronization therapy, since reduced EF with dilation alone is a qualifying indication.", "B": "Refer for a primary-prevention ICD, since LVEF is 35 percent or lower despite optimal therapy.", "C": "Obtain gated SPECT first, since blood-pool EF values cannot be used for defibrillator decisions.", "D": "Continue medical therapy 3 more months, since 6 months of therapy is required before a device."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring the QRS duration requirement", "C": "Doubting radionuclide EF for device criteria", "D": "Overstating the required duration of therapy"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-023_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for a primary-prevention ICD, since LVEF is 35 percent or lower despite optimal therapy.): Primary-prevention ICD criteria include LVEF of 35 percent or less despite at least 3 months of guideline-directed therapy, NYHA class II or III, and expected survival over a year. Cardiac resynchronization requires a wide QRS, which is 104 ms here. Four months already satisfies the duration rule, and radionuclide EF is accepted for device decisions."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 44-year-old woman six weeks after her last doxorubicin dose has new dyspnea and orthopnea at rest. Equilibrium blood-pool imaging shows a dilated left ventricle with globally reduced contraction and LVEF near 18 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the blood-pool study after the planned chemotherapy course before changing any cardiac therapy.", "B": "Withhold further cardiotoxic chemotherapy, start heart failure therapy, and refer to cardio-oncology.", "C": "Implant a defibrillator now, since an EF under 35 percent is an immediate primary prevention indication.", "D": "Give the next doxorubicin cycle at half dose, since reduced dosing limits further heart damage."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying action on a severe drop in EF", "C": "Skipping medical therapy before device decisions", "D": "Assuming dose reduction makes continuing safe"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-022_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold further cardiotoxic chemotherapy, start heart failure therapy, and refer to cardio-oncology.): An EF of 18 percent with a dilated ventricle and symptoms means anthracycline cardiomyopathy. The first priority is to stop further cardiotoxic exposure and begin heart failure therapy under cardio-oncology care. Defibrillators are considered only after at least three months of optimized medical therapy, and giving more drug or waiting leaves the patient at high risk."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man had an anterior STEMI four years ago treated medically and now has dyspnea on exertion. Resting gated SPECT is acquired and the short-axis and long-axis cine are reviewed with the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 48 percent. Which territory and interpretation best fit the abnormal wall?", "options": {"A": "Left circumflex territory with matched lateral wall scar; the regional loss alone explains the dyspnea.", "B": "Left anterior descending territory with preserved perfusion and stunning; revascularize within the next several days.", "C": "Right coronary territory with matched scar of the inferior wall; the LVEF should remain normal afterward.", "D": "Left anterior descending scar, with the LVEF only mildly reduced despite a large regional loss."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mapping anterior segments to the circumflex", "B": "Treating reduced counts as preserved perfusion", "C": "Mapping anterior segments to the right coronary artery"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-047_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left anterior descending scar, with the LVEF only mildly reduced despite a large regional loss.): The mid and apical anterior, anteroseptal, septal and apical segments are the left anterior descending territory. Reduced resting counts with severely reduced motion mean scar, and a mildly reduced global EF can coexist with a large regional abnormality when the remaining walls compensate. Stunning would require preserved counts."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with HER2-positive breast cancer is scheduled to start trastuzumab. Her baseline gated blood-pool LVEF is 54 percent with symmetric contraction on the cine, she has no cardiac symptoms and her blood pressure is controlled. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Start a defibrillator evaluation before trastuzumab because the fraction is below 55 percent", "B": "Start trastuzumab and defer all cardiac imaging until symptoms appear", "C": "Start trastuzumab and monitor ejection fraction about every three months during therapy", "D": "Delay trastuzumab until ejection fraction rises above 65 percent"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats a normal fraction as a device indication", "B": "waits for symptoms instead of scheduled monitoring", "D": "applies an invented high ejection fraction requirement"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-040_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start trastuzumab and monitor ejection fraction about every three months during therapy): An LVEF of 54 percent is within the range accepted to begin trastuzumab, and the cine shows symmetric contraction. Left ventricular function is reassessed about every three months while the drug continues, because early decline is often asymptomatic. No threshold of 65 percent exists, waiting for symptoms misses early cardiotoxicity, and a defibrillator has no role at a normal fraction."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 61-year-old woman on anthracycline therapy reports new exertional dyspnea. She has no known coronary disease and her blood pressure is normal. Her baseline gated blood-pool LVEF was 60 percent. The repeat study shows symmetric contraction and a processed LVEF of 46 percent. Which interpretation and plan are best?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the study in three months, since the symptoms are probably not cardiac in origin", "B": "A 14-point fall below 50 percent signals cardiotoxicity, so stop it and start therapy", "C": "Refer for a defibrillator now, since the ejection fraction has fallen so sharply from baseline", "D": "The fraction of 46 percent is within normal limits, so continue the drug unchanged for now"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "delays action despite a significant decline with symptoms", "C": "applies a device indication to a fraction above 35 percent", "D": "ignores the large fall from baseline"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-044_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 14-point fall below 50 percent signals cardiotoxicity, so stop it and start therapy): A fall of 14 points to a value below 50 percent with new symptoms meets criteria for cardiotoxicity, so the anthracycline is stopped and heart failure therapy started with oncology involvement. A fraction of 46 percent is not normal for her and the drop is beyond the reproducibility of the method. Delay risks further damage, and a defibrillator is indicated only at 35 percent or less after therapy."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "The motion software reports an abrupt upward shift of 16 mm in a Tc-99m stress study with 4.8 mm pixels. The laboratory policy is to repeat the acquisition when an abrupt vertical shift exceeds 3 pixels. Which decision follows from the policy?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the study only if the shift occurred after the first quarter of projections.", "B": "Repeat the acquisition, since 16 mm is about 3.3 pixels and exceeds the 3 pixel limit.", "C": "Proceed and read normally, since a shift under 20 mm is considered insignificant.", "D": "Proceed with motion correction, since 16 mm is about 1.6 pixels and well under the limit."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "adds a timing rule not in the policy", "C": "uses an invented millimeter cutoff", "D": "converts using 10 mm per pixel"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-098_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the acquisition, since 16 mm is about 3.3 pixels and exceeds the 3 pixel limit.): Sixteen millimeters divided by 4.8 millimeters per pixel gives about 3.3 pixels, which is over the 3 pixel limit, so the policy calls for reacquisition. Using the wrong pixel size cuts the shift in half, an invented 20 mm cutoff is not the stated policy, and the policy has no timing condition."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58 year old woman with a prior anterior infarction is referred for viability testing before possible revascularization. Rest gated SPECT shows apical counts below 20 percent of peak with dyskinetic apical motion, while the basal and mid lateral walls keep normal counts and thickening. What is the best interpretation of the apical findings?", "options": {"A": "Hibernating myocardium with recovery potential, so revascularizing the left anterior descending artery should restore apical function.", "B": "Breast attenuation of the anterior wall, so the study should be repeated prone first.", "C": "Normal apical thinning, so the finding needs no comment and no further evaluation.", "D": "Transmural scar forming an aneurysm, so apical function will not recover; benefit depends on other walls."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "reads very low counts as viable", "B": "attributes dyskinetic scar to attenuation", "C": "calls scar normal thinning"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-072_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transmural scar forming an aneurysm, so apical function will not recover; benefit depends on other walls.): Counts under roughly 50 percent of peak with outward systolic bulging mark thin, nonviable transmural scar. Hibernating muscle keeps substantial uptake and thickens, and attenuation does not cause paradoxical motion. Because the apex will not regain function, the decision to revascularize should rest on whether the preserved lateral and basal territories are jeopardized and viable."}, {"id": 906, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man with persistent ectopy has gated SPECT with software EF 50 percent and sparse late frames. The cardiologist suspects heart failure and needs a trustworthy LVEF within days to guide therapy and diuretic dosing. Which next test is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Repeat gated SPECT with double the dose so late frames reach adequate counts.", "B": "Accept the 50 percent EF, since it is near the lower limit and software measured it.", "C": "Use phase analysis on the same cine to get the EF, needing no clean frames.", "D": "Echocardiography, or a blood-pool study with arrhythmia rejection, to measure LVEF directly."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Raising dose for a trigger problem", "B": "Accepting a software number without validation", "C": "Using phase analysis to rescue a bad cine"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-014_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Echocardiography, or a blood-pool study with arrhythmia rejection, to measure LVEF directly.): A new measurement is needed because the existing gated data are distorted. Echocardiography or an arrhythmia-rejecting blood-pool study samples function independently of the failed SPECT trigger and can be performed promptly. More dose does not fix timing, phase analysis relies on the same flawed frames, and the printed value should not be accepted just because the software produced it."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist prepares a gated blood-pool study on a 63-year-old woman with a reported LVEF of 52 percent on a prior echocardiogram. The physician asks which tracer is used so that the ventricular counts remain proportional to blood volume throughout the study. Which agent is correct?", "options": {"A": "Technetium-99m sestamibi, which is taken up by myocardial mitochondria", "B": "Thallium-201, which redistributes from the myocardium back to blood over several hours", "C": "Technetium-99m labeled red blood cells, which stay within the vascular space", "D": "Technetium-99m macroaggregated albumin, which lodges in the pulmonary capillaries"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "chooses a perfusion tracer for a blood-pool study", "B": "chooses a potassium analog whose distribution changes over time", "D": "chooses a lung perfusion agent that leaves the blood pool"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-043_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Technetium-99m labeled red blood cells, which stay within the vascular space): Equilibrium radionuclide ventriculography uses red blood cells labeled with technetium-99m, so the tracer stays inside the circulating blood and counts track chamber volume across the cardiac cycle. Sestamibi and thallium are myocardial perfusion agents that accumulate in muscle, and macroaggregated albumin is trapped in pulmonary capillaries, so none of them provides a stable blood-pool signal."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 69 year old man is evaluated six days after circumflex occlusion treated with PCI nine hours after symptom onset. Rest gated SPECT shows outward bulging of the mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral walls with preserved counts, and LVEF is 33 percent. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Patient motion between gated frames producing false bulging, so repeat the cine after the study.", "B": "Contained pseudoaneurysm after rupture, so urgent cardiac surgery referral is the next step.", "C": "Established lateral aneurysm, so plan surgical aneurysmectomy to recover ejection fraction on the gated images.", "D": "Stunned lateral myocardium with preserved perfusion, so dyskinesis may recover over weeks of therapy."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "blaming motion artifact for reproducible regional bulging", "B": "mistaking bulging for pseudoaneurysm without evidence of rupture", "C": "calling stunning an established aneurysm"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-034_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stunned lateral myocardium with preserved perfusion, so dyskinesis may recover over weeks of therapy.): Preserved counts show perfusion and myocyte integrity were restored by early PCI, so the abnormal motion in segments 11 and 12 is stunning, which can improve over days to weeks. True aneurysm needs scar with reduced counts, and rupture would cause hemodynamic collapse or a flow defect, not preserved uptake. Reproducible bulging in a defined territory is not motion artifact."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54 year old woman, 150 cm tall and 49 kg, has normal stress and rest Tc-99m sestamibi perfusion. The gated short-axis and long-axis cine shows a small ventricle with vigorous contraction, and the software reports an ejection fraction of 78 percent. Her referring physician asks what the number means. How should this value be handled in the report?", "options": {"A": "A small cavity inflates gated SPECT ejection fraction, so report normal function without calling it supernormal.", "B": "Repeat the gated acquisition longer, since low counts push the reported ejection fraction upward.", "C": "Report supernormal systolic function and advise echocardiography to exclude hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from this value alone.", "D": "Widen the endocardial contour at end systole so the ejection fraction falls to a usual value."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "blames count statistics for partial volume effect", "C": "trusts the software number ignoring cavity size", "D": "manipulates contours to fit expectation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-067_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A small cavity inflates gated SPECT ejection fraction, so report normal function without calling it supernormal.): Gated SPECT has limited spatial resolution, so in a small ventricle the end-systolic cavity looks almost obliterated and the end-systolic volume is underestimated. That inflates the ejection fraction in small women and small hearts, and the report should carry that caveat. Calling it supernormal invites needless hypertrophy work-up, a longer acquisition does not fix a resolution effect, and hand editing contours is not a valid correction."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man has an exercise SPECT whose raw projections are speckled with the heart barely above background, and no repeat is possible because he has moved to another country. The slices look noisy with a possible small apical defect. How should the study be handled?", "options": {"A": "Report definite ischemia, since low counts mark the stress image as abnormal", "B": "Report with a stated limitation of poor count density and no firm call on small defects", "C": "Report the apical defect as definite scar because noise cannot create a small defect on slices", "D": "Report a normal study because noisy slices tend to hide abnormalities"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating low counts as a sign of ischemia", "C": "Believing noise cannot mimic a defect", "D": "Calling a non diagnostic study normal"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-120_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report with a stated limitation of poor count density and no firm call on small defects): Low counts add statistical noise that can both mimic and hide small defects, so a firm call is unsafe. The honest report states the reduced quality and the limited ability to exclude or confirm small abnormalities, with a repeat advised if the clinical question needs it. Calling the study definitely abnormal or normal is not supported by noisy data."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with large breasts and atypical chest pain has supine sestamibi SPECT. The anterior count reduction is read with the gated short-axis cine, and LVEF is 60 percent. What is the most likely cause of the anterior count reduction?", "options": {"A": "Septal flash from left bundle branch block producing a count reduction in the anteroseptal wall segments.", "B": "Apical thinning as a normal variant causing reduced anterior counts in the basal wall segments.", "C": "Breast attenuation of anterior photons, since normal motion and thickening argue against an anterior infarct.", "D": "Left anterior descending scar because the anterior wall has reduced counts and reduced motion."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using the left bundle branch block septal pattern", "B": "Applying the apical normal variant to basal segments", "D": "Reading count loss as scar despite normal motion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-060_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Breast attenuation of anterior photons, since normal motion and thickening argue against an anterior infarct.): Reduced anterior counts with normal motion and thickening suggest soft tissue attenuation, here from breast tissue. Scar would abolish thickening. Septal flash and apical thinning are different findings, and apical thinning affects the apex rather than basal anterior segments."}, {"id": 912, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old woman has gated SPECT with software LVEF 60 percent. The acquisition ECG shows T waves almost as tall as the R waves, and the cine's late frames are sparse. What is the most likely cause and best fix?", "options": {"A": "The photopeak window sat off center; recenter it at 140 keV and reprocess the same data.", "B": "The trigger likely counted T waves as beats; reposition electrodes to raise R-wave height, then repeat.", "C": "Too few projection angles were collected; increase to 64 stops over 180 degrees and rerun.", "D": "Scatter from bowel activity darkened late frames; repeat after a fatty meal and a longer delay."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blaming energy window offset for trigger error", "C": "Blaming projection sampling for frame loss", "D": "Applying a perfusion artifact remedy to gating"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-009_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The trigger likely counted T waves as beats; reposition electrodes to raise R-wave height, then repeat.): The gating circuit times frames from detected R waves. A T wave of similar height can trip the trigger, so beats are double-counted and cycle lengths look artificially short. Late frames then lose counts. Electrode placement that raises R-wave amplitude fixes the source. Bowel scatter, projection number and energy window are image-quality issues that do not alter frame timing."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 72-year-old man had Tc-99m sestamibi stress imaging, and the rotating raw projections show intense extracardiac activity beside the inferior wall. He has already left the department and the tracer cannot be reinjected today. The inferior wall appears reduced. Which report conclusion is most defensible?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal study and skip the inferior wall because the other walls look normal.", "B": "Report a fixed inferior infarct and recommend secondary prevention based on the reduced inferior counts alone.", "C": "Report mild inferior ischemia and recommend a repeat study only if symptoms worsen.", "D": "Call the inferior wall indeterminate because of adjacent activity, and weigh gated function and clinical data."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "dismisses an uninterpretable wall", "B": "reads artifact as infarct", "C": "calls ischemia without evidence"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-096_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call the inferior wall indeterminate because of adjacent activity, and weigh gated function and clinical data.): When repeat imaging is not possible, an honest report names the limitation and avoids calling disease or normality in the affected wall. The reader then supports the conclusion with gated wall motion and thickening and the clinical picture. A fixed infarct or ischemia claim reads artifact as disease, and a normal call ignores a wall that cannot be judged."}, {"id": 914, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A gated SPECT report for a woman with a small heart lists an end-diastolic volume of 53 mL and an ejection fraction of 78 percent. A fellow asks you to work out the end-systolic volume implied by those two numbers and to say what the result tells you about the reliability of the ejection fraction. What is your answer?", "options": {"A": "About 12 mL, a cavity so small that partial volume effects likely inflate the ejection fraction.", "B": "About 22 mL, a mildly enlarged cavity, so the ejection fraction is accurate but slightly low.", "C": "About 75 mL, found by subtracting the percentage figure directly from the diastolic volume.", "D": "About 41 mL, a normal cavity, so the stated ejection fraction is accepted as measured."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "applies the wrong fraction to diastolic volume", "C": "subtracts a percentage from a volume", "D": "uses stroke volume as end systolic volume"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-069_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 12 mL, a cavity so small that partial volume effects likely inflate the ejection fraction.): End-systolic volume equals end-diastolic volume times one minus the ejection fraction, so 53 mL times 0.22 gives about 12 mL, with a stroke volume of about 41 mL. A cavity that small is only a few voxels wide at end systole, which gated SPECT cannot resolve well, so the end-systolic volume is underestimated. The other values come from using stroke volume or subtracting a percentage from millilitres."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old man with chest pain has a rubidium-82 PET showing reduced uptake in the lateral wall and relatively increased uptake in the septum on both stress and rest images. Wall motion is normal and LVEF is 60 percent. The attenuation CT appears shifted 8 mm left. How should the study be reported?", "options": {"A": "Balanced ischemia from left main disease, because stress and rest images show matching regional count changes", "B": "Fixed lateral scar, because a defect that does not reverse indicates prior infarction in that wall", "C": "Probable attenuation correction artifact; fix registration, reprocess, and expect normal perfusion once aligned", "D": "Normal study needing no comment, because septal uptake normally exceeds the lateral wall uptake"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats matching count shifts as multivessel disease", "B": "reads any fixed defect as scar", "D": "calls a registration artifact a normal variant"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-068_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Probable attenuation correction artifact; fix registration, reprocess, and expect normal perfusion once aligned): A fixed lateral reduction with relatively high septal counts and normal motion, in the presence of a registration shift, is the classic misregistration artifact. Aligning the CT and reprocessing is the next step. Scar would usually have reduced thickening. Balanced ischemia would not give a lateral-septal count change that is identical at rest. The shift needs to be corrected, not ignored."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man has exercise Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Clip 1 is post-stress and Clip 2 is rest. Inferior and inferoseptal walls are hypokinetic after stress and normal at rest, with LVEF about 47 percent after stress and 62 percent at rest. A colleague suggests viability imaging. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Viability testing is needed, since the right coronary artery cannot show stunning", "B": "Viability testing is needed because a 15 point LVEF fall implies infarction here", "C": "Viability testing is not needed, since normal rest motion shows reversible stunning", "D": "Viability testing is needed because the inferior wall barely moves after the stress"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inventing an artery that cannot stun", "B": "Equating an EF fall with infarction", "D": "Using post-stress motion to infer scar or hibernation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-130_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-130_2.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Viability testing is not needed, since normal rest motion shows reversible stunning): Viability tests answer whether chronically dysfunctional myocardium at rest can recover. This wall moves normally at rest, so it is already viable, and the post-stress loss of motion is stunning of the inferior and inferoseptal segments from severe ischemia in the right coronary territory. The next question is revascularization risk, not viability."}, {"id": 917, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man has Tc-99m sestamibi imaging started soon after treadmill exercise, and the rotating raw images show the heart gradually rising relative to the fixed line. The reader reviews them before reconstruction. What is the likely physiologic basis of this drift?", "options": {"A": "Heart rate and lung volumes return toward baseline after exercise, so the heart gradually rises.", "B": "The patient slides down the table as muscles relax after exercise.", "C": "The tracer slowly redistributes from the myocardium into the liver and bowel during acquisition.", "D": "The camera head drifts upward as the gantry warms during a long acquisition."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "gets the drift direction and cause wrong", "C": "confuses tracer kinetics with positional change", "D": "blames equipment instead of physiology"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-107_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heart rate and lung volumes return toward baseline after exercise, so the heart gradually rises.): Upward creep reflects physiologic recovery after exercise, with breathing depth and heart rate settling and the heart moving cephalad. Tracer movement changes counts, not position, and gantry warming is not a recognized cause. Sliding down the table would move the heart in the other direction."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old man has treadmill stress Tc-99m sestamibi imaging. On the rotating raw projections, the reader notes the heart position relative to the fixed horizontal line changing across the acquisition and must separate upward creep from a sudden patient shift. Which feature best distinguishes creep?", "options": {"A": "It affects only the last projection of the acquisition.", "B": "It is a gradual physiologic drift in one direction, not a sudden change in position.", "C": "It appears only on rest studies when patients fall asleep on the table.", "D": "It is caused by a collimator fault that shifts the field of view."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "gives an incorrect extent of motion", "C": "assigns creep to the wrong study type", "D": "blames equipment for physiologic drift"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-109_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is a gradual physiologic drift in one direction, not a sudden change in position.): Creep is the heart slowly moving as exercise effects fade, typically in one direction over many projections, whereas a patient shift is sudden. It occurs after stress exercise rather than during resting sleep, affects the entire acquisition, and is not a collimator fault."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 45-year-old woman about to start anthracycline chemotherapy has a resting equilibrium gated blood-pool study with both ventricles contracting normally and LVEF near 62 percent. Which value is the generally accepted lower limit of normal for resting LVEF on this study type?", "options": {"A": "About 40 percent, the cutoff that guidelines use to define heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.", "B": "About 50 percent; values at or above that are commonly accepted as normal on blood-pool imaging.", "C": "About 35 percent, the level used to define candidacy for a primary-prevention defibrillator.", "D": "About 60 percent, below which radionuclide results are usually read as cardiotoxicity in treated patients."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using the HFrEF cutoff as the normal limit", "C": "Confusing the ICD threshold with normal", "D": "Setting the limit too high"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-016_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 50 percent; values at or above that are commonly accepted as normal on blood-pool imaging.): On resting radionuclide ventriculography, an LVEF of roughly 50 percent or higher is regarded as normal, and many laboratories set the limit between 50 and 55 percent. The 35 percent value is a treatment threshold for defibrillators, 40 percent marks heart failure with reduced EF, and 60 percent would label many healthy people abnormal."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man had exercise Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Watch Clip 1 (acquired after stress) and then Clip 2 (acquired at rest). LVEF is about 48 percent after stress and 60 percent at rest, with poor lateral and inferolateral motion only after stress. What does this pattern represent?", "options": {"A": "Transient post-ischemic dysfunction that recovers by the time of the rest study", "B": "Permanent loss of contraction from prior infarction that remains unchanged on the rest study", "C": "Chronic dysfunction from long standing reduced flow that improves after revascularization", "D": "A temporary change caused by tracer decay between the two separate acquisitions"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Describing scar instead of reversible dysfunction", "C": "Describing hibernation instead of stunning", "D": "Blaming physics for a physiologic change"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-127_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-127_2.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transient post-ischemic dysfunction that recovers by the time of the rest study): Stunning is contractile dysfunction that outlasts a brief episode of severe ischemia, appears on post-stress gated images, and resolves by the rest study. Hibernation is chronic and present at rest, and scar is permanent. Decay changes counts but not wall motion."}, {"id": 921, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 65 year old man had an anterior infarction two years ago. Gated SPECT shows severe hypokinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex with counts near 28 percent of peak, and LVEF is 48 percent. Which statement is best?", "options": {"A": "Ejection fraction of 48 percent excludes a large infarct, so low counts reflect apical thinning.", "B": "Extensive anterior and apical scar, with LVEF preserved by contraction of the remote walls.", "C": "Hibernating anterior myocardium, because counts near 28 percent of peak fall in the viable range.", "D": "Right coronary scar, since the posterior descending artery supplies the septal segments involved."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "using global EF to dismiss a large regional defect", "C": "misjudging the count threshold for viability", "D": "assigning septal anterior segments to the right coronary artery"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-036_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Extensive anterior and apical scar, with LVEF preserved by contraction of the remote walls.): Anterior, anteroseptal and apical segments (1, 2, 7, 8, 13, 14, 17) are left anterior descending territory. Counts near 28 percent of peak mean scar, and viable tissue generally keeps counts above about 50 percent. Remote walls can hyperkinetically compensate, so LVEF may remain near normal despite a large scar. Apical thinning does not explain a defect this extensive."}, {"id": 922, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A woman with large breasts undergoes SPECT with CT based attenuation correction to reduce breast artifact. After correction, a new inferior defect appears that was not present on the uncorrected images. The reader is unsure how to proceed. Which check should the physician make first?", "options": {"A": "Report true inferior ischemia, since correction reveals disease that was hidden before.", "B": "Confirm the CT map aligns with the emission data, since misregistration creates artifacts.", "C": "Repeat the CT at higher current, since map noise usually causes a new defect.", "D": "Stop using correction for later patients, since it cannot be trusted whenever a wall changes."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assumes correction reveals real disease", "C": "blames CT noise first", "D": "abandons the technique after one case"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-087_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm the CT map aligns with the emission data, since misregistration creates artifacts.): CT based correction depends on matching the attenuation map to the emission image, and even a small shift can move soft tissue onto the wrong wall and produce an artifact, commonly in the inferior or anterior wall. Checking registration is the first step. Higher CT current does not fix alignment, and assuming real disease skips the quality check."}, {"id": 923, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old woman undergoes adenosine infusion alone for stress Tc-99m sestamibi imaging. Review of the rotating raw projections shows poor image quality, and the study will be repeated next week. Which change to the stress protocol is most likely to improve the next acquisition?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the time per projection so patient motion has less effect on the data.", "B": "Switch the vasodilator to dobutamine to reduce splanchnic blood flow during injection.", "C": "Add low level treadmill exercise to the vasodilator infusion if she can walk safely.", "D": "Increase the injected activity to raise heart counts relative to neighboring structures."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confuses count statistics with extracardiac clearance", "B": "chooses a stressor that does not reduce splanchnic flow", "D": "thinks dose changes the heart to bowel ratio"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-092_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add low level treadmill exercise to the vasodilator infusion if she can walk safely.): Vasodilator stress alone increases splanchnic flow and subdiaphragmatic uptake, while adding low level exercise lowers that uptake and improves heart to bowel contrast. More dose scales heart and bowel activity together, shorter projections only reduce counts, and dobutamine does not reduce gut flow the way exercise does."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 57-year-old woman on trastuzumab for breast cancer had a baseline LVEF of 62 percent. A repeat gated blood-pool study reports 50 percent, and the institution's lower limit of normal is 53 percent. She has no symptoms and the cine shows symmetric contraction. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Stop trastuzumab permanently and refer for a defibrillator to prevent sudden cardiac death", "B": "Continue trastuzumab as scheduled and repeat the study in six months' time", "C": "Continue trastuzumab unchanged, since 50 percent is close to normal and she has no symptoms", "D": "Hold trastuzumab, start cardioprotective therapy and repeat the study in about four weeks"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "jumps to permanent cessation and device therapy", "B": "extends the monitoring interval despite a significant fall", "C": "compares to the normal range and ignores the 12-point fall"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-039_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold trastuzumab, start cardioprotective therapy and repeat the study in about four weeks): The fall of 12 points to a value below the institutional lower limit meets the usual criterion for withholding trastuzumab, even without symptoms. Standard practice is to hold the drug, begin heart failure therapy and repeat the ejection fraction in about four weeks to decide on resumption. Permanent cessation and a defibrillator are not warranted at 50 percent, and continuing unchanged or waiting six months ignores a significant decline."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old man with exertional chest pain has a gated rubidium-82 PET. The reader notes uptake reduced to about half of peak in the mid and apical anterior wall, the apical septum and the apex, with reduced thickening in those same segments, and the inferior and lateral walls look normal. Which coronary artery supplies the abnormal region?", "options": {"A": "The left circumflex artery, because the anterior wall is supplied by its obtuse marginal branches", "B": "The right coronary artery, because apical segments are usually supplied by its distal branches", "C": "The posterior descending artery, because septal segments are supplied by its perforator branches in most hearts", "D": "The left anterior descending artery, since the anterior, septal and apical segments lie in its territory"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confuses lateral and anterior territories", "B": "assigns the apex to the right coronary territory", "C": "uses the inferior septum to explain an apical septal defect"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-059_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The left anterior descending artery, since the anterior, septal and apical segments lie in its territory): Mid and apical anterior, apical septal and apical segments fall in the left anterior descending territory, and reduced thickening in the same walls supports a flow-limiting lesion or scar there. The circumflex supplies the lateral wall, and the right coronary or posterior descending supplies the inferior wall and basal inferoseptum, both of which are normal here."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 69-year-old man with a prior anterior infarct is sent for rubidium-82 PET before defibrillator evaluation. The images show a dilated ventricle with uniformly reduced contraction and relatively homogeneous uptake, and the gated LVEF is 40 percent. Which action is best supported by this study alone?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the PET at higher dose to confirm that perfusion defects are absent in every segment", "B": "Report LVEF and volumes, then start guideline-directed heart failure therapy and reassess before any device decision", "C": "Recommend immediate primary-prevention defibrillator implantation because the LVEF is below fifty percent on this PET", "D": "Recommend revascularization of the left anterior descending artery without any further viability testing of the territory"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats a normal perfusion pattern as an acquisition error", "C": "uses the wrong EF threshold for device implantation", "D": "assumes a prior infarct territory is viable"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-058_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report LVEF and volumes, then start guideline-directed heart failure therapy and reassess before any device decision): Primary-prevention defibrillator criteria generally require an LVEF of 35 percent or less after at least three months of guideline-directed therapy, so 40 percent does not meet them. The sensible plan is to report function and volumes and optimize medical therapy. Revascularization needs evidence of ischemia or viable myocardium. A homogeneous perfusion pattern is a result, not a reason to repeat at higher dose."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and no symptoms has a nondiagnostic exercise ECG because of resting repolarization changes, so he undergoes stress SPECT. Gated cine shows normal motion and thickening in all walls with normal perfusion. A referring physician asks whether this changes his short-term cardiac risk. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Diabetes cancels the value of a normal study, so he stays high risk and needs angiography.", "B": "His risk stays intermediate until a second stress study with another tracer confirms the result.", "C": "Gating does not predict outcome, so only the perfusion images count and the cine adds nothing.", "D": "Normal perfusion and wall motion mean low event risk and no further ischemia testing."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "overrides a normal result because of diabetes", "B": "demands unneeded confirmatory testing", "C": "dismisses prognostic value of gated function"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-003_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Normal perfusion and wall motion mean low event risk and no further ischemia testing.): A normal gated SPECT study, with normal perfusion and preserved function, carries a very low annual event rate even in patients with diabetes. Preserved wall motion adds to the prognostic value of the perfusion data rather than being irrelevant. Repeating the test or going straight to angiography is not supported when the study is normal and the patient has no symptoms."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 62-year-old woman has a rubidium-82 PET with a mild lateral wall count reduction, normal thickening and a gated LVEF of 60 percent. The reader notes the CT attenuation map is shifted 12 mm to the patient's left relative to the emission data. What is the best next step before issuing the report?", "options": {"A": "Recommend invasive angiography for the circumflex territory based on the isolated lateral wall defect", "B": "Repeat the entire stress and rest injection protocol, since misregistration cannot be fixed", "C": "Realign CT and emission data, reprocess attenuation correction, and reread before finalizing", "D": "Report a mild lateral perfusion defect because the normal thickening excludes any artifact explanation"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "refers on an artifact-affected finding", "B": "believes misregistration is not correctable by reprocessing", "D": "ignores a known misregistration"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-066_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Realign CT and emission data, reprocess attenuation correction, and reread before finalizing): A 12 mm shift is large enough to create a lateral wall artifact. The correct response is to realign the CT to the emission data, reprocess and reread. Normal thickening makes true disease unlikely, but it does not remove the need to fix the registration. Reinjection is not needed because only the processing was wrong, and angiography should not follow from an uncorrected artifact."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man had a stent placed in the obtuse marginal branch two years ago and now has recurrent exertional dyspnea. Gated blood-pool cine in the left anterior oblique view yields an LVEF of 48 percent, and the reader notes lateral hypokinesis while the other walls contract normally. Which vessel territory corresponds to the abnormal wall motion?", "options": {"A": "Left circumflex territory, supplying the basal and mid lateral segments", "B": "Left anterior descending territory, supplying the anterior wall and apex", "C": "Right coronary territory, supplying the inferior wall and posterior septum", "D": "Distal left anterior descending territory, supplying the apical septum only"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "assigns lateral wall to the left anterior descending artery", "C": "reads lateral wall as part of the right coronary bed", "D": "places the abnormality at the apex instead of the lateral wall"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-032_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left circumflex territory, supplying the basal and mid lateral segments): The cine shows hypokinesis of the lateral wall with normal motion elsewhere. The lateral segments, numbers 5, 6, 11, 12 and 16, are supplied by the left circumflex and its obtuse marginal branches, matching the earlier stent. The anterior wall and apex belong to the left anterior descending artery, and the inferior wall and posterior septum to the right coronary artery."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 has recurrent chest pain. Her prior SPECT had noisy projections and a dim, mottled heart with no motion. She asks for an alternative that gives better image quality in obesity. Which test is the best choice for her next evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the same one day SPECT protocol with a smaller dose to cut noise", "B": "Gated blood pool imaging at rest, which assesses ischemia without a stress test", "C": "Thallium-201 planar imaging because it avoids attenuation from a thick chest wall", "D": "Rubidium-82 or ammonia PET perfusion with CT based attenuation correction"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lowering dose when counts were already inadequate", "B": "Using a function study to answer a perfusion question", "C": "Believing planar imaging escapes attenuation in obesity"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-116_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rubidium-82 or ammonia PET perfusion with CT based attenuation correction): PET uses higher energy photons, coincidence detection and measured attenuation correction, which keeps image quality high in large patients. Repeating SPECT with less activity lowers counts further. Planar thallium has more scatter and no tomography, and a rest blood pool study measures ejection fraction rather than inducible ischemia."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A gated blood-pool region-of-interest analysis for a 60-year-old man gives raw left ventricular counts of 17,200 at end-diastole and 10,000 at end-systole. The background region counts 1,200 over the same area in both frames. The cine shows reduced motion in a single wall. What is the correct LVEF?", "options": {"A": "82 percent, from stroke counts divided by the end-systolic counts", "B": "55 percent, from the end-systolic fraction of the end-diastolic counts", "C": "42 percent, from the raw counts without subtracting the background", "D": "45 percent, from background-corrected counts of 16,000 and 8,800"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses end-systolic counts as the denominator", "B": "reports the residual fraction instead of the ejected fraction", "C": "forgets to subtract background counts from both frames"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-035_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (45 percent, from background-corrected counts of 16,000 and 8,800): Subtract the 1,200 background counts from each frame to get 16,000 at end-diastole and 8,800 at end-systole. The stroke counts are 7,200, and 7,200 divided by 16,000 equals 0.45, or 45 percent. Using raw counts gives 7,200 over 17,200, which is 42 percent. The end-systolic fraction is 55 percent, and dividing by end-systolic counts gives an inflated 82 percent."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and a prior anterior infarction is referred for a viability opinion before revascularization. On gated SPECT the mid anterior, anteroseptal and apical segments are akinetic. Which feature best separates scar from stunning in these akinetic segments?", "options": {"A": "A motion abnormality that follows a single coronary artery territory on the gated cine images.", "B": "Markedly reduced resting counts matching the motion abnormality, which indicates scar rather than stunning.", "C": "Left ventricular ejection fraction below 40 percent measured on the gated study images.", "D": "Degree of reduced systolic wall thickening measured in those akinetic segments on the gated cine."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "using territory pattern to separate scar from stunning", "C": "using global EF to judge regional viability", "D": "relying on thickening instead of uptake for viability"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-041_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Markedly reduced resting counts matching the motion abnormality, which indicates scar rather than stunning.): Both scar and stunning produce akinesis and reduced thickening, and both can follow a vascular territory, so those features do not separate them. Counts do: scar shows markedly reduced uptake (here segments 7, 8, 13, 14 and 17 near 25 percent), while stunning keeps resting uptake preserved. Global EF cannot localize viability."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old man with an old anterior infarction has taken maximal guideline directed medical therapy for more than three months. Gated SPECT shows a dilated left ventricle, an apical segment that bulges outward in systole, and an ejection fraction of 32 percent. What implication should the report state for his referring cardiologist?", "options": {"A": "Ejection fraction is 35 percent or less, so assess him for a primary prevention defibrillator.", "B": "Gated SPECT cannot be used here, so a left ventriculogram must precede any device decision.", "C": "The apical aneurysm itself indicates defibrillator implantation, whatever the ejection fraction happens to be.", "D": "An ejection fraction below 50 percent is the guideline threshold, so a device is clearly indicated."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "rejects gated SPECT for ejection fraction", "C": "treats aneurysm alone as a device criterion", "D": "uses the wrong ejection fraction threshold"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-071_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ejection fraction is 35 percent or less, so assess him for a primary prevention defibrillator.): Primary prevention defibrillator criteria after infarction rest on an ejection fraction of 35 percent or less despite at least three months of optimal therapy, more than 40 days from the infarct, and suitable functional class. Gated SPECT gives a reproducible ejection fraction, and 32 percent meets the number. The aneurysm alone is not a criterion, 50 percent is far too high, and a ventriculogram is not required."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "On a quantitative rest SPECT study in a 58 year old man, the mildly hypokinetic anterior and apical segments have counts about 72 percent of peak. Using the standard 5 point perfusion scoring scale, how should the perfusion in those segments be scored?", "options": {"A": "Score 2, moderately reduced, covering counts from 30 to 49 percent of peak.", "B": "Score 3, severely reduced, covering counts from 10 to 29 percent of peak.", "C": "Score 1, mildly reduced, covering counts from 50 to 69 percent of peak.", "D": "Score 0, normal, because counts reach 70 percent or more of peak."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "using the moderate band for near normal counts", "B": "using the severe band for near normal counts", "C": "shifting the normal band upward"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-038_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Score 0, normal, because counts reach 70 percent or more of peak.): On the 0 to 4 scale used for summed scores, 0 is normal at 70 percent or more of peak, 1 is mild at 50 to 69, 2 is moderate at 30 to 49, 3 is severe at 10 to 29, and 4 is absent below 10. A value of 72 percent therefore scores 0, even though the cine shows mild hypokinesis, which is graded separately."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A woman with moderate breast size has a mild anterior defect on sestamibi SPECT, and the gated images show the same wall thickening normally and moving well. How does this gated finding help distinguish breast attenuation from a true infarct in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Normal thickening excludes coronary disease in every wall, so no other reading is needed.", "B": "Thickening is measured on the ungated images, so gating adds nothing to artifact separation.", "C": "Normal thickening in the defect wall suggests artifact, since scar thickens poorly.", "D": "Normal thickening proves the defect is a true infarct, since gating detects scar as brighter walls."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "overgeneralizes from one wall", "B": "denies the value of gated data", "D": "inverts the role of thickening"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-088_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Normal thickening in the defect wall suggests artifact, since scar thickens poorly.): Scar does not thicken in systole, so a perfusion defect that thickens normally on gating is most likely a soft tissue artifact. This is one of the main reasons for gating in women with large breasts. It does not exclude disease in other walls, and it does not come from the ungated images."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 58-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and a normal resting ECG has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT at a heart rate of 66 beats per minute. The perfusion images are read as unremarkable. Review of the gated short-axis and vertical long-axis cine is next. Which statement best describes the gated left ventricular function?", "options": {"A": "All walls move inward and thicken normally, so global function is preserved without stress-induced dysfunction.", "B": "The apex moves less than other walls, suggesting apical hypokinesis and a possible distal infarct.", "C": "Inferior wall motion is reduced, consistent with right coronary stunning after regadenoson administration in this patient.", "D": "The septum thickens less than the lateral wall, suggesting a septal abnormality in the LAD territory."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "reads normal apical thinning as hypokinesis", "C": "invents inferior stunning from normal motion", "D": "treats lower septal counts as reduced motion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-001_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (All walls move inward and thicken normally, so global function is preserved without stress-induced dysfunction.): On a normal gated study every segment moves toward the center of the cavity and brightens as it thickens between end-diastole and end-systole. The apex and basal septum are normally dimmer and thinner, which lowers their counts without reducing motion. Because wall motion and thickening are both intact, the study shows no stress-induced dysfunction or regional motion abnormality, and the apical, septal and inferior readings are all artifacts of normal anatomy."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 49-year-old man is imaged with rest and regadenoson stress rubidium-82 PET. After processing, a mild lateral defect appears and wall motion is normal. The fused display shows the CT attenuation map offset about 8 mm from the emission data. Which statement best describes how to confirm the defect is an artifact?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the stress injection with a higher regadenoson dose and see whether the defect enlarges", "B": "Compare the defect with the CT calcium score and report ischemia if any calcium is present", "C": "Realign the CT to the emission data and reprocess; a normal result afterward confirms an artifact", "D": "Use the uncorrected emission images alone and accept the defect if it is also visible there"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats a processing problem as a pharmacologic one", "B": "equates calcium with a perfusion defect", "D": "trusts uncorrected data for a final answer"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-070_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Realign the CT to the emission data and reprocess; a normal result afterward confirms an artifact): The reliable test is realignment and reprocessing: if the defect disappears after the CT matches the emission data, it was an artifact. Pharmacologic repeat tests ischemia, not the processing. Calcium does not prove a perfusion defect. Uncorrected images have their own attenuation artifacts and are only an adjunct."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man with a myocardial infarction eight years ago has stable exertional dyspnea. Resting gated SPECT with Tc-99m tetrofosmin is performed and the short-axis cine is read with the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 45 percent. Which statement best describes the abnormal region and its implication?", "options": {"A": "Mid inferior scar in the right coronary territory; refer for revascularization to recover function there.", "B": "Matched mid lateral scar in the circumflex territory; revascularizing that bed would not recover function.", "C": "Mid lateral stunning in the circumflex territory; repeat imaging in weeks because preserved perfusion predicts recovery.", "D": "Lateral wall attenuation with normal thickening; no further evaluation is needed for the regional abnormality."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning lateral segments to the wrong vessel", "C": "Calling reduced perfusion stunning", "D": "Treating a motion abnormality as an attenuation artifact"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-046_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Matched mid lateral scar in the circumflex territory; revascularizing that bed would not recover function.): Mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral segments belong to the circumflex territory. Reduced resting counts matched with severely reduced motion and thickening indicate scar, so revascularization is unlikely to restore function. Stunning requires preserved perfusion. The inferior wall is right coronary territory, and attenuation does not abolish thickening."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63-year-old man has an echocardiographic ejection fraction of 55 percent one week ago. On regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT, the post-stress gated images show a dilated cavity, global hypokinesis, an ejection fraction of 22 percent and no focal perfusion defect. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "A normal variant, since post-stress ejection fraction is usually about 20 points lower than rest.", "B": "A gating error from ectopy explains the result, so the acquisition should be repeated first.", "C": "New dilated cardiomyopathy, since the low value is his true baseline and the echocardiogram was wrong.", "D": "A marked post-stress fall suggests extensive balanced ischemia, so proceed to coronary angiography."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats a huge drop as normal", "B": "assumes a gating artifact without evidence", "C": "dismisses the baseline echocardiogram"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-016_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A marked post-stress fall suggests extensive balanced ischemia, so proceed to coronary angiography.): A fall of more than 5 to 10 points from rest to post-stress, together with a dilated cavity, is a high risk sign even when relative perfusion looks even. It points to left main or severe multivessel disease with balanced ischemia. A normal post-stress drop is only a few points, and there is no evidence of ectopy, so repeating the test would delay an indicated angiogram."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman has dyspnea after a remote myocardial infarction. Her electrocardiogram shows sinus rhythm with a narrow QRS. A gated blood-pool cine in the left anterior oblique view gives LVEF 48 percent, with one segment of reduced thickening visible. Which interpretation best explains the abnormal motion?", "options": {"A": "Septal dyskinesis from left bundle branch block, with the lateral wall contracting normally", "B": "Global hypokinesis from dilated cardiomyopathy, with only mild regional variation between walls", "C": "Lateral wall hypokinesis from prior circumflex infarction, with a normally contracting septum", "D": "Apical dyskinesis from a left ventricular aneurysm, with the basal walls contracting normally"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "invokes a conduction abnormality the narrow QRS excludes", "B": "reads uniform dysfunction where only one wall is abnormal", "D": "places the abnormality at the apex rather than the lateral wall"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-034_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lateral wall hypokinesis from prior circumflex infarction, with a normally contracting septum): The cine shows reduced thickening of the lateral wall alone, in keeping with scar in the circumflex territory after the remote infarction. A narrow QRS rules out left bundle branch block, so paradoxical septal motion is not expected and the septum contracts normally. An apical aneurysm would show dyskinesis at the apex, and dilated cardiomyopathy would affect all walls with a lower fraction."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70 year old man with right dominant circulation, three months after inferior infarction, has gated SPECT. The basal and mid inferior and inferoseptal walls and the inferior apex bulge outward in systole with counts near 25 percent of peak, and LVEF is 39 percent. Which statement is best?", "options": {"A": "Right coronary scar with inferior aneurysm; echocardiography should assess mitral regurgitation and thrombus.", "B": "Left anterior descending scar, since septal perforators supply the inferoseptal segments involved on the gated images.", "C": "Hibernating inferior wall that should improve with PCI of the posterior descending artery branch.", "D": "Circumflex scar, because left dominance gives the circumflex the whole inferior wall and septum."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "assigning inferoseptal segments to the left anterior descending artery", "C": "calling dyskinetic scar viable hibernating myocardium", "D": "ignoring right dominance stated in the scenario"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-043_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right coronary scar with inferior aneurysm; echocardiography should assess mitral regurgitation and thrombus.): Segments 3, 4, 9, 10 and 15 (inferoseptal, inferior and inferior apex) are right coronary territory in a right dominant system. Outward bulging with counts near 25 percent is scar with aneurysm, and inferior infarcts can disturb the posteromedial papillary muscle and form thrombus, so echocardiography is sensible. Recovery after PCI is not expected from scar."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 60-year-old woman has Q waves in leads III and aVF after a past myocardial infarction and an LVEF of 42 percent on gated blood-pool imaging. A segment-based report lists the abnormal region from the cine. Which group of 17-segment model numbers best matches the region that moves poorly?", "options": {"A": "Segments 3, 4, 9, 10 and 15, the inferoseptal and inferior walls", "B": "Segments 1, 2, 7, 8 and 13, the anterior and anteroseptal walls", "C": "Segments 14 and 17, the apical septum and apex alone", "D": "Segments 5, 6, 11, 12 and 16, the inferolateral and anterolateral walls"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "reads the septum as anterior wall in the left anterior oblique view", "C": "limits the abnormality to the apex only", "D": "lists the lateral wall segments for the inferior wall"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-037_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Segments 3, 4, 9, 10 and 15, the inferoseptal and inferior walls): The abnormal motion involves the inferior and inferoseptal walls, which in the 17-segment model are basal inferoseptal, basal inferior, mid inferoseptal, mid inferior and apical inferior, numbers 3, 4, 9, 10 and 15. These belong to the right coronary artery territory and match the inferior Q waves. The lateral set is numbers 5, 6, 11, 12 and 16, and the anterior set is 1, 2, 7, 8 and 13."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52 year old man had primary PCI of the distal left anterior descending artery two days ago for anterior infarction. Rest gated SPECT shows the apical anterior, apical septal and apical segments are akinetic with preserved counts, and LVEF is 37 percent. What is the best interpretation and plan?", "options": {"A": "Fixed apical scar, so implant a primary prevention defibrillator now because LVEF is 37 percent.", "B": "Apical thinning artifact, so repeat acquisition with attenuation correction to confirm normal motion.", "C": "Right coronary infarction, so plan angiography of the dominant right coronary artery.", "D": "Stunned apical myocardium; reassess LVEF weeks later before deciding on a defibrillator."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "ignoring the waiting period for ICD after infarction", "B": "labeling true akinesis as apical thinning artifact", "C": "assigning apical anterior segments to the right coronary artery"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-025_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stunned apical myocardium; reassess LVEF weeks later before deciding on a defibrillator.): The apex and anterior apical wall belong to the distal left anterior descending territory. Akinesis with preserved rest counts two days after reperfusion is the stunning pattern, and the low EF partly reflects that stunning. Guidelines favor reassessing LVEF after recovery time (about 40 days after infarction or 90 days after revascularization) before an ICD. Apical thinning is a count artifact, not akinesis."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 59-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction five months ago and now has stable class II dyspnea. He has taken maximally tolerated carvedilol, sacubitril-valsartan and spironolactone for four months. Electrocardiogram shows sinus rhythm with QRS 98 ms. The gated blood-pool cine is processed to an LVEF of 32 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for a primary-prevention defibrillator, since LVEF is 35 percent or less despite adequate medical therapy", "B": "Implant a cardiac resynchronization defibrillator, because the ventricle is dilated and contracts poorly", "C": "Repeat the blood-pool study in six months on the same drugs before deciding", "D": "Refer for left ventricular assist device evaluation to treat the dilated, poorly contracting ventricle now"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "applies resynchronization therapy without a wide QRS", "C": "delays the device decision after therapy period is complete", "D": "uses an advanced therapy for stable class II symptoms"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-025_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for a primary-prevention defibrillator, since LVEF is 35 percent or less despite adequate medical therapy): The cine shows a dilated left ventricle with globally reduced contraction, and the processed LVEF of 32 percent meets the 35 percent or lower criterion. He is more than 40 days past infarction, in class II, and has completed at least three months of guideline therapy, so a primary-prevention defibrillator is indicated. Resynchronization needs a wide QRS, which he lacks. Waiting longer adds risk without new information, and an assist device is reserved for advanced, refractory failure."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with nonischemic cardiomyopathy has been hospitalized three times this year for decompensated failure. She is intolerant of higher beta-blocker doses and has class IIIB to IV symptoms despite diuretics and neurohormonal therapy. Gated blood-pool processing gives LVEF 18 percent with a dilated cavity. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Increase the beta-blocker to target dose and repeat the study next month", "B": "Place a primary-prevention defibrillator alone and continue the current outpatient regimen with closer clinic follow-up", "C": "Order a stress perfusion study to look for reversible ischemia as the cause", "D": "Refer to an advanced heart failure center for evaluation for ventricular assist device or transplant"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "pushes a drug dose the patient has already failed", "B": "treats sudden death risk but ignores refractory pump failure", "C": "assumes ischemic cause in a known nonischemic cardiomyopathy"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-026_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer to an advanced heart failure center for evaluation for ventricular assist device or transplant): An LVEF of 18 percent with a large cavity, recurrent admissions and persistent class IIIB to IV symptoms marks advanced heart failure that needs advanced therapy evaluation. A defibrillator addresses arrhythmic death but not the failing pump. The beta-blocker has already been limited by intolerance. The cardiomyopathy is known to be nonischemic, so a perfusion study would not change management."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with frequent ectopy has stress SPECT whose gated cine is poor, with software EF 55 percent and a visible reversible inferior defect. A fellow proposes discarding the whole study as uninterpretable. Which part of the study remains reliable?", "options": {"A": "End-systolic volume remains valid because it comes from the frames with the cleanest counts.", "B": "Wall motion and thickening remain valid because they derive from summed images, not frame data.", "C": "Transient ischemic dilation cannot be measured because it requires clean gated frames to compare volumes.", "D": "Summed perfusion images and defect extent remain valid, since all counted beats contribute to them."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a clear frame guarantees a correct volume", "B": "Believing regional function does not depend on gating", "C": "Wrongly claiming TID depends on gating"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-013_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Summed perfusion images and defect extent remain valid, since all counted beats contribute to them.): Perfusion images are formed from counts across the whole cycle, so rhythm problems barely change them. Motion, thickening and volumes all depend on correct frame assignment. Transient ischemic dilation compares ungated stress and rest cavity size, so it does not need gating. The study should be reported with a stated functional limitation, not discarded."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and known severe circumflex stenosis is evaluated for viability. Resting gated SPECT is reviewed with the short-axis and long-axis cine and the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 37 percent. What is the best conclusion about the abnormal wall?", "options": {"A": "Normal resting uptake shows the lateral wall is undiseased, so the stenosis cannot explain this akinesis.", "B": "Viable left anterior descending myocardium that should improve after revascularization of the anterior wall vessel.", "C": "Viable circumflex-territory myocardium with preserved counts; revascularization may improve lateral wall motion and the LVEF.", "D": "Nonviable lateral scar, so revascularizing the circumflex vessel is expected to give no benefit."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing preserved resting uptake with absent disease", "B": "Assigning lateral segments to the anterior descending artery", "D": "Equating akinesis with scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-049_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Viable circumflex-territory myocardium with preserved counts; revascularization may improve lateral wall motion and the LVEF.): Akinetic lateral segments with preserved resting counts mean the myocytes are alive but not contracting, consistent with stunned or hibernating myocardium in the circumflex territory with a known culprit. Such segments can recover after revascularization. Akinesis alone does not prove scar, and preserved uptake does not exclude a flow-limiting stenosis."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60-year-old woman has treadmill Tc-99m sestamibi imaging. Software reports an 18 mm upward drift. The reader must decide from the sinogram whether this is creep or a sudden jump. Which sinogram appearance indicates creep, and what is the usual handling?", "options": {"A": "A smooth gradual slope across projections, usually corrected with motion correction software.", "B": "A single sharp step at one projection, usually corrected with motion correction software.", "C": "A single sharp step at one projection, which indicates a failed center of rotation.", "D": "A smooth gradual slope across projections, which requires reacquisition of the study every time."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "describes abrupt motion with a correctable plan", "C": "confuses motion step with calibration fault", "D": "assumes slow drift cannot be corrected"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-106_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A smooth gradual slope across projections, usually corrected with motion correction software.): Creep looks like a smooth slope in the sinogram trace as the heart rises across the acquisition, and software correction tracks that continuous change well. Abrupt movement appears as a step and often needs reacquisition. A center of rotation fault does not produce a step in one patient's sinogram."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old man had a large anterior ST elevation myocardial infarction three days ago with primary PCI to the proximal left anterior descending artery. Gated SPECT with resting Tc-99m sestamibi shows akinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex with preserved counts there. What does this pattern most likely represent?", "options": {"A": "Transmural scar in the anterior wall, so preserved counts are artifact and an ICD is planned.", "B": "Chronic hibernation needing urgent repeat revascularization, since akinesis after PCI means the stent has failed.", "C": "Stunned myocardium after reperfusion, since motion is lost with preserved counts and may recover in weeks.", "D": "Microvascular obstruction without viability, since akinetic segments after reperfusion do not recover function."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "calls preserved counts artifact", "B": "confuses acute stunning with stent failure", "D": "assumes akinetic segments never recover"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-017_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stunned myocardium after reperfusion, since motion is lost with preserved counts and may recover in weeks.): Early after reperfusion, myocardium can remain akinetic although it is perfused and alive, which is stunning. The preserved resting counts show intact cell membranes and flow, so recovery over days to weeks is expected. Deciding on scar or an ICD this early is premature, and a successful PCI does not imply stent failure."}, {"id": 950, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 62 year old man had an anterior infarction four months ago and takes guideline therapy. Gated SPECT shows paradoxical outward motion of the apical anterior, apical septal and apical segments with counts near 25 percent of peak, and LVEF is 39 percent. Which interpretation best reconciles the EF and the regional findings?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse nonischemic cardiomyopathy, since reduced ejection fraction implies global hypokinesis of every segment.", "B": "Stress cardiomyopathy likely to recover over several weeks without further left ventricular treatment.", "C": "Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with an apical pouch and preserved myocyte uptake in the segments.", "D": "Apical scar with aneurysm explains the low ejection fraction while remote walls contract normally."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assuming low EF always means global hypokinesis", "B": "applying stunning time course to a four month old scar", "C": "confusing apical aneurysm scar with hypertrophic pouch"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-044_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apical scar with aneurysm explains the low ejection fraction while remote walls contract normally.): The outward apical bulge with markedly reduced counts (segments 13, 14 and 17, left anterior descending territory) is scar, and a localized aneurysm can lower EF substantially while other segments move normally. Stress cardiomyopathy shows preserved counts and recovers early, and a hypertrophic pouch would show preserved uptake, not an infarct scar pattern."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with dyspnea and prior chemotherapy has a rest rubidium-82 PET. The gated images show a dilated left ventricle with weak, uniform contraction in all walls, and the measured LVEF is 30 percent. She asks what the number means for her heart. Which statement is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "An LVEF of 30 percent is normal for a dilated ventricle, so no treatment is needed", "B": "Pump function is severely reduced, fitting dilated cardiomyopathy, and needs therapy and workup", "C": "Gated PET EF is unreliable below fifty percent, so repeat the study with SPECT", "D": "Weak contraction in every wall proves multivessel coronary disease, so she needs bypass surgery"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "believes dilation makes a low EF normal", "C": "doubts PET gating at low EF", "D": "equates global dysfunction with surgical coronary disease"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-055_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pump function is severely reduced, fitting dilated cardiomyopathy, and needs therapy and workup): An LVEF of 30 percent is severely reduced, and global hypokinesis with a dilated cavity fits a nonischemic or end-stage cardiomyopathy. It needs workup and heart failure therapy. Dilation does not normalize the EF. Global dysfunction is not specific for surgical coronary disease. PET gated EF is accurate across the range, so repeating it with SPECT is not needed."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 67-year-old woman presents with exertional chest discomfort and a gated rubidium-82 PET. Uptake is reduced to about half of peak in the basal and mid inferoseptal and inferior walls and in the apical inferior wall, with reduced motion there. Which vessel is the most likely culprit?", "options": {"A": "The first diagonal artery, because inferior segments below the base are fed by its apical branches", "B": "The left anterior descending artery, because the whole septum including its inferior part is its distribution", "C": "Right-dominant right coronary artery, because inferior and inferoseptal segments share its posterior descending distribution", "D": "The left circumflex artery, because inferior and septal segments are fed by its terminal marginal branches"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "gives an inferior wall a diagonal origin", "B": "assumes the entire septum is anterior descending territory", "D": "assigns septal segments to the circumflex"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-061_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right-dominant right coronary artery, because inferior and inferoseptal segments share its posterior descending distribution): The basal and mid inferoseptal and inferior segments plus the apical inferior segment are the right coronary artery territory when it is dominant. The inferoseptum is supplied by the posterior descending artery, while the anteroseptum belongs to the left anterior descending. The circumflex supplies lateral segments, and diagonal branches supply anterolateral and anterior segments."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61 year old woman had a rest sestamibi injection 60 minutes before imaging. On the rotating projection images the heart sits in the middle of the field, no motion is visible, and liver and bowel lie beneath the heart without overlapping it. What is the most appropriate action before reporting the study?", "options": {"A": "Process the study as acquired, since liver is separate and motion absent.", "B": "Apply a spatial filter that suppresses liver, since any liver activity in the views causes artifact.", "C": "Ask her to drink water and repeat acquisition after 30 minutes to clear liver activity.", "D": "Reposition her prone and repeat, since the inferior wall cannot be assessed with liver in view."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "assumes liver activity creates artifact", "C": "repeats a study that has no problem", "D": "orders prone imaging for a nonexistent problem"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-078_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Process the study as acquired, since liver is separate and motion absent.): Liver and bowel appear on every rest image, and they degrade the inferior wall only when they overlap it. Here a gap separates them from the heart and there is no motion, so nothing needs correcting. Water, repeat imaging, and aggressive filtering would add time or distort the data without any benefit."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A gated blood-pool study on a 66-year-old woman shows noisy late frames, though the processed LVEF of 55 percent looks consistent with her history. The physician asks which part of the study is most affected by the count loss in the late frames. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "Ejection fraction, which relies mainly on the final frame of the cycle", "B": "End-systolic counts, which are obtained from the first frames after the R wave of each beat", "C": "Background counts, which are taken from the late frames of every beat", "D": "Late diastolic filling indices, which depend on the last frames, are the least reliable"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assumes EF depends on the last frame instead of end-diastole and end-systole", "B": "places end-systole in the first frames instead of mid-cycle", "C": "misplaces background measurement in late frames"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-048_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Late diastolic filling indices, which depend on the last frames, are the least reliable): Frame count loss at the end of the cycle mainly damages measurements that use late diastole, such as filling rates and the atrial contribution. Ejection fraction relies on end-diastolic counts from the first frame and end-systolic counts near mid-cycle, which are less affected, so the reported value remains usable. Background is drawn from a region outside the ventricle, not from specific frames."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 70-year-old man has an old inferior infarct. On resting gated SPECT the five inferior segments are scored 3 on the 0 to 4 scale for resting perfusion and all other segments score 0, while the cine shows the same segments akinetic. What is the summed rest score and its percent of maximum?", "options": {"A": "Summed rest score 15, which is 22 percent of the 68-point maximum.", "B": "Summed rest score 5, counting abnormal segments rather than adding their severity scores.", "C": "Summed rest score 15, which is 88 percent of the 17-segment total.", "D": "Summed rest score 15, which is 29 percent of a 51-point maximum."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Counting segments rather than adding scores", "C": "Dividing the score by the segment count", "D": "Using three points rather than four as the per-segment maximum"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-055_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Summed rest score 15, which is 22 percent of the 68-point maximum.): The summed rest score adds each segment score: five segments times 3 equals 15. The maximum is 17 segments times 4, or 68, and 15 divided by 68 is about 22 percent. A 51-point maximum uses 3 per segment, counting segments gives 5, and dividing by 17 mixes units."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea has a rest Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT study, and the technologist reviews the rotating raw projection images before reconstruction. Which statement best describes how intense activity beside the heart on the raw data affects the reconstructed short-axis slices?", "options": {"A": "Scatter from the hot structure raises counts uniformly in every wall, hiding all perfusion defects.", "B": "Filtered backprojection can lower apparent counts in the wall next to the hot structure.", "C": "Reconstruction places extra counts in the apex because the apex lies farthest from the detector.", "D": "The hot structure produces a defect that follows a coronary territory and reflects true disease."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assumes uniform rather than local effect", "C": "mislocates the artifact to the apex", "D": "reads artifact as true vessel disease"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-090_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Filtered backprojection can lower apparent counts in the wall next to the hot structure.): Ramp filtered backprojection creates negative lobes around an intense focus, so counts in the adjacent myocardium are artificially reduced. The effect is local, tied to the wall touching the hot structure, and does not respect a coronary territory. Uniform count increase and apical extra counts are not the pattern produced by this reconstruction error."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66 year old man has stress sestamibi SPECT. The technologist notes only that he found lying with arms raised uncomfortable. On the rotating raw cine you see the heart centered, no frame to frame shift, and no unusual hot extracardiac focus. Reconstruction would take ten minutes. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reconstruct only the gated images, since comfort problems affect perfusion images alone.", "B": "Repeat the acquisition now, since any complaint of discomfort means unseen motion occurred.", "C": "Reconstruct and read the study, since the raw data show no motion or extracardiac problem.", "D": "Reinject sestamibi and repeat the stress acquisition so reader counts will be higher."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "splits the reading arbitrarily", "B": "assumes discomfort proves motion", "D": "orders extra radiation for an adequate study"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-080_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reconstruct and read the study, since the raw data show no motion or extracardiac problem.): A complaint of discomfort is a reason to inspect for motion, not proof of it. The cine here shows none, so the study is adequate, and repeating it would add radiation or time with no gain. Reading only the gated images or reinjecting is unjustified when the raw data are good."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59-year-old man has exercise Tc-99m sestamibi imaging, and the rotating raw projections show the heart moving relative to the horizontal line partway through. The reconstructed stress images show an anterior wall defect. How should this defect be treated in the report?", "options": {"A": "Report anterior scar, since a defect present on a single acquisition is fixed.", "B": "Correct the defect by raising the display scale until the anterior wall looks normal.", "C": "Treat it as unreliable, check the sinogram, and repeat the acquisition before calling ischemia.", "D": "Report anterior ischemia, since the defect appears in the territory of the left anterior descending artery."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assumes a single study defect is fixed", "B": "changes display scale to hide the defect", "D": "maps an artifact to a coronary territory"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-099_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat it as unreliable, check the sinogram, and repeat the acquisition before calling ischemia.): Misregistration from sudden movement during acquisition can create spurious defects in any wall, including territories that fit a coronary pattern. Confirming on the sinogram and repeating the acquisition gives data that can support a diagnosis. Labeling ischemia or scar accepts the artifact as disease, and display changes do not restore lost position information."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57-year-old man with diabetes, smoking history and exertional chest pressure presents with new dyspnea. Gated SPECT shows a dilated cavity, global hypokinesis, an ejection fraction of 30 percent and no focal perfusion defect. Which statement and next step are best?", "options": {"A": "Cardiac amyloidosis is the likeliest cause, so technetium pyrophosphate imaging should replace any coronary assessment.", "B": "The study is unreliable because global hypokinesis cannot be assessed, so repeat it with dobutamine.", "C": "Normal relative perfusion excludes obstructive disease, so the cause is nonischemic and coronary assessment is unnecessary.", "D": "Normal relative perfusion cannot exclude balanced multivessel disease, so coronary or CT angiography should follow."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "jumps to amyloid despite angina and risk factors", "B": "calls global hypokinesis unassessable", "C": "treats normal relative perfusion as excluding CAD"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-014_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Normal relative perfusion cannot exclude balanced multivessel disease, so coronary or CT angiography should follow.): Relative perfusion imaging compares each region with the brightest region, so balanced reduction in all territories can look normal. With exertional chest pressure, diabetes and tobacco use, the pretest probability of obstructive disease is high, and new heart failure guidelines support a coronary assessment. Amyloid evaluation is useful in other settings, but it should not displace the coronary question here."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64 year old woman with a prior circumflex infarct is being considered for revascularization. Gated SPECT shows akinesis of the basal, mid and apical lateral segments with counts near 25 percent of peak, and LVEF is 43 percent. What benefit from revascularizing the circumflex is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Large improvement, since akinetic segments with any detectable uptake recover after bypass grafting surgery.", "B": "Improvement limited to the apex, since apical lateral segments recover before basal and mid segments.", "C": "Improved ejection fraction, since akinesis in five segments reflects stunning that will resolve over weeks.", "D": "Little benefit because the lateral wall is transmural scar; revascularize territories with proven ischemia."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treating any residual uptake as viable myocardium", "B": "claiming a fixed apical first recovery order", "C": "labeling matched defect as stunning"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-035_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Little benefit because the lateral wall is transmural scar; revascularize territories with proven ischemia.): Segments 5, 6, 11, 12 and 16 are the lateral wall. Counts near 25 percent matched to akinesis represent scar, which does not recover. Stunning and hibernation keep resting counts at or above about 50 percent, and recovery does not follow a fixed apex to base order. Revascularization is better aimed at viable or ischemic territories."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61 year old woman with prior circumflex stenting has gated SPECT before a decision on repeat intervention. The lateral wall is moderately hypokinetic with counts near 50 percent of peak, and LVEF is 53 percent. Which statement best describes recovery potential?", "options": {"A": "Dense transmural scar with no recoverable function, since moderate count reduction means irreversible necrosis.", "B": "Mixed viable and scarred lateral myocardium, so recovery is uncertain and viability testing may help.", "C": "Normal study, since an ejection fraction of 53 percent places the lateral wall within limits.", "D": "Fully viable stunned wall, since uptake above 30 percent of peak predicts recovery after reperfusion."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "calling moderate count reduction transmural scar", "C": "letting global EF override regional findings", "D": "using the wrong viability threshold"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-037_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mixed viable and scarred lateral myocardium, so recovery is uncertain and viability testing may help.): Counts near 50 percent of peak sit on the boundary, scored as moderately reduced, and matched hypokinesis suggests partial thickness scar mixed with viable muscle in the circumflex territory. Recovery is therefore uncertain, and FDG PET or MRI can refine it. Viability thresholds are higher than 30 percent, global EF does not rule out regional disease, and moderate loss is not dense transmural scar."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old man with a BMI of 41 had a one day Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The raw projections look noisy and the heart is dim and mottled, with the diaphragm seated high. There was no patient motion. For his next SPECT, which protocol change best improves count statistics?", "options": {"A": "Image earlier after injection so more tracer is still concentrated in the heart and lungs", "B": "Use a weight adjusted higher Tc-99m dose and a longer time per projection", "C": "Keep the fixed dose given to a 70 kg patient and apply a sharper reconstruction filter", "D": "Shorten the time per projection so the noise is averaged over many more rapid views"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Thinking earlier imaging raises myocardial counts", "C": "Ignoring weight adjusted dosing and trying to fix noise by filtering", "D": "Believing shorter views reduce noise"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-115_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a weight adjusted higher Tc-99m dose and a longer time per projection): Photon attenuation and scatter in a large patient lower the counts reaching the camera, so projections are noisy. Raising the administered activity within weight based guidance and lengthening the acquisition raise counts. Shorter views and sharper filters worsen noise, and imaging early leaves more lung and liver background rather than more myocardial counts."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 61-year-old man has exertional chest pressure. After treadmill stress with Tc-99m sestamibi, Clip 1 is the post-stress gated study and Clip 2 is the rest gated study. Lateral and inferolateral walls move poorly after stress and normally at rest, with LVEF about 47 percent after stress and about 62 percent at rest. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Hibernating myocardium that needs viability testing before further work up", "B": "Fixed scar in the circumflex territory, because the wall moves poorly after stress", "C": "Measurement variability, since a 15 point difference falls within repeat study error", "D": "Post-stress stunning of the circumflex territory, marking severe and extensive ischemia"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing stunning with hibernation", "B": "Calling a reversible change scar although rest wall motion is normal", "C": "Believing repeat variability reaches 15 points"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-123_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-123_2.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Post-stress stunning of the circumflex territory, marking severe and extensive ischemia): Wall motion that fails after stress but is normal at rest, with LVEF falling 15 points, is post-stress stunning from a severe ischemic insult. This high-risk marker points to the left circumflex territory here. Repeat variability is about 5 points, scar would be abnormal at rest, and hibernation is chronic dysfunction at rest."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man with diabetes has a stress SPECT. The technologist notes an apparent mild anterior wall count reduction on the uncorrected slices. The raw cine shows a slow 14 mm sideways drift with no vertical motion, and gated wall thickening looks normal. Which interpretation and action is best?", "options": {"A": "Reversible LAD ischemia, because the reduction is limited to the anterior wall territory", "B": "Breast style soft tissue attenuation, which needs no change in processing or review", "C": "Probable motion artifact; apply motion correction and confirm the apparent defect resolves", "D": "Fixed anterior scar from prior infarction, because reduced counts on one wall indicate necrosis"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Naming a vessel territory without checking raw data quality", "B": "Choosing the wrong artifact for a patient with this habitus", "D": "Reading an artifact as scar from count loss alone"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-113_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Probable motion artifact; apply motion correction and confirm the apparent defect resolves): Normal thickening argues against scar or ischemia in the same wall, and the raw cine shows a sideways drift that explains smeared counts. The correct approach is to correct motion and see whether the apparent defect disappears. Scar needs reduced thickening, ischemia needs a stress to rest change, and attenuation is fixed to body anatomy rather than to a drift during acquisition."}, {"id": 965, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 55-year-old woman had exercise Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Clip 1 was acquired after stress and Clip 2 was acquired at rest. Inferior and inferoseptal walls have reduced motion and counts after stress, but move normally at rest. LVEF is about 48 percent after stress and 60 percent at rest. Does diaphragmatic attenuation explain this?", "options": {"A": "No; attenuation stays fixed between studies, so this is right coronary stunning", "B": "Yes; a 12 point LVEF fall is typical of diaphragmatic attenuation in the inferior wall", "C": "No; the pattern points to the circumflex artery because the inferior wall is involved", "D": "Yes; inferior counts fall after stress in most patients, and wall motion follows counts"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Inventing an attenuation related EF change", "C": "Mixing up right coronary and circumflex territories", "D": "Linking motion changes to count loss from attenuation"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-128_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-128_2.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No; attenuation stays fixed between studies, so this is right coronary stunning): Attenuation lowers counts at the same place on both studies and does not change wall motion. Here motion is normal at rest and poor after stress, so the finding is post-stress stunning of the inferior and inferoseptal walls, which belong to the right coronary distribution in a right dominant system."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 66-year-old man is seen six weeks after an inferior myocardial infarction treated with stenting. He has no symptoms and takes a beta-blocker and an ACE inhibitor. Gated blood-pool processing reports LVEF 42 percent, with reduced motion of the inferior wall only. Which statement about defibrillator candidacy is correct?", "options": {"A": "A primary-prevention defibrillator is indicated now, since the infarction is recent and regional dysfunction persists", "B": "A defibrillator is indicated because inferior infarction carries higher arrhythmic risk than anterior infarction", "C": "A primary-prevention defibrillator is not indicated, since LVEF exceeds 35 percent and he is asymptomatic", "D": "A defibrillator is indicated if repeat imaging within a week shows LVEF below 45 percent"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "ignores the ejection fraction threshold and the waiting period", "B": "applies infarct location rather than EF to device decisions", "D": "uses an invented 45 percent threshold"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-036_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A primary-prevention defibrillator is not indicated, since LVEF exceeds 35 percent and he is asymptomatic): Primary-prevention defibrillator criteria after infarction require LVEF of 35 percent or less measured at least 40 days after infarction, or 30 percent or less in class I, with adequate medical therapy. His EF of 42 percent is above the threshold and he has no symptoms, so a device is not indicated. Recent infarction alone, a threshold of 45 percent, and infarct location do not define candidacy."}, {"id": 967, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 47-year-old man, BMI 36, has exercise stress with Tc-99m tetrofosmin. The stress images show a mild inferior wall count reduction absent on a prior rest study. Review of the rotating raw images is the next step. Which interpretation and action fit best?", "options": {"A": "Suspect neighboring extracardiac activity, check gated thickening, and repeat stress imaging if doubt remains.", "B": "Call inferior ischemia in the right coronary territory and recommend revascularization planning without further review.", "C": "Attribute the finding to diaphragmatic attenuation and apply a larger breast marker next time.", "D": "Accept the rest study as definitive and discard the stress images from the final report."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "calls reversible defect ischemia without checking artifact", "C": "applies wrong artifact correction", "D": "discards stress data wrongly"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-091_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suspect neighboring extracardiac activity, check gated thickening, and repeat stress imaging if doubt remains.): A reduction that appears on one study and not the other should prompt a look at the raw data for neighboring activity, which lowers inferior counts through reconstruction. Normal gated thickening in the same wall supports artifact, and reacquiring after clearance settles it. Calling ischemia skips this check, a breast marker is irrelevant here, and discarding stress data removes the very study being tested."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68-year-old man had an inferior myocardial infarction ten years ago and now has atypical chest discomfort. Resting gated SPECT is reviewed with the short-axis and long-axis cine and the resting perfusion images. LVEF is 48 percent. Which description of territory and extent is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Inferior right coronary stunning involving about 18 percent of the ventricle, expected to recover spontaneously.", "B": "Inferior right coronary scar involving two of the 17 segments, about 12 percent of the ventricle.", "C": "Inferolateral circumflex scar involving three of the 17 segments, about 18 percent of the ventricle.", "D": "Inferior right coronary scar in three of 17 segments, about 18 percent of the ventricle."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Calling reduced perfusion stunning", "B": "Omitting the apical inferior segment", "C": "Assigning inferior segments to the circumflex"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-051_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inferior right coronary scar in three of 17 segments, about 18 percent of the ventricle.): The basal, mid and apical inferior segments are the right coronary territory in a right-dominant system. Reduced resting counts matched with severely reduced motion indicate scar, and three of 17 segments is about 18 percent of the myocardium. Stunning needs preserved counts, and the inferolateral wall is circumflex territory."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 68 kg man had a gated stress Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT with the correct dose given 45 minutes before imaging. The projections are speckled with the heart barely above background. The acquisition record shows 10 seconds per projection instead of the usual 25 seconds. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Reconstruct with a ramp filter alone, which sharpens the speckle into clearer wall counts", "B": "Inject a second full dose, then image again for the same short time", "C": "Accept the study and report it with attenuation correction to restore the missing counts", "D": "Reacquire the study with the standard time per projection, since dose and uptake were fine"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing a sharper filter fixes low statistics", "B": "Adding dose instead of correcting the acquisition setting", "C": "Expecting correction software to create counts not acquired"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-119_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reacquire the study with the standard time per projection, since dose and uptake were fine): Counts scale with time per projection, so the shortened acquisition explains the poor statistics while dose and uptake were fine. The remedy is to repeat the scan with the standard time while the tracer remains in the myocardium. Software correction cannot add missing counts, extra tracer is unnecessary, and a ramp filter amplifies noise."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 63 year old man with a narrow QRS had a large anterior infarction five months ago and now has exertional dyspnea. Gated SPECT shows paradoxical outward motion of the anterior wall, septum and apex with markedly reduced counts there, and LVEF is 33 percent. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Transmural anterior scar with aneurysm; contrast echocardiography should exclude apical left ventricular thrombus.", "B": "Left bundle branch block related septal motion, so the perfusion defect reflects conduction artifact.", "C": "Stunned anterior myocardium expected to recover within weeks, so repeat imaging after continued medical therapy.", "D": "Hibernating anterior wall with preserved viability, so surgical revascularization should proceed to raise ejection fraction."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "attributing dyskinesis to conduction delay despite narrow QRS", "C": "applying a stunning time course to a five month old infarct", "D": "ignoring severely reduced counts in the akinetic wall"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-026_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transmural anterior scar with aneurysm; contrast echocardiography should exclude apical left ventricular thrombus.): Outward systolic bulging with counts near 25 percent of peak in the left anterior descending territory (segments 1, 2, 7, 8, 13, 14, 17) is a scar based aneurysm, not viable muscle. Aneurysms predispose to thrombus, so further imaging is reasonable. The QRS is narrow, which rules out left bundle branch block motion, and stunning does not persist five months after the event."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 60 year old asymptomatic man had an anterior infarction five years ago. Gated SPECT shows mild hypokinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex with counts near 72 percent of peak, and LVEF is 59 percent. Which statement describes the extent of scar best?", "options": {"A": "Large transmural scar, since mild hypokinesis in seven segments predicts ejection fraction below 40 percent.", "B": "Normal study, since an ejection fraction of 59 percent excludes any prior anterior infarct.", "C": "Small nontransmural anterior and apical scar, with LVEF preserved by normal motion elsewhere.", "D": "Apical thinning artifact falsely reducing apical counts, so the study is read as free of scar."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confusing number of segments with severity", "B": "assuming normal EF excludes old infarct", "D": "labeling a left anterior descending territory finding as artifact"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-040_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Small nontransmural anterior and apical scar, with LVEF preserved by normal motion elsewhere.): Counts near 72 percent are at the lower edge of normal and the hypokinesis is mild, which indicates a small nontransmural scar spread over left anterior descending segments 1, 2, 7, 8, 13, 14 and 17. Severity, not segment count, drives EF. Normal EF does not exclude prior infarct, and thinning artifact would not affect the anterior wall and septum."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 57 year old man has exertional dyspnea and a chronically occluded left anterior descending artery with collateral filling. Rest gated SPECT shows severe hypokinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex with preserved counts, and LVEF is 42 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the perfusion study with thallium reinjection before deciding about the apical segments.", "B": "Dobutamine echocardiography is needed first because gated SPECT cannot separate viable from scarred myocardium.", "C": "Viable anterior territory myocardium; revascularization is favored and may improve regional function and ejection fraction.", "D": "Left anterior descending scar, so medical therapy alone is appropriate since recovery is not expected."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "ordering a redundant test when counts are preserved", "B": "believing SPECT cannot assess viability", "D": "reading dysfunction as scar despite preserved counts"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-032_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Viable anterior territory myocardium; revascularization is favored and may improve regional function and ejection fraction.): Preserved resting counts in a dysfunctional left anterior descending territory (segments 1, 2, 7, 8, 13, 14, 17) with a chronic occlusion and collaterals describes hibernating myocardium. Uptake above about 50 to 60 percent of peak supports viability, so revascularization is expected to help. Further viability testing would add little because the SPECT already answers the question."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "During a gated blood-pool acquisition for a 55-year-old man, the technologist notes that the late frames of the cardiac cycle lose counts while the early frames look normal. The processed LVEF is 55 percent. Which finding at the time of acquisition would confirm the cause of this pattern?", "options": {"A": "A wide spread of R-R intervals on the histogram, with many beats shorter than average", "B": "A low count rate in the detector from a poor red cell labeling efficiency overall", "C": "An energy window shifted too high on the camera with reduced sensitivity in all frames", "D": "A uniformly narrow R-R histogram centered on the average heart rate"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "blames labeling, which dims all frames and not only late ones", "C": "blames the window, which would reduce counts in every frame", "D": "describes a regular rhythm that would give clean late frames"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-047_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A wide spread of R-R intervals on the histogram, with many beats shorter than average): Frames are filled in fixed time steps after each R wave, so when many beats are shorter than average the cycle ends before the late frames collect counts, and only those frames look dim. A wide R-R histogram with many short intervals shows this. Poor labeling or a misset energy window would lower counts across the whole cycle, and a narrow histogram would give uniform frames."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old man on anthracycline therapy had a baseline LVEF of 58 percent processed with a manually drawn background region. The follow-up study, with identical camera and view, is processed automatically and reports 50 percent. The cine shows symmetric contraction. What is the most appropriate next step before acting on the apparent decline?", "options": {"A": "Reacquire the study after a second injection using a new background protocol", "B": "Average the two reported fractions and record 54 percent", "C": "Reprocess both studies with the same background method and operator before judging any change", "D": "Report an 8-point decline and recommend stopping the anthracycline at the next scheduled visit"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "adds radiation instead of reprocessing the stored data", "B": "blends values processed by different methods", "D": "acts on a decline that may come from processing differences"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-042_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reprocess both studies with the same background method and operator before judging any change): Background region placement strongly affects counts-based ejection fraction, so values from manual and automatic processing are not directly comparable. The stored data should be reprocessed with the same method and operator, then compared. Reporting a decline now risks wrongly stopping therapy, averaging mixes inconsistent methods, and a second injection exposes the patient to more radiation when the original data can simply be reprocessed."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man with frequent premature beats has a gated SPECT cine whose last frames are sparse, and the software prints an EF of 50 percent with normal perfusion. A trainee asks which statement best explains why the printed EF cannot be trusted.", "options": {"A": "Gated EF is calculated from the lateral wall alone, which is poorly sampled in ectopic cycles.", "B": "Gated EF depends on defect size, so a perfusion defect makes the value invalid.", "C": "Counts in systole exceed camera count-rate capacity, flattening the volume curve at end-systole.", "D": "Late-diastolic frames are undersampled, so the volume curve is distorted and EF is misestimated."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misdescribing how EF is calculated", "B": "Linking EF validity to perfusion defect size", "C": "Inventing a camera saturation mechanism"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-015_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Late-diastolic frames are undersampled, so the volume curve is distorted and EF is misestimated.): The gated EF comes from the whole left ventricular volume curve across frames. When late-diastolic frames receive fewer counts, the curve near end-diastole is distorted, so end-diastolic volume and the resulting ratio shift. Perfusion defect size does not invalidate EF, the calculation uses the whole cavity, and camera count rates in SPECT cardiac studies are far below saturation."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A technologist prepares a resting gated blood-pool study in a 41-year-old man; the images will show both ventricles contracting normally with LVEF near 60 percent. The goal is the highest-quality red cell labeling for clean images without free pertechnetate in the stomach or thyroid. Which preparation best fits?", "options": {"A": "In vitro labeling with a commercial kit, which has higher efficiency than in vivo methods.", "B": "In vivo labeling after a stannous pyrophosphate injection, because stannous ions stay held inside the cells.", "C": "Tc-99m pertechnetate given alone, because free pertechnetate stays inside the vessels during the entire study.", "D": "Intravenous Tc-99m sulfur colloid, because it stays in the circulating blood pool and gives clear images."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overrating the in vivo method", "C": "Ignoring pertechnetate leakage into tissues", "D": "Choosing a liver-spleen agent for blood pool"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-018_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (In vitro labeling with a commercial kit, which has higher efficiency than in vivo methods.): Commercial in vitro kits label red cells outside the body and give the highest efficiency, with the least free pertechnetate in thyroid, stomach and bladder. In vivo labeling is easiest but less efficient. Sulfur colloid is taken up by the reticuloendothelial system, and free pertechnetate leaves the circulation, so neither gives a stable blood-pool image."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A gated SPECT study reports LVEF 55 percent. The beat-length histogram shows a broad spread with many beats outside the window, and the last cine frames look sparse. The perfusion images are otherwise diagnostic. How should the report handle the functional data?", "options": {"A": "Add 5 points to the EF to compensate for lost end-diastolic frames.", "B": "Report perfusion normally, say gated function is limited by rhythm, and qualify any EF given.", "C": "Withhold the whole study as nondiagnostic, since gating failure invalidates the perfusion images too.", "D": "Report 55 percent as measured, because the quality flag only affects regional thickening, not global EF."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inventing an unsupported correction factor", "C": "Overcalling a gating failure as total failure", "D": "Believing global EF is immune to poor gating"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-004_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report perfusion normally, say gated function is limited by rhythm, and qualify any EF given.): Perfusion images are summed over all accepted counts and stay interpretable when gating fails. Global EF and regional function depend on even frame sampling, so they need an explicit limitation statement. There is no validated correction factor, and global EF is affected along with regional motion. Calling the whole study nondiagnostic throws away usable perfusion information."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A technologist reports a gradual upward drift of 10 mm on a Tc-99m stress acquisition with 6.4 mm pixels. The laboratory corrects gradual drift in software when it is under 2 pixels and repeats the acquisition otherwise. What should be done?", "options": {"A": "Proceed without correction, since gradual drift has no effect on the reconstruction quality of the study.", "B": "Apply motion correction and proceed, since 10 mm is about 1.6 pixels and under the limit.", "C": "Repeat the acquisition, since any gradual drift should be repeated.", "D": "Repeat the acquisition, since 10 mm is about 3.1 pixels and over the limit."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assumes drift has no effect", "C": "ignores the stated correction limit", "D": "divides by 3.2 instead of 6.4"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-110_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply motion correction and proceed, since 10 mm is about 1.6 pixels and under the limit.): Ten millimeters divided by 6.4 millimeters per pixel is about 1.6 pixels, which is under the 2 pixel limit, so correction in software is permitted and the study can proceed. Using the wrong pixel size doubles the estimate, the policy sets a limit rather than repeating all drift, and uncorrected drift can blur the heart."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 70-year-old man with diabetes has exertional dyspnea and a resting LVEF of 42 percent on gated blood-pool imaging. The technologist reports reduced wall motion confined to the lateral wall, with the remaining walls moving normally. He has no angina. Which statement best explains the reduced ejection fraction and guides the next step?", "options": {"A": "Regional lateral dysfunction lowers the fraction, so stress perfusion imaging should seek ischemia or scar", "B": "Septal conduction delay lowers the fraction, so a pacing evaluation comes first", "C": "Global myocardial disease lowers the fraction, so cardiomyopathy therapy alone is enough to treat him", "D": "The fraction is a measurement artifact from background subtraction, so repeat the study"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "attributes the reduction to septal motion without a conduction abnormality", "C": "reads one abnormal wall as a global process", "D": "blames processing for a visible regional abnormality"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-033_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regional lateral dysfunction lowers the fraction, so stress perfusion imaging should seek ischemia or scar): The reported lateral wall motion abnormality is regional, so the mildly reduced LVEF of 42 percent comes from regional dysfunction, and the cause could be scar or reversible ischemia in the circumflex territory. Stress perfusion imaging separates the two and guides revascularization. Global therapy alone ignores the regional pattern, a processing artifact would not produce a reported regional motion abnormality, and no conduction delay is present."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with diabetes and atypical chest pain completes regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Perfusion is normal, there is no transient cavity dilation, and the gated cine shows uniform wall motion and thickening. Her primary physician worries about balanced three-vessel disease. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for coronary angiography because diabetes places her in a group where normal imaging is unreliable.", "B": "Repeat imaging with rubidium-82 PET to exclude balanced three-vessel disease before the normal study is reported.", "C": "Report normal and manage risk factors, since preserved function argues against balanced ischemia.", "D": "Add coronary calcium scoring first so the normal SPECT result is not issued alone."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "treats diabetes as an indication to ignore results", "B": "invokes balanced ischemia despite normal function", "D": "delays reporting for an unneeded test"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-006_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report normal and manage risk factors, since preserved function argues against balanced ischemia.): Balanced multivessel disease usually leaves clues such as transient ischemic dilation, post-stress dysfunction or a falling ejection fraction, and none are present here. A normal study with preserved function carries a low event rate, so the right step is a clear report and risk factor management. PET or angiography would add cost and radiation without a finding to justify them."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 62-year-old man with severe emphysema has an unreadable echocardiogram because of poor acoustic windows, so a gated blood-pool study is done and shows LVEF 28 percent. The referring physician asks why the radionuclide value can be trusted despite the dilated cavity. Which property of the method explains this?", "options": {"A": "The ejection fraction is derived from counts, so it needs no assumption about ventricular shape", "B": "Sampling heart rhythm over many beats makes ejection fraction independent of heart rate", "C": "Ventricular volume is calculated from the long axis using a fixed ellipsoid formula derived from echo", "D": "The study images the myocardium directly, so wall thickening is measured in millimeters"}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "claims rate independence instead of count-based calculation", "C": "applies the geometric model used by echocardiography", "D": "confuses blood-pool imaging with myocardial perfusion tracer uptake"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-028_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The ejection fraction is derived from counts, so it needs no assumption about ventricular shape): Equilibrium blood-pool imaging measures counts in the ventricle at end-diastole and end-systole, and counts are proportional to blood volume. The fraction therefore does not rely on cavity shape, which makes it dependable in dilated or distorted ventricles and when echo windows are poor. The tracer stays in the blood pool rather than the myocardium, volumes are not computed from a formula, and rate does not make the measurement independent."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman has rest and regadenoson stress rubidium-82 PET. The stress images show a lateral wall defect that is absent on the rest images. Gated wall motion is normal. The CT attenuation map is shifted 8 mm leftward on the stress study only. What is the best interpretation of the stress finding?", "options": {"A": "A true stress-induced lateral defect from circumflex ischemia, since it is present on the stress images", "B": "Likely artifact from stress misregistration; correct the registration and reprocess before interpreting", "C": "Drug-induced coronary steal, which should be reported as possible ischemia in the lateral wall territory", "D": "A fixed lateral scar from prior infarction, which would also appear on the rest images"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "reads the stress-only change as ischemia", "C": "invokes steal for an alignment effect", "D": "ignores that registration differs between the two scans"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-069_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Likely artifact from stress misregistration; correct the registration and reprocess before interpreting): A misregistered CT on one study creates a wall-specific artifact that can mimic ischemia. The defect appears only on the stress study where the map is shifted, so registration should be checked and the study reprocessed. Normal wall motion makes true ischemia less likely, and a scar would also appear at rest. Steal would reduce motion at stress in the territory as well."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 57-year-old man with diabetes had a Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT study read as a mild inferior defect. A repeat Rb-82 PET at regadenoson stress is now displayed with sharp wall edges and uniform uptake, and gated LVEF is 55 percent. What is the most reasonable interpretation of the two studies?", "options": {"A": "The PET is falsely normal because rubidium cannot detect balanced three-vessel ischemia, so recommend angiography", "B": "Normal PET suggests the SPECT inferior defect was diaphragmatic attenuation, so report no ischemia or infarct", "C": "The SPECT result is definitive and the PET should be overridden since sestamibi extraction is higher", "D": "Both studies are abnormal because a mild SPECT defect persists on any later PET examination"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "applies a balanced ischemia pitfall to a normal study", "C": "believes sestamibi extraction exceeds rubidium extraction", "D": "assumes defects carry over between modalities"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-052_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal PET suggests the SPECT inferior defect was diaphragmatic attenuation, so report no ischemia or infarct): Attenuation-corrected PET with normal perfusion and function argues strongly against a true inferior perfusion defect, leaving diaphragmatic attenuation on SPECT as the best explanation. Balanced ischemia is a limitation of relative perfusion imaging but is not a reason to call a clean study false, and it would typically show reduced EF or transient dilation. Rubidium extraction falls at high flow, not above sestamibi. A SPECT artifact does not obligate a PET defect."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a prior inferior infarct has gated SPECT with software EF 50 percent. His ECG during acquisition showed every third beat premature, and the cine's last frames look dim. Which interpretation of the EF and corrective plan is best?", "options": {"A": "The EF is accurate and only volumes are affected, so report it and omit end-diastolic volume.", "B": "The dim late frames show thinned inferior scar, so accept the EF and report reduced function.", "C": "The software sums counts that include rejected beats, so no further EF review is needed here.", "D": "The EF is unreliable; reprocess with stricter ectopic rejection, then reacquire the study if needed."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming ratio values escape the distortion", "B": "Reading a technical artifact as infarct scar", "C": "Misunderstanding how rejected beats are handled"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-006_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The EF is unreliable; reprocess with stricter ectopic rejection, then reacquire the study if needed.): Regular ectopy shortens some cycles, starving late frames of counts. Scar causes a fixed regional count and thickening deficit visible in every frame, not a progressive loss across the cycle. Both volumes and the ratio are distorted, so the EF needs reprocessing with better rejection or a repeat acquisition. Rejected beats are excluded from the gated frames, not included."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 71-year-old woman had exercise SPECT. Post-stress gating coincided with frequent premature beats, and its late frames are sparse; rest gating was clean. Post-stress LVEF is 55 percent and rest LVEF 56 percent. A colleague wants to report no post-stress stunning. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Apply attenuation correction to the post-stress cine so late frames recover and the EF stands.", "B": "Stunning cannot be excluded; post-stress gated data are unreliable, so say the comparison is limited.", "C": "Near-identical rest and post-stress EF exclude stunning, so call the study low risk for ischemia.", "D": "Repeat the rest study at once with a doubled dose to validate the post-stress gating result."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating beat-length loss as an attenuation artifact", "C": "Trusting matched EF numbers from a flawed acquisition", "D": "Choosing the wrong study to repeat"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-005_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stunning cannot be excluded; post-stress gated data are unreliable, so say the comparison is limited.): Post-stress stunning is judged by comparing post-stress with rest function, but only when both gated sets are valid. Sparse late frames show the post-stress set is distorted, so equal EF values prove nothing. The rest study is already clean. Attenuation correction operates on the reconstruction and cannot restore frames lost to beat-length variation."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A physician explains to a fellow why a 61-year-old man's inferior count reduction on SPECT coexists with an LVEF of 64 percent and normal inferior thickening on the gated cine. Which mechanism best explains the inferior count reduction with normal function?", "options": {"A": "Tracer redistribution lowers inferior counts on the delayed images while the gated cine remains normal.", "B": "Patient motion during the acquisition reduces inferior counts and spares wall function in the same region.", "C": "Reduced flow reserve in the right coronary territory lowers tracer uptake while resting motion stays normal.", "D": "Soft tissue and subdiaphragmatic organs absorb and scatter inferior photons, lowering counts but not motion."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using redistribution to explain a fixed artifact", "B": "Using motion artifact for an inferior defect", "C": "Applying flow reserve to a fixed resting defect"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-063_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Soft tissue and subdiaphragmatic organs absorb and scatter inferior photons, lowering counts but not motion.): Photons from the inferior wall pass through diaphragm and abdominal tissue, which absorb and scatter them, so counts fall while myocardium still thickens normally. Reduced flow reserve would show on stress, redistribution is a delayed-imaging phenomenon, and motion artifact does not selectively dim the inferior wall."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old woman of normal habitus had a two day Tc-99m sestamibi stress study planned with about 25 mCi (925 MBq). The projections are speckled and the heart barely stands above background. The dose log shows 8 mCi (296 MBq) was injected. No motion occurred. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the stress study on another day with the intended dose", "B": "Give the missing 17 mCi (629 MBq) now and image again within the hour", "C": "Triple the time per projection on this study and then report it as adequate", "D": "Reconstruct with maximum smoothing, then report all walls as normal on the noisy slices"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reinjecting at the same visit despite tracer already given", "C": "Trying to buy back a large dose shortfall with time", "D": "Smoothing noise and calling a non diagnostic study normal"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-118_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the stress study on another day with the intended dose): Count density is proportional to injected activity and acquisition time, but triple time is impractical and risks motion. A threefold dose shortfall is best corrected by repeating the stress on another day with the proper dose. Smoothing hides noise without adding information, and giving more tracer immediately adds dose and background from the first injection."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 58-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and no angina undergoes rubidium-82 PET. Perfusion counts are homogeneous at rest, the left ventricle looks dilated with globally reduced thickening, and the gated LVEF is 40 percent. Coronary calcium is minimal. What is the best interpretation to give the referring physician?", "options": {"A": "Nonischemic pattern; report LVEF 40 percent, LV dilation, and recommend cardiomyopathy workup", "B": "Probable three-vessel ischemia, because global hypokinesis with normal counts means balanced coronary stenosis is present", "C": "Technically inadequate study, since gated LVEF cannot be reported with a dilated ventricle", "D": "Large prior infarct, because an LVEF of 40 percent indicates extensive transmural scar"}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "reads balanced ischemia into homogeneous perfusion", "C": "treats dilation as a reason to withhold EF", "D": "equates moderate EF reduction with scar"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-056_1.mp4"], "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nonischemic pattern; report LVEF 40 percent, LV dilation, and recommend cardiomyopathy workup): Uniform perfusion with a dilated ventricle, diffuse hypokinesis and minimal calcium favors cardiomyopathy over a regional coronary substrate. Balanced ischemia can occur but is less likely with normal counts and no stress-related change, and it would not be the lead conclusion. Scar would show a fixed defect. Gated EF is still valid in a dilated ventricle, so the number is reported along with the volume."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 52-year-old woman is referred for gated SPECT before elective noncardiac surgery. The processing software reports a resting left ventricular ejection fraction of 64 percent with the cine showing brisk contraction. Which statement best describes this value on a gated SPECT study?", "options": {"A": "It is hyperdynamic, since any value above 60 percent is abnormal and suggests hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.", "B": "It is normal, since gated SPECT normal values begin near 50 percent and women run higher.", "C": "It is mildly reduced, since gated SPECT considers normal ejection fraction to start at 70 percent.", "D": "It is indeterminate, since gated SPECT ejection fraction is not valid until echocardiography confirms the measurement."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "uses 60 percent as the upper normal limit", "C": "uses the wrong normal threshold", "D": "claims gated SPECT is not valid for ejection fraction"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-004_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is normal, since gated SPECT normal values begin near 50 percent and women run higher.): Gated SPECT lower limits of normal sit around 50 percent, with women averaging a few points higher than men because of smaller cavities. A value of 64 percent therefore falls inside the normal range. Hyperdynamic function is usually described above roughly 70 percent with a small cavity, and gated SPECT ejection fraction is validated against MRI and echocardiography well enough to be used without confirmation."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 66-year-old man has a rest Tc-99m tetrofosmin study. A resident reports that extracardiac activity on the rotating raw images looks nearly as bright as the myocardium and abuts the inferior wall, and proposes ignoring it because it is below the heart. The inferior wall appears slightly reduced. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with reading, and lower the display threshold so the inferior wall appears normal.", "B": "Agree, because activity below the heart level cannot create an artifact in the adjacent myocardium.", "C": "Re-inject a second rest dose immediately so heart counts exceed the extracardiac activity.", "D": "Explain that activity near myocardial level can still distort the adjacent wall, so reacquire later."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "adjusts display to hide the defect", "B": "assumes a brightness threshold above which artifacts occur", "C": "believes a repeat dose fixes the ratio"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-094_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that activity near myocardial level can still distort the adjacent wall, so reacquire later.): Reconstruction artifacts depend on how close and how intense the neighbor is, and activity close to myocardial level can still reduce adjacent counts. Clearing time and rehydration followed by reacquisition is the clean answer. Changing the display only hides the issue, and a second dose raises both heart and gut activity, so the ratio barely moves."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 68-year-old man with frequent ventricular ectopy on telemetry has regadenoson Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. The software reports a gated LVEF of 55 percent, but the gated short-axis cine looks noisy and unstable toward the end of the cycle. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "The dim late frames reflect tracer decay, so repeat imaging tomorrow after a larger injected activity.", "B": "Gating is unreliable from beat-length variation; tighten rejection, then reprocess or reacquire.", "C": "The 55 percent EF stands because perfusion images are good; report it with wall motion findings.", "D": "Widen the energy window to recover late-frame counts, then recalculate the EF from the same data."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blaming isotope decay instead of the trigger", "C": "Assuming good perfusion quality validates gated function", "D": "Changing the wrong acquisition parameter"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-001_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gating is unreliable from beat-length variation; tighten rejection, then reprocess or reacquire.): Premature beats shorten some R-R intervals, so the last frames of the cycle collect fewer counts and the volume curve is distorted. The software still prints an EF, but it is not trustworthy. Review the beat-length histogram and the ECG trigger, tighten the acceptance window, and reprocess or reacquire. Good perfusion quality does not validate gating, decay does not cause a within-cycle count fall, and the energy window does not govern frame sampling."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 53-year-old man with chest pain has normal perfusion on SPECT and a software LVEF of 60 percent. Review shows broad beat-length variability and sparse late frames. The referring physician plans to cancel a pending echocardiogram based on this EF. What should the report say?", "options": {"A": "The study is nondiagnostic and must be repeated before any conclusion about perfusion is reached.", "B": "The EF is valid but volumes are inaccurate, so keep the echo to measure chamber size.", "C": "The 60 percent EF is valid because normal perfusion implies preserved function; cancel the pending echo.", "D": "Perfusion is interpretable, but the EF is unreliable and should not replace the echocardiography."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating failed gating as failed perfusion", "B": "Assuming ratio output survives a distorted cycle", "C": "Equating normal perfusion with verified function"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-010_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perfusion is interpretable, but the EF is unreliable and should not replace the echocardiography.): Perfusion uses all counts and remains valid. Gated EF depends on even sampling across the cycle, which this study lacks, so the 60 percent number cannot support clinical decisions. A normal perfusion pattern does not prove normal function. The report should state the limitation and leave the echo in place when ventricular function matters."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 64-year-old woman had treadmill stress Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT. Two clips are provided: Clip 1 after stress and Clip 2 at rest. The anterior, anteroseptal and apical walls are akinetic after stress and normal at rest, with LVEF about 52 percent after stress and 62 percent at rest. Which action fits best?", "options": {"A": "Treat with medical therapy alone and repeat the study in about one year", "B": "Order PET viability imaging first, since the anterior wall is normal at rest", "C": "Refer for angiography of the left anterior descending artery given the stunning", "D": "Refer for right coronary angiography because the septum is involved"}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a high-risk marker as low-risk", "B": "Applying viability testing to a wall that is not dysfunctional at rest", "D": "Matching septal involvement to the wrong artery"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-126_1.mp4", "clips/CLI-126_2.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for angiography of the left anterior descending artery given the stunning): Anterior, anteroseptal and apical walls belong to the left anterior descending artery. Stunning in this territory marks severe, extensive ischemia, so angiography is reasonable. Viability testing is for resting dysfunction, and the septum here is supplied by the anterior descending artery."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 60-year-old man with exertional dyspnea and a history of hypertension has a gated blood-pool study with both ventricles contracting normally and an LVEF near 62 percent. The referring physician asks what the study implies about his symptoms and the next step.", "options": {"A": "Normal LV contraction implies right ventricular failure, to be confirmed by right heart catheterization soon.", "B": "Systolic function is preserved, so seek diastolic or noncardiac causes and consider echocardiography.", "C": "A normal EF excludes a cardiac cause of exertional dyspnea, so the cardiac workup can stop.", "D": "Normal LV contraction implies right ventricular failure, to be confirmed by right heart catheterization."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inferring RV failure from normal RV contraction", "C": "Equating normal EF with a normal heart", "D": "Misnaming a normal EF as recovered"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-021_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Systolic function is preserved, so seek diastolic or noncardiac causes and consider echocardiography.): A preserved EF does not exclude heart failure with preserved EF, ischemia or valve disease, and it says little about filling pressures. The study shows the right ventricle also contracts normally, so right failure is not suggested. Echocardiography adds diastolic indices and valve data, which are the logical next step for dyspnea with a normal EF."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "performance_of_imaging_tests_including_instrumentation_protocols_and_processing", "question": "A 52-year-old woman on trastuzumab for breast cancer had a baseline blood-pool LVEF of 61 percent. Today both ventricles contract normally with LVEF near 56 percent, and she is asymptomatic with normal blood pressure. How should the result be handled?", "options": {"A": "Hold trastuzumab because any fall from baseline on a repeat study signals cardiotoxicity.", "B": "Start an ICD evaluation because EF has fallen below the usual 60 percent level.", "C": "Stop therapy because the value dropped under 60 percent, which marks the lower end of normal.", "D": "Continue therapy with routine surveillance, since the fall is under 10 points and EF stays normal."}, "level": "application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Calling cardiotoxicity from a small decline", "B": "Applying device criteria to a normal EF", "C": "Using 60 percent as a hard limit"}, "video": ["clips/CLP-019_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue therapy with routine surveillance, since the fall is under 10 points and EF stays normal.): Cardiotoxicity monitoring uses a decline of at least 10 points to a value below the lower limit of normal, about 50 to 53 percent. A drop from 61 to 56 percent meets neither condition, so treatment can continue with routine follow-up. Normal EF does not require a defibrillator, and 60 percent is not a cutoff."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 67-year-old man with angina and a known chronically occluded proximal left anterior descending artery has ischemic cardiomyopathy. Gated SPECT shows akinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex, an ejection fraction of 40 percent and resting counts in those segments close to the remaining walls. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "The anterior akinesis is a gating artifact, so the study should be repeated before treatment decisions.", "B": "These segments are scar because they are akinetic, so medical therapy and ICD evaluation come next.", "C": "Viability cannot be judged from sestamibi counts, so fluorodeoxyglucose PET is needed before revascularization is discussed.", "D": "The anterior wall is viable and likely hibernating, so evaluation for revascularization is appropriate now."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "calls a regional abnormality an artifact", "B": "equates akinesis with scar", "C": "claims SPECT counts cannot show viability"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-019_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The anterior wall is viable and likely hibernating, so evaluation for revascularization is appropriate now.): Sestamibi counts that remain well preserved in an akinetic territory show viable myocardium, which in a patient with a chronic LAD occlusion and angina is consistent with hibernation. Such segments often improve after revascularization, so the next step is evaluation for it. Motion alone cannot separate scar from viable muscle, and a clear count signal does not need a confirmatory PET."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 71 year old woman develops chest pain and a modest troponin rise after the death of her husband. Coronary angiography shows no obstructive disease. Rest gated SPECT shows apical anterior, apical septal and apical akinesis with preserved counts, and LVEF is 43 percent. Which diagnosis best accounts for the findings?", "options": {"A": "Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with increased apical counts and no recoverable wall motion abnormality.", "B": "Completed distal left anterior descending infarction needing revascularization to restore apical function.", "C": "Stress cardiomyopathy with apical stunning that crosses coronary territories and recovers over weeks.", "D": "Chronic hibernation from severe left anterior descending stenosis, requiring FDG PET before any decision."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "confusing apical akinesis with apical hypertrophy", "B": "reading akinesis as scar despite preserved counts and clear arteries", "D": "assuming hibernation despite unobstructed coronary arteries"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-028_1.mp4"], "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stress cardiomyopathy with apical stunning that crosses coronary territories and recovers over weeks.): A emotional trigger, unobstructed coronary arteries, apical akinesis and preserved counts form the pattern of stress (takotsubo) cardiomyopathy, which is stunning rather than necrosis. Scar would show reduced counts, and hibernation needs a flow limiting stenosis that angiography excluded. Apical hypertrophy shows thickened walls with vigorous systolic motion, not akinesis."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 59 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has gated SPECT. The cine shows the mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral walls are akinetic, and the quantitative polar map reports resting counts near 25 percent of peak in those same segments. The rest of the left ventricle moves normally. LVEF is 37 percent. Which statement is best?", "options": {"A": "Right coronary scar, because inferolateral segments depend on the posterior descending artery.", "B": "Hibernating lateral myocardium that should improve with revascularization given retained wall thickness on the gated images.", "C": "Distal left anterior descending scar, since the left anterior descending supplies the mid lateral wall.", "D": "Circumflex marginal territory scar with matched perfusion and motion loss, leaving little viable lateral myocardium."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "assigning lateral segments to the right coronary artery", "B": "ignoring severely reduced uptake when judging viability", "C": "assigning lateral segments to the left anterior descending artery"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-029_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Circumflex marginal territory scar with matched perfusion and motion loss, leaving little viable lateral myocardium.): Segments 11 and 12 (mid inferolateral and mid anterolateral) belong to the circumflex system. Counts near 25 percent matched to akinesis mean scar, and recovery after revascularization is unlikely because viable myocardium typically keeps counts above about 50 percent of peak. The left anterior descending and right coronary arteries do not supply the mid lateral wall."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A nuclear cardiology laboratory wants to reduce upward creep on treadmill stress Tc-99m sestamibi studies, which show the heart rising over the acquisition when imaging starts too soon. Which delay between exercise injection and the start of acquisition is generally recommended to reduce it?", "options": {"A": "Wait 4 hours so the myocardial tracer concentration reaches equilibrium.", "B": "Wait 90 minutes so all hepatobiliary activity has cleared before the stress acquisition begins.", "C": "Start within 2 minutes of the end of exercise to catch peak uptake.", "D": "About 15 to 30 minutes after injection, allowing heart rate and breathing to settle."}, "level": "recall", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "applies a thallium redistribution timeline", "B": "applies a rest imaging delay", "C": "applies urgency that raises post exercise motion"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-108_1.mp4"], "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 15 to 30 minutes after injection, allowing heart rate and breathing to settle.): For technetium agents after exercise, imaging typically begins 15 to 30 minutes after injection, which lets the heart rate and ventilation settle and reduces creep while hepatobiliary activity falls. Very early imaging increases drift, a four hour wait matches a different tracer concept, and ninety minutes is unnecessarily long and delays imaging."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "interpretation_of_nuclear_cardiology_imaging_tests", "question": "A 49-year-old woman with chest pain has supine Tc-99m tetrofosmin SPECT, and the fixed anterior count reduction is read together with the gated cine and the perfusion images. Calculated LVEF is 60 percent. What is the best next step before angiography is recommended?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the study with Tl-201 because its lower energy photons penetrate breast tissue more easily.", "B": "Confirm normal gated anterior thickening and report probable breast attenuation rather than disease.", "C": "Report anterior scar and defer treatment because normal function does not change the interpretation of counts.", "D": "Refer for left anterior descending angiography because anterior count reduction indicates obstructive disease in that vessel."}, "level": "diagnose", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the energy and penetration relationship", "C": "Dismissing gated function in interpretation", "D": "Equating anterior count loss with obstructive disease"}, "video": ["clips/CLI-062_1.mp4"], "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm normal gated anterior thickening and report probable breast attenuation rather than disease.): A fixed anterior count reduction with normal anterior motion and thickening is typical of breast attenuation, so gated function should be used to lower the probability of disease before angiography is considered. Tl-201 is attenuated more than Tc-99m agents. Scar would reduce thickening."}];
